{[%evp 0,61,19,-12,-27,-33,-27,-42,-31,-47,-19,-19,-9,-17,57,42,42,62,21,23,23,16,65,70,103,20,30,11,4,22,19,17,62,11,11,48,108,162,328,234,234,224,250,281,272,252,324,338,616,474,322,346,538,569,1152,1394,1598,1526,1939,28516,28521,29982,29983,29984]} 1. c4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. g3 Bb4 4. e4 Bxc3 5. bxc3 O-O 6. f3?! {One of the controversial ideas tested by Magnus Carlsen in online chess.} (6. Bg2 {leads to a normal theoretical position.}) 6... b5!? {This pawn sacrifice is typical when White's light-squared bishop is already on g2, and after cxb5 a6 bxa6 Bxa6 its black counterpart can take a powerful position. Here White's bishop is more active, the position opens up, and White's bishop pair can become powerful.} (6... Re8 {[%csl Yc6][%cal Yc7c6,Yd7d5] is more normal, and now Carlsen has tried two plans:} 7. Nh3 ({and} 7. Kf2!? c6 8. d4 d5 9. cxd5 cxd5 10. dxe5 Rxe5 11. c4! d4 12. Ne2 Nc6 13. Nf4 Nd7 14. Nd5 {1/2 (67) Carlsen,M (2863)-Caruana,F (2835) lichess.org INT 2020} f5 $132) 7... c6 8. Nf2 d5 9. cxd5 cxd5 10. Bg2 b6 11. O-O Ba6 12. Re1 Nc6 $13 {1/2 (70) Carlsen,M (2863)-Aronian,L (2773) lichess.org INT 2020}) 7. d4! {[%mdl 32]} (7. cxb5 d5!? $44) 7... exd4 8. cxd4 bxc4 9. e5 Nd5 10. Bxc4 Bb7 {A bit too passive.} (10... Nb6 11. Bd3 (11. Bb3 d6) 11... Bb7 $13 {[%cal Yd7d6] was more accurate.}) 11. Nh3 d6 (11... Nb6 {gives White a nice possibility to weaken Black's kingside:} 12. Qd3!? Nxc4 13. Ng5! g6 14. Qxc4 $36) 12. O-O ({Here, too,} 12. Qd3! {was appropriate.}) 12... Nd7 13. Re1 dxe5 14. dxe5 N7b6 (14... N5b6!? 15. e6! (15. Bf1? Nxe5!) 15... Ne5! 16. exf7+ Kh8 17. Be2 Qf6 (17... Qxd1 18. Bxd1 Nxf3+ 19. Bxf3 Bxf3 20. Ng5 $18 {[%cal Rc1a3]}) 18. Nf4 Qxf7 19. Bb2 $36) 15. Bb3 Qe7 (15... c5!? 16. Ba3! $36) 16. e6! fxe6 {After this capture White's knight powerfully enters the game.} (16... f6! {was relatively better although White kept advantage due to his strong e6 pawn.}) 17. Ng5 Rf6 18. Qc2 Rg6? {Black loses at once.} (18... g6 19. Bb2 c5 {, giving up an exchange, was a lesser evil. White should win in any case, e.g.} 20. Bxf6 Qxf6 21. Ne4! ({but not} 21. Nxe6? c4 22. Bxc4 Rc8) 21... Qd4+ (21... Qxf3 22. Qxc5 Rc8 23. Qd4 $18 {[%cal Re4g5]}) 22. Qf2 Qxf2+ 23. Kxf2 c4 24. Bd1 {etc.}) 19. Rxe6! $18 Qxe6 20. Nxe6 Rxe6 21. Bb2 Rae8 22. Bd4 Kh8 23. Qf5 Bc8 24. Qh5 c5 25. Bxc5 Re5 26. Qf7 Bh3 27. Bf8 Re1+ 28. Rxe1 Rxe1+ 29. Kf2 Rf1+ 30. Ke2 Nc3+ 31. Kd3 1-0

