1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Be7 5. Bg5 h6 6. Bh4 O-O 7. e3 Ne4 8. Bxe7 Qxe7 9. Rc1 c6 10. Qc2 Nxc3 11. Qxc3 dxc4 {A dubious decision: Black allows White's bishop to get to c4 in one go.} (11... Nd7 {is more accurate and common.}) 12. Bxc4 b6 (12... Nd7 13. Be2!? Re8 14. O-O e5 15. Rfe1 exd4 16. exd4 Nf8 17. d5 Bg4 18. h3 Bh5 19. dxc6 bxc6 20. Bf1 Qb7 21. Rxe8 Rxe8 22. Nd4 $16 {1-0 (68) Bischoff,K (2533)-Senff,M (2449) Germany 2006}) 13. O-O {Too conventional.} (13. Be2!? Bb7 14. Ne5 Rc8 15. O-O $142 {as it is not easy for Black to push ...c6-c5 without concessions:} (15. Bh5 {(Hort) is not very promising due to} g6 16. Bf3 c5 {as} 17. Bxb7 Qxb7 {[%cal Yb8d7]} 18. Ng4 {doesn't work:} Qxg2 19. Nf6+ Kg7 20. dxc5 Qxh1+ 21. Ke2 Qxc1 22. Qxc1 Rxc5! ({but not} 22... Kxf6? 23. cxb6! Rxc1 24. b7)) 15... Nd7 (15... c5 16. b4!?) 16. Nxd7 Qxd7 17. Qb3 (17. b4!?) 17... Qe7 (17... c5 {leads to an isolated pawn}) 18. Bf3 {etc.}) 13... Nd7 {More accurate than} (13... Bb7 14. Ne5!? (14. e4 c5 15. d5 exd5 16. exd5 Nd7 17. Rfe1 Qd6 (17... Qf6!? 18. Bb5 (18. Ne5!?) 18... Qxc3 19. bxc3 Nf6 {Hort} 20. c4 a6 21. Bc6 $14) 18. Nh4!? g6! (18... Qf6 19. Qxf6 Nxf6 20. d6 $14 {1-0 (29) Hort,V-Donner,J Wijk aan Zee 1970}) 19. Qe3 Rfe8! 20. Qxh6 Ne5 $132) 14... Nd7 15. Nxd7 Qxd7 16. Be2! Rfc8 (16... Qe7 17. Bf3 Rac8 18. Qb3 Rc7 19. Qa4 c5 20. Bxb7 Rxb7 21. dxc5 bxc5 22. b3 $14 {1-0 (41) Frydman,P-Gershman,J Buenos Aires 1939}) 17. b4 (17. Rfd1 Qe7 18. Bf3 c5 19. Bxb7 Qxb7 $11 {1/2 (27) Kieninger,G-Schmidt,P Heidelberg 1949}) 17... a5 18. a3 axb4 19. axb4 Qd5 20. Bf3 Qb5 21. Rb1 Ra2 22. Rfc1 $14 {1-0 (40) Huzman,A (2570)-Ravia,M (2330) Israel 1996}) 14. Rfd1 (14. b4 Bb7 15. Be2 Rfc8 16. Rfd1 {1/2 (16) Kantsler,B (2455)-Ravia,M (2370) Petah Tiqwa 1996} a5 $132) 14... Bb7 15. h3 (15. e4 Rfd8 16. Qe3 c5 17. h3 cxd4 18. Nxd4 Rac8 19. f3 Ne5 $11 {1-0 (36) Sanikidze,T (2426)-Becker,M (2330) Dresden 2007}) 15... c5 $11 {Black has solved his opening problems.} 16. d5 {A harmless attempt. Black's queenside pawns will not be less strong than White's central pawns, which are not too advanced.} exd5 17. Bxd5 Bxd5 18. Rxd5 Nf6 19. Rd3 Rad8 20. Rcd1 Rxd3 21. Qxd3 Rc8 22. a4 c4 23. Qc2 Qe4 24. Nd4 c3 25. Qb3 Qd5 26. Qc2 Qe4 27. Qxe4 Nxe4 {[%mdl 4096]} 28. b3 Nd2?! {A risky adventure. It was better to take another route:} (28... Nc5!? {(K.Sakaev)} 29. Kf1 Na6 (29... a5 30. Ke2 Na6 {[%cal Ya6b4]}) 30. Nc2 Nc5 $11) 29. Rc1 a5 (29... c2?! 30. b4) 30. Rc2 (30. f3 c2 {[%cal Yc8c3]}) 30... Nb1 31. Rc1 (31. f3 Na3 32. Rc1 c2 33. Kf2 Rc3 {, and White can't make progress.}) 31... Nd2 32. Rc2 Nb1 33. Nb5 Kf8 (33... Rd8 34. Rc1 Rd3 (34... c2 35. Rxc2 Rd1+ 36. Kh2 Nd2 37. Kg3 Nxb3 38. Rc6 {is risky for Black}) 35. g4 c2 36. Rxc2 Rxb3 37. Rc8+ Kh7 38. Rc6 Rb4 39. Nd6 {, and White fights on.}) 34. f3 Rd8 35. Nd4 {White could have kept winning chances by means of} (35. Kf2! Rd2+ 36. Rxd2 cxd2 37. Ke2 Ke7 38. g4) 35... Rc8 36. Rc1 Na3 37. Nc2 Nxc2 38. Rxc2 {White eventually wins the c3-pawn in this rook ending but Black manages to trade all the queenside pawns, after which a theoretically drawn endgame arises.} Rc5 39. Kf2 b5 40. axb5 Rxb5 41. Rxc3 g6 42. f4 (42. g4 h5!) 42... h5 {It is useful for Black to put his rook pawn here as it makes his task easier. White's further attempts are absolutely fruitless.} 43. Rc8+ Kg7 44. Ra8 Rxb3 45. Rxa5 Rb2+ 46. Kf3 Rb3 47. Ra7 Rc3 48. Re7 Rb3 49. Kg3 Rb2 50. Kf3 Rb3 51. g4 hxg4+ 52. hxg4 Rb1 53. Rd7 Rf1+ 54. Ke4 Rg1 55. g5 Ra1 56. Ke5 Ra3 57. e4 Ra5+ 58. Kd6 Ra6+ 59. Ke5 Ra5+ 60. Rd5 Ra4 61. Rc5 Ra1 62. Rc2 Rf1 63. Rc7 Re1 64. Ra7 Rg1 65. Ra4 Rf1 66. Ra6 Re1 67. Kd4 Rd1+ 68. Ke3 Re1+ 69. Kf3 Rf1+ 70. Kg3 Re1 71. e5 Re3+ 72. Kf2 Rb3 73. Rd6 Ra3 74. Rd8 Rc3 75. Ke2 Ra3 76. Rd3 Ra1 77. Ke3 Re1+ 78. Kd4 Rf1 79. Ke4 Ra1 80. Rd7 Ra4+ 81. Rd4 Ra5 82. Rc4 Kf8 83. Rc8+ Kg7 84. Rc7 Kf8 85. Kd4 Ra4+ 86. Rc4 Ra5 87. Rc8+ Kg7 88. e6 fxe6 89. Rc5 Ra7 90. Ke5 Rf7 91. Rc4 Rf5+ 92. Kxe6 Kg8 93. Rc8+ Kg7 94. Rc7+ Kg8 95. Rc8+ Kg7 1/2-1/2

