1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. Bg5 h6 6. Bxf6 Qxf6 7. e3 Nd7 8. Rc1 g6 9. Be2 (9. Bd3 {is more common, with a usual continuation being} dxc4 10. Bxc4) 9... Bg7 (9... dxc4 10. Bxc4 {was possible here, too.}) 10. cxd5 {[%mdl 32] White goes for the classic Karlsbad structure, for which his rook is not placed properly on c1 but neither is Black's bishop on g7.} ({He can include mutual castling first:} 10. O-O O-O (10... dxc4 11. Bxc4 {again leads to a common position}) 11. cxd5 (11. e4 dxe4 12. Nxe4 Qf4 13. Qc2 f5 14. Nc3 Qc7 15. Rfd1 e5 16. d5 {0-1 (31) Kanep,M (2505)-Fridman,D (2653) Riga 2012} e4 $132) (11. Qc2 Qe7 12. Rfd1 a6!? 13. a3 Rb8 14. h3 Kh8 15. Nd2 Rd8 16. b4 b5 17. c5 e5 18. Nb3 Nf8 19. dxe5 Qxe5 20. Nd4 Qc7 21. h4 Ne6 $132 {1/2 (48) Aronian,L (2816)-Kasimdzhanov,R (2684) Istanbul 2012}) 11... exd5 12. b4 a6 (12... Nb6 {1/2 (17) Tosic,M (2440)-Kosic,D (2455) Kladovo 1993} 13. b5!) 13. a4 Qd6 14. Qb3 (14. b5!?) 14... b5 15. axb5 axb5 16. e4 {1/2 (42) Devereaux,M (2329)-Horton,J Port Erin 2003} Nb6! $11 17. exd5 $140 Nxd5 18. Nxd5 Be6!) ({If} 10. b4 a6 11. a4 {then} dxc4 12. Bxc4 O-O 13. O-O Qe7 $11 14. b5?! axb5 15. axb5 c5 16. Be2 cxd4 17. Nxd4 Nb6 18. Ra1 Rxa1 19. Qxa1 e5 20. Nf3 Be6 $36 {0-1 (60) Kolbus,D (2416)-Malakhatko,V (2556) Port Erin 2005}) 10... exd5 11. b4 {[%cal Yb4b5]} a6 (11... Qd6 {is less accurate:} 12. b5 O-O 13. O-O Nb6 14. bxc6 bxc6 (14... Qxc6 15. Ne5 Qe8 16. Nd3 Bd7 17. Nc5 Bc6 18. Qb3 Qe7 19. a4 Qg5 20. Rfd1 Rad8 21. Nb5 Nc8 22. Bf3 a6 23. Nc3 $16 {1/2 (61) Shchekachev,A (2537)-Bajarani,U (2424) Paris 2011}) 15. Na4 Re8 16. Nc5 Rb8 17. Re1 Nd7 18. Nd3 Ba6 19. Nf4 Bb7 20. Qa4 a6 21. Nd3 Rec8 22. Qa5 $14 {1-0 (46) Shchekachev,A (2528)-Lamorelle,J (2277) Paris 2012}) 12. a4 O-O 13. b5 axb5 14. axb5 {The classic minority attack leads to a typical position with the weak c6-pawn (in the event of b5xc6 b7xc6) or weak b- and d-pawns in Black's camp (in the event of ...c6xb5 or b5xc6 xc6). Usually Black opts for the former setup as it is difficult for White to convert such a small advantage. Often (in the present game, too) White refrains from the early capture on c6 so that the b-file remains closed, and he can sometimes attack the b7-pawn.} Qd6 15. O-O Nb6 16. Qb3 Rb8 17. Nd1 {The knight goes to d3 and then possibly to c5 or e5.} Bf5 18. Nb2 Rfc8 19. Nd3 Bxd3 {A simple way to disrupt White's plan. However, the exchange of Black's light-squared bishop has a drawback as it is a good defender of his position.} 20. Qxd3 c5 {Another typical transformation. Black prefers to have an isolated central pawn, safely protected by the perfectly placed b6 knight. In many cases (including this one) it is quite enough for equality.} 21. dxc5 Rxc5 $11 {However, the World Champion is famous for regularly outplaying his rivals in quiet equal positions.} 22. h4 Na4 (22... h5 {was quite possible (and would have been probably played against any other opponent):} 23. Ng5 Rxc1 24. Rxc1 Ra8 $11) 23. h5 Rbc8?! {Another timorous move.} (23... Nc3 $142 24. hxg6 Qxg6 $11) 24. Rxc5 Nxc5 {The knight is hardly placed better on c5 than on b6.} 25. Qc2 {Now the destruction of Black's queenside is inevitable. He must switch to defence.} gxh5 (25... Qe6 26. Rc1! $36) 26. Nd4 Qg6 27. Nf5 $14 {[%csl Gf5]} Bf8 28. Rd1 Qe6 29. Rc1 Nb3! {[%mdl 256] A correct simplification.} 30. Qxc8 Nxc1 31. Qxc1 Qxf5 {The opposite-coloured bishops should secure Black a draw.} 32. Qc7 Qb1+ 33. Bf1 d4! 34. exd4 (34. Qxb7 dxe3 35. fxe3 Qe1 {[%cal Yf8d6,Ye1g3]}) 34... Qd1 (34... Qe4 $142 ! $11 35. Bc4 $140 Qe1+ 36. Kh2 Qxf2) 35. Qe5! (35. Qxb7 Qxd4 $11 {[%cal Yf8c5]}) 35... Bg7 36. Qe8+ Bf8 37. Qd8 Kg7 (37... Qd2 $142 !? 38. d5 Kg7) 38. Qd5! {White is trying all possible chances.} b6 39. Qe5+ Kg8 40. Qf6 Bg7 (40... Bb4 $142 ! 41. Qxb6 (41. Qxh6 Qxd4 {[%cal Yb4c5]}) 41... Kg7 {[%cal Yb4c3,Yc3d4]} 42. Qd8 Bc5! {, which is not easy to find, though.}) 41. Qxb6 Bxd4 42. Qxh6 Qg4 43. Qd6 Qd1 44. Qd8+ Kh7?! {A serious inaccuracy.} (44... Kg7! 45. Qg5+ Kh7 {was still a clear draw.}) 45. Qc7! {White's passed pawn now moves forward.} Kg7 46. b6 Qg4! {The only way to create counterplay.} (46... Qd2?? 47. Qg3+ Kh7 48. b7) 47. b7 Qh4! 48. g3 Qf6 49. Qc2 Qe5 {Black has stopped White's pawn at the last moment. The draw is still highly probable.} 50. Qd3 Ba7 51. Qf3 Qf6 52. Qe2 {The queen exchange leads to a draw, e.g.} (52. Be2 Qxf3 53. Bxf3 Kg6 54. Kg2 f5 55. Be2 Bb8 (55... f4 56. gxf4 Bb8 {is good enough}) 56. Kh3 Be5 57. f4 Bc7 58. Kg2 (58. Kh4 Bd8+) 58... h4! $11) 52... Qc3 53. Kh2 Qd4 54. Qf3 Bb8? {Surprisingly, this is a big mistake.} (54... h4! {was correct as} 55. g4 Qe5+ 56. Kg2 Bb8 {lead to a dead draw.}) 55. Kh3 Bc7 (55... Qf6 56. Qxf6+ Kxf6 57. Kh4 Kg6 58. Be2 {- Black's bishop is placed wrongly, unable to give a check, therefore he loses the h5-pawn and the game.}) 56. Be2 Bb8 57. Bd1 {[%cal Rf3h5] White doesn't want to calculate the queen endgame after} (57. Qxh5 Qxf2 58. Qg5+ Kf8 59. Qd8+ Kg7 60. Qxb8 Qxe2) 57... f5 {Desperation.} 58. Be2 f4 (58... Qg4+ 59. Qxg4+ hxg4+ 60. Bxg4 fxg4+ 61. Kxg4 $18 {White's pawns easily prevail as his king goes to the queenside at the decisive moment.}) 59. Qxh5 Qxf2!? 60. Qg5+ Kf7 $138 {White lost on time unaware of the time rate. He still needed several accurate checks to construct the mating net:} (60... Kf7 61. Bc4+ Ke8 62. Bb5+! {Only this; if Black's king escapes to the queenside, it will be safe!} Kf7 63. Qf5+ Kg7 64. Qd7+! Kf6 65. Qd8+! Kg7 (65... Kf7 66. Bc4+ Kg6 67. Qg8+ Kf6 68. Qf8+ Kg5 69. Qg7+ {leads to the same}) 66. Qe7+ Kh6 67. Qf6+ Kh7 68. Bd3+ Kg8 69. Bc4+ Kh7 70. Qf7+ Kh6 71. Qf8+! Kg5 72. Qg7+ Kf5 73. g4+ Ke4 74. Qg6+ Kd4 75. Qb6+ $18) 0-1

