1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+ Nd7 4. O-O Nf6 (4... a6 {is the another main line, but after} 5. Bd3 Ngf6 {White doesn't have to play 6.e1, he can immediately answer with} 6. c3 {followed by c2, d4.}) 5. Re1 a6 6. Bd3 b5 7. c4 g5!? $146 {A very interesting and ambitious novelty by Topalov. Nowadays ...g5 becomes typical in these type of position. In case of White captures the  Black has some play on the g-file and in case of White ignores, Black continues with ...g4 and takes some pressure in the centre.} ({Last year Nakamura played} 7... Ne5 {against Carlsen,} 8. Bf1 Nxc4 9. a4 Bd7 10. d4 cxd4 11. Nxd4 e5 12. axb5 exd4 13. Bxc4 axb5 14. Rxa8 Qxa8 15. Bb3 Be7 16. Qxd4 $14 {and White had a comfortable position, Carlsen-Nakamura, Zuerich rapid 2014.}) 8. Nxg5 {Carlsen takes the gauntlet.} (8. cxb5!? {was the alternative,} g4 9. e5!? dxe5 10. Ng5 Nb6 {with a very complicated position.}) 8... Ne5 9. Be2 bxc4 10. Na3 (10. Nc3!? {was also possible,} Rg8 11. d4 cxd3 12. Bxd3) ({or} 10. Nf3!? Nd3 11. Bxd3 cxd3 12. e5! Nd5 (12... dxe5 13. Nxe5 {and White has a dangerous initiative since} Bb7? {loses instantly,} 14. Qa4+! Nd7 15. Nxf7! Kxf7 16. Qb3+ Kg7 17. Qxb7 $18 {and White wins.}) 13. Na3 Rg8 $132 {with a double-edged fight.}) 10... Rg8 11. Nxc4?! {A pretty piece sacrifice, but objectively this is not so good!} (11. Nf3?! {could have been met by} Nd3 12. Bxd3 cxd3 13. Nc4 (13. e5 dxe5 14. Nxe5 Qd5 $17) 13... Be6 $15 {and Black looks fine.}) ({Probably} 11. d4 {was the most accurate, but} cxd3 12. Bxd3 h6 13. Nf3 Nxd3 14. Qxd3 d5! $132 {and Black has a nice counterplay.}) 11... Nxc4 (11... Rxg5? {is bad, because of} 12. d4 $16 {and White is better.}) 12. d4 {This was the point of the piece sacrifice.} Nb6 13. Bh5 (13. dxc5 dxc5 14. Qxd8+ Kxd8 15. Nxf7+ Ke8 16. Ng5 e5 $15 {and two s are not enough.}) 13... Nxh5 14. Qxh5 Rg7 (14... Rg6! {was more accurate. The  is much more active, and after} 15. Qxh7 Rg7 16. Qh8 cxd4 17. Nh7 Rxh7 18. Qxh7 e5 $17 {and Black has a wonderful position.}) 15. Nxh7 Qd7 (15... Bg4 16. Qh4 Rxh7 17. Qxh7 cxd4 18. Bh6 Bxh6 19. Qxh6 e5 20. f4 {and still White has some counterchance.}) 16. dxc5 dxc5 17. e5? {Decisive mistake.} (17. Nxf8 {was the only move} Qh3! (17... Qg4 18. Qxg4 Rxg4 19. Nh7 f6 20. Ng5! fxg5 21. g3! $18 {followed by f3 and the  is trapped.}) 18. Qxh3 Bxh3 19. g3 Nc4 (19... Kxf8 20. Bh6 {might even favour White as Black's pieces are not well coordinated.}) (19... Rg8 20. Nh7 f6 $13) 20. Bh6 Rg8 $13) 17... Qc6! ({The natural} 17... Bb7?? {is losing immediately, as} 18. Nf6+ exf6 19. exf6+ $18) 18. f3 ({Now} 18. Nf6+ {could have been met by} Kd8! $17 {and Black is better.}) 18... Qg6! {A good move, Black forces to exchange of s and he avoids White's practical chances.} 19. Nf6+ Kd8! {The black  simply slides over to safety.} 20. Qxg6 Rxg6 21. Ne4 Bb7 22. h4 Rc8 23. h5 Rg8 24. Bd2 Nc4 25. Bc3 Bh6 26. Rad1+ Ke8 27. Rd3 Bf4! $19 {Very precise play by Black, his position should be winning.} 28. Nf2 Bc6 29. Nh3 Bg3 30. Re2 Bb5 31. Rd1 Bc6 32. Nf2 Bxe5! {A beautiful tactical solution! White's position is now hopeless.} 33. Ng4 (33. Bxe5 Bxf3 34. Rde1 Bxe2 35. Rxe2 Nxe5 36. Rxe5 f6 $19) 33... Bxc3 34. bxc3 Kf8 35. Kf2 Rh8 36. Ne5 Nxe5 37. Rxe5 Be8 38. g4 f6 39. Re6 Bb5 40. Rde1 Rc7 0-1

