{Kramnik used to be a difficult opponent for Magnus, but while the balance had obviously changed, 2016 had become an extreme, the score being 7-0 with no draws before this game! Only 1 was classical chess, but even so, quite an extraordinary situation between the world ranking nos. 1 and 2!} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. Re1!? {Like their game in Qatar (still 2015!), where a draw sufficed for Magnsu to clinch first.} Nd6 6. Nxe5 Be7 7. Bf1 Nxe5 8. Rxe5 O-O 9. d4 Bf6 10. Re1 Re8 11. c3 Rxe1 12. Qxe1 Ne8 13. Bf4 d5 14. Bd3 g6 15. Nd2 Ng7 16. Nf1!? {Deviating from the Qatar game, which went 16.f3. This move looks like just swapping down to a draw, but Magnus had a specific idea in mind.} Bf5 17. Bxf5 Nxf5 18. Ne3 Nxe3 19. Qxe3 Qd7 20. Re1 {Obviously the small details are in White's favour. Anyhow they seem too small to matter. Personallly I always suffered as Black against 5.e1, but told myself it's you, not the position there is something wrong with. Just give it to Kramnik, and he will show how to draw I used to think. He, however, also commits an inaccuracy. Best would be first ...h5! creating "luft" for the king, and even later maybe the h-pawn can be used positively for an attack. It's hard to believe Black should be in any trouble; now, however, it is another matter:} c6? 21. Bh6! {Suddenly Black is in dire straits. He can in no way activate his queen, as White has back rank mate motifs with the queen sacrifice e8+!. Thus there is no alternative to the exchange of bishops:} Bg7 22. Bxg7 Kxg7 23. Qe7! {The big differance with the disappearance of the bishops, is that white gets access to e7.} Rd8 ({The computer suggests exchanging with} 23... Qxe7 24. Rxe7 Rb8 {but any human would say that} 25. f3 Kf6 26. Rc7 Ke6 27. Kf2 {is just terrible for Black who needs to keep b7 and f7 defended, meaning White can in peace and quiet try and break through with king and pawns on either side of the board.}) 24. h4 h5 25. Re3 b6 26. Qe5+ Kg8 27. Kh2 a5 28. f3 a4 29. Kg3 b5 30. Kf4 {This position led to quite a discussion in the players room, where participants used to gather when their games had finished. The engines shows only a moderate edge for White, but quite a few said that White should be just winning. There is a lot of logic to that statement. g5 is a threat, and also g4 next could prove very difficult to handle for Black.} Qd6? ({But turning on the engines, I cannot prove a deceisive edge against} 30... Rf8! {Much in the style of the game would be} 31. a3!? {trying to claim zugzwang!} ({The obvious point is that} 31. Kg5? {is impossible due to} f6+) ({and after} 31. g4 hxg4 32. fxg4 f5! {Black seems to hold. The point is} 33. g5 ({and} 33. Qe6+ Qxe6 34. Rxe6 fxg4+ 35. Kxg4 Kg7 36. Rxc6 Rf2! {seems to create just enough counterplay.}) 33... Qh7! {creates counterplay, and}) {But} 31... Qd8 ({e.g.} 31... Qc8 32. g4 hxg4 33. fxg4 f5 (33... Qd8!?) 34. g5 {now is completely winning, as now the queen cannot creat threats in the h-file.}) 32. Qe7 Qb8+ 33. Kg5 Qg3+ 34. Kh6 Qf4+ 35. Qg5 {looks like leading to a dominating ending, but there are sublte differences compared to the game. After} Qxg5+ 36. Kxg5 {looks better, but after} (36. hxg5 h4! {surprisingly seems very solid for Black, and White's "active" king on h6, also is somewhat boxed in.} (36... Rc8? 37. g3)) 36... Kg7 37. Re7 Rd8 {Black has a much better defensive setup than in the game, as the white king will not reach e5. My intuition tells me White must have a way in these lines, but I do not see it.}) 31. Qxd6 Rxd6 32. Re8+ Kg7 33. Ke5 Rd7 34. a3! {A complete zugzwang! King moves loses to f6, rook moves to d6, and 34...f6+ 35.e6 threatens the rook, and when it moves, 36.e7+ forces an easily winning pawn ending. So Kramnik has no choice, but to start shedding pawns, just to obtain a bit of counterplay.} g5 {.} 35. hxg5 h4 36. f4 Kg6 37. Rg8+ Kh7 38. Rc8 Kg7 39. Rxc6 Re7+ 40. Kxd5 Re2! {White is winning, but at least Black has become active, and after taking either b2 or g2, he can create a passed pawn of his own.} 41. Rh6? {Now actually it becomes tricky.} ({The simple way was} 41. Kc5! {combining threats against Black's queenside pawns, with advancing his own d-pawn winning easily.}) 41... Rxb2 42. Rxh4 Rb3 43. Kc5 Rxc3+ 44. Kxb5 Rxa3 45. f5 Ra1 46. Kb4 a3 47. g4 Rd1! {White might have missed a possible win, which is understandable in a blitz game. Now however it seems to be a certain draw, as Kramnik sacrifices his main trump, the passed pawn, but in exchange gets White's d-pawn and forces a kind of fortress-like situation, where White despite his 2 extra pawns still can't make any progress.} 48. Kxa3 Rxd4 49. Kb3 Rf4 50. Kc3 Ra4 51. Kd3 Ra3+ 52. Kc4 Ra4+ 53. Kb5 Rf4 54. Kc6 Ra4 55. Kd6 Ra6+ 56. Kc7 Ra7+ 57. Kb6 Ra4 58. Kb5 Rf4 59. Kc5 Ra4 60. Kc6 Rb4 61. Kd5 Ra4 62. Ke5 Ra5+ 63. Kd6 Ra6+ 64. Kc5 Ra4 65. Kc6 Rb4 66. Kd5 Ra4 {And here the players agreed a draw.} 1/2-1/2

