1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 Nxd5 {This is known as the Semi-Tarrasch Defence. It has a solid reputation but White usually gets a small edge in the middlegame owing to more central control. However, this is not to say that White always gets better chances in practice, especially against a very well-prepared player like Kramnik.} 5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 c5 7. Nf3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Bxd2+ 10. Qxd2 O-O 11. Bc4 Nd7 ({The other normal line is} 11... b6 12. O-O Bb7 13. Rfe1 Nc6 14. Rad1 {aiming for more central control.}) 12. O-O b6 13. a4!? {This plan of quickly ramming the queenside seems to give Black more problems in practice.} Bb7 14. Bd3 e5 {Kramnik's improvement over their game in Norway Blitz earlier this year.} ({Their previous blitz encounter continued} 14... Rb8 15. Rfe1 h6 16. a5 b5 17. Rac1 a6 18. Qb4 Rc8 19. Nd2 Rxc1 20. Rxc1 Ne5? (20... Nb8! {aiming for c6 is much better.}) 21. Bxb5 axb5 22. dxe5 Qd3 23. h3 Rd8 24. Rc7 Ba8 25. Qe7 Rf8 26. Qd6 Qe2 27. f3 Qe3+ 28. Kh2 Qf4+ 29. Kh1 b4 30. a6 h5 31. a7 h4 32. Qxb4 Qxe5 33. Qc5 Qxc5 34. Rxc5 Rd8 35. Nc4 Kh7 {1-0 (35) Carlsen,M (2851)-Kramnik,V (2801) Stavanger NOR 2016}) 15. Qe3! $146 {The World Champion is well prepared!} (15. d5 Nc5 16. Bc2 (16. Qe3 f5 $132) 16... f5! $11 {and Black gains equal chances in both lines.}) 15... exd4 16. Nxd4 Nc5 17. Bc2 Re8 18. f3 Qf6 19. a5 Rad8 20. Rfd1 $14 {White is slightly better due to his better pawn structure, more central control and room for his pieces.} Ba6 21. axb6 axb6 22. Ba4!? {A creative way to get rid of the strong knight on c5.} (22. Nb3 {trading the knight seems also like a good idea, but Magnus probably didn't think he could create enough problems for Black after} Nxb3 23. Bxb3 Rxd1+ 24. Rxd1 Rd8 25. Rxd8+ (25. Bd5 Bc4) 25... Qxd8 26. Bd5 Qc7 $11) 22... Nxa4 23. Rxa4 b5 (23... Bb7 24. Rb4 $36) (23... Bc8 24. Rb4 $36) 24. Rb4 Rd7 25. Rc1 {Even though Black has defensive resources, Magnus likes playing positions where he has long-term pressure, creating small problems for the enemy one after another and taking advantage of inaccuracies.} Red8?! {Just like this.} (25... h6 $142 26. Rc6 Re6! {a small tactic comes to the rescue} 27. Nxe6?? (27. Rc5 Re5 $11) 27... Rd1+ 28. Kf2 Qh4+ $19) (25... Rc8 {could also keep the balance but it's difficult to judge whether this trade is beneficial or not during play.}) 26. e5 {Gaining more space, and introducing e6 ideas in the future.} Qb6 27. Rc6! Qa7 28. Nf5 Qxe3+ ({Throwing in checks} 28... Rd1+ 29. Kf2 R1d2+ 30. Kg3 $14 {doesn't change the evaluation of the position.}) 29. Nxe3 {Magnus has converted a slightly better middlegame game to a superior ending. Now let's watch and learn from the World Champion's endgame technique.} Ra8 30. Kf2 g6 31. h4! {With the idea of h5-h6. In case you haven't noticed from his games (any time-control!), Magnus is fond of using "rook pawn thrusts."} h5 {.} 32. e6! {perfect timing, now that Black has already weakened g6} fxe6 33. Rxe6 Kh7 34. Rf4 (34. Rb6! $16 {[%cal Gb4f4,Gf4f6,Ge4e6] making sure the bishop is tied down before activating the b4-rook} Raa7 35. Re4) 34... Bc8 35. Rb6 Ra2+ 36. Kg3 Re2 37. Re4 Bb7 38. Nd5! Rxe4 39. Nf6+ Kg7 40. Nxd7 Re7 {A critical moment, as evidenced by Magnus' long pause before he made his next move.} 41. Nb8! {Magnus chooses the move that keeps the best practical winning chance.} (41. Nc5 Bc8 42. Rxb5 Re5 {looks drawish due to the unpleasant pin. While White may be able to break the pin, the resulting minor piece ending gives Black good drawing chances especially against an endgame virtuoso like Volodya.} 43. Ra5 Kh6 44. Nb3 Rxa5 (44... Re3 {may be more stubborn}) 45. Nxa5 g5! 46. Nc4 (46. hxg5+ Kxg5) 46... Ba6) (41. Rxb7 b4! {is Kramnik's defensive trick} 42. Kf4 (42. Nc5?? Rxb7 43. Nxb7 b3 {the passed pawn is too fast}) 42... b3 43. Rxb3 Rxd7 $11) 41... Rc7 42. Rxb5 Kf6 {There's different ways to continue here but the next moves show both sides shuffling pieces to manage their time. The onus is on White to do something, because he has to extricate his knight from b8 to make any sort of progress.} 43. Rb2 Kf5 44. Rb6 Re7 45. Rb5+ Kf6 46. Kf4 Rc7 47. Rb6+ Kg7 48. Rd6 (48. Kg5 Rc5+ 49. Kf4 Rc7 {doesn't do anything.}) 48... Rc4+ 49. Kg3 Rc7 50. Rd7+! {A well-judged decision to transform the game into a minor piece ending.} (50. Nd7!? {is also playable} Rc6 51. Rd4 $14) 50... Rxd7 51. Nxd7 {The important thing to see here is the possibility of getting the white king on to g5 where it'll be winning.} Ba6 (51... Bc6 52. Nc5 Kf6 53. Kf4 {with e4 to follow then g5.}) (51... Kh6 52. Kf4! {compared to the minor piece ending arising after 41.c5, Black cannot trade a set of pawns here which changes the evaluation of the situation big time.}) 52. Nc5 (52. Kf4 {also works} Bf1 53. g3 Be2 54. Nc5 Bd1 55. Ne4 Kf7 56. Nc3! {kicking the knight out and liberating the king from the defence of f3} (56. Nd2 Kf6)) 52... Bf1 53. Ne4 Kf7 54. Nd2 Bd3 {It's only a matter of time for White to execute the winning plan: get the king to g5, then prepare & execute the f4-f5 pawn break.} 55. Kf4 Ke6 56. Kg5 Kf7 57. Nb3 Bf1 58. g3 Kg7 59. Nd4 Bc4 60. f4 Bd3 61. f5 {Tada!} gxf5 62. Nxf5+ Kh7 63. Ne3 {The h-pawn will eventually drop off, so Black resigned.} 1-0

