1. Nf3 e6 2. g3 b5 3. e4 Bb7 4. Qe2 c5 5. Bg2 a6 6. O-O Nf6 7. e5 Nd5 8. b3 {One thing is for sure - both players were looking to play original chess from the 1st move!. We have now a mix of different openings on the board, and a rich position.} Nc6 9. Bb2 Be7 10. Nc3!? (10. c4 {was another way to play the position} bxc4 11. bxc4 Nb6 12. Nc3 O-O {White wants to play e4 now, but ...a4 would be annoying then} 13. Rab1!? d5! 14. exd6 Qxd6 15. Ne4 Qc7 16. Rfe1 Rad8 $13 {If Black is able to regroup nicely, he could even look for an advantage, since the d-pawn could be a problem forever.}) 10... Nxc3 11. dxc3 O-O 12. Rad1 Qc7 13. c4 Rad8 14. h4 (14. cxb5 axb5 15. Qxb5 Ra8! $132 {threatening ...xa2 and ...a6.}) 14... f6!? 15. exf6 Bxf6 16. Bxf6 gxf6 {Carlsen plays very creatively, but the weaknesses around his king will be a potential problem in any position where the queens are maintained.} (16... Rxf6!?) 17. cxb5 (17. c3!? {limiting the knight on c6 was an interesting idea} bxc4 (17... b4? 18. Qe3! {hitting c5} Ne7 19. cxb4 cxb4 20. Rfe1 $16) 18. Qxc4 $14) 17... axb5 18. Qxb5 Nb4! $132 19. Qe2 Nxa2 20. Qc4 (20. Ra1 Nb4 21. Rfd1 $14 {was a better way to organise the pieces. Now Black's centre can't advance easily and White could claim a small advantage, mostly because of the better security of his king.}) 20... Kh8 21. Ra1 Ra8 22. Rfe1?! d5 23. Qg4 e5 {Black suddenly is taking over the initiative!} 24. h5 Bc8 25. Qh4 Bb7 26. Nh2?! (26. Qg4!? {I don't know if Black would keep on playing if Kramnik repeated the position once again.}) 26... Nb4 27. Rxa8 Bxa8 28. c4 Nc2 {I like this move, without pushing his central structure even more.} (28... e4!? 29. cxd5 f5 {led to interesting play, now Black will take on d5 and wants to grab b3 as well. White finds nice counterplay:} 30. Nf1 Bxd5 31. Ne3 Bxb3 32. h6! {and with the pawns on light squares, the black king feels even more unsafe (since the a1-h8 diagonal is now open)} Qf7 33. Ra1 Be6 34. Qf4 Qf6 35. Ra7 $44) 29. Rd1 Nd4 30. cxd5 (30. Ra1!? {a tough move to play in time trouble} Bb7 31. Bxd5 Bxd5 32. cxd5 Qd6 33. Qe4 f5 34. Qd3 Qxd5 35. Ra7 $132) (30. Bxd5? Bxd5 31. cxd5 Qd6 {and the queen achieves a commanding position on d5} 32. Qe4 f5 33. Qe3 Qxd5 34. Re1 Re8 $17) 30... Qf7 31. Qe4 Rd8 32. h6? (32. Qh4! {threatening g4, Black has to act fast} Bxd5 33. Bxd5 Rxd5 34. Ra1! {there are many ideas connected with the activation through the a-file. A weakened king will always suffer somehow.} Qf8 (34... Rd8 35. Ra7! Qe6 (35... Qxa7? 36. Qxf6+ $18) (35... Nf3+ 36. Nxf3 Rd1+ 37. Kg2 Qxa7 38. Qxf6+ Qg7 39. Qe6 $40) 36. Qg4 $16 {with a lot of pressure}) 35. Ra7 $36 {and White clearly has the better chances against the vulnerable black king.}) 32... Bxd5 33. Qg4 Bxb3 $17 {Now Black is a solid pawn up and has a dangerous passer on the c-file.} 34. Ra1 Be6 35. Qe4 Qg6 36. Ra8 Rg8 (36... Rxa8!? {similar to the game} 37. Qxa8+ Qg8 $17) 37. Rxg8+ Bxg8 38. Qxg6? {Now White is losing.} (38. Qa8! {was still very complicated, Black's king does not feel safe, meaning it is not easy to push the c-pawn forward} c4 39. Be4 Qg5 40. Nf3 Nxf3+ 41. Bxf3 {threatening the decisive f8, followed by d5} c3 42. Qc8 Qxh6 43. Qxc3 $17 {with a long endgame to come, where White has great drawing chances.}) 38... hxg6 39. Ng4 c4 40. Nxf6 c3 41. Be4 c2 {Doesn't spoil anything, but} (41... Nf3+ {would force a win right away} 42. Kg2 Nd2 43. Bd3 (43. Bc2 Bb3 44. Ne4 (44. Bd3 e4! 45. Nxe4 Nxe4 46. Bxe4 Bd5 $19) 44... Bxc2 45. Nxc3 Kh7 $19) (43. h7 Bb3 44. Bd3 e4! $19) 43... Bc4 44. Bc2 Bb3 {-- 43.c2}) 42. Bxc2 Nxc2 43. Nd7 e4 44. Nf6 e3 45. fxe3 Nxe3 46. g4 Nd5 47. Nxd5 {You can never blame anyone for playing this, because the winning plan for Black is subtle. Probably without time, Kramnik thought he had reached a drawn endgame.} (47. Ne4! {was the most resilient, since the game sequence loses by force} Nf4 48. Kf2 Bd5 {achieving the best possible setup} 49. Ng5 (49. Nf6 Bc6 50. Ke3 Ne6 51. Kf2 g5 {now Black simply goes on to take h6 and gradually wins g4, and the game} 52. Ke3 Nd8 53. Kd4 Nf7 54. h7 Kg7 $19) 49... Ne6! 50. Nxe6 Bxe6 51. g5 Kg8 52. Ke3 Kf8 53. Kd4 Bg8 54. Ke5 Ke7 $19) 47... Bxd5 48. g5 Kg8 49. Kf2 Kf8 50. Ke3 Bg8 51. Kd4 Ke8!! {A beautiful finesse from the world champion.} (51... Ke7 {still wins, but Black would need to reach the position with e8 anyway. For example:} 52. Ke5 Kf7 53. Kd5! (53. Kd6 {would actually help Black, because the win becomes very intuitive:} Ke8 54. Ke5 Ke7 55. Kd4 Kd6 56. Ke4 Ke6 57. Kf4 Kd5 $19 {After reaching the 5th rank, the g5-pawn is lost, and so is the game}) 53... Kf8+ 54. Kd4 Ke8!) 52. Ke4 Kd7 53. Kd3 (53. Ke5 Ke7 $19 {followed by d6-e6 and the invasion of the black king-}) 53... Ke6 54. Ke4 Bh7 {Reaching the 5th rank with his king, and winning the game.} 0-1

