{The day before Carlsen had lost an unbelievable game against Grischuk. After losing his objectivity in a completely equal position he had to suffer for a long time in an ending a pawn down. Nakamura on the other hand was outplayed by Aronian after unconvincing preparation in a Spanish line.} 1. d4 {[%emt 0:00:03]} d5 {[%emt 0:00:10] I was following the live commentary where former World Champion Garry Kasparov joined Yasser Seirawan and Jennifer Shahade in the commentary room. When asked which opening he would have recommended Nakamura against Carlsen he immediately responded: King's Indian, of course!} (1... Nf6! 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7!) 2. c4 {[%emt 0:00:06]} e6 {[%emt 0:00:03]} 3. Nc3 {[%emt 0:00:07]} Be7 {[%emt 0:00:04] But instead Nakamura goes for the solid Queen's Gambit, a decent opening of course, but in the specific tournament situation I would tend to agree with Kasparov. After his loss on the previous day it would have been more unpleasant for Carlsen to face concrete problems in a sharp line, where on top of that it's easier for Nakamura to unfold his full potential.} 4. Bf4 {[%emt 0:01:23]} Nf6 {[%emt 0:00:16]} 5. e3 {[%emt 0:00:10]} O-O {[%emt 0:00:29]} 6. a3!? {[%emt 0:00:22] The main point of this line is to prevent 6...bd7, so 6...b6 is considered the right reply now, because a3 is not that useful in the b6-setup.} c5 {[%emt 0:08:20] A risky choice, because Carlsen has lots of experience in this line with both colours.} (6... b6!) (6... Nbd7?! 7. Nb5!) 7. dxc5 {[%emt 0:00:11]} Bxc5 {[%emt 0:00:07]} 8. Nf3 {[%emt 0:00:06]} Nc6 {[%emt 0:00:56]} 9. Qc2 {[%emt 0:00:37]} Qa5 {[%emt 0:21:02]} (9... Re8 {was played by Carlsen himself in his last World Championship match with Anand.} 10. Bg5 Be7 11. Rd1 Qa5 12. Bd3 h6 13. Bh4 dxc4 14. Bxc4 a6 15. O-O b5 16. Ba2 Bb7 17. Bb1 Rad8 18. Bxf6 Bxf6 19. Ne4 Be7 20. Nc5 Bxc5 21. Qxc5 b4 $11 {1/2 (41)-1/2 (41) Anand, V (2792)-Carlsen,M (2863) Sochi 2014}) 10. Rd1 {[%emt 0:00:13]} Be7 {[%emt 0:01:43]} 11. Be2 {[%emt 0:00:22]} Ne4 {[%emt 0:02:11] I wonder what went wrong with Nakamura's preparation, because this line is considered dubious already for quite a long time.} (11... dxc4 12. Bxc4 Nh5 {is another line, where White gets a small plus.}) 12. cxd5 {[%emt 0:01:39]} Nxc3 {[%emt 0:00:16]} 13. Qxc3 {[%emt 0:00:07]} (13. bxc3!? {seems also pleasant for White.} exd5 14. O-O Bf6 15. c4 Be6 16. Bg5 dxc4 17. Bxf6 gxf6 18. Bxc4 Qc5 19. Rc1 Qxc4 20. Qxc4 Bxc4 21. Rxc4 $16 {1-0 (37) Giri,A (2752)-Kasimdzhanov,R (2700) Rosmalen 2014}) 13... Qxc3+ {[%emt 0:00:15]} 14. bxc3 {[%emt 0:00:03]} exd5 {[%emt 0:00:10]} 15. Rxd5 {[%emt 0:00:05]} Bxa3 {[%emt 0:04:29]} 16. Nd4! {[%emt 0:00:52] A strong centralization move} Nxd4 {[%emt 0:03:50]} (16... a5 {is the other move that has been played here.} 17. Rb5 (17. Kd2!? a4 18. Kc2 Be7 19. Bf3 $14) 17... a4 18. Kd2 Be7 19. Ra1 Ra7 20. Bg5 f6 21. Bf4 Rd8 22. Bc7 Re8 23. Bb6 Ra8 24. Bc4+ Kf8 25. Ne6+ Bxe6 26. Bxe6 Bd6 27. Bd5 $16 {1-0 (41) Sokolov,I (2657)-Meier,G (2608) Budva 2009}) 17. exd4 {[%emt 0:00:13]} b6 {[%emt 0:05:32]} 18. Kd2 $14 {[%emt 0:03:18] It's time to make a cut and evaluate the position that has arisen. White has a protected passed pawn in the centre, while Black has one on the a-file. The White king is centralized and his other pieces are also active. Black on the other hand has an awkward piece placement. Especially his bishops are struggling to find good spots, as will also be demonstrated by the game later on. All in all White has a pretty decent and riskfree advantage, normally a dream position for our World Champion.} Be6 {[%emt 0:10:29]} 19. Rb5 {[%emt 0:00:14]} Bd7 {[%emt 0:00:11]} 20. Rb3 {[%emt 0:03:19]} Be7 {[%emt 0:00:02]} 21. Bf3 {[%emt 0:05:04]} Ba4 {[%emt 0:06:33] The other bishop has emerged to the edge of the board.} (21... Rac8 22. Ra1 $18 {loses already a pawn!}) 22. Rb2 {[%emt 0:00:49]} Rad8 {[%emt 0:00:04]} 23. Ra1 {[%emt 0:07:42]} b5 {[%emt 0:00:06] The only way to prevent losing material, but now Black has built a cage for his own bishop.} 24. Bc6 {[%emt 0:12:46]} a6 {[%emt 0:00:07]} 25. Bb7 {[%emt 0:09:42]} Bd6 {[%emt 0:00:22]} 26. Be3 {[%emt 0:00:23]} a5 {[%emt 0:01:30]} 27. Bc6 {[%emt 0:00:37] logical and efficient play so far. The weak pawns are attacked and become even weaker by advancing.} Rb8 {[%emt 0:01:07]} 28. d5 $18 {[%emt 0:00:44]} Rfd8 {[%emt 0:07:30]} (28... Be5 {[%cal Gb5b4]} 29. f4 Bf6 30. Kd3 $18 {is no better.}) 29. Kd3 {[%emt 0:00:34] White is in full control!} Bf8 {[%emt 0:03:41]} 30. Bd4 {[%emt 0:01:35]} f6 {[%emt 0:01:47]} 31. Ke4 {[%emt 0:07:14]} Bd6 {[%emt 0:09:18]} 32. c4?! {[%emt 0:01:49] This is not the kind of technical play to which we are accustomed from Magnus. Although this is winning there was no need to hurry.} ({Instead the position could have been improved slowly but surely with e.g.} 32. g3!? $18 {[%cal Gf2f4] intending to improve the position to the maximum, before c4 is carried out. Black can't do anything.}) 32... Bb4 {[%emt 0:00:33]} 33. Ba7 {[%emt 0:04:40]} f5+! {[%emt 0:06:37] Nakamura is always capable of finding some tricks to create problems for his opponent.} 34. Kf3 {[%emt 0:01:41]} Rbc8 {[%emt 0:02:42]} 35. cxb5 {[%emt 0:05:04]} Bxb5 {[%emt 0:00:12]} 36. Bxb5 {[%emt 0:00:08]} Bc3 {[%emt 0:00:04]} 37. Rab1 {[%emt 0:01:20]} (37. Rba2 Bxa1 38. Rxa1 Rxd5 39. Rxa5! Kf8 40. Bb6 Rb8 41. Bc4 Rxa5 42. Bxa5 $18 {should be winning for White, but there is still some technical work ahead of him. I give a sample line to illustrate how play may continue. White has to display some accuracy to break down Black's defence.} g6 43. Bd2 Kg7 44. Bc3+ Kf8 45. Ke3 Rc8 46. Kd4 Re8 47. g3 Kg7 48. h4 Kh6 49. Bb5 Re6 50. Kd5 Re7 51. Kd6 Rb7 (51... Re4 52. f3! $18 {pretty domination!}) 52. Be2 Ra7 53. Bd4 Ra4 54. Be3+ Kg7 55. Bb5 Re4 56. Bc6 Rb4 57. Ke7 Rc4 58. Bd5 Ra4 59. Bd2 Ra3 60. Bb4 Ra7+ 61. Ke8 h5 (61... Ra6 62. Bc3+ Kh6 63. Kf8 $18) 62. Bc3+ Kh6 63. Kf8 Rc7 64. Bd4 Rc8+ 65. Kf7 Kh7 66. Be3 Rc3 67. Kf6 Ra3 68. Kg5 Ra6 69. Bf4 Rb6 70. Bf7 Kg7 71. Bc4 Kh7 72. Be5 Rb7 73. f3 Ra7 74. Kf6 Kh6 (74... Rb7 75. Bf7 Rb6+ 76. Kg5 $18) 75. Bc3 Rc7 76. Bd2+ Kh7 77. Bf7 Rc6+ 78. Kg5 Kg7 (78... f4 79. Bxf4 (79. g4!? $18) 79... Kg7 80. Bxg6 Rxg6+ 81. Kxh5 $18) 79. Be8 $18 Ra6 80. Bc3+ Kh7 81. Bf6 $18) 37... Bxb2 {[%emt 0:00:05]} 38. Rxb2 {[%emt 0:00:04]} Rxd5 {[%emt 0:00:04]} 39. Be3? {[%emt 0:00:40] This doesn't make any sense. Maybe Magnus just blundered 39...b8, because afterwards I saw him shaking his head in frustration on the livestream.} (39. Ba4! $18 {It's obvious that White should prevent a rook exchange.} Rc3+ 40. Ke2 Re5+ 41. Kd2 Rc8 42. Rb7 Kf8 43. Be3 Rc4 44. Bb3 Rb4 45. Rxb4 axb4 46. Kd3 $18) 39... Rb8 {[%emt 0:01:21]} 40. Bc4 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Rxb2 {[%emt 0:00:00]} 41. Bxd5+ {[%emt 0:00:12]} Kh8 {[%emt 0:00:00] White could have achieved the same position without the black a-pawn! Although he has still winning chances this makes quite a huge difference. Especially from a psychological point of view White's chances of success have dramatically decreased.} 42. Bd4 {[%emt 0:05:06]} Rb1 {[%emt 0:05:54]} 43. Ke2 {[%emt 0:12:22]} a4 {[%emt 0:02:20]} 44. g3 {[%emt 0:00:56]} a3 {[%emt 0:08:56]} 45. Kd2 {[%emt 0:01:21]} h5 {[%emt 0:02:29]} 46. h4 {[%emt 0:06:57]} Kh7 {[%emt 0:01:18]} 47. Bc4 {[%emt 0:09:31]} g6 {[%emt 0:09:32]} 48. Kc2 {[%emt 0:04:30]} Re1 {[%emt 0:05:08]} (48... Rb8!? {It's not clear how White makes progress against this setup either. The rook cuts the king from the a-pawn.}) 49. Be3 {[%emt 0:02:43]} (49. Kb3? a2 $19) (49. Bc5 f4! 50. Bxa3 (50. gxf4 Re4) 50... fxg3 51. fxg3 Re8 {is like the game.}) 49... f4! {[%emt 0:00:33] a good pawn exchange!} 50. Bxf4 {[%emt 0:07:23]} (50. gxf4 Rh1 $132) 50... a2 {[%emt 0:00:06]} 51. Bxa2 {[%emt 0:00:06]} Re2+ {[%emt 0:00:02]} 52. Kb3 {[%emt 0:00:26]} Rxf2 {[%emt 0:00:04] This is already a convincing fortress! A major problem for White is that Black sometimes has the possibility to sacfrice his rook for the dark-squared bishop if he manages to exchange g-pawns afterwards.} 53. Bb1 {[%emt 0:00:07]} Re2 {[%emt 0:01:01]} 54. Kc4 {[%emt 0:00:09]} Kg7 {[%emt 0:00:27]} 55. Kd5 {[%emt 0:00:24]} Re1 {[%emt 0:04:59]} 56. Bc2 {[%emt 0:01:14]} Re2 {[%emt 0:01:53]} 57. Bd3 {[%emt 0:00:46]} Re1 {[%emt 0:00:47]} 58. Be4 {[%emt 0:02:03]} (58. Be5+ Kh7 59. Ke6 {White cannot make progress this way, because Black can solve his problems radically:} Kh6! 60. Kf6 Rxe5! 61. Kxe5 g5! $11 62. hxg5+ Kxg5 63. Be2 h4 64. g4 h3 $11) 58... Rd1+ {[%emt 0:01:31]} 59. Ke5 {[%emt 0:00:37]} Re1 {[%emt 0:00:35]} 60. Bd2 {[%emt 0:01:04]} Re2 {[%emt 0:02:01]} 61. Bc3 {[%emt 0:01:04]} Kh6 {[%emt 0:01:01]} 62. Bb4 {[%emt 0:01:57]} Rf2 {[%emt 0:01:25] White is unable to cross the f-file.} 63. Bc5 {[%emt 0:00:24]} Rf1 {[%emt 0:00:18]} 64. Bb4 {[%emt 0:01:29]} Rf2 {[%emt 0:01:16]} 65. Be7 {[%emt 0:02:02]} Rf1 {[%emt 0:01:21]} 66. Bf6 {[%emt 0:04:15]} Rg1 {[%emt 0:01:52]} 67. Bg5+ {[%emt 0:02:29]} Kg7 {[%emt 0:00:05]} 68. Bf4 {[%emt 0:00:45]} Re1 {[%emt 0:00:04]} 69. Kd5 {[%emt 0:00:09]} Rd1+ {[%emt 0:01:22]} 70. Ke6 {[%emt 0:00:44]} Re1 {[%emt 0:00:07]} 71. Ke5 {[%emt 0:00:07] I think about here Nakamura claimed a draw on a three-fold-repetition. The position has indeed been reached for a third time, but while now it's Black´s turn to move, in the earlier versions it was White to move. So after adding some extra time to Magnus' clock the game continued.} Re2 {[%emt 0:00:33]} 72. Kd5 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Re1 {[%emt 0:01:02]} 73. Bd3 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Kh7 {[%emt 0:02:41]} 74. Kd4 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Kg7 {[%emt 0:00:19]} 75. Be3 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Ra1 {[%emt 0:03:14]} 76. Ke4 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Ra4+ {[%emt 0:03:16]} 77. Kf3 {[%emt 0:00:03]} Ra3 {[%emt 0:00:34]} 78. Bb5 {[%emt 0:00:24]} Rb3 {[%emt 0:00:23]} 79. Be8 {[%emt 0:00:10]} Rb1 {[%emt 0:00:36]} 80. Bc6 {[%emt 0:02:12]} Rb4 {[%emt 0:01:10]} 81. Bd2 {[%emt 0:02:24]} Rb6 {[%emt 0:00:24]} 82. Bc3+ {[%emt 0:00:20]} Kh6 {[%emt 0:00:14]} 83. Bd5 {[%emt 0:00:06]} Rb1 {[%emt 0:00:58]} 84. Kf4 {[%emt 0:01:26]} Rf1+ {[%emt 0:02:44]} 85. Ke5 {[%emt 0:00:08]} Rg1 {[%emt 0:04:57]} 86. Bd2+ {[%emt 0:04:47]} Kg7 {[%emt 0:00:01]} 87. Bf4 {[%emt 0:00:13]} Re1+ {[%emt 0:00:23]} 88. Kd6 {[%emt 0:00:20]} Kf6 {[%emt 0:00:02]} 89. Bf3 {[%emt 0:04:34] This allows Black to end his suffering:} (89. Bc6 Rd1+ $11) 89... Kf5! {[%emt 0:00:55]} 90. Kd5 {[%emt 0:00:08]} Rf1 {[%emt 0:00:31]} 91. Be4+ {[%emt 0:01:04]} Kg4 {[%emt 0:00:50]} 92. Bxg6 {[%emt 0:00:35]} Rxf4 {[%emt 0:00:06]} 93. gxf4 {[%emt 0:00:08]} Kxf4 {[%emt 0:00:03]} 94. Bxh5 {[%emt 0:00:17]} Kg3 {[%emt 0:00:05]} 95. Bd1 {[%emt 0:00:07]} Kxh4 {Very tenacious defence by Nakamura and another disappointment for Carlsen. In my opinion it's only a matter of time, till Carlsen finds back his form. In an interview during this game Garry Kasparov was asked whom he saw as the biggest threat to Carlsen recently. His answer was telling: Magnus Carlsen!} 1/2-1/2

