[ECO "C24"]
[WhiteElo "2843"]
[BlackElo "2822"]
[PlyCount "153"]

1. e4 e5 2. Bc4 {The Bishop's Opening, played five times by Magnus Carlsen in classical games. It fits his style, since a positional battle begins, and there is not much theory. In 2014, the very same Fabiano Caruana with Black beat Carlsen in the Sinquefield Cup.} Nf6 3. d3 c6 4. Nf3 (4. Qe2 Be7 (4... d5! $36 {is the best way to deal with e2}) 5. Nf3 d6 6. c3 Nbd7 7. Bb3 O-O 8. O-O a5 9. d4 a4 10. Bc2 Re8 11. Re1 Bf8 12. Qd1 b5 $17 {with a balanced middlegame, the reigning world champion scored a win over world's number 1 woman, Hou Yifan. 1-0 (61) Carlsen,M (2843)-Hou,Y (2654) Karlsruhe/Baden-Baden 2018}) 4... d5 5. Bb3 Bb4+! {The main move here. Black provokes the c2-c3 advance, so White loses the option to play c3 at a later stage. Carlsen played 6.c3 in the aforementioned game.} 6. Bd2 {This is another way to play it.} (6. c3 {happened in the aforementioned game, between these two players back in 2014.} Bd6 7. Bg5 dxe4 8. dxe4 h6 9. Bh4 Qe7 10. Nbd2 Nbd7 11. Bg3 Bc7 12. O-O Nh5 13. h3?! Nxg3 14. fxg3 Nc5 15. Bxf7+ {Black was able to stop this pseudo-initiative and converted the game on move 34} Kxf7 16. Nxe5+ Kg8 17. Ng6 Qg5 18. Rf8+ Kh7 19. Nxh8 Bg4 20. Qf1 Nd3 21. Qxd3 Rxf8 22. hxg4 Qxg4 $17 {0-1 Carlsen,M (2877)-Caruana,F (2801) Sinquefield Cup 2nd Saint Louis 2014 (3)}) 6... Bxd2+ 7. Nbxd2 a5 {Threatening the old and good bishop trap with ...a4. I only found one high-level reference in classical games here, where White played 8.a3.} 8. c3 (8. a3 a4 9. Ba2 Nbd7 10. O-O O-O 11. Re1 Re8 12. d4 {Trying to start central play did not lead to any advantage for White:} exd4 13. exd5 Rxe1+ 14. Qxe1 cxd5 {Black has the isolated pawn, but has well placed pieces (...f8-e6 is a possible plan) and a b2-pawn to attack with ...b6} 15. Nb1? {too slow} (15. Nxd4 {was possible} Qb6 16. Nf5 Qxb2 17. Ne7+ Kf8 18. Nxd5 Nxd5 19. Bxd5 Qd4 $11 {followed by ...f6}) 15... d3?! (15... Nb8! 16. Nxd4 Nc6 $17 {would give a nice advantage for Black}) 16. cxd3 Nc5 17. d4 Nce4 18. Nc3 Be6 $11 {½-½ (27) Shirov,A (2713)-Rodshtein,M (2641) Rogaska Slatina 2011}) 8... Nbd7 9. exd5 {A typical reaction, when Back develops his knight to d7. It is logical, because now Black can not play ...c6 anymore, an ideal move whenever the c6-pawn goes to d5.} cxd5 {[%csl Rd7][%cal Rb8c6]} 10. O-O O-O 11. Re1 Re8 12. Nf1 b5 13. a4!? b4 14. cxb4 axb4 {We have already reached a very imbalaced position, starting from a very modest opening.} 15. Ne3 Bb7 16. d4! {A very nice concept from the World Champion, as we shall see:} e4 17. Ne5 Nxe5 (17... Rxe5!? {was playable, but it hardly looks like Black is already in need to give away an exchange.} 18. dxe5 Nxe5 19. Qd4 Nc6 20. Qd2 d4 21. Nf5 Ra5! 22. Ng3 Re5 $44 {With a very original position. It looks very shaky for Black, but the central pawns are strong, giving good compensation.}) 18. dxe5 Rxe5 19. Qd4 Re7 20. Rac1! {Getting the pawn back with 20.xb4 was possible, but the whole concept of sacrificing a pawn in a seemingly normal position just goes to show how brilliant Carlsen is sometimes.} (20. Qxb4 Bc6 {[%cal Re7b7] The main downside for taking the pawn is the bishop on b3 that becomes loose now. Ideally, you want to place your minor pieces in a way they can't be attacked easily.} 21. Qd4 Rb7 22. Rec1 {We have a similar position compared to the game, but here Black is in full control of the b-file and should equalise:} Qb6 23. Qxb6 Rxb6 24. a5 Rxb3 25. Rxc6 Rxb2 26. Rd6 $11 {Black is a pawn up, but he will soon lose it, when the endgame will be simply equal.}) 20... Rd7 21. Red1 (21. Qxb4? {doesn't make sense now:} d4 $17) 21... h6 22. Rc5 $44 {I feel that here White wants to make small improvements, like dc1 and h3, and only then think about taking any decisive action. The d5-pawn is weak, and Black always has to count on the a4-pawn advancing at some point.} Ra5 23. Rxa5 (23. Rdc1!? {would be similar}) 23... Qxa5 24. h3 Kh7 25. Rc1 Rc7? {In the post-mortem, Caruana mentioned that he simply misevaluated the following position, thinking he would have good activity with his queen, but soon he realised it was a bad idea. Now White is in full control.} (25... Qa6! {would be a smart move, anticipating White's c5 followed by a5, with ...e2!} 26. Rc5 Qe2 $132) 26. Rxc7 Qxc7 27. Qxb4 $16 Qc1+ 28. Bd1 Ba6 29. Qd4 {We have the same position as before, without the b4-pawn. Now, compared with the middlegame, it is way more valuable to take the pawn back and start advancing the passed a and b-pawns, since they are free and very frightening! Also, there are no rooks to create direct counterplay against them.} Be2 30. Kh2 Bxd1?! (30... Qc7+! {forcing White to weaken a little bit} 31. g3 (31. Kg1 Qc1 {repeats the position.}) 31... Bxd1 32. Nxd1 h5 {Being able to play ...h4 at some point and create threats against your opponent's king is usually good. Here it could have been a nice way to look for counterplay.} 33. Kg1 Qc1 $36 {with ...e3 ideas.}) 31. Nxd1 Qc7+ 32. Kg1! {Now there is no need to play g3, like in the 30...c7+ line.} Qc1 33. b4 e3 {The same idea I mentioned earlier, but the big difference is that with the pawn on g2, White's king is much safer.} 34. fxe3 $18 {White is winning. With flawless technique, Carlsen converts the full point:} Ne4 35. Qxd5 Nd2 36. Qf5+ Kh8 37. Qg4 f5 38. Qe2 Ne4 39. Qe1 Qa1 40. a5 Nd6 41. Qd2 {Slowly untangling the position. That is common when you have a lot of additional material but your opponent is active. You have to find space for small improvements and soon the position will play itself.} Nc4 42. Qd4 Qc1 43. Kf1 {Trying to bring the king closer so White can advance his pawns further.} Nxe3+ {Caruana goes for the queen endgame, that should also be hopeless, but the position is very bad anyway.} 44. Qxe3 Qxd1+ 45. Kf2 Qc2+ 46. Kg3 g5 {Black does not want to weaken his king, but he's lost otherwise, so he has to try something like this.} 47. Qe5+ Kh7 48. Kh2! {King safety always in first place in queen endgames!} f4 49. Qd5 Qa4 50. Qf7+ Kh8 51. Qg6! {Simplifying into a clear win:} Qxb4 52. Qxh6+ Kg8 53. Qxg5+ Kh7 54. Qh5+ Kg7 55. Qg5+ Kh7 56. h4 Qd6 57. Qh5+ Kg7 58. Qg5+ Kh7 59. h5 {Somewhat cold-blooded to allow ...f3+, but it's all good for White. The good thing is that g6+ is a very strong threat now.} f3+ 60. g3 f2 61. Qg6+ Kh8 (61... Qxg6 62. hxg6+ Kxg6 63. Kg2 $18 {and the game ends immediately.}) 62. Qxd6 f1=Q 63. Qh6+ Kg8 64. Qe6+ (64. Qg6+ Kh8 65. h6?? {would be a way to mess up the position!} Qg2+! {forcing stalemate} 66. Kxg2 $11) 64... Kh8 65. Qe3 {With three pawns up, White has to be just a little bit careful.} Qb5 66. Qc3+ {Finding a good square for the queen.} Kh7 67. g4 Qd5 68. Qc7+ Kg8 69. Kg3 {[%cal Gc7g3]} Qe6 (69... Qd3+ 70. Kh4 $18 {and Black has no more checks already.}) 70. Qd8+ Kh7 71. Qd3+ Kh8 72. a6 Qe5+ 73. Kh3 Qa1 74. Qd8+ Kh7 75. Qe7+ Kh6 76. Qe3+ Kh7 77. a7 {It is nice to see that Carlsen used the same technique always - defending the pawn from the diagonal (a5, c3 - a6, d3 - a7, e3). Now Black resigned, since it's impossible to avoid White giving a check somewhere and then queening that pawn. A superb win from the World Champion!} 1-0

