{[%mdl 2]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 c5 5. c3 d5 {[%mdl 512] It was not a good decision for Fabiano Caruana to sac the c5-pawn, taking into consideration that Magnus Carlsen is especially strong in this kind of position.} 6. dxc5 O-O 7. O-O {This line was examined in CBM 129, annotations to Kamsky-Karjakin.} a5 {This attempt to encircle the c5-pawn proves fruitless.} (7... Nc6 8. Qb3 Qa5 9. Qa3 Qc7 10. Bg5 Ne4 11. Be3 e5 12. Nbd2 f5 13. Rad1 Nf6 14. b4 Re8 15. Nb3 Ne4 16. b5 Ne7 17. Qc1 $16 {1-0 (41) Kamsky,G (2725)-Karjakin,S (2706) Wijk aan Zee 2009}) (7... Qc7!? 8. b4 e5 {deserves attention. In the following game Black obtained decent compensation for the pawn:} 9. Bb2 Rd8 10. Nbd2 Bf5 11. Qb3 (11. c4!?) 11... Nc6 12. a3 b6 13. cxb6 axb6 14. Rfd1 h6 15. e3 Be6 16. Qc2 {1/2 (16) L'Ami,E (2626)-Bok,B (2527) Haarlem 2013}) 8. Be3 (8. Nd4!? e5 9. Nb5 Na6 10. Bg5 e4 11. Bf4 Bd7 12. N1a3 b6 13. cxb6 Qxb6 14. c4 dxc4?! 15. Nd6 $16 {1/2 (90) Harika,D (2525)-Muzychuk,M (2462) Antakya 2010}) (8. c4!? Na6 9. Nc3 dxc4 10. Ne5!? Nxc5 11. Be3 Nfe4 12. Nxe4 Nxe4 13. Nxc4 Nd6 14. Nxd6 Qxd6 15. Qxd6 exd6 16. Rad1 $14) 8... Nc6 (8... Ng4 9. Qd2 {, and Black can't recapture the pawn anyway.}) 9. Na3 a4 10. Qc1! e5 {Black's pawn centre doesn't fully compensate for his missing pawn.} 11. Rd1 Qe7 12. Nb5 {[%csl Rd6]} Be6 13. Ng5 (13. Nd6 {was less clear in view of} b6) 13... Bg4! 14. Nd6! (14. f3?! Bc8 15. Nd6 h6 16. Nh3 Bxh3 17. Bxh3 b6 $13) 14... h6! (14... Bxe2 15. Rxd5! Nxd5 16. Bxd5 Nd8 17. Qc2 Bg4 18. Qe4 $16 {[%csl Gd5,Gd6,Ge4]}) 15. Nf3 Kh7 {An unnecessary loss of a tempo.} (15... b6 $142 ! {- a typical undermining although White keeps the better prospects after} 16. Ne1! (16. Bxh6? bxc5) 16... e4! (16... Bxe2 17. Rxd5 Nxd5 18. Bxd5 bxc5 19. Bxc5 {[%csl Gc5,Gd5,Gd6]} Ra5 20. Qe3) 17. f3 exf3 18. exf3 Be6 19. Nd3) 16. h3 Be6 17. b4 {This move unexpectedly gives Black good counterplay.} (17. Ne1 $142 {[%cal Ye1d3]}) 17... axb3 18. axb3 Rxa1 19. Qxa1 Ne4 20. Nd2 f5?! (20... Nxd6 21. cxd6 Qxd6 22. Nc4 Qd8 23. Bc5 Rg8 (23... Re8 24. Nd6 Bf8 25. Nxb7 Qb8 26. Qa6) 24. Bxd5 Bxd5 25. e4 Nd4 26. exd5 Nxb3 27. Qa3 Qc8 28. Qxb3 Qxc5 29. d6 $16 {[%csl Gd6]}) (20... d4! 21. Bxe4 (21. N2xe4 dxe3 22. fxe3 f5 23. Nd2 e4 $44 {[%csl Rg2]}) 21... dxe3 22. fxe3 Bxh3 $44 {White's king is unsafe, and Black's attacking chances should not be underestimated.}) 21. N2xe4 dxe4 (21... fxe4 22. Qb1 {[%cal Yc3c4] e.g.} h5 23. c4 d4 24. Bc1) 22. Qb1! f4 23. Bd2 e3 {This is now harmless.} 24. Be1 {Safely protecting White's king while his other pieces are very active. Black has no compensation for the pawn whatsoever.} Bf5 25. Qc1 h5 26. fxe3 fxg3 27. Bxg3 Qg5 28. e4! Qxg3 29. Rd3 Qh4 30. exf5 gxf5 31. e4! {White seizes the light squares.} fxe4 (31... f4 32. Nf5) (31... Bh6 32. Qd1) 32. Bxe4+ Kh8 33. Qe3 Rf4 34. Bg2 Qe7 35. Qe2 Qh4 36. b4 {The rest is clear. Black has no real counterplay anymore, and the advance of White's queenside pawns decides the game.} e4 37. Nxe4 Ne5 38. Rd5 Kg8 39. b5 Rf5 40. c6 bxc6 41. bxc6 Qe7 42. Nd6 Rg5 43. Nb5 Qe6 44. Rd8+ Kh7 45. Qe4+ Rg6 46. c7 Qa6 47. c8=Q Qa1+ 48. Kf2 Qb2+ 49. Ke1 1-0

[Event "Gashimov Memorial-A 1st"]
