1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. h3 Nc6?! {Does Magnus Carlsen laugh at his rivals? He can play anything at all and then outplay them!} 7. d5 {The most logical reply. Otherwise Black has the idea ...e7-e5 d4-d5 c6-d4!:} (7. Be2 e5 8. d5 Nd4 9. Nxd4 exd4 10. Qxd4 Re8 11. Qd3 Qe7 12. Bg5 h6 13. Bxf6 Bxf6 $132 {[%csl Re4]}) (7. Be3 e5 8. d5 Nd4! 9. Nxd4 exd4 10. Bxd4 (10. Qxd4 Ng4 11. Qd2 Nxe3 12. Qxe3 f5! $44) 10... Nxe4 11. Nxe4 Qh4 12. g4 Re8 13. Bg2 Bf5 14. O-O Bxe4 15. Bxg7 Bxg2 16. Kxg2 Kxg7 17. Qd4+ Qf6 18. Qxf6+ Kxf6 19. Rfe1 {1/2 (19) Lutz,C (2580)-Finkel,A (2465) Leeuwarden 1994}) (7. Bg5 h6 8. Be3 e5 9. d5 Nd4! (9... Ne7 10. Qc1 Kh7 11. Bd3 $14 {1-0 (28) Chernin,A (2600)-Uhlmann,W (2500) Austria 1993}) 10. Nxd4 exd4 11. Qxd4 (11. Bxd4 Nxe4! 12. Nxe4 (12. Bxg7 Re8!) 12... Qh4! 13. g4 Re8 14. Bg2 Bf5! $11) 11... Ng4 12. Qd2 Nxe3 13. Qxe3 {Here Black's compensation for the pawn is less impressive than in the 7.e3 line as his position is weakened by ...h7-h6. Still, after} f5 14. Bd3 Bd7 {he can't complain.}) 7... Nb4 (7... Nb8 8. Be2 c6 9. O-O a6 10. Bg5 b5 11. dxc6 b4 12. Bxf6 exf6 13. c7 Qxc7 14. Nd5 Qb7 15. Nd4 Nd7 16. Qd2 Re8 17. Rfe1 $16 {1-0 (42) Zubarev,A (2583)-Lagarde,M (2501) Sautron FRA 2012}) 8. Be2 (8. Bg5 e6 9. a3 Na6 10. dxe6 Bxe6 11. Be2 Nc5 12. Qc2 Qe8 13. Nd2 Bd7 14. O-O Bc6 15. Bd3 Nfd7 16. Rfe1 {1/2 (16) Bagirov,V (2500)-Zaitsev,I (2445) Podolsk 1992}) (8. a3!? {A logical idea: to push Black's knight away to a6 before he has played ...a7-a5; besides, White's bishop can now go to d3-c2, where it is placed better than on e2.} Na6 9. Bd3 Nc5 10. Bc2 e6 (10... a5 11. Be3) 11. O-O exd5 12. cxd5 Re8 13. Re1 a5 14. Bf4 a4 15. Rc1 $14 {1-0 (36) Berkovich,D (2445)-Zaitsev,I (2445) Podolsk 1992}) 8... e6 9. Be3 (9. O-O Re8 10. Bg5 exd5 11. exd5 h6 12. Be3 Na6 13. Qd2 Kh7 14. Rfe1 Nc5 15. Bxc5 dxc5 16. Bd3 Bd7 17. Ne5 Re7 18. Re3 Ne8 19. Rae1 $14 {1/2 (26) Iotov,V (2569)-Libiszewski,F (2478) Marrakesh 2010}) 9... Re8 10. Nd2 a5 11. O-O Bd7 12. Re1 b6 13. Rc1 Kh8 14. a3 Na6 15. Qc2 e5 16. Rb1 Ng8 {Finally we have a normal King's Indian, and Black's position is quite decent as he will now play ...f7-f5.} 17. b4 f5 18. Nb5!? (18. Nf3!? Nf6! (18... Bh6 19. Bxh6 Nxh6 20. Bd3 {[%cal Yc2e2,Yc4c5]}) 19. Bd3 f4 20. Bc1 Rg8 21. Qe2 {[%cal Rc4c5]} Bc8 $13) 18... Bh6 (18... f4 19. Bxb6! cxb6 20. Nxd6 Rf8 21. c5 $16 {[%csl Gc5,Gd5]}) 19. Bxh6 Nxh6 20. exf5 (20. Qc3!? f4 21. Nb3 {[%cal Yc4c5]}) 20... gxf5 (20... Nxf5!? 21. Ne4 axb4 22. axb4 Bxb5 23. cxb5 Nb8 24. Bg4 Nd7 $14 {/=}) 21. f4!? exf4 22. Qc3+ Kg8 23. Bh5 Nf7 24. Rxe8+ Qxe8 25. Qf6 Qf8 26. Bxf7+ Qxf7 27. Qg5+ Qg7 28. Qxf4 $14 {The position has clarified. Black's kingside attack is now out of the question, and his misplaced a6 knight guarantees White an edge.} axb4 29. axb4 Re8 30. Nd4 (30. Rb3 {[%cal Rb3g3]} Qe5) 30... Qe5 31. Qg5+ Qg7 32. N2f3! Qxg5 33. Nxg5 Kg7 34. Nge6+ Kf6 35. Rf1 Bxe6 36. Nxe6 {[%cal Rg2g4]} h5?! (36... Kg6! $14) 37. g4 (37. b5!? Nc5 38. Rxf5+ Kxf5 39. Ng7+ Kf4 40. Nxe8 Nd3 41. Nxc7 Ne5 {Black recaptures one of the missing pawns but the other one remains - and good winning chances for White.}) 37... hxg4 38. hxg4 Nxb4 39. Rxf5+ Kg6 40. Nf4+ Kg7 41. g5 {White still pushes on but the number of pawns is dramatically decreasing.} c6! 42. Kf2 (42. Rf6 cxd5 43. cxd5 Nxd5! 44. Nxd5 Re5 45. Rxd6 Rxg5+ $11) 42... cxd5 43. cxd5 Re5 44. Ne6+ Kg6 45. Rf6+ Kh5 46. Kf3 Re3+! 1/2-1/2

