1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. Nc3 Nxc3 6. dxc3 Be7 7. Be3 Nc6 8. Bc4!? {This move is almost unexplored on a high level.} O-O ({After} 8... Bf6 9. Qe2 O-O 10. O-O-O Re8 11. h3 Be6 12. Bxe6 Rxe6 13. g4 {Dubov,D - Fridman,D Tbilisi 2017, White had a definite initiative on the k-side and eventually won.}) (8... Be6 9. Bxe6 fxe6 {, as was checked in a few games doesn't seem to fully solve Black's problems in view of} 10. h4! Qd7 11. Ng5 e5 12. Qd5 Bxg5 13. hxg5 $14) 9. Qd2 ({Another possibility was} 9. Qe2 Re8 10. O-O-O Bg4 {, though White's queen is placed rather awkwardly here. For instance,} 11. h3 Bxf3 12. gxf3 Bg5 13. f4 Bxf4 14. Bxf7+ Kxf7 15. Qc4+ Kf8 16. Qxf4+ Qf6 $11) 9... Bf5 ({A move deserving serious attention is} 9... Ne5!? 10. Nxe5 dxe5 11. O-O-O Qxd2+ 12. Rxd2 Bf5 13. Re1 Rfe8 {, and then in 1 correspondence game Black managed to slowly liquidate White's pressure along central files. However, play of such a character would most likely suit Magnus.}) 10. O-O-O Qd7 11. Kb1 $146 {This typical prophylactic move is a novelty.} ({The previously played} 11. Rhe1 Rae8 12. Bd5 Be6 13. c4 Bf6 14. Bg5 Bxg5 15. Qxg5 h6 {led to equal position in Takacs,B - Horvath,I Hungary 2008}) ({Another interesting try was} 11. h3!? Na5 12. Bd3 {, and the effect} Qa4 13. Kb1 Nc4 {doesn't lead to full equality:} 14. Bxc4 Qxc4 15. b3 Qa6 16. Nd4 Bd7 17. c4 $14) 11... Rfe8 12. h4 {The most aggressive. Magnus is intending to push his kingside pawns as soon as possible.} (12. h3 Be4 13. Bd5 Bf6 14. g4 g6 {looks very solid for Black.}) 12... Bf8 {This move looks somewhat passive, but Fabiano searches for counterplay along the e-file.} (12... Bf6 13. Bg5! Be5 14. Nd4 Bxd4 15. cxd4 d5 16. Bf1 {would have yielded White a definite advantage due to the pair of bishops.}) 13. h5 h6 14. Be2! {After this move Black can hardly stop g2-g4} Bg4 (14... a6 15. Nh2 Be4 16. Rhg1 $36) 15. Nh2 Bxe2 16. Qxe2 Ne5 {This move is connected with loss of time, since White is going to push the k-side pawns and attack the knight.} ({A safer one was} 16... Re4!? 17. Nf1 (17. g4 {is well met by} Rae8 18. Qd3 d5! 19. Qxd5 Qxd5 20. Rxd5 Bd6 $132) 17... Rae8 18. Nd2 R4e5 19. g4 Qe6 {White is still somewhat better, but Black's counterplay shouldn't be underestimated.}) 17. Bc1 Qc6 18. f4 (18. g4!? Qc4 19. Qxc4 Nxc4 20. b3 Ne5 21. c4 $14 {also would have kept Black under long-lasting pressure.}) 18... Nc4 19. Qd3 Qe4 {Black manages to liquidate into the endgame, but it doesn't fully solve his problems.} 20. g4 Ne3 21. Rde1 Qxd3 22. cxd3 Nd5 23. Reg1! {White reasonably prefers to keep both rooks on the board.} (23. g5?! {would be premature:} Rxe1 24. Rxe1 g6! {and the initiative is almost gone.}) 23... Re6?! ({Much better was} 23... Re2! 24. g5 Kh7 25. Ng4 Rae8 {, and the activity of rooks offers Black definite counter-play. For instance,} 26. g6+ (26. f5 hxg5 27. Bxg5 Be7 28. Bxe7 R8xe7 29. c4 Nf6 $11) 26... Kg8 27. f5 f6 28. c4 Nb4 $132) 24. g5 Ne7?! {This is too passive.} ({After} 24... Kh7 25. Ng4 Re2 26. f5 hxg5 27. Bxg5 Be7 28. Bxe7 Rxe7 29. h6 g6 30. fxg6+ fxg6 31. Rf1 $16 {The h-passer also would have put Black in danger, but it was the lesser evil.}) 25. gxh6 Rxh6 26. f5 Rh7 27. Ng4 (27. f6! {was stronger:} Nd5 28. fxg7 Rxg7 29. Rf1 Re8 30. h6 Rg6 31. Nf3 $18 {and Black doesn't seem to withstand the pressure.}) 27... Kh8 28. f6 Ng8 29. fxg7+ {This inaccuracy enables Black to activate the pieces.} ({More energetic was} 29. h6! Nxf6 ({Black is almost paralysed also after} 29... gxf6 30. Rf1 Re8 31. Be3 c5 32. c4) 30. Nxf6 gxf6 31. Re1 {White is dominating over the board, and he should be able to win.}) 29... Rxg7 30. Be3 c5! {It's necessary to cover the d4-square.} 31. Bf4?! {Another step in the wrong direction.} ({A much stronger move was} 31. Bd2! Re8 (31... Kh7 32. Nf2 $16) 32. c4 Re6 33. Bc3 f6 34. Rg3 $16 {and Black is still doomed to passive defence.}) 31... Re8! 32. Ne3 Rxg1+ 33. Rxg1 Re6 {Black's pieces are slowly getting out now.} 34. Nd5 Nf6 35. Nc7 Re2 36. Nb5 Re6! {A cool-blooded reply, that makes it difficult for White to make real progress.} 37. Rf1?! {Spoiling all the advantage.} ({The last chance was} 37. Nxa7 Kh7 38. Rh1 Ng4 39. Nb5 Nf2 40. Rf1 Nxd3 41. Bxd6! Bxd6 42. Rxf7+ Kh6 43. Rd7 Re1+ 44. Kc2 c4 45. Rxd6+ Kxh5 $14 {Black is very active, but the draw isn't guaranteed yet.}) 37... Kg8 38. Nc7 {Accepting a draw by repetition.} (38. Nxa7 Nxh5 39. Bc1 Rf6 40. Rg1+ Rg6 {is fine for Black.}) 38... Re2 39. Nb5 Re6 40. Nc7 Re2 41. Nb5 Re6 1/2-1/2

