1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. f3 d5 5. a3 Be7 6. e4 dxe4 7. fxe4 e5 8. d5 Bc5 9. Bg5 (9. Nf3 {is more common.}) 9... O-O (9... h6 {is usually played here. An old example:} 10. Bh4 Bd4 11. Nf3 Bxc3+ 12. bxc3 Qd6 13. Bd3 Nbd7 14. O-O O-O 15. Kh1 Nc5 16. Qe2 Bg4 17. Qe3 Nfd7 18. Bg3 Bxf3 {1/2 (18) Moskalenko,V (2500)-Chekhov,V (2480) Leningrad 1989}) 10. Nf3 Bg4 {Black has a different concept. Anticipating White's long castling, he doesn't want to weaken his queenside by means of ...h7-h6. However, the present game shows that it is not easy to equalise in any case.} (10... h6 11. Bh4 Bg4 12. Bd3 (12. h3!?) 12... Nbd7 13. h3 Bxf3 14. Qxf3 c6 15. O-O-O Bd4 16. Ne2 Qb6 17. Nxd4 exd4 18. Bxf6 Nxf6 19. e5 cxd5 20. exf6 dxc4 21. fxg7 Rfc8 22. Bf5 c3 23. Qf2 cxb2+ 24. Kb1 Rc3 25. Rd3 $18 {1-0 (47) Yakimenko,A (2178)-Stojcevski,Z (2352) Sunny Beach 2011}) (10... a5!? 11. Qd2 Qd6 12. Be3?! (12. O-O-O) 12... Na6 13. Bd3 Bd7 14. O-O Qb6 15. Rae1 Ng4 $36 {0-1 (57) Kantorik,M (2289)-Solodovnichenko,Y (2430) Zvolen 2000}) 11. h3 Bxf3 12. Qxf3 Nbd7 (12... Bd4 13. Ne2! Bxb2 $140 ?! 14. Ra2 Bd4 15. Nxd4 exd4 16. e5 $16) 13. O-O-O Bd4 14. Ne2! {[%mdl 4]} c5 (14... Nc5?? 15. Nxd4 exd4 16. e5 h6 17. Bxf6 gxf6 18. exf6 {1-0 (18) Petrosian,K (2340)-Suess,M Budapest 1990}) ({But perhaps it was better to refrain from the text move keeping a possibility to play ...c7-c6 later on:} 14... Re8 {Black even sacrifices a pawn but obtains the e5-square if White takes on d4:} 15. Nxd4 (15. Kb1 c6!) 15... exd4 16. Rxd4 Qe7 17. Bd3 $140 Ne5 18. Qe3 c5 19. dxc6 bxc6) 15. g4 a5? (15... a6! {[%cal Yb7b5,Ya8b8] was now a must.}) 16. Kb1 Ra6 17. Ng3 {[%mdl 128] Suddenly it turns out that Black can hardly develop his attack since he can't push ...b7-b5. Meanwhile, White's assault goes slowly but smoothly.} g6?! {This weakening move makes White's attack unstoppable. It was better to refrain from it, even allowing White's knight will a powerful position on f5:} (17... Rb6 {is useless, e.g.} 18. Rh2 a4 19. h4 Rb3 20. Rd3) (17... a4 $142 18. Nf5 Qa5 19. Rh2 Rb6 {[%cal Rb6b3]} 20. Bc1 $140 Ne8! {[%cal Ye8d6]}) 18. h4 a4 19. Rh2 (19. Bh6 Ne8 (19... Re8? 20. g5 Nh5 21. Nxh5 gxh5 22. Bh3) 20. Bxf8 Rf6 21. Qd3 Kxf8 {wins an exchange but White wants more.}) 19... Qa5 20. Bd2 Qc7 21. g5 Ne8 22. h5 Rb6 23. Bc1 Rb3 24. Qg4 Nb6 25. Be2 Nd6 26. Rdh1 Bxb2 {Desperation.} (26... Nbxc4 27. Bxc4 Nxc4 28. hxg6 fxg6 29. Qe6+ Qf7 30. Rxh7! Qxe6 31. dxe6 $18) 27. Bxb2 Nbxc4 28. Bxc4 Nxc4 29. hxg6 Qb6! {[%mdl 256] Magnus Carlsen finds the most stubborn defence. It is not easy for White to find the decisive blow over the board.} 30. g7 (30. gxf7+ $142 ! Rxf7 31. Nh5! Rxb2+ 32. Ka1 Rxh2 33. Nf6+ Kg7 34. Rxh2 $18 {(K.Landa)}) 30... Rd8 31. Qh4 Rxb2+ 32. Ka1 Rxh2 33. Rxh2 Qg6 34. Nf5 Re8 35. Qg4 {[%mdl 64] White fails to find the winning idea:} (35. Qf2 b6 (35... Nd6 36. Rh6) 36. Rxh7! Qxh7 37. Nh6+ $18) 35... Qb6 36. Qh3 (36. g6! Qxg6 (36... fxg6 37. Nh6+ Kxg7 38. Qd7+) 37. Qh3 $18 {[%cal Rh2g2]}) 36... Qg6 37. d6? {Wrong!} (37. Qf3! {[%cal Rh2h7] was still good enough.}) 37... Nxd6 $8 38. Nxd6 Rd8! 39. Nc4? (39. Nc8!! Kxg7 40. Ne7 Rd1+ 41. Ka2 Qe6+ (41... Qb6 42. Qxh7+ Kf8 43. Qh6+) (41... Qxg5 42. Nf5+ Kf8 43. Ne3) 42. Qxe6 fxe6 43. Rh6 {still gave White some winning chances but Hikaru Nakamura had no chances to find such niceties that day.}) 39... Qxe4 40. Qh5?? (40. Ne3 $13) 40... Rd3! {Black starts a decisive counter-attack.} 41. Rh4 Qf5 42. Qe2 b5 43. Nd2 Qxg5 (43... Rxa3+ 44. Kb2 Rg3 {was even stronger but the World Champion correctly judges that his pawn avalanche will be much stronger than White's knight anyway.}) 44. Qxd3 Qxh4 45. Ne4 Kxg7 46. Qf3 (46. Qd5 Qf4 47. Nxc5 Qd4+ $19 {}) 46... Qf4 47. Qg2+ Kf8 48. Kb2 h5 49. Nd2 h4 50. Kc2 b4 51. axb4 cxb4 52. Qa8+ Kg7 53. Qxa4 h3 54. Qb3 h2 55. Qd5 e4 56. Qh5 e3 57. Nf3 e2 58. Kb3 f6 59. Ne1 Qg3+ 60. Ka4 Qg1 61. Qxe2 Qa7+ 0-1

