{[%evp 0,71,19,38,40,41,51,32,72,38,11,13,23,27,60,54,57,30,35,5,0,-29,1,1,0,-7,-1,-22,-16,-33,-9,-35,-38,-28,-30,-30,-14,-31,-46,-50,-53,-65,-68,-92,-92,-135,-135,-141,-125,-251,-242,-242,-206,-216,-210,-224,-224,-233,-243,-222,-202,-213,-200,-190,-200,-222,-209,-215,-232,-236,-246,-253,-288,-271] Having started the tournament with 9 draws, Magnus chances of competing for first place seemed theoretical, yet in the 10th round he got the exact results he needed, not only winning his own game vs. So, but also the leaders faltering meaning suddenly going into the last round there was actually a real chance! Still, apart from Ding not winning his game, beating MVL with the black pieces was not exactly an easy task, but at least the Frenchman also needed a win as this was the only chance he had left for being in the top 4 of the Grand Chess Tour standings meaning we had two players both willing to fight and take risks!} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 {Maxime for many years was a big fan of 3.Nc3, but recently seemed to have shifted to the Rossolimo. The popular choice among the elite, Magnus faced it no less than 4(!) times in the Sinquefield Cup!} g6 4. Bxc6 bxc6!? {In the WC-match but also the previous tournaments Magnus went for the more solid 4...dxc6, but earlier in the tournament he did experiment with capturing towards the centre against Anand, so this more ambitious more hardly came as a surprise to Vachier.} 5. d4!? {The main line, at least if you look at number of games is 5 0-0, but Anand also played this more direct approach against Magnus.} cxd4 ({Vishy was better after} 5... Bg7 6. dxc5 Qa5+ 7. Nbd2 Qxc5 8. O-O d6 9. Re1 f6 10. a3 Nh6 11. b4 Qh5 12. c4 {, even if the game ended in a solid draw.}) {Magnus, however, had not lost faith in Black's positional potential, but alters the move order while striving for a similar structure.} 6. Qxd4 f6 7. O-O d6 8. c4 c5 {The Indian prodigy Praggnanandhaa played like this earlier this year in the B-group of Wijk Aan Zee, earning a draw against the higher rated Kovalev. It's easy to see the attraction: Black has a broad stabilised centre and the pair of bishops, in many ways leaving the bigger burden on White, having to prove that his initiative is not only temporary.} 9. Qd3 Bg7 10. b3 {Its hard to see why MVL did not play 10.Nc3 first.} Nh6 {But Magnus does not pick up the gauntlet, yet.} ({Maybe he just wanted to tempt Black to play} 10... f5 {when} 11. e5 Bb7 12. Bb2 Nh6 13. Ng5!? {is an interesting pawn sacrifice aiming for the e6 square, before Black manages to stabilize with ...Nf7.}) 11. Nc3 Rb8 12. Bd2 {At first sight maybe a strange looking move, but not much action is expected on the long diagonal, as Black plans to keep it close, while instead MVL has ambitions on the kingside.} O-O 13. Rae1 Nf7 {A fascinating position. Modern chess suffers from symmetry and too much balance, while here there is so much life and complexity. Stockfish somewhat prefers White, suggesting moves like 14.Nh4!? preparing 15.f4, when Black should either respond with ...e6 planning ...f5, or reroute the knight to d4, via e5-c6. Its just a very interesting position, and Maxime adds his own flavour, playing the ever so popular flank-pawn push!} 14. h4?! (14. Nh4!? {[%cal Gf2f4]} e6 {[%cal Gf6f5]} (14... Ne5 {[%cal Ge5c6,Gc6d4]})) 14... Rb7!? {As White did not want the bishop on the diagonal, Black chooses not to financhetto his bishop but instead the rook! But there is logic: as Black wants his bishop remaining on c8, eying the kingside and a potential ...f5, why not just improve the rook's prospects? Still it is an extraordinary move, at least to me. It's "mysterious" in many ways, illustrating perhaps that in standard positions we have a good understanding based on experience of where the pieces belong, while under extraordinary circumstances like this one, one gets a more polarised view of the difference between an average grandmaster and the World Champion. ...Rb7 never would have entered my mind, while the game shows it makes sense, a lot of sense!} 15. h5 g5 {15...f5 also has logic, but Magnus keeps the position closed for the moment, also the pawn at h5 can now become a target, forcing White to act.} 16. Nh2?! {Probably like 14 h4?! a step in the wrong direction.} ({The computer seems to indidate} 16. Ne2 {[%cal Ge2g3,Gg3f5] rerouting the knight to the kingside and the f5 square especially. Still the position is so complex and unfamiliar that both for the players and commentators it is very hard to say. Black could then either continioue with ...e6 covering the f5 square, ...Be6 intending ...Qd7 and ...a5 but maybe best, just like with 14...Rb7!?, improving the position while remaining flexible is the key, meaning} Kh8! (16... Be6 {[%cal Gd8d7]}) (16... e6 {[%csl Ge6][%cal Ge6f5]})) 16... Ne5 17. Qg3 Kh8 18. f4 {With hindsight this is easy to criticize based on the result, but MVL is just consistently following his planned concept.} gxf4 19. Bxf4 Rg8 20. Re3 Nc6 {Vacating the diagonal as preparation for ...f5 when the e5 and d4 squares not only become relevant for Black's knight, but certainly now also the bishop.} ({The computer, however, prefers} 20... Qf8!? 21. Qh4 Bh6 22. Rg3 Bxf4 23. Rxg8+ Kxg8 24. Qxf4 Nf7!? {with ...Qh6 or ...Qg7-g5 as the follow-up.}) 21. Qf2 f5 22. Nf3? {Speaking with MVL just after the game, the Frenchman said he thought his position here was already very bad. It is easy to understand why one would think so, Black's position has enormous potential and the pieces are about to spring to life.} ({But the computer does not suffer from such generalisation, and just states that} 22. exf5! Bxf5 (22... Bd4 23. Ne2 Bxe3 24. Bxe3 {gives White sufficient compensation for the exchange.}) 23. Rg3 {really is no big deal.} {After} Qe8 (23... Bd4 {is now parried by} 24. Be3) {White can play} 24. Ne2! (24. h6 Bxc3 25. Rxc3 e5 {shows why the rook is excellently placed on b7.}) 24... Qxh5 25. Rg5 Qf7 26. Be3 {with sufficient compensation for the pawn, leading to an equal position.}) {Yet to stress it once again: these positions are so unfamiliar, meaning the complexity of playing them is uncomparable to more standard positions where one can rely on experience.} 22... Bxc3! {Exchanging the strong bishop for a knight that is out of play!? Perhaps, but that just goes to show how one can easily create false narratives in chess. In reality Black speeds up the attack by removing a bishop that is A) in the way when Black wants to attack via the g-file, and B) would be locked in behind its own pawns, when Black now in the following wants to play ...e5 and ...f4.} 23. Rxc3 e5 24. Rd3!? {Maxime realises the severity of his troubles, and tries complicating.} (24. Bh2 f4 {would be a positional tragedy with Black doubling in the g-file, the knight reaching d4 etc. etc. The computer instantly evaluates it as -4! indicating the hopelessness.}) 24... exf4! {Praising a World Champion for taking a piece is maybe to much, but this does involve selfpinning his rook to be taken by a white pawn. Magnus took his time reassuring himself that while at first sight it looks dangerous, Black's king is safe and White's not:} 25. Qb2+ Rbg7 26. h6 fxe4 27. Rd2 ({No tactics are working, the funniest try being} 27. hxg7+ Rxg7 28. Ne5!? {, but} Nxe5 29. Qxe5 exd3! {wins easily, not that it is the only way either.}) 27... exf3 28. hxg7+ Rxg7 29. Rxf3 Qg5 {Generally the game is over. Magnus has two minor pieces for the rook, and in the following reroutes the bishop cleverly to the g8 square safeguarding his king.} 30. Rdf2 Nd4 31. Rxf4 Bf5 32. b4 Be6! 33. Rf8+ Bg8 34. bxc5 dxc5 35. Qb8 Qe3 36. Qd6 {Threatening 37.Rxg8+ Rxg8 38 Qf6+ with a perpetual.} Qc1+ 37. Kh2 Qg5 38. Qd5 Qh4+ 39. Kg1 Qe7 {Controlling e5, so there is no perpetual, also, Black creates threats of his own.} 40. Qh5 Qe3 41. Qh4 Ne2+ 42. Kh1 (42. Kh2 Qe5+ {also instantly finishes the game.}) 42... Qc1+ 43. Rf1 Ng3+ 44. Kg1 ({If} 44. Qxg3 {then Black interpolates} Qxf1+ {before taking White's queen.}) 44... Qe3+ {And as 45 R1f2 Qe1+ 46 Rf1 Ne2+! wins the queen and mates at the same time, Maxime resigned. This earned Magnus a what just 2 days earlier seemed a completely unlikely playoff. A dicipline he has dominated to the extend that it almost became mythical like he could never lose with his last loss dating back more than 10 years tgo his teenage days. yet Ding won, and did it in a convincing way. While the result on the day was bad, it does however also put into perspective what an achievement Magnus playoff streak was. It takes you to show up every time giving your absolute best, even when a World Championship is at stake.} 0-1

