1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d4 exd4 5. O-O a6 6. Bxc6 dxc6 7. Nxd4 Be7 8. f3 O-O 9. Be3 Nd7 10. Nc3 Ne5 11. Qe2 Bb4 12. Nd1 c5 13. Nb3 c4 14. Nd4 c5 15. Nf5 Bxf5 16. exf5 Re8 17. Nf2 Nc6 18. Ne4 Nd4 19. Bxd4 Qxd4+ 20. Kh1 Qxb2 21. a3 Ba5 22. Rab1 Qe5 23. Rxb7 Qxf5 24. Qxc4 Qe6 25. Qxc5 Bd8 26. Qd4 Be7 27. Rb6 Qa2 28. c4 Qxa3 29. c5 a5 30. Ra1 Rad8 31. Nd6 Bf6 32. Rxa3 Bxd4 33. Nxe8 Bxc5 34. Rd6 Rxe8 0-1

[Event "Sinquefield Cup 7th"]
[Site "Saint Louis"]
[Date "2019.08.27"]
[Round "10"]
[White "Carlsen, Magnus"]
[Black "So, Wesley"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C54"]
[WhiteElo "2882"]
[BlackElo "2776"]
[PlyCount "85"]

{[%evp 0,57,19,38,25,7,30,27,27,7,3,9,16,2,13,-5,17,-8,10,0,24,2,3,-8,3,13,26,24,27,27,22,19,4,5,6,-13,-24,-17,-24,-13,0,-34,-42,-5,0,-13,-19,7,53,53,44,-12,8,0,0,0,0,0,0,0]} 1. e4!? {Starting off with 9 draws the strategic situation for the world champion was pretty simple, nothing short of 2 wins would leave any chance of first place. This being his 6th white, it was still the first game where Magnus opened with the king's pawn, having recently preferred a more 1.d4 based approach, but in the moment of need, it felt like time for a surprise.} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Wesley is loyal to his solid approach, inviting to a Berlin.} 3. Bc4 {This used to be considered basically inferior to 3.Bb5 and the Ruy Lopez, but as this position appeared 10(!) times in the Sinquefield Cup, it indicates the present preferences of the top players. Compared to 3.Bb5 it has the advantage of leading to less forced lines.} Bc5 4. O-O Nf6 5. d3 O-O 6. c3 d6 7. h3 h6!? ({Earlier in the tournament Wesley had tried the more extravagant} 7... a6 8. a4 Ba7 9. Re1 Kh8!? {ambitiously aiming for ...Ng8 and ...f5, but fearing Magnus preparation he sensibly chooses one of the main lines.}) 8. Re1 a6 9. a4 a5!? {Obviously a strange looking move, moving the a-pawn continiously. But there is logic behind it:} ({After the immediate} 9... Be6 {, then} 10. Bxe6 fxe6 11. b4 Ba7 12. Nbd2 {leads to troubles for Black like in e.g. Anand-Carlsen from Stavanger 2017 as White's expansion on the queenside makes a lot of difference in the ...fxe6 structures. The space itself obviously, but also that a rook on a2 will defend the f2-pawn eventually.}) {Now, however, Black uses the possibility to fix the queenside structure. One could argue why not 8...a5 then? It is a reasonable move, played by Ding, but there potentially White has the option of a3+b4, while now the b4-square is definitely under Black's control.} 10. Nbd2 Be6 {As explained previously, the benefit of having played ...a5 is that Black now can play ...Be6 without fearing the ...Bxe6 fxe6 structures, and So obviously exploits that:} 11. Bb5!? Na7 12. d4 Nxb5 13. dxc5!? {Earlier this year in Wijk Aan Zee, Giri took back on b5 against Anand, with the game ending in a quick draw.} Na7 14. b3 Re8 {Still common ground, White's concept is that 14...Nc6 is met by 15 cxd6 cxd6 ( 15...Qxd6? 16 Ba3! wins an exchange, and is the point of 14 b3) 16Ba3 Re8 17 c4! when the resulting pawn structure heavily favours White, explaining why So had to put his rook on e8, in order to be able to take back on d6 with the queen.} 15. cxd6 Qxd6 16. Ba3 c5 {Grischuk played 16...Qa6 against MVL in the 2017 World Cup, but while he got a good position in the game, the computer very much prefers 16...c5.} 17. Nc4!? {Over the board inspiration, and making the inclusion of b3 having a positive effect. MVL had a later game with Karjakin in 2019 where he continued 17.Qe2, but Magnus' move is, despite the exchange of queens, somewhat more aggressive.} Qxd1 18. Raxd1 Bxc4 {For a human, an obvious choice, but the computers actually insists that 18...Nd7 also is a reasonable move as after 19 Ne3, which seems to nicely give White control over the d5 square, after 19...Red8 20 Nd5 Rb8, it indeed is difficult for White to make any reasonable progress. Being a human being, So, however, obviously took the knight.} 19. bxc4 {Explaining the games to the commentators, Magnus said that this was paying a bit of hommage to Mikhail Botvinnik who in numerous games showed the dynamic potential of this exact pawn structure where the doubled pawns obviously are potential weaknesses and thus the position is much sharper than it appears at first sight: Black needs to hurry, as seen in the game White's potential is huge if the position stabilizes.} b6?! ({The computers strongly suggest} 19... Rac8! {the idea being to protect the b7-pawn from c7, and then regrouping with ...Nc8/d6 immediately attacking White's fragile c4-pawn. At first the computers says Black is better, but after a while settles on} 20. Nh2 (20. Nh4 g6 21. f3 {followed by g4/Ng2-e3 leads to similar positions.}) 20... Rc7 21. Nf1 Nc8 22. Ne3 Nb6 23. Rb1 Nxa4 24. f3 {when White has sacrificed a pawn, and offers a second on c3, but as compensation gets serious initiative combing threats via the b-line with the knight landing on d5. White is neither better nor worse, but the position is very interesting with rich possibilities for both to outplay the opponent.}) {So instead chooses solidity, but this permanently places the initiative with White.} 20. Nh4 Rad8 21. f3 g6 22. g3 {22.g4 intending the same knight regrouping as in the game, but at the same time stopping Black's ...Nh5 had merits, but Magnus wanted to keep his own kingside structure as flexible as possible.} Nh5 23. Kf2 Nc6?! {Again Wesley's choice is more solid than good.} (23... Nc8 {23...Nc8!? would reroute the knight to d6, attacking the c-pawn and after} 24. Ng2 Nd6 25. Ne3 f5 {create some counterplay.}) {But So believes in the solidity of his position.} 24. Bc1 Kg7 25. Be3 Rxd1 ({Once again} 25... Ne7 26. Ng2 f5! {was called for, creating some counterplay, but its repeating the previous remark, as Black's play is based on the wrong assumption that he has an impregnable fortress.}) 26. Rxd1 Rd8 27. Rb1! {Keeping a pair of rooks is an absolute neccesity.} Rb8 28. Ng2 Nf6 29. Ke2 Ne8 30. Bf2 Nd6 31. Ne3 Ne7? {Blacks position was bad, much worse than it looks, but with this it becomes just plainly lost. But again: Black thinks he has a fortress.} 32. Nd5! Nxd5 33. cxd5 Rb7 34. Kd3 {One could argue a slight inaccuracy, the computer prefers 34 c4!?.} f5 {This created a weakness on e5, but as White anyway could force that with f4xe5 after a black ...f6, it makes no difference.} ({But even after} 34... c4+ 35. Kd2 Nc8 36. Bc5! {followed by the f4 break, Blacks position will collapse rather quickly.}) 35. c4 fxe4+!? {Facing reality, So alternates his plan, tries to complicate.} ({The problem is, as Magnus happily explained after the game, that the "fortress" after} 35... Kf6 36. g4 f4 37. Be1 g5 38. Bc3! {has one problem: Yes, everything is solididly protected and White can do no harm to Black's position on his own. But as Black is not allowed to "pass", Zugzwang here plays its deadly consequence. Black has to make a move, and that makes his position instantly collapse. The rook needs to protect the b-pawn, but if it goes to b8, it allows Bxa5. The knight on d6 is needed there to protect the rook on b7, else Bxa5 again is possible, and as the king is stuck on f6 protecting e5, and Black has run out of pawn moves, this is a pure and illustrative zugzwang. Black's position will fall like a cardhouse, when removing one bit, will eventually make it all fall.}) 36. fxe4 Rf7 {A good try, even allowing the hope for the trap below.} 37. Rxb6 (37. Be3 Rf3 38. Rxb6?? Rxe3+!) 37... Rxf2 38. Rxd6 Rf3+ 39. Ke2?! {Sloppy technique.} (39. Kd2 Rf2+ 40. Ke3 Rc2 41. Kd3 Ra2 42. Rc6 {was a very trivial win. as White gets connected passed pawns while keeping Black's counterplay to a minimum.}) 39... Rc3 40. Re6 Rxc4 41. Rxe5 Rxa4 42. Re7+ Kf6? {Returning the favour and making the win trivial.} (42... Kf8! {would surprisingly force a huge degree of precision on White's part.} 43. Re6! {is the only winning move.} ({After} 43. d6? Rd4 44. e5 a4 45. Ke3 a3 46. Ra7 Rd5! {White cannot successfully advance his connected passed pawns, as after} 47. Ke4 ({or} 47. Kf4) 47... Rd4+! {the king is forced back, and Black will just return the rook to d5, again attacking the e5 pawn forcing a repetition.}) 43... Rb4 {again forces precision, as} 44. Kf3! {is needed. The difference is that after} a4 45. Rxg6 a3 46. Ra6 Rb3+ {now} 47. Kg4! {is available, the point being that after} c4 48. d6 c3 49. Ra8+ Kf7 50. d7 c2 {White simply plays} 51. d8=Q {soon mating the black king, but if the white king were on f4, then 51...c1Q would be a check, reversing the table.  Very much a line that the World Champion would be able to calculate, but still an unneccesary risk to take indeed.}) 43. d6 {44.e5+ follows next, and the pawns marches through.} 1-0

