1. Nf3 c5 2. e4 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. g3 e5 7. Nde2 Be7 8. Bg2 b5 (8... O-O {is the other main line.}) 9. Nd5 {A quite rare move, because sometimes White delays this  move.} Nbd7 10. Nec3 Bb7 11. a4 Nxd5 $146 {A novelty by Grischuk.} (11... bxa4 12. Rxa4 O-O 13. O-O Nxd5 14. Nxd5 Bxd5 15. exd5 f5 16. Qe2 Nc5 17. Rc4 $14 {Mestre Bellido-Colon Garcia, Catalunya 2013, and White was more comfortable.}) (11... O-O 12. axb5 axb5 13. Rxa8 Bxa8 14. Nxb5 (14. O-O!?) 14... Bxd5 (14... Nxd5 15. exd5 Qa5+ 16. Nc3 $14 {and White is a healthy  up.}) 15. exd5 Qa5+ 16. Nc3 Rb8 17. O-O Bd8 18. Kh1 Qb4 19. Qe2 Bb6 20. Nb1! $14 {Lutikov-Magerramov, Moscow 1983.}) 12. Nxd5 Nf6 13. axb5 (13. Nxe7!? {was also an interesting option,} Qxe7 14. axb5 axb5 15. Rxa8+ Bxa8 16. Qd3 $14 {and White has a slight advantage.}) 13... Nxd5 14. exd5 axb5 15. Rxa8 Qxa8 16. Qd3 O-O 17. O-O ({The greedy} 17. Qxb5? {does not work, because of} Qa1 18. O-O Ba6 $17 {and Black wins an exchange.}) 17... Bc8 ({The solid} 17... Qa6 {was also possible.}) 18. Be3 Bd7 19. Rc1 {The start of a deep plan.} (19. f4 Bf6 $11) 19... h6 20. Qd1 Qb7 21. Ra1 Ra8 22. h4 Ra6 23. b3 Bd8 24. c4 bxc4 25. bxc4 Bb6 26. c5! {The only practical chance! With this move Carlsen creates complications.} Bxc5 27. Bxc5 dxc5 28. d6 Qb6 (28... Rxa1! {was more accurate,} 29. Qxa1 e4 30. Qe5 Qb1+ 31. Kh2 e3! 32. fxe3 c4 $11 {and Black has also a dangerous passed-.}) 29. Rxa6 Qxa6 30. Bd5! {White's  on d6 and the weakness on f7 create a slightly uncomfortable position for Black. With a couple of accurate moves Black should be out of trouble, but in a practical game, it is always difficult.} Qc8? {A passive retreat.} (30... Qb6! {was more accurate} 31. Be4 (31. Qh5 Qb1+! {an important resource,} 32. Kg2 Qf5 $11 {with equality.}) (31. Kg2 Be8 $11) 31... c4 32. Qd5 Qb8 33. Qxe5 c3 $11 {is also drawish.}) 31. Qb3! {Now White exerts some pressure, it is difficult for Black to find moves.} Be8 32. Qc3! {Another accurate move, now White gains back a .} c4 (32... Qf5! {was the best reply,} 33. Qa5!? {A very nice deep move by Mr. Houdini!} (33. Qxc5 g5!) 33... Qd7 34. Qxc5 Qb5 {and the passed d- gives White a slight edge, but Black should hold with perfect play.}) 33. Bxc4 Bd7 34. Qb3 Qe8 35. Qf3 Kf8 36. h5?! (36. Bd5! $16 {was more accurate to avoid Black's counterplay.}) 36... Kg8? {An error in reply.} (36... e4! {should have played} 37. Qc3 (37. Qe3 Qe5 38. Qb6 e3!) 37... e3! 38. Qxe3 (38. fxe3 Qe4 $132) 38... Qxe3 39. fxe3 Bc6 $11 {and Black will survive.}) 37. Qe4 {Now White is fine again.} Bc6 38. Bd5 Bd7 {Of course Black can't exchange the s, because the d- is decisive.} 39. Kg2 Kh8 {Black can only defend passively.} 40. f4 {A tricky move by Carlsen, moreover just on move 40! So still Grischuk needs to play 1 move before the time control.} exf4? {Losing immediately, but it was a difficult situation especially in time trouble.} (40... f5 41. Qxe5 Qxh5 42. Qe7 Kh7 43. Bf3 (43. Qxd7? {is bad, because of} Qe2+ $11 {and perpetual checks.}) 43... Qe8 44. Qxe8 Bxe8 45. Bd5 Bd7 46. Kf3 $18 {should be winning for White.}) (40... f6! {was tougher} 41. fxe5 fxe5 42. g4 (42. Qf3 Qb8) 42... Qc8 {and Black has some chance to survive.}) 41. Qxe8+ Bxe8 42. Bxf7! $18 {This is the point! Now Black has no hope left.} Bc6+ 43. Kf2 fxg3+ 44. Kxg3 Bd7 45. Bg6 {and Black resigned.} (45. Bg6 Kg8 46. Kf4 Kf8 47. Ke5 (47. Bf5 Ba4) 47... Kg8 48. Bf5 Ba4 49. d7 Bxd7 50. Bxd7 {and thanks to the g7-, Black gets a zugzwang.} Kh7 (50... Kf7 51. Be6+ Ke7 52. Kf5 $18) 51. Kf5 Kg8 52. Kg6 Kh8 53. Kf7 Kh7 54. Bf5+ Kh8 55. Kf8 $18) 1-0

