1. d4 {[%emt 0:00:04]} Nf6 {[%emt 0:00:07]} 2. c4 {[%emt 0:00:05]} e6 {[%emt 0:00:05]} 3. Nf3 {[%emt 0:00:05]} b6 {[%emt 0:00:08] The Queen's Indian Defence didn't really come as a surprise to us. It was one of the openings we thought Magnus might be attracted to.} 4. g3 {[%emt 0:00:11]} Bb4+ {[%emt 0:00:09]} 5. Bd2 {[%emt 0:00:18]} Be7 {[%emt 0:00:12] This on the other hand is a very rare sideline. We didn't really pay too much attention to this line but still we made sure that Vishy knew some important details here.} 6. Nc3 {[%emt 0:01:19]} Bb7 {[%emt 0:00:09]} 7. Bg2 {[%emt 0:00:13]} c6 {[%emt 0:00:11]} 8. e4!? {[%emt 0:06:07] Probably the critical test for the whole line} (8. O-O d5 {would lead to positions which are mostly seen in the Catalan. It has to be said that Black is very solid here, so Vishy decided to choose a more active option.} 9. Ne5 O-O 10. Bf4 Nbd7 {is the mainline here but it's not easy to prove anything as White. Many games played by GM Tiviakov can be found here.}) 8... d5 {[%emt 0:00:37]} 9. exd5! {[%emt 0:01:26]} (9. Qe2 dxc4! (9... O-O?! 10. e5!) 10. O-O O-O 11. Rfd1 b5 {and I don't think White has enough compensation for the pawn here}) (9. e5 Ne4! 10. cxd5 cxd5 11. O-O O-O 12. Re1 Nxd2 13. Qxd2 Ba6 {and Black looks fine to me.}) 9... cxd5 {[%emt 0:00:09]} 10. Ne5 {[%emt 0:00:22]} O-O {[%emt 0:00:33]} 11. O-O {[%emt 0:00:48]} Nc6 {[%emt 0:00:22]} (11... Nbd7?! 12. Re1 Rc8 13. Rc1 $14 {It's slightly better for White and also it's not easy to suggest a good move for Black here} Ba8 14. Bg5!? (14. cxd5 Nxe5 15. Rxe5 exd5 16. Bg5 $14) 14... h6 15. Bxf6 Nxf6 16. cxd5 Nxd5 17. Nxd5 exd5 18. Qa4 $14) 12. cxd5! {[%emt 0:06:22] Only this one can pose any problems for Black.} (12. Bf4 Na5 13. Rc1 Rb8! {the most precise} (13... dxc4 {this would be a natural reaction but I'm not entirely convinced it would be enough for equality} 14. Bxb7 Nxb7 15. Nxc4 Rc8! (15... Na5?! 16. Ne3! (16. Nxa5 bxa5 17. Be5 Rc8 {with a balanced position}) 16... Rc8 17. d5 Bc5 18. dxe6 fxe6 19. Ng4! {A very important resource, White is better thanks to his pawn structure}) 16. Nb5 (16. Ne3 Bb4) 16... Na5! (16... a6?? 17. Na7 Ra8 18. Nc6 $18) 17. Nxa5 Rxc1 18. Qxc1 bxa5 19. Nxa7 Qxd4 20. Nc6 Qd7 21. Rd1 Nd5 22. Rd4 {and despite the fact that probably Black is solid enough, White's position looks a bit more pleasant}) 14. Nd3 (14. cxd5 Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Bxd5 $11) 14... Rc8 (14... dxc4!? 15. Bxb8 Bxg2 16. Kxg2 Qxb8 17. Ne5 Rd8 {and Black has decent compensation}) 15. cxd5 (15. c5 Nc6!) 15... Nxd5 16. Nxd5 Bxd5 17. Rxc8 Qxc8 18. Bxd5 exd5 19. Re1 Nc6 $11) 12... Nxe5 {[%emt 0:01:44]} 13. d6! {[%emt 0:02:20] Of course that's the point of White's idea.} (13. dxe5 Nxd5 $11) 13... Nc6 {[%emt 0:00:24]} (13... Bxg2? 14. dxe7 Qxe7 15. dxe5 Bxf1 16. exf6 Qxf6 17. Qxf1 $14) 14. dxe7 {[%emt 0:00:59]} Qxe7 {[%emt 0:01:39]} 15. Bg5 {[%emt 0:00:18]} h6 {[%emt 0:01:05]} 16. d5! {[%emt 0:03:20]} Na5 {[%emt 0:00:14]} (16... Rad8? 17. Bxf6 Qxf6 18. dxc6 Rxd1 19. Rfxd1 Bc8 20. Rac1 {and despite what engines are showing, practically speaking Black's position is very bad.}) 17. Bxf6 {[%emt 0:11:29]} Qxf6 {[%emt 0:00:37]} 18. dxe6 {[%emt 0:07:03]} Qxe6?! {[%emt 0:04:23]} (18... Bxg2!? {sacrificing a pawn was quite an interesting option but it has to be said that it works mainly thanks to tactical reasons} 19. exf7+ (19. Kxg2 Qxe6 20. Qf3 Rad8 {and the position is just equal}) 19... Qxf7 20. Kxg2 Nc4 21. Qb3!? (21. Qc2 Rad8 22. Rfd1 (22. Rad1?! Rd2!! 23. Rxd2 Ne3+ 24. Kg1 Nxc2 25. Rxc2) 22... Qf3+ 23. Kg1 Rde8 {and Black's initiative is quite obvious}) (21. Qe2 Rae8 22. Ne4 Nxb2! $11) 21... Rad8 22. Rae1 (22. Rad1 Rxd1 23. Nxd1 Rd8 24. Re1 Rd2 {and I believe here Black has enough compensation to make a comfortable draw}) 22... Rd2 23. Re2 Kh8! 24. Kg1! (24. Rxd2? Qf3+! $19) 24... Rxe2 25. Nxe2 Nd2 26. Qxf7 Rxf7 27. Rc1 Nf3+ 28. Kg2 Nxh2! {was the final point of Black's play.}) 19. Re1 {[%emt 0:01:48]} (19. Bxb7!? Nxb7 20. Qf3 Nc5 21. Rfe1 Qg6 22. Nd5 $14 {was also an interesting option. White's position is slightly better but it wouldn't be easy to increase the advantage. Vishy decided that a more complex continuation would give him more chances.}) 19... Qf6 {[%emt 0:01:08]} 20. Nd5! {[%emt 0:17:18]} Bxd5 {[%emt 0:12:23]} (20... Qxb2?! 21. Re2! Qa3 22. Re3 Qb2 23. Rb1! {and Black's position would be already quite difficult} Qxa2 24. Ra1! Qc4 25. Rxa5 bxa5 26. Ne7+ Kh8 27. Bxb7 Rad8 28. Qf3 {and White has a very strong initiative.}) 21. Bxd5 {[%emt 0:01:00]} Rad8 {[%emt 0:02:09]} 22. Qf3 {[%emt 0:02:26]} Qxb2 {[%emt 0:11:06]} (22... Qxf3 23. Bxf3 $14 {is a typical position where the bishop is simply better than the knight.}) 23. Rad1 {[%emt 0:01:03]} Qf6! {[%emt 0:13:01] A very practical decision. White has a very strong initiative which will be easier to handle in the endgame.} (23... Rd6? 24. Bxf7+! Rxf7 25. Re8+ Kh7 26. Qxf7 Rxd1+ 27. Kg2 {and there is no good defence} Qf6 (27... Rd6 28. Qf5+ Rg6 29. Re6 $18) 28. Qg8+ Kg6 29. Re6 $18) 24. Qxf6 {[%emt 0:01:29]} gxf6 {[%emt 0:00:08]} 25. Re7 {[%emt 0:01:19]} Kg7 {[%emt 0:03:27] Till this point I think Vishy had played an excellent game but here he missed his chance to pose more problems for Black.} 26. Rxa7? {[%emt 0:07:46]} (26. Rc7! {stopping ...c6 was neccessary at first} a6 27. Kg2! {another very important detail, now White has a xf7 threat} Kh8! {is the best way to play for Black} (27... b5? 28. Bxf7 Rxd1 29. Bh5+ Kg8 30. Bxd1 {it's already very difficult}) 28. Bf3 Rxd1 29. Bxd1 Kg7 30. Ra7 $16 {and White eventually will take the pawn back without exchanging anything on the queenside. I don't know how big exactly are White's winning chances but it's clear that it would be very difficult for Black to defend it successfully.}) 26... Nc6 {[%emt 0:00:55]} 27. Rb7?! {[%emt 0:09:28] This allows Black to exchange the last pawns on the queenside, so the weakness of the kingside doesn't matter any more.} (27. Ra4 {was the last chance to continue the game a bit} Rd7! 28. Bb3 Rxd1+ 29. Bxd1 Rd8 {and Black should hold this position fairly simply thanks to his active pieces.}) 27... Nb4 {[%emt 0:00:09]} 28. Bb3 {[%emt 0:00:07]} Rxd1+ {[%emt 0:00:05]} 29. Bxd1 {[%emt 0:00:07]} Nxa2 {[%emt 0:00:07]} 30. Rxb6 {[%emt 0:00:06]} Nc3 {[%emt 0:00:17]} 31. Bf3 {[%emt 0:00:10]} f5 {[%emt 0:00:14]} 32. Kg2 {[%emt 0:00:41]} Rd8 {[%emt 0:01:22]} 33. Rc6 {[%emt 0:01:35]} Ne4 {[%emt 0:00:08]} 34. Bxe4 {[%emt 0:00:08]} fxe4 {[%emt 0:00:04]} 35. Rc4 {[%emt 0:00:09]} f5 {[%emt 0:00:14]} 36. g4 {[%emt 0:00:14]} Rd2 {[%emt 0:00:34]} 37. gxf5 {[%emt 0:00:15]} e3 {[%emt 0:00:08]} 38. Re4 {[%emt 0:00:12]} Rxf2+ {[%emt 0:00:07]} 39. Kg3 {[%emt 0:00:04]} Rxf5 {[%emt 0:00:05]} 1/2-1/2

