1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 Bf5 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 h6 7. Bd3 $146 {This novelty can hardly pose Black new problems. The inclusion of h7-h6 does not affect the position much, so the resulting positions should be compared with the known 6.d3 line.} ({In his previous rapid game, Magnus went for the most ambitious:} 7. Qb3 Ra7 8. cxd5 cxd5 9. Ne5 e6 10. Bd2 {Black should have played:} Be7 ({and here instead of:} 10... Bd6? 11. Qa4+ Nc6 (11... Nbd7 12. Nb5 $18) 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. Qxc6+ $16 {1-0 Carlsen,M (2837)-Bologan,V (2732)/Astana KAZ 2012 (54)}) 11. Rc1 O-O 12. O-O Nfd7 {with a solid position. For instance,} 13. Nxd7 Nxd7 14. a4 Nb8 15. e4 dxe4 16. d5 Nd7 17. Be3 Nc5! (17... Bc5?! 18. Nxe4 Bxe3 19. Qxe3 Qb6 20. Ng3 $36 {½-½ Aronian,L (2802)-Carlsen,M (2826)/London 2011/CBM 146 (39)}) 18. Qc4 ({Black is clearly no longer at risk in the event of:} 18. Qc2 Ra8 19. dxe6 fxe6 20. Bxc5 Bxc5 21. Nxe4 Bd4 $11) 18... b6 19. g4 exd5 (19... Bh7!? 20. dxe6 Nxe6 21. Rfd1 Qb8 {is also fine for Black}) 20. Qxd5 Qxd5 21. Nxd5 Be6 22. Nxe7+ Rxe7 $11) 7... Bxd3 (7... e6 8. Bxf5 exf5 9. Qc2 g6 10. O-O Bd6 $11) 8. Qxd3 e6 9. O-O Bb4 10. Bd2 O-O 11. Rfd1 {This position is quite topical with the pawn on h7. Black has to solve a problem of b4 now - 11...bd7? is a tactical blunder (12.xd5!)} Bxc3 {The simplest way to complete the developement.} ({Protecting the bishop has its drawbacks:} 11... a5 12. a3 Bxc3 (12... Be7 13. e4 dxe4 14. Nxe4 Nxe4 15. Qxe4 Nd7 16. Bf4 $14) 13. bxc3 Nbd7 (13... dxc4 14. Qxc4 Qd5 15. Qe2 $14) 14. cxd5 cxd5 15. c4 $14) ({It was possible to keep the bishop on the board by means of} 11... Ba5!? 12. Rab1 Nbd7 13. b4 Bc7 14. e4 Nb6 15. cxd5 cxd5 16. e5 Nfd7 $132) 12. Bxc3 Nbd7 13. b3 {White is marginally better due to a space advantage, but it would be difficult to make progress.} Qe7 {At this stage of the game Hikaru correctly avoids any active actions.} (13... Ne4 14. Bb2 a5 15. Ne5 Nxe5 16. dxe5 Qe7 17. Qc2 $14) 14. Rac1 Rac8 (14... a5 15. Nd2 Rfd8 (15... a4 16. b4 Nb6 17. c5 Nbd7 18. b5 $14) 16. h3 Nf8 17. e4 dxe4 18. Nxe4 Nxe4 19. Qxe4 Ng6 20. a4 Qg5 21. g3 Rd7 22. h4 Qh5 23. Rd3 Ne7 24. Rcd1 $14) 15. Qe2 Ne4?! {This move looks natural, but the knight does not have much of a job to do there. Moreover, now White's plan of seizing the centre by means of f2-f3 and e3-e4 becomes more realistic.} ({A solid alternative was} 15... Rfd8 {completing the flexible setup. The play may continue:} 16. Ne1 dxc4 17. bxc4 c5 18. Qb2 b6 19. f3 (19. dxc5 Nxc5 20. Bxf6 Qxf6 21. Qxb6 Rxd1 22. Rxd1 Qc3 $11) 19... cxd4 20. Bxd4 Rc6 $11) 16. Bb2 Rfd8 17. Ne1 Nd6 {Allowing the unpleasant pin, but Black's position lacks any constructive ideas.} (17... Qg5 18. f3 Nd6 19. c5 (19. e4?! dxc4 20. bxc4 b5 $132) 19... Nf5 20. e4 Nh4 21. g3 $14) (17... dxc4 18. Qxc4 Nef6 19. Nd3 $14) (17... Nef6 18. Nd3 $14) 18. Ba3 $14 f5 {Hikaru is trying to stop e3-e5 at any cost, but the weakness of the dark squares in his camp is obvious now.} (18... Nf6 19. Nd3 Qc7 20. f3 $14) 19. Nd3 Nf6 20. Bb4 Qc7 21. Qf3 {Magnus decides to activate the queen and is ready to enter the endgame.} ({Black is definitely also under pressure after} 21. f3!? Nf7 22. Be1 Qe7 23. Bh4 $14) 21... dxc4 {This exchange can hardly suit Black - the pawn structure is better for White now. However, Hikaru was trying to push e6-e5.} (21... Nf7 22. h4 Re8 $14) 22. bxc4 Nf7 23. a4 {A positional threat of fixing the pawn structure is unpleasant, so Black is forced to take a decision now.} a5 {Creating one more potential weakness.} ({I would prefer} 23... b6 24. Qf4 Qxf4 25. Nxf4 Re8 (25... c5 26. Ba3 $14) 26. a5 bxa5 27. Bxa5 e5 28. dxe5 Nxe5 $14 {in the resulting position White has a stable positional advantage, but the material is reduced.}) 24. Be1 b6 25. Qg3 {Black cannot avoid an inferior endgame.} (25. Qf4!?) 25... Qxg3 26. hxg3 {[%csl Ra5,Rb6][%cal Gc4c5]} Ra8 {A sad necessity. There was no other way to save losing a pawn.} (26... c5 27. dxc5 bxc5 28. Bxa5 Rd6 29. Be1 Rcd8 30. Nb2 $16) 27. f3 Rdb8 {A correct idea. Pushing b6-b5 may simplify the position and ease Black's task.} 28. Rc2 (28. Rb1 Nd7 29. c5 (29. Kf2 Nd6 30. Rdc1 c5 $14) 29... bxc5 30. Nxc5 Nxc5 31. dxc5 Rxb1 32. Rxb1 Ne5 $14) 28... b5 (28... Nd7!? 29. Rb1 e5 $14) 29. Nc5! {The most ambitious continuation. Magnus is not ready to swap the queenside pawns.} ({Things could be much easier for Hikaru after} 29. cxb5 cxb5 30. axb5 Rxb5 31. Ra1 e5 $14) 29... bxc4 ({After} 29... b4 30. Nxe6 Ra7 31. Nc5 Nd7 32. Nb3 $14 {the strong b4 doesn't fully compensate for the loss of a pawn.}) 30. Rxc4 Nd5 (30... e5 31. Kf2 Nd5 32. Rdc1 Rb2+ (32... exd4 33. Rxd4 Rb2+ 34. Rd2 Rb4 35. Re2 Rb6 36. e4 fxe4 37. Rxe4 $14) 33. R4c2 Rxc2+ 34. Rxc2 exd4 35. exd4 $14) 31. Bd2 e5 32. e4 {A double-edged decision. Magnus gets an excellent square for his knight, but now there are not many pawns left on the board.} (32. Ne6!? Rb6 33. dxe5 (33. Rdc1 exd4 34. Rxd4 $14) 33... Nxe5 34. Rc5 Nd7 35. Rc2 $14) 32... fxe4 33. Nxe4 (33. fxe4?! Nf6 $11) 33... Nb6?! {This natural decision is wrong. Nakamura liquidates the last queenside pawn, but his pieces lose harmony.} (33... exd4 $142 34. Rxd4 Rb2 35. Rc1 Nd8 36. Nc5 Ra2 $14) 34. Rxc6 Nd8 ({I would prefer} 34... Nxa4 35. Ra1 Nb6 ({after} 35... Nb2 36. d5 a4 37. d6 a3 38. d7 Nd8 39. Rc3 $16 {White's passed pawn is very strong}) 36. Rxa5 Rxa5 37. Bxa5 Na4! 38. dxe5 (38. d5? Rb5 $11) 38... Nxe5 39. Rc7 Rf8 40. g4 $16 {and Black has a very difficult position, but drawing chances cannot be understimated.}) 35. Rg6! {A good practical decision - now Black has an unpleasant choice, so there are more chances for an additional mistake!} (35. Rd6 Nf7 (35... Nc4 36. Rd5 exd4 37. Rxd4 $16) 36. Re6 Nxa4 37. dxe5 Nb2 38. Ra1 a4 39. Bc3 Nc4 40. f4 $16) 35... Nc4 36. dxe5 Kh7? {A decisive mistake.} ({It was necessary to exchange the strong bishop:} 36... Nxe5 37. Rd6 Nc4 38. Rd7 Nb6 39. Bf4 Nxd7 40. Bxb8 Rxb8 41. Rxd7 $16) 37. Rg4 Nxe5 38. Rh4 Ndf7 (38... Ndc6 39. g4 Kg8 40. Bc3 $18) 39. Bc3 {Combining play on the both sides of the board.} Rb3 (39... Rd8 40. Rb1 Rd3 41. Rb5 Nd7 42. Bxa5 $18) 40. Rd5 Re8 41. Rf4! {Brilliant technique by the World Champion. Now Black just loses a second pawn.} (41. Rxa5 Ng6 42. Rg4 Rxe4 43. Rxe4 Rxc3 $16 {deserves much more efforts.}) 41... Re7 42. Bxa5 $18 {The rest is a matter of accuracy, since Black is unable to create any threats.} Ng6 43. Rff5 Nfe5 44. Rd1 {The rook is going to support the passed a4.} Nc4 (44... Ra3 45. Bb4 $18) 45. Rc1 Nxa5 46. Rxa5 Ra3 47. Rcc5 {Restricting the knight. White is not in a hurry.} Ra2 48. Kh2 Rd7 ({The following illustrative line shows, that Black is helpless:} 48... Rb7 49. Ra8 Rbb2 50. Nf6+! gxf6 51. Rc7+ Ne7 52. Rxe7+ Kg6 53. Rg8+ Kf5 54. g4+ Kf4 55. Re4#) 49. Ra6 Ne7 50. g4 Rb7 ({Or} 50... Nd5 51. Rxd5 Rxd5 52. Nc3 Rdd2 53. Nxa2 Rxa2 54. a5 $18) 51. Rb5 Rc7 52. Nc5 Rc6 (52... Ng6 53. Kg3 $18) 53. Rxc6 Nxc6 54. Rb7 {Reminding Black about the king.} Nd4 55. Kh3 Kg8 56. Rb4 Ne2 57. g5 Ng1+ (57... hxg5 58. Kg4 Ng1 59. g3 Ne2 60. Ne4 $18) 58. Kg3 Ne2+ (58... hxg5 59. Ne4 g4 60. f4 g6 61. Nf6+ Kg7 62. Nxg4 $18) 59. Kg4 hxg5 60. Kxg5 Ng1 61. Rg4 1-0

