1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. f3 O-O 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Nh5 7. Nh3 f5 {An aggressive continuation. Instead of trying to equalise, Black wants to obtain a double-edged position.} 8. e3 (8. Bg5 {is the main alternative - see Khairullin-Adams with annotations by GM A.Mikhalchishin in CBM 136.}) 8... d6 (8... Nc6 9. Bd3 d6 {is hardly good as White pushes c4-c5 at an appropriate moment:} 10. O-O (10. g3?! e5 11. Nf2 Qe8 12. O-O Na5 13. Qe2 b6 14. Rb1 Ba6 15. c5 Bxd3 16. Nxd3 Nf6 17. dxe5 dxe5 18. f4 exf4 19. Qa2+ Kh8 20. exf4 Rd8 $17 {1/2 (39) Abergel,T (2499)-Adams,M (2735) Liverpool 2008}) 10... Qe8 11. a4 e5?! (11... b6!?) 12. c5! dxc5 13. Ba3 b6 14. dxc5 Rf6 15. f4 e4 16. Be2 g6 17. c4 Ng7 18. Bb2 Rf7 19. cxb6 cxb6 20. c5 $16 {1-0 (30) Golizadeh,A (2472)-Bacallao Alonso,Y (2583) Istanbul 2012}) (8... c5!? 9. Bd3 Nc6 10. O-O Nf6 11. d5?! Ne5 12. e4 fxe4 13. fxe4 d6 14. Ng5 h6 15. Nf3 Nxd3 16. Qxd3 Qe8 17. Ra2 exd5 18. exd5 Qe4 $15 {1/2 (33) Abdalla,L (2322)-Leitao,R (2620) Sao Paulo 2011}) 9. Be2 (9. f4 Nf6 10. Bd3 e5 11. O-O e4 12. Be2 Kh8 13. Nf2 Nc6 14. h3 b6 15. g4 Na5 $132 {0-1 (27) Swapnil,S (2336)-Komarov,D (2540) Calicut 2007}) (9. Nf2 {is too passive:} Nc6!? (9... e5 {(a bit too early)} 10. Bd3! {- 9.Bd3} (10. f4 Nf6 11. fxe5 dxe5 12. c5 Nc6 13. Bc4+ Kh8 14. O-O b6 15. Bb5 Bb7 16. Rb1 Ne7 17. Be2 Ne4 $15 {1/2 (54) Volkov,S (2622)-Izoria,Z (2606) Saint Vincent 2005}) (10. Be2 Qe7 11. c5 Nc6 12. cxd6 cxd6 13. a4 {1/2 (13) Mensch,E (2336)-Nikolaidis,I (2525) Nancy 2006})) 10. a4 (10. Bd3 e5 11. O-O Qh4 12. Qe1 Bd7 13. g3 Qe7 14. Qd1 Rae8 15. Ra2 Nf6 16. f4 e4 17. Be2 Qf7 18. a4 Na5 $15 {1/2 (31) Cebalo,M (2505)-Komarov,D (2530) Bratto 2006}) 10... b6 11. Bd3 Na5 12. O-O Ba6 13. Qe2 Qh4 14. Bd2 Rae8 15. e4 e5 16. c5 Bxd3 17. Qxd3 fxe4 18. fxe4 dxc5 19. dxc5 Rd8 20. Qe2 Nb3 21. Qc4+ Kh8 22. Qxb3 Rxd2 {0-1 (57) Kozakov,M (2457)-Fedorchuk,S (2633) France 2009} 23. Ra2 $15) (9. Bd3 Qh4+ (9... e5 10. Nf2 (10. O-O Nc6 11. f4 Qe8 12. fxe5 dxe5 13. Ra2 e4 14. Be2 Nf6 15. a4 Kh8 16. a5 Rg8 17. Nf4 Ne7 18. Qb3 c6 19. Qb4 $14 {0-1 (40) Geller,E-Pogats,J Szczawno Zdroj 1950}) 10... Qe8 11. O-O Nf6 12. Qc2 Qh5 13. c5 Nc6 14. Bb5 Nd8 15. cxd6 cxd6 16. a4 $36 {1-0 (73) Koberl,F-Pogats,J Budapest 1951}) (9... c5!?) 10. Nf2 Nc6 11. Qc2 (11. Ra2 e5 12. g3 Qe7 13. d5 Nd8 14. g4 e4 $132 {0-1 (42) Sagalchik,G-Dautov,R (2515) Minsk 1988}) 11... e5 12. g3 Qe7 13. O-O Bd7 14. Bd2 Kh8 15. Kg2 Rae8 16. Rae1 Qd8 $11 {0-1 (56) Wiersma,E (2419)-Swinkels,R (2459) Netherlands 2009}) 9... c5! $146 {[%mdl 40] A good plan. Black fixes the c4-pawn, therefore eliminating the c4-c5 idea altogether.} (9... Nc6 {deserves more attention here than with d3:} 10. O-O Qe8!? (10... e5 11. c5!?) 11. Nf2 b6 12. g4?! fxg4 13. fxg4 Nf6 14. Qc2 Bb7 15. g5 Nd7 16. e4 e5 $132 {1-0 (34) Posedaru,B (2318)-Soltanici,R (2373) Bucharest 2011}) 10. O-O Nc6 11. g4? {Here, too, this advance is bad.} (11. f4 Nf6 12. Bf3 Na5 {[%csl Rc4]}) (11. d5 Na5 {[%cal Ye6e5]}) (11. e4 fxe4 12. fxe4 Rxf1+ 13. Bxf1 Nf6 14. Nf2 e5 $11) (11. Nf2!? {[%cal Ye3e4] was probably the most flexible keeping both ideas: e3-e4 and f3-f4.}) 11... fxg4 (11... Qh4! {, recommended by GM E.Gleizerov, was very strong:} 12. Nf4 (12. gxh5 Qxh3 13. f4 b6 14. Bf3 Bb7 $17 {[%csl Rc1,Rc4,Rh5]} 15. dxc5 dxc5 16. Qd7 Rf7! 17. Qxe6 Nd8 18. Qe8+ Rf8 $19) (12. Kg2 fxg4 13. fxg4 Rxf1 14. Qxf1 Nf6 15. Qf2 Qh6 $17) 12... Nxf4 13. exf4 Na5 $17 {[%cal Yb7b6]} (13... cxd4 $143 14. cxd4 e5 15. Be3! exd4 16. Bxd4 Nxd4 17. Qxd4 fxg4 18. fxg4 Bxg4 19. f5 h5 20. Rf4 {, and White should survive})) {However, after the simple text move Black keeps an advantage, too.} 12. fxg4 Nf6 13. Nf2 h6 14. e4 {This move weakens White's position but it is very difficult for him to find a good plan anyway.} e5 15. d5 Ne7 (15... Na5!? {pressurising the c4 pawn.}) 16. g5 hxg5 17. Bxg5 Qe8 18. Qd3 Qg6 19. Qg3 Bd7 20. Kh1 Rf7 (20... Nxe4? 21. Nxe4 Qxe4+ 22. Bf3 {[%cal Rg5e7]} Rxf3 $8) 21. Qh4 Raf8 22. Rae1 Qh7 23. Qxh7+ Nxh7 $17 {In this ending Black's knights are stronger than White's bishops.} 24. Be3 Ng6 25. Nd3 Rxf1+ 26. Rxf1 Rxf1+ (26... Nf6!? 27. Nf2 Nf4 {is quite good but the minor piece ending is also difficult for White.}) 27. Bxf1 {[%mdl 4096]} Nf6 28. Nf2 Nf4 29. h4 (29. Bxf4 exf4 30. e5 dxe5 31. Nd3 Ng4! 32. h3 (32. Nxc5 Bf5) 32... e4! 33. Nxf4 (33. Nxc5 Nf6 {[%csl Ge4,Gf4]}) 33... Ne3 {[%cal Re3d1]} 34. Be2 g5 $17) 29... Ng6 (29... Ng4 30. Nxg4 Bxg4 {[%cal Rg4f3,Yg4d1] was very strong, probably enough for a win.}) 30. Bg5 Nh7 31. Bd8 Kf7 32. Kh2 Nf6 (32... Ke8 33. Bg5) 33. Kg3 Nf4 34. Kf3 Ba4 35. Nh3 Bd1+ 36. Ke3 Ng6 (36... Ng4+ 37. Kd2 Nh2 {is probably insufficient for a victory due to} 38. Nxf4 Nxf1+ 39. Kxd1 exf4 40. Ke2 Ne3 41. Bg5 Nxc4 42. Bxf4 b5 (42... g6 43. Kd3 b5 44. a4) 43. h5 {[%csl Rg7]}) 37. Ng5+ Ke8 38. Ne6 Nxh4 39. Bc7 Kd7 40. Bb8 {White has created some counterplay.} Bg4 (40... a6!? 41. Nxg7 Bc2!? 42. Bh3+ Ke7 43. Nf5+ (43. Bc8 b5 44. Bxa6 bxc4 45. Bxc4 Bxe4 46. Kf2! Ng4+ 47. Kg3 Ne3 $17) 43... Nxf5+ 44. Bxf5 Bb3 {keeping winning chances}) 41. Nxg7 a6 42. Bd3 (42. a4 a5 43. Ne6 Ne8 {[%csl Ra4]}) 42... Ng2+ 43. Kd2 Ke7 (43... b5!? 44. cxb5 axb5 45. Bc2 (45. Bxb5+? Kc8 46. Bxd6 Nxe4+) (45. Ne6!? c4 46. Bc2) 45... c4 46. Ne6 Bxe6 47. dxe6+ Kxe6 48. Ba7 Nf4 $17) 44. a4 Kf7 45. Bxd6 (45. Nf5 Bxf5 46. exf5 Ke7 {is very unpleasant for White but the piece sacrifice is hardly sufficient either.}) 45... Kxg7 46. Bxe5 Nh4? ({Black could have stopped White's pawns by means of} 46... Bf3! {[%cal Rg7f7]} 47. d6 Bg4! {(recommended by E.Gleizerov)} (47... Kf7? 48. Bxf6 Kxf6 49. e5+ Ke6 50. Bf5+ Kf7 51. Bg6+! $11) {e.g.} 48. Bg3 Nh5 49. Bf2 Nhf4 50. Bxc5 Nxd3 51. Kxd3 Kf6 {, and he should win in a long term.}) 47. Bg3 Ng6 (47... Nf3+ 48. Ke3 Nd7 49. Be2 Nfe5 50. Bxe5+ Nxe5 51. Kf4! Bxe2 52. Kxe5 Bxc4 53. a5 {(E.Gleizerov), and White is not worse.}) 48. e5 {White's connected passed pawns guarantee him a draw.} Nd7 (48... Nh5 49. Bh2 Nh4 50. Ke3 Nf3 51. Bf4) 49. e6 Nde5 50. Bxg6 Nxg6 51. Bd6 Kf6 52. Bxc5 Ke5 53. Ke3 Bd1 54. Bb6 Bxa4 55. Bc7+ Kf6 56. Kd4 Ke7 57. c5 Nh4 58. c4 Nf3+ 59. Kc3 Ng5 60. Kb4 Bd1 61. Bg3 Nxe6 62. dxe6 Bf3 63. Bh4+ 1/2-1/2

