{[%evp 0,91,26,26,29,29,29,-16,0,-3,37,37,61,9,-2,46,48,48,29,41,41,53,54,23,36,20,20,36,36,13,13,-3,2,5,2,1,11,15,0,0,0,7,4,7,-14,-14,0,-24,15,13,2,0,10,20,7,7,55,29,38,48,64,66,89,95,142,130,164,171,175,136,195,190,196,186,195,196,196,193,207,196,202,218,232,245,242,260,275,277,268,292,286,305,318,349]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 d5 3. c4 e6 4. Nc3 c5 5. cxd5 cxd4 6. Qxd4 exd5 7. e4 Nc6 8. Bb5 dxe4 9. Qxd8+ Kxd8 10. Ng5 Be6 11. Bf4!? {An almost new idea in a position where White always went 11.0-0, 11.xe6+ or 11.xc6. However, I do not think it is a threat to the whole line.} Bb4 {The natural response.} 12. O-O-O+ $146 (12. Nxe6+ fxe6 13. Bxc6 bxc6 14. Ke2 Ke7 15. Rac1 Bxc3 16. Rxc3 Nd5 17. Bg5+ Kd6 18. Rc4 Rhb8 19. Bc1 Rb4 $11 {½-½ (45) Tomashevsky,E (2695)-Bukavshin,I (2607) Vladivostok 2014}) 12... Ke7 13. a3 Bxc3 14. Bd6+ Ke8 15. bxc3 h6 16. Nxe6 fxe6 17. Bxc6+ bxc6 18. Rd4 Kf7 {All this seemed pretty logical, and Black's position should be fine - perhaps just a little more difficult to play.} 19. Rhd1 Rhd8 20. c4 a5 21. Bc5 Rdb8 (21... Rxd4 22. Rxd4 e3!? (22... Rb8!?) 23. fxe3 e5 24. Rd6 Rc8 {looks equal to me.}) 22. Kc2 Rb7 23. Rb1 Rab8?! (23... Rxb1 24. Kxb1 e3!? 25. fxe3 e5 26. Rd6 Rc8 27. Bb6 Ke7 $11) 24. Rxb7+! Rxb7 25. Rd6! {Black has tricked himself: now he's losing one of his queenside pawns.} Nd7 (25... Rc7 26. Bb6 $14) 26. Rxc6 Ne5 27. Ra6 Nxc4 28. Kc3 Nb2 29. Rxa5 Nd3 30. Bd4 Rc7+? ({It was the last moment for Black to play} 30... e5! {- though after} 31. Bxe5 {both ...xe5 and ...xf2 look pretty bad for Black.}) 31. Kd2 Rc4 (31... e5 32. Ra7! $18) 32. Ra7+ Kg6 33. Rxg7+ Kf5 34. Be3 Nc5 35. g4+ Ke5 36. Ra7 Nb3+ 37. Kd1 Rc3 38. Bxh6 Rd3+ 39. Kc2 Nd4+ 40. Kb2 Kd5 41. Be3 e5 42. h4 Rb3+ 43. Ka2 Rc3 44. h5 Rc2+ 45. Kb1 Re2 46. Rc7 {That's slightly too many pawns for White, and as ...e1+ will be answered by c1, White will just have to push them.} 1-0

