{[%evp 0,58,26,23,92,84,111,107,93,76,122,55,94,66,110,88,106,104,95,74,74,39,36,22,38,19,-11,-19,-2,-2,0,-52,-34,-30,-21,-48,-14,-37,-34,-85,-38,-30,-53,-136,-115,-90,-43,-52,-1,-82,43,-96,2,-14,-1,-47,0,0,-24,-456,-474]} 1. Nf3 c5 2. e4 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e6 7. f3 b5 8. a3!? {A new trend installed by Magnus Carlsen himself in similar positions.} Bb7 9. g4 (9. Qd2 {was Carlsen's move against Svidler just a couple of weeks earlier.}) 9... h6 10. Qd2 Nbd7 11. O-O-O Rc8 12. Kb1!? (12. h4 {is the other critical move.}) 12... d5 13. exd5 Nxd5 14. Nxd5 Bxd5 {We reached a critical position.} 15. Rg1!? (15. a4 {is a strange move, although it was played by Leinier Dominguez:} Bc5 16. axb5 O-O 17. Be2 axb5 18. Nxb5 Qb6 19. Bxc5 Rxc5 $44 {½-½ (44) Dominguez Perez,L (2760)-Kulaots,K (2560) Dortmund 2019}) (15. Be2!? Bc5 16. Rhg1 O-O (16... Qc7!?) 17. h4 Ne5 18. f4! Nc4 19. Bxc4 bxc4 20. g5 h5 21. g6 Qxh4 {After this move the game went nuts.} (21... Qf6!?) 22. f5 Qh3? (22... Qe4!?) 23. gxf7+ Rxf7 24. fxe6 Rb7 25. Rg5 Bxd4 (25... Be4 26. Rdg1 $18) 26. Qxd4 $18 {0-1 (45) Ponomariov,R (2712)-Wojtaszek,R (2730) Khanty-Mansiysk 2017}) 15... Bc5 16. f4 (16. g5 hxg5 17. Rxg5 Kf8 {looks okay for Black: no reason to castle now that the h-file is open!}) 16... Nf6 17. Bd3?! (17. Bg2! {was much stronger:} Qc7 18. Rge1 {and g5 is coming soon, making the g2/d5 tension unpleasant to meet for Black. White may also play f5 soon.} (18. g5?! hxg5 19. fxg5 Ne4 $15)) 17... Qc7 18. Qe1 {This move seems to prevent ...e4, but Magnus played it anyway, and Ding didn't seem to be willing to punish it!} (18. Rge1?! Nxg4 $15) 18... Ne4? (18... Be7!?) 19. h4? (19. Nxe6! fxe6 20. Bxe4 Bxe4 21. Bxc5 Bxc2+ 22. Kxc2 Qxc5+ 23. Kb1 Rc6 24. Qe4 O-O 25. h3 $14) 19... Qb7?! {These positions are strategically excellent for Black, but as long as queens remain alive; where to put the black king is a bit of a problem, and it is difficult to play accurate moves.} (19... Qb6!? 20. b4 Be7 21. Nb3 Qc6 22. Bxe4 Bxe4 23. Bc5 Bd5 $13) 20. c3? {Inviting Black to a party Carlsen isn't going to decline!} (20. Nb3! $14) 20... Bxa3! 21. f5 (21. bxa3 Nxc3+ 22. Ka1 Nxd1 23. Qxd1 Be4 $17) 21... b4 22. c4 Nc3+! 23. Ka1 Bxc4 24. fxe6 O-O! 25. Bxc4 Rxc4 {Funy to see all these black pieces hang, but Black is just better!} 26. Rd2 (26. bxa3 Nxd1 27. Qxd1 Qe4 $17) (26. bxc3? bxc3 $19) 26... Bxb2+! 27. Kxb2 (27. Rxb2 Qb6 28. Nb3 Qb5 29. Nc1 Qa4+ 30. Na2 b3 $19) 27... b3?! (27... Na4+! 28. Ka1 (28. Kb1 Qe4+ 29. Ka1 fxe6 30. Bf2 Nc5 $19) 28... b3 $19 {was the correct move order, according to the engine.}) 28. exf7+ Qxf7 29. Nxb3? ({After} 29. Rf2 $8 Na4+ 30. Kb1 Qg6+ 31. Nf5 Kh7 32. Qf1 $8 {White would have some chances left.} Nc3+!? 33. Kb2 Ra4! 34. Rf4! (34. Kxc3? Qc6+ $19) 34... Qc6 35. Rxa4 Nxa4+ 36. Ka3 Nb6 37. Bxb6 Qxb6 38. Qe1 Qc5+ 39. Qb4 Rxf5! 40. Qxc5 Rxc5 41. Kxb3 $17) 29... Rb4 0-1

