1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f3 h6 8. Be3 b5 9. a3!? Nbd7 10. Qd2 Bb7 11. O-O-O h5 12. Kb1 Be7 13. Rg1! $146 {A huge improvement on 13.Qe1, previously played by Carlsen.} Rc8 14. Be2 Nb6 (14... Ne5!? {is definitely worth consideration.}) 15. Qe1! {Now that move makes sense - as White already threatens to push e5.} Nfd7 (15... Qc7 {definitely makes sense too. A logical follow-up would be} 16. Qg3 Nc4 17. Bxc4 bxc4 {while} 18. Qxg7? {would allow} Rg8 19. Qh6 e5! {with the idea} 20. Nf5 Rg6 21. Qh8+ Kd7 $19) 16. g4 {Logical, once the knight is gone from f6 - so White can recapture on g4 with his rook.} hxg4 17. Rxg4 Bf8?! {It is rarely very good to set your pieces ready for the next game! Several moves lead to unclear positions here.} (17... g6!? 18. Rxg6 {(the move MVL admitted to have overlooked beforehand)} Rxc3! (18... e5 19. Qg1! exd4 20. Bxd4 Rf8 21. e5 {is also a mess, but could go wrong for Black}) (18... fxg6?? 19. Nxe6 $18) 19. Nxe6 (19. Qxc3 Na4! 20. Qd2 fxg6 21. Nxe6 Qc8 $13) 19... Qc8 $8 {And now the engine suggests a fun drawing line:} 20. Ng7+ Kf8 21. Rh6 Rxh6 22. Bxh6 Rxc2 23. Nf5+ Ke8 24. Nxe7 Kxe7 25. Qh4+ f6 26. Bf4 Rxb2+ 27. Kxb2 Na4+ 28. Kb1 Nc3+ 29. Kb2 Na4+ $11) (17... Rh7!? {also looks playable. Any time the queen leaves its protection of the c3-knight, Black will sac on c3 and play ...Na4.}) (17... Kf8!? {also looks reasonable, if} 18. Qg3 {then} Nf6! 19. Rxg7 Nh5 20. Bh6 $8 Nxg3 21. Rh7+ Kg8 22. Rg7+ Kf8 $11 {is another fun draw!}) ({Even} 17... g5 {is suggested by the engine, but it looks somewhat more suspicious.}) 18. Rg2 {Not the most percussive move, although Black's position remains unpleasant to play.} (18. Bg5! Qc7 19. Rh4 {exchanging Black's very important h8-rook (that had both an attacking and defensive role) was very disturbing.}) 18... Ne5 19. f4 (19. Bc1!? {was an interesting way to wait for Black to put a piece on the c-file, so ...Rxc3 becomes impossible.} Qc7 (19... Nec4? 20. b3 $16) 20. Qg1! Nbc4 21. b3 Nxa3+ 22. Bxa3 Qxc3 23. Rxg7! $40) 19... Nec4 20. Bc1 Qc7 21. Bxc4 (21. Rd3!?) 21... Nxc4 22. Rd3 g6!? {Black needs to activate his f8-bishop!} 23. b3 Bg7 {It is important to keep the tension. Retracting the c4-knight would allow Bb2, after which White would have no attacking prospects.} (23... Nb6 24. Bb2) 24. Qd1 (24. bxc4 bxc4 {(...Qb6+)} 25. Nxe6 $8 {(or 24.Nxe6 first)} fxe6 26. Rdg3 {would be another critical line.}) 24... Qa5?! {Tempting but wrong.} ({Black had to play the more quiet} 24... Qb6 {to "defend" the c4-knight.}) 25. f5? {Bluff? But it did work!} (25. Nxe6! {was powerful:} fxe6 (25... Bxc3 26. bxc4 bxc4 27. Rxc3 Qxc3 28. Qxd6! $18) 26. bxc4! Bxc3 (26... bxc4 27. Rxd6 Qxc3 28. Rxe6+ Kf7 29. Qd7+ Kg8 30. Re5! $18) 27. Rxd6 {and Black may survive the attack, but will remain worse, e.g.} Rc6 28. Rxg6 Rxd6 29. Qxd6 Bc8 30. Qb8 Qd8 31. f5 bxc4 32. fxe6 Bxe6 33. Qxd8+ Kxd8 34. Rxe6 Rxh2 35. Bb2! $16) 25... Ne5? ({Black should have taken the offered pawn with} 25... exf5! {- after} 26. Re2 f4!? {it would be difficult for White to continue his attack.}) ({Of course not} 25... Nxa3+? 26. Bxa3 Qxa3 27. fxg6 $40) 26. b4 (26. fxg6 {was strong as well, if} Rxc3 27. gxf7+ Kxf7 {White has several wins, for example} 28. Rxg7+! Kxg7 29. Nxe6+ Kf7 30. Rxd6 $18) 26... Qb6 27. fxg6! {After that move, Black's position collapses. Maxime's best chance then was the clock: with around 8 minutes vs. 3.} Nxg6 (27... Nxd3 28. gxf7+ Kxf7 29. Rxg7+! Kxg7 30. Qg4+ {with a devastating attack.}) 28. Be3 {The easy option.} (28. Nxe6 {was the hard one:} Bxc3 29. Rxd6 Rc6 (29... Bc6 30. Rxg6! fxg6 31. Ng5!? $18) 30. Rd8+ Ke7 31. Bg5+ {followed with Qd7#.}) 28... Bh6 29. Ndxb5 {Logical, but not the strongest.} (29. Bg1 {was the simple option, keeping bishops alive.}) (29. Nxe6! {was again the hard one:} Bxe3 30. Rxd6 Rc6 (30... Bc6 31. Nd5 $18) 31. Rd8+ Ke7 32. Qd7+ Kf6 33. e5+! {and mate to follow.}) 29... Bxe3 30. Nxd6+ Ke7 31. Nxb7? {Slowly Magnus is giving Black practical chances.} ({The strongest move was pretty hard to find:} 31. Nxf7! Kxf7 32. Rd7+ Kf6 33. Qf3+ Bf4 34. Ne2! e5 35. Nxf4 exf4 (35... Nxf4 36. Qg4 $18) 36. Rxb7! Qxb7 37. e5+ {and wins.}) 31... Ne5!? {A good practical try.} ({After} 31... Qxb7 32. Rxe3 {Black wouldn't be without chances, but objectively he shouldn't be able to prove compensation for the two pawns.}) 32. Rxe3 Nc4! {That was the idea behind 31...Ne5.} ({Of course not} 32... Qxe3? 33. Qd6+ Kf6? 34. Nd5#) 33. Rd3?! (33. Rf3! Qxb7 34. Qd4 {would probably have finished off the game quickly.}) 33... Qxb7 34. Na4 {White had other ways to defend the a3-pawn, like 34.Qc1, but that one isn't bad.} Rhd8 35. Nc5?? {Finally a serious mistake by White! Magnus blunders his two extra pawns in just one move.} (35. Rxd8 Rxd8 36. Qe1 Nxa3+ 37. Ka2 {and White is just winning.}) 35... Rxd3 36. Nxd3 (36. Qxd3 Rxc5 {was surely missed by the Norwegian.}) 36... Nxa3+ 37. Kc1? ({Better was} 37. Ka2 Nb5 38. Qg4 Qxe4 {which is around equal.}) 37... Qxe4 {Suddenly, material is equal and White's king is in trouble!} 38. Rf2 Qe3+? (38... Qd4! {would have won the game: White is defenseless against 39...Qa1+.}) 39. Kb2 Nc4+ 40. Ka2 Nd6 41. Qh5 {The white king could escape and White is more or less fine again.} Nf5 42. Re2 Qg1 43. Qf3 ({Of course not} 43. Qxf5?? Rxc2+! {deflecting the white rook.}) 43... Qb6 44. Ne5 Qb5 {Black's position remains much easier to play.} 45. c4? (45. Qe4! {was necessary.}) 45... Qa4+ 46. Qa3 Qxa3+ 47. Kxa3 f6 {The c4-pawn falls and Black is technically winning. What a turnover in game 9 of the match!} 48. Nd3 Rxc4 49. Nc5 Nd4 50. Re4 a5!? 51. Ka2 Rxb4 (51... axb4? 52. Nxe6! {was the last trick to avoid.}) 52. Nd3 Nb5! {A cute way to exchange rooks.} 53. Rxb4 (53. Nxb4 Nc3+ $19) 53... axb4 54. Nxb4 f5 55. Kb2 Kf6 56. Kc2 Nd4+ 57. Kd3 Nf3 58. h3 e5 59. Ke3 Nh4 60. Nd5+ Kg5 61. Ne7 Ng6 62. Nc8 Nf4 {Black's technical has been irreprochable. Now the h3-pawn falls as well.} 63. h4+ Kxh4 64. Ne7 Kg5 65. Kf3 Ng6 66. Nd5 e4+ 67. Ke3 Ne5 68. Kd4 Ng4 69. Nc7 Nh2 70. Ke3 Nf1+ 71. Kf2 Nd2 72. Ke3 Nc4+ 73. Kd4 Nd6 74. Ne6+ Kg4 75. Ke3 Kg3 76. Nd4 (76. Nf4? Nc4+ $19) 76... f4+ 77. Ke2 Kg2 78. Ne6 f3+ 79. Ke3 Kg3 80. Nf4 Nc4+! 81. Kxe4 f2 82. Ne2+ Kg4 0-1

