1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f3!? h6 8. Be3 {An interesting idea, similar to another idea that Grischuk had used several times against MVL: 6.Be3 Ng4 7.Bg5 h6 8.Bc1!?. Basically, the idea is that in opposite castle attacks, Black's kingside might be more fragile if the pawn stands on h6 instead of h7 (notably after White plays g4-g5).} b5 9. a3!? {A useful move, as Black is going to aim for ...b4 sooner or later.} Nbd7 10. Qd2 Bb7 11. O-O-O (11. g4?! d5! $15) 11... h5!? {A very logical move. Maxime judged that his "extra tempo" ...h6 wasn't bringing anything positive and went ...h5 just as if his pawn came from h7.} (11... Rc8!? {or 11...Be7!? were two very natural options.}) ({Whereas} 11... d5?! 12. exd5 Nxd5 13. Nxd5 Bxd5 14. Nf5 {looks very dangerous for Black.} (14. Kb1!?)) 12. Kb1 Be7 13. Qe1?! {A dubious one, as the game will prove.} (13. Rg1! $146 {was Magnus' improvement in their next encounter in this line... just two days later! See annotations of Game 9 (rapid) of the match.}) 13... Rc8 14. Rg1 Qc7 15. g3?! (15. Be2 d5 $132) 15... Ne5 (15... d5!? {could lead to crazy lines:} 16. Bf4 (16. exd5 Nxd5 17. Nxd5 Bxd5 18. Nf5? {fails to} Qxc2+ $19) 16... e5 17. exd5 Nxd5 18. Nxd5 Bxd5 19. Rg2!? (19. Bh3 O-O! 20. Bxd7 Qxd7 21. Bxe5 Bc5 $44) 19... exf4 20. Nf5 Nf6 21. Re2 Be6 22. Nxg7+ Kf8 23. Nxe6+ fxe6 24. Bh3 Rd8 {and Black should be at least no worse.}) 16. Rg2 {A fun manoeuvre, which I had never seen in this exact setup. The rook on the second rank will proove useful. On the other hand, this is far too slow in such a complex position.} O-O (16... Nfd7!?) 17. h3 d5 18. f4 Nc4 19. e5 (19. Bxc4?! dxc4 $17) 19... Nxe3! {MVL correctly judged that he should swap his knight for the dark-squared bishop.} 20. Qxe3 Ne4 21. Nxe4 dxe4 22. Re2 ({A direct attack would just fail:} 22. h4? Rfd8 23. g4 Bc5 24. c3 (24. gxh5 Bxd4 25. Rxd4 Rxd4 26. Qxd4 e3! 27. Re2 Rd8 $19) 24... Bxd4 25. cxd4 hxg4 $17) 22... Bd5!? (22... Rfd8 23. Bg2 b4! 24. axb4 Bxb4 {would have given Black great compensation as well.}) 23. Bg2 Qc4 24. Nb3 Bc5! 25. Nxc5 Qa2+ 26. Kc1 Rfd8! {What a tactical continuation! White should be very careful now.} 27. c4! (27. Bxe4 {leads to a draw:} Qa1+ 28. Kd2 Bxe4+ 29. Nd3 Qxb2 30. Qxe4 Rc4 31. Qf3 Qc3+ (31... Rxd3+!? 32. Qxd3 Rd4 $11) 32. Ke3 Qd4+ $11) ({But} 27. c3?? {is bad due to} Qa1+ 28. Kc2 Bb3+!! 29. Kxb3 Qxd1+ 30. Ka2 Rxc5 $17) 27... bxc4!! (27... Bxc4 {fails to} 28. Rxd8+ Rxd8 29. Rd2 $18) 28. Red2 (28. Na4!? {leads to incredibly fun lines:} Bc6! 29. Rxd8+ Rxd8 30. Nc3 Qa1+ 31. Nb1 (31. Kc2?? Ba4+ 32. Nxa4 Qd1+ 33. Kc3 Qb3#) 31... Rd3 32. Qg1 Ba4 33. Bxe4 Bb3 34. Bxd3 cxd3 35. Re1 (35. Rd2? Ba2 $17) 35... Ba2 36. Qd4 Qxb1+ 37. Kd2 Qc2+ 38. Ke3 Bd5 39. Qxd3 Qg2 {and Black should be able to make a draw.}) (28. b4 {is a cute way to force a draw:} Qa1+ 29. Kc2 Qa2+ $11) 28... Qa1+!? (28... Rxc5 29. Qxc5 Rb8 {was another good option:} 30. Bxe4 (30. Rc2 e3! 31. Qxe3 Bxg2 $17) 30... Bxe4 31. Rd8+ Rxd8 32. Rxd8+ Kh7 33. Qb4 Qb1+ 34. Kd2 Qc2+ 35. Ke1 $11) 29. Kc2 Qa2 30. Kc1 (30. Na4?? c3! $19) 30... Qa1+ 31. Kc2 Qa2 32. Kc1 {An unusual move repetition!} 1/2-1/2

