1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+ Nd7 4. d4 cxd4 5. Qxd4 a6 6. Bxd7+ Bxd7 7. c4 e5 8. Qd3 b5!? {A very logical move. Black attacks White's  centre in order to lessen the pressure against d5.} 9. Nc3 (9. Na3 {has also been played, but} Qb6 10. O-O h6 11. b3 Nf6 12. Re1 Be7 13. Nd2 O-O 14. Nf1 Bc6 15. Ng3 Bd7 16. Be3 Qb7 17. Nc2 bxc4 18. bxc4 Rfc8 $132 {and Black looks nicely placed, Tiviakov-Babula, Dresden 2007.}) 9... bxc4 10. Qxc4 Be6 11. Qd3 (11. Qc6+ {leads only to repetition of moves after} Bd7 12. Qc4 Be6 $11) (11. Nd5 Qa5+ 12. Qc3 (12. Bd2 Bxd5 13. exd5 Qb5 $13) 12... Qxc3+ 13. Nxc3 h6 14. b3 Rc8 15. Bb2 Nf6 16. O-O Be7 17. Rfd1 O-O 18. Nd2 Rc6 $13 {D.Howell-Shanava, Istanbul ol 2012}) 11... h6 {This important move avoids  or g5.} ({In the event of} 11... Nf6?! 12. Bg5 Be7 13. Bxf6 Bxf6 14. O-O {followed by d5 with a slight advantage for White.}) 12. O-O Nf6 13. Rd1 $146 {The first novelty in the game. The idea can be seen in the next moves.} (13. Be3 Be7 14. Nd2 d5 15. exd5 Nxd5 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Qxd5 Bxd5 18. Rfc1 Kd7 19. Nc4 Bxc4 20. Rxc4 Rhc8 $11 {Yu Ruiyuan-Zhou Jianchao, Tianjin 2013. Black equalised.}) 13... Be7 14. Ne1!? {A very typical plan in these  structures. The white  goes to c2-e3 and d5.} O-O 15. Nc2 Qb6 16. Ne3 Rfc8 17. b3 {A prophylactic move, Carlsen prepares for d2.} ({The immediate} 17. Ned5 {is probably too early, as} Nxd5 18. Nxd5 Bxd5 19. Qxd5 Rab8 (19... Qb4 20. b3) 20. b3 Rc2 21. Be3 Qb4 {and it is not so easy to improve the position with White.}) 17... a5 18. Bd2 Qa6 {Anand decides to exchange the .} 19. Be1 Nd7 20. f3 {Carlsen protects the e4 , which wasn't hanging at the moment, but after the exchange of , than cd5 c5 and the  is protected.} Rc6 21. Qxa6 Rcxa6 22. Ned5! (22. Ncd5 {was also possible} Bd8 (22... Bg5 23. Nc4 $14) 23. Rac1 $14 {and White is slightly better.}) 22... Bd8 23. Nb5 {This is the point of why Carlsen put the e3  on d5.} Rc8 (23... Rc6 {was more precise} 24. Rac1 (24. Bf2 Rb8) 24... Rxc1 25. Rxc1 Bxd5 26. exd5 Nf6 27. Nxd6 Nxd5 {and still Black can hope for equalisation.}) 24. Bf2 (24. Rac1 {was an interesting alternative.} Rxc1 25. Rxc1 Bxd5 26. exd5 Bb6+ 27. Bf2 $14) 24... Kh7 {A prophylactic move, which avoids e7 checks.} 25. Kf1 $14 {Another logical move by Carlsen. The  is coming towards the centre.} Rcc6?! (25... g6 {was another option preparing for g7.} 26. Rac1 Rac6 27. Be3 $14) 26. Rac1 Bg5 27. Rc3 (27. Rxc6! {was stronger} Rxc6 28. Ndc7 Rc2 29. Nxe6 fxe6 30. Rxd6 $16 {and White is better.}) 27... Bxd5 28. Rxd5 Rxc3 29. Nxc3 Rc6 30. Be1 Nc5 31. Nb5 (31. h4!? Bd8 (31... Be7 32. Ke2 $14) 32. Nb5 Nb7 33. Ke2 $14) 31... Nb7 32. h4 Be3 33. Ke2 Bc5 34. h5 Bb4 35. Bd2?! ({In the event of} 35. Bxb4 axb4 36. a3 bxa3 37. Nxa3 g6 38. hxg6+ Kxg6 39. Nc4 {is still also slightly better for White.}) 35... g6 36. a3 Bxd2 37. hxg6+ Kxg6 38. Kxd2 h5 39. g3 f6 40. Na7 Rc7 41. Nb5 Rc6 42. Ke2 Kf7 $11 {Anand has made some precise moves, so he has levelled the position.} 43. b4 axb4 44. axb4 Ke6 45. Rd3 Rc4 46. Rb3 d5 47. Kd3 Rc6 48. exd5+ Kxd5 49. Rc3 f5 50. Nc7+ Kd6 51. Ne8+ Kd5 52. Rxc6 Kxc6 53. Ng7 Nd6 54. Nxh5 e4+ 55. fxe4 Nxe4 56. Kd4 Kb5 57. g4 fxg4 58. Kxe4 g3 59. Nxg3 Kxb4 1/2-1/2

