{[%evp 0,118,43,27,27,15,15,15,10,20,38,31,30,8,11,17,30,15,14,-17,-17,-21,-14,-14,-5,-5,-4,-7,-4,-9,34,7,19,19,38,42,7,7,55,63,59,44,58,52,65,51,51,55,44,49,49,16,50,30,51,51,54,68,57,8,59,65,69,-101,-101,-93,-118,-118,-118,-144,-144,-140,-137,-150,-137,-137,-148,-148,-151,-151,-159,-268,-287,-287,-270,-270,-270,-270,-200,-234,-277,-256,-258,-253,-253,-284,-324,-324,-205,-205,-175,-175,-227,-181,-173,-279,-73,-73,0,0,0,-26,-23,-103,-77,-132,-161,-198,-160,-29989,-29990]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 c5 5. cxd5 exd5!? {Generally known to be a mistaken move order, but as the world champion played it 4 times recently in rapid and blitz games, he might not fully agree. The "refutation" is supposed to be 6.Bg5, but against Vidit and Wojtaszek Magnus scored 1½/2 even if both times he was somewhat worse after the opening. Aronian, like his compratiot and second Melkumyan, chooses just to transpose to the standard lines of the Tarrasch defence.} 6. g3 Nc6 7. Bg2 cxd4!? {Kasparov, and thus everybody else following him, used to play 7...Be7 back in the days, but recently Dubov has brought attention to this rare idea, employing it in numerous games.} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 9. Nxc6 (9. Nb3 {is also a relevant move, as after} Bb6 {White can win a pawn in numerous way, but with Black always getting good practical compensation. Even so, Magnus played like this as White excactly against Dubov on the final day of the blitz, managing to tame the black initiative and eventually win the game.}) 9... bxc6 10. O-O O-O 11. Qc2 Bb6 12. Na4 Bd7 13. b3!? {Melkumyan took on b6 followed by b4, but Levon has a different concept in mind, just playing very logical common-sense moves.} Re8 14. Bb2 {It would be easy to describe this position as just pleasant for White. Finachettoed bishops, the knight well placed dominating Black's bishop and controlling the weak square on c5. Black has "hanging" pawns, and even should he get ready for e.g. ...Rc8 and ...c5, there will be Bxf6 followed by Bxd5. Pleasant position for White, with no real counterplay for Black? Well, these days we know better:} h5! 15. h4!? {Aronian take measures to prevent the black pawn from reaching h3.} (15. Rfd1 h4 16. Nxb6 axb6 17. Rd4 h3 18. Bf3 {was another interesting option, with the computer suggesting} c5!? 19. Rxd5 Nxd5 20. Bxd5 Qg5 21. Bxa8 Rxa8 {as the optimal line of play, with Black having just about sufficient compensation for the pawn.}) 15... Ne4 16. e3 Rc8 17. Nxb6 axb6 18. Qd1! {Taking White's perspective, one might say that first he fixed Black's weak pawn, and now he attacks it! As 18... g6 is out of the question due to the a1-h8 diagonal, Black has to find a tactical way to justify his play.} Bf5!? ({The computers suggest} 18... Bg4 19. f3 Nxg3 20. fxg4 Qxh4 21. Qf3 f5 {which indeed leads to an interesting positions, but Magnus has another idea in mind.}) 19. Qxh5 Qd7 {Ulf Andersson once jokingly stated that if you have an isolated pawn and blunder it, the good news is that you have no more weaknesses! While if you blunder one of your hanging pawns, then things are indeed really bad..... Maybe there is a similar logic to attacking with your flank pawn, in the sense that if you lose it, no big deal, it was after all just a flank pawn! White now indeed is a full pawn up, but the knnight on e4 is dominating, with h4 having weakened the g3 pawn, meaning f3 never really is possible.} 20. Qd1 (20. Bxe4 {would lead to a very interesting position after} dxe4!? 21. Qg5 f6! 22. Bxf6 Bg4! {White now is two full pawns up, but the white squares around his own king are permanently damaged, and the bishop on g4 also making sure d1 is covered, meaning White can not really access the d-line. The computer calls it slightly better for White, but from a practical point of view, it could be easier to play for Black.}) 20... c5 21. Re1 Rcd8?! {Too much common sense!} (21... Rc6! {was stronger, caveman style, swinging the rook to g6 or even h6 attacking the weakened structure. With the computer saying Black is already better!}) 22. Rc1 Bh3 23. Qf3 f6 24. Bxh3 Qxh3 25. Rcd1 b5!? {Just to sit and wait was also fine, but Magnus, as always, has bigger ambitions than just splitting the point.} 26. Qg2 Qe6 27. f3 Nd6 28. g4 d4 29. Qd2 ({At first the engines says that Aronian missed} 29. Qc2! {with a major edge, but then moderates its statement to Black having quite decent compensation after} Qd5 30. Kg2 f5! 31. g5 f4! 32. e4 Qe5 {where indeed his prospects again seem no worse.}) 29... Qd5 30. exd4 c4 31. Ba3? {A peculiar blunder, but most likely Aronian thought the f3 pawn untouchable as Bxd6 would then win a piece, having confused that Black can just exchange on e1 first, and then retake on d6.} (31. Kf2! {was the better move, leading to equality and a likely draw.}) 31... Qxf3 32. Qg2 Rxe1+ 33. Rxe1 Qc3! 34. Rd1 b4 35. Bb2 {The obvious move, but} (35. Bc1! {covering e3 would put up a better fight.}) 35... Qe3+ 36. Qf2 Qh3 37. Qg2 Qe3+ 38. Qf2 Qe4 39. Qg2 c3 40. Bc1 c2! 41. Rf1 Qxd4+ 42. Kh2 Qe5+ 43. Kg1 Qc5+ 44. Kh2 Nf7 {Materially technically its equal, but the c2-pawn is such a big trump that Black is winning.} 45. g5 Qe5+ 46. Kh1 Rd1 47. g6 Nd8 48. Qf3 Rxf1+ 49. Qxf1 Qe4+ 50. Qg2 Qxh4+ 51. Kg1 Qd4+ 52. Kh2 Qd1? 53. Qe4! {Following the game online, the spectators suddenly got a surprise here, as from Magnus for a while being around +5, the engine-evaluation dropped to 0.00!White threatens mate on e8, and retreating the queen, would mean losing the c2-pawn so Magnus only chance is taking his king for a walk.} Kf8 54. Qxb4+ Ke8 55. Qb5+?! {Nothing wrong with the move itself, but complicating things.} (55. Qa4+ Ke7 56. Ba3+ Ke6 57. Qe4+! {would eventually lead to a draw by perpetual check.}) 55... Ke7 56. Qc5+? Ke6 57. Qc8+ Ke5 58. Bb2+? Ke4 {Things have gotten difficult, but 59 a4! might still actually hold. However, the game came to a for Aronian very brutal end:} 59. Qb7+?? (59. a4!) 59... Nxb7! 0-1

