1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. f3 Nc6!? 4. d5 Ne5 5. e4 d6 6. Nc3 Bg7 (6... c6 7. f4 Ned7 8. Nh3 Nc5 9. Nf2 Bg7 10. Be2 O-O 11. Be3 cxd5 12. cxd5 Qa5 13. O-O $14 {wasn't satisfying for Black in the game Ding Liren (2804)-Svidler,P (2756) Porto Carras 2018, 1-0 (41).}) 7. f4 Ned7 8. Be3!? (8. Nh3 O-O 9. Be2 Nc5 10. Nf2 {used to be the main line, but then Black found} e5!? 11. fxe5 (11. dxe6 fxe6 12. Be3 b6 13. Qc2 Bb7 14. O-O-O Nfd7 $13 {½-½ (51) Cheparinov,I (2703)-Najer,E (2706) Kocaeli 2017}) 11... Ne8! 12. Bf4 (12. exd6 Nxd6 $44 {0-1 (32) Laznicka,V (2679)-Salgado Lopez,I (2622) Dubai 2014}) 12... Qe7! 13. Rc1 Bxe5 14. Bxe5 Qxe5 15. b4 Nd7 16. O-O a5 17. a3 axb4 18. axb4 c5!? 19. dxc6 bxc6 $13 {½-½ (33) Moiseenko,A (2635)-Baron,T (2531) Douglas 2019.}) 8... O-O 9. Be2 e6 10. dxe6 fxe6 11. h4!? ({Black could never prove equality after} 11. Nh3 {- so I actually wonder what was Magnus' idea.} b6 12. O-O Bb7 13. Qc2 (13. Bf3!? Rb8 14. Qc2 Qe7 15. Rad1 a6 16. b4 Ba8 17. a3 Bh6 18. Bc1 $14 {0-1 (50) Vocaturo,D (2607)-Svidler,P (2760) Antalya 2017}) 13... Nc5 14. Bf3 e5 (14... Nfd7 15. b4 Na6 16. a3 Qe7 17. Rad1 $16 {½-½ (58) Dziuba,M (2556)-Tomczak,J (2615) Poland 2017}) (14... Rb8 15. b4 Ncd7 16. Rae1 Qe7 17. a3 $14 {½-½ (35) Baldauf,M (2493)-Esipenko,A (2616) Karlsruhe 2019}) 15. f5 a5 16. g4 h6 17. Rad1 Nh7 18. Qg2 g5 19. Nf2 Bf6 20. Nh1 Bc6 21. Ng3 $16 {1-0 (69) Fedoseev,V (2680)-Trjapishko,A (2512) Yaroslavl 2019}) 11... b5 $146 (11... b6 12. h5 Bb7 (12... Nxh5 13. Bxh5 gxh5 14. Qxh5 Bxc3+ 15. bxc3 Qe7 {may be playable}) 13. hxg6 hxg6 14. Qc2 e5 15. f5 Nc5 16. Bxc5 dxc5 17. Rd1 Qe7 18. g4 Ne8 19. Nf3 $16 {1-0 (28) De Jong,J (2454)-Wagner,D (2490) Netherlands 2014}) (11... e5?! 12. f5 $16) (11... Qe7!? $146 {may also be playable:} 12. h5 (12. g4!?) 12... gxh5 13. Bxh5 Nxh5 14. Qxh5 Bxc3+ 15. bxc3 Rf7 $132) 12. cxb5 Bb7 13. Qb3! Kh8 (13... Nxe4?? 14. Nxe4 Bxe4 15. Qxe6+ $18) 14. Bf3 e5 15. Nh3! {Black has practical compensation for the pawn, but not enough of it objectively.} Nh5 (15... a6 {looked natural. After} 16. O-O-O!? (16. Ng5 exf4 17. Ne6 Qe7 18. Nxf8 fxe3 19. Ne6 Ne5 $44) 16... axb5 17. h5 gxh5 18. f5 {Black's position remains difficult.}) 16. Bxh5 gxh5 ({Better may have been} 16... exf4 17. Nxf4 Nc5! 18. Bxc5 Rxf4 19. g3 Rf8 20. O-O-O gxh5 $16) 17. f5 Nf6 18. Nf2 {A good but timid move.} ({When watching the game, I was thinking Aronian would definitely have dared to play} 18. Ng5 {if his opponent wasn't Magnus Calsen!}) 18... a6 19. b6!? (19. bxa6 Bxa6 {opens columns and diagonals, which White prefer to avoid.}) 19... cxb6 20. Bxb6 (20. Rd1 b5 21. O-O {was enough for a clear advantage.}) 20... Qd7 21. Rd1 Rac8 (21... Rfb8 22. Qe6!? (22. Qc2!?) 22... Qxe6 23. fxe6 Re8 24. Bc7 Rxe6 25. Rxd6 Rxd6 26. Bxd6 Rd8 27. Bxe5 Re8 28. Bd4 Bxe4 29. O-O {and Black is far from equalizing, for example:} Bg6 30. Nh3 Ne4 31. Bxg7+ Kxg7 32. Nxe4 Rxe4 33. Rf4 Re2 34. Rf2 Re4 35. Nf4 $16) (21... Rab8 22. Ba7 Rbc8 23. Be3 $16) (21... d5 22. exd5 Qxf5 23. O-O {doesn't bring Black enough compensation either.}) 22. Be3 h6 23. Rh3 d5!? (23... Ba8 24. Qe6 $16) 24. exd5 Ba8 25. Rf3 Rb8 26. Qc2 Rfc8 27. Qe2 (27. Qd2 Kh7 28. Nfe4 Nxe4 29. Nxe4 {looked winning to me, but after} Rb5! {Black does keep some chances.}) 27... Qf7 28. Qd2 (28. Rg3! {would have won the game:} Nxd5 (28... Bxd5 29. Qd2 $18) 29. Nxd5 Bxd5 30. Bxh6! Bxh6 31. Rg6! $18) 28... Kh7 29. Nfe4 Nxe4 30. Nxe4 Rd8 31. f6 Bf8 32. Qc2 Kh8 33. Nc3 Rb4 34. Qf2 Bxd5 35. Bc5? {Tempting, but bad!} (35. Rf5!? {was strong.}) ({So was the clever} 35. Bb6!? Rd7 36. Bc5! Bxf3 (36... Rd4 {no longer works due to} 37. Bxf8 $18) 37. Rxd7 Qxd7 38. Bxb4 Bxb4 39. Qxf3 Qd4 40. Qg3 $18) 35... Rd4!! {Suddenly Black is back in the game.} 36. Bxd4 Bxf3 37. gxf3 exd4 38. Rxd4 Rb8? (38... Qxf6 39. Rxd8 Qxd8 {with great drawing chances for Black, due to the weakness of the h4-pawn.}) 39. Qh2 Rb6 40. Nd5 Qe6+ 41. Qe2 Qg8 42. Kf1 Re6 43. f7? {At first, that looked to me like an amazing shot - but it is a bad move!} (43. Qh2 Rc6 44. Rd1 {and Black doesn't have any compensation.}) 43... Qxf7 44. Rf4 Qg6! {Probably the ressource overlooked by Aronian.} 45. Rxf8+ (45. Re4 Rc6 $132) 45... Kg7 46. Qd1 (46. Rg8+ Kxg8 47. Ne7+ Rxe7 48. Qxe7 Qb1+ $11) 46... Kxf8 47. Nf4 Qe8 48. Nxe6+ Qxe6 {White has an extra pawn, but can't pary both of Black's threats: ...Qxa2 and ...Qh3+.} 49. Qd8+ Kf7 50. Qc7+ Ke8 51. Qb8+ Kd7 52. Qb7+ Kd8 53. Qb8+ Kd7 54. Qa7+ Ke8 55. b3 Qh3+ 56. Ke2 Qxh4 {The only thing Black should be careful of is not to exchange queens under bad circumstances. But it is certainly not going to happen!} 57. Qb8+ (57. Qxa6 Qh2+ 58. Kd3 h4 $11) 57... Ke7 58. Qc7+ Kf8 59. Qc5+ Kg7 60. Qe5+ Kg8 61. a4 Qh1 62. Qb8+ Kf7 63. Qf4+ Ke8 64. Qb8+ Kd7 65. Qa7+ Kd8 66. Qd4+ Ke8 67. Qe5+ Kd7 68. Qd4+ Ke8 69. b4 Qg2+!? ({There was nothing wrong with} 69... h4 {either.}) 70. Qf2 Qg5! {Now it's an easy draw.} 71. f4 (71. Qe3+ Kd7 $11) 71... Qd5 72. Qe3+ Kf7 73. Qa7+ Ke8 74. Qc5 Qa2+ 75. Kf3 Qxa4 76. Qxh5+ Ke7 77. Qe5+ Kf7 78. Qd5+ Kg7 79. Qd4+ Kf7 80. Qd5+ Kg7 81. Qd4+ Kf7 1/2-1/2

