1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 d5 3. Bf4 c5 4. e3 Nc6 5. Nbd2!? {An interesting move order, threatening to take on c5.} cxd4 {Let's see two major nuances between the moves 5.c3 and 5.Nbd2.} (5... Qb6 6. dxc5! Qxb2 (6... Qxc5 7. c4 e6 8. a3 Be7 9. b4 Qb6 10. Bd3 $14 {1-0 (39) Wei Yi (2748)-Le Tuan Minh (2485) Ashgabat 2017}) 7. Rb1 Qc3 (7... Qa3 8. Bb5 (8. Nd4? e5 9. Nb5 Qa5 10. Bg3 Bxc5 $19 {0-1 (36) Nguyen Ngoc Truong Son (2630)-Sethuraman,S (2646) Riyadh 2017}) 8... a6 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. O-O g6 11. c4! $146 Bg7 (11... Qxc5 12. Qa4 $16) 12. cxd5 Nxd5 13. Be5 O-O 14. Bxg7 Kxg7 15. Qc2 $14) 8. Bb5 (8. Rb3 Qxc5 9. Qb1 e6 10. Rxb7 Bd6 11. Rb5 Qa3 12. Rb3 Qc5 $11 {½-½ (44) Gupta,A (2626)-Kollars,D (2503) Zalakaros 2017}) 8... e6 (8... Bd7 9. O-O e6 10. Be2 Na5 11. Ne5 Bxc5 12. Nb3 $36 {0-1 (49) Harikrishna,P (2726)-Caruana,F (2828) Saint Louis 2019}) 9. O-O Be7 (9... Qxc5?! 10. c4 Be7 (10... a6 11. Bxc6+ bxc6 12. Ne5 Bd6 13. Nd3 {1-0 (13) Mikaelyan,A (2474)-Tabatabaei,M (2548) Yerevan ARM 2017}) 11. Nb3 Qa3 12. Ne5 a6 13. Bxc6+ bxc6 14. Nxc6 Bd7 15. Nxe7 Qxe7 16. Be5 O-O 17. cxd5 Nxd5 18. e4 f6 19. Bd4 Nf4 20. Bc5 $16 {1-0 (29) Naiditsch,A (2702)-Wells,P (2447) Antalya 2017}) (9... Bxc5?! 10. Be5 Qa3 11. Bb2 Qa5 12. Bxf6 gxf6 13. c4 $44 {1-0 (54) Deac,B (2549)-Schnider,G (2430) Graz 2018}) 10. Ne5 (10. Nd4 Qxc5 11. N2b3 Qb6 12. Nd2 Qc5 13. N2b3 Qb6 14. Nd2 Qc5 $11 {½-½ (15) Li Shilong (2495)-Harikrishna,P (2734) China 2018}) 10... Bd7 11. Nd3 {The latest trend.} (11. Ndf3 Qxc5 (11... O-O 12. e4 Rfd8 13. Nxd7 Nxd7 14. exd5 exd5 15. Re1 Qxc5 16. Bd3 Nb6 17. Rb5 Qa3 18. Rb3 Qc5 19. Ne5 $36 {1-0 (24) Kravtsiv,M (2655)-Ghosh,D (2536) Bhubaneswar 2018}) 12. c4 Nxe5 13. Nxe5 Bxb5 14. Rxb5 Qc8 15. Qb1 O-O 16. Rxb7 Bc5 $11 {½-½ (38) Kravtsiv,M (2635)-Csonka,B (2481) Hungary 2019}) (11. Nxd7 Nxd7 12. e4 O-O?! (12... a6! $13) 13. exd5 exd5 14. Ne4 dxe4 15. Qxd7 Qxc5 16. Qxb7 Nd4 17. Be3 $14 {½-½ (49) Livaic,L (2474)-Loiseau,Q (2443) Zadar 2018}) 11... O-O (11... Rc8 12. Nb3 O-O 13. a3!? $146 {avoiding ...Nb4; White wants to go 14.f3 with the idea Bg3-Be1.}) 12. Bc7! {White is trying to trap the black queen!} b6! {With this move, Black seems to hold.} (12... Ne8?! 13. Rb3 Qf6 14. Bg3! {is already bad for Black, and was played in many games.}) (12... Rac8? 13. Rb3 $18) 13. a4!? Ne8 14. Be5!? (14. Rb3?! Qa5 15. Nb2 Nxc7 16. Ndc4 {White gets the black queen, but at the cost of too many pieces.} dxc4 17. Nxc4 Be8 18. Nxa5 Nxa5 19. Rb1 Nxb5 20. axb5 Bxc5 $15 {0-1 (41) Tang,A (2507)-Balakrishnan,P (2411) Saint Louis 2018}) 14... Nxe5 15. Rb3 Qa5 16. Nxe5 Bxb5 17. axb5 Bxc5 (17... bxc5 18. Nd7 c4 $44) 18. Nd7 Nd6 19. Nxf8 Rxf8 20. c3 Nxb5 21. Qa1 Na3 (21... Nd6! $11) 22. Qb2 Nc4 23. Nxc4 dxc4 24. Rb5 Qa6 25. Ra1 Rd8 26. h4 Qc8 27. Qa2 a5 28. Qxc4 $14 {1-0 (54) Nabaty,T (2639)-Fridman,D (2627) Bremen 2019}) (5... Nh5 6. dxc5 Nxf4 7. exf4 Qa5 (7... e6 8. Nb3 Bxc5 9. Nxc5 Qa5+ 10. c3 Qxc5 11. Bd3 g6 12. g3 Bd7 13. Qe2 d4 14. Rc1 O-O 15. O-O $14 {½-½ (56) Blohberger,F (2447)-Blagojevic,D (2497) Skopje 2018}) 8. c4!? $146 (8. Bd3 Qxc5 9. O-O g6 $132 {0-1 (33) Gajewski,G (2623)-Santos Ruiz,M (2583) Dresden 2019}) 8... Qxc5 9. a3 e6 10. Bd3 Be7 11. O-O O-O 12. Rc1 Qd6 13. g3 {and White's position looks more pleasant.}) 6. exd4 Qb6!? {Why not!? Against almost any move, White would follow with c3 and transpose into a regular London opening position.} 7. Nb3 Bg4 ({Later in the match, Aronian tried to improve on this game with} 7... a6 {but also failed to equalize:} 8. Be2 Bg4 9. a4 e6 10. a5 Qd8 11. Ne5 Bxe2 12. Qxe2 Rc8 13. c3 $36 {1-0 (36) Carlsen,M (2872)-Aronian,L (2775) London 2019}) (7... Bf5 8. a4!? a6 9. a5 Qd8 10. c3 (10. Bd3 Bxd3 11. Qxd3 e6 12. O-O Be7 13. Ne5 O-O 14. Nxc6 bxc6 15. Rfc1 Qd7 16. c4 Qb7 $11 {0-1 (45) Kravtsiv,M (2685)-Bok,B (2622) Batumi 2018}) 10... e6 11. Ne5 Be7 12. Be2 O-O 13. O-O Nd7 14. Nd3 {could be the kind of positions Magnus wanted to play.}) 8. a4!? a6 (8... a5?! 9. Bb5 e6 10. h3 Bxf3 11. Qxf3 Bb4+ 12. c3 Be7 13. Nd2 $14 {1-0 (66) Unuk,L (2343)-Pychova,N (2201) Rymanow-Zdroj 2017}) 9. a5 Qa7?! (9... Qd8 {was certainly a bit more natural.}) 10. c3 e6 11. Be2 ({I couldn't see a good reason not to play} 11. h3!? {except that after} Bxf3 12. Qxf3 b5 13. axb6 Qxb6 {the b3-knight is unprotected, but it isn't a big deal:} 14. Qd1 {and White is definitely better.}) 11... Nh5 12. Be3 (12. Be5!? $14) 12... Qb8 13. h3 Bxf3 14. Bxf3 Nf6 15. Be2 Bd6 16. Bd3 O-O 17. O-O Qc7 18. Re1 {Black's position is almost equal, but he's simply running out of useful moves. In order to get counterplay, he should play ...b6 or ...e5 at some point, but in the game he could never do it under good conditions.} Kh8 19. Qf3 Rae8 (19... e5 20. dxe5 Nxe5 21. Qf5 $14) 20. Bg5 Ng8 21. Re2 Nge7 (21... f6 22. Bd2 f5!? {is a good try.} (22... e5? 23. Qxd5 $18) 23. Rae1 Nf6! 24. Rxe6 Rxe6 25. Rxe6 Ne4 26. Bxe4! dxe4 27. Qh5 Qd7 28. d5 Nd8 29. Rxd6 Qxd6 30. c4 $14) 22. Qh5 Ng6 23. Bc2 Kg8 24. Bd2!? {Not giving Black time for ...f6 and ...Qf7.} Nb4 25. Bb1 Nc6 26. Bd3 Rb8 (26... e5 27. Rae1! e4? 28. Qxd5 $18) 27. Be3 Rfe8?! (27... b6 28. axb6 Rxb6 29. Nc5 $16) (27... Rbe8! 28. g3 f5! 29. Rae1 Rf7 30. Bd2 Nf8 $132 {The f-rook is important to prevent Bxf5 ideas.}) 28. g3! {White slowly makes progress.} Nf8 (28... f5 29. Rae1 $16) 29. Bc2 b6 {Aronian probably felt like he had waited enough!} (29... Re7 30. h4 $14) 30. axb6 Rxb6 31. Bc1!? (31. Nc5!?) 31... Reb8 32. Nc5 e5 {Again - this looks like desperation! But White's position is no longer that easy to play.} (32... a5 33. Nd3 $16) 33. Nxa6 Qa7 34. dxe5 g6 35. Qg4 Nxe5 36. Qa4 Rc8 37. Be3 d4! 38. Bxd4? {And Carlsen blunders with an extra hour on the clock!} (38. Nc5! {was the right move, with the idea of 39.cxd4, while} Qxa4 39. Nxa4 {is just bad for Black.}) (38. cxd4? Nf3+ 39. Kh1 Qb7 $44) 38... Nf3+? ({Missing} 38... Rc4! 39. b4 (39. Qa2 Nf3+ 40. Kg2 Nxd4 41. cxd4 Rxd4 42. Be4 Ne6 $44) 39... Nf3+ 40. Kg2 Nxd4 41. cxd4 Rxd4 $11) 39. Kg2 Nxd4 40. Qxd4 Ne6 41. Rxe6!? (41. Qe3 {is close to a win, but Black seems to hold with a series of only moves:} Bc5 $8 42. Nxc5 Qxa1 43. Nd7 Rxb2 44. Qh6 Kh8 45. Nf6 Nf8 46. Ne8 Qa8+ $8 47. Be4 Rxe8 48. Bxa8 Rbxe2 {with serious drawing chances for Black.}) 41... fxe6 42. Be4 Rf8 43. Ra2 Rb7 44. Qxa7 Rxa7 45. b4 Rb8? {In time trouble, Aronian couldn't find how to limit the damage of the white pawns.} (45... Rc8! {would have made White's task far more difficult:} 46. b5 Rxc3 47. b6 Rf7 48. Rd2 Rc4 $8 49. Bf3 Rc3 $8 {and there is no win for White, e.g.} 50. Bg4 Rc6 51. Bxe6 Rxb6 52. Bxf7+ Kxf7 $14) 46. c4! {Now White pushes his pawn and wins.} Rb6 (46... Bxb4 47. Nxb4 $18) 47. b5 Kf7 48. Rc2 (48. Ra4 {with the idea Bc6 and c5 might have been stronger. A very instructive manoeuvre by the way!}) 48... Rbxa6 49. bxa6 Rxa6 50. c5 Be5 51. c6 Ra7 52. h4 {With a few minutes left and an hour less against the World Champion, chances to hold this endgame are close to zero.} Kf6 53. Rd2 Bc3 54. Rd3 Bb4 55. Kh3 Be7 56. f3 e5 57. Rc3 Bd6 58. Rc1 h6 59. Rd1 Be7 60. Kg4 Kf7 (60... h5+ 61. Kh3 {would be a success for White: he managed to get the black pawns on light squares and will now go the other way around with his king, or push g4.}) 61. Rb1 Bd6 62. h5! {This is even worse for Black.} gxh5+ 63. Kxh5 Kg7 64. Rd1 Bb4 65. Rd5 Re7 66. Bf5 {The bishop goes to d7 and the gave is over.} 1-0

