1. c4 e5 2. g3 Nc6 3. Bg2 h5 {Advances of h- and g-pawns in the opening have become popular in recent decades. Here it is dubious, though.} 4. Nc3 {White ignores Black's provocation, which is a good approach. More popular alternatives:} (4. h4 {- then Black can make use of the g4 square, say,} Bc5 5. Nc3 d6 6. e3 Nf6 7. Nge2 Bg4 {etc.}) (4. h3 {but then this pawn can become vulnerable after ...d7-d6, ...c8-e6 and ...d8-d7}) (4. Nf3!? Bc5 (4... e4 5. Nh4 g5 6. Nf5 d5 7. cxd5 Qxd5 8. Ne3 $14) ({the exchange sacrifice:} 4... h4!? 5. Nxh4 Rxh4 6. gxh4 Qxh4 {- is not approved by engines but can be unpleasant for White in a practical game}) (4... Be7 5. h4 Nf6 6. d3 O-O 7. Nc3 d6 8. Bg5 g6 9. O-O Ng4 10. Bxe7 Nxe7 11. d4 $14 {1/2 (43) Zude,E (2407)-Epishin,V (2574) Hofheim 2017}) 5. Nc3 d6 6. e3 Nge7 7. O-O Bb6 {1/2 (53) Flores,D (2569)-Bachmann,A (2614) Asuncion 2015 CBM 191 [Marin, Mihail]} 8. Re1 h4 9. Nxh4 g5 10. Nf3 g4 11. Nh4 Rxh4 12. gxh4 Ng6 $44 {M.Marin}) 4... h4 5. e3 (5. d3 {is less flexible as it allows} Bc5 (5... d6 6. e3 Nf6 7. a3 a5 8. Bd2 Bd7 9. Qc2 Qc8 10. h3 hxg3 11. fxg3 Ne7 12. O-O-O c6 13. g4 Be6 14. Nf3 e4 15. dxe4 Ng6 {1/2 (15) Rydl,J (2413)-Polasek,J (2316) Prague 2015}) (5... Bb4!? 6. Bd2 d6 7. Nd5?! Bxd2+ 8. Qxd2 h3! $36 {0-1 (34) Deshmukh,A (2253)-Venkatesh,M (2517) Pune 2019}) 6. e3 d6 7. a3 a5 8. b3 Nf6 9. Bb2 Bg4 10. Qd2 h3 11. Bf3 Qd7 $132 {1/2 (58) Kriebel,T (2445)-Pechac,J (2431) Slovakia 2016}) (5. g4 {gives Black good counterplay after} h3! 6. Nxh3 ({or} 6. Bxh3 d6 $44) 6... Qh4 7. e3 $140 d5! {[%cal Rc8g4]} 8. cxd5 Nb4) ({or} 5. Nf3 h3 6. Bf1 Nf6!? (6... d6 7. d4 Bg4 8. d5 Nce7 9. e4 g6 10. Be2 Bh6 11. Bxh6 Nxh6 12. Nd2 $14 {0-1 (43) Ponkratov,P (2589)-Popov,I (2596) Sochi 2016}) 7. d4 exd4 8. Nxd4 Bc5 9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. Qd3 Qe7 11. Bf4 Ba6 $132 {0-1 (19) Larochelle,M (2210)-Lawson,E (2381) Montreal 2013}) 5... Nf6 (5... Bc5 {is now less apropriate due to} 6. Nge2 {[%cal Yd2d4] , e.g.} hxg3 7. hxg3 Rxh1+ 8. Bxh1 d6 9. d4 Bb4 10. d5 Bxc3+ 11. Nxc3 Nce7 12. e4 $14 {1-0 (43) Rangel,D (2334)-Alboredo,J (2192) Barueri 2019}) 6. Nge2 (6. d4 Bb4 7. d5 {1/2 (26) Dragun,K (2584)-Dubov,D (2699) Batumi 2019} Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 Na5 9. Qa4 b6 $15 {[%csl Rc4]}) (6. d3 Bb4 7. Bd2 Bxc3 8. Bxc3 d5 9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Qb3 Nxc3 {1/2 (10) Zueger,B (2417)-Maier,C (2404) Switzerland 2005}) 6... h3 {Perhaps too fast.} (6... d6 $142 {keeping various options.}) 7. Bf3 e4?! {A risky pawn sacrifice.} (7... d6 8. d4 g5!? {deserved attention although after} 9. O-O {White's position looks more attractive.}) 8. Nxe4 (8. Bxe4 Nxe4 9. Nxe4 Ne5 $44) 8... Ne5 9. N2c3 (9. Nf4!?) 9... Nxc4 {Black has regained the pawn but White has got a clear development advantage.} (9... Nxe4 10. Bxe4 Nxc4 {is more accurate but after} 11. d4 {White's position is more pleasant anyway.}) 10. Nxf6+ Qxf6 {This queen can get under attack.} 11. Nd5 {Probably unnecessary.} (11. O-O {, then d2-d4 is simpler.}) 11... Qd6 12. d4 c6 13. Nc3 Qb4?! {Poor queen can't find a good place.} (13... Qg6 14. e4 Bb4 $14 {was relatively better.}) 14. a3 Qa5 15. O-O d5 16. e4! {White's development advantage makes this break almost decisive.} dxe4 17. Bxe4 (17. Re1 $142 ! {[%csl Ye4][%cal Ye1e4] , and if} f5 {then} 18. Nxe4 fxe4 19. Rxe4+ {, then d4-d5 with a killing attack. Such niceties are not for a rapid game, though.}) 17... Be6 18. d5?! {This break doesn't quite work now.} (18. Qe2 $142 {[%cal Yf1d1]} Be7 19. Bf4 {keeping initiative.}) 18... cxd5 19. Bxd5?! {[%mdl 16384] This recapture is unfortunate, too. Black obtains a stable plus.} (19. Nxd5 O-O-O 20. b4 Qb5 21. Nc3 $8 Rxd1 22. Nxb5 Rxf1+ 23. Kxf1 {, and now after, say,} Nd6 {Black is not worse.} ({or} 23... Rh5)) (19. b4!? Bxb4 20. Nxd5 $13) 19... Rd8 20. Qe2 Rxd5 21. Qxc4 $15 {} Re5?! {Black can't keep precision either.} (21... Rd1 $142 ! {Simplifications are favorable for Black, whose king is uncastled.}) 22. Qd3 Qc5 23. Qf3 Qc8 (23... Bd5 $142 24. Nxd5 Qxd5 $11) 24. Bf4 Rc5 25. Ne4 Rc6 (25... Qc6 $142) 26. Ng5! Be7 27. Nxe6 fxe6?! {[%csl Re6]} (27... Rxe6 28. Rac1 $36) 28. Be5 Bf6 29. Bxf6 O-O 30. Qg4 Rxf6 31. Qxh3 {As a result of Black's inaccuracies, White has won a pawn, which is not easy to convert, though, as Black's pieces are very active.} Rc2 32. b4 Qc6 33. Rad1 Qb6 34. Qg2 Rc3 35. Qe4 Rxa3 36. Rd2 (36. Rd7 $142 Ra2 37. Qxb7 $16) 36... Raf3 37. Kg2 a6 38. Rc2 Rf8 {White has given the pawn back but he is still better due to his superior pawn structure and safer king. Here, however, Magnus Carlsen lost due to disconnection.} 0-1

