{[%evp 0,42,58,6,55,68,82,82,67,67,67,67,65,18,58,16,52,-19,-42,-42,-42,-65,-44,-143,57,10,10,9,27,25,21,64,32,35,49,-31,-19,-67,-53,-128,27,27,27,27,336]} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Rg1!? {This rare move became famous when Ivanchuk used it in a rapid game to defeat Kasparov. From all White's options this move is ranked 12th by number of games played, but it's certainly more dangerous as the current game proves. Recently this variation has been all the rage and unsurprisingly so. The positions are fresh and highly unexplored and the newest Neural Network based engines are on board with it. The variations spread out in all sorts of directions here, so instead of going too deep into one of them, I will try to give an overview of what is all going on here.} b5 ({Another remarkable game in this line went} 6... Nc6 7. g4 g6 (7... Nxd4 8. Qxd4 {now taking with the bishop loses outright, but taking with the knight is also quite dubious as Black is massively lagging behind in development.} Nxg4 (8... Bxg4? {loses-} 9. Rxg4! Nxg4 10. Qa4+ Qd7 11. Bb5! {the point.} axb5 12. Qxa8+ Qd8 13. Qxb7) 9. Nd5 {White has very strong compensation- Black should not get into this.}) 8. g5 Nd7 9. h4 Nxd4 10. Qxd4 Ne5 11. Kd1!? {Van Foreest,J-Firouzja, Wijk aan Zee 2020.} Rg8 12. Nd5 Bg7!? {is the better move. This position on it's own deserves a survey!} (12... Be6 13. Qb4 Rb8 14. Be3! {is a natural improvement over the game. Black has some questions to answer. Both kings are uncastled, but White has more space overal and clearer play.} (14. f4 Nc6 15. Qb6 {½-½ (36) Van Foreest,J (2644)-Firouzja,A (2723) Wijk aan Zee 2020}))) ({Other critical options are the typical moves} 6... e6 {'?!' would fully justify g1 for whoever after} 7. g4 {wants to follow it up with g5 and h4, winning a full tempo over g1, compared to the 6.h3 e6 7.g4 variation in the very same Najdorf.}) (6... e5 7. Nb3 Be6 {is the traditional way of dealing with this system and it remains quite reliable and also quite simple. The endgame that arises after} (7... h5!? {is possible here and on the 6th move as well. One thing to note is that after} 8. h3 Be6 9. Be3 {it is important not to follow Sanan Sjugirov who got a lost position in his off-hand blitz match against Magnus Carlsen.} d5? (9... h4!? {is a possible improvement, intending bd7 and the typical Najdorf play}) ({or the immediate} 9... Nbd7!? {or perhaps something else, but definitely not d5?.}) 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Nxd5 Qxd5 12. Qxd5 Bxd5 13. O-O-O Be6 14. Na5 $18 {1-0 (36) Carlsen,M (2876)-Sjugirov,S (2662) chess24.com INT 2020}) 8. g4 d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Nxd5 Qxd5 11. Qxd5 Bxd5 12. Be3 {this endgame actually arises more or less by force.} Nc6 13. O-O-O O-O-O 14. Bb6 {The critical direction.} Rd6 {and now White has a lot of moves, but perhaps one of the reasons that 6.g1 is back in fashion in general, is that here after} 15. Rg3 {the engines give White a small plus. Still Black is quite solid after for example} Be7 16. g5 Be6 {intending h6 at the right moment, trying to simplify the game.}) (6... g6 7. g4 Bg7 (7... Nc6) 8. g5 Nfd7 9. Be3 {Black has no better square for his b8 knight than c6. 7...c6 is in fact more flexible, as Black has some xd4 options, like in the Van Foreest-Firouzja game.}) (6... h5!? {is a funky move that the engines don't dislike. White has three different ways of developing here, g5, e3 or the flexible h3. The positions are in a way still typical, as the usual Najdorf structure is there, but with g1 h5 included, they are very unexplored.} 7. h3 {here again Black has the choice, between e5, c6, b5 and so on. Completely unexplored territory!} (7. Be3) (7. Bg5)) 7. g4 {Another attempt by Ian, who was a step ahead of Magnus here and must have clearly surprised him with this one.} (7. a4!? b4 8. Nd5 e6 {I felt this was the most practical option, but here too, the position gets crazy.} (8... Nxd5 9. exd5 {is a very strange position, also deserves further investigation.}) (8... Nxe4!? {is very brave. Usually I would say that this position needs a separate survey, but here I would say it needs two separate surveys. White has many ways to play for compensation here.}) 9. Nxf6+ Qxf6 10. Be3 Bb7 11. Qd2 {the main idea, it allows Black to trade the e4-pawn for the b4-pawn, but White keeps the momentum and doesn't waste time to defend the e4-pawn.} Bxe4 12. f3 Bb7 {deviating from 12...d5, which has been played before in the only game that reached this position. Motylev-Kokarev 2019.} 13. Qxb4 Qe7 14. Nb3!? {was the right way to get the position Ian wanted to get.} (14. Qb6?! {this being a blitz game, we both did a mix up here.} Qd7! 15. O-O-O Qc8? {I probably tried reconstructing what I knew, but just e7! was good here.} (15... Be7! 16. Nb3 {was the reason I didn't do this, but here I have 2 strong options c6! or d5! with an enormous advantage.} Bd5) 16. Nb3 Nc6 {and now we transposed to a position that Ian probably wanted to get.} 17. Bd4 {1-0 (76) Nepomniachtchi,I (2784)-Giri,A (2764) chess24.com INT 2020}) 14... Nc6 15. Qb6 Qd7 16. O-O-O Qc8 {and now we transposed to a position that Ian probably wanted to get.} 17. Bd4 {1-0 (76) Nepomniachtchi,I (2784)-Giri,A (2764) chess24.com INT 2020 here I played e5, but alternatively the engines suggest} Nxd4 18. Qxd4 Qb8! {intending a7!, with a very unclear position, that needs to be checked more carefully. Both sides also had alternatives along the way.}) 7... Bb7 8. g5 {'!?' it is possible to defend the e4-pawn, but then Black is quite happy with the b5/b7 order.} (8. Bg2 {has been played more frequently, but that's not why Ian decided to give 6.g1 a try!}) 8... Nxe4 ({Carlsen deviates from the previous game which went} 8... Nfd7 9. a3 (9. Be3!? {could already be an improvement, as} b4 10. Nd5 {seems quite dubious for Black, who is much behind in development.}) 9... g6 10. h4 Bg7 11. h5 Nc6 12. Be3 Nc5 {was seen in Nepomniachtchi-Carlsen, chess24.com 2020, and now strong would have been} 13. h6! Bxd4 14. Bxd4 Nxd4 15. Qxd4 e5 16. Qe3 {followed by 0-0-0. The pawns on g5 and h6 remain quite unpleasant even in the long run.}) 9. Nxe4 Bxe4 10. a4!? ({A new idea which poses Black some serious practical problems. In previous games White exclusively opted for} 10. Qg4 Bb7 11. Bg2 Bxg2?! (11... Qc7! {is a simple improvement, when White doesn't have enough compensation most likely.}) 12. Qxg2 Nd7? {it was still possible to avoid the trap with 12...a7!, but a few people fell for this one actually -} 13. g6! {very beautiful idea!} hxg6 14. Ne6! {Boom! The point. White won in (17) Belousov,D (2274)-Thing,B (2222) Moscow 2019. fxe6 is not possible due to xg6 mate, while if the queen moves, xa8 is incoming.} Rxh2 {is a creative defensive attempt, but after} 15. Qxa8 fxe6 16. Qxa6 {White is up in material and is winning.}) 10... e5 {this is possible, but alternatives b4 and bxa4 are definitely to be studied.} ({One month after the present game the only other GM game saw} 10... b4 {'!?' which is at least a safer continuation for Black.} 11. Qe2 d5 12. f3 Bg6 13. f4 e6 (13... Be4!?) 14. Be3 (14. f5?! Bc5! 15. fxg6 Bxd4 16. gxf7+ Kxf7 $15) 14... Bd6 15. h4 O-O 16. O-O-O Nd7 (16... b3!) 17. h5 Be4 18. Bg2 Nc5 19. Bxe4 dxe4? (19... Nxe4!) 20. Kb1 Nxa4 21. f5 exf5 22. Nxf5 Nc3+ 23. bxc3 bxc3 24. Rxd6 Rb8+ 25. Kc1 Qa5 26. Nh6+ gxh6 27. gxh6+ Kh8 28. Qxa6 Qb4 29. Rb6 {1-0 Lagarde-Brunello, Trieste 2020.}) (10... bxa4!?) 11. axb5! Be7 {'?'} ({The critical move is accepting the challenge with} 11... exd4 {Black has to accept the sacrifice.} 12. Qxd4 {and now d5 or e7, both are possible. The position is crazy complex, g3-e3 lift is a typical idea and White also hopes to get his queenside pawns going at some point. Complicated stuff.} d5 13. Rg3!? {though it's understandable one isn't eager about entering this position as Black. For the piece, White has one pawn, ideas to regain the piece with e3 (pinning the bishop on e4), while it's not simple for Black completing development as the queen on d4 is eyeing the pawn on g7.}) 12. Rg4! {White is forcing the e4 bishop to decide, what to give up, the h1-a8 diagonal, or the crucial f5-square for the knight.} axb5 (12... Bg6 13. Bg2 d5 14. Be3! O-O (14... Bh5 {allows} 15. Nf5! Bxg4 16. Qxg4 O-O 17. Bxd5 {and the rook is trapped.}) (14... exd4 15. Rxd4 O-O 16. Bxd5 {and White will regain the material with interest.}) 15. Ne2 {is great for White.}) (12... Bb7 13. Nf5 O-O 14. Rga4! {and White obtains a very annoying pawn on a6.}) (12... d5 {allows} 13. Rxe4! dxe4 14. Nf5 Qxd1+ 15. Kxd1 {when Black is still in deep trouble.}) 13. Bxb5+ Nd7 {according to the engine f8 was a better defence, but that looks even worse.} ({In hindsight,} 13... Kf8 {is the best option for Black, though also in that case after} 14. Bd2 {White retains the better prospects.}) 14. Bd2! {very strong, developing the bishop and connecting the queen to the rook.} ({A great move, which forces Black to take a decision about his bishop on e4.} 14. Rxa8 Qxa8 $13) (14. Rxe4? Rxa1 $19) 14... Bb7 ({If Black intends to retain control over the f5-square with} 14... Bg6 {then White obtains a totally dominant position after} 15. Nc6 Qc8 16. Rxa8 Qxa8 17. Ra4 Qb7 18. Qg4 {followed by a7; Black is busted.}) 15. Nf5 {now white is winning, thanks to this superstrong knight and the potential for a strong attack on the kingside.} O-O 16. Rxa8?! {the most direct, but not so easy actually.} ({Ian is naturally playing for a mating attack and I doubt many people would have considered shifting play to the queenside with the sick} 16. Rga4!! {'!' when surprisingly Black's position is already collapsing as too many vulnerable points in his position require protection, e.g. was winning more trivially, apparently, because now after} Nb6 (16... Rxa4 17. Rxa4 {and Black is unable stopping both threats Ra7 and Ba5.} Nb6 18. Ra7! {hits that vulnerable b7 bishop.}) 17. Rxa8 Bxa8 (17... Nxa8 18. Ra7 {wins a piece.}) 18. Bb4 Nd5 (18... Bxg5 19. Qxd6 $18) 19. Rxa8! Qxa8 20. Qxd5 Qxd5 21. Nxe7+ Kh8 22. Nxd5 $18) 16... Bxa8 17. Rh4 {With the idea of Qh5.} g6! {only move} ({The only move, as} 17... Bxg5? 18. Bxg5 Qxg5 19. Bxd7 Qg1+ 20. Kd2 (20. Ke2?? Bf3+!) 20... Qxf2+ 21. Qe2 {is just an extra piece for White.}) 18. Qg4 {White appears to be crushing his opponent but things are not so simple.} Nc5 {'!' Magnus tries to fight and correctly so.} (18... gxf5 19. Qh5 {leads to mate.}) 19. Qh3? ({Again, the most natural option chosen by Nepo is not White's best! The incredible} 19. Kf1!! {with the idea to prepare a5 wins for White after this prophylaxis wins, but obviously this all has little to do with the reality. Computer stuff and that too, on quite some depth...} Be4 (19... h5 {is met by} 20. Rxh5! gxh5 21. Qxh5 Ne4 22. Ba5! $18) 20. Ba5!! Bxf5 ({Relatively best, since after} 20... Qxa5 21. Nxe7+ Kg7 {White gives mate by force} 22. Rxh7+ Kxh7 23. Qh4+ Kg7 24. Qh6#) 21. Bxd8 Bxg4 22. Bxe7 Rb8 23. Rxg4 Rxb5 24. Bxd6 Ne6 25. Ra4 {and the remaining endgame is pretty hopeless for Black, e.g} Nxg5 26. c4! {and now} Rxb2 27. Ra8+ Kg7 28. Bxe5+ {even drops the rook.}) (19. Ba5? {simply fails now to} Qxa5+! {check!}) 19... h5? ({After this blunder it's game over, but probably the World Champion already didn't really like his position anyway, which may explain the reason he didn't go for the critical} 19... Bxg5! {would actually save Black. I am curious what he missed here, but with little time on the clock, it could frankly be anything.} 20. Rxh7 Bxd2+! (20... Bf6? 21. Nh6+! Kxh7 22. Nxf7+ {and White wins the queen.}) 21. Kxd2 {otherwise gxf5 and there is no mate as the h6-square is protected.} Qg5+ {'!' when the variations are becoming pretty insane, but certainly worth playing through!} (21... gxf5? 22. Rh8+ Kg7 23. Qh6#) 22. Kc3 (22. Kd1? {is simply bad, on account of} gxf5 23. Rh8+ Kg7 24. Qh7+ Kf6 25. Rxf8 Bf3+! 26. Be2 Qg1+ 27. Kd2 Ne4+ 28. Kd3 Bxe2+ 29. Kxe2 Qxf2+ 30. Kd3 Qd4+ 31. Ke2 Qd2+ 32. Kf1 Qf2#) (22. Ne3?! {is an interesting alternative, though after} Ne4+ 23. Kc1 Nxf2 24. Rh8+ Kg7 25. Qh7+ Kf6 26. Rxf8 Qxe3+ 27. Kb1 Bd5! {only Black can be better.}) 22... Ne4+! (22... gxf5? 23. Rh8+ Kg7 24. Qh7+ Kf6 25. Rxf8 Ne4+ 26. Kb3 Bd5+ 27. c4 {and there no more any good checks as the black queen fails to join the counterattack against the white king.}) 23. Kb4! (23. Kb3? Bd5+ 24. c4 Nc5+ 25. Ka2 Ra8+ 26. Kb1 Be4+) (23. Kd3? Qd2+ 24. Kc4 Bd5#) (23. Kc4? Bd5+!! 24. Kb4 (24. Kxd5 Nf6+! 25. Kc6 Nxh7 $19) 24... Qd2+ 25. Ka3 Ra8+ 26. Ba4 Rxa4+ 27. Kxa4 Nc5+ 28. Kb5 Qe2+! 29. Kb4 (29. Kb6 Qa6+ 30. Kc7 Qb7+ 31. Kd8 Qd7#) 29... Qc4+ 30. Ka5 Nb7+ 31. Kb6 Qc5+ 32. Ka6 Qa5#) 23... Qd2+ (23... gxf5 24. Rh8+ Kg7 25. Qh7+ Kf6 26. Rxf8 Qd2+! 27. Ka3! (27. Kb3? Bd5+ 28. c4 Nc5+ 29. Ka2 Qa5+ 30. Kb1 Be4+ 31. Kc1 Qe1#) (27. Kc4? Bd5#) (27. c3? Qxb2+ 28. Ka5 Qxc3+ 29. Ka6 (29. Kb6 Qd4+! 30. Ka5 Qa7+ 31. Kb4 Qc5+ 32. Ka5 Qc7+ 33. Ka4 (33. Kb4 Qc3+ 34. Ka4 Nc5#) 33... Nc3+ 34. Kb3 Bd5+ 35. Kb2 Nd1+! 36. Kb1 Ba2+! 37. Kxa2 Qc2+ 38. Ka3 Qb2+ 39. Ka4 Nc3+ 40. Ka5 Qxb5# {What a shame that Black didn't see all these variations! ;-)}) 29... Qa1+!! 30. Kb6 Qd4+ 31. Kc7 Qa7+ 32. Kc8 (32. Kd8 Qb8+ 33. Kd7 Nc5#) 32... Qb7+ 33. Kd8 Qb8+ 34. Kd7 Nc5#) 27... Qa5+ 28. Ba4 Qc5+ 29. Ka2 (29. b4?? Qc3+ 30. Ka2 Bd5+ 31. Kb1 Qxb4+ 32. Kc1 (32. Bb3 Nd2+ 33. Kb2 Bxb3 {and Black wins.}) 32... Qa3+ 33. Kd1 Qa1+ 34. Ke2 Bc4+ 35. Kf3 Qh1+ 36. Ke3 f4#) 29... Qc4+ (29... Bd5+? 30. Ka1 $18 {and the checks are over.}) 30. Ka3 (30. Ka1? Qxa4+) (30. Kb1? Nd2+! 31. Kc1 Qf1+ 32. Kxd2 Qxf2+ 33. Kc3 (33. Kd1 Bf3+ 34. Kc1 Qe1#) (33. Kc1 Qe1#) (33. Kd3 Be4+ {leads to the same as 33.c3.}) 33... Qd4+ 34. Kb3 Bd5+ 35. Ka3 Qc5+ 36. b4 Qc3+ 37. Bb3 Qa1+ 38. Ba2 Qxa2#) 30... Qc5+ {with a draw.}) 24. Ka3! (24. Ka4? {runs into} Nc5+ 25. Ka3 Qa5+ 26. Ba4 Qxa4#) 24... Qa5+ 25. Ba4 Qc5+ 26. Ka2 (26. Kb3?? Bd5+) 26... Qc4+ {with a perpetual.} 27. Ka3 (27. Kb1? {is beautifully met by} Qf1+ 28. Qxf1 Nd2+ 29. Kc1 Nxf1 {and White can't avoid the loss of material, as both the rook and knight are hanging.}) 27... Qc5+ {and the repetition is inevitable, as playing on with} 28. b4? {allows} Qc3+ 29. Qxc3 Nxc3 30. Rh3 Nxa4 31. Ne7+ Kg7 32. Kxa4 Kf6 {and the knight is trapped.}) 20. Rxh5 {'!' White is crushing here.} gxh5 21. Qxh5 Ne6 {and not waiting for g6 Black resigned.} (21... Ne6 {and without waiting for his opponent to execute the mating variation, the World Champion resigned, in view of} 22. g6! fxg6 23. Qxg6+ Kh8 24. Qh6+ Kg8 25. Qxe6+ Kh8 (25... Rf7 26. Nh6+) 26. Qh6+ Kg8 27. Qg7# {A wonderful prelude to their upcoming World Championship match in Dubai, november/december 2021!}) 1-0

