1. d4 e6 2. Nf3 f5 3. c4 Nf6 4. Nc3 {0-1 Anand,V (2797)-Carlsen,M (2865) Grenke Chess Classic 3rd Baden-Baden 2015 (4) Aronian does not seem too keen to allow the Stonewall, possibly under the influence of the game} Bb4 {The opening has turned into a Nimzo-Dutzh hybrid, supposed to be fine for Black, but still complicated. The reader should compare with the lines examined in my pseudo-Owen article for CBM 166. The main difference is the early commitment of Black's queen.} 5. Qb3 Qe7 6. Bd2 {Unpinning the knight and preparing to re-capture with the bishop on c3.} (6. Bg5 O-O 7. g3 c5 {This looks slightly inconsequent with the previous play.} (7... b6!?) 8. Bg2 Nc6 9. dxc5 Qxc5 10. Bxf6 Rxf6 11. a3 Bxc3+ 12. Qxc3 $14 {Gelfand,B (2704)-Morovic Fernandez,I (2549) Cap d'Agde 2002 CBM 092 [Finkel,A] (½-½, 26), with comfortable play for White.}) (6. a3 {transposes to some sort of 4.c2 Nimzo-Indian.} Bxc3+ 7. Qxc3 O-O 8. e3 b6 9. Bd3 Bb7 10. b3 d6 11. Bb2 Nbd7 12. O-O-O Ne4 13. Qc2 {Ivanov,J (2370)-Schmouchkovitch,M (2003) Sautron 2007 EXT 2008 [RR] (1-0, 23)} a5!? $132) 6... b6 (6... Bxc3 {, played for the sake of continuing with ...d7-d6, looks a bit premature:} 7. Bxc3 d6 8. g3 Nbd7 9. Bg2 Ne4 10. O-O Nxc3 11. Qxc3 O-O 12. Rfe1 e5 13. e4 f4 14. c5 dxc5 15. dxc5 Nxc5 16. Rac1 b6 17. b4 Na6 18. Nxe5 $14 {Wang,Y (2704)-Agdestein,S (2583) Amsterdam 2008 (1-0, 35)}) 7. g3 $146 {This way of developing is familiar, but in this concrete position the last move is a novelty.} (7. e3 Bb7 8. Be2 O-O 9. O-O Bxc3 10. Bxc3 Ne4 11. d5 {This offers Black a clear developing plan by conceding the control over the c5-square.} ({It makes sense to keep the threat d4-d5 in reserve with either} 11. Rad1 {or}) (11. Be1) 11... Na6 12. Rad1 d6 13. dxe6 {Another important concession, clearing the long diagonal.} (13. Be1 Nac5 14. Qc2 a5 15. Nd4 e5 16. Nb5 {[%cal Gf2f3]}) 13... Nac5 14. Qc2 Nxe6 15. b4 a5 16. a3 f4 $36 {Laurine,L-Keres,P Tallinn 1937 EXT 2001 (0-1, 24). In the database this game is reported as featuring the pointless sacrifice 6...b5 instead of 6...b6.}) 7... Bb7 8. Bg2 Nc6!? {An ambitious continuation, aiming at active piece play.} ({The main alternative is} 8... Bxc3 9. Bxc3 Ne4 10. O-O O-O 11. Be1 d6 $13) 9. O-O (9. a3 {also leads to interesting play:} Nxd4! (9... Bxc3 10. Bxc3 $14 {[%csl Rb7,Rc6]}) 10. Nxd4 Bxg2 11. axb4 Bxh1 12. f3 c5 (12... Bg2 13. Ncb5 {[%csl Ga8,Gc7,Ge8]}) 13. bxc5 bxc5 14. Ndb5 (14. Nc2 {offers the bishop the time to escape with} Bg2) 14... O-O 15. Kf2 a6 16. Na3 Ng4+! 17. Kg1 (17. fxg4 fxg4+ 18. Kg1 Bc6 $17 {[%csl Rg1][%cal Gc6h1]}) 17... Rab8 18. Qc2 Bxf3 19. exf3 Ne5 $13 {with unbalanced play, from the strategic and material points of view.}) 9... Na5 10. Qc2 Nxc4 11. Nb5! {In view of this counter-blow the pawn sacrifice turns into a temporary one.} Nxd2 (11... Bxd2 12. Nxc7+ Kf7 13. Nxa8 $16) 12. Nxc7+ Kf8 13. Nxd2 Bxg2 14. Kxg2 Rc8 15. Nf3 {Removing the knight from the attacked square and taking g5 under control.} (15. Nxe6+? {is premature due to} Kf7 16. Ng5+ Kg6 $17) 15... Qd8 ({But now Black has to count with the aforementioned threat:} 15... Bd6? 16. Nxe6+! Kf7 17. Neg5+ Kg6 18. Qb3 $16 {The main point is that the g5-knight is defended.}) 16. Rac1 Ke7 (16... Bd6? 17. Nxe6+) 17. Qa4 {A simple way of getting out of the pin.} Rxc7 18. Qxb4+ d6 19. Qd2 {Black seems to have solved the main strategic problems, but the king's presence in the centre invites him to caution.} h6 {This weakens the g6-square for the sake of preventing g5.} (19... Qd7 20. Rxc7 Qxc7 21. Qg5 Kf7 22. d5 Nxd5 23. Qh5+ g6 24. Ng5+ Ke8 25. Qh4 $44) (19... Nd5 20. Qg5+ Ke8 $8 {[%csl Gc7,Gd8,Gg7]} 21. Qh5+ $36) 20. Kg1 Qd7 {Slightly careless or maybe just provocative.} (20... Kf7!? $11) 21. Rxc7 Qxc7 22. d5! {A thematic break, partly putting Black off balance.} Nxd5 23. Nh4 {[%csl Gg6]} Rc8 ({Defending both f5 and g6 with the king does not work:} 23... Kf6?! 24. Qd4+ Kf7 25. Nxf5 $36) 24. Nxf5+ exf5 25. Qxd5 Qc5 26. Qd3 Qe5 27. Rd1 Rc6 $14 {White has the better structure and, despite Black's piece activity, is entitled to count on some minimal but lasting advantage.} 28. Rd2?! {Unnecessarily leaving the back rank undefended.} (28. e3) (28. Qa6) 28... Qe4! {Due to the threat ...c1+ White cannot avoid simplifying to an endgame in which the centralised position of the black king turns into a trump.} 29. Qxe4+ fxe4 30. f3 exf3 31. exf3 d5! {The previously backward pawn suddenly starts being threatening.} 32. Rxd5 {This leads to a worse ending.} (32. Kf2 Kd6 33. Ke3 Rc1 34. Kd4 $11) 32... Rc1+ 33. Kg2 Rc2+ 34. Kh3 Rxb2 35. a4 a5! {Suddenly the a4-pawn is doomed in view of the threat ...b4.} (35... Rb4 36. a5 $11) 36. Re5+?! {The start of a time consuming plan.} ({As usual in the endgame the highest priority should have been activating the king.} 36. Kg4 Rb4+ (36... Rxh2 37. Rb5 $11) 37. Kh5 Rxa4 38. Kg6 Rb4 39. Kxg7 {White has strong counterplay which seems to be enough for reaching a draw even though Black would not have exhausted his active ideas:} a4 40. f4 a3 41. Rd1 Ra4 (41... b5 42. Re1+ Kd6 43. f5 $132) 42. Re1+ Kd6 43. f5 b5 44. f6 b4 45. Ra1 Ra7+ (45... b3? 46. f7 Ra7 47. Rxa3 $18) 46. f7 Ke6 47. Re1+ Kd5 48. Ra1 Kc4 49. h4 b3 50. Rxa3 Rxf7+ 51. Kxf7 b2 52. Ra4+ Kc5 53. Ra5+ Kc6 54. Ra6+ Kb7 55. Rxh6 b1=Q 56. Rg6 $11 {with a reliable fortress and a draw.}) 36... Kd6 37. Rf5 Rb4 38. Rf7 Rxa4 39. Rxg7 Rc4 {With respect to the lines above the white king is much more passive, tipping the balance in Black's favour.} 40. f4 Rc7 41. Rg8 (41. Rg6+ Kc5 42. f5 a4 43. f6 b5 44. Rg7 Rc8) 41... Ra7 42. f5 a4 43. f6 a3 44. Rg7 Ra8 45. f7 a2 0-1

