1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 Bc5 5. Nb3 {A common line nowaday where White normally castles to the queenside, heading for sharp play.} Bb6 6. Nc3 Nf6 7. Qe2 a5 {A standard push, but in this particular position very rare. The main continuations are 7...d6 and 7...0-0.} 8. e5 $146 {A principled novelty, but both players continued to play quickly, clearly having the variation analysed until a certain point.} (8. a4 O-O (8... Nb4 9. Bg5 h6 10. Bh4 d6 11. O-O-O Qe7 12. f3 Bd7 13. Kb1 g5?! 14. Bf2 Bxf2 15. Qxf2 Be6 16. Nd4 $16 {½-½ (81) Volokitin,A (2687)-Eljanov,P (2707) Kiev 2013}) 9. Be3 d5 10. Bxb6 cxb6 11. O-O-O Re8 12. f3 Bd7 13. Qf2 dxe4 14. fxe4 Rc8 $15 {1-0 (49) Carlsson,P (2485)-Hector,J (2483) Borlange 2014}) 8... O-O! {This sacrifice is forced. Things are getting exciting from the very beginning.} 9. exf6 a4 10. Nd5 (10. fxg7? {would lose a crucial tempo. The white pawn on g7 actually contributes to the defence of Black's monarch.} Re8 11. Be3 axb3 12. cxb3 Nd4 13. Qh5 Ra5! (13... Nc2+? 14. Kd2 Ra5 (14... Nxa1?? 15. Bd3 $18) 15. Nb5 Nb4 16. Bg5 f6 17. Bxf6 Qxf6 18. Qxe8+ Kxg7 19. Kd1 Rxb5 20. Bxb5 Qd4+ 21. Kc1 Qf4+ 22. Kd1 Qd4+ $11) 14. Nb5 (14. Nd5 Nc2+ 15. Kd2 Nb4 16. Bc4 Nxd5 17. Bxd5 Qf6 18. Bxb6 cxb6 $19) 14... Rxe3+! 15. fxe3 Qe7 16. O-O-O Nxb5 17. Bd3 Bxe3+ (17... Qc5+ 18. Qxc5 Bxc5) 18. Rd2 h6 19. Qf5 Bxd2+ 20. Kxd2 {Otherwise, White will lose his queen after the knight's check.} Qg5+ 21. Qxg5 hxg5 22. Bxb5 Rxb5 23. Re1 Kxg7 24. Re8 Ra5 25. Rxc8 Rxa2 26. Kc2 Ra6! 27. Rxc7 Rc6+ 28. Rxc6 bxc6 $19) 10... Re8 11. Be3 axb3 12. Qg4 g6 13. Bc4! {Development above all!} Nb4! {The preparation probably ended somewhere here, as from this point players started to consume more time. Magnus found the right way to complete his development with the temporary pieece sacrifice.} (13... bxc2 14. O-O d6 15. Qf4 Re5 16. Nxb6 cxb6 17. Bxb6! Qf8 {is unlikely to be good for Black.}) 14. Nxb4 d5 15. Qf4 (15. Qg5 dxc4 16. Nd5 Bc5! {It is necessary to bring the bishop into defence.} 17. cxb3 cxb3 18. O-O h6 19. Qxh6 Bf8 20. Qg5 bxa2 21. Rfd1 {The queen has no square to hide, but Black will get enough material for it.} Bf5 22. h4 Ra6 23. Ne7+ Bxe7 24. Rxd8 Bxd8 25. Bd4 Bb1 {Black is not worse.}) 15... dxc4 16. O-O bxc2 17. Nd5! {Back to the key square.} (17. Bxb6 cxb6 18. Nd5 Re6 19. Qxc4 Kh8 20. Qxc2! Qxd5? (20... Rd6! {must be played.} 21. Nc3 Bf5 22. Qb3 Qxf6 $11) 21. Rad1 Qe5 22. Rd8+ Re8 23. Qc7! Qe6 24. h4! {A remarkable position. White is a piece down, but dominating.} (24. Rfd1? Bd7 25. Qxd7 Raxd8 26. Qxd8 h5 {Compared to the line 24.h4 White can't now play 27.d7 in view of the mate on the back rank. The queen must go back to d3 or d2 and White loses his extra pawn.}) 24... Kg8 25. Rfd1 Bd7 $8 26. Qxd7 Raxd8 27. Qxd8 h5 28. Qd7 $16) 17... Re6 {Now the queen can protect the king from f8.} 18. Qxc4 Bxe3 19. fxe3 b5! 20. Qc5 Bb7 21. Ne7+ Kh8 (21... Rxe7 22. fxe7 Qd2 23. Rf2 Qd1+ 24. Rf1 Qd2 {could force a repetition, but Carlsen is playing for a win.}) 22. Qxc2 Raa6 {Black has managed to develop both his rooks via the sixth rank. There is a lot of unusual stuff in this game!} 23. Rac1?! {It's tempting to attack the c7, but it was more important to fight for the d-file.} (23. Rad1 Rad6 24. Qb3 Rd2 25. Rxd2 Qxd2 26. Rf2 Qe1+ 27. Rf1 Qe2 28. Rf2 $11) 23... Rxf6 24. Rxf6 Rxf6 25. Qxc7 Qd2?! {Just missed the square.} (25... Qd3! 26. Qc3 Qxc3 27. Rxc3 Rd6 28. Rc7 Be4 {Black is a pawn down at the moment but seriously pressing. The rook is penetrating to d2, while the white knight is dominated by the bishop.}) 26. Qb8+ {Now the game is going to end in a spectacular draw.} Kg7 27. Qg8+ Kh6 28. Qf8+ Kh5 29. Rc5+ g5 30. Rxg5+! Kxg5 (30... Kh4 31. g3+ Kh3? {would lose to a long sequence of checks.} (31... Kxg5 32. Qg7+ Rg6 33. Qe5+ {leads to the same perpetual as in the game. The inclusion of the move g2-g3 is of no significance.}) 32. Rh5+ Kg4 33. Qg7+! (33. Rh4+?? Kf3 {and Black is mating!}) 33... Kxh5 34. g4+ Kh4 35. Qxf6+ Kh3 36. Qh6+ Kxg4 37. Qf4+ Kh5 38. Qxf7+ Kh6 39. Qf6+ Kh5 40. Qe5+! Kh6 41. Nf5+ Kg6 42. Qe6+ Kg5 43. Qh6+ Kg4 44. h3+ Kxf5 45. e4+ {winning the queen.}) 31. Qg7+ Rg6 32. Qe5+ f5 (32... Kh6 33. Nf5+ Kh5 34. Ng3+ Kh6 35. Nf5+ {is another version of perpetual check.}) 33. h4+ Kh5 34. Qxf5+ Kh6 35. Qf8+ Kh5 36. Qf5+ Kh6 37. Qf8+ Kh5 38. Qf5+ {[%mdl 64] Great game by both players!} 1/2-1/2

