1. d4 g6 2. c4 Bg7 3. e4 d6 4. Nf3 e5 5. Nc3 ({It's too early to swap queens:} 5. dxe5 dxe5 6. Qxd8+ Kxd8 7. Nc3 c6 {Later on Black puts the king on c7 and has no problems. Don't believe your engine that shows an advantage for White here, this position is equal.}) 5... Nc6 (5... exd4 6. Nxd4 Nc6 {is another story.} ({or} 6... Ne7)) 6. Bg5 {Provoking some sort of a concession.} (6. d5 Nce7 7. Be2 f5 8. exf5 Nxf5 (8... gxf5? {is over optimistic. After} 9. Ng5 {I managed to win against a strong opponent rather quickly:} Nf6 10. Bh5+ Kf8? 11. Bf7! h6 12. Ne6+ Bxe6 13. Bxe6 $18 {1-0 (27) Postny,E (2637)-Delchev,A (2622) Haguenau 2013}) 9. O-O Nf6 {leads to one of the typical structures. I believe that White should be slightly better, though it won't be easy to establish a blockade on e4 without letting black's knight get to d4.}) 6... f6 (6... Nf6? {loses material by force after} 7. dxe5 dxe5 8. Qxd8+ Nxd8 9. Bxf6 Bxf6 10. Nd5 $18 {[%csl Rc7,Rf6]}) 7. Be3 Nh6 {The knight goes to f7, which is in harmony with Black's previous move.} 8. h3 (8. dxe5 dxe5 9. Qxd8+ Kxd8 10. O-O-O+ Bd7 11. h3 Kc8 12. c5 Nf7 13. Bc4 Ncd8 14. b4 c6 15. Nd2 Be6 16. Be2 Bh6 17. Bxh6 Nxh6 18. Nc4 Ndf7 19. a4 Kc7 $11 {0-1 (60) Karpov,A (2725)-Seirawan,Y (2600) Roquebrune 1992}) 8... Nf7 9. c5?! {A standad break, but too early in this case.} ({The simple} 9. Be2 {looks like a better try for an opening advantage.} O-O 10. O-O f5 (10... Bd7 11. c5! exd4 12. Nxd4 dxc5 13. Nxc6 Bxc6 14. Bxc5 Nd6 15. Qb3+ Kh8 16. Bb5! $14 {½-½ (56) Keres,P-Spassky,B Moscow 1965}) 11. dxe5 dxe5 12. Qxd8 Ncxd8 (12... Rxd8 13. Nd5 fxe4 14. Nd2 $14) 13. Nd5 fxe4 14. Nd2 Ne6 15. Nxe4 Nf4 16. Bf3 Nxd5 17. cxd5 $14 {½-½ (70) Bhat,V (2483)-Nakamura,H (2697) Mainz 2008}) (9. d5 {is always an option, of course. However, it seems that White can benefit more if keeps the centre flexible, at least for a while.}) 9... dxc5 10. dxe5 (10. dxc5 Qxd1+ 11. Rxd1 Be6 $11) 10... Nfxe5 11. Nxe5 fxe5 {Massive simplifications to follow. Black has very little to worry about. All this has already appeared before.} 12. Bb5 (12. Bxc5 Qxd1+ 13. Rxd1 Bd7 14. Bc4 O-O-O $11 {½-½ (17) Petkov,V (2480)-Jovanovic,Z (2543) Sibenik 2006}) 12... Bd7 (12... Qxd1+ 13. Rxd1 Bd7 14. Bxc5 O-O-O 15. O-O Nd4 $11 {1-0 (36) Kortschnoj,V (2665)-Kagan,S (2465) Beersheba 1978}) 13. Bxc6 {Anish decided to play it safe, not allowing the knight togo to d4 by simply eliminating it.} (13. Qd5 Nd4 14. Bxd7+ Qxd7 15. Qxb7 O-O (15... Nc2+? {is just a waste of time, as the knight will have to go back to d4 soon.} 16. Ke2 Rd8 17. Rhd1 Nd4+ 18. Kf1 $16) 16. O-O Rfb8 17. Qa6 Rxb2 18. Rab1 Rb6 19. Qc4+ Qe6 20. Qxc5 Rab8 {with a balanced position.}) 13... Bxc6 14. Bxc5 Bf8 {The bishop could achieve nothing from g7.} 15. Bxf8 (15. Be3 Qxd1+ 16. Rxd1 Bd6 {followed by castling to the queenside is also equal.}) 15... Rxf8 16. O-O (16. Qg4 Qf6 (16... Qe7 {is also fairly safe.}) 17. O-O-O Rd8 18. Rxd8+ Kxd8 19. Rd1+ Ke7 {White doesn't have enough resources to harass black's king. It's important that White's knight is restricted.}) 16... Qxd1 17. Rfxd1 Rd8 {The game quickly simplified into an equal endgame.} 18. Rxd8+ Kxd8 19. Rd1+ Ke7 20. Nd5+ Bxd5 21. Rxd5 Ke6 22. f3 c6 23. Rd3 Rf7 24. Kf2 Rd7 {The exchange of rooks doesn't change the evaluation of the position as drawish.} 25. Rxd7 Kxd7 26. h4 c5 27. g3 b5 28. b3 Ke6 29. Ke3 h6 30. f4 h5 1/2-1/2

