1. e4 {[%emt 0:00:00]} e5 {[%emt 0:00:00]} 2. Nf3 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Nf6 {[%emt 0:00:00]} 3. Nxe5 {[%emt 0:00:00]} d6 {[%emt 0:00:00]} 4. Nf3 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Nxe4 {[%emt 0:00:00]} 5. Qe2 {[%emt 0:00:00] A minor choice, generally renowned for leading to a drawish ending.'} Qe7 {[%emt 0:00:00]} 6. d3 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Nf6 {[%emt 0:00:00]} 7. Nc3 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Qxe2+ {[%emt 0:06:40]} 8. Bxe2 {[%emt 0:00:00]} g6 {[%emt 0:00:12]} ({On schematic grounds, I might have preferred} 8... c6 {[%csl Rc3] to close off all options of b5 and 'dominate' the c3-knight. The followup could then be ...d5 and ...g4, with the idea of trying to exchange the light-squared bishop for anything, and exchange the pair of dark-squared bishops.}) 9. Nd4 {[%emt 0:05:37]} a6 {[%emt 0:04:25]} 10. Bf4 {[%emt 0:05:06] Now this pressures the d6-pawn, so as to prevent ...c6 following a fianchetto. Black has little choice but to play ...c6 one way or another, and he will be left with doubled pawns.'} Bg7 {[%emt 0:00:33]} (10... Nbd7 {is the maximalist move, trying to prevent f3 altogether.}) 11. h3 {[%emt 0:00:43]} Bd7 {[%emt 0:06:15]} 12. Bf3 {[%emt 0:00:36]} Nc6 {[%emt 0:00:15]} 13. Nxc6 {[%emt 0:00:14]} Bxc6 {[%emt 0:00:05]} 14. Bxc6+ {[%emt 0:00:13]} bxc6 {[%emt 0:00:06]} 15. O-O-O {[%emt 0:00:50]} Kd7 {[%emt 0:06:33] Objectively, there is nothing whatsoever wrong with Black's position, and as long as one doesn't get carried away with premonitions of White kings appearing on a5 after the rooks come off (echoes of Rubinstein) then it is also easy enough to play.'} 16. Rhe1 {[%emt 0:01:14]} Rhe8 {[%emt 0:04:33]} 17. Ne4 {[%emt 0:00:42]} (17. Bd2 {[%cal Ge1e8,Gd1e1] may have been wisest, focusing on the task of exchanging all the rooks as quickly as possible and then placing pawns on light squares.}) 17... Nd5 {[%emt 0:07:57]} (17... Nxe4 18. dxe4 Re6 19. f3 Rae8 20. Bd2 {[%cal Gc2c4,Gc1c2] is also fine but now White's moves are definitely easier to find, starting with c4, Kc2, b3.}) 18. Bd2 {[%emt 0:00:11]} f5 {[%emt 0:02:42]} 19. Ng5 {[%emt 0:00:11]} h6 {[%emt 0:00:09]} 20. Nf3 {[%emt 0:00:09]} g5 {[%emt 0:04:07]} 21. c3 {[%emt 0:02:59]} c5 {[%emt 0:07:56]} 22. Kc2 {[%emt 0:02:16]} Bf6 {[%emt 0:00:30]} ({The computer wants} 22... a5 {but it can never be good to allow this pawn to be fixed.}) 23. Rxe8 {[%emt 0:01:08]} Rxe8 {[%emt 0:01:29]} 24. Re1 {[%emt 0:00:11]} Rf8 {[%emt 0:05:21]} 25. Rh1 {[%emt 0:01:51]} Re8 {[%emt 0:06:55]} 26. g3 {[%emt 0:01:59]} g4 {[%emt 0:09:09]} 27. Ng1 {[%emt 0:00:38]} Bg5 {[%emt 0:00:14]} 28. Kd1 {[%emt 0:06:20]} a5 {[%emt 0:11:46]} 29. Ne2 {[%emt 0:02:30] One of several acceptable moves.} (29. a4 Rb8! $15 {was the point and White is being stretched a bit thin.}) 29... a4 {[%emt 0:00:13]} (29... Rb8!? 30. b3 a4 31. Bxg5 hxg5 {is a little bit testing, perhaps}) 30. Bxg5 {[%emt 0:05:53]} hxg5 {[%emt 0:00:15]} 31. hxg4 {[%emt 0:00:09]} fxg4 {[%emt 0:00:04]} 32. Rh5 {[%emt 0:00:22]} Re5 {[%emt 0:01:40]} 33. Rh7+ {[%emt 0:00:31]} Ke6 {[%emt 0:07:59]} 34. a3 {[%emt 0:13:54]} (34. d4 {screams out to be played, but there is nothing much going on here either:} Rf5 35. c4 Nb6 36. b3 axb3 37. axb3 Rf3 $11 {Nevertheless, this was marginally safer for White than the game.}) 34... Rf5 {[%emt 0:00:09]} 35. c4 {[%emt 0:02:06]} Nf6 {[%emt 0:05:21]} 36. Rh2 {[%emt 0:00:45] It looks slightly dangerous to a human to allow ...xf2, because ...d7-e5 with pressure will follow soon.'} Rf3 {[%emt 0:00:52]} 37. Kc2 {[%emt 0:00:51]} Nd7 {[%emt 0:00:45]} 38. d4 {[%emt 0:05:54] Else White is in very obvious difficulties.} Nb6 {[%emt 0:00:17]} (38... Rf5 {is a sensible engine suggestion; this keeps a bit of pressure by not clarifying the situation of the d4-pawn.} 39. Kd3 (39. Rh7 Nb6 40. dxc5 Rxc5 $36) 39... Rf7 {An engine move; what was your first clue?} 40. Rh6+?! Ke7 41. Rh2 cxd4 42. Nxd4 Ne5+ 43. Kc3 c5 $15) 39. dxc5 {[%emt 0:00:42]} dxc5 {[%emt 0:00:07]} 40. Nc3 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Ke5 (40... Ke5 {[%emt 0:00:00]} {Presumably the expected continuation was:} 41. Nd1 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Nxc4 42. Rh5 Kd4 43. Rxg5 Ne5 44. Rg7 {when White is completely out of the woods.}) 1/2-1/2

