1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 Na5 5. Nge2 Be7 ({In the last and decisive game of an old Candidates' quarter-final, Black hurried with the exchange of bishops exchange, thus offering White a wider choice at a later moment.} 5... Nxc4 6. dxc4 Be7 7. O-O d6 {Now, 8.f4 would transpose to our game, but Larsen thought} 8. b3 {to be more flexible and he eventually proved to be right, at least from a practical point of view, see Larsen,B-Portisch,L/Porec 1968/Candidates/[ChessBase] (1-0, 28)}) 6. O-O d6 7. f4 ({With the bishop alive, it would make little sense to bury it with} 7. b3?) 7... Nxc4 8. dxc4 c6 9. Kh1 O-O $146 {Not fearing the kingside attack.} ({I did not find anything wrong with the more cautious} 9... h5 10. f5 h4 11. Qd3 Bd7 12. h3 Qc7 13. Be3 O-O-O {Zinn,L (2410)-Kovacs,L (2385)/Decin 1975/EXT 2003 (1/2, 21)}) 10. f5 {If allowed to play g4-g5, White would get a very strong attack. With the d5-square under firm control of the white pawns, a breakthrough seems to be difficult to cary out, but Carlsen thought differently.} b5!? 11. cxb5 d5 12. exd5 (12. bxc6 d4 13. Na4 Nxe4 $17) 12... cxd5 13. Bg5 Bb7 $44 {A brief glance at the position reveals Black's compensation. However, the ulterior course of the game proves that White's position is easier to handle, since Black cannot advance any of his pawns without giving away part of his dynamic potential.} 14. Ng3 h6 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Qe2 Qe7 17. Rad1 Rad8 18. Nh5 e4 19. Nxf6+ ({It would have made some sense to keep the tension. The h5-knight may cause some troubles still, for instance with xg7, while the f6-bishop is not going to run away.} 19. Qg4!?) 19... Qxf6 20. Na4 Ba8 (20... Bc8!? {[%csl Gf5]}) 21. c3 d4 22. cxd4 Rxd4 23. Nc3 Rxd1 24. Nxd1 Rd8 25. Qe3 a6 26. a4 axb5 27. axb5 Rd3 28. Qa7 Bd5 29. Nc3 e3 30. Nxd5 Rxd5 31. Qxe3 Qxb2 32. Qe8+ Kh7 33. Qxf7 Qxb5 34. Qg6+ Kg8 35. Ra1 Rxf5 36. Ra8+ Rf8 37. Rxf8+ Kxf8 38. h3 1/2-1/2

