1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 c6 5. Bg2 d5 {Black indicates that he is fine with a draw, by entering the infamous ...c6, ...d5 setup against the Fianchetto KID.} 6. Nbd2 (6. O-O O-O 7. cxd5 cxd5 8. Nc3 {brings us to 'the big position', where Black decides between, in the first instance, ...c6, ...e6 or ...e4. My personal favourite, and the most commonly played, is ...e6.} e6 9. Ne5 {This is most testing.} (9. b3 Nc6 10. Ne5 (10. Ba3 Re8 11. e3 Qa5 12. Bb2 Bd7) 10... Nxe5 11. dxe5 Ng4 12. e4) (9. Bf4 Nc6 10. Ne5 Nh5 $132) 9... Bd7!? {Used in a previous Mamedyarov game.} (9... Nfd7 {is a common choice, inviting the repetition. An independent line now is:} 10. f4! Nxe5!? (10... Nc6 11. b3! Ndxe5 (11... Re8 12. Ba3 Qa5 13. Bb2 Ne7 14. Qd3 {is also nice for White.}) 12. dxe5 f6 13. exf6 Bxf6 14. Bb2 $14) 11. fxe5 f6 12. e4!? {A new idea of mine, as far as I know.} fxe5 13. Rxf8+ Qxf8 14. exd5 exd4 (14... exd5 15. Nxd5 Nc6 $14) 15. Ne4 exd5 16. Ng5! h6 (16... Nc6? 17. Bxd5+ Kh8 18. Nf7+ Kg8 19. Qf3 h5 20. Bf4 $32) 17. Bxd5+ Kh8 18. Nf7+ Kh7 19. Bxh6! Bxh6 20. Nxh6 Qxh6 21. Qxd4 $40 {Black has to find a series of precise moves to survive.}) 10. Bg5 h6 11. Bf4 Nc6 12. Qd2 Kh7 13. Rfd1 Nh5 14. e4 Nxf4 15. Qxf4 dxe4 16. Nxe4 Qe7 17. h4 Rad8! $11 {Mamedyarov,S-Artemiev,V Moscow 2017}) 6... a5 7. b3 a4 8. Ba3 axb3!? {I don't always understand why Black players are so keen to resolve this tension, because the pawn on a4 is so rarely edible.} (8... O-O 9. O-O Ne4 $132) 9. axb3 O-O 10. O-O Re8 11. e3 ({The most direct way is} 11. Bb2 Na6 12. Ne5 Bf5 13. f3 {but here Black can take care of himself, for instance after} Qb6 $11 {. Therefore White leaves the bishop on a3, which represents a form of tension.}) 11... Bf5 12. Qe2 {How very subtle.} Na6 {This move tries to be too careful, and gives White a couple of interesting possibilities for play over the next 5 moves or so.} (12... Nbd7 {was also possible, but maybe Black was concerned about} 13. cxd5 cxd5 14. Qb5 $36 {and White has a brief burst of activity.}) 13. Ne5 Ne4 14. cxd5 cxd5 15. Nxe4 (15. Rfc1!?) 15... Bxe4 16. Bxe4 (16. Qb5!?) 16... dxe4 17. Qb5 Rb8 18. Qc4 Bxe5! $11 {Fully equalising the game, even if this is not obvious yet.} 19. dxe5 Qd3 20. Rac1 (20. Qxd3 exd3 21. Rad1 b5!! {is one of the points, even if it doesn't get any objective advantage after} 22. Bb2 $11) 20... Qxc4 21. Rxc4 b5 {As in the previous note, Black targets b3.} 22. Rc6 (22. Rxe4 b4 23. Bb2 Nc5 24. Rc4 Nxb3 25. Rd1 Rec8! $15) 22... Ra8 23. Rfc1 Nb8 24. Rc8 Kf8 25. Bb4 Na6 26. Rxa8 Rxa8 27. Ba5 Ke8 28. Rc6 Kd7 29. Rb6 Nc5 30. b4 (30. Rxb5 {requires either superhuman calculation or an appetite for suffering.} Nxb3 31. e6+! (31. Rxb3 Rxa5 $15 {is certainly drawable, but definitely will not be fun.}) 31... Kxe6 32. Bb4 Nc1 33. Rb6+ {Black cannot escape with the pawn, but if you saw this position from afar, you would still be a lot more concerned than the engine is about White's survival chances after} Kd7 34. Rb7+ Kc6 35. Rxe7 Nd3 36. Bc3 f5!? $13) 30... Nd3 31. e6+ (31. Rxb5 Kc6 32. Rb6+ Kd5 33. Rb7! {White keeps enough activity.}) 31... fxe6 32. Rxb5 Rc8 33. Rb7+ Kd6 34. b5 Rc1+ 35. Kg2 Rc2 36. Kg1 {A sign that the position has become practically difficult, and indeed, inaccuracies begin to creep in.} g5 37. Rb8 Rb2 (37... e5!?) 38. Rd8+ Kc5 39. b6 Rb5 40. Kg2?! (40. g4 {still keeps the game level, because Black doesn't have any easy mating configurations.}) 40... g4 41. h3 h5 42. hxg4 hxg4 {Now it is a little bit troublesome to draw this.} 43. Bc3 e5 44. Rg8 Kc4 {One way to initiate the last roll of the dice.} (44... Rxb6!? 45. Rxg4 Rb3 46. Ba5 Rb2 47. Rxe4 Rxf2+ 48. Kg1 Re2 $15 {White still has quite significant issues to surmount.}) 45. Rc8+ Kd5 46. Rd8+ Kc5 47. Rc8+ ({Why not} 47. Rg8 {?}) 47... Kxb6 48. Rb8+ Kc5 49. Rxb5+ {Clearly, White backed his intuition that this endgame would be drawn.} Kxb5 50. Kf1 Ka4 51. Ke2 e6 52. Kf1 Kb3 53. Ba5 Kc2 54. Ke2 Nc1+ 55. Ke1 Nd3+ (55... Kd3 $11 {is possible, but Black doesn't get anywhere near the progress that he needs, which is, very simply, forcing the White king to f1 at such a moment that ...d1/d2 is then possible.}) 56. Ke2 Nc1+ 57. Ke1 Nd3+ 58. Ke2 Nc1+ 1/2-1/2

