1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. Bg5 Nbd7 6. e3 Qa5 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Rc1 {This rare continuation has recently become quite popular.} Nxc3 ({White meets} 8... Bb4 {with} 9. a3 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 {sacrificing his a3-pawn but getting good compensation: the bishop pair and a strong centre.}) 9. bxc3 Ba3 (9... Qxa2 10. Bd3 Bd6 11. O-O Qa5 12. c4 $44 {E.Postny}) 10. Rc2 b6 (10... O-O 11. Bd3 e5 {is another way to activate the c8-bishop. In the following game Black obtained a decent position:} 12. O-O Re8 13. e4 Be7 14. Be3 Qc7 15. h3 b5 16. Nh2 Bb7 17. f4 c5! 18. fxe5 c4 19. Be2 Bxe4 20. Rb2 Qc6 $13 {1/2 (51) Vovk,Y (2608)-Antipov,M (2488) Riga 2013}) 11. Be2 (11. Bd3 {is more common but a way to pose any problems to Black is yet to be found:} Ba6 12. O-O Bxd3 13. Qxd3 O-O 14. c4 (14. e4 Rfe8 15. e5?! (15. Bf4 $142 {E.Postny}) 15... h6 16. Bh4 c5 17. Nd2 cxd4 18. cxd4 Rac8! 19. Nc4 Qb5 20. f4 Rc7 21. Qxa3 Rxc4 22. Rxc4 Qxc4 23. Bf2 Qc7 24. Rc1 Qb7 25. Qd6 Nf8 $11 {0-1 (57) Gelfand,B (2740)-Carlsen,M (2872) London 2013 CBM 154 [Postny,E]}) (14. Rb1 Rfe8 15. Bh4 Rac8 16. c4 Bd6 17. Ng5 Qf5 18. Qxf5 exf5 19. Bg3 Bxg3 20. hxg3 c5 21. Nf3 Nf6 22. Kf1 Ne4 $11 {1/2 (85) Moiseenko,A (2703)-Dreev,A (2679) Jakarta 2013}) 14... h6 15. Bh4 (15. Bf4!? {E.Postny}) 15... Rfe8 16. Nd2 (16. e4 e5 17. d5 Nc5 18. Qe2 Qa4 $11 {1/2 (38) Malakhatko,V (2548)-Michiels,B (2489) Antwerp 2011}) (16. Rd1!? {E.Postny}) 16... Be7 17. Bxe7 Rxe7 18. c5 bxc5 19. Nb3 Qa4 {1/2 (19) Cmilyte,V (2525)-Gurevich,M (2596) Konya 2012} 20. Nxc5 Nxc5 21. Rxc5 Qxa2 22. Rxc6 a5 $11) 11... Ba6 12. O-O Bxe2 13. Qxe2 O-O {White has a pawn advantage in the centre but it should not worry Black too much as he has good development and prospects of counterplay with ...e6-e5 or ...c6-c5.} 14. e4 $146 (14. Rd1 Rfe8 (14... e5!?) 15. Bf4 (15. e4 Rac8 16. Bf4 {preventing ...e6-e5} c5 17. Nd2 b5 (17... cxd4 $142 18. Nc4 Qa6 $11) 18. dxc5 Nxc5 19. Nb1 e5 20. Bg3 a6 21. c4 b4 22. Rd5 Qa4 23. f3 b3 24. axb3 Qxb3 25. Nxa3 Qxa3 26. Bf2 Ne6 27. g3 $14 {1/2 (61) Cmilyte,V (2515)-Dreev,A (2673) Caleta ENG 2014}) 15... Rac8 16. c4 Be7 17. e4 {looks formidable but Black finds a way to create counterplay:} Qa4 18. h3 Nf8 19. Be3 Ng6 20. g3 c5 21. d5 exd5 22. Rxd5 Bf6 23. Qd3 Ne7 24. Rd6 Nc6 25. a3 Be5 26. Rd5 Bc7 27. Kg2 Ne7 28. Rd7 Nc6 29. Rd5 Ne7 30. Rd7 Nc6 {1/2 (30) Tomashevsky,E (2697)-Dreev,A (2670) Loo RUS 2014}) (14. Bf4!? {- this move order is yet to be tried.}) 14... Rac8?! {I don't like this standard move. What's its idea? After ...c6-c5 White plays d4-d5 anyway, and the c-file remains closed.} (14... c5 15. d5 $36) (14... Rfe8!? {[%cal Ge8e2,Yc6c5]}) 15. e5 {[%mdl 32] The same plan as in Gelfand - Carlsen (see above) but White's queen is placed on e2 better than on d3.} Qa4 {Black prevents f3-d2 (-c4 or e4).} (15... c5? 16. d5! exd5 17. e6 fxe6 18. Qxe6+ Rf7 19. Ne5 $18 {E.Gleizerov}) 16. c4 Rfe8 ({Perhaps it was slightly more accurate to play} 16... h6 {first:} 17. Bh4 (17. Be3 Rfd8 {[%cal Yd7f8]}) 17... Rfe8 {[%cal Yd7f8,Yf8g6]}) 17. Rd1 c5 ({Probably it was better to wait:} 17... Nf8 {But that means that the initiative belongs to White.}) 18. d5 {[%csl Ra3]} exd5 19. Rxd5 (19. cxd5! {was more energetic:} Nxe5 20. Nxe5 Rxe5 21. Qxe5 Qxc2 22. Re1 $44 {[%csl Gd5] E.Gleizerov}) 19... Nf8 (19... Nb8 $142 {[%cal Yb8c6] to keep the e5-pawn under fire (recommended by E.Gleizerov). The position remains unclear in that case.}) 20. h4 h6 21. Be3 Ng6?! {This position of the knight is unsafe in view of h4-h5.} (21... Rc7 $142 {[%cal Yc7e7] The knight can pick its way out later on.}) 22. Qd3! {Preventing ...g6-e7-f5.} Re6 (22... Nf8 {(admitting his mistake)} 23. h5 $36 {[%cal Yf3h4,Yh4f5]} Rcd8!? {was probably a better possibility.}) 23. h5 Ne7 24. Rd6 {Carlsen plays in a positional manner. The rook exchange on e6 weakens Black's pawn structure.} (24. Rd8+ {was better but required accurate calculation:} Rxd8 25. Qxd8+ Kh7 26. Rd2 Bc1 (26... Qxc4? 27. Bxh6! gxh6 28. Qf8 {[%cal Rf8f7,Rd2d8]}) 27. Rd7 Bxe3 28. fxe3 Qxa2 29. Rxe7 Rxe7 30. Qxe7 Qxc4 31. Qxa7 b5 32. Qa6 {, and White should win as} Qc1+ 33. Kh2 b4 {doesn't work due to} 34. Qd3+ Kg8 35. e6! fxe6 36. Qd8+ Kh7 37. Ne5 {[%cal Re5g6]}) 24... Bb4 25. Rc1 Re8 26. Rxe6 fxe6 27. Nh4 {[%cal Yg2g4,Yg4g5,Yh4g6]} Qc6 (27... Qxa2? 28. Qd7 Kf7 29. Ng6 {[%cal Rg6f4]} Qa6 30. Rd1 {[%cal Yd1d3,Ye3h6,Yd3f3]} Qc8 31. Qxa7 $18 {[%cal Yd1d7] E.Gleizerov}) 28. a3 Ba5 29. Rd1 Qc7 30. Ng6 Nxg6 31. Qxg6 Qf7 32. Rd3! {Preventing ...a5-c3. White is practically a piece up.} a6 (32... Qxg6 33. hxg6 Kf8 34. Kf1 Ke7 35. Bf4! {[%cal Yf4g3,Yg3h4] , and Black is completely helpless}) 33. a4 Rf8 34. g4 {The advance of the kingside pawns decides the game.} Qe8?! 35. Rd6 Qxa4 (35... Qxg6 36. hxg6 Re8 37. f4 $18) 36. Qxe6+ Kh8 37. Bxh6! {White accurately calculates that Black's subsequent rook sacrifice doesn't give him a perpetual.} Qa1+ 38. Kg2 Rxf2+ 39. Kxf2 Qe1+ 40. Kg2 Qe4+ 41. Kh3 Qh1+ 42. Kg3 Qe1+ (42... Be1+ 43. Kf4 Qh2+ 44. Kg5 Bh4+ 45. Kg6 Qc2+ 46. Qf5 $18) 43. Kf4 Bd2+ 44. Rxd2 Qxd2+ 45. Kf5 gxh6 46. Qe8+ Kg7 47. Qe7+ (47. Qe7+ Kg8 48. Kg6 Qd3+ 49. Kxh6 Qe3+ 50. g5 $18) 1-0

