1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 d5 5. Nf3 dxc4 6. Bg5 b5 {Carlsen needs to look for positions, that are full of life even with the black pieces. His title as champion and fantastic rating oblige him to win many games even against very strong opponents.} 7. a4 c6 8. g3 {Mamedyarov keeps his cool and develops the bishop in style of the Catalan Opening. His choice is certainly sensible, since there is no direct refutation of the daring idea used by Black.} (8. axb5 cxb5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Qe4 (10. e3 Bb7 11. b3 Nd7 12. bxc4 bxc4 13. Nd2 a5 14. Bxc4 Rg8 15. d5 Nb6 16. Qxh7 Rf8 17. Bb5+ Ke7 18. Qd3 Nxd5 19. Na2 Bxd2+ 20. Qxd2 Qb6 21. Rb1 $11 {0-1 (33) Ballesteros Perez,F-Hernandez Onna,R (2445) Las Palmas 1991}) 10... Qd5 11. Qg4 Nd7 12. g3 Bb7 13. Bg2 Qd6 14. O-O Bxc3 15. bxc3 a6 16. e4 h5 17. Qh4 O-O-O 18. Rfd1 Kb8 19. Ne1 f5 20. e5 Qc7 $11 {0-1 (68) Postl,A (2315)-Lerner,K (2605) Graz 1995}) 8... Bb7 9. Bg2 Nbd7 10. O-O Qb6 {Black is playing with fire as the opponent has excellent pieces and a slight development advantage. On the other hand Black's advantage of an extrae pawn can play a significant role later in the game.} 11. e4 a6 (11... Bxc3 12. bxc3 O-O 13. a5 Qc7 14. Bf4 Qc8 15. Bd6 c5 16. e5 Nd5 17. Ng5 g6 18. a6 Bxa6 19. dxc5 Bb7 20. Ne4 Re8 21. Qd2 f5 22. exf6 N5xf6 $11 {1/2 (50) Wilhelmi,C (2325)-Arnold,J (2190) Budapest 1996}) (11... O-O 12. Be3 Bxc3 13. bxc3 c5 14. dxc5 Nxc5 15. Nd2 a6 16. Rab1 Qc6 17. Nxc4 bxc4 18. Bxc5 Qxc5 19. Rxb7 Rab8 20. Rfb1 Rxb7 21. Rxb7 Ng4 $11 {0-1 (32) Costello,C (2070)-Petersen,S (2165) Copenhagen 1996}) 12. Rfd1 {White deserves credit for playing very sensibly in the virtually unknown line. The pieces are taking the optimal squares and their activity should promise compensation for the missing material.} (12. Be3 O-O 13. Ne5 Rad8 14. Rad1 c5 15. axb5 axb5 16. dxc5 Bxc5 17. Nxd7 Nxd7 18. Bg5 f6 19. Bf4 Ne5 20. Bxe5 fxe5 21. Rxd8 Rxd8 $17 {0-1 (28) Sajn,S (1896)-Kozamernik,J (2172) Bled 2001}) 12... h6 13. Be3 (13. Bf4 O-O 14. Na2 Be7 15. Nc3 Rad8 $15) 13... O-O 14. d5 {When all the forces are mobilised all good players know that waiting can only help the adversary. Mamedyarov is opening the direct fight with a logical shot in the centre.} (14. Ne5 c5 15. Na2 Ba5 16. dxc5 Nxc5 17. axb5 axb5 18. Nxc4 (18. b4? cxb3 19. Qxc5 Qxc5 20. Bxc5 Rfd8 $19) 18... bxc4 19. Qxc4 Bxe4 20. Bxc5 Qc6 $15) 14... c5 {World champions are also only human and have to tread carefully in questions of risk. The more ambitious move 14...c5!? was probably deemed to be too messy, even though Black would keep somewhat better chances.} (14... Bc5!? 15. dxe6 fxe6 16. e5 Nd5 17. Bf4 Bc8 18. Qd2 b4 19. Ne4 c3 $15) 15. a5 (15. dxe6 Qxe6 16. Nd5 Nxd5 17. exd5 Qf6 $15) 15... Qd8 {Mamedyarov is well known as a player who always carries a sharp knife in his bag. It makes no sense to provoke the tiger with the unseemly move 15...xa5.} (15... Bxa5 16. dxe6 fxe6 17. e5 Nd5 18. Bxh6! Bxc3 19. Qg6 Rf7 20. Ng5 Nxe5 21. Qh7+ Kf8 22. bxc3 Nf6 23. Bxg7+ Rxg7 24. Qh8+ Ng8 25. Bxb7 $11) 16. dxe6 (16. Nh4 exd5 17. exd5 Ne5 $17) 16... fxe6 17. Nh4 Bxc3 {The knight on c3 is eliminated as it might possibly head to the centre and join the forces heading towards black king.} (17... Qc7 18. Bf4 e5 19. Bd2 Bxc3 20. Bxc3 $11) 18. bxc3 Qe8 (18... Qc7 19. Bf4 e5 20. Bc1 Rfe8 21. Nf5 Nf8 $13) 19. f4 Rd8 {Carlsen is not worried about the seeming passivity of his army, it is more important to defend the critical squares like g6.} (19... Ng4 20. Bc1 Qf7 21. Rd6 Rad8 22. h3 Ngf6 23. Be3 $11) 20. h3 (20. Rd6 e5 21. Re1 $11) 20... Rf7 21. Rd6 (21. g4 e5 22. f5 Nf8 23. Bxc5 Rfd7 24. Rxd7 Qxd7 $15) 21... e5 22. f5?! {With his faulty decision Mamedyarov chooses the road to perdition. It was not necessary to tempt the collapse, but White was probaby not suspecting the level of danger.} (22. Re1! Nh5 23. Qd2 Rc8 (23... Nxg3 24. fxe5 Qxe5 25. Ng6 Qe8 26. e5 Bxg2 27. Qxg2 Nf5 28. Bf2 $11) 24. Kh2 $11) 22... Nf8! 23. Bxc5? {[%mdl 2048] White could not resist the temptation to restore material equality on the board. Unfortunately this strategy helps to improve the black pieces and they will spring to life with astonishing force. It was necessary to look for ways to limit the damage.} (23. Rad1 Rxd6 24. Rxd6 Qc8 25. Ng6 Nxg6 26. fxg6 Rd7 27. Qd2 Qc7 28. Rxd7 Qxd7 29. Qxd7 Nxd7 30. h4 Kf8 31. Bh3 Ke8 32. Bxd7+ Kxd7 33. Bxc5 (33. Bxh6? b4 $17) 33... Ke6 34. Kf2 Bxe4 $15) 23... Rxd6 24. Bxd6 Rd7 25. Bxf8 {If every step leads in the wrong direction the result may be somewhat depressing. The last chance for White to put up resistance was tied to the move 25.b4.} (25. Bb4 N8h7 (25... Rd3 26. Re1 Rxg3 27. Qf2 Rd3 $17) 26. Ng6 Ng5 27. Qf2 Rd3 28. h4 Ngxe4 29. Qb6 Bd5 $17) 25... Qxf8 26. Rd1 (26. Qf2 Bxe4 27. Ng6 Qe8 28. Bf1 Nd5 29. Rc1 Qb8 $17) 26... Qc5+ 27. Kh2 (27. Kh1 Qe3! (27... Bxe4 28. Bxe4 Rxd1+ 29. Qxd1 Nxe4 30. Qd8+ Kf7 31. Qd7+ Qe7 32. Qd5+ $18) 28. Rxd7 Nxd7 29. Qd1 Qd3 $19) (27. Kf1 Bxe4 28. Bxe4 Rxd1+ 29. Qxd1 Nxe4 30. Qd8+ Kh7 31. Qb6 Qc8 32. Kg2 Qd7 $17) 27... Bxe4! {Black would at any time gladly pay top dollar for the ideal coordination of his forces as in this position. The only problem that has to be dealt with is the counterplay against his own king.} (27... Bxe4 28. Bxe4 Rxd1 29. Qxd1 Nxe4 30. Ng2 (30. Qd8+ Kh7 31. Ng6 Qf2+ $19) 30... Kh7 31. Qc2 Qd5 32. Ne1 Nf6 $19) 0-1

