1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 dxc4 {Magnus Carlsen has had to face the Vienna Variation of the Queen's Gambit several times in 2019. In each game he came up with a different poisonous idea, which he had probably all prepared for his World Championship match against Fabiano Caruana.} 5. e4 Bb4 6. Bg5 ({In Wijk aan Zee, he tried the dangerous pawn sacrifice} 6. Bxc4) 6... c5 7. Bxc4 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 Qa5 10. Bb5+ ({Some months later against Levon Aronian, he came up with the "hit and run" novelty} 10. Bd2) ({Finally, a few weeks before the present game, Carlsen tried} 10. Nb5 Nxe4 11. Qd4 {against the same opponent, and tortured him for quite a long time.}) 10... Bd7 ({Who would like to test the World Champion in the main line} 10... Nbd7 {?}) 11. Bxf6 gxf6 12. Bxd7+ Nxd7 13. O-O Qxc3 ({This has been tried a few times by Alexander Morozevich and Radoslaw Wojtaszek in the past, and more recently by Wei Yi and Alexander Bluebaum. The main move} 13... a6 14. Rb1 {is considered holdable for Black, though not without considerable suffering.}) 14. Qa4 O-O 15. Nxe6 (15. Qxd7?! Rad8 16. Qxb7 Qxd4 $11 {let Black equalize too easily in Leitao-Morozevich, World Teams Championship Bursa 2010}) 15... Nb6 16. Qd4 Rfc8 (16... Qxd4 17. Nxd4 $14 {Antic-Zontakh, Jagodina 1998}) 17. Qxc3 Rxc3 {My own preparation simply ended on an equal sign here. However correct this assessment may objectively be, Magnus Carlsen understands that Black will have to solve practical problems. Indeed, in endgames with rook(s) and knight, activity and king safety are important factors.} 18. Nf4 (18. Nd4 Rc4 19. Rfd1 Na4! $11 {Gajewski,G (2616)-Wojtaszek,R (2706) Warsaw 2012}) 18... Rc5 19. Rfd1 ({Black held the balance after} 19. g4 Kf8 20. Nh5 Ke7 21. Ng3 Rd8 22. Rfc1 Na4 23. Rd1 Nc3 $11 {Ponomariov,R (2758)-Morozevich,A (2737) Saratov 2011}) 19... f5 20. exf5 Rac8 ({I do not quite get Anand's logic behind delaying the capture on f5, as he actually slightly worsens his position compared with the line} 20... Rxf5 21. g3 (21. Rd4 Rc8) 21... Rc8 22. Rd3 Rc2 23. Ra3 a6 $11) 21. f6! Rf5 22. g3 Rxf6 23. Rd3 {Now the difference is clear: the c8 cannot leave the back rank because of d8+ and h5+. This nuance allows White to grab the initiative.} Rfc6 24. Ra3 Rc1+ 25. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 26. Kg2 Nc8 ({After} 26... a6 27. Rb3 Rc6 28. Kf3 $14 {, both black pieces have difficulties to move.}) 27. Rd3 Rc7 28. Rd8+ Kg7 29. h4 Ne7 30. Nh5+ Kh6?! ({It is understandable that Vishy Anand rejected} 30... Kg6 {because of the dangerous line} 31. g4 f5 32. Nf4+ (32. Rd6+ Kf7 33. gxf5 Rc5 34. Rf6+ Kg8 35. Ng3 Ra5 $11) 32... Kf7 33. Rh8 fxg4 (33... Kg7 34. Rb8 Kf7 35. g5 $14) 34. Rxh7+ Kg8 35. Rh5 {. But Black should actually be able to hold after} Rc2 36. Ra5 b6 37. Rxa7 Nf5 $13 {The game continuation is definitely more unpleasant for him.}) 31. Nf6 Kg6?! ({Anand starts drifting and the position gets from unpleasant to worse. Obviously, no one will teach him that activity is paramount in endings. There must be something he did not like in the line} 31... Rc2 32. Rd7 Nf5 33. Ne4 Kg7 {, for instance} 34. Kf3 Rxa2 35. Kf4 Ra5 36. h5 {, but he should certainly have preferred this over the game continuation.}) 32. Ne8 Rc6 33. Rd7 Re6 (33... Nc8 34. f4 $16) 34. h5+! ({Remember what I wrote about activity?} 34. Rxb7 Nc8 35. Nc7 Re7 {paralyzes both white pieces. White can still try to disentangle with} 36. g4 {, but after} h5! {, things are anything but clear.}) 34... Kh6 35. Nd6 f5 (35... Kxh5 36. Nxf7 $16) 36. Nxb7 (36. Rxe7? Rxd6 37. Rxb7 Ra6 $11) 36... Ng8 37. Nd6 Nf6 38. Nxf5+ Kg5 39. Nd4 (39. Ng7 Ra6 {is less convincing}) 39... Nxd7 ({It is always difficult to choose between variations which all lead to a worse position. Anand decides to exchange rooks right away in order to reduce his opponent's activity. The alternative} 39... Ra6 40. f4+! Kxh5 41. Rg7 Rxa2+ 42. Kh3 Ra5 43. Nc6 Kh6 44. Rxh7+ Kxh7 45. Nxa5 {would lead to another knight ending where White is a clear pawn up. Assessing over the board whether it is winning is very difficult. I believe that Anand's feeling was right, and that the game continuation offers better practical chances.}) 40. Nxe6+ Kxh5 41. Kf3 Kg6 42. Kf4?! (42. Ke4 Kf6 43. Nd8 h5 44. Nc6 a6 45. Kd5 Kf5 46. f3 {was perhaps more unpleasant for Black, as his king is restricted.}) 42... Kf6 43. Ng5 h6 44. Ne4+ Ke6 45. Kg4 Ke5 46. f3 Kd4! {This surprising idea might have been underestimated by Carlsen when he chose his 42nd move.} 47. Kf5 (47. Kf4 a5! 48. g4 Ne5 $132) 47... Ke3! 48. f4 h5 49. Ng5 (49. Nf6? Nxf6 50. Kxf6 Kf3 51. Kg5 Kxg3 52. f5 h4 53. f6 h3 54. f7 h2 55. f8=Q h1=Q $11) 49... Nc5 (49... Kf2 50. g4 hxg4 51. Kxg4 Ke3 {is a draw as well.}) 50. Kg6 Kf2 51. f5 Kxg3 52. Ne6!? (52. Kxh5 Kf4 53. Kg6 Nd7 $11) 52... Nd7 53. Kxh5 Kf3 54. Kg5 Ke4 55. Nc7 a5 56. Nb5 Kd3 57. Kg6 a4 58. Kf7 Ne5+ 59. Kf6 Nf3 60. Ke7 Kc2 61. f6 Ng5 (61... Kb2 62. a3 Ng5 $11 {would have been possible as well}) 62. Nd4+ {My engine now proudly shows an evaluation of around +8 ... Evidently a case for programmers to work on! The position is still a draw.} Kb2 63. Ne6 Nh7 64. f7 Kxa2 65. Ng5 a3 66. Nxh7 Kb1 67. f8=Q a2 {Well done, Vishy! The white knight is so far away that this endgame can be evaluated exactly as a queen ending.} 68. Qf5+ Kb2 69. Qe5+ Kb1 70. Qe4+ Kb2 71. Qe2+ Kb1 72. Nf6 a1=Q 73. Nd5 Qg7+ 74. Kd6 Qf8+ 75. Ke5 Qe8+ 76. Kd4 Qxe2 77. Nc3+ Ka1 78. Nxe2 {A formidable battle, where Magnus Carlsen was close to inflicting yet another endgame defeat to Vishy Anand. But this time, the Indian managed to hold a draw. With hindsight, it is hard to point out a moment where White missed a win. Perhaps the alternative on move 42 would have been more difficult to deal with for Black.} 1/2-1/2

