1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 dxc4 {Compared to other QGD positions, the Vienna has been a rather rare guest in supertournaments lately.} 5. e4 Bb4 6. Bg5 c5 7. Bxc4 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 Qa5 10. Nb5 {An interesting sideline.} (10. Bd2 {was almost a novelty when the world champion employed it succesfully against Aronian:} O-O 11. Qe2 e5 12. Nb3 Qc7 {with a complex struggle in: Carlsen,M (2845)-Aronian,L (2763) Karlsruhe/Baden Baden GER 2019, 1-0 (38).}) (10. Bb5+ {it is the most common move with many forced variations to follow.}) 10... Nxe4 11. Qd4 O-O 12. Qxe4 a6 13. O-O (13. Bd3 f5 14. Qe2 axb5 15. O-O {is a more usual move order.}) 13... axb5 14. Bd3 f5 15. Qe2 Nc6 (15... Bd7 {was also played quite often.}) 16. Bd2 Qc7 {The first new move of the game.} (16... e5 17. Bxb5 Be6 18. a4 Qc7) 17. Bxb5 f4 {An interesting move, playing against White's dark-squared bishop while also preparing to enhance his own bishop's scope. However, it implies a strategic risk by handing White the e4 square.} (17... e5 {was a more conservative approach with the idea} 18. f3 Be6 19. Rfe1 (19. a4 Na5 $13 {[%cal Ga5b3]}) 19... e4!) 18. f3 {Obviously White cannot allow f4-f3.} e5 19. Rfe1 Bd7 20. a4! {We can say that the World Champion got the position he wanted from the opening: not so much to begin with, but plenty of play left to outplay his impressive opponent.} (20. Bxc6 {would most likely have called it a day even with Carlsen's fantastic technical abilities:} Bxc6 21. Qxe5 Qf7! {The most precise as Black's queen will be more useful than White's.} (21... Qxe5 {is also possible:} 22. Rxe5 Rad8 {Black also has good drawing chances in view of White's split queenside pawns and the drawing tendencies of the opposite coloured bishops.}) 22. Qe6 Rad8 23. Qxf7+ Rxf7 24. Re2 g5 $44) 20... Kh8 21. Rad1 (21. Bxc6 Bxc6 22. Qxe5 Qxe5 23. Rxe5 Rxa4 24. Rxa4 Bxa4 {would not give many winning chances as the only way to get f4 would be via a rook swap:} 25. Re4 (25. h4 Bc6 26. Rc5 h6 $11 {[%cal Gg7g5]}) 25... Bc6 26. Rxf4 Rxf4 27. Bxf4 Kg8 {with a trivial draw.}) 21... Rae8 (21... h6 {to defend against back rank threats was safer for Black:} 22. Bc1 Bf5 23. Ba3 Rf6 $13) 22. Bc1 {Now it is not that easy to find a good plan for Black.} e4 {Here this thematic idea doesn't achieve its aim as the game is too open.} 23. fxe4 Ne5 24. Bxd7 Nxd7 25. Rf1! {White shows that also f4 can be a weakness.} Nc5 26. Ba3! {The point of the World Champion's idea.} Rxe4 27. Qb5?! {A very natural move but it allows Black to escape to an easily drawn endgame.} (27. Qh5! {would give good winning chances:} Re5 (27... b6 28. a5 $16) 28. Rxf4 Rg8 (28... Rfe8 29. Rdf1! {White needed to see this in advance.} Kg8 30. Qg4 $16) 29. Qf3 (29. Rf7 Rxh5 30. Rxc7 Nxa4 31. c4 b5 (31... b6 32. Rd5 $16 {again puts Black in trouble.}) 32. Rd5 $16) 29... Ne6 30. Bd6 Qb6+ 31. Qf2 Qb3 32. Bxe5 Qxd1+ 33. Qf1 Qd5 34. Rf5 $16) 27... Re5 28. Rde1 (28. Bxc5 Qxc5+! (28... Rxc5 29. Rxf4 Rg8 30. Rf7! Rxb5 (30... Qxf7 31. Qxc5 {With queens on Black is in trouble.}) 31. Rxc7 $16 {A different version as White's rooks are now very active.}) 29. Qxc5 Rxc5 30. Rxf4 Kg8 31. Rb4 (31. Rxf8+ Kxf8 {is very similar to the game.}) 31... Rxc3 32. Rxb7 h6 {Thanks to his very active rooks Black shouldn't have much trouble drawing.}) 28... Rc8 29. Rxe5 Qxe5 30. Bxc5 Qxc5+ 31. Qxc5 Rxc5 32. Rxf4 h6 33. Rf3 (33. Rb4 Rxc3 34. Rxb7 Ra3 {would also be trivial for Black.}) 33... Kg8 {Anand confidently shows the endgame is an easy draw.} 34. Rd3 Ra5 35. Rd7 Rxa4 36. Rxb7 Ra2 37. h4 Kh7 38. Kh2 Rd2 39. Rc7 Kg6 40. Kg3 (40. Kh3 {would not change much, for example} h5 41. Kg3 Kf6 42. Kf3 g6 43. g3 Rd3+ 44. Kf4 Ke6 {and White can hardly make any progress.}) 40... Kh5 41. Rc5+ g5 42. Kh3 Rd3+ 43. g3 Re3 44. Kg2 Rd3 45. Rc4 Re3 46. hxg5 hxg5 47. Kh3 Rd3 48. Rc8 g4+ 49. Kg2 Rd2+ 50. Kf1 Rc2 51. c4 Kg5 52. c5 Kf6 53. c6 Ke7 54. c7 Kd6 55. Rd8+ Kxc7 56. Rd4 Kc6 57. Kg1 Kc5 58. Rxg4 Kd5 {The longest game of the first round that probably left the World Champion thinking he could have achieved more.} 1/2-1/2

