[PlyCount "89"]

1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 {This system, a combination of London, Colle and similar ideas, is getting popular nowadays.} Bf5 {I will give here one recent example:} (2... c5 3. e3 Nc6 4. Nf3 e6 5. c3 Bd6 6. Bg3 Nf6 7. Nbd2 O-O 8. Bb5 {Recently this move replaced the usual 8.d3.} Ne7 9. Bd3 Qb6 10. Rb1 Ng6 11. Bxg6 hxg6 12. Bxd6 Qxd6 13. Ne5 b6 14. Qf3 Ba6 15. h4 Nd7 16. Nxd7 Qxd7 17. h5 g5 18. h6 f6 19. Qh5 Bd3 $132 {0-1 (38) Gelfand,B (2734)-Inarkiev,E (2730) Magas (rapid) 2016}) 3. e3 e6 4. Bd3 {The world champion is playing cautios.} (4. c4 Bxb1 5. Qxb1 Bb4+ 6. Kd1 dxc4 7. Bxc4 Bd6 8. Bg3 c5 9. Bb5+ Kf8 10. Nf3 Bxg3 11. hxg3 Nf6 12. Qc2 cxd4 13. Nxd4 g6 14. Ke2 Kg7 15. Rac1 $14 {1-0 (70) Georgiev,K (2628)-Godena,M (2495) Padova 2014. This is just one example how the game may continue.}) 4... Bxd3 5. Qxd3 c6 6. Nd2 Nf6 7. Ngf3 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. h3 Nbd7 10. a4 c5 (10... a5 11. e4 dxe4 12. Nxe4 Nxe4 13. Qxe4 Qb6 14. c4 {with similar play to the Slav Defence where White is doing the same thing with Bd3 and early exchange of the bishops.}) 11. a5 c4 {I am not so sure about this move, after that White knows exactly what to do.} (11... Rc8 12. c3 Rc6 {with balanced play.}) 12. Qe2 b5 13. axb6 axb6 14. Ne5! {Of course, White's play is straingtforward in a way.} Nxe5 (14... Qc8 15. e4 Rd8 16. c3 b5 17. Bg5 Qb7 {looks solid for Black, but so is White's position.}) 15. dxe5 Nd7 16. Qg4 Re8 (16... Qc7 17. Nf3 Kh8 18. h4 Ra5 19. h5 h6 20. Nd4 Rfa8 21. Rxa5 Rxa5 22. Bg3 Ra2 23. f4 {Such type of play I guess Carlsen would do. The position is interesting, perhaps easier to play with White.}) 17. h4 f5 (17... Rxa1 18. Rxa1 Bxh4 19. Bh6 g6 20. Nf3 Be7 21. Ra7 $44) 18. Qh3 {With the idea to play g4.} Rxa1 19. Rxa1 c3?! 20. bxc3 Qc7 21. c4 Nxe5? (21... dxc4 $13) 22. Qg3! Bd6 (22... Bf6 23. Kh1! { f3}) 23. cxd5 (23. c5! bxc5 24. Ra6 Rd8 25. Rxd6 Qxd6 26. Bxe5 $16) 23... exd5 24. Nb3 {It is clear now that Black misplayed the position somehow. White is better.} Qxc2 25. Nd4 Qc8 26. h5 Qd7 27. Rb1 Bc7 28. f3 Qf7 29. Ra1 Bd6 30. Ra6 Qxh5 31. Rxb6 Nc4 32. Rxd6 Nxd6 33. Bxd6 $16 {White managed to take control of a game and result is two pieces against rook where pieces are really at good places.} Rxe3 34. Be5 Qg6 35. Qf4 Re1+ 36. Kf2 Ra1 37. Qd2 Ra8? (37... Qa6 $142) 38. Nxf5! $18 Qe6 39. Qg5 g6 40. Nh6+ Kf8 41. Ng4 Ke8 42. Nf6+ Kf7 43. Nxh7 Ra4 44. Qd8 Ra2+ 45. Kg1 1-0

[Event "Bilbao Masters 9th"]
[Site "Bilbao"]
[Date "2016.07.17"]
[Round "5"]
[White "Giri, Anish"]
[Black "Carlsen, Magnus"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "D22"]
[WhiteElo "2785"]
[BlackElo "2855"]
[PlyCount "123"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3 a6 4. e3 Bg4 {Magnus, as usual, stays away from the main theoretical lines.} 5. Bxc4 e6 6. h3 Bh5 7. Nc3 Nc6 (7... Nf6 8. g4 Bg6 9. Ne5 Nbd7 10. Nxg6 hxg6 11. Bf1! {is a typical idea. The bishop is going to g2 and White normally has slight, but stable advantage.}) 8. O-O Nf6 9. b3 (9. a3 Bd6 10. b4 {is a bit more active way to handle this position.}) ({Another option is} 9. Qe2 Bd6 10. Rd1) 9... Bd6 10. Bb2 O-O 11. Be2 Qe7 12. Rc1 (12. Ne5 Bxe2 13. Nxc6 Bxd1 14. Nxe7+ Bxe7 15. Rfxd1 $11) (12. Nh4 Bxe2 13. Qxe2 Rad8 14. Nf3 Nd5 15. e4 Nf4 16. Qe3 e5 17. dxe5 Bxe5 18. Rad1 Bxc3 19. Bxc3 Ne6 $11 {½-½ (30) Stojanovic,M (2509)-Edouard,R (2620) Switzerland 2016}) (12. Nd2 {is also an option, but Black doesn't have problems to equalize.}) 12... Rad8 13. Ne5 (13. Na4 Ne4 14. Ne5 Bxe2 15. Nxc6 Bxd1 16. Nxe7+ Bxe7 17. Rfxd1 {is extremely safe for White, though it's objectively equal.}) 13... Nxe5 (13... Bxe2 14. Nxc6 Bxd1 15. Nxe7+ Bxe7 16. Rfxd1 {is again equal, but Magnus wants to keep more pieces on the board.}) 14. Bxh5 Ng6 15. Bxg6 $146 {It's not a mistake, but I wouldn't give my bishop for no reason.} (15. Bf3 c6 16. Ne4 Bb8 17. Nxf6+ Qxf6 18. Be4 Ne7 19. Qf3 (19. g3 $142 {[%cal Ge4g2]}) 19... Qh6 20. Rc4 Bd6 21. Bb1 f5 $11 {1-0 (64) Guichard,P (2342)-Velten,P (2422) Condom 2013}) 15... hxg6 16. Qf3 {An inaccuracy.} (16. Qc2 c6 (16... e5?! 17. Ne4 {and White is suddenly better.}) 17. Rfd1 Bb8 ({In case of} 17... e5?! 18. d5 {the white queen doesn't get into the e5-e4 kick, as after 16.f3.}) 18. e4 Ba7 $11) 16... c6 17. Rfd1 (17. e4?! e5 18. d5 cxd5 19. Nxd5 Nxd5 20. exd5 e4 {followed by 21...f5 with pleasant prospects for Black.}) (17. Na4 {makes sense.} e5 18. dxe5 Bxe5 19. Bxe5 Qxe5 20. Nc5 {I wouldn't say that White is pressing, but at least it's a constructive idea for him.}) 17... e5 18. dxe5 (18. Ne4?! Nxe4 19. Qxe4 f5 20. Qc2 e4 {is better for Black thanks to space advantage and superior bishop.}) 18... Bxe5 19. Rxd8 Rxd8 20. Rd1 b5! {After a series of slight inaccuracies Giri found himself in a position where he has to be cautious.} 21. g3?! {There were more useful moves than this.} ({The pawn is taboo:} 21. Qxc6?? b4 {and Black is winning material.}) ({For instance} 21. Qe2 {would be accurate enough.}) 21... Rxd1+ 22. Qxd1 (22. Nxd1? Bxb2 23. Nxb2 Qd7 {[%cal Gd7d2,Gd7h3] underlines the drawback of 21.g3.}) 22... c5 {Black's pawn majority on the queenside is now a factor.} 23. Qd3 Kh7! {Hiding the king away from possible check on d8.} ({The following line gives a clear hint why 23...Kh7 is useful:} 23... Qe6?! 24. Qd8+ Kh7 {[%tqu "En","How should White continue?","","","c3e4","",5]} 25. Ne4! Nxe4 26. Qh4+ Kg8 27. Qd8+ {and White delivers a perpetual check.}) 24. Nd1 Bxb2 25. Nxb2 Qe6 26. g4 Qc6 {[%cal Gc6f3]} 27. Qe2 Ne4 28. Qc2 (28. f3 {looks safer.}) 28... Ng5 29. f4 Ne6 ({The knight will not come out after} 29... Nxh3+? 30. Kh2 Qf3? 31. Qg2!) 30. Qg2 Qd6 31. Qe2 Qd5 (31... g5 32. Qd3+ Qxd3 33. Nxd3 c4 34. bxc4 bxc4 35. Nb4 gxf4 36. Nxa6 fxe3 37. Nb4 {White will not have problems to hold, as his a-pawn will distract the black knight, while White's king is close to Black's passed pawns.}) 32. Qg2 Qd6 33. h4 {I am surprised that Anish didn't repeat moves by 33.e2. Perhaps, he got concerned about 33...g5.} Nc7 34. h5 gxh5 35. gxh5 Qe7 (35... Qd5 36. Qxd5 Nxd5 37. Kf2 Nc3 38. Nd3 Nxa2 39. Nxc5 a5 40. Ke2 Kh6 41. Kd2 Nb4 42. Nb7 a4 43. bxa4 bxa4 44. Nd6 Kxh5 45. Nxf7 {Very few material left, so White holds.} Nd5 46. e4 (46. Ne5? Nxe3!) 46... Nxf4 47. Kc3 Kg4 ({Black can't win also after} 47... Kg6 48. Nd6) 48. Kb4 Nd3+ 49. Kxa4 $11) 36. Qf3 Qd7 37. Nd1 Qd5 38. Qe2 ({After} 38. Qxd5? Nxd5 39. Kf2 Kh6 {White will lose a pawn and his knight is still passive.}) 38... Qf5 39. Nf2 Nd5 40. Kg2 (40. e4? Nxf4 $19 {[%csl Re2]}) 40... c4 41. bxc4 bxc4 42. Kg3! c3 43. Qxa6 c2 (43... Nb4 44. Qc4 c2 45. a3 Nd5 46. Qd3 Ne7 {The same position arose in the game, just with White's pawn on a2. This makes no difference.}) 44. Qd3 Ne7 (44... Qxd3 45. Nxd3 Nxe3 46. Kf3 Nd5 47. Nc1 Kh6 48. Ke4 Nb6 49. Kd3 Kxh5 50. Kxc2 Kg4 51. Nd3 $11) 45. Qc4 Qxh5 46. Qxc2+ Nf5+ 47. Kg2 Qg6+ 48. Kh2 Qg3+ 49. Kh1 g6 50. Qe2 Nxe3 51. Ne4! Qxf4 52. Qh2+ {A queen exchange is a huge relief for Giri.} Qxh2+ 53. Kxh2 Nc4 54. Kg3 Kg7 55. a4 f5 56. Ng5 Kf6 57. Kh4 {Black's knight has to guard the a-pawn and therefore can't help to break the blockade on the kingside.} Na5 58. Nh7+ Kg7 59. Ng5 Nc6 60. Ne6+ Kf6 61. Ng5 Ke5 62. Nh3 {Anish played for a draw since the opening, got himself into trouble, but defended tenaciously and eventually got away with half a point.} 1/2-1/2

