1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nf3 e6 5. Be2 Bb4+ {Quite a rare move, but Magnus already had some experience (not really positive) in this line before.} 6. c3 ({His previous game} 6. Nbd2 Nd7 7. O-O Ba5 8. Nb3 Bc7 9. Ne1 f6 ({Perhaps,} 9... Ne7!? {is a definite improvement.}) 10. Bh5+ g6 11. Be2 g5 12. Bd3 Bg6 13. exf6 Qxf6 14. Qg4 $36 {Sjugirov,S - Carlsen,M / Khanty-Mansiysk 2010, White has got a dangerous initiative and eventually won the game.}) 6... Ba5 7. O-O Nd7 8. a4 $146 {Anish comes up with an interesting novelty: this advance yields White an additional opportunity of developing the knight via a3.} (8. Nbd2 {was recently tested on the top level as well:} Bc7 9. Re1 (9. Ne1 Ne7 10. Nd3 O-O 11. Nf3 h6 $11 {Grischuk,A - Andreikin,D / ICC INT 2016}) 9... h6 10. Nf1 Ne7 11. Ng3 Bg6 12. Nh4 Bh7 13. f4 c5 14. Bd3 $14 {1-0 (35) Grischuk,A - Andreikin,D ICC INT 2016}) 8... Bc7 (8... Ne7?! {can be effectively met by} 9. Nh4! c5 10. Nxf5 Nxf5 11. Bd3 $36) 9. Nbd2 {This move seems inaccurate: now Black is able to challenge the centre, so the artificial manoeuvre b4-a5-c7 is justified.} ({The correct move was} 9. Re1! {now White is well prepared for} f6 (9... h6 10. Nbd2 Ne7 11. Nf1 O-O 12. Ng3 Bg6 13. b4 $14 {offers White a pleasant space advantage}) 10. Bd3 Bxd3 11. exf6 Qxf6 12. Qxd3 h6 13. Qe2 $36) 9... f6 10. Re1 (10. exf6 {doesn't pose Black any problems:} Ngxf6 11. Nh4 O-O 12. f4 Ne4 13. Nxf5 exf5 $11) 10... Nh6 (10... fxe5 11. Nxe5 Nxe5 12. dxe5 Ne7 13. Bh5+ Bg6 $132) 11. Nf1 fxe5 12. dxe5 ({The intermediate} 12. Bg5 {could bother Black too:} Qb8! 13. Bxh6 gxh6 14. Nxe5 Nxe5 15. dxe5 O-O $132) 12... O-O 13. Bxh6 gxh6 14. Qd4? {This awkward way of protecting the central pawn is a serious concession.} ({It was possible to maintain the balance by means of} 14. Ng3! Nxe5 ({After} 14... Bg6 15. Nd4 Qe7 16. Bg4 Nxe5 17. Nxe6 Rf6 18. Nxc7 Qxc7 19. Nh5 {White's position is not worse, at the least}) 15. Nxe5 Bxe5 16. Nxf5 exf5 17. Bd3 Qf6 18. Qf3 $11 {In the subsequent position with bishops of different colours Black's extra pawn is of a symbolic value.}) 14... Be4 {As a result, the e5 would fall in a worse situation.} 15. Ng3 ({In case of} 15. N1d2 Bb6 16. Qb4 a5 17. Qd6 Bf5! 18. Qa3 Qb8! 19. Bf1 Qa7 $17 {White would lose a pawn anyway.}) 15... c5 ({Another decent way of collecting the pawn was} 15... Bxf3!? 16. Bxf3 Qg5 17. h4 Qg6 18. Bh5 Qg7 19. Qg4 Qxg4 20. Bxg4 Rae8 $17) 16. Qe3 Bxf3 17. Bxf3 Qg5!? {Magnus reasonably prefers to simplify matters.} (17... Nxe5 18. Bg4 Qf6 19. Bh3 Rae8 $17) 18. Qxg5+? {Alas, Anish fails to provide proper resistance - there was no reason for improving Black's pawn structure.} (18. Rad1 Qxe3 19. Rxe3 Nxe5 20. Bh5 c4 $17) 18... hxg5 19. Bg4 Rae8 20. Re2 Nxe5 21. Bh5 Rd8 22. Rd1 ({The most stubborn was} 22. b3 Kg7 23. h3 Rf6 24. Rd1 {and Black still has to find a way to make progress.}) 22... c4! {Now Black manages to secure an excellent spot in the opponent's camp.} 23. Nf1 Nd3 ({White resigns in view of} 23... Nd3 24. Rxe6 Nxb2 25. Rb1 Nxa4 26. Rxb7 Nc5 $19) 0-1

