1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 O-O 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 d5 {The newest trend in the 4.c2 variation.} 7. e3 {The practice of the last years has shown that White risks falling behind in development if he spends a tempo on g5. The game move saves an important tempo, but faces White with the risk of remaining with a passive dark-squared bishop, especially after the exchange of the light-squared bishops.} (7. Bg5 dxc4 8. Qxc4 b6 9. Rd1 Ba6 10. Qa4 Bb7 (10... Qd7 {Nepomniachtchi,I (2711)-Amin,B (2609)/Ningbo 2011/CBM 144/[Marin,M] (1-0, 46)}) 11. f3 Nbd7 {Dreev,A (2697)-L'Ami,E (2623)/Aix-les-Bains 2011/CBM 142/[Marin,M] (1/2, 43)}) (7. Nf3 dxc4 8. Qxc4 b6 {Golod,V (2590)-Almasi,Z (2720)/Rijeka 2010/CBM 136/[Marin,M] (1/2, 18)}) 7... b6 8. cxd5 {White decides to play against the hanging pawns. His slight delay in development makes this approach rather risky.} (8. Nf3 Ba6 (8... Bb7 {Vitiugov,N (2694)-Karjakin,S (2723)/Khanty Mansiysk 2009/CBM 134/[Marin] (0-1, 47)}) 9. b4 {Grachev,B (2652)-Bartel,M (2618)/Khanty Mansiysk 2009/CBM 134/[Marin] (1/2, 21)}) 8... exd5 ({The dynamic} 8... Qxd5 {is also playable:} 9. f3 (9. Be2 c5 10. Bf3 Ne4 11. Qc2 f5 12. Ne2 cxd4 13. exd4 Nc6 14. Be3 Ba6 $132 {Narciso Dublan,M (2551)-Fedorchuk,S (2650)/Salou 2011/CBM 142 Extra (1/2, 36)}) 9... Ba6 (9... Rd8!? {would prevent or at least delay e3-e4.}) 10. Bxa6 Nxa6 11. e4 Qb7 12. Bg5 Nd7 13. Ne2 $14 {Michiels,B (2501)-Winants,L (2540)/Belgium 2012 (1/2, 60)}) ({A previous game between the same opponents went:} 8... Nxd5 9. Qc2 Ba6 10. Bxa6 Nxa6 11. e4 Ne7 12. Bg5 h6 13. Bxe7 Qxe7 14. Qc6 e5 15. Qb5 exd4 16. Qxa6 Qxe4+ 17. Kf1 Rfe8 $44 {Carlsen,M (2823)-Kramnik,V (2791)/Moscow 2011/CBM 144 (1/2, 35)}) 9. Bd3 $146 {White takes measures against the occupation of the e4-square.} (9. Nf3 Ne4 10. Qc2 Bf5 $132 {Vasilevich,T (2399)-Galojan,L (2373)/Plovdiv 2010/CBM 138 Extra (1/2, 36)}) 9... Ba6! {As a general rule, it is the side playing against the hanging pawns who is interested in exchanging these bishops. Here, we have enough elements to justify Black's last move. Strategically, it is always a good idea to spoil the enemy's bishop pair. Dynamically, the white bishop has moved already, which transforms the next move into a loss of time.} (9... Ne4 10. Qc2 Bf5?! 11. Ne2 $14) 10. Bxa6 Nxa6 11. Qd3 (11. Nf3 c5 $36) 11... Qc8 12. Ne2 ({With such poor development, White cannot afford to play} 12. b4 c5 {. General exchanges on c5 would leave the light squares desperately weak.}) 12... c5 13. b3 {Taking measures against the threatened ...c5-c4.} cxd4!? {This seems to be the best moment to release the tension.} ({After just a few neutral moves, such as} 13... Qb7 14. O-O Rac8 15. Bb2 {Black would face some small problems already.}) 14. Qxd4? {Too ambitious. White wants to play against the Isolani, but underestimates the significance of his delay in development.} (14. Nxd4? {is almost as bad:} Nc5 15. Qb5 (15. Qe2 Nxb3 16. Nxb3 Qc3+ $17) (15. Qb1 Qa6) (15. Qc2 Qa6) 15... a6 16. Qxb6 Rb8 17. Qc6 Nxb3 18. Qxc8 Rfxc8 $32) (14. exd4 $11 {would have led to plain equality.}) 14... Nc5 {White has problems defending the b3- and d3-squares. Castling becomes a remote dream.} 15. Qd1 (15. Rb1 Qf5 $36) 15... Qa6 16. Nf4 (16. O-O Nxb3 $17) 16... d4 {Strong enough, but maybe a bit premature.} (16... g5?! 17. Bb2 $132) ({Black could have prepared the central break with:} 16... Rad8!? 17. b4 (17. Bb2 d4! 18. Bxd4 Rxd4 19. Qxd4 Nxb3 20. Qb2 Nxa1 21. Qxa1 Qa5+ 22. Ke2 Qb5+ $40 {[%cal Gf8d8]}) 17... g5 18. bxc5 (18. Bb2 d4) 18... gxf4 $36) 17. b4 {The only chance to stay in the game.} (17. exd4? Rfe8+ 18. Be3 g5 19. Ne2 Nd3+ 20. Kf1 Nd5 $40) 17... dxe3 ({The unexpected resource} 17... Nd5!? {would have offered better chances for retaining an attack:} 18. Nxd5 (18. bxc5 Nxf4 19. exf4 Rfe8+ 20. Kd2 bxc5 $19) 18... Nd3+ 19. Kd2 Nxf2 20. Qf1 Ne4+ 21. Ke1 d3 $40) 18. bxc5 Qa5+ 19. Kf1 Rad8 20. Qc2 Qb5+ {Resigning himself to a draw by perpetual.} 21. Ke1 (21. Kg1? Qxc5 $19) 21... Qa5+ 22. Kf1 Qb5+ 23. Ke1 Qa5+ 1/2-1/2

