1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e5!? {After using the Sveshnikov Variation in the WCh match and frequently in tournaments, afterwards Magnus now switched to the "Kalashnikov", curiously named after the Russian machine gun. This game explains why!} 5. Nb5 d6 6. N1c3 a6 7. Na3 Be7 8. g3 Nf6 9. Bg2 b5?! {This being a rapid game, criticising the players' opening accuraccy seems too harsh.} ({But here Carlsen had the excellent} 9... Bg4! {with the idea of} 10. f3?! Be6 11. Nd5 Nxd5 12. exd5 Qa5+ {followed by 13...Qxd5 as now the bishop on g2 is blocked by its own f3-pawn.}) 10. Nd5 Nxd5 11. exd5 Nb8 12. O-O Nd7 13. c4 O-O {Played in the true Kalashnikov spirit. Black is not trying to defend his queenside with moves like ...b4, but hopes that the attack on the kingside will be sufficient.} 14. cxb5 axb5 15. Nc2 {15.Nxb5 was met by 15...Ba6 but Nepomniachtchi eyes the c6 square instead.} f5 16. Nb4 Bf6 17. Nc6 Qe8 18. a4 Nc5 19. Be3 Nxa4 20. Qc2 Bd7 21. b3 {Commenting on every move of a rapid game is not fair, the computer will of course suggest a number of improvements. White's play is logical, trying to force Black's knight to retreat to c5 when after taking it White keeps a promising position. But from here Magnus just plays the following complications perfectly:} f4! 22. bxa4 fxe3 23. axb5 Rxa1 24. Rxa1 Bg5! {White's passed b-pawn obviously is a huge asset, so Black has to hurry. This threatens on f2, and if White plays 25 fxe3 then 25...Bxe3+ followed by 26...Rf2 and 27...Bb6 gives Black a dominant position. Ian replies with the logical and critical} 25. b6 exf2+ 26. Kh1 Bc8 27. Rf1 Qf7 28. Qe2 {At first sight there are reasons for White to be optimistic. The f2-pawn is securely blockaded, and while White has no imminent counter threats, he can improve the situation with h4 and Kh2 next, safeguarding his king. By logic, Black should act immediately, but how?} e4! {The idea is 29 Qxe4 Ba6, attacking the rook on f1, and thus breaking the bloackade. Nepoom spots an ingenious counter-resource:} 29. h4!? {The point is that after 29...Bh6 30.Bxe4 Bh3 White now has 31.Ne7+!! as the black bishop no longer defends e7. 31...Qxe7 loses to 32 Bxh7+ and after 31...Kh8 32 Bg2 is now possible, the point being that 32...Bxg2 33 Kxg2 Qxd5+no longer is possible as the knight from e7 protects the d5-pawn!} e3!! {An amazing counter! Black just calmly gives up a piece, what matters is to keep his connected passed pawns and advance them.} (29... Bh6 30. Bxe4 Bh3 31. Ne7+!! {[%csl Ge7,Yh6]} Kh8 (31... Qxe7 32. Bxh7+) 32. Bg2 Bxg2+ 33. Kxg2 {[%csl Ge7][%cal Yf7d5]}) 30. hxg5 Re8! {Protects e3 and calmly prepares ...Qg6xg5 followed by a deadly check in the h-file.} 31. Kh2! {With the idea of 31...Qg6 32 Bh3!, White again forces Black to show his attacking ceativity. And once again he does not fear further material losses to advance his pawns:} Bg4!! 32. Qxg4 e2 {Like Labourdonnais! Magnus' idea is not just to win a rook, but to actually queen the pawns! Black's threat is 33...e1Q, however, with the detail that 33.Nd8!? is met by 33...exf1N+!  White has no reasonable defence, so Nepo tries fighthing it with two pieces for the queen, but loses in the long run of course.} 33. b7 Qxb7 34. Rxf2 e1=Q 35. Qf5 Qe3 36. Rf3 Qe2 37. Nd4 Qe5 38. Qg4 Qbxd5 39. Nf5 Qde6 40. Qh5 Qg6 41. Qg4 h5 42. Qc4+ d5 43. Qb5 Qxg5 44. Qd7 h4 45. Rf1 hxg3+ 46. Kg1 g6 47. Bxd5+ Kh8 {And having run out of reasonable checks, White resigned.} 0-1

