d still require patience and technique.}) 40... Bxf4 41. gxf4 Rb7! $19 {Transferring the rook behind the  is the most technical solution. White's rook will remain passive and Black will use zugzwang to make an inroad.} 42. Ra2 Ra7 {Edouard,Romain : 'The rook behind the passed pawn, obviously! The endgame is hopeless for White.'} 43. Kg3 Ra4 44. Kh4 Kg6 45. Kg3 Kf6 46. h3 h5 47. Kh4 Kg6 (47... Rxf4+? 48. Kxh5 Rf3 49. Kh4 $11) 48. Kg3 Kg7 {Edouard,Romain : 'Black is mavoeuvring cleverly so at some point either White or Black will play h4 and the kingside will be blocked, eliminating any kind of counterplay.'} 49. Kf3 ({After} 49. Kh4 Kh6 50. Kg3 Kg6 {Edouard,Romain : '-+'} 51. Kh4 (51. f3 Kf6 52. Kh4 Rxf4+ 53. Kxh5 Rxf3 54. h4 Rg3 55. Rc2 f4 $19) 51... Rxf4+ 52. Kg3 Ra4 53. f4 Ra8 54. Kh4 {[%cal Ra2a3] Black must realise xa3 is a threat - thus the only way to win is} Kf6! 55. Kg3 Ra4 $19) 49... h4 50. Ke2 Kf6 51. Kd1 Ke6 52. Kc1 (52. Ke2 Kd5 53. Kd3 Rxf4 {} 54. Rxa3? Rf3+) 52... Kd5 53. Kb1 (53. Kd2 Kc4 54. Kc2 Ra8 55. f3 Ra7 $19 { is a typical zugzwang.}) 53... Ke4 54. Rd2 Kf3 55. Ka2 Kg2 (55... Kg2 56. Rd3 Rxf4 57. Rxa3 Rxf2+ $19 {[%csl Gf5] and the passed pawn advances decisively.}) 0-1

