 {, but here he again missed a tactical chance. After the game he said intution was screaming for} 23. Kh1! {and rightly so, Black is in trouble:} Bg6 (23... axb3 24. Rg1+ Kh8 25. e6! f6 26. Qf5 $18 {} Qe8 27. Be5!) 24. Rg1 Kh7 25. Rxg6 fxg6 26. Rg1 Rf5 27. Bc2 Qd7 28. Rg4 $18 { and White's attack will break through.}) 5... exd4 6. e5 ({Dubov quickly found followers with} 6. b4 Bb6 7. e5 Ne4 8. Bd5 Nxc3 9. Nxc3 dxc3 10. Bg5 Ne7 11. O-O h6 12. Bh4 O-O 13. Re1 Qe8 14. Bb3 a5? (14... Nf5 15. Qd3 d5!? $13) 15. Bf6 a4 16. Bc4 Ng6 17. Qc2! ({Even stronger than} 17. Qd3 {Dubov,D (2702)-Karjakin,S (2752) Moscow 2020}) 17... Kh8 (17... d5 18. exd6 Be6 19. Bxc3! cxd6 20. Qxg6 fxg6 21. Rxe6 Qc6 22. Rxg6+ Qxc4 23. Rxg7+ Kh8 24. Rc7+ Qxc3 25. Rxc3 $16) 18. Qxc3 gxf6 {Even more convincing is} 19. Qd2 $142 ! (19. exf6 Qd8 20. Re7! d5 21. Qd2 Kh7 22. Ng5+! (22. Bd3? Qd6 23. Ng5+ Kg8 24. Ne6 Qe5 25. Re1 Qxf6 26. Nxf8 Nxe7 27. Nh7 Qd4 28. Kh1 Nf5 $19 {Horvath,D (2445)-Pacher,M (2394) Budapest 2021}) 22... hxg5 23. Qxg5 Bxf2+ 24. Kxf2 dxc4 25. Re4 Kg8 26. Rh4 {[%cal Rh4h8] and the only defence against h8 is the sad} Qxf6+ 27. Qxf6 Nxh4 28. Qxh4 $16 {/+-}) 19... Kh7 20. exf6 Qd8 21. Ng5+! hxg5 (21... Kg8 22. Nxf7 Rxf7 23. Qxh6 Qxf6 24. Re8+ $18) 22. Qxg5 d5 23. Bd3 Bxf2+ 24. Kh1! {[%cal Rg5h5,Rd3g6]} (24. Kxf2? Ra6! 25. Bxa6 bxa6 $13) 24... Rh8 25. Re7 $18) 6... d5 7. Bb5 Ne4 8. cxd4 Bb6 9. Nc3 O-O 10. Be3 Bg4 11. h3 Bh5 12. Qc2 {A topical position. Carlsen has had his problems with this line, although until now he has avoided losing a game.} Bg6 ({Magnus has also tried the experimental} 12... f5 13. Nxd5 Ba5+ 14. Ke2 Nxd4+!? 15. Bxd4 Nd2 16. Qc5 Ne4 17. Qc2 ({Repeats moves, but} 17. Qc4!? Bf7 18. Nf6+ gxf6 19. e6 a6 $132 {is not so clear either.}) 17... Nd2 18. Qc5 Ne4 19. Qc2 {½-½ (19) Aronian,L (2773)-Carlsen,M (2863) Lichess.org INT 2020}) (12... Nxc3 13. bxc3 f6 14. Bxc6 ({After the solid} 14. exf6 Qxf6 15. Be2 Bg6 16. Qb3 Qd6 17. O-O {Chigaev recommended} h6!? {The follow-up} 18. a4 Be4 19. Nd2 Bf5 20. Rfe1 Rad8 21. Bg4 Na5 22. Qd1 c6 23. g3 Qg6 24. Bxf5 Qxf5 25. Qg4 Bc7 26. Qxf5 Rxf5 27. Kg2 Re8 $11 {Vitiugov,N (2728)-Tomashevsky,E (2706) Izhevsk 2019/ led to a roughly equal endgame.}) 14... Bxf3 $142 ! (14... bxc6 15. exf6 Bxf3 (15... Qxf6 16. Ne5 Qd6 17. O-O c5 18. f4 c4 19. Kh1 Bg6 20. f5 Be8 21. Rae1 Rb8 22. Bf4 $16 {Kravtsiv,M (2623)-Petrosyan,T (2469) Al Ain 2015}) 16. fxg7 Rf7 17. gxf3 Qd6 18. Rg1 c5 19. Rg5 cxd4 20. cxd4 Rxg7 21. Rxg7+ Kxg7 {Here Black has problems both with his passive b6 and his exposed g7.} 22. Ke2 $16 {Giri,A (2764)-Carlsen,M (2862) chess24.com rpd INT 2020}) 15. gxf3 bxc6 16. f4 Qe7 {White should be more modest,} 17. Qd1 (17. a4 a5 18. h4 c5 19. Qb3 cxd4 20. cxd4 Kh8 21. Qxd5 Rad8 22. Qc4 fxe5 23. fxe5 {Poetsch,H (2516)-Lobzhanidze,D (2422) Germany 2019} (23. dxe5!?) 23... c5! 24. dxc5 Qxe5 $44 {[%csl Re1]}) (17. Qe2 c5 18. Qb5 fxe5 19. fxe5 cxd4 20. Qxd5+ Kh8 21. cxd4 Rad8 22. Qc4 c5 $17 {Sanal,V (2569)-Deac,B (2626) Caleta 2020}) 17... Rad8 18. O-O c5 19. Rc1 c4!? $132 {[%cal Yc7c5] c5 is about equal}) 13. Qb3 Ne7 14. O-O (14. Nh4 c5! 15. Nxg6 hxg6 16. dxc5 Nxc5 17. Bxc5 Bxc5 18. O-O Bd4 19. Rad1 Bxe5 20. Nxd5 Nxd5 21. Rxd5 Qf6 $11 {Ivanchuk,V (2731)-Kramnik,V (2793) Antalya 2013}) 14... c6 15. Bd3 Nf5 {Carlsen doesn't repeat his game against Aronian and opts for a more common continuation.} (15... Ng3 16. Rfd1 Bxd3 17. Rxd3 Ngf5 18. Na4 (18. Bg5!? $14) 18... f6 19. Nxb6 axb6 20. Re1 fxe5 21. Nxe5 Ng6 22. Nxg6 hxg6 23. Bf4 b5 24. a3 Qh4 25. Be5 Rae8 26. Qc3 Nd6 $11 {So,W (2770)-Le,Q (2709) chess24.com INT 2020/ Although Black equalised and drew in the end, his strategy seems suspect and I think Wesley wouldn't have minded playing this line again.}) (15... Nxc3 16. Qxc3 Bxd3 17. Qxd3 Ng6 {seems passive, but after} 18. Ng5 Qd7 19. f4 f6 20. Nf3 (20. exf6 gxf6 21. Nf3 Ne7 22. Nh4 {Lomasov,S (2559)-Vokhidov,S (2494) Moscow 2019} Nc8! $11) 20... fxe5 21. Nxe5 Qf5 22. Qxf5 Rxf5 23. Rae1 Nxe5 24. fxe5 Rxf1+ 25. Kxf1 Kf7 26. Ke2 Rf8 27. Kd3 Ke6 {Kovalev,V (2648)-Vokhidov,S (2512) chess.com INT 2020/ Black managed to build a solid fortress.}) ({The most played move is} 15... Nd2 16. Nxd2 (16. Bxd2 Bxd3 17. Rfe1 Bg6 18. Na4 Be4 19. Nxb6 axb6 20. Bb4 Re8 21. Bxe7 Rxe7 22. Nd2 Bg6 23. Qc3 c5!? 24. dxc5 Rc8 $132 {Aronian,L (2765)-Vidit,S (2702) Kolkata blitz 2018}) 16... Bxd3 17. Rfe1 (17. Rfd1 Nf5 18. Nxd5 Be2 19. Re1 Qxd5 20. Qxd5 cxd5 21. Rxe2 Bxd4 22. Nf3 Bxe3 23. fxe3 Rac8 24. Rd1 Ne7 25. e4 dxe4 26. Rxe4 Rfd8 27. Red4 Nc6 28. Rd7 Kf8 29. Kf2 Ke8 $11 {/, Inarkiev,E (2693)-Ding,L (2772) Antalya 2017}) 17... Bg6 18. Na4 {and now:} {Black should play more enterprisingly -} Ba5 $142 !? (18... Nf5 19. Nxb6 axb6 (19... Qxb6 20. Qc3 f6 21. Nf3 Rae8 (21... Nxe3 {is an antipositional swap, White gets pressure after} 22. Rxe3 Rae8 23. Rae1 $36 {Lobanov,S (2515)-Malakhov,V (2691) Sochi 2018}) 22. Bf4 Qd8 23. g4! Ne7 24. exf6 $16 {Melkumyan,H (2663)-Fakhrutdinov,T (2501) chess.com blitz INT 2020}) 20. a3!? (20. a4 f6 21. exf6 Qxf6 22. Nf3 Nxe3 23. Qxe3 {Harsha,B (2474)-Liang,A (2575) Manavgat 2018} c5!? $132) 20... b5 21. Qc3 Qe7 22. Nf3 Ra4 23. Bg5 Qc7 24. b3 Raa8 25. g4 Ne7 26. Qb4 $36 {Wen,Y (2588)-Dai,C (2388) Shenyang 2019}) 19. Nc5 (19. Qxb7 Nf5 $44 (19... Bc2 {Briand,A (1896)-Eugene,F (2300) Sables d'Olonne 2019} 20. Nc5 $16)) 19... Bxd2 20. Bxd2 b6 21. Nb7 Qd7 22. Nd6 Nf5 23. Nxf5 Bxf5 24. Rac1 Rfc8 25. Bb4 c5 26. dxc5 a5 27. c6 Rxc6 28. Rxc6 Qxc6 $11 {Gavrilescu,D (2473)-Ovsejevitsch,S (2602) Hamburg 2020}) (15... Qd7 16. Rfe1 Nxc3 17. Qxc3 Bxd3 18. Qxd3 Ng6 19. Bd2 f6 20. e6 Qd6 21. Re2 Rfe8 22. Rae1 Re7 {Aronian,L (2773)-Carlsen,M (2863) chess24.com blitz INT 2020/ Although Black managed to win in the end, at this point his position wouldn't be ideal after e.g.,} 23. a3 $14 {[%csl Ge6][%cal Yd2b4,Yd3f5,Yh3h6]}) (15... Kh8?! 16. Nh4 c5 17. Nxg6+ fxg6 18. Bxe4 cxd4 19. Nxd5 Nxd5 20. Bd2 $16 {Jones,G (2677)-Aronian,L (2780) Douglas 2018}) 16. Qc2!? ({More common is} 16. Rfe1 {and now:} Nxe3 (16... Nxd4 17. Bxd4 Bxd4 18. Nxe4 dxe4 19. Bxe4 Bxe4 20. Rxe4 Bb6 21. a4!? (21. e6 fxe6 (21... Qd5!? $11) 22. Rxe6 Kh8 23. a4 Qc7 24. Rae1 Qf7 25. Qc3 Rae8 26. Rxe8 Rxe8 27. Rxe8+ Qxe8 28. a5 Bd8 29. Qb4 Qe7 $11 {So,W (2770)-Le,Q (2709) chess24.com INT 2020}) 21... Bc5 22. e6 fxe6 23. Qxb7 Qb6 24. Qxb6 axb6 25. Rc1 $14 {Matlakov,M (2701)-Deac,B (2621) Changsha 2019}) 17. Rxe3 Ng5 18. Nxg5 (18. Ne2 Ne6 19. Rd1 Qd7 20. Qc2 {Kovalev,V (2650)-Notkin,M (2449) Minsk 2018} f5!? $11 {.}) (18. Bxg6 fxg6 19. Ne2 Ne6 20. a4 Rf7 21. Qc3 a5 22. Qd2 g5 23. Rb1 Qe7 24. Rb3 Bd8 25. Ne1 Qc7 26. Rf3 Rxf3 27. Nxf3 Be7 $15 {Firouzja,A (2582)-Sunilduth Lyna,N (2573) Manavgat 2018}) 18... Qxg5 19. Bxg6?! (19. Ne2 f6!? $132) {Black can flick in} 19... Bxd4! (19... Qxg6 20. Rd1 f6 21. Na4 fxe5 22. Nxb6 axb6 23. dxe5 $36 {Hracek,Z (2563)-Freisler,P (2249) Plzen 2020}) 20. Rd3 Bxe5! $15) 16... Nxc3 $146 {A novelty, which boosts White's . It may well be the result of being surprised by White's previous move.} ({Until now Black did OK after} 16... Nxe3 17. fxe3 Ng3 18. Bxg6 hxg6 19. Rf2 (19. Rfe1 Ba5 20. Rab1 Qe7 21. a3 Bxc3 22. Qxc3 Ne4 23. Qd3 c5 24. dxc5 Nxc5 25. Qxd5 Rad8 26. Qc4 Nd3 $11 {/, Wen,Y (2586)-Esipenko,A (2636) Moscow 2019}) 19... Qd7 20. Kh2 Nf5 21. Re1 (21. Qd3 f6 22. g4 Ne7 23. Kg1 fxe5 24. Nxe5 Qe6 25. Raf1 Rxf2 26. Rxf2 Bc7 $132 {Drygalov,A (2465)-Korneev,O (2547) Kazan 2018}) 21... f6 22. g4 Ne7 23. Kg2 fxe5 24. Nxe5 Qe6 25. Nf3 Qd6 26. e4 dxe4 27. Qxe4 Rae8 28. Qe5 Qxe5 29. Rxe5 Bc7 30. Re1 Nd5 $11 {Legemaat,G (2351)-Mudra,O (2311) email 2018}) 17. bxc3 Nxe3 18. fxe3 Bxd3 19. Qxd3 f6 20. c4 Bc7? ({White's central expansion requires accuracy. Correct was} 20... fxe5! 21. Nxe5 Qd6 $132 {[%cal Yc6c5] , Black prepares c5 and prevents} 22. c5? Bxc5) 21. cxd5 Qxd5 (21... fxe5 22. dxc6 bxc6 23. Rac1 $36 {[%csl Rc6]}) ({or} 21... cxd5 22. Qb3 $16 {[%csl Rd5] also isn't ideal, Black's weaknesses are under pressure.}) 22. exf6 gxf6 {Trying to choose the lesser evil.} (22... Rxf6 23. e4 $16 {[%cal Re4e5,Rd3b3,Rf3g5]  b3,g5,e5 is also extremely unpleasant.}) 23. e4 {Loosening the  is unnecessary.} (23. Nh4 $142 ! $16 {White has a clear strategic advantage and doesn't have to worry about} Qd6?! 24. Nf5 Qh2+ 25. Kf2 {[%csl Rh2]}) 23... Qd7 ({More resilient was} 23... Qe6!?) 24. Rad1 (24. Nh4!?) 24... Rad8 ({Black can't survive} 24... f5 25. Qb3+ Qf7 (25... Kh8 26. Ne5 $18) 26. Qxf7+ (26. d5 $16) 26... Rxf7 27. Ng5 $16 {/+-}) 25. Nh4 {[%mdl 32] Finally transferring the knight to the f5-outpost cements White's positional advantage; Black faces a difficult defence.} Kh8 26. Nf5 c5 ({One can understand Carlsen is not too thrilled about e.g.,} 26... Qe6 27. d5 $16 {and wants to change the character of the position.}) 27. d5 Be5 28. Rb1 b6 29. Qc4 Rfe8 30. Kh1 Bd4 31. Rf4 Rxe4!? ({This final attempt to muddy the waters is the best practical chance.} 31... Be5 32. Rf3 ({or} 32. Rg4 $16 {forces Black to just mark time and passively wait, how his opponent will increase his pressure.})) 32. Rxe4 Qxf5 33. Rbe1 Rxd5? (33... Qxd5? 34. Re8+ Kg7 35. R1e7+ Kh6 36. Qc1+ $18 {loses quickly, but the same goes for the text move.}) ({Only} 33... Be5! $16 { enables Black to continue fighting.}) 34. Rg4 h5 35. Re8+ Kh7 36. Re7+ Kh8 37. Qc1 {[%cal Rc1h6] The final blow, the mating threat will cost Black heavily.} 1-0

