{[%evp 0,69,27,27,17,-22,-11,-16,-3,-3,4,-57,-42,-23,-30,-30,-28,-48,-24,-35,-33,-60,-6,-137,-62,-39,11,42,38,35,64,49,118,24,73,113,88,107,108,108,122,122,120,107,103,102,150,133,130,130,143,95,105,127,165,122,120,98,55,55,41,41,95,95,151,158,152,152,213,227,260,260]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 Bb4 5. Qa4+ Nc6 6. a3 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 O-O (7... Ne4 {would be another big theoretical debate. The most recent games went} 8. Bf4!? g5!? (8... Nxc3 9. Qc2 Ne4 10. e3 g5 11. Be5 f6 12. Bg3 h5 13. h4 Nxg3 14. fxg3 g4 15. Qg6+ Kf8 16. Ne5? (16. Ng5!) 16... Qe7 17. cxd5 exd5 18. Bb5 Nxe5 19. dxe5 f5 20. O-O Qf7 21. Qg5 Qe7 $11 {½-½ (40) Edouard,R (2640)-Santos Ruiz,M (2581) Los Cancajos 2020}) 9. Be3 Nxc3 10. Qc2 Ne4 11. g3 f6 12. Bg2 Ne7 13. O-O Nf5 14. Nd2 Nxd2 15. Qxd2 c6 16. f4 g4 17. c5 h5 18. Bf2 h4 $132 {½-½ (35) Eljanov,P (2672)-Anton Guijarro,D (2675) Los Cancajos 2020}) 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bh4!? Bd7 (9... g5 10. Bg3 Ne4 11. Qc2 {is very dangerous for Black as well, e.g.} b6 (11... h5 12. Ne5 h4 13. f3!? $146 Nd6 14. c5! $36) 12. e3 Ba6 13. Bd3 Na5 14. Ne5 Kg7 15. cxd5 Bxd3 16. Qxd3 exd5 17. f3 Nxg3 18. hxg3 Qd6 19. Kf2 Nc6 20. f4 $40 {1-0 (46) Galego,L (2471)-Duda,J (2739) Batumi 2018}) 10. Qc2!? Na5 11. g4!? $146 {A very clever idea - typical of modern chess! At first the engine doesn't like it but it quickly changes its mind.} g5?! {Probably not a good move, but honestly it was difficult to find one, especially over the board!} (11... dxc4 12. g5 hxg5 13. Bxg5 {followed by Rg1 just looks scary for Black.}) ({Same after} 11... Nxc4 12. g5 hxg5 13. Bxg5 Re8 14. Ne5 {followed by g1.}) 12. Bg3 Ne4 (12... Nxg4? 13. h4! $16) 13. h4 {Black's position is a nightmare already.} Nxg3 (13... Nxc4 14. hxg5 hxg5 15. e3 Ncd6 16. Nd2 Kg7 17. Be5+! f6 18. Bxd6 cxd6 19. f3 $18) 14. fxg3 gxh4 15. Rxh4 Kg7 16. g5 (16. e4!? {was even stronger according to the engine.}) 16... Rh8 17. Ne5! {The g5-pawn is untouchable!} Nxc4 (17... hxg5? 18. Rxh8 $18) 18. gxh6+ Kf8 19. Nxc4?! (19. Qc1! {was more precise - so the black queen can never come back into the action.}) 19... Qg5 (19... dxc4 20. Qd2 Qf6 21. Bg2 {would be better for White - but also not as strong as 19.c1.}) 20. Kf2 dxc4 21. Qc1! Qf6+ 22. Qf4 Ke7 (22... Qxf4+ 23. gxf4 Bc6 24. Bg2 $14) 23. Qxf6+ Kxf6 24. Bg2 Rab8 25. Rh5 Kg6 26. Rah1 f5 27. g4! Rh7 (27... fxg4 28. Be4+ Kf6 29. Kg3 $18) 28. g5? ({Magnus should just have kept the tension with} 28. Kg3 {followed by f4.}) 28... Bc6 {Suddenly Black has a sort of blockade and is fully back in the game.} 29. Bf3 Bxf3?! (29... Bd5! $11 {was better - but in fact almost any move was better than the game one, helping White advance with his king.}) 30. Kxf3 Rd8?! ({Black had to react with} 30... c5 31. e3 b5 {and I believe he would have serious chances to make a draw.}) 31. Kf4 Rd5? ({Black had a last chance to play} 31... c5 32. e3 b5 {and keep chances.}) 32. R5h3! c5 {Now it's too late!} 33. Re3! Rd6 (33... Kf7 34. Rg1 $18) 34. dxc5 Ra6 35. Rd1 1-0

