1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 {It is kind of funny that Carlsen has played only 4 classical games in the Scotch Game since he became a grandmaster. Probably another reason for Wesley So to try it against the reigning World Champion.} Bb4+!? {An off-beat variation, forcing White to play c3, and removing that ideal square for the b1-knight. Magnus himself said he wasn't expecting the Scotch and just thought about playing some variation to try and develop his pieces.} (4... Nf6 {is the most popular response, with well-known variations after the most ambitious 5.xc6 followed by 6.e5, or the simpler 5.c3.}) (4... Bc5 {when White has options between the old moves xc6 and e3, or the more fashionable b3 followed by c3, e2, e3 or g5 and possibly castling queenside.}) 5. c3 Be7?! {should be inferior for Black. We arrive in a Philidor-type position, where White has sound pieces and a space advantage.} (5... Bc5!? {is also possible, now in the xc6 and b3 variations, the b1-knight would like to go to c3.} 6. Be3 Bb6 {and here White would be happy to play c3-d5 as well, justifying the ...b4+ and ...c5 manoeuvre.} (6... Qf6? {now this wouldn't make any sense, since the pawn is already on c3 (this is played in the line after 4...c5 where White still has the pawn on c2)}) 7. Nf5! g6 8. Bxb6 axb6 9. Ne3 Nf6 10. f3 Qe7 (10... O-O 11. c4 {and White tries to keep a positional advantage. Black has an interesting piece sacrifice with ...d5 now:} d5 12. cxd5 Nxe4 13. fxe4 Qh4+ 14. Kd2 Qxe4 15. Bd3 Qd4 16. Qb3 Re8 17. Re1 Ne5 18. Na3 Nxd3? (18... Bd7!? $40) 19. Qxd3?! (19. Nac2! $18) 19... Qxb2+ 20. Nac2 Bd7 (20... Qe5! $132) 21. Qc3 $16 {1-0 (30) Danin,A (2581)-Zeller,F (2402) Wunsiedel 2016}) 11. Be2!? (11. c4 Qb4+ 12. Qd2 Qxd2+ 13. Kxd2 {with a pleasant endgame} d6 14. Nc3 Kd8 15. Be2 Re8 16. Rac1 $14 {1-0 (40) Yu,Y (2662)-Salem,A (2570) Kocaeli 2013}) 11... Qc5 12. Qd2 h5?! 13. Na3 Qe7? 14. Nd5 Nxd5 15. exd5 Ne5 16. d6! Qxd6 17. Qxd6 cxd6 18. Nb5 Ke7 19. a4 $18 {1-0 (47) Duda,J (2693)-Degraeve,J (2592) Chartres 2017}) 6. Nxc6 bxc6 7. Bd3 (7. Bc4!? Nf6 8. e5 $36) 7... d6 8. O-O Nf6 9. Re1 O-O 10. Nd2 {I like White's position here, the knight will be well placed on f3, Black has to be watching for moves like e5.} Re8 11. Nf3 Nd7 {A natural reaction - the knight stands well on c5 in these kind of structures, but this will be the start of his problems as well.} 12. Bf4 Nc5 13. Bc2 Bg4?! (13... Ne6!? 14. Be3 c5 {followed by ...b8 and ...a5} 15. b4!? {trying to avoid that plan} cxb4 16. cxb4 $14) 14. h3 Bh5 15. Be3! {Threatening to destroy Black's structure; this is the key move to look for an advantage. Carlsen said in the post-mortem that his position started to deteriorate now, after this and 16.a4!, that he missed.} Nd7 (15... Qb8 16. Bxc5 dxc5 17. Rb1 $16 {has to be ugly for Black.}) 16. Ba4! c5 (16... Ne5? 17. g4! Nxf3+ 18. Qxf3 Bg6 19. Bxc6 $18 {is simply lost for Black.}) 17. g4!? (17. e5! {was a very interesting pawn sacrifice:} Bxf3 (17... Nxe5 18. g4! Bg6 19. Nxe5 dxe5 20. Bxe8 $18) (17... Rb8 18. b3 Bxf3 19. Qxf3 Nxe5 20. Qe4 Rf8 21. Rad1 $44) 18. Qxf3 Nxe5 19. Qe4 {hitting the e8-rook} Rf8 20. Rad1 {threatening to take on c5} Qc8 21. Bxc5 (21. b4!? cxb4 22. cxb4 $44 {trying to maintain the pressure, without exchanging anything.}) 21... dxc5 22. Qxe5 Bd6 23. Qe4 $16 {with good prospects.}) 17... Bg6 18. e5 (18. Bc6! Rb8 19. b4 {was a very strong hidden idea} h5! {with a double-edged position, that should be safe enough for White} (19... cxb4? 20. Qa4! {winning the e8 exchange by force, because now b6 is under control} (20. Bxa7?! Ra8! 21. Bxa8? Qxa8 22. Be3 Bxe4 $19 {is horrible for White}) 20... Nb6 21. Bxb6 axb6 22. Bxe8 Qxe8 23. Qxe8+ Rxe8 24. cxb4 $18 {should be simply winning}) 20. bxc5 hxg4 21. hxg4 $16) 18... Rb8 19. Bf4? {Now many simplifications take place, Carlsen equalises and gets the initiative suddenly.} (19. b3! $16 {was simple and strong, Black still has to solve many problems.}) 19... Rxb2! 20. exd6? (20. Bxd7! {was the best chance to equalise} Qxd7 21. exd6 cxd6 22. Ne5 Qc7 (22... Qc8 23. Nxg6 hxg6 24. Bxd6 $11) 23. Nc4 $11) 20... Bxd6! 21. Rxe8+ Qxe8 22. Bxd6 cxd6 23. Qxd6 (23. Nd2 {the computer suggests this very sad idea to survive, a hard decision to make after giving away a pawn. On the other hand, there is a c4 threat} d5 24. Nb3 Qe7 25. Bxd7 Qxd7 26. Nxc5 Qd6 27. Qd4 h5 $36) 23... Qe2! {Maybe So missed this move.} 24. Qg3 Nf8! $17 {White has many problems now, with the weak king, weak pawns and having to control Black's initiative.} 25. Re1 Rb1! 26. Rxb1 Bxb1 {At least the a2-pawn will be lost, White is in deep trouble.} 27. Bc6 (27. a3 Be4! {with mating ideas as well} 28. Nh2 Qe1+ 29. Nf1 Qb1! {threatening the unstoppable ...d3} 30. f4 Bd3 31. Qf3 c4 $19 {Black will take on c3 at the proper time and win the game.}) 27... Bxa2 $19 28. Qd6 Qc4! 29. Ne5 Qxc3 {With no counterplay left, and two pawns down, White had to resign.} 0-1

[Event "Amber-rapid 20th"]
[Site "Monte Carlo"]
[Date "2011.03.22"]
[Round "9"]
[White "Anand, Viswanathan"]
[Black "Carlsen, Magnus"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C86"]
[WhiteElo "2817"]
[BlackElo "2815"]
[PlyCount "86"]

1. e4 e5 {After my 100% record with the French Defence was ruined against Karjakin, (to be fair I was not even close to retaining it) I had to play something else.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 {Declaring my readiness to suffer in a slightly worse ending against the World Champion.} 4. Qe2 {A surprise; Anand avoids the Berlin endgame from time to time, but usually with 4.d3.} a6 {For whatever reason I suddenly got very creative here, and started considering several reasonable and unreasonable options like ...e7, ...d6 and ,,,g6. After a while I came to my senses and played a normal move.} 5. Ba4 Be7 {Most sensible people play ...b5 first here, and indeed it makes sense not to give White the option of retreating the bishop to c2.} 6. c3 d6 7. O-O O-O 8. d4 Bd7 9. d5 Nb8 10. Bxd7 Nbxd7 11. c4 {Black should be a little bit worse here. However, as my position seemed a whole lot sounder than it did at this point in the blindfold game, (notably, no tripled pawns) I was not that worried.} g6 (11... a5 {here or on one of the next few moves makes little sense to me, as White will soon play b4 anyway, and I will have given up the b5-square and possibly the a-file for very little.}) 12. Nc3 Nh5 13. Rb1 Ng7 14. b4 {I was playing way too quickly around here to consider subtleties like the possibility of playing c5 here, which closes the position and makes it hard to create anything serious on the queenside for White in the near future. However, when I hit upon this idea a few moves later I also remembered a game Anand-Short with the same structure, which Anand won in good style so I did not really regret choosing another plan.} (14. Ne1 {followed by d3 is desirable strategically, but it allows} Bg5 {and after exchanging the dark-squared bishops Black must be fine.}) 14... f5 15. Be3 Rb8 (15... Nf6 16. Rfd1 {with c4-c5 to come looks very pleasant for White as I don't really want to take on e4. Thus, the knight is better placed on d7.}) 16. Rfd1 {Considering what happened in the game, it might be suggested that the rook should have been put on c1 instead. The rook would be very useful on d1 if the centre was to be opened, but as soon as I close the position with f4, the rook is naturally not doing much here.} (16. c5 dxc5 17. bxc5 f4 {works out well for Black.}) 16... Qe8 17. a4 f4 {There isn't really much else for Black to do here. Without the light-squared bishops White will never be mated on the kingside of course, but at least I will manage to create some counterplay and not give White a free hand on the kingside.} 18. Bd2 g5 19. h3 (19. Ne1 Qg6 20. f3 h5 21. Nd3 g4 {was another option for White. White is clearly at least ok here, but as long as White is not breaking through on the queenside any time soon Black certainly has chances.}) 19... h5 20. Nh2 Qg6 21. f3 Ne8! {It is important to put this knight on f6 (it was just an obstruction on g7) rather than the other knight, which has a very useful defensive function on d7.} 22. Rdc1 Nef6 23. Kf1? {The critical moment, where Anand chooses the wrong defensive plan.} (23. Nd1 {with the intention of putting this knight on f2 and stopping g4 would be one move too late:} g4 24. hxg4 hxg4 25. Nxg4 Nxg4 26. fxg4 Bh4! {followed by ...f6 with a very strong initative.}) (23. Kh1 g4 24. Rg1 {would have been better, as Black is not breaking through any time soon, and White might even have ideas of playing g3 some day. On the other hand, White is clearly not going to do any serious damage on the queenside either, with the knight on h2 and rook on g1.}) 23... Kf7 24. Ke1?! {The continuation of a bad plan. White has not enough time to protect everything after Black invades on the g-file.} g4 25. Kd1 gxh3 26. gxh3 Rg8 27. Kc2 Qg2 28. Rh1 Qxe2 29. Nxe2 Rg2 30. Kd3 Nb6 {Now White is losing a pawn by force.} 31. Rbg1 Rbg8 32. Rxg2 (32. Ng4 Rxg1 33. Nh6+ Kg6 {just doesn't work.}) 32... Rxg2 33. a5 (33. Nc3 Nxc4 {is no better.}) 33... Nxc4 34. Bc1 b5 35. axb6 Nxb6 36. Nf1 Nh7 {Intending ...h4 followed by ...g5, and} 37. h4 (37. Rg1 Rf2 {doesn't help.}) 37... Nf8 38. Bd2 Ng6 39. Be1 Na4 {White is almost in complete zugzwang. As is easy to see, neither of his knights can move, and after} 40. Rg1 (40. Kd2 Nb2 {his king is stalemated too.}) 40... Rxg1 41. Nxg1 Bxh4 {Black does not have an immediate breakthrough, but with two extra pawns the game is obviously decided.} 42. Bd2 Ke7 43. Nh3 Kd7 {...c7-c6 is next, and White has no chances of saving the game. A nice, smooth win, even if it was not Anand's finest hour.} 0-1

