1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 g6 4. O-O Bg7 5. c3 Nf6 6. Re1 O-O 7. d4 a6 8. Bd3 d5 9. e5 Ne8 10. dxc5 Bg4 11. Be2 Nc7 12. Nbd2 a5 13. h3 Bc8 14. Bd3 Ne6 15. Nb3 a4 16. Nbd4 Nxc5 17. Bc2 Qa5 18. Bd2 Rd8 19. Qc1 Ne6 20. b4 Qc7 21. Nxc6 bxc6 22. Be3 c5 23. bxc5 Nxc5 24. Qa3 Ne6 25. Rab1 d4 26. Nxd4 Bxe5 27. Nb5 Qc6 28. Rb4 Bb7 29. Be4 Qd7 30. Bxb7 Qxb7 31. Rxa4 Bf4 32. Rxa8 Rxa8 33. Qb3 Bxe3 34. Rxe3 Nf4 35. Rf3 Qe4 36. Kh2 Nd5 37. c4 Qe5+ 38. g3 Nf6 39. a3 Ne4 40. Qc2 Ng5 41. Re3 Qc5 42. h4 Ne6 43. Qe2 Rc8 44. Rc3 h5 45. Kg2 Kf8 46. Qe4 Kg8 47. Qd5 Qb6 48. a4 Qa6 49. c5 Qxa4 50. c6 Rd8 51. Qc4 Qxc4 52. Rxc4 Rb8 53. c7 Rc8 54. Rc1 1/2-1/2

[Event "Tata Steel-A 82nd"]
[Site "Wijk aan Zee"]
[Date "2020.01.24"]
[Round "11"]
[White "Duda, Jan Krzysztof"]
[Black "Carlsen, Magnus"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "C54"]
[WhiteElo "2758"]
[BlackElo "2872"]
[PlyCount "86"]

{The game was played on 11th round, and after a free day which felt no fun at all. Magnus had just overcome a drawing crisis and had scored 3/3 in the last rounds. Still he was down to Caruana by half a point, so I assumed he would go for an even higher winning streak. Furthermore the free day made me paranoic about his opening preparation. After much thought I decided to stay loyal to my 1.e4 and see what happens, without certainity though.} 1. e4 e5 {So after all this hours spend on repeating all kinds of Sicilian variations, French Defence, Modern, Scandinavian and even Alekhine Defence, he just played 1...e5. A true surprise.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 ({Not even going for} 3... Nf6 {as he played against Van Foreest a couple of rounds earlier}) 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 {So the Italian game. The only prep I had done was that I wasn't going to play a set up with Bg5. Not a brilliant stuff, but at least I had played the Italian 32 times in my recent years.} 6. Nbd2 a6 7. Nf1 {Pretending to save a tempo on Re1 later on, but most importantly avoid ...Ng4/...f5 stuff.} (7. Bb3 h6 8. Nf1 d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Ng3 O-O 11. O-O Re8 12. Re1 Nf6 13. h3 Be6 14. Bc2 Bd5 15. Nh4 Qd7 16. Nhf5 Bf8! {That's the point!} 17. f4 Rad8 18. fxe5 Nxe5 19. d4 Ng6 20. Rf1 Qc6 21. Rf2 Ne4 $11 {1/2 (43) Duda,J (2739)-Mamedyarov,S (2820) Batumi 2018; with comfortable equality for Black.}) 7... h6 8. Ng3 O-O 9. O-O {I didn't remember the exact move order from my game against Mamedyarov. Obviously I wasn't willing to allow Bf8 anymore. Luckily here Black can hardly play d5, because his e5-pawn would be too weak. My opponent's next move deprives him of this option once and for all.} Ba7 10. Bb3 {I spent some time choosing between the game continuation and the more positional 10.a4.} Re8 11. h3 Be6 12. Bc2 d5 13. exd5 {By transposition we have arrived to a position which is quite tricky for unprepared Black player to face, and was popular some time ago. The good thing was I had analysed this stuff a couple of years ago, back in times, when setups with a4 or Bg5 hadn't been yet so well known. The bad thing was that I couldn't have recalled anything and I was facing the World Champion} Nxd5 {Black has other recaptures as well:} (13... Qxd5 14. Re1 Rad8) (13... Bxd5 14. Re1 Qd7 15. Nh4 (15. Be3!? {is a slower approach}) 15... Rad8 16. Nhf5 Kh8 17. d4 {this is a 'forced draw' when Black defends right} exd4 18. Nxg7 Rxe1+ (18... Kxg7? 19. Bxh6+ Kh8 20. Qd2 $40 {with a very powerful attack 1-0 (29) Ganguly,S (2660)-Kollars,D (2436) Caleta 2016}) 19. Qxe1 Kxg7 (19... Bxg2!? 20. Kxg2 Qd5+ (20... d3) 21. Kf1 Qb5+ 22. Kg2 Qd5+ 23. Kf1 {1/2 (23) Laine,J (2311)-Gerhards,G (2304) ICCF email 2018}) (19... d3!?) 20. Bxh6+ Kh8 (20... Kxh6? 21. Qd2+) 21. Qd2 (21. Bg5) 21... Rg8 (21... Nh7) 22. Qf4 Qd6?? {Vishy didn't handle it well, but it was a blitz game after all.} (22... Nh5! 23. Bg7+ Kxg7 24. Nxh5+ Kf8 25. Qh6+ Ke7 26. Re1+ Kd8 27. Nf6 Rxg2+ 28. Kf1 Rxf2+ 29. Kxf2 d3+ 30. Re3 Bxe3+ 31. Kxe3 Qe6+ 32. Kxd3 $11) 23. Qh4 Qxg3 24. fxg3 {1-0 (26) Giri,A (2773)-Anand,V (2804) Stavanger 2015}) 14. Re1 Qd6 15. Nh4 Qe7 16. Nhf5 ({On the correspondence level, the following endgame is quite common:} 16. Qh5 Rad8 17. d4 Nf4 (17... exd4? 18. Bxh6 gxh6 19. Qxh6 $40) (17... Nf6 18. Qf3 Bd5 (18... exd4 19. Nhf5 Qf8 20. Nxh6+) 19. Qd3 $36) 18. Bxf4 exf4 19. Nf1 (19. Ngf5 Qg5 20. Qxg5 hxg5 21. Nf3 g6 22. Nh6+ Kg7 23. Ng4 f6) 19... Rd5 20. Qxd5 Bxd5 21. Rxe7 Nxe7 {Maybe in corr chess this is an easy draw, but to my eyes it looks as comfortable edge for White, at least in terms of OTB chess.}) 16... Qf6 17. Qf3? {As it turned out - the bluff. But in the game I was pretty confident, I guess I mixed up variations. The other logical continuation is probably better, but it involves a queen sac.} (17. Qg4 {with the following line in mind:} g6 18. Nxh6+ Kf8 19. Ne4! Bxf2+ 20. Nxf2 Bxg4 21. Nhxg4 {with a crazy game ahead. Objectively my choice was bad, but it's refutation was anything but easy.}) 17... g6? {Frankly I didn't see this. But both alternatives are better:} (17... Kh7 18. d4 g6 {and the engine suggests} 19. Ne4 {which feels entirely wrong} (19. Be4 exd4) 19... Qxf5 20. Ng5+ Qxg5 21. Bxg5 hxg5 22. dxe5 {and in general terms three pieces should be better than the queen, but thanks to Qh5+ White is only marginally worse.}) ({or} 17... Kh8! 18. d4 (18. Re4 g6 19. Nh4 {is a disaster. After} Qg7 {the black pawns will start rolling.}) 18... g6 19. Be4 {Magnus calculated this far, but he either didn't see or misevaluated the next move} exd4! {The refutation. White has to go for a sad endgame after} 20. Nxh6 Qxf3 21. Bxf3 Kg7 22. Bd2 {which might be holdable but no more than that.}) 18. Nxh6+ Kg7 19. Qxf6+ Nxf6 20. Ne4 (20. Bd1 {is the alternative mentioned by Magnus, but Black obviously has some compensation for the missing pawn:} Rad8 21. Be2 Nd5 22. Ng4 Nf4!? (22... f5 23. Nh2 Nf4 24. Bxf4 exf4 25. Ngf1 $14 {1/2 (74) Harikrishna,P (2737)-Sethuraman,S (2640) Baku 2015})) 20... Nh7 ({In the game I also wasn't entirely sure about} 20... Nxe4 21. dxe4 Rh8 22. Ng4 Bxg4 23. hxg4 Rh4 {but here both} 24. Kf1 ({and} 24. Bd1 {should lead to some edge for White})) 21. g4 (21. h4 {is probably better, but I was a bit unease about my h6-knight.} Rf8!? {A sophisticated move.} (21... f5 22. Ng5 Bd5 23. Nxh7 Kxh7 24. g4) (21... Rad8 22. g4 f5 23. gxf5 gxf5 {1-0 (75) Rabiega,R (2498)-Feller,S (2573) Germany 2017} 24. Ng3! {is a far better version of my game.}) 22. g4 f5 {Now with the f5-pawn safely guarded, White has to go Ng5 and the position becomes a complicated one with h6-knight indeed being hanging.}) 21... f5 22. gxf5 gxf5 23. Ng3 f4 24. Ngf5+ Kg6 {For some reason I was expecting only} (24... Kf6 {which according to engine doesn't change much:} 25. d4 Ng5 26. Ng4+ Kf7 27. Kg2 Bd5+ 28. Kf1 {with similar equality.}) 25. d4 Ng5 26. Kg2! Bd5+ 27. Kf1 {Black has a perpetual but apparently nothing better. Of course at this moment I didn't expect Magnus to take repetition.} Nf3 28. Rd1 (28. Re2 {I calculated this valid alternative as well, but it felt a bit unnatural with the potential ...Bc4 in the air.}) 28... Rad8 29. Ne7+! {The simplest, and I think he missed it} Kxh6 30. Nxd5 Nfxd4! {Luckily for Magnus he had this escape. Now the game should peter out to a draw.} 31. cxd4 Rxd5 32. dxe5 Rxd1+ 33. Bxd1 Kg5 34. h4+ Kf5 35. Bc2+?! {Played based on a miscalculation.} ({After the normal} 35. Bd2 {it's Black who would have to work for a draw.} Bd4) 35... Kg4 36. Bd1+?! (36. Bd2 {was still a lesser evil.}) 36... f3 37. Bg5 Nxe5 38. Bc2! {Luckily I have this move, which with a correct follow-up guarantees draw.} ({In the intended variation} 38. Rc1 Nd3 39. Rc4+ {I completely forgot Black has} Kh3! {and he is already winning now.}) 38... Nc4 39. Re1 Rxe1+ 40. Kxe1 Nxb2 41. Bf6! Nc4 42. Bg6 Bc5 {It seems as if Black is going to stop the h-pawn, but my next move disturbs his coordination.} 43. Bf7! (43. h5 Bf8 $15 {would be very unpleasant to defend even if defensible.}) 43... Kf5 {Offering a draw.} (43... Bb4+ 44. Kd1! Nd6 45. Be6+ Kh5 (45... Kf4 46. h5 Ne4 47. Bd4 Kg5 48. Bd5 $11) 46. Bd5 Kg4 47. Be6+ {with a draw.  An interesting battle, and my first draw in classical chess against Magnus!}) 1/2-1/2

