1. c4 g6 2. Nc3 c5 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 Nc6 {In this symmetrical line White doesn't really aim for an opening advantage, he is just heading for a long-term positional fight.} 5. a3 d6 6. Rb1 {[%cal Gb2b4]} a5 {Preventing the expansion b2-b4.} 7. e3 e5 {This move is Black's main choice. It is directed against the advance d2-d4, but has a clear downside - it weakens the d5 square.} ({In the event of} 7... Nf6 {White continues in the same style:} 8. Nge2 O-O 9. d3 {achieving a flexible position.}) 8. Nge2 Nge7 9. O-O O-O 10. d3 Rb8 11. Bd2 Be6 {Now Black is threatening ...d6-d5, so White must react accordingly.} (11... Bf5 {makes less sense. White just shouldn't reply with e3-e4, blocking his light-squared bishop.} {After} 12. Qc2 Qd7 13. Nb5 {[%cal Ge2c3] he has fine play on the light squares.}) 12. Nd5 {The main continuation.} (12. Qb3 f5 13. Nd5 Nxd5 14. cxd5 Bf7 15. Qc2 Ne7 16. e4 b5 $11 {0-1 Gurevich,M (2641)-Lautier,J (2675)/Moscow 2002/CBM 090 (41)}) 12... b5 (12... f5?! 13. Nec3 Kh8 14. b4 {is the kind of position that White is striving for. His play on the queenside is more effective than Black's attempts on the opposite flank.}) 13. cxb5 Rxb5 (13... Bxd5 14. Bxd5 Nxd5 15. bxc6 Qb6 16. Qa4 {[%csl Ra5]} Ra8 17. Nc3 Ne7 18. e4 Qxc6 (18... Nxc6?? {falls into a fork:} 19. Nd5 Qb7 20. Qxc6! Qxc6 21. Ne7+ $18) 19. Qc4 {keeping a slight pressure.}) 14. Qa4 Qb8 15. Nc7!? {Only this move is a novelty!} (15. Nxe7+ Nxe7 16. Nc3 Rxb2 17. Qxa5 Bf5 18. Rxb2 Qxb2 19. Ne4 Bxe4 20. Bxe4 d5 21. Bf3 Rc8 $11 {½-½ Sehner,N-Teipelke,H/Germany 1991/Corr 2000 (35)}) 15... Qxc7!? {Aronian goes for an exchange sacrifice. It wasn't necessary, as Black could equalise after the obvious 15...xb2.} (15... Rxb2 16. Nxe6 fxe6 (16... Rxb1?! 17. Nxf8 Bxf8 18. Bxc6 Nxc6 19. Qxc6 Rb2 20. Rd1 Qb3 21. Nc3 Rxd2 22. Rxd2 Qxc3 23. Re2 Qxa3 24. Qd5 $16 {[%cal Ge2a2]}) 17. Nc3 d5 18. Rfc1 e4 19. Rxb2 Qxb2 20. Rc2 Qa1+ 21. Rc1 Qb2 $11) 16. Qxb5 Rb8 17. Qa4 Bb3 18. Qh4 Bc2 19. Qc4 Bb3 ({In the event of} 19... Bxb1 20. Rxb1 {White has a stable advantage thanks to pair of bishops and control over the light squares.}) 20. Qc1 {Carlsen has grounds not to repeat moves, but to play for a win.} Qb6 21. Re1 d5 {Black's superiority in space yields him a certain compensation for the exchange.} 22. Nc3 Rd8?! (22... a4! {It is useful to fix the queenside..} 23. e4 d4 24. Nd5 Nxd5 25. exd5 Ne7 $44) 23. e4! d4 24. Nd5 Nxd5 25. exd5 Rxd5?! {Another exchange sacrifice! It worked fine in the current game, but, frankly speaking, is too brave to be true.} (25... Bxd5?! 26. Bxd5 Rxd5 27. Qc4 Rd7 28. b4 $16) (25... Ne7 {was a worthy alternative.} 26. Bg5 f6 27. Rxe5! Qc7 (27... fxe5? 28. Bxe7 Rc8 29. Bh3 $18) 28. d6 Qxd6 29. Qxc5 Qxe5 (29... Qxc5 30. Rxc5 fxg5 31. Rxa5 $14) 30. Qxe5 fxe5 31. Bxe7 $14) 26. Bh6 Bf6?! {Levon is logical. It would not be natural to retreat with the rook, even though it was objectively better.} (26... Rd7 27. Bxg7 Kxg7 28. Bxc6 Qxc6 29. Rxe5 Qf3 30. Qxc5 Bd5 31. Rxd5 Rxd5 {with sufficient compensation for the pawn.}) 27. Bxd5 Bxd5 28. Qd1? {Here and on the next few moves Magnus rejects the idea of opening lines by the slightly risky move f2-f4.} (28. f4 e4 (28... Qb5 29. Qc2 $18 {[%csl Rc5,Re5][%cal Gb1c1]}) 29. dxe4 Bb3 30. e5 Be7 31. f5 $16) 28... Be7 29. h4 ({Again} 29. f4 {, though less effective than on the previous move, but still was called for.}) 29... Qb7 30. Kh2 ({Once again, it was necessary to play more actively:} 30. f4 f6 31. Kh2 {Later on White will take out his major pieces, to bring the queen on f2, double the rooks on the e-file, etc.}) 30... f6 {Stabilising the central pawn chain.} 31. Qe2 Nd8! 32. Bd2? {Here it was the last moment when Magnus could stir up complications and try to make use of his extra material.} (32. b3 Bxb3 (32... Nf7 33. Bd2 Bf3 34. Qf1 $13 {Compared to the continuation in the game, here White has certain prospects on the queenside.}) 33. h5 {White can harass the opponents' king. Here is a sample line:} a4 34. hxg6 hxg6 35. Qg4 Kh7 36. Qh3 Kg8 37. f4 Qf3 38. fxe5 Be6 39. Qg2 Qh5+ 40. Kg1 Qxh6 41. exf6 Bxf6 42. Rb6 $16 {[%csl Re6,Rf6]}) 32... Bf3 33. Qf1 a4 {After a series of passive moves Magnus found himself in a miserable position. White can't open a single line for his major pieces.} 34. Qh3 h5 35. Ba5 Nf7 36. Qe6? {Typical time-trouble reflex.} (36. Bd2 $17) 36... Bd5 {The activity of queen was imaginary, it has to retreat.} 37. Qh3 (37. Qb6 Qd7 {[%cal Gd7f5] and White can't cover the holes near his king.}) 37... Qb5 38. Bc7 Qxd3 $19 39. g4! {The best practical chance.} Qd2 40. Kg1 Be6 (40... hxg4 41. Qxg4 Qh6 {and White is defenseless against tche threats f5 or g5!}) 41. Qg2 hxg4 {Black has already too many pawns and is dominating.} 42. Rbc1 Kg7 {Instead, the pawn on b2 could be comfortably captured.} 43. Bb6 c4?! (43... f5 $19 {[%cal Gf5f4]}) 44. Qc6 g3!? 45. fxg3 Bh3 46. Qf3?! {Believe me, I am not harsh in my evaluations, one has to bear in mind that both players are in time trouble from the very beginning!} (46. Qh1! Bg4 47. Qg2 Qd3 {This way White could keep the b2 alive, but Black retains the initiative.}) 46... Qxb2 47. Ba7 Qb3 {47...c3 was, naturally, more convincing. The move in the text is typical for time trouble, when a side having an advantage tries to reduce the material as much as possible, so as not to give the opponent unnecessary practical chances. This often leads to the opposite effect.} 48. Qxb3 cxb3 49. Bxd4 Bxa3 50. Ra1 Bb4 51. Reb1 exd4 (51... Bf5 52. Rxa4 Bxb1 53. Rxb4 exd4 54. Rxb3 Bc2 $19) 52. Rxa4 Bc5 53. Ra5 Bb6 54. Rb5 Bc7 55. R5xb3 Be5 {The position remains winning for Black, but now he has to work at it for some time.} 56. Kf2 Nd6 57. Kf3 Be6 58. Ra3 Bd5+ 59. Ke2 Nb5 60. Ra5 Nc3+ 61. Kf2 d3 62. Rxd5 Nxd5 63. Rb7+ Bc7 64. Kf3 Kh6 65. Rb3 Bxg3! {A neat tactical trick, winning an important pawn.} 66. Rxd3 (66. Kxg3 d2 67. Rd3 Nc3 68. Rxd2 Ne4+ $19) 66... Nf4 67. Rd8 Nh5 {Now Black's win is just a matter of time.} 68. Kg4 f5+ 69. Kh3 Be5 70. Ra8 Bf6 71. Ra6 Kg7 72. Ra7+ Kf8 73. Rb7 Be7 74. Ra7 Kf7 75. Ra6 Nf6 76. Kg3 Ne4+ 77. Kh3 Bc5 78. Rc6 Bd6 79. Ra6 Kg7 80. Ra8 Be5 81. Ra7+ Kh6 82. Rb7 Nf6 83. Re7 Ng4 84. Ra7 Bf6 85. Rf7 Kh5 {There were many mistakes along the way. However, in conditions of rapid chess, where the time trouble starts from the very beginning, you can't demand too much even from elite players.} (85... Kh5 86. Rh7+ Nh6 $19 {[%csl Rh4]}) 0-1

