[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "B31"]
[WhiteElo "2752"]
[BlackElo "2875"]
[PlyCount "136"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 {when faced with this move against the same opponent in a blitz game in 2018 Aronian chose 3.c3} 3. Bb5 g6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 {Carlsen has had quite an extensive experience in this positions from his match with Caruana} 5. d3 Qc7 {Black's idea is to play a quick e5 and then perhaps f6-h5 to prevent in advance f4 ideas. Compared to the previous game where he chose 6...c7, he keeps his  on f8 to protect c5} (5... Bg7 6. O-O Qc7 {was the similar idea played in the World Championship match} 7. Re1 e5 8. a3 Nf6 9. b4 O-O 10. Nbd2 Bg4 11. h3 Bxf3 12. Nxf3 cxb4 13. axb4 a5 14. bxa5 Rxa5 15. Bd2 (15. Rxa5 Qxa5 16. Bd2 {was at least interesting}) 15... Raa8 16. Qb1 Nd7 17. Qb4 Rfe8 18. Bc3 b5 $11 {½-½ (49) Caruana,F (2832)-Carlsen,M (2835) London 2018}) 6. O-O e5 7. Be3 Nf6 8. Qe1!? {practically a novelty. White's idea is the typical c3 putting some pressure on e5.} (8. Nbd2?! Ng4 {White has to give up his pride and joy}) (8. h3 {was possible as well}) 8... Bg4 {of course Carlsen doesn't allow his opponent to execute his idea} (8... Be7 $143 9. Qc3 Nd7 10. a4 {[%cal Gb1d2] White has a harmonious position}) 9. Nbd2 Nh5! {a strong move that is keeping an eye of f4 breaks while also preparing f6} (9... Be7?! 10. h3 Bxf3 (10... Be6 11. Ng5 $16) 11. Nxf3 O-O 12. Nh2) 10. a3 (10. h3 Be6 11. Ng5 Bd7) 10... Be7? ({a move like} 10... f6! {to consolidate e5 was needed for exemple} 11. Qb1 (11. h3 Be6 12. Qe2 {does not make a lot of use of 8.e1}) 11... a5 12. b4 cxb4 13. axb4 Bxb4 14. Qb3 (14. Qxb4 {it's interesting but doesn't give White more than compensation} axb4 15. Rxa8+ Bc8 16. Nc4 Kf7 17. Bc5 Kg7 $15 {Black's  is quite safe}) 14... Qc8 $13 {[%cal Gg4e6]}) 11. Qb1! {not only original but also very strong. Now Black cannot stop the thematic b4 and he faces serious problems} (11. Rb1 a5 {goes nowhere}) 11... Nf4 {Black is also trying to get some counterplay} ({we can see White idea after} 11... a5 12. b4 axb4 (12... cxb4 13. axb4 a4 14. Qb2 f6 15. d4 $16) 13. axb4 Rxa1 14. Qxa1 cxb4 15. Nxe5 Bf6 16. Bd4 $16) 12. b4 Ne2+ (12... b6 13. bxc5 bxc5 {the engine claims just a small edge but I am pretty sure nobody would really enjoy his time as Black in this position. However, it was probably the lesser evil as Black's activity after 12...e2 will not amount to much}) 13. Kh1 Nd4 14. Ng1! {Also introducing c3 ideas and the  cannot go back to e6} (14. bxc5 Nxf3 15. Nxf3 Bxf3 16. gxf3 {was also possible but it is understandable that Aronian didn't want to spoil the pawn strucuture around his }) 14... g5 {since his strategical position is compromised Black needs to try something} (14... cxb4 15. axb4 g5 {preventing f4} (15... O-O 16. f4 $36) 16. c3 Ne6 {would for exemple keep the material equal but Black is still facing a difficult struggle} 17. Nc4 b5 (17... O-O 18. Bxa7 {is already possible} b5 19. Bb6 Rxa1 20. Qxa1 Qb8 21. Ne3 Bh5 22. Qa6 $16) 18. Na5 $16) 15. bxc5 Bxc5 16. Bxg5 {White needs to accept the gift one way or the other. However it's not at all clear how Black will take advantage of the opening of the g file} Rg8 17. Bh4 Be7 18. Bg3 (18. Bxe7 Qxe7 19. f3 Be6 (19... Bh5 20. Nb3 Qg5 21. Rf2 Qe3 22. Nh3 {Black's attack ends here}) 20. c3 $16 {also looked grim for Black}) 18... O-O-O {Black needs also a shelter for his } 19. Nc4 f6 20. a4 (20. f4 Be6 21. fxe5 Rxg3 {even though White would still be close to winning, it would create some unnecessary mess}) 20... Ne6 (20... h5 {was perhaps worth a try to muddy the waters a little for exemple} 21. f3 Be6 22. c3 Bxc4 23. cxd4 Ba6 {and at least Black has some active play}) 21. f3 Bh5 22. Bf2 {now, after all the dust has settled, White is a clear pawn up with easier play} Bc5 (22... Kb8 23. a5 {doesn't make Black's life any easier}) 23. Bxc5 Nxc5 24. Ne2 {getting the second  back into play} Bf7 25. Ne3 Qa5 (25... h5 26. a5 h4 {is not doing much after} 27. h3 {White's two  around his  assure him against any attacking Black options}) 26. Qe1 Qxe1 {the  swap was the only chance for Black to create some resemblance of counterplay on the queenside} 27. Rfxe1 a5 28. Kg1 {White continues the logical play activating his } h5 29. h4 {White could allow the pawn to get to h4 but it is more logical to block in on a light square} Kc7 30. Kf2 Be6 {now White makes a few passing moves with the  before returning to the right plan of the d4 break} 31. Rg1 Rh8 32. Rh1 Rd7 33. Rhd1 Bf7 (33... Rhd8 34. Ng3 Bf7 {it was an idea to make it harder for White to achieve the central break although it would still be a rather desperate fight} 35. Ngf5 Ne6 36. Rdb1 {now White can play at his leisure combining play on the queenside with a g4 break at the right moment}) 34. d4 exd4 35. Rxd4 Rxd4 36. Nxd4 Rd8 37. Ne2 {a good regrouping} (37. Nef5 Bg6 38. Ke3 {was also nice for White}) 37... Rd2 38. Ke1 Rd8 39. Nf5 Ra8 {Black tries to activate his  via a6, b6} 40. Nc3 Be6 41. Ne3 Kd6 {Black take his  closer to the kingside to be better place against g4 ideas} (41... Ra6 {doesn't achieve its aim} 42. Kf2 Rb6 43. Rh1! $18 {[%cal Gg2g4] the White Knights are doing a very good job protecting White's weaknesses and now g4 become unstoppable} (43. g4 hxg4 44. fxg4 Rb4 45. Kf3 Bg8 {would give Black some chances})) 42. Rd1+ Ke7 43. Rd4 {White has centralised his pieces and is ready for the winning f5 following by g4} Ra6 44. Kd2 Rb6 45. Nf5+ {this is also winning} (45. Rd5! {would finish off the world champion in style} cxd5 46. Nexd5+) 45... Bxf5 46. exf5 Rb2 47. Rc4 Kd6 48. Ne4+ {here it is, I believe, where things started to go wrong for Aronian. Objectively 48.e4 was the best choice. However, playing with such a demanding time control, it feels like he invested too much of his remaining time on this move} (48. g4 {was also a valid choice} b5 (48... Rb4 49. Rxb4 axb4 50. Na2 Nxa4 51. Nxb4 {also White looks totally winning}) 49. axb5 cxb5 50. Rd4+ Ke7 51. Rd5 $18) 48... Nxe4+ 49. Rxe4 Ra2 50. g4 hxg4 51. fxg4 Kd5 52. Kd3 Ra3+ 53. c3 b5 54. h5? {left with just a few minutes on the clock White goes wrong} (54. g5 b4 55. Rg4! b3 56. g6 Ra1 57. g7 {perhaps Aronian forgot he would be queening with check?!}) 54... b4? {very tempting but giving White chances again} (54... Ra1 55. axb5 cxb5 56. Re6 (56. Re8 Rd1+ 57. Ke2 Rh1 58. Kf3 a4 {Black has enough activity to draw}) 56... Rg1 57. Rxf6 Rxg4 58. Rb6 Rg3+ 59. Kd2 b4 60. cxb4 axb4 61. f6 Ra3 62. Ke2 Ra7 63. Kf3 Kc5 $8 64. Re6 b3 65. Kg4 b2 66. Re1 Kd5 (66... Ra1? 67. f7 $18) 67. Rb1 Ke6 {was not such a trivial draw}) 55. Rd4+ Ke5 56. Rc4 Rxa4 (56... b3 57. Re4+ Kd6 58. h6 Ra1 59. Re2 Rh1 60. Kc4 Rxh6 61. Re6+ Kc7 62. Kxb3 {would be very difficult for Black}) 57. h6?! {White misses his last winning chance} (57. Re4+! Kd5 58. g5 Ra3 (58... fxg5 59. f6 $18) 59. Re3!! $18 {would be not that easy to find even with time on the clock} (59. gxf6?? Rxc3+ 60. Kd2 Kxe4 61. f7 Rd3+ 62. Kc2 Rd8 {would end tragic for White})) 57... Ra1 58. cxb4 (58. Re4+! {was the last practical chance that would still be a draw if both players found amazing resources} Kd5 59. g5 b3 (59... fxg5 60. c4+ Kd6 61. f6 $18) 60. Re2 fxg5 61. c4+ Kd6 62. c5+ Kd5 63. f6 b2 64. Rxb2 Ke6 65. Rb6 Kxf6 66. Rxc6+ Kf5 (66... Ke5? {allows White to push his h pawn to the 7th rank} 67. Rg6 Kf5 68. h7 Rh1 69. Rg7 $18) 67. Rd6 Rh1 68. Kd2 Rh2+ 69. Kc3 Ke5 (69... Rh1? 70. Kb3 Rh4 71. c6 $18) 70. Kb3 Rh4 71. Rg6 Kf5 72. c6 Kxg6 73. c7 Rxh6 (73... Rh3+ 74. Kc2 Rh2+ $11) 74. c8=Q Rh4! {would still be a draw}) 58... axb4 59. Rxc6 {now is very easy for Black} (59. Re4+ Kd5 60. g5 fxg5 61. f6?? Ra3+ $19 {is where we see the difference with 58.cb4 included}) 59... Ra3+ 60. Kc4 Rc3+ 61. Kb5 Rh3 62. Kxb4 Rxh6 63. Kc5 Rh1 64. Re6+ Kf4 65. Rxf6 Kxg4 66. Kd6 Kg5 67. Ke7 Ra1 68. Rf8 Ra7+ {After Aronian's brilliant 11.b1! the World champion looked in very big danger. To say he managed a miraculous escape it would probably be a small understatement.} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Norway Chess Armageddon 7th"]
[Site "Stavanger"]
[Date "2019.06.05"]
[Round "2"]
[White "Aronian, Levon"]
[Black "Carlsen, Magnus"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "B31"]
[WhiteElo "2752"]
[BlackElo "2875"]
[PlyCount "86"]

{Armageddon was an interesting experiment, posing lots of challenges to the players. Less than 30 minutes after often, like in this example, a gruelling classical game with emotional ups and downs, the players have to re-adjust and get ready for completely new circumstances.The slow pace of the previous game is changed to a much faster format, the scoring of the game has changed in the sense that only 2 results are possible, White winning the game, or Black taking the full point with either a draw or a win, and as well the colors are not reversed meaning often a repetition of the opening duel from the classical might be repeated with the players either haven gotten a few pointers from their on-site or online seconds, or rush to their rooms to briefly check it wth their engines. Considerig the players also have basic human needs, like getting something to eat in the brief break between games, despite the focus on the Armageddon blunders, one can only be impressed with the level of play in general, and this game specifically.} 1. e4!? {Aronian has been known as a 1 d4/c4/Nf3 player, but has recently managed to add 1 e4 succesfully to his repertoire.} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 {Caruana's choice in the first 3 white games in the WCh match. As Black did well against it in the match, the pendulum swung to 3 d4. but now in Norway Chess both Aronian and later Caruana managed to pose Magnus opening problems.} g6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 5. d3 Bg7!? {By far the main move.} ({But in the classical game Magnus had experimented with the much rarer} 5... Qc7!? 6. O-O e5 {The point of omitting 5...Bg7 is exactly that Blacks bishop thus keeps a choice, and, like in the game, gives Black the flexibility of using it on the f8-a3 diagonal. But Aronian manages to put his finger at a sore spot:} 7. Be3 Nf6 8. Qe1 Bg4 9. Nbd2 Nh5 10. a3 Be7 {Around here Magnus went to the confession booth, saying he quite liked his position, as it wasn't really clear what White could do next?} 11. Qb1! {However, convincingly answered that question. The problem with the bishop not being on g7 is highlighted after} a5 12. b4! axb4 ({even worse,} 12... cxb4 13. axb4 Bxb4? 14. Qxb4! axb4 15. Rxa8+ {wins also the rook on h8 again due to the bishop missing on g7.}) 13. axb4 Rxa1 {when} 14. Qxa1 {now attacks the e5 pawn.}) 6. h3 e5 7. O-O Qc7!? {Different timing, but the same idea. Black wants to protect the e5-pawn, giving himself the option of putting the knight on f6. Aronian had to battle it twice, but seemed to like it so much that he actually tried it out with colors reversed against Caruana!} 8. Be3 ({Not only having 2 hours for the whole game, unlike Aronian's 10 minutes, Caruana in round 9 came up with} 8. a3!? {against Magnus. It seems innocent, but after} Nf6 9. Be3 c4 10. Nc3 cxd3 11. Qxd3 O-O 12. Rfd1 Re8 13. Bc5 Bf8 14. Bxf8 Kxf8 15. Rd2 {Caruana did pose some opening problems.}) 8... b6 9. Nbd2 Ne7! {This might seem illogical, but as White has set himself up for attacking the e5-pawn after a black ...Nf6 with Nc4, Magnus switches plans, aiming for an attack with ...f5, as Whites misplaced bishop on e3 will win him some time based on the ...f4 threat.} 10. a3 O-O 11. b4 cxb4 12. axb4 f5 13. Nc4 Be6 14. Ng5 Bd7 15. Qe2?! {My main point is that this game generally very much have the quality of a classical game, but this move might still illustrate some difference. While looking logical, it serves no specific purpose and plays into Black's hands.} ({Given more time Aronian would likely have played} 15. Ra3! {when Qa1 next both puts pressure on e5, as well along the a-line. And if} h6 16. Nf3 f4 17. Bd2 g5 18. Bc3 Ng6 {then} 19. d4 {has quite more force than in the game.}) 15... h6 16. Nf3 f4 17. Bd2 g5 18. Bc3 Ng6 19. d4 {The passive 19.Nh2 was to be considered, but Aronian logically plays in the centre to counter Black's attack on the flank.} exd4 20. Bxd4 g4! {In an ideal world Black would like ...h5 and ...g4, but in a sharp battle very every tempo counts, one cannot care about trifles such as pawn structure, so the World Champion hurries to create some threats.} 21. hxg4 Bxg4 22. c3?! {Like in move 15 a logical move, covering the d4 bishop.} ({However,} 22. Ncd2! {while looking like a retreat, protects the colleague on f3 and meets} Bxd4 {with} 23. Qc4+!) 22... c5 23. bxc5 bxc5 24. Bxg7 Qxg7 25. Nd6 {The point of White's play is that 25...Nh4? now is met by 26.Qc4+, and after} Kh8! 26. Nf5!? {This pawn sacrifice seem to deflect Black's attention from the kingside just in time, as after 26...Bxf5 27 exf5 Rxf5 Black's threats have dissapeared and e.g. 28 Qd3 gives full compensation.} Rxf5! {This, however, is a cold awakening. Black now keeps the bishop on g4 instead of the rook on f8, which means imminent threats to the white king, with 28...Bxf3 as the obvious, but hard to prevent threat.} 27. exf5 Nh4 28. f6! {Objectively the best move, but} (28. Rxa7!? {deserves to be mentioned, as} Bxf3! 29. Rxa8+ Kh7 {while looking crushing, actually allows the funny} 30. Ra7! {when White very much stays in the game, even if Black is better after} Qxa7 31. Qd3!? (31. gxf3? Qg7+ {mates.})) 28... Qxf6 29. Ra6?! {Black again threatens ...Bxf3 followed by mating in the g-line, and Aronian tries to maximise his counterplay as quickly as possible.} ({Objectively better, however, was} 29. Rfe1! Bxf3 30. Ra6!! {Now easiest is} Bxe2 (30... Qg7) ({or} 30... Qg5 31. Qe5+ {keeps White very much in the game.}) 31. Rxf6 Kg7 32. Rxf4 Ng6 33. Rf5 Bd3 34. Rxc5 a5 {when stopping Black's passed pawn will take so much effort that White has no realistic hopes of winning - asking the question of is it even relevant to discuss lines where White saves a draw in an Armageddon game!?}) 29... Qg7 30. Rxh6+ Qxh6 31. Qe5+ Qg7 32. Qxg7+?! ({Much better was} 32. Nxh4 {as after} Qxe5 33. Ng6+ Kg7 34. Nxe5 {unlike in the game, White's knight will not get into trouble, but nevertheless Black easily will get the desired draw anyway.}) 32... Kxg7 33. Nxh4 a5! 34. Ra1 a4 35. Ra3 Kf6 36. Kf1 ({The computer says that White's best chance by far is} 36. Nf3 {, but that's a chance to make a worthless draw as after} Bxf3 37. gxf3 {obviously Whites winning chances are zero. Aronian does what one has to do, but so does Magnus.}) 36... Ke5 37. Ke1 Kd5 38. f3 Bh5 39. Kd2 Be8 40. Kc1 Kc4 41. Nf5 Ra6 42. Ng7 Bg6 43. Kd2 Kd5 {The confusion he tried to create only got his knight trapped, and White resigned.  The question is to what standard we should just evaluate such games? That it was great television Norwegian TV2 had no doubts about during their national broadcast.} 0-1

