1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 b5 3. Bg2 Bb7 4. Na3 a6 ({I'm not entirely sure what's wrong with} 4... b4 5. Nc4 Nc6 {perhaps} ({Or} 5... a5 {when Black will gain a tempo on the knight with ...d5 later}) 6. Nd4 d5 7. Nxc6 Bxc6 8. Na5 {gives White some kind of strategic edge.}) 5. c4 b4 6. Nc2 e6 ({Black can also play the immediate} 6... c5 {which should at least avoid the strategic grip that Black suffers in the game.} 7. O-O e6 8. d4 cxd4 9. Ncxd4 Be7 10. b3 O-O 11. Bb2 d6 12. Qd2 Qb6 13. a3! bxa3 14. Bxa3 {Efimenko,Z (2648)-Moiseenko,A (2707) Dubai 2014, and White was better. I always think these lines where Black has already set the pawn structure on the queenside are better for White, because Black seems to have less dynamism in his position.}) 7. d4 a5 ({Strangely enough there have already been many games played from this position. This came as a surprise to me and shows in a way how bad my theory is as I've never even seen this line before.} 7... c5 8. d5! {A typical thrust in the Queen's Indian} exd5 9. Nh4 g6 10. Ne3 Bg7 11. Nxd5 Nxd5 12. Bxd5 Nc6 13. Ng2 O-O 14. Nf4 {Lenderman,A (2561)-Abdumalik,Z (2344) Caleta 2014, when White had a strategic advantage.}) (7... Be4 {might be the most solid approach.} 8. O-O Be7 9. Bg5 a5 10. Ne5 Bxg2 11. Kxg2 O-O 12. Qd3 d6 13. Qf3 Ra7 14. Nd3 {Vaganian,R (2568)-Stark,L (2432) Germany 2010 also gave some advantage for White. Looking through these games and also the results, you wonder if the whole line is just bad for Black.}) 8. O-O Be7 ({This was Black's last chance for the limiting} 8... Be4 {perhaps in view of what happened in the game, he should have taken it}) 9. d5! {Now I think White is clearly better as it's not easy for Black to create anything in the centre.} Na6 (9... exd5 10. Nfd4 c6 11. cxd5 Nxd5 12. Nf5 {looks like a very pleasant version of a pawn sacrifice line that often occurs in the Queen's Indian} O-O 13. e4 $36) 10. Nfd4 Nc5 11. Re1 O-O 12. e4 e5 13. Nf5 $16 {Already White has a clear strategic advantage. The black bishop on b7 bites on granite while by contrast White is very well placed to build up a quick attack on the kingside. The "Kasparov" knight on f5 in particular casts a spell on the position. I think what we now see in this game and also other games in Shamkir is why Carlsen could now be called "The Pitbull" What he demonstrated in this tournament and has been doing for a number of years is that when he gains an advantage it's incredibly hard to shake his grip, rather like a pitbull. It was quite scary to see in this game that Maxime lost without making an easily identifiable mistake. Perhaps he just played a slightly dubious opening. But that's enough. Kramnik make a slight tactical error against Magnus in their game just out of the opening and that was enough to ensure a loss. It's a quite frightening level. Watching the world snooker championships this year, I watched a frame where Chinese player Ding played a slightly poor break-off shot against Judd Trump and never got another shot, as Trump potted a long red and then cleared all the balls without any effort. I think it's fair to say it's similar in chess now, that one slight mistake can be enough to cost you the game, or at least to make life very difficult.} d6 14. Bg5! {Straightforward and effective. White "freezes" the knight on f6.} (14. Qf3 Nfd7!) 14... Nxd5?! {Allowing White a tactical possibility.} ({I would have played} 14... h6 {because it's a reaction move to g5, and I like reaction moves. I tend to follow just what my opponent does.} 15. Bh4!? (15. Bxh6? gxh6 16. Qd2 Bc8!) (15. Be3!? {can be met by} Bc8! (15... Nfxe4? 16. Bxe4 Nxe4 17. Qg4 Ng5 18. h4 h5 19. Nxe7+ $18) 16. Qf3? Ncxe4) (15. Bd2!? Nd3! 16. Bxh6 Nxe1 17. Bxg7 Bc8 (17... Nxc2! 18. Qxc2 Bc8 $19) 18. Nce3 Nxg2 19. Kxg2 {although this is hard to believe after} Re8) 15... Kh7 16. f4 Ng8 17. Nxe7 Nxe7 18. Ne3 {I must confess also doesn't look a barrel of laughs for Black, but it might be hard to crack his position after} f6) 15. Bh6! {Magnus rarely misses a chance like this. I think it was Anish Giri who half-jokingly suggested at Wijk Aan Zee that Magnus was just a trickster.  Still it's easy to underestimate with all these top guys just how good they are at little tactical tricks and trapping their opponents.} gxh6 16. Qg4+ Bg5 17. cxd5 Kh8 18. h4 Bf6 {Black is a pawn up, but that's the end of the good news. His pawn structure is a mess, his king remains unsafe in the long term while White enjoys a strategic strangehold on the light squares on the kingside.} 19. Nce3 Bc8 20. Qf3 Bg7 21. Bh3 Rg8 22. Bg4 Qf6 23. Bh5 Bxf5 {Not a move you would ever want to play, but White had the threat of g4.} 24. Nxf5 c6 25. dxc6 Rac8 26. Qd1! Rxc6 27. Qd5 Rgc8 28. Rad1 Bf8 29. Qxf7 (29. Bxf7! {was also strong - Black's position is in general something of a disaster and perhaps White could have finished him off much sooner- but if you were Magnus Carlsen wouldn't you be tempted to go into an ending?} Na4 30. Rd2 Rc2 31. Ree2 $16) 29... Qxf7 30. Bxf7 Na4 31. Re2 Rc1 32. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 33. Kg2 Nc5 34. b3 Rc3 35. Kh3 Nd7 36. Be6 Nc5 37. Bd5 Nd7 38. Ne3 Nf6 39. Be6 Rc5 40. Nc4 Kg7 {This is a gruesome ending to defend for Black, obviously. Carlsen already has a more than formidable reputation as one of the best endgame players in history, and even equal positions are extremely hard to hold against him, but a position like this where Black is effectively dominated is really bad news.} 41. f3 Ne8 42. Rd2 Nc7 43. Bg4 a4 44. Nxd6 Bxd6 45. Rxd6 a3 46. Bd7! {I doubt I would even see this move, which dominates the knight on c7 (which is probably why I'm not 2870.)} ({I would have just carried on with} 46. Rd7+ Kf6 (46... Kg8 47. f4! {and I don't see a decent move for Black, maybe he can punt} Nb5 48. Be6+ Kf8 49. f5 Rc6 {but} 50. Rf7+ Ke8 51. Rxh7 {looks decisive}) 47. Rxh7 {presumably Magnus was concerned about possible counterplay for Black if he is ever to capture the white a-pawn.} Rc2 48. Rxh6+ Ke7 49. Rb6 Rxa2 50. Rxb4 {seems to be winning but Black retains some hopes while the a-pawn remains alive.}) 46... Rc2 47. Bc6 ({Certainly not the hasty} 47. Rc6? Rxc6 48. Bxc6 Ne6 49. Bd5 Nd4! {which could get embarrassing.}) 47... Rxa2 ({Perhaps the last hope was} 47... Na6 {keeping as much material on the board as possible.} {however the accurate} 48. Bd5! {wins easily;} Nc5 ({The desperate} 48... Nc7 {seems to fail;} 49. Bc4 ({The immediate} 49. Rd7+ Kf6 50. Bc4 {is also good enough} Rxc4 51. bxc4 b3 52. axb3 (52. Rxc7?? bxa2) 52... a2 53. Rd1 Na6 54. Ra1 Nb4 55. Kg4 $18) 49... Ne8 50. Rd7+ Kf6 51. Rf7+ Kg6 52. h5+! Kxh5 53. Rf5+ Kg6 54. Bf7+ Kg7 55. Bxe8 Rxa2 56. Rf7+ Kg8 57. Rb7 $18) 49. Rc6 {should also soon force resignation.}) 48. Rd7+ Kf6 49. Rxc7 Rc2 50. Rxh7 Kg6 51. Rc7 Kf6 52. h5 Rc1 (52... a2 53. Ra7 Rxc6 54. Rxa2 Rc3 (54... Kg5 55. Ra5!) 55. Kg4 Rxb3 56. Ra6+ Kg7 57. Rb6 {is a fairly trivial win.}) 53. Rh7 a2 54. Bd5 1-0

