1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. c3 Nf6 4. Be2 {This side system isn't as harmless as it looks - White is aiming for building up a strong pawns centre.} g6 5. O-O Bg7 6. Bb5+ Nc6 {Sergey chooses a relatively rare theoretical continuation.} (6... Bd7 {is much more popular. There are some practical tests:} 7. Bxd7+ Qxd7 (7... Nbxd7 8. Re1 O-O 9. d4 cxd4 10. cxd4 d5 11. e5 Ne4 12. Nbd2 Nxd2 13. Bxd2 Qb6 14. Re3 Rac8 15. h4 $14 {Sagalchik,G (2525)-Serper,G (2535) New York 1996}) 8. Re1 O-O 9. d4 Na6 10. d5 c4 11. Nbd2 Nc5 $132 {Adams,M (2685)-Anand,V (2735) Dortmund 1996}) 7. d4 Qb6 (7... O-O 8. d5 Nb8 9. Re1 Bg4 10. Nbd2 Nbd7 11. h3 Bxf3 12. Nxf3 a6 13. Bf1 b5 14. Bf4 $14 {Chadaev,N (2580)-Gelfand,B (2738) Astana 2012}) (7... Bd7 8. d5 Ne5 9. Bxd7+ Nfxd7 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. a4 O-O 12. Bf4 Qb6 13. Qe2 c4 14. Bxe5 Bxe5 15. a5 Qb3 16. Na3 $14 {Lombardy,W - Hort,V Zagreb 1969}) 8. Ba4 ({The drawback of protecting the bishop by means of} 8. Na3 {was admitted by} cxd4 9. cxd4 d5! $11 {in Rasik,V (2516)-Zhigalko,A (2601) Czechia 2013}) 8... cxd4 9. cxd4 O-O 10. d5 {This position was seen only in 1 preceding game!} Nb8 $146 {This move looks somewhat artificial, but the knight is heading to c5 or e5.} ({The previously played} 10... Na5 11. Nc3 ({White hardly can pretend on having advantage also after} 11. Re1 Bg4 12. Nbd2 Rfc8 13. h3 Bd7 14. Bc2 e6 $132) 11... Bg4 12. h3 Bxf3 13. Qxf3 Rac8 14. Qe2 Nc4 {was fine for Black in Palit,S (2421)-Gopal,G (2558) Kolkata 2014}) 11. Nc3 ({Delaying the development of b1 would hardly bother Black:} 11. Re1 Bg4 12. Nbd2 Qc7 13. h3 Bd7 14. Bc2 Rc8 15. Bb1 a5 $132) 11... Bg4 12. h3 Bxf3 13. Qxf3 Nbd7 14. Rb1 ({The more aggressive} 14. Be3 Qxb2 15. Rfc1 {only leads to simplifications:} Qa3 16. Rab1 Nc5 17. Bxc5 Qxc5 18. Rxb7 Nxe4! 19. Qxe4 Bxc3 20. Qf3 Qa3 $11) 14... Rfc8 {Black has completed a harmonious setup. The position is closed yet, so White's bishops do not guarantee White any advantage.} 15. Bc2 Ne5 (15... a6!? 16. Qe2 Qa5 17. Bd2 Ne5 $132) 16. Qe2 Nfd7 17. Bg5 {The e7 isn't really hanging, but the presense of White's bishop on g5 is somewhat annoying.} h6 ({Deserves some attention also} 17... Bf6!? 18. Bxf6 Nxf6 19. Kh2 Nc4 20. Bd3 Qd4 $11 {The activity of Black's pieces doesn't allow to develop a serious attack on the kingside.}) 18. Bh4 g5 {A double-edged decision. Black manages to secure the e5, but this advance exposes the light squares around the king.} 19. Bg3 Qa6 ({Another interesting way of handling the position was} 19... Ng6!? 20. Rfc1 Be5 21. Bxe5 Ndxe5 22. g3 Rc4 $132) 20. Qd1 Rc4? {The first cause of Sergey's troubles!} ({There was no reason from deviating from the most aggressive} 20... Nc4 21. Bd3 Qb6 (21... Nc5 22. Be2 Bxc3 23. bxc3 Qxa2 24. Ra1 Qb3 25. Qd4 {looks risky for a human eye}) 22. Qe2 Nde5 $132) 21. Kh1 {This is the point! Most of Black's pieces are on the queenside, so White manages to develop a powerful attack now.} Rac8 22. f4 gxf4 23. Bxf4 Qb6 {The queen is coming into defence, but it takes a long time...} 24. Qh5 Nf6 ({The pawn sac wouldn't bring Sergey any relief:} 24... Qd8 25. Bxh6 Nf6 26. Qh4 Ng6 27. Qg5 Qf8 28. Bxg7 Qxg7 29. Rf5 $16) 25. Qf5 Qd8 (25... Ng6 26. Bd2 Qd8 27. Bb3 R4c7 28. Be3 $16) 26. Bb3 Rd4 27. Bxe5 dxe5 {As it often happens, the presense of bishops of different colours favours the attacking side!} 28. Rbd1 ({Worth attention is also} 28. Rbc1!? Qd7 29. Qxd7 Nxd7 30. Ne2 Rxc1 31. Rxc1 Rd2 (31... Rxe4 32. Ng3 Rf4 33. Rc7 $36) 32. Rc8+ Bf8 33. Ng3 $36 {Even though liquadating into the endgame is Black's undisputable achievement, the position is still very unpleasant.}) 28... Qd7 29. Qf3 Rb4 ({Perhaps, it was better to swap the rooks, though after} 29... Rxd1 30. Qxd1 b5 31. Qd3 a6 32. Ne2 $36 {it wouldn't easy to find a constructive defenseve plan.}) 30. Rd2! {The rook would be very useful along 2nd rank.} Rf8 31. g4! {As usual, World Champion's play is very energetic and impressive.} a5? {Black cannot afford wasting a tempo for counter-attacking measures.} ({The only way to provide resistance was} 31... Nh7 32. h4 Rb6 33. Rg1 Rf6 34. Qg3 $16 {White still has to find a way to make progress.}) 32. Rg2 Nh7 33. h4 {Now White's attack lasts itself.} Rb6 34. g5 Kh8 ({In case of} 34... Rg6 35. Rfg1 Kh8 36. Ba4 Qc8 37. Rh2 $18 {Black would be almost paralysed.}) 35. Rfg1 f5 {A desperate attempt to complicate the matters.} 36. Qh3 Rb4 37. gxh6 Bxh6 {The king is too exposed, so White has many winning ways now.} 38. Qg3 (38. Rg6 Bf4 39. Qg2 $18 (39. d6 $18)) 38... Nf6 39. Qg6 Ng4 40. Rxg4 1-0

