1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+ Nc6 4. O-O Bd7 5. Re1 e5 6. c3 g5?! {This super-aggressive move is not a novelty but this is its first appearance at the top-GM level. It is quite dubious since White's kingside is not weakened by h2-h3 or in any other way. Common continuations:} (6... a6) ({and} 6... Nf6) 7. d4! {[%mdl 32] A logical counter-attack in the centre.} g4 8. Nfd2 exd4?! {Another crazy move, opening the e-file for White's rook.} (8... h5?! 9. dxc5 dxc5 10. Nc4 Qc7 11. Ne3 $16 {1-0 (16) Lisek,J (2302)-Espig,T (2273) Germany 2007}) (8... cxd4 9. Bxc6 Bxc6 10. cxd4 h5 {is probably a better choice although White keeps an edge, of course.}) 9. Na3!? (9. Bxc6!? bxc6 10. cxd4 cxd4 11. Nc4 c5 12. e5 $16) 9... Qf6 (9... Bg7 10. Ndc4! Be6 11. Bf4) 10. cxd4 cxd4 11. e5 {Black manages to castle queenside so this break misses the target.} (11. Nac4 $142 O-O-O 12. Bxc6 Bxc6 13. Nb3 $16) 11... dxe5 12. Nac4 O-O-O 13. Bxc6 Bxc6 14. Nxe5 Bd5?! {Black can't organize his defence without developing pieces.} (14... Ne7 $142) 15. Qxg4+ Qe6 (15... Be6 16. Qg3 {[%cal Rd2e4]}) 16. Qxe6+ (16. Qxd4!? Bxg2 17. Qxa7) 16... Bxe6 {Black has managed to exchange queens but the endgame is still clearly worse for him: he is behind in development, and his pawns are weak.} 17. Ndf3 Bb4 18. Rd1 Rd5 (18... Ne7 {was relatively better but after the loss of the d4 pawn Black would have hardly survived long.}) 19. Bf4 Bc5 20. Rac1 Kd8 21. Nxf7+! Bxf7 22. Be5 Ne7 23. Bxh8 Nc6 24. Rxc5 (24. b4 Bb6 25. b5 Rxb5 26. Bxd4 {was even simpler.}) 24... Rxc5 25. Nxd4 Kc7 26. Nxc6 Kxc6 {[%mdl 4096]} 27. a3 Rd5 28. Rxd5 Kxd5 {Two extra pawns are quite enough to win, despite opposite-coloured bishops.} 29. f3 Kc4 30. Kf2 Kd3 31. Bc3 b5 32. g4 a6 33. Kg3 Ke3 34. f4 Ke4 35. f5 h5 36. h3 hxg4 37. hxg4 1-0

