{[%evp 0,149,19,31,14,1,-3,4,26,1,-1,5,2,-1,9,3,3,27,35,0,14,-18,-24,-62,-50,-15,-34,-35,-28,-26,-52,0,0,0,-7,-19,-31,-18,106,69,66,75,12,-26,-13,-30,-13,0,37,36,-12,-4,35,-33,-46,-33,-33,-31,12,19,19,19,57,65,80,77,95,95,68,0,0,0,59,-17,-6,105,125,110,146,127,144,154,217,229,239,241,241,247,248,251,265,263,262,264,274,263,263,262,263,261,270,211,206,231,233,224,255,245,253,245,253,232,242,249,280,285,288,247,290,297,297,297,319,324,323,334,335,328,328,295,313,315,315,311,334,317,330,320,324,326,346,322,346,325,332,331,331,331,351,356,361,356]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 O-O 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 d5 7. Bg5 c5 {[%mdl 512] This aggressive continuation has brought Black good results so far.} 8. dxc5 d4 9. Qf3 {Quite a rare retreat.} (9. Qc2 e5 {is rather satisfactory for Black, e.g.} 10. e3 h6 11. Bh4 Qe7 12. Be2 Re8 13. Rd1 a5 $44 {1/2 (40) Nepomniachtchi,I (2730)-Karjakin,S (2763) Moscow 2011 CBM 146 [Krasenkow,M]}) ({or} 9. Qg3 Nbd7 10. Nf3 e5! 11. e3 (11. Nxe5?! Nxc5 12. Nd3 Nce4 13. Bxf6 Nxf6 14. e3 Re8 15. O-O-O dxe3 16. fxe3 Bg4 $17 {1/2 (75) Georgiev,K (2669)-Kosintseva,N (2552) Caleta 2011}) 11... Re8 12. O-O-O Nxc5 13. Nxe5 Bf5 $132 {1/2 (38) Mchedlishvili,M (2633)-Boruchovsky,A (2461) Baku 2013}) 9... Nbd7 (9... Nc6 {is less accurate as White simplifies the game keeping an extra pawn:} 10. O-O-O e5 11. Bxf6 Qxf6 12. Qxf6 gxf6 13. e3 $14 {1/2 (21) Lenic,L (2662)-Boruchovsky,A (2542) Batumi 2018}) ({The immediate} 9... e5 {is quite possible:} 10. e3 (10. Bxf6!? Qxf6 11. Qxf6 gxf6 12. e3 dxe3 13. fxe3 Nd7! 14. b4 a5 $44) 10... Nbd7 11. O-O-O?! {1-0 (57) Theodorou,N (2554)-Raja,H (2472) lichess.org INT 2020} (11. exd4 $142 exd4 12. O-O-O Nxc5 $13) {, and now} 11... e4 {[%cal Yd4d3] was very strong.}) 10. e3 h6 (10... e5!? {- see 9...e5}) 11. Bxf6 Nxf6 12. O-O-O e5 $44 {Black has avoided the exchange of queens and has good compensation for the pawn as White's king is unsafe.} 13. Ne2 Bg4 (13... d3 14. Nc3 Bg4 15. Rxd3! Qa5 16. Qg3 (16. Qxb7!?) 16... Qxc5 17. e4 Be6 $44 {1-0 (47) Vidit,S (2701)-Ganguly,S (2621) Makati 2018}) 14. Qg3 ({Now} 14. Qxb7? Rb8 15. Qxa7 ({or} 15. Qc6 Qa5) 15... Ne4 {is favourable for Black.}) 14... Ne4 {A new but hardly a good move.} ({Black obtained a satisfactory position in the following game with a classical time rate:} 14... d3 15. f3 Be6 16. Nc3 Qc7 17. Bxd3 Qxc5 18. Kb1 {1/2 (76) Riazantsev,A (2654)-Inarkiev,E (2707) Geneva 2017 , and now} Rfd8 $44 {would be a good option} ({instead of the more passive} 18... Rfe8 {played in the game})) 15. Qxg4 Nxf2 16. Qf3 Nxd1 17. exd4 Nxb2 18. Kxb2 {Two minor pieces are stronger than the rook in this position.} Rc8 (18... exd4 19. Nf4 {[%cal Yf1d3]} Re8 20. Bd3 Re3 21. Qd1 $14 {[%cal Yf4d5]}) 19. d5! Rxc5 20. Nc3 (20. Nc1 {[%cal Yc1b3] was even stronger but White probably feared} b5 {, which, in fact, could have been parried by} 21. Nb3 Rxc4 (21... bxc4?! 22. Nxc5 Qb6+ 23. Nb3 cxb3 24. Bd3 $18) 22. Bxc4 bxc4 23. Na1! Qb6+ 24. Ka2 Rb8 25. Qc3 {, and Black doesn't get enough compensation for the missing piece.}) 20... b5! {Black must urgently create counterplay, otherwise White's minor pieces will crush him.} 21. Be2 (21. cxb5 Qg5 $132) (21. Ne4!? Rc8 (21... Rc7 22. d6) 22. cxb5 f5 23. Nc3 {is too subtle for a rapid game.}) 21... bxc4 22. Ka2 f5 23. Rb1 Qg5 24. Rd1 e4 25. Qf2 $14 {White has consolidated his position, his blockading c3 knight is especially strong.} e3 (25... Qe7!? 26. Qd4 Qd6) 26. Qg3 {The side having minor pieces against a rook should rather avoid simplifications. Magnus Carlsen was certainly concerned about his king but it was not under attack so he could have concentrated on advancing his passed pawn:} (26. Qf3 $36 {[%cal Yd5d6]}) 26... Qxg3 27. hxg3 Kf7 (27... Rb8!? {, activating the rook and cutting off White's king.}) 28. Rf1 {A possible continuation but White misses a chance to bring his king closer to the centre:} (28. Kb2) 28... Ke7 29. Rf3 Kd7 30. Bd1! ({White doesn't want to exchange rooks after} 30. Rxe3 Re8 {or go for an unclear ending after} 31. Rf3 Rxd5! 32. Nxd5 Rxe2+) 30... Rf7 (30... Re8?? 31. Ba4+) 31. Ba4+ Kd8 32. Rxe3 Re7 33. Rf3 Re5 34. d6?! (34. Bc6 $142 {[%csl Gc6]}) 34... h5 35. d7 g5 (35... Ra5 $142 ! 36. Kb2 (36. Rf1 Re3 37. Rb1 Rxa4 38. Nxa4 Kxd7 $13) 36... g5 37. Rf1 Re3 $132) 36. Rf1! {[%cal Rf1b1]} Ra5 37. Rh1! {This way White keeps his material advantage.} Rxa4 38. Nxa4 Re2+ 39. Kb1 Kxd7 40. Rxh5 Re3 41. Rxg5 Rxa3 42. Nb2 Rc3 43. Rxf5 Rxg3 44. Rf2 $18 {The rest is easy.} Rg4 45. Rc2 c3 46. Nd1 Ke6 47. Nxc3 Ke5 48. Re2+ Kd4 49. Kc2 a5 50. Kd2 Rg3 51. Nd1 Rd3+ 52. Kc1 Rg3 53. Kc2 a4 54. Rd2+ Kc4 55. Nb2+ Kb4 56. Nd3+ Kc4 57. Ne5+ Kc5 58. Kb2 a3+ 59. Ka2 Kb4 60. Rc2 Re3 61. Nc6+ Kb5 62. Nd4+ Kb4 63. Re2 Rg3 64. Nc2+ Kc4 65. Nxa3+ Kd3 66. Rb2 Rg4 67. Nc2 Ke2 68. Nd4+ Ke3 69. Nf3 Re4 70. Kb1 Kf4 71. Kc1 Kg3 72. Kd1 Re8 73. Ne1 Re3 74. Kd2 Ra3 75. Ke2 1-0

