{[%mdl 2] Prematch games between the challenger and the title-holder always have an increased psychological significance. In this case Carlsen did put his opponent under serious pressure, but in the end Caruana escaped and went on to score a resounding tournament victory.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 {Already a slight surprise.} ({Carlsen more often plays} 2... e6 {, this appeared in round 5. After} 3. Nf3 d5 4. g3 Bb4+ 5. Bd2 Be7 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O Nbd7 8. Qc2 c6 9. Bf4 Ne4 10. h4!? ({Magnus undoubtedly wouldn't mind repeating} 10. Nc3 g5! 11. Be3 Nd6 12. b3 Nf5 13. g4 Nxe3 14. fxe3 b5! 15. e4 b4 16. exd5 bxc3 17. dxc6 Nb8 18. Qe4 f5 19. gxf5 exf5 20. Qd5+ Qxd5 21. cxd5 Na6 $17 {Eljanov,P (2760)-Carlsen,M (2844) Wijk aan Zee 2016.}) 10... Ndf6 ({Black has also tested} 10... h6 {, or the immediate}) (10... f5) 11. Nbd2 Nxd2 12. Nxd2 Nh5 13. Be3 f5 14. Bf3 Qe8 (14... f4 15. Bxh5 fxe3 16. fxe3 Qd6 17. Rxf8+ Bxf8 18. Kf2 e5 $44 {gives Black some compensation, but certainly not more than that.}) 15. Bg5 Nf6 16. e3 b6 17. Rac1 Ba6 18. a3 Ne4 19. Bxe7 Qxe7 20. Be2 Bb7 21. b4 Rac8 22. c5 e5 23. dxe5 Qxe5 24. Nxe4 fxe4 25. Qc3 Qf5 $132 {Meier,G (2648)-Carlsen,M (2843) Karlsruhe/Baden-Baden 2018 Black had reasonable , but overestimated his chances, got into serious trouble and escaped only due to Meier's inaccuracy.}) 3. Nf3 {The World Champion plays the Grünfeld from time to time, but Fabiano is not interested.} ({However, after} 3. Nc3 {in round 3 we saw} Bg7 {After} 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. Be3 exd4 8. Nxd4 Re8 9. f3 c6 10. Bf2 d5 11. exd5 cxd5 12. O-O Nc6 13. c5 a6 14. Qd2 Qa5 15. Rfe1 Qxc5 16. Ne6 Qa5 17. Nxg7 Kxg7 18. a3 d4 19. Bxd4 Nxd4 20. Qxd4 Rd8 21. Qe3 Bd7 22. Rad1 Be6 23. Rxd8 Rxd8 24. Rd1 Rxd1+ 25. Bxd1 Qd8 26. Be2 Qd6 27. f4 Bd7 28. Bf3 Bc6 29. Bxc6 Ng4 30. Qg3 Qc5+ 31. Kf1 Ne3+ 32. Ke2 Nf5 33. Qf2 Qxc6 34. g4 Qc4+ 35. Kd2 Nd4 36. Qe3 $11 {Bluebaum,M (2631)-Carlsen,M (2843) Karlsruhe/Baden-Baden 2018. Black even despite protracted efforts couldn't manage to disrupt the balance.}) 3... Bg7 4. e3 ({Caruana has already played} 4. Nc3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e4 {in the past, this time he opts for the Anti-Grünfeld he had prepared already for the Berlin candidates.}) 4... O-O 5. Be2 d6 {Black puts his pawns on the dark squares in King's Indian fashion. This is an important moment, deciding the character of the game and he also had other options:} ({Transposing into a Benoni} 5... c5 6. d5 e6 7. Nc3 exd5 8. cxd5 d6 {was long considered a good solution:} {However, after} 9. Nd2!? (9. O-O Bg4 10. h3 Bxf3 11. Bxf3 Nbd7 12. a4 a6 13. g4 c4 (13... h6 $13) 14. Be2 Rc8?! (14... Ne5 15. f4 Nd3 16. Bxd3 cxd3 17. Qxd3 h5 $44) 15. g5 Ne8 16. f4 Qe7 17. Ra3 Rc5 18. Bf3 Ra5 19. Bd2 Nc5 20. Qe2 Nb3 21. Ne4 Nxd2 22. Qxd2 Qd8 23. Qb4 b5 24. axb5 Rxb5 25. Qxc4 Qb6 26. Qc6 Bxb2 27. Qxb6 Rxb6 28. Ra2 $16 {Morozevich,A (2758)-Kramnik,V (2769) Mexico City 2007. For details see the notes to this game in CBM 120 by Marin.}) 9... Na6 ({Black can play the setup with} 9... Nbd7 {, but then White has no reason to hurry with e4.}) 10. O-O Nc7 11. e4 {Black even despite his extra tempo due to e3-e4 still has his problems:} Re8 12. a4 Rb8 ({After the more usual} 12... b6 13. Re1 Rb8 {Black has to reckon with} 14. Bb5!?) 13. f3 a6 14. a5 Bd7 15. Nc4 Bb5 16. Bg5 Bxc4 (16... h6 17. Bh4 Bxc4 18. Bxc4 b5 19. axb6 Rxb6 20. Qd2? (20. Qc2 $13 {/ is similar to the following note}) 20... Nxe4! 21. Bxd8 Nxd2 22. Bxc7 Rb7 $17 {Granda Zuniga,J (2646)-Lopez Martinez,J (2508) Ayamonte 2006}) 17. Bxc4 b5 18. axb6 Rxb6 19. Na4 (19. Qc2 h6 20. Bh4 $13 {/ is similar to the following note} g5 21. Bf2 Nd7 22. Ra2 Qf6 {Hamitevici,V (2430)-Jumabayev,R (2599) London 2015} 23. Nd1! $36 {[%cal Yd1e3,Ye3f5]}) 19... Rb4 20. b3 (20. Qd3!?) 20... Qc8 21. Bf4 Qd7 22. Ra2 Nh5 23. Be3 (23. Bd2 {is more natural}) 23... Rbb8 24. Qd2 Nb5 25. g4 Nf6 26. Nb2 Qc8 (26... h5 27. g5 Nxe4! 28. fxe4 Nc3 29. Rxa6 Qh3! $44 {is a fully valid engine suggestion, but even a strong human GM would hardly dare to play like this...}) 27. Bf4 Nd7 28. Bxb5 axb5 29. Bxd6 Rb6 30. Bg3 c4 31. bxc4 (31. Qc2!?) 31... bxc4 32. Qe2 Rb4 33. Bd6 Rb6 34. Bg3 Rb4 {½, Caruana,F (2784)-Grischuk,A (2767) Berlin ct 2018}) ({Grünfeld intentions are represented by} 5... d5 6. cxd5 Nxd5 {and now:} {More direct and energetic is} 7. e4 (7. O-O Nb6 (7... c5!? 8. dxc5 (8. e4 Nf6 9. e5 Nd5 10. dxc5 Nc6 11. Bc4 e6 12. Qe2 Qa5 13. a3 Qxc5 14. b4 Qe7 15. Bb2 a6 16. Nbd2 b5 17. Bxd5 exd5 18. Rac1 Bd7 19. Nb3 Rfc8 20. Rfe1 Bg4 21. h3 Bxf3 22. Qxf3 Nxe5 23. Qxd5 Qd7 $11 {Ginsburg,G (2526)-Ftacnik,L (2568) Nuremberg 2010}) 8... Na6 (8... Nf6?! 9. Nbd2 Qc7 10. Nb3 Rd8 11. Nfd4 e5 12. Nb5 Qe7 13. Nd6 Ne4 14. Nxc8 Rxc8 15. Bf3 Nxc5 16. Nxc5 Rxc5 17. Qa4 Rc7 18. Bd2 Na6 19. Ba5 Rcc8 20. Bd5 Nc5 21. Qa3 e4 22. f3 $16 {[%csl Ga5,Gd5] Laznicka,V (2666)-Naumann,A (2560) Germany 2014}) 9. Bxa6 bxa6 10. e4 Nb4 11. Qxd8 Rxd8 12. Nc3 Bb7 13. Bg5 Kf8 14. Rad1 Nd3 15. Nd5 Bxd5 16. Rxd3 Bxa2 17. Rfd1 Rxd3 18. Rxd3 Ke8 19. c6 Rc8 20. Rd7 Bf6 21. Bxf6 exf6 22. Rxa7 Rxc6 $11 {Novkovic,M (2373)-Mesaros,F (2266) Austria 2014}) 8. Nc3 (8. e4 {transposes to 7.e4 and is still more usual.}) 8... Nc6 9. h3 a5 {is slightly similar to index D76, but here White's bishop is not fianchettoed and this lessens his chances to get an opening pull:} 10. Qd2 (10. Re1 a4 11. Bb5 a3 (11... e5!? 12. Bxc6 exd4 $13) 12. bxa3 e5 13. Bxc6 exd4 14. exd4 bxc6 15. Bg5 f6 16. Qb3+ Kh8 17. Bf4 Bf5 18. Ne4 Nd5 19. Bg3 Bxe4 {½, Shengelia,D (2547)-Rustemov,A (2520) Germany 2009} 20. Rxe4 Qd7 $11 {[%cal Yf8b8]}) 10... e5 11. d5 Nb4 12. e4 f5 13. Ng5 fxe4 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 Qe7 16. Nxe4 Qxe6 17. Bg4 Qc6 18. Ng5 Rae8 19. Qc3 h6 20. Qxc6 Nxc6 21. Ne6 Rf7 22. Be3 Nc4 23. Rac1 Nxe3 24. fxe3 Rxf1+ 25. Kxf1 h5 26. Nxg7 Kxg7 27. Bd7 Rf8+ 28. Ke2 Nb8 29. Ba4 c6 30. Rc5 $11 {Laznicka,V (2672)-Ragger,M (2680) Chartres 2017 White's compensation was sufficient only to hold the balance.}) 7... Nb6 8. O-O {and now: transposes to 7.e4 and is still more usual.} Nc6!? (8... Bg4 9. Nbd2 Nc6 ({But} 9... e6?! {is too passive:} 10. h3 Bxf3 11. Nxf3 Nc6 12. Be3 Qe7 13. Qb3 Rad8 14. Rfd1 a6 15. Rac1 Qe8 16. Bf1 h6 17. d5 $16 {Grischuk,A (2785)-Nepomniachtchi,I (2717) Riga rpd 2013}) 10. d5 Nb8 (10... Bxf3 11. Nxf3 Ne5 12. Nxe5 Bxe5 13. f4 Bg7 14. a4 a5 15. Qb3 Qd6 16. Be3 Qb4 17. Qxb4 axb4 18. a5 Nd7 19. e5 b6 20. Bb5 Nc5 21. Bc6 Ra7 22. axb6 Rxa1 23. Rxa1 cxb6 24. Ra7 $16 {/+-, Ivanchuk,V (2710)-Tabatabaei,M (2482) Doha 2015}) 11. a4 c6 12. a5 N6d7 (12... Nc8 13. Re1?! (13. h3 $142) (13. Qb3!?) 13... cxd5 14. Qb3 Nd6 15. e5 Ne8 16. Nd4 (16. Qxb7 Nc7 $11) 16... Bc8 17. N2f3 Nc6 18. a6 Nxd4 19. Nxd4 bxa6 20. Nc6 Qc7 21. Qxd5 Bb7 22. Bf3 Rb8 23. Qc4 Bxc6 24. Qxc6 Qxc6 25. Bxc6 Rb6 26. Ba4 Nc7 $15 {Belanoff,S (2049)-Koch,C (2141) Chessfriend.com 2003}) 13. h3 Bxf3 14. Nxf3 Nf6 15. Qb3 Qc8 16. Bg5 (16. dxc6! Nxc6 17. e5 $36) 16... Nxe4 17. Bxe7 Re8 18. d6 Nxd6 19. Bxd6 Rxe2 20. Ng5 Qd7? ({Black should have fought for the open file with} 20... Qe8! 21. Qxb7 Nd7 $132) 21. Rae1 Rxb2 22. Qc4 Na6 23. Re7 Qf5 24. Rxf7 Qd5 25. Rxg7+ Kxg7 {Grischuk,A (2785)-Nepomniachtchi,I (2717) Riga rpd 2013} 26. Be5+! Qxe5 27. Qf7+ Kh6 28. Qxh7+ Kxg5 29. f4+ Qxf4 30. Qe7+ $18) 9. Be3 (9. d5 Ne5 10. Nxe5 Bxe5 11. a4 a5 12. Qb3 e6 13. Bf3 exd5 14. exd5 Qh4 15. g3 Qb4 16. Qxb4 axb4 17. Re1 {Krishna,C (2401)-Puranik,A (2469) Abu Dhabi 2016} Nc4! $36) 9... Bg4 (9... f5 10. exf5 Bxf5 11. Nc3 Qd6 12. Qd2 Rad8 13. Rad1 Qb4 14. Qc1 Bg4 15. d5 Bxf3 16. Bxf3 Nd4 17. Bxd4 Bxd4 18. Rfe1 Qc5 19. Re2 Nd7 20. Ne4 Qxc1 21. Rxc1 Bb6 22. Ng5 Nc5 23. b4 Nd3 24. Rd1 h6 25. Ne6 Nf4 26. Re4 Nxe6 27. Rxe6 Rd6 28. Rxe7 Rf4 $44 {/,Akdag,M (2560)-Cruzado Duenas,C (2595) email 2013}) 10. Nbd2 e5!? 11. d5 Nd4 12. h3 Bd7 13. Nb3 Qe7?! (13... Nxf3+ 14. Bxf3 Nc4 $11) 14. Nfxd4 exd4 15. Bxd4 Qxe4 16. Bf3 Qf4 17. g3 Qg5 18. h4 Qf5 19. Bxg7 Kxg7 20. Nc5 Rab8 21. Re1 $14 {Dimakiling,O (2353)-Laylo,D (2497) Jakarta 2013}) ({There is also the interesting and flexible} 5... b6 6. O-O Bb7 7. Nc3 {, but here Black should already show his cards:} d5 (7... e6 8. b3 (8. d5!?) 8... d5 9. Bb2 Nbd7 10. cxd5 (10. Qc2 {or}) (10. Rc1 {give White better chances for an edge.}) 10... exd5 11. a4 a5 12. Ba3 Re8 13. Nb5 Ne4 14. Nd2 Ndf6 15. Nxe4 Nxe4 16. Bd3 Re6 17. Rc1 c6 18. Nc3 Nxc3 19. Rxc3 c5 20. Bb5 cxd4 21. exd4 Re4 $11 {/, Caruana,F (2795)-Vachier Lagrave,M (2788) chess.com blitz INT 2016}) 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. Bd2 (9. Qb3 e6 10. Rd1 Nd7 11. Bd2 c5 12. Rac1 Qe7 13. Nxd5 Bxd5 14. Qa3 Rfc8 15. Bc3 a5 16. dxc5 Bxc3 17. Rxc3 Rxc5 18. Bb5 Rd8 19. Bxd7 Bxf3 20. gxf3 Rxd7 21. Rxd7 Rg5+ 22. Kf1 Qxd7 23. Rd3 Rd5 24. Rxd5 Qxd5 $11 {Dubov,D (2629)-Vachier Lagrave,M (2751) Doha 2014}) 9... Nd7 (9... c5?! 10. dxc5 Nxc3 11. Bxc3 Bxc3 12. bxc3 Nd7 (12... bxc5 13. Qa4 $36) 13. cxb6 axb6 14. Qd4 Qc7 15. Rfb1 Ra5 16. Qb4 e6 17. Qe7 Qd8 18. Qxd8 Rxd8 19. Rd1 Bc6 20. Nd4 Ba4 21. Rdb1 e5 22. Nb3 $14 {/, Giri,A (2793)-Vachier Lagrave,M (2744) Baku 2015 For more details see the notes to this game in CBM 169 by Marin.}) 10. Nxd5 Bxd5 11. Bc3 (11. Qc2 $142 ! {} c5 12. e4 Bb7 13. d5) 11... c5 12. Rc1 e6 13. Qa4 a6 ({This complicates the issue; a simpler way was} 13... Qe7! 14. Rfd1 Rfc8 $11 {as in Dubov - Vachier Lagrave above.}) 14. Qa3 Qb8 15. dxc5 Bxc3 16. Qxc3 bxc5?! (16... Rc8 $142 ! {is still close to equality.}) 17. Nd2 a5 18. a4 Qb7 19. e4 Bc6 20. b3 Rfb8 21. Qe3 Qc7 22. Rfd1 Rb4 23. Nc4 Rab8 24. Rc3 Nf6 25. f3 $16 {Nakamura,H (2793)-Nepomniachtchi,I (2751) Zuerich 2017}) 6. Nc3 Nc6!? ({Black more often plays} 6... Nbd7 7. O-O (7. d5 e5 8. dxe6 fxe6 9. O-O e5 10. b3 Kh8 11. Bb2 Qe7 12. Rc1 Nc5 13. b4 Nce4 14. Nxe4 Nxe4 15. Nd2 Nxd2 16. Qxd2 {Harikrishna,P (2763)-Nakamura,H (2787) chess.com blitz INT 2016} Be6 $11) 7... e5 {, but this is practically the King's Indian Attack (C00) with opposite colours. I wrote an article about this line in CBM 181, the extra tempo should favour White. After} 8. Qc2 (8. b4!? {deserves attention now and on move 9.}) 8... Re8 9. Rd1 e4 10. Nd2 Qe7 11. b4 Nf8 12. Bb2 h5 13. Rac1 Bf5 14. Nb3 N8h7 15. d5 h4 16. Nd4 Bd7 17. c5 (17. h3!?) 17... dxc5?! (17... h3! $132 {[%csl Rg1]}) 18. bxc5 Qxc5 19. Qb3 Qb6 20. Ncb5 c5 21. dxc6 bxc6 22. Nd6 Rf8 23. Ba3 Qxb3 24. axb3 Rfb8 25. Nxc6 Bxc6 26. Rxc6 Rxb3 27. Bc5 $44 {, Grischuk,A (2785)-Caruana,F (2779) Elancourt 2013. White has more than enough for the  and Black must tread with great care. For details see the notes to this game in CBM 157 by Krasenkow.}) 7. d5 ({After} 7. O-O e5 {the pressure against d4 makes it more difficult for White to retain the central tension as in the 6...bd7 lines. The advance} 8. d5 (8. Qc2 Re8 9. dxe5 dxe5 10. Rd1 Bd7 11. e4 Qc8 12. h3 Be6 13. Nd5 Nd7 14. b4 Nd4 15. Nxd4 exd4 16. Bb2 c6 17. Nf4 c5 18. bxc5 Qxc5 19. Qd2 Bxc4 20. Rac1 b5 21. Bxc4 bxc4 22. Bxd4 Bxd4 23. Qxd4 Qxd4 24. Rxd4 Nb6 25. Nd5 Red8 26. Nxb6 axb6 $11 {Ragger,M (2632)-Onischuk,V (2594) Dubai blitz 2014}) 8... Ne7 9. b4 (9. e4 {is the Classical KID, but White is a tempo down - normally it's his move here.}) 9... a5 10. bxa5 Rxa5 11. Nd2 Nd7 12. Nb3 Ra8 13. a4 f5 14. Ba3 e4 15. Qc2 b6 16. Bb4 Nc5 17. a5 {Kuzubov,Y (2638)-Onischuk,V (2614) Lvov rpd 2016} Na6 18. Ba3 bxa5 $11 {} 19. Nxa5 c5!) 7... Nb4 {Here we are gradually entering uncharted territory.} ({The passive} 7... Nb8 8. O-O Na6 {gave White a distinct pull after} 9. Nd4 e5 10. dxe6 fxe6 11. Rb1 e5 12. Nc2 Be6 13. b4 Nb8 14. e4 Nc6 15. Be3 Nh5 16. Bxh5 gxh5 17. Nd5 h4 18. h3 Kh8 19. Qh5 a5 20. Bg5 Qd7 21. b5 Bxd5 22. cxd5 $16 {Hoffmeyer,F (2377)-Paehtz,E (2453) Germany 2018}) 8. a3 {Yermolinsky criticises this move and he is probably right.} (8. O-O $142 e6!? (8... c6 9. e4 a5 10. e5 dxe5 11. a3 e4 12. axb4 exf3 13. Bxf3 cxd5 14. cxd5 (14. Nxd5 $142 !? Nxd5 15. Bxd5 $11 {/}) 14... Bf5 15. bxa5 Rxa5 16. Rxa5 Qxa5 17. Qb3 Nd7 18. Be3 Nc5 19. Bxc5?! (19. Qb5 $11) 19... Qxc5 20. Qxb7 Be5 $44 {Barcza,G-Taimanov,M Copenhagen 1965}) (8... a5 9. e4 e6 10. dxe6 (10. h3?! Re8 11. dxe6 Bxe6 12. Re1 Bd7 13. Bd3 a4 14. a3 Nxd3 15. Qxd3 Be6 16. Nd5 Nd7 17. Bg5 Ne5 18. Nxe5 Qxg5 19. Nf3 Qd8 20. Rad1 c6 21. Nf4 Qa5 22. Nxe6 Rxe6 23. Re2 Rae8 $11 {/, Lobron,E (2485)-Romanishin,O (2560) Reggio Emilia 1986}) 10... Bxe6 11. Nd4 $14) 9. e4 Re8 10. a3 Na6 11. Be3 (11. dxe6!? {followed by d4 is still a better position, than Caruana got in our game.}) 11... exd5 12. exd5 Nc5 13. Bd4 a5 14. b3 Bd7 15. Nd2 Nfe4 16. Ndxe4 Nxe4 17. Nxe4 Rxe4 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 $11 {Vrolijk,L (2433)-Heimann,A (2574) Karlsruhe 2018 Black has comfortable equality and it's White, who must tread with care.}) 8... Na6 9. Nd4 (9. O-O Nc5 10. Nd4 a5 11. b3 e5 12. dxe6 fxe6 13. Bb2 e5 14. Nf3 Be6 15. b4 Nce4 16. Nd2 Nxd2 17. Qxd2 Qd7 18. Nd5 Qf7 19. Nxf6+ Bxf6 20. Qc2 Bg7 21. Bd3 c6 22. e4 $11 h5 (22... Rfc8!? {[%cal Yd6d5]}) 23. c5 dxc5 24. bxc5 a4 25. Bc1 Rad8 26. Be3 Bb3 {½, Ginsburg,G (2443)-Vachier Lagrave,M (2794) Germany 2018 This game was played a month later, than ours.}) 9... e5 10. dxe6 $146 {A novelty, Caruana feels it's necessary to open the centre.} (10. Nc2 Nc5 11. b4 Nb3 12. Rb1 Nxc1 13. Rxc1 Nd7 14. O-O f5 15. Na4 a5 16. c5 axb4 17. Nxb4 dxc5 18. Nxc5 Nxc5 19. Rxc5 e4 20. Qb3 Kh8 21. Rfc1 Be5 $36 {Breutigam,M (2340)-Brendel,O (2375) Germany 1996}) (10. Nb3 Nd7 11. O-O f5 {is also a pleasant version of the King's Indian for Black.}) 10... fxe6 11. O-O e5 12. Nb3 ({White wants to keep his central pawns mobile. However, a more natural move was} 12. Nf3!? c6 (12... Nc5 13. b4 {and as the knight can't go to e4, Black is almost forced to play the adventurous} e4!? 14. Nd4 $14) 13. b4 $13 {with similarities to Ginsburg-MVL above.}) 12... c6 13. e4 Nc7 14. f4!? {Continuing in the spirit of 10.dxe6, Caruana seeks activity even if it weakens his setup.} (14. Be3 Ne6 $15 {[%cal Yc6c5,Ye6d4] is unattractive for White.}) 14... Ne6 {While the text move is very logical, even} (14... Nfe8!? $13 {makes good sense; Black wants to activate his g7.}) 15. f5 (15. fxe5 dxe5 {[%cal Ye6d4] is pleasant for Black, so Caruana continues to seek complications.}) 15... Nd4 ({The main alternative is} 15... Qb6+!? {However, Magnus maybe didn't like} 16. c5!? (16. Kh1 Nd4 17. Nxd4 (17. c5 Qxb3 18. Qxb3+ Nxb3 19. Bc4+ d5 20. Bxb3 Nxe4! 21. Nxe4 Bxf5 22. Ng3 Bd3 $36 {[%csl Gd5,Ge5]}) 17... exd4 18. Na4 Qa5 19. b4 Qd8 $15 {[%csl Gd4,Re4]}) 16... Nxc5 17. Bc4+ d5 ({Complications after} 17... Be6 18. fxe6 Nxb3+ 19. Kh1 Nxa1 20. e7+ Kh8 21. exf8=Q+ Rxf8 22. Ba2 $44 {[%csl Ra1]}) ({or} 17... Ne6+ 18. Kh1 d5 19. exd5 cxd5 20. Nxd5 Nxd5 21. Qxd5 gxf5 22. Bd2! (22. Nc5? Rd8 23. Na4 Qc7 24. Qc5 Qf7 $17) 22... Kh8 23. Ba5 Qc6 24. Qxc6 bxc6 25. Bb4 $13 {/ rather favour White.}) 18. Be3 dxc4 19. Nxc5 Qc7 (19... Qxb2? 20. N5a4 Qb3 21. Qc1 {[%csl Rb3][%cal Ra1b1]}) 20. Qe2 Qf7 21. g4 b6 22. N5a4 $15 {/ Although here the engine gives Black an advantage, the issue is not quite clear, as the extra pawn isn't making itself felt and White has kingside counterplay.}) 16. Be3? {Only this definitely tips the scales in Black's favour.} (16. Nxd4?! exd4 17. Qxd4 Ng4 18. Qd3 (18. Qd2 Qb6+ 19. c5 Qxc5+ 20. Kh1 gxf5 $15) 18... Qb6+ 19. Kh1 Nf2+ 20. Rxf2 Qxf2 21. Be3 Qh4 22. g3 Qe7 (22... Qh3?! 23. Bf1 {with repetition.}) 23. Bf4 Be5 $15 {also isn't ideal, White doesn't have enough for the exchange,}) ({The best was} 16. Bd3 $142 ! Qb6 17. Nxd4 exd4!? (17... Qxd4+ $143 18. Kh1 gxf5 19. exf5 e4 20. Bc2 Qxd1 21. Bxd1 $11 {and White has nothing to fear}) 18. Na4 (18. Ne2 Ng4 (18... Nxe4 19. fxg6! Rxf1+ 20. Qxf1 hxg6 21. Bxe4 d3+ 22. Kh1 dxe2 23. Qxe2 Qd4 24. Bxg6 Bg4 $132) 19. Rb1 $13) 18... Qd8 19. b4 $13) 16... Nxe2+ {Now Black's central pawns get moving.} (16... c5?! 17. g4 $36 {suits White.}) 17. Qxe2 gxf5 18. exf5 d5 19. cxd5 cxd5 20. Rad1 d4 21. Bg5 {More or less forced.} ({The piece sacrifice} 21. Nxd4 exd4 22. Bxd4 Qe8 ({or} 22... Bd7 $15 {/ doesn't give sufficient compensation and is more suitable for shorter time-controls.})) 21... Qb6 22. Qc4+ Rf7! 23. Na4 (23. Nd5 Nxd5 24. Qxd5 Bd7 $17 {[%cal Rd7c6] only helps Black to get out his forces.}) 23... Qc7 24. Qxc7 (24. Nac5 Ne4 $17) 24... Rxc7 25. Nac5 b6 26. Bxf6 ({Relatively best, after} 26. Nd3 Ba6 27. Rfe1 Nd7 28. f6 Bxd3 29. Rxd3 Bf8 $17 {[%csl Gd4,Ge5,Rf6] Black's pawns are far more dangerous and have better support, than the lone warrior on f6.}) 26... bxc5 {[%csl Gc5,Gd4,Ge5]} (26... Bxf6 $143 27. Ne4 $132) 27. Bxg7 Kxg7 28. Nd2?! {The  won't gain a foothold on e4.} ({A more resilient way was} 28. Rc1 c4 29. Nd2 Ba6 30. f6+ Kf7 31. Rf5 Ke6 32. Rh5 $15 {/}) (28. f6+ {might transpose into the above line.}) 28... Bb7 29. f6+ Kf8 ({Carlsen wants to avoid complications, but} 29... Kf7!? {[%cal Ya8g8]} 30. Nc4 Ke6 $17 {is possibly even stronger.}) 30. Rde1 (30. Nc4 {gives Black a pleasant choice between} Ba6 ({and} 30... e4)) 30... Re8 31. Ne4 {[%cal Gf6f7]} Bxe4 (31... c4? 32. f7 $18 {indicates the threat, so Black chops off the e4.}) 32. Rxe4 c4 33. g4 Rb8 {Good, but Black was already spoilt for choice.} ({Both} 33... Kf7 $17 {and}) (33... d3 $17 {give him excellent winning chances. Yermolinsky gives the pretty line} 34. h4 Kf7 35. g5 Ke6 36. h5 Rg8 37. Rg4 Rb7 38. g6 hxg6 39. hxg6 Rf7! $19 {and White's pawns drop off.}) 34. Rxe5 (34. Rf2 Rcb7 $19 {doesn't help, White must seek active counterplay.}) 34... Rxb2 35. Rd5 c3 36. Rd8+ Kf7 37. Rh8! Rbb7! 38. Rxh7+ Kg6 39. Rxc7 Rxc7 40. Kf2 c2 (40... d3? 41. Ke3 d2 42. Kd3 $11 {and White retains the f6.}) 41. Rc1 d3 42. Ke3 Rd7 43. Kd2 Kxf6 {[%mdl 4096] A longer series of logical and more or less forced moves led to an interesting  endgame. Black has a distinct advantage, as his pawns are further advanced and can be quickly supported by his , but bringing home the full point still requires precision, as the position is already very simplified.} 44. h4 Ke5 45. Rf1 Kd4 46. h5 Re7 {Straightforward and should have been sufficient.} ({Even stronger was} 46... a5! {, taking away the b4-square from White's . After} 47. h6 Re7 48. Rf4+ Kd5 49. Rf1 Kc4 50. Rf4+ Kb3 {White has to return} 51. Rf1 {and} Re2+ 52. Kxd3 Rh2 53. g5 Rh5 {disarms White's pawns, so the c2 will decide the issue.}) 47. Rf4+ Kd5 48. Rf1 Kc4 49. Rf4+ Kd5 50. Rf1 Kc4 51. Rf4+ Kb3 52. Rb4+ Kxa3 53. Rc4 Kb3 54. Rc8 (54. Rc5 a5 55. h6 a4 56. g5 Kb4! ({Black's  is misplaced after} 56... a3? 57. g6 a2 58. g7 {so he improves it with tempo.}) 57. Rc8 a3 58. Kxd3 a2 59. Rb8+ Kc5 60. Ra8 Re8! 61. Rxe8 a1=Q 62. Rc8+ Kb6 63. Rxc2 Qe5 $19 {and the mobile queen picks up White's pawns.}) 54... a5? {Here this advance doesn't have the idea from move 46, it's too slow and throws away the win.} ({This could be achieved only by the paradoxical prophylaxis} 54... Rh7!! {, but after} 55. Kxd3 ({Other moves don't help either:} 55. Rd8 Rc7 56. Kc1 Kc3 57. g5 Rb7 $19) (55. Rb8+ Ka2 56. Rc8 a5 57. Kxd3 Kb1 58. Rb8+ Kc1 59. Rc8 Kd1! 60. Rxc2 Rd7+ $19) (55. Rc5 {allows} a5! {} 56. Rxa5 Rc7 57. Ra1 Kb2 58. Rh1 Rg7! 59. h6 Rxg4 60. h7 Rh4 $19) 55... Rd7+ 56. Ke4 a5 57. h6 a4 58. g5 a3 59. g6 {Black must in advance see another only move} Rd8! 60. Rxc2 (60. Rxd8 c1=Q) 60... Kxc2 61. g7 a2 62. h7 a1=Q 63. g8=Q Qa8+ $19 {Unlike for an engine, calculating this over the board would be really a great feat. Right after the game the players themselves were not aware of this "simple" win, thinking that Black had already squandered his chances earlier.}) 55. h6 Re2+ ({The golden moment is already gone,} 55... a4 56. g5 a3 (56... Rh7 57. Rb8+ Kc4 58. Ra8 $11) 57. g6 Kb2 (57... a2? 58. g7 $18 {[%csl Rb3] turns the tables}) 58. h7 Re8 59. Rc7 a2 60. Rb7+ $11 {leads only to repetition.}) 56. Kxd3 Rh2 57. g5 Rh3+ 58. Kd2 Rh2+ 59. Kd3 {White's pawns are too far advanced and Black must give a perpetual.} 1/2-1/2

