1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. Re1 Nd6 6. Nxe5 Be7 7. Bf1 Nf5 8. Nf3 O-O 9. d4 d5 10. c3 Bd6 11. Nbd2 Nce7 12. Bd3 Ng6 13. g3 c6 14. Nf1 Bd7 15. Qc2 Qc8 16. h4 Nfe7 17. Ne3 Re8 18. Bd2 Nf8 19. Re2 f6 20. Rae1 Be6 21. c4 Qd7 22. c5 Bc7 23. b4 Bf7 24. Nf5 Nxf5 25. Bxf5 Qd8 26. Rxe8 Bxe8 27. Qb3 Bd7 28. Bxd7 Qxd7 29. a4 Re8 30. Rxe8 Qxe8 31. b5 Qe4 32. bxc6 bxc6 33. Kg2 Ne6 34. Qb7 Bd8 35. Qd7 g5 36. hxg5 fxg5 37. Kg1 Qb1+ 38. Be1 Qe4 39. Bd2 Qb1+ 40. Ne1 Qe4 41. Nf3 1/2-1/2

[Event "London Classic 7th"]
[Site "London"]
[Date "2015.12.11"]
[Round "7"]
[White "Carlsen, Magnus"]
[Black "Nakamura, Hikaru"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D11"]
[WhiteElo "2834"]
[BlackElo "2793"]
[PlyCount "155"]

{3 rounds to go, and not much positive could be said of the World Champion's experiences in the Grand Chess Tour as of yet. But when, despite a total minus score, you still have a chance in the overall standings it's hard not to be motivated, especially when facing the leader, Nakamura.} 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6!? {An interesting surprise by Nakamura, who has recently, at least result-wise, done quite well with 2...e6 against Magnus. But having suffered in the opening in St. Louis, as well as trying to be the first to surprise in this crucial game, Hikaru goes back to the Slav.} 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 {Magnus' preferred line, giving Naka his chance to spring his surprise:} Bg4!? {Against Magnus, Naka has played only 4...a6.} 5. h3 Bh5 6. cxd5!? {Played after prolonged thought. It's a rare move at the top level these days, but serves the purpose of not having to face Nakamuras preparation in the main lines.} cxd5 7. Nc3 e6 8. g4 Bg6 9. Ne5 Nfd7! {Due to the exchange of c-pawns the c6-square is available for the b8-knight, and like this then Black gets a very harmonious position.} 10. Nxg6 hxg6 11. Bg2 Nc6 12. e4!? {An interesting way to change the structure, as Black was completely fine after} (12. O-O f5 {in Tregubov- Anand from Corsica 2014. Maybe 11...f5!? would be Black's most exact move-order, but as Black is fine in the game, it is of minor importance.}) 12... dxe4 13. Nxe4 Bb4+ 14. Nc3 Nb6 15. O-O O-O (15... Nxd4 {was interesting, as} 16. Bxb7 Rxh3 {would give Black an overwhelming attack, should White take the exchange. However by playing less materialistically with} 17. Bg2 Rh8 18. Bf4 {White keeps decent compensation for the sacrificed pawn. But Nakamura seems reluctant to play his normal aggressive style in this game, bearing in mind that a draw would keep his tournament lead, and effectively eliminate Magnus from the fight for overall 1st place.}) 16. d5 exd5 17. Nxd5 Bc5 18. Nc3 Bd4 19. Qf3 Qf6?! {Again, sticking to his drawing strategy.} 20. Qxf6 Bxf6 21. Bf4 Rad8 22. Rad1 {After a not too impressive opening, White has gained some advantage, with basically a free pair of bishops. Even so in a symmetrical structure without queens Black's position should be drawable, even if unpleasant. But now Nakamura plays more according to his instincts and unbalances the position instead of the more obvious rook-swaps in the d-file.} Bxc3 23. bxc3 Na4 24. c4 Nc3 25. Rd2! {A small, but important move. Like this White keeps opposing in the d-file, while simultaneously covering the e2-square.} Rxd2 26. Bxd2 Ne2+ {As the bishop has vacated f4, this check is no longer dangerous, but} (26... Nxa2 27. Bxc6! bxc6 28. Ra1 {would just trap the knight, so Black has no choice.}) 27. Kh2 Rd8 28. Be3 Nc3 29. a3 Rd3 30. Rc1 Nd1 31. Be4 Rd7 32. Bc5 Nb2 33. Rc2 Na4 34. Be3 {The position has stabilised, White keeps his pair of bishops, and Black had to withdraw his knight.} Nb6 35. c5!? {To some extent limiting the black-squared bishop, but even if} (35. Bxb6 axb6 36. Rb2 {also is promising, the risk is that some sort drawn rook-ending a pawn up, could easily end up being the result of White's effort. So Magnus wisely retains the tension.}) 35... Nd5 36. Rd2 Nf6 37. Rxd7!? {It would seem to me, there was logic in keeping the rooks, having more pieces to try and complicate matters, but Magnus believed the black fortress was in no way impregnable.} Nxd7 38. Kg3 Kf8 39. f4 Nf6 40. Bf3 Ke7 {The time-control has been reached, without too much having happened in the game so far. White does of course have the bishop pair, but Black's position is solid, without obvious weaknesses, and White's structure is somewhat shattered, and the knights have strongholds on c6 and f6. It is easy to understand if Nakamura felt reasonably confident he woud draw this position without too much trouble. For a start it's hard to see how White can improve his position, and the next move in many ways looks like worsening it!} 41. f5! gxf5 42. gxf5 {What has White achieved by this exchange? For a start all his pawns are now isolated, as well as the f6-stronghold having become even more solid. Even so there has been considerable progress for White. His king can proceed further up the board, combined with pushing the h-pawn, and one advantage of the bishops over the knights, is that it is the bishop who at any moment can choose to exchange itself. Right now xc6 followed by g5 is not a threat, as after ...f8 xf6 gxf6 the pawn ending is drawn as the black king is in time with counterplay on the h-file while the white kings walks to a5, but Black constantly has to be on his guard.} Kd7 43. Kf4 Ne8 {.} 44. Kg5! {As so often, the king becomes a powerful piece in the endgame, and is here excellently placed on g5. After 44...f6 45.xc6+ followed by 46.d4 comes close to forcing a winning pawn ending, but Black seems to draw by a tempo, meaning just 45.h4! being the right response keeping the tension and further improving the white position. And after the tempting 44...f6+ White just plays 45.h5! when xc6+ followed by g6 becomes a decisive threat so Black has no choice, but just has to keep biding his time.} Ke7 (44... Nf6 45. h4!) (44... f6+ 45. Kh5!) 45. Bf4 a6 46. h4 Kf8 47. Bg3 Nf6 48. Bd6+ Ke8 49. Kf4 Nd7 50. Bg2 Kd8 51. Kg5 Ke8 52. h5 Nf6 53. h6! {After patiently making slight improvements to his position, White now plays the very essential swap of pawns on the kingside. It does reduce the material left on the board, but much more importantly undermines the f6-square and as well manages to break through even further with the king.} Nh7+ 54. Kh5 Nf6+ 55. Kg5 Nh7+ 56. Kh4! {Stepping out of the reach of the black knights, leaving Black no choice but to take and allow the white king in.} gxh6 {.} 57. Kh5 Nf6+ 58. Kxh6 Ng4+ 59. Kg7! {White has made huge progress, and almost made it look like the position after move 40 was a forced win. But Black's resistance is in no way broken, as the downside of the white king having reached g7, is that the f5-pawn is much harder to defend and Nakamura instantly tries to maximise his counterplay against it.} Nd4 60. Be4 Nf2 61. Bb1! {The chessboard is just big enough for the bishop to hide from the black knights while keeping f5 protected.} Ng4 {.} 62. Bf4 f6 {Fixing the f-pawn and basing his counterplay on attacking it.} ({Harassing the bishops will not really work, e.g.} 62... Ne2 63. Bc7 Nc3 64. Bc2 Ne3 65. Bd3 {illustrates that Black would just be making 1-move threats with no purpose.}) 63. Be4 Nf2 64. Bb1 Ng4 65. Be4 Nf2 {Black's counterplay makes a lot of sense. In order to proceed, White has to give up the f-pawn further reducing material.} 66. Bxb7 Nd3 {The first 40 moves indeed were nothing special, while the next 25 constitute a very instructive ending, where White breaks through a very solid looking fortress. But the brilliancy and tactical complications are here very unusually kept for the last stage of the game, happening after even 6 hours of play.} 67. Kxf6!! {A stunning piece sacrifice, and the only way to win!} ({It looks like} 67. Bd6? Nxf5+ 68. Kxf6 Nxd6 69. Bc6+! Kd8 70. cxd6 {would lead to an easily winning ending for White, but surprisingly this position is a draw! White simply has no way to get to the queenside with his king, without allowing the knight to harass the white pawns:} Nb2 71. Ke6 Nc4 72. a4 a5 73. Kd5 Ne3+ 74. Kc5 Ng4 {being the principal line. After} 75. Kb6 Ne5 76. Bb5 Nf7 {Black is just in time, as} 77. d7 Ne5 78. Kxa5 Nxd7 {is a draw, as Black's king reaches the crucial c8-square in the pawn ending.}) 67... Nxf4 68. Ke5! {This is the point. The king attacks both knights, which are forced into a purposeless existence, simply defending each other.} Nfe2 ({After} 68... Nde2 69. Bxa6 {the knights are completely stuck,}) ({and} 68... Nxf5 69. Kxf5 Nd3 70. c6 Kd8 71. Bxa6 {leaves White two pawns up, with no fortress in sight, so Nakamuras plays his best chance.}) 69. f6? {A mistake that could have ruined an otherwise great game and tournament comeback.} (69. c6! {would have crowned his efforts, as Black has no other choice than to give up a knight straightaway, as 69...d8 70 f6! will lead to the promotion of a white pawn, but after} Nxc6+ 70. Bxc6+ Kf8 71. Bd5! {it makes a huge difference compared to the game, that the white pawns are still on a3 as well as on f5. The difference is clearly seen after} Nc3 72. Bc4 a5 {as White now doesn't have to defend the a4-pawn and can just simply play} 73. Kd4 Nd1 74. a4 Ke7 (74... Nb2 {would have been relevant were the white pawn on f6, but here after} 75. Bb3 Ke7 76. Kc3 Nxa4+ 77. Bxa4 Kf6 {the bishop can defend the pawn, due to it still being on a white square.}) 75. Bb3 Nf2 76. Kc5 Kf6 77. Be6 {and again it helps that the pawn is on f5 and White wins by just collecting a5 and then queening the advancing a-pawn while his pawn and bishop securely defend each other.}) 69... a5 70. a4 {Looks winning, as it puts Black in a kind of zugzwang, and, as 70.c6 b5 leads nowhere, the logical continuation.} Kf7 71. Bd5+ Kf8?? {looks like the solid and obvious move, keeping the promotion square under control, however it leaves the f6-pawn unattacked, which proves fatal.} (71... Kg6! {would have seen Black just escape, as after} 72. c6 Nxc6+ 73. Bxc6 Kf7 74. Bd5+ Kf8 {it makes a deciding difference that the white pawns are now on a4 and f6, and thus much easier to attack. After} 75. Bc4 Nc3 76. Bb3 {Black just bides his time with} Ke8 {and} 77. Kd4 Ne2+ {almost by magic draws. The crucial point is} 78. Kc4 ({and as well} 78. Kc5 Nc3! {threatening to take on a4, and after the bishop takes back, then ...f7 clearing off the last pawn. It still feels very counter-intuitive to me, that this, as well as the ending analysed above both are drawn for Black, but the computer does verify that there is no way, White can exploit his seemingly big superiority.}) 78... Nc1! {attacking the bishop, and then having ...f7 next winning the f6-pawn,}) {Even so, the drama was not over, as in the commentary room, the computers indicated that White had only one way to exploit the favour, and Magnus took almost all of his remaining 2 minutes on the clock, before calmly retreating his king.} 72. Ke4! {The point is that is the king reaches e3, the knihts are doomed, as the king attacks both, and c4 next will just pick one up for free, so Nakamura tries:} Nc2 73. c6 Nc3+ 74. Ke5! {The king has done its job and now returns to its more natural place, helping the passd pawns. The threat, is c7 queening, so taking on a4 is forced, but fails as...} Nxa4 75. Bb3 {...is a simple fork, picking up one of the knights, while still keeping the 2 passed pawns, leaving Black in a hopeless position.} Nb6 76. Bxc2 a4 77. c7 Kf7 78. Bxa4 ({And seeing that} 78. Bxa4 Nc4+ 79. Kd5 Nb6+ 80. Kc5! {is hopeless and that after} Nc8 (80... Nxa4+ 81. Kc6!) 81. Bd7 Na7 82. Kb6 {White soon queens, Nakamura resigned. A lot was still to come, before Magnus had pulled of a spectacular last minute comeback, to take both the tournament and the tour, but without this win, the possibility would not have been there.}) 1-0

