{A look at the tournament standings, as well as keeping day 5 in Paris in mind, this might have been the perfect moment for solidity and protecting one's lead. But the World Champion said that in Leuven he wanted to loosen up a bit, so here we go!} 1. c4 e5 2. e3 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 {In the rapids Anand had played 3...b4 here. Magnus was about to play an excellent game, but lost the thread after Black defended well, and had to count himself lucky that he saved that game.} 4. g4!? {If you ever should want to play the Grob, this is probably as good as it gets. Not exactly a recommendation.} Bb4 {Aronian has played 4...h6 5.g2 d6 against Rapport, and Short-Docx went 4...d5 5.g5 d4 6.gxf6 dxc3 both leading to atypical and balanced positions.} 5. g5 Bxc3 6. bxc3 Ng8 {MVL's concept has a definite logic, the g5-pawn is weak and e.g. 7.h4 h6 is excellent for Black. White's pieces are in their starting positions, but Carlsen now unleashes them with amazing energy over the following moves.} 7. d4! Qxg5 8. d5 Nd8 9. d6 c6 10. Nf3 Qf5 11. Rg1 Ne6 {This is the kind of position where one of the sides will end up busted. Give Black the time to consolidate, say ...f6 ...b6 ...b7 and White is doomed. Alas:} 12. e4! {Speed matters more than material, and being already a pawn down Magnus accelerates his development by shedding another.} Qxe4+ 13. Be3 {Now 14.d3 trapping the black queen is threatened, and if 13...f4 then 14.g5 f6 15.d2 d3+ 16.xd3 xd3 17.xg7 gives White a winning attack, so Maxime has to retreat:} Qf5 (13... Nf4 14. Rg5 f6 15. Nd2 Nd3+ 16. Bxd3 Qxd3 17. Rxg7 $18) 14. Ng5 Nf4 {A logical move stopping 15.d3 as well as the threat of xe6 followed by xg7.} 15. Bxf4! {Again, its not a matter of "small" factors like number of pawns or the pair of bishops. Black is not allowed to consolidate whatever that costs in traditional values.} Qxf4 16. Rg4 Qf6 17. Ne4 Qh6 18. Qf3 {The game has his first "slow" moment in a while, with White not having a direct threat. Quite logically Black uses it to catch up in development and trying to swap off the dominating knight on e4, but oddly the better move would be 18...g6!? intending to put the queen on g7.} Nf6 ({My computer comes up with the following line:} 18... g6 19. Rg5 Qg7 20. Qg3 Kf8 21. Rxe5 f6 22. Ra5 b6 23. Ra3 {which is a strange mix of logic and absurdity.}) 19. Rxg7 {Tempting, and very much in the style of this game.} ({Even so} 19. Nxf6+ {was objectively better as after} Qxf6 20. Qxf6 gxf6 21. c5! {White has excellent compensation for the 2 sacrificed pawns,} b6 22. Bc4 Bb7 23. Rb1! {being an instructive sample line, if} b5 24. Bb3 {Black's bishop ends up locked in, and White will eventually collect a number of black pawns.}) 19... Qxg7? (19... Kf8! {was better,} {Less fancy, but better would be} 20. Rg3 ({as} 20. Nxf6 Kxg7 21. Nh5+ Kf8 22. c5 (22. Nf6 Qf4) 22... e4! {defends well, e.g.} 23. Qf5 Rg8 {when it's difficult to sufficiently strengthen the white attack.}) 20... Nxe4 21. Qxe4 Qxd6 22. Qh4 {keeping sufficient counterplay to balance the position.}) 20. Nxf6+ Kd8 21. Qf5 {Now however Black's position is hopeless.} Re8!? ({If} 21... Qf8 {then} 22. Rd1! {is completely paralysing, making Black practically unable to move any piece.} (22. Qxe5?! Qe8! {fights})) 22. Nxe8 Kxe8 23. c5 {Logical, hemming in Black's position,} ({but the more direct} 23. f4 exf4 24. Kd2 {followed by e1+! would have been just as strong.}) 23... b5 24. Bd3 f6 25. Ke2 Kf7 26. Rh1!! {White has numerous ways to win, the game-move however has beauty to it, almost in mathematical fashion solving the puzzle of how to get a white rook in the g-file as fast as possible.} Rb8 27. Kf1! Qg6 28. Qf3 Qh6 29. Rg1 Ba6 30. Qg4! {Not allowing ...g8, as well as nailing Black down to the defence of the d7-pawn.} Rd8 31. Rg3 e4 32. Qxe4 Re8 33. Qg4 Bc8 34. Kg2 (34. Bc2! {would have ended things here and now, but the result is never in doubt anyway.}) 34... Qg5 35. Qf3 Qd5 36. Qxd5+ cxd5 37. Bxh7 Re5 38. f4 Re2+ 39. Kf1 Rxh2 40. Bg8+ Kf8 41. Bxd5 Rh8 42. Kf2 Ba6 43. Ke3 Ke8 44. Kd4 Kd8 45. Rg7 Bc8 46. c6 dxc6 47. Bxc6 a5 48. Kc5 {An extraordinary game, in which the sequence from move 7 to 27 is impressively energetic and creative in itself, but being from a blitz game obviously makes it something quite special.} 1-0

