{Day 5 of the Grand Chess tour was supposed to be a duel between leaders Magnus Carlsen and Hikaru Nakamura, but while 9 rounds seems too little to catch a 3 point lead, for MVL actually it was 1 too many, as it allowed Magnus with a last rounds win vs. So to catch up, and force a tiebreak.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4!? {The time when we stopped rolling our eyes seeing this move is long gone, and nowadays its not even played to avoid theory, but rather it has become a topical discussion on its own.} g6 3. e3 Bg7 4. Nf3 d6 5. Be2 Nh5!? {In Stavanger Maxime had played 4...0-0, but here his 4th move gave Black this extra option, which, had Magnus feared it, 5.h3 would have prevented.} 6. Bg5 h6 7. Bh4 g5 8. Nfd2 Nf4 {A very human move, intending to weaken the white pawn structure, but it's noteworthy that the computer prefers just ...gxh4.} 9. exf4 gxh4 10. c3 c5 11. dxc5 dxc5 12. Na3 O-O 13. O-O (13. Qc2 {intending 14.0-0-0 also was relevant, but Magnus aims at exploiting Black's weakened structure in a more positional manner.}) 13... Nc6 14. Re1 h3 15. g3 e5 16. fxe5 Nxe5 17. Nf3 Nxf3+ 18. Bxf3 Qf6 19. Nc4 Rd8 20. Qe2 Be6 21. Bg4?! {Looks textbook, but surprisingly allows sufficient counterplay.} (21. Ne3 {controlling the white squares, leaves a pleasant edge after} Rd7 22. a4 Rad8 23. Rad1 {when White keeps considerable pressure.}) 21... Bxg4 {Good,} ({but even better was the direct} 21... b5 {as} 22. Bxe6 ({White would play} 22. Ne3 {, but then Black has an improved version of the game.}) 22... Qxe6 23. Qxe6?! fxe6 {leaves Black way too active with ...b4 and ...d2 to follow.}) 22. Qxg4 b5 23. Ne3 Rd2 24. Re2 Rad8 25. Rae1 Rxe2 26. Rxe2 Qe6! {Just in time, saving the pawn on h3 and forcing a holdable ending.} 27. Qxe6 fxe6 28. Kf1 b4 29. cxb4 cxb4 30. Rc2 b3! 31. axb3 Rb8 32. Rc6 Rxb3 33. Rxe6 Rxb2 34. Nf5 Bf8 35. Nxh6+ Kg7 36. Ng4 a5 37. Ra6 Ra2 {The day certainly hasn't gone well for Carlsen, a 3 point lead, as well as the opening advantage in this game are gone, in other words: It's time to save the day!} 38. f4! {Obviously risky, Black's plan of pushing the pawn to a3, then going ...b2 and ...a2 threatening ...b1+ followed by a1= is close to unstoppable, but Magnus judged that his own trumps would arrive just in time.} a4 39. f5 Bb4! {Black has to vacate the f8-square for his king, as} (39... a3? 40. Ra7+! Kh8 41. Nf6 {wins, even if the line is complex, but} Bg7 42. Ra8+ Bf8 43. Rxf8+ Kg7 44. Nd7 Ra1+ 45. Ke2 a2 46. f6+! {which wins as after} Kg6 47. Ne5+! {it becomes trivial as 48.a8 will happen next, and the black motif of 48...h1 49.xa2 xh2+ now is insufficient as the f-pawn will queen or if ...xf6, the knight joins the defence with g4+.}) 40. Ra7+ Kf8 41. f6 Ra1+?! {Not a mistake in the sense of changing the objective evaluation of the position, but after} (41... a3! {it is White who has to be careful} {and e.g. avoid} 42. Ne5?? {losing to} (42. Ra6 {still holds, but it's not obvious, it's White who then plays for the win.}) 42... Ra1+! {when White would lose a piece.}) 42. Ke2 a3 43. Nh6 Re1+?! {Dealing with the threat of a8 mate, but} (43... Ke8 {would have been an easier road to the draw as White's best would be} 44. f7+ {, but as the game shows, White prefers his pawn on f6 to create threats.}) 44. Kd3 Re6? {An important square for the black rook, but paradoxically occupying it immediately, means losing control over it!} (44... Bd6! {would prevent what happens next:}) 45. Ra8+ Re8 46. Ra6! Rc8? ({The absolutely last chance was} 46... Re5 {intending 47...a5, even if White keeps serious winning chances after} 47. Ra4) 47. g4! Rd8+ 48. Ke4 Ke8 49. g5 Bf8 50. Kf5 Rd2 51. Ra8+ Rd8 52. Rxd8+ Kxd8 53. g6! a2 54. g7 Bxg7 55. fxg7 a1=Q 56. g8=Q+ Kc7 57. Qg3+ Kd7 58. Qxh3 Qg7 59. Kf4+ Kc7 60. Nf5 Qb2 61. Qh7+ Kc8 62. Qg8+ {And with 63.g7+ exchanging off the queens happening next, Maxime resigned. Not in any way a fantastic game, especially for Carlsen's standards, but some days things just do not work, and then getting the job done anyway is very much part of a successful sporting career. A draw was still to be made to clinch the Paris title, and with solid defensive moves the World Champion forced his opponent to take risks, and agreed to a repetition of moves in a superior position putting an end to a rollercoaster of a day.} 1-0

