1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. Qa4 Be7 6. Bg2 c6 7. Nc3 O-O 8. O-O d5 9. Ne5 Qe8 {This line was examined in CBM 93, annotations to Kalinitschew - Graf by GM A.Finkel. As that game was played many years ago, a lot of new material has appeared since then.} 10. b3 (10. cxd5 {is White's most popular option but Black has no problems after the exchange of queens. Here is a relatively recent example:} cxd5 11. Qxe8 Rxe8 12. Bf4 Nbd7 13. Nxd7 Nxd7 14. Rfc1 Rac8 15. a4 g5 16. Bd2 Bb4 17. e4 dxe4 18. Bxg5 Bxc3 19. bxc3 Bd3 20. a5 f6 21. Bf4 Nf8 22. f3 exf3 23. Bxf3 Ng6 24. Bd6 Red8 25. Bb4 Rc7 26. Ra3 Ne7 27. c4 Bg6 28. axb6 axb6 29. Ra6 Nc8 30. d5 exd5 31. c5 Rdd7 32. Ra8 bxc5 33. Rxc5 Rxc5 34. Bxc5 Rc7 35. Bd4 Be4 $11 {1/2 Socko,B (2641)-Markus,R (2567)/Dresden 2007/CBM 118 (50)}) (10. Qb3 {was played by GM Sh.Mamedyarov in a rapid game. He won with the help of his opponent:} Nfd7 11. cxd5 Nxe5 12. dxe5 cxd5 13. Rd1 Nd7 (13... Nc6! $36) 14. e4 dxe4 15. Nxe4 Nxe5 16. Bf4 Nc4 17. Nd6 Nxd6 18. Bxa8 Nb7 19. Bxb7 Bxb7 20. Rac1 Qa8 21. Rd7 Bf6 22. Qd3 $13 {1-0 Mamedyarov,S (2742)-Ibanez Aullana,J (2301)/Villarrobledo 2008/CBM 126 Extra (29)}) 10... b5 (10... Ne4!? 11. Nxe4 Bb5 12. cxb5 cxb5 13. Qa3 Bxa3 14. Bxa3 f6! 15. Nd6 Qd8 16. Bh3!? fxe5 17. Bxe6+ Kh8 18. dxe5 $44) 11. Qa5 Bd8 (11... bxc4!? 12. bxc4 Qd8 13. Qa4 Nfd7!) 12. Qa3 Be7 13. c5 {In this structure White keeps some pressure. His c5-pawn can't be undermined as Black's pawn is already on b5, and preparing ...e6-e5 is not easy for Black either.} Bb7 14. Qb2 a5 15. Nd3 b4 16. Na4 Nbd7 17. a3 $146 (17. Bf4 Ba6 18. Rfd1 Qd8 (18... Nh5!?) 19. a3 bxa3 20. Qxa3 Nh5 21. Bd6 Bxd6 22. cxd6 Bxd3 23. Rxd3 Nhf6 24. Rc1 Nb8 25. Rdc3 Ne8 26. Rxc6?! Nxc6 27. Rxc6 Nf6 28. Nc5 Rb8 29. d7 Ra8 $13 {1-0 Beliavsky,A-Antoshin,V/Soviet Union 1978/EXT 2010 (42)}) 17... Ba6 18. Rd1 Bxd3 19. Rxd3 Ne4 {[%cal Yf7f5]} (19... bxa3!? 20. Rxa3 (20. Qxa3 e5) 20... e5 21. dxe5 Ng4) 20. f3 (20. Bxe4 dxe4 21. Rd1 f5 22. Qc2 bxa3 23. Rxa3 Qh5 $132) 20... Nef6 {After provoking the weakening f2-f3, ...e6-e5 can be more effective.} 21. Rd1 e5 22. e3 (22. dxe5 Nxc5! 23. exf6? Bxf6 24. Qb1 Nxa4 25. bxa4 Qxe2) 22... bxa3 23. Qc2 Bd8 {[%cal Yd8c7] A standard manoeuvre. The bishop protects the crucial a5-pawn and b6-square, as well as aiming at White's kingside.} 24. Bxa3 Rb8 25. Re1 Qe6 26. Nb2 Re8 27. Nd3 Bc7 28. Bb2 e4 29. Nf2 exf3 30. Bxf3 Ne4 31. Nxe4 dxe4 32. Bd1 Nf6 {[%csl Gd5]} 33. Bc3 Nd5 34. Bxa5 {This pawn is not important anymore as Black has an excellent kingside counterplay, and White's b3 pawn can't move forward.} Ra8 35. Qd2 Qg6 {[%cal Yh7h5]} 36. Kh1?! (36. Ra4 $142 Rxa5 37. Rxa5 h5! $44) 36... Qh6 37. b4?! (37. Kg1 Rxa5 38. Rxa5 Bxa5 39. Qxa5 Nxe3 40. Qd2 Nf5 41. Qxh6 gxh6 (41... Nxh6 42. b4 $13 {[%cal Yd4d5]}) 42. Bc2 Nxd4 43. Rxe4 Rxe4 44. Bxe4 Nxb3 45. Bxc6 Nxc5 $11) 37... Bxg3 38. Rg1 Bh4 39. Bb3 Bg5?! (39... Qxe3 $142 {although the position after} 40. Qxe3 Nxe3 41. b5 (41. d5 Nxd5 42. b5 Bf6 43. Ra2 Reb8 44. b6 Bd4) 41... Bf6! (41... cxb5 42. d5 $44 {[%csl Gc5,Gd5]}) 42. bxc6 Bxd4 43. Ra2 {[%csl Gc6] was not easy to evaluate. GM Magnus Carlsen prefers a simpler course but White obtains passed pawns anyway, with parity of material.}) 40. Bxd5 Bxe3 41. Bxf7+ Kxf7 42. Qa2+ Qe6 43. Rgf1+ Kg8 44. Qxe6+ Rxe6 45. Rad1 Bg5 46. d5 cxd5 47. Rxd5 Bf6 48. Rd6 Rxd6? (48... Kf7 $142 {Still, White keeps winning chances after} 49. Rxe6 Kxe6 50. Rc1 Bg5 51. Rc2) 49. cxd6 {[%mdl 4096]} Bd8 (49... e3 50. Re1 ({but not} 50. Rxf6 gxf6 51. Kg2 Kf7 52. d7 Ke7 53. d8=Q+ Rxd8 54. Bxd8+ Kxd8 55. Kf3 Kc7 56. Kxe3 Kc6 57. Ke4 Kb5 58. Kf5 Kxb4 59. Kxf6 Kc5 60. Kg7 h5 $11) 50... Kf7 51. Rxe3 {, and White should win.}) 50. Rf5? {Missing the victorious} (50. Bc7! e3 51. b5 Bh4 52. d7 e2 53. Rg1! (53. Rc1? Rf8! 54. Kg2 Kf7 55. Kf3 (55. b6 Ke6 56. b7 Kxd7) 55... Ke6+ 56. Kxe2 Kxd7 $11) 53... e1=Q (53... Bf2 54. Rc1) (53... Rf8 54. b6 Rf1 55. Bg3) 54. Rxe1 Bxe1 55. d8=Q+ Rxd8 56. Bxd8 Kf7 57. b6 Bc3 58. b7 Be5 59. Bh4 Bb8 60. Bg3 Ba7 61. b8=Q Bxb8 62. Bxb8 $18 {Not an easy line to find!}) 50... Bxa5 51. bxa5 Rd8! 52. Rd5 (52. a6 Rxd6 53. Ra5 Rd8 54. a7 Ra8 55. Kg2 Kf7 56. Kf2 Ke6 57. Ke3 Kd6 58. Kxe4 Kc6 59. Ke5 Kb6 60. Ra1 Rxa7 61. Rxa7 Kxa7 62. Ke6 Kb6 63. Kf7 g5 $11) 52... Kf7 53. a6 Ke6 54. Rd4 Kd7 55. Rb4 e3 56. Kg2 Rf8 57. a7 e2 1/2-1/2

