1. c4 g6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 c6 6. O-O d5 7. Qa4 {A rare move, which Black meets with a novelty.} a6 $146 {[%cal Yb7b5][%mdl 8] Very logical.} (7... Nbd7 8. cxd5 Nb6 9. Qa3 cxd5 10. Nc3 Bf5! (10... Nc4 11. Qb4 e6 12. Bf4 Re8 13. Ne5 Bf8 14. Qb3 $14 {1-0 Portisch,L (2645)-Ivkov,B (2535)/Petropolis 1973/IZT (37)}) 11. b3 Ne4 12. Nd1 a5 13. Bf4 Nc8 14. Rc1 Ncd6 $11 {0-1 Oll,L (2615)-Norwood,D (2545)/London 1994/EXT 2000 (42)}) (7... Nfd7!? 8. Qc2! (8. Na3 Nb6 9. Qc2 Bf5 10. Qc3 Be4 11. c5 N6d7 12. Bf4 b6 $15 {0-1 Grigorian,K (2485)-Sveshnikov,E/Moscow 1973/URS-ch (27)}) (8. cxd5 cxd5 9. Nc3 Nb6 10. Qd1 Nc6 11. Bf4 Bf5 $11 {1/2 Pigusov,E (2520)-Huzman,A (2500)/Sverdlovsk 1987/TD (17)}) 8... Nb6 (8... dxc4!? 9. Qxc4 e5) 9. c5 N6d7 10. Bf4 Re8 11. Nbd2 e5 12. dxe5 Nxe5 13. Nxe5 Bxe5 14. Bxe5 Rxe5 15. e4 dxe4 16. Qc3 Qe7 17. Nxe4 $14 {1/2 Sargissian,G (2673)-Volokitin,A (2678)/Germany 2008/CBM 123 (27)}) (7... Bf5!? 8. Nc3 Nbd7 9. cxd5 Nb6 10. Qb3 Nfxd5 11. Rd1 Qd6 12. Bd2 Nxc3 13. Bxc3 Qe6 14. d5 cxd5 15. Bxg7 Kxg7 16. Nd4 Qf6 17. Nxf5+ Qxf5 18. a4 Rfd8 19. a5 Nc4 20. Qxb7 e6 $11 {1/2 Carlsen,M (2690)-Svidler,P (2728)/Morelia/Linares 2007/CBM 117/[Marin,M] (29)}) 8. Bf4 (8. c5!? {is principled, e.g.} b6 9. cxb6 Qxb6 10. Qa3!? {[%cal Yb1c3,Yc3a4]}) 8... Be6 (8... b5!?) 9. cxd5 {Harmless.} (9. c5!?) 9... cxd5?! {This doesn't fit in with 8...e6.} (9... Nxd5 $142 ! $11 10. Bd2 $140 c5!) 10. Qb3 Ra7 11. Ne5 (11. Nc3 Nc6 12. Rac1 Na5 13. Qb4 Nc6 14. Qa3 $140 Ne4 15. Rfd1 Qb6 $132) 11... Ne4?! (11... Nfd7 12. Nxd7 Qxd7 13. e3 $14 {[%cal Yb1c3]}) (11... Nh5!? 12. Bd2 Bxe5! {Korchnoi's idea, typical for this kind of position.} (12... Nc6!? 13. Nxc6 bxc6 14. e3 Nf6) 13. dxe5 Nc6 14. Bh6 d4 15. Qa3 Ng7 $132 {[%cal Ye6d5]}) 12. Rd1 Nd7 13. Nc3 {[%csl Rd5]} Nxc3 14. Qxc3 (14. bxc3!? Nf6 15. a4 $14 {[%csl Ra7,Rb7][%cal Gb1b7]}) 14... Ra8 (14... Nxe5 15. dxe5 b5 16. Qd4 Rd7 17. Rdc1 $16) 15. Qb3 $16 a5 {A desperate attempt to break away.} (15... Nb6 16. Rac1 $16 {[%csl Rb6]}) 16. Bxd5 a4 17. Qxb7 Rb8 18. Nxf7?! {There was no need to go for a position with a material imbalancel. White could have kept his material advantage by means of} (18. Qc6 Bxd5 (18... Rb6 19. Bxe6! Rxc6 20. Nxc6 Qe8 21. Bc4 $16 e5 $140 22. Bg5!) (18... Nxe5 19. dxe5 Rb6 20. Qc4 $16) 19. Qxd5 Nxe5 20. Qxd8 Rfxd8 21. dxe5 $16) 18... Rxb7 19. Nxd8 Bxd5 20. Nxb7 Bxb7 21. Rac1 (21. Rdc1!? Bd5 22. Rc7 Rd8 23. e3) 21... Bd5 22. Rc7 Rd8 23. a3 Kf7 24. Rd2 Nf6 25. f3 Bb3 26. Be5? {[%mdl 8192] This blunder lets White's advantage slip but it was very difficult to convert it after, say,} (26. Rc5 Nd5 27. Be5 Bh6 28. Rd3) 26... Ne8! 27. Rc5 Bxe5 28. Rxe5 Nd6 29. Rc5 Nc4 30. Rd3 Nxb2 31. Re3 Rxd4 32. Rc7 Be6 {Black is materially better now but White's counterplay should be enough for him to hold his ground.} 33. Ra7 (33. Rc6!? Bd7 34. Rc7) 33... g5 (33... Nd1!? 34. Re5 Nc3 $15) 34. Rc3 Rc4 35. Re3 Rc2 36. Re4 Rc3 37. h4 h6 38. hxg5 hxg5 39. Ra6 Ba2 40. Ra7 Be6 41. Ra6 Bc4 42. Rc6 Rxa3 43. Rcxc4 Nxc4 44. Rxc4 1/2-1/2

