{[%evp 0,82,27,15,12,33,69,72,64,55,55,48,51,51,51,51,66,27,77,77,89,35,68,29,69,41,45,53,37,45,16,34,80,59,149,29,37,2,1,3,-37,-22,-19,-147,-18,-33,-2,-25,-39,-39,-39,-39,13,-93,3,-14,5,-46,-13,-13,-34,-16,-16,-16,-16,-13,-33,-31,-14,-49,-28,-25,-17,-126,-39,-206,-223,-231,-235,-236,-249,-257,-273,-281,-278]} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 d6!? {Double homage paid: to the founder of the understanding of the importance of pawn-structure in chess, Philidor, but also to Dubov who recently has revived the opening named after the great Frenchman.} 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Be7 6. Bf4 {White obviously has a lof of decent set-ups, but this being the most ambitious, aiming for opposite castling.} O-O 7. Qd2 c6 8. O-O-O b5 9. f3 b4 10. Nb1?! {The computer gives 10 Na4 as much better for White. In an interview Dubov was asked about the fact that the computer said the Philidor was better for White, answering along the lines of: "Yes, I can also read what the computer says".} a5 11. g4 a4 12. h4 c5 {Black might look less well placed for a pawn storm, but partly there is no alternative to a race to the opponent's king, partly his pawns are closer to the target as well as the knight on b1 being out of play.} 13. Nf5 Bxf5 14. gxf5 Nc6 15. h5 Nd4 16. Qg2 Kh8 {Maybe the key position in the game.} 17. Be3 ({Seing what happens in the game, not to mention Alphazero,} 17. h6!? {looks obvious. But Black has the stunning reply} g5!! 18. Be3 {is best} (18. Bxg5 Rg8) (18. Qxg5 Rg8 $19) {, but after} 18... Nd7 {followed by 19...Bf6 starts a strong counterattack on the black squares, as well as completely securing the black king, so that White wins a pawn on d4 is of very little relevance.}) (17. c3 {is the computer move, when after} bxc3 18. bxc3 Qb6 {Black gets a strong attack for the sacrificed piece.}) 17... h6! {Game over! Black's king now is completely safe, and will remain so, while Black starts taking his swings at the white monarch.} 18. Bd3 ({If} 18. Rg1 {then} Rg8 {simply defends and White has no reasonable way of continiuing his attack.}) 18... Nd7 19. Rhg1 Bf6 20. f4 Re8 {The computers evaluation is is fascinating. -3! Despite material equality, Black's attack means he is completely winning.} 21. Bxd4 cxd4 22. Nd2 Nc5 23. Nc4 b3 24. Kb1 bxc2+ 25. Bxc2 Rb8 26. Bd3 Rb4? {Magnus should have "kept it simple", just reinforcing his position with moves like} (26... Qc7) ({or} 26... Qe7) {Now suddenly it becomes concrete, and White gets a chance.} 27. a3 Rb3 28. Bc2 Rb8 29. e5! dxe5 30. fxe5 Rxe5 31. Nxe5 Bxe5 {Black obviously is fine, and practically, with the safer king has a position that is easier to play. But Dominguez is very much back in the game.} 32. Bd3 Bf6 33. Qc2 Qa5 34. Rg2 Nxd3 35. Qxd3 Qd5 36. Re2 Kh7 37. Qe4?? {Returns the favour, but Magnus with little time on the clock, still had to find a beautiful combination, but the World Champion indeed does deliver when needed:} Rxb2+!! 38. Kxb2 d3+ {The bishops primary task perhaps was defensive towards the g7-pawn, but at the same time it did point in the direction of White's king, with the potential now being relased.} 39. Kc1 Qc5+ 40. Kd2 Qc2+ 41. Ke1 Bh4+! {The sting in the end. If 42 Qxh4 Qxe2 is mate, and 42 Kf1 Qxd1+ also wins trivially, so Lenier resigned.} (41... Bh4+! 42. Qxh4 (42. Kf1 Qxd1+) 42... Qxe2#) 0-1

