[Event "Norway Chess 6th"]
[Site "Stavanger"]
[Date "2018.06.03"]
[Round "6"]
[White "So, Wesley"]
[Black "Carlsen, Magnus"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D13"]
[WhiteElo "2778"]
[BlackElo "2843"]
[PlyCount "87"]

{People like to say that I don't play my best against Carlsen. Don't think I hadn't noticed that, but it took my dad Renato to explain it to me.   He told me it is like being a very young and talented soccer player and having pictures of Pele all over your room and knowing by heart every game he ever played. You think about him, dream about him, grow up wanting to be as good as him and when playing by yourself even pretend you are him. And then one day Pele suddenly appears on the field. You can’t move. You can't breathe. Everything is a blur. Which goal posts are yours? You feel like you might faint. Or die. You are overwhelmed because he is older, bigger and has years of experience on you. You see that you are a kid with over size dreams.   Playing Carlsen in Norway is probably like playing Pele in Brazil. It has good points in that the focus is always on him and no one really cares about your games unless you lose to him. You can be sure of a dismissive attitude from the Norwegian commentators and a local audience who only came to see one player (who is that brown boy anyway?).   I appreciate this because it’s very freeing not to carry the burden of expectation. It allows me to focus on the excellence of the Altibox Norway Chess experience (which is one of the best organized events in Europe - a professional chess players dream tournament).   I really love Stavanger and after a meal of my favorite food (salmon), I went to play my game against the favored son of Norway, whom in my 3.5 years of professional play, I had never beaten in a classical game.} 1. d4 {Considering how well prepared Magnus is in the opening these days, I decided to stick to normal lines and not challenge him on the intricacies of the Ruy Lopez.} d5 2. c4 c6 3. cxd5 (3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 {is usually how my games with Magnus go. I've had this position against him with both colors.}) 3... cxd5 {Apparently the commentators announced I was playing for a draw by taking on d5... A shocking analysis, as we'd only traded one pawn!} 4. Bf4 Nf6 ({I thought he'd start with} 4... Nc6 5. Nc3 {and now he has the extra option:} e5!? (5... Bf5 6. e4!?)) 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Nf3 a6 ({I was expecting} 6... Nh5 7. Bd2 Nf6 {which seems to be the most solid variation, but Magnus went for the sideline.}) (6... Bf5 7. e3 e6 8. Qb3 Bb4 9. Ne5 {has become very fashionable lately. Yaroslav Zherebukh beat Ray Robson with it just a couple of months ago in the US Championships.}) 7. Rc1 Bf5 8. e3 {I had a little experience with this variation, having played it against Ivanchuk almost a decade ago when I was thirteen!} Rc8 (8... e6 9. Qb3 Ra7 {is how my game with Ivanchuk went, in World Cup 2009.}) 9. Be2 e6 10. O-O Nd7 {He played this quite fast, so I assumed he was still following his opening ideas.} (10... Be7 11. Qb3! Na5 12. Qa4+ {is supposed to be better for White due to} Nc6 13. Bxa6!) (10... Bd6 11. Bxd6 Qxd6 12. Na4 {is not a nice position for Black. Usually he wouldn't want to trade his dark-squared bishop in the Exchange Slav.}) 11. Na4 Be7 {This quietly threatens to attack on the kingside with ...g5-h5.} 12. h3 {Prophylaxis against the aforementioned idea.} O-O ({Magnus castled quite fast. Apparently still} 12... g5!? {is very good here. I won't give it an exclamation mark since the position remains very unclear, but it is definitely a good alternative.} 13. Bh2 h5 {Now the kingside pawns look threatening. The position is quite weird and analysis shows it's not easy to play for both sides:} 14. Nd2 (14. Ne1 g4 15. Bd3 Bxd3 16. Nxd3 Rg8 17. Kh1 $13) 14... g4 (14... b5 15. Nc3 Nf6 16. a4 b4 17. Ncb1) 15. hxg4 hxg4 16. Bxg4 Bd3! 17. Be2 Bb4! 18. Nf3 Be4 $132) 13. a3 {Preparing to push my pawns forward. White is simply faster and better equipped to seize the initiative on the queenside.} Na5 (13... b5 14. Nc5 Nxc5 15. dxc5 Bf6 16. Nd4 Bxd4 17. exd4 Qf6 18. Be3 e5 19. dxe5 Qxe5 20. Qd2 $14 {is a long forcing line that favors White.}) 14. Nc5! Nc4 15. b4 Nxc5 (15... b5 16. Nxa6 Nxa3 17. Nc5 Nc4 18. Ra1 $14) 16. dxc5 ({And not} 16. bxc5 b6 $11) 16... Nxa3?! (16... b6 17. Bxc4 dxc4 18. Qxd8 Bxd8 19. Bd6 $16) ({With hindsight} 16... b5! {bolsters the knight and it might be the best defense.} 17. a4 Nb2 18. Qd2 Nxa4 19. Nd4 Bg6 {is very unclear. White has compensation for the pawn but perhaps not much more.}) 17. Nd4 Be4 18. f3 Bg6 19. Qb3 {I wasn't really worried about sacrificing the a-pawn since his knight is sidelined in many lines.} Nc4 20. Bxc4 dxc4 21. Qxc4 Qe8!? {Interesting option and one I could hardly expect. Black keeps the bishop pair for now and he wants to kick my knight away from its central outpost by making the ...e6-e5 break work.} ({I thought he would go for} 21... Bg5 {which is the most natural move, and now} 22. Bxg5 Qxg5 {since I am slightly better, but not much more than that. In fact during the game I couldn't see anything clearly. My dark squares are a little vulnerable and Black is ready to fight for control of the d-file.} 23. Qc3 {is what I was thinking to play, and then:} Rfd8 24. Rfd1 h5 (24... Qe5 25. Kf2 Qf6 26. Ne2 $14) 25. Nb3 Bf5 26. Kh1! Qg3 27. Qe1 $14 {with a few accurate moves White will manage to keep a nagging edge, but over the board the position is never really crystal clear.}) 22. Bg3 {Just a quiet non-commital move, because I couldn't find anything definitive. I decided to just make a move and see what Magnus intended to do next.} (22. c6!? e5! (22... b5 23. Qc3 $18 {is just winning for White, the c-pawn is simply too strong.}) 23. Bxe5 bxc6 24. Nxc6 Bxb4 {was the line that Magnus calculated. Black holds here.}) (22. e4 e5 23. Bxe5 Bxc5 24. bxc5 Qxe5 {is better for White, as the bishop on g6 is buried for the time being, but I think Black can hold here too.}) 22... e5 (22... Bg5 {is better.} 23. Rfe1 Qd7 $14 {it's not that easy to break Black's defenses.}) 23. Nb3 Bd8? {This just blunders a pawn. Magnus missed 26.d6 in the variation below.} (23... e4 {is an option but I suppose he wasn't attracted to it.} 24. f4 Bf5 25. Nd4 Bd7 26. f5 $16) (23... Bf5 {also does not fully equalize:} 24. Bxe5 Be6 25. Qc3 Bxb3 26. Qxb3 Bxc5 27. Rxc5 Rxc5 28. Bxg7! $36) (23... h6! {is the best defense, but over the board it is hard to see why.}) 24. Qd5 {Grabbing the gift.} Qb5?! {Another mistake.} (24... Bc7 {is perhaps a better defense.} 25. Na5 (25. Qxb7 Bd3 26. Rfd1 Bb5 27. Nd2 $16) 25... Bxa5 26. bxa5 Qe7 27. Qxe5 Qxe5 28. Bxe5 Bd3 29. Rfd1 Bb5 {White is a clear pawn up, but the presence of opposite colored bishops give Black good holding chances. I would estimate Black's chances to draw around 30-40%.}) 25. Bxe5 Be7 (25... Qxb4 26. Bd6 {is what he overlooked.}) 26. Qd2 {I consolidate the pawn of course. Here I was very much delighted with my position.} (26. Na5! b6 27. Nb7 {is the computer's fastest way to destroy any Black resistance. Suffice to say that I did not even see 27.b7 during the game.}) 26... Rfd8 27. Bd6 Bf6 28. e4 h6 {White is definitely winning now. The only question is how to convert my advantage. Having a winning position does not mean a thing unless you can carefully nurse your advantage to a win. As a famous player once pointed out: 'Anyone can get an advantage, but only the BEST players convert them.'} 29. Nd4 Bxd4+ 30. Qxd4 Re8 ({Trading pawns does not help at all:} 30... a5 31. bxa5 Qxa5 32. Ra1 $18 {when the b7 pawn is going to fall.}) 31. Rfe1 Kh7 32. g4! {We were already quite short on time here that's why I didn't hesitate to attack!} (32. f4 f5 {is what I wanted to prevent. The last thing we want is for Black to have some sort of a light-square blockade.}) 32... f6 33. f4 Qc6? {After this blunder Black should be totally 100% lost.} (33... Bf7 {is clearly a better defense. The queen is just better placed on b5.} 34. f5 a5 35. bxa5 Ra8 {and if Black can activate a rook along the a-file he has some saving chances.}) 34. f5 Bf7 35. h4 {Here I knew for a fact that I was winning, however converting advantages is never easy, as you can see from the past games. For example there is a famous game of Magnus back in 2014, in a 4.f3 Nimzo. His opponent was totally winning for many moves, even reaching (computer evaluation) +15 at some point, but nevertheless he managed to find all the worst moves and in the end succeeded in losing the game.} Ra8 36. Rc2 (36. Rc3 {was my first intention and would be very strong. I rejected it because of} a5 37. g5 axb4 (37... Bh5 38. Rg3 {and compared with the rook on g2 there is a big difference: Black has no 38...f3! counterplay.}) 38. g6+ Bxg6 39. fxg6+ Kxg6 {and Black has two pawns but hey, a piece is a piece!}) 36... a5 37. g5 Bh5! {A very good sneaky last try, and one which I underestimated.} 38. g6+ {In Norway there is no time increment or delay until move 60, so time trouble is definitely an issue. Here I used up all my time calculating until I found a series of moves that would bring me to move 40 safely.} ({I was hesitant to open the g-file, but simplest is} 38. gxf6 gxf6 39. b5! {(A very important move, closing the a-file and opening the b-file for White.)} Qxb5 40. Rb2 Qd7 41. Qxf6 Rg8+ 42. Kh2 {when Black has absolutely no counterplay.}) 38... Kh8 39. b5! Qxb5 40. Rb2 Qc6? {Magnus used up almost all of his remaining time and made another mistake. This makes the game very easy to convert.} (40... Qd7 {would have made the game harder for me, but as my friend Anish Giri pointed out White still wins easily with the right moves:} 41. Qd5! (41. Reb1 Bf3 42. Rxb7 Qc6 43. e5 Qc8! {is what I missed in my calculations back in move 37.}) 41... Qc6 42. Qd3 a4 43. Rb6 Qc8 44. Qd5! {White just plays the same position a tempo down as in the game. No worries.} a3 (44... Ra7 45. Qf7! a3 46. c6 a2 47. Rxb7 $18) 45. Rxb7 Rg8 (45... a2 46. Rxg7) 46. c6 a2 47. Ra1 $18) 41. Rb6 Qc8 42. Qd5 a4 (42... Ra7 43. Qf7 {threatens c6 and wins. Due to back rank issues Black's counterplay comes way too late.} Bf3 44. c6 Rxe4 45. Rxe4 Bxe4 46. Bf8 $18 {and mate next move.}) 43. Rxb7 Rg8 (43... Bxg6 44. fxg6 Qg4+ 45. Kf2 {does not do much either, as I have g3 to counter his check on h4.}) 44. c6 {Threatening c7 and b8, therefore Magnus finally resigned! This was a clear big win for me, being my first victory against him in classical chess, and it also evened up the standings in the tournament since we were now co=leaders with +1, and only three games to go.} (44. Rc7 Qe8 45. Qd2 a3? 46. Qxh6+!! {is another way to win. In the after game interview, Ian Rogers asked me if I played especially focused because I was provoked by a statement my opponent made before the game. It seems he said that I am a player who makes boring draws and he could draw me at will. Actually, having no interest in social media I was completely unaware of the comments. When they told me, I burst out laughing because after all, so what? Sometimes I do make boring draws. Sometimes.}) 1-0

