1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f3 e5 7. Nb3 Be6 8. Be3 h5 (8... Be7 {is the other main line.}) 9. Qd2 Nbd7 10. Nd5 Bxd5 11. exd5 g6 12. Be2 Bg7 13. O-O a5 {A rare move, probably Black wants to avoid the a5 variation.} (13... O-O {is the most popular move with a lot of top games.}) 14. a4 O-O 15. Bb5 $146 {The first new move in the game. It is very logical to put the  on this square.} (15. Nc1?! {(Palekha-Kovchan, Serpukhov 2003) was weaker, because} Nb6! 16. c4 e4! $36 {with good activity for Black.}) 15... Qc7 16. c4 b6 17. h3 {A useful move to control the g4-square.} ({In the event of} 17. Rae1 Nc5 18. Nc1 e4 $132 {with some counterplay for Black.}) 17... Nc5 (17... e4?! {is dubious, as} 18. f4 $16 {and White has avoided ...g4; he is clearly better.}) 18. Nxc5 bxc5 19. Ra3! {A smart move! White prepares for b3, c6, b5 and attack the a5-.} Nh7 20. Rb3 f5 21. Bc6 Rab8 (21... Ra7!? {was the alternative,} 22. Qe1 ({The immediate} 22. Rb5 {could have been met by} f4 23. Bf2 Ng5 $36 {threatening ...e4 and Black looks nicely placed.}) 22... f4 23. Bd2 Ng5 24. Rb5 e4! {This break-through is the point!} 25. fxe4?! Bd4+ 26. Kh1 Qc8! {Threatening ...xh3.} 27. Bxf4 Nxh3 28. Bxd6 (28. gxh3? Qxh3+ $19) 28... Nf2+ 29. Rxf2 ({If} 29. Kh2 Qg4 $19 {and mate soon.}) 29... Bxf2 30. Qxf2 Rxf2 31. Rb8 Qxb8 32. Bxb8 Rh7 $17 {at first sight this is a very strange position, hard to evaluate. The engine gives winning for Black, but by human measures especially in a practical game this is complicated enough.}) 22. Rb5 f4 23. Bf2 e4! {A great -sacrifice to create counterplay on the kingside.} 24. Qc2! {A very high-level reply! White doesn't take, just plays a quiet move.} ({In the event of} 24. fxe4 Qe7 25. Qd3 (25. Qc2 f3 $40) 25... Qe5 $44 {with good compensation for the .}) 24... Qe7 (24... exf3? 25. gxf3 $16) (24... e3!? {would be interesting,} 25. Be1 Rxb5 26. axb5 Kf7 $132 {followed by ...d4 with a double-edged fight.}) 25. Qxe4 Qxe4 26. fxe4 Ng5 {Suddenly they have exchanged the s; White is a  up, but Black has some counterplay for it.} 27. Re1 Nf7 28. Rb1 ({In the event of} 28. b3 Bc3 29. Rc1 Bb4 {and it's not clear how White can improve his position.}) 28... Ng5 29. Rxa5 (29. Re1 $11 {would be a repetition.}) 29... Rb3! {A great move to create counterplay!} (29... Nxe4? {could have been met by} 30. Rb5! $16 {and the a- is unstoppable.}) 30. Rb5 Rd3 31. Re1? {The most natural move, but this is a decisive mistake!} (31. a5? {is too slow, because of} f3! 32. a6 fxg2 33. a7 Nxh3+ 34. Kxg2 Rxf2+ 35. Kh1 Rdd2! 36. a8=Q+ Kh7 $19 {and White can't avoid the mate!}) ({The brilliant} 31. e5!! {can only hold the position,} Bxe5 32. Re1 f3 33. h4! fxg2 34. hxg5 Rd2 35. Rxe5! dxe5 36. d6! Rfxf2 37. Bd5+ Kf8 38. Rb8+ $11 {and perpetual checks.}) 31... f3! {Black has a strong attack even without s too.} 32. h4 Nf7?! {Black is missing the chance.} (32... fxg2! {was the winning move,} 33. hxg5 Rd2 34. Be3 Rc2 $19 {and White can't avoid the ...e5, ...f1 followed by ...h2 winning plan. Of course in a practical game, it isn't so easy to find.}) 33. a5 Ne5 34. a6 Rd2! {The correct reply!} ({The immediate} 34... fxg2? {could have been met by} 35. Re2! $18 {and White has protected his position and the a- is unstoppable.}) 35. a7 fxg2 36. a8=Q Nf3+ 37. Kxg2 Nxe1+ 38. Kf1 Rxf2+ 39. Kxe1 Rxa8 40. Kxf2 Rf8+ 41. Ke2 Rf4 {The position is simplifying, still White needs some accurate moves to reach the draw, but for Carlsen it is not a difficult task.} 42. b4! {The best way!} cxb4 43. c5 Be5 ({The greedy} 43... dxc5?! {is dubious, as} 44. d6! {and suddenly the white s are becoming fast.}) (43... Rxe4+ 44. Kf3 Rxh4 45. cxd6 $11) 44. cxd6 Bxd6 45. Kd3 Kf7 46. Rb6 Bc5 47. Rb5 Bd6 48. Rb6 Bc5 49. Rb5 {What a great fight!} 1/2-1/2

