1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 c5 3. Nf3 e6 4. e3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. d4 cxd4 7. exd4 Nxc3 {A rare but solid continuation. Black usually plays} (7... Be7) ({or} 7... Bb4) 8. bxc3 Qc7 {This immediate attack on White's c3-pawn, preventing f1-d3, is the only way for Black to justify his previous move.} (8... Be7 9. Bd3 {is comfortable for White.}) 9. Bd2 {This move looks passive but other defences have their drawbacks, too:} (9. c4 Bb4+ 10. Bd2 Bxd2+ 11. Qxd2 b6 12. Be2 O-O 13. O-O Nd7 14. a4 Bb7 15. a5 Rfd8 16. Qe3 Rac8 17. Rfc1 bxa5 18. Nd2 Nb6 $11 {1/2 (93) Timman,J (2615)-Fressinet,L (2711) Malmo 2014}) (9. Bb5+ Nd7 10. O-O Bd6 11. d5 O-O 12. dxe6 fxe6 13. h3 Nf6 14. Re1 e5 15. Ba3 Bxa3 16. Qb3+ Kh8 17. Qxa3 Bd7 18. Rxe5 {1/2 (18) Ghaem Maghami,E (2596)-Kramnik,V (2777) Berlin 2015}) (9. Bb2 Nd7 (9... Bd6 10. Bd3 Nd7 11. O-O O-O 12. c4 Rd8 13. Qe2 Nf8 14. Qe4 Bd7 15. Qg4 Ng6 16. Qh5 Bf4 17. Rfe1 Bc6 18. Ng5 Bxg5 19. Qxg5 Qf4 $11 {1-0 (92) Khismatullin,D (2649)-Ghane Gardeh,S (2398) Moscow 2011}) 10. Bd3 Ba3!? 11. Qb3 Bxb2 12. Qxb2 O-O 13. O-O b6 14. Be4 Rb8 15. Rac1 b5 16. Rfe1 Bb7 $11 {0-1 (44) Klimentov,E (2196)-Kharitonov,A (2553) Voronezh 2015}) (9. Qc2 Nd7 (9... b6!? 10. Bb5+ Nd7 11. O-O a6 12. Bd3 Bb7 13. Ng5?! Bd6 14. f4 Nf6 15. Bd2 Rc8 16. Rae1 h6 17. Ne4 Nxe4 18. Bxe4 O-O $11 {1/2 (63) Milov,V (2669)-Houska,J (2392) Gibraltar 2009}) 10. Bd3 b6 11. O-O Bb7 12. Ng5 (12. Re1 Bd6 13. Be4 Bxe4 14. Rxe4 Nf6 15. Rh4 Rc8 16. Bd2 h6 17. Re1 Nd5 18. Qe4 Nf6 19. Qb1 Nd5 20. Rg4 Kf8 $13 {1-0 (33) Adams,M (2724)-Houska,J (2401) London 2007}) (12. Be4 $11) 12... Bd6 13. h3 h6 (13... Bh2+?! 14. Kh1 Bf4 15. Re1 Bxg5 16. Bxg5 h6 17. Bd2 Nf6 18. c4 O-O 19. a4 $14 {0-1 (36) Riazantsev,A (2646)-Kramnik,V (2777) Berlin GER 2015}) 14. Ne4 Be7 $11) 9... Nd7 10. Bd3 b6 {Black can develop his kingside bishop first:} (10... Be7!? 11. O-O b6 12. Re1 Bb7 13. Ng5 Nf6 14. Re3 h6 15. Rh3 Bd6 16. Qe2 O-O 17. Ne4 Bxe4 18. Bxe4 Nxe4 19. Qxe4 Rfe8 $11 {0-1 (29) Dickenson,N (2250)-Levitt,J (2445) Maidstone 1994}) (10... Bd6 11. O-O b6!? (11... O-O 12. Qe2 Nf6 13. Ne5 Bxe5 14. dxe5 Nd7 15. Rfe1! (15. Rae1 Nc5 16. Bb1 b6 17. Qh5 g6 18. Qh4 Ba6 19. c4 Nd7 $13 {0-1 (52) Hou,Y (2673)-Dimitrov,R (2445) Bastia 2014}) 15... Nc5 16. Bc2 $40) 12. Re1 (12. d5 Nc5 13. Bb5+ Bd7 14. Bxd7+ Qxd7 15. c4 O-O 16. Be3 Rac8 17. Qd4 Rfe8 18. Rad1 e5 $15 {0-1 (28) Bern,I (2345)-Ogaard,L (2395) Oslo 2006}) 12... Bb7 13. c4 O-O 14. Rc1 (14. Bc3 Rfe8 15. Ng5?! h6 16. Nf3 Rad8 $15 {0-1 (60) Rantanen,Y (2308)-Barbosa,O (2501) Pattaya 2015}) 14... Rac8 15. d5 Bf4 (15... Nc5!? 16. Bxh7+ $140 Kxh7 17. Ng5+ Kg6) 16. Ng5 Bxg5 17. Bxg5 Nc5 18. Re3 f6 19. Rh3 Nxd3 20. Qxd3 {1-0 (25) Nurkic,S (2415)-Majeric,Z (2345) Tuzla 2007} g6 $13) 11. Ng5 {White tries to exploit Black's delay in the development of his kingside but doesn't gain anything.} (11. O-O Bb7 12. c4 Bd6 {- see above}) 11... Nf6 12. Qf3 Rb8 13. O-O Bd6 14. Qh3 h6 15. Rfe1 (15. Nf3!? {is more logical, preventing castling. However, Black's position is solid enough in any case:} Bb7 (15... O-O? 16. Bxh6 gxh6 17. Qxh6) (15... Bf4 {is less accurate in view of} 16. Bxf4 Qxf4 17. Ne5 {[%csl Rf4]} O-O 18. g3 Qd2 19. Rfc1 {[%cal Rc1c2]}) 16. Rae1 Bxf3! {(the simplest: White's strong knight should be eliminated)} 17. Qxf3 Bxh2+ 18. Kh1 Bd6 19. d5 O-O 20. dxe6 Rbe8 $11) 15... O-O 16. Nf3 Bf4 17. Bxf4 Qxf4 18. Ne5 Bb7 {Black's queen is safe here.} 19. Rad1 (19. g3 Qd2 20. Rec1 Qb2 21. a4 Rfc8 {etc. - Black's extra tempo is crucial}) 19... Qg5 20. c4 Rbd8 21. Qg3?! ({As usualwith hanging pawns, it was better for White to keep queens on the board:} 21. Bf1 $11) 21... Qxg3 22. hxg3 {A marginal plus now belongs to Black as White's hanging pawns are rather weak. Still, a draw is the most probable outcome.} Rfe8 ({The immediate} 22... Nd7 {is more accurate as the most appropriate position of Black's rooks is yet to be determined.}) 23. f3 Nd7 24. Kf2 Kf8 25. Bc2 Nxe5 26. Rxe5 Re7 27. Bb3 Red7 28. Ke3 {, and the World Champion failed to break down White's defences:} g6 29. g4 Bc6 30. d5 exd5 31. cxd5 Bb7 32. f4 f6 {(forcing a draw)} 33. Re6 Bxd5 34. Rxd5 Rxd5 35. Bxd5 Rxd5 36. Rxf6+ Kg7 37. Rc6 Ra5 38. Rc2 Kf6 39. Rc6+ Kg7 40. Rc2 Kf6 1/2-1/2

