1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 g6 5. Nc3 Bg7 6. Be2 O-O 7. O-O b6 {Quite a passive system leading to a small plus for White. However, Black's position is solid and difficult to break.} 8. a4 {Among other options seen in practice:} (8. b4) (8. b3) (8. cxd5) (8. Bd2) (8. Qc2) (8. Ne5) (8. Qb3) 8... a5 (8... Bf5 9. a5 Nbd7 10. cxd5 cxd5 11. a6! Nb8 12. Ne5 Nfd7 13. Bf3 Nxe5 14. dxe5 Nc6 15. Bxd5 Rc8 16. e4 Bd7 17. f4 $16 {1-0 (35) Golod,V (2576)-Meenakshi,S (2310) Port Erin 2006}) (8... Nbd7 9. cxd5! cxd5 10. b3 Bb7 11. Ba3 Ne4 12. Rc1 Rc8 13. Nxe4 dxe4 14. Rxc8 Qxc8 15. Nd2 Re8 16. Qb1 e5 17. Nc4 Qa8 18. Nd6 $14 {1/2 (47) Zhou,J (2574)-Yu,R (2548) China 2014}) (8... Ba6 9. b3 (9. a5 dxc4 10. axb6 axb6 11. Bxc4 b5 12. Be2 b4 13. Na4 c5! $11 {1/2 (41) Szoen,D (2452)-Orzech,D (2394) Rewal 2007}) 9... c5 10. Ba3 $36 cxd4?! 11. Nxd4 Bb7 12. Bf3 Re8 13. cxd5 Nxd5 14. Ndb5 e6 15. Nxd5 Bxd5 16. Nd6 $18 {1-0 (26) Koziak,V (2489)-Orzech,D (2472) Rewal 2008}) (8... Bb7 9. a5 e6 10. b4 dxc4 11. Bxc4 Nd5 12. Nxd5 exd5 13. Bd3 b5 14. Nd2 a6 15. Nb3 Nd7 16. Qf3 f5 17. Qg3 Rf7 18. Bd2 $14 {1/2 (39) Bukavshin,I (2517)-Bu,X (2670) Moscow 2012}) 9. cxd5 (9. Qb3!? e6 10. Bd2 Nbd7 (10... Na6 {[%csl Yb4] looks more logical}) 11. Rfd1 Qc7 12. Rac1 Qb8 13. Bd3 Bb7 14. cxd5 exd5 15. e4 dxe4 16. Nxe4 Nd5 17. Bc4 Re8 18. Nc3 N7f6 19. Ne5 $16 {1-0 (32) Arutinian,D (2561)-Petrosyan,T (2315) Jermuk 2012}) 9... cxd5 (9... Nxd5 {makes some sense here since the knight can take the comfortable b4-square; however, White can comfortably bypass it:} 10. e4 Nb4 11. Be3 ({or} 11. h3 Nd7 12. Bg5 Ba6 13. Rc1 Re8 14. Bxa6 Rxa6 15. Qe2 Ra8 16. Rfd1 Qb8 17. Qc4 Qb7 18. e5 Nf8 19. d5 $14 {1/2 (33) Roeder,M (2440)-Sapis,W (2440) Cappelle la Grande 1995}) 11... Bg4 12. h3 Bxf3 13. Bxf3 N8a6 14. d5 Nc5 15. Qe2 Qe8!? 16. Rfd1 Rc8 17. dxc6 Qxc6 18. e5 $14 {1/2 (32) Anand,V (2800)-Wang,Y (2732) Nanjing 2010 CBM 139 [Edouard,R]}) 10. b3 Ne4?! {[%mdl 32] Black doesn't just neglect development but also trades White's "excess piece", i.e. his c3-knight, which only blocks his activity on the c-file. Additionally, Black obtains a vulnerable e4-pawn. Not surprisingly, he gets into trouble.} (10... Bb7 11. Ba3 Nc6 12. Rc1 Rc8 {looks more appropriate.}) 11. Nxe4 dxe4 12. Nd2 Bb7 13. Ba3 f5 (13... Nc6 $142 {but still after} 14. Rc1 Rc8 15. Bb5 {[%cal Yd1e2] White is clearly on top.}) 14. Rc1 Kh8 15. Nc4 Nd7 16. d5! Rc8 17. d6 e6 $8 {[%csl Gd6]} 18. b4! {White must open up the queenside to reach Black's blockading d7-knight.} axb4 19. Bxb4 Bd5 20. a5! bxa5 21. Bxa5 Qe8 22. Qa4 Bc6 23. Qb4 Rb8 24. Nb6! Ne5? (24... Nxb6 $142 25. Bxb6 Qd7 {- although the queen is not the best blockader, White still had to work hard to make use of his passed pawn.}) 25. Qc5 Ba8? {This loses instantly but gymnastics like} (25... Rf7 26. Bc3 Rfb7! 27. Bxe5 Rxb6 {would have hardly rescued Black either.}) 26. Bc3! Nd7 (26... Qc6 27. Qxe5! $18) (26... Nf7 27. Bb5 Qd8 28. Nd7) (26... Bd5 27. Nxd5 exd5 28. Qxd5 $18) 27. Bxg7+ Kxg7 28. Nxd7 Qxd7 29. Qe5+ {[%cal Rc1c7]} 1-0

