{[%evp 0,144,80,23,109,61,115,115,128,56,130,65,123,72,114,100,131,72,95,83,90,57,95,56,56,29,29,15,15,3,24,-11,2,-11,-3,-5,-10,1,8,0,19,-17,45,33,39,25,42,9,111,-7,-7,-45,-66,-97,-97,-94,-8,-22,-22,12,12,0,35,-131,55,55,55,68,68,-79,65,-55,51,-50,-50,-50,57,3,3,3,11,-14,-14,-77,-120,-125,-141,-144,-144,-132,-139,-149,-132,-133,-144,-144,-122,-122,-124,-124,-110,-111,-106,-144,-145,-143,-106,-145,-106,-106,-106,-105,-66,-73,-88,-125,-118,-138,-112,-138,-148,-149,-133,-139,-136,-148,-152,-155,-149,-210,-242,-420,-692,-712,-722,-712,-722,-722,-956,-992,-992,-992,-992,-1002,-1002,-1002,-1002]} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 a6!? {The so-called Dragadorf, as the name indicates a hybrid between the Dragon and the Najdorf. A well-known Simon Williams speciality!} 8. Qd2 h5 {Black prevents Bh6 swapping of the "dragon" bishop. White has a number of options here, but Lenier chooses the setup most popular vs. the dragon, relocating his bishop to b3.} 9. Bc4 Nbd7 10. O-O-O b5 11. Bb3 Nc5 {The Dragadorf shows its flexibility. In a pure Dragon Black's knight would be on c6 typically, but here in a more Najdorf-like manner it gets to c5, eyeing the b3 bishop.} 12. Kb1 Bb7? {A logical move, but a tactical oversight.} 13. Rhe1?! (13. Nf5! {would have been very strong, as} gxf5 14. Bxc5 dxc5? 15. Qg5! {wins instantly.}) 13... Rc8 14. Bg5 O-O 15. Nd5 e6 16. Nxf6+ Bxf6 17. h4 Bxg5 18. hxg5 Kg7 {Now a fairly equal position has arisen, anyway this game is not noteworthy for the opening, but for the ensuing tactical endgame:} 19. Qe3 Qc7 20. Rd2 b4 21. Red1 Rfd8 22. Qf4 Qe7 23. Ne2 a5 24. e5 d5 25. g4 (25. Bc4! {would have been a nice positional move, relocating the bishop, exploiting that} dxc4 26. Qf6+! {means that the bishop on c4 cant be taken.}) 25... hxg4 26. Qf6+ Qxf6 27. gxf6+ {Here the fun starts:} Kh6!? {Instantly losing, but in a practical game perhaps the better choice.} ({Objectively best was} 27... Kf8! 28. Rh1 Ke8 {which is the only way Black escapes the mating net, meaning the position is roughly equal.}) 28. Rh1+? (28. Rd4!! gxf3 29. Rg4!! {This spins a beautiful mating net. The 2 principal lines are:} Kh5 (29... g5 30. Nf4! {When 31.h1 follows or the more complex:}) 30. Rg3!! g5 31. Nf4+!! Kh4 32. Rdg1!! gxf4 33. Rg7 {When mate in the h-line is unavoidable. Finding this as White is not impossible, but in a rapid game, not to mention it being against the world champion it is indeed excusable that Dominguez misses his chance for a brilliancy.}) 28... Kg5 29. f4+ Kf5 30. Rf1 g5 {Opening a back-door and thus preventing the threat of 31.g3+ mate.} 31. Nd4+ Kg6?! {Perhaps expecting the check coming from g3, Carlsen withdraws his king. However:} (31... Ke4! {would have been brilliant and even stronger. White has no meaningful way to chase the "centralised" black king, and this the white centre just crumbles.} 32. Re2+ (32. fxg5 Kxe5 {Followed by ...Ne4 is easy.}) 32... Kxd4 33. Rd1+ Nd3! 34. Rxd3+ Kc5 35. fxg5 Rg8 {with a winning ending.}) 32. f5+ exf5 33. Nxf5 g3 34. Rg2 Ne4 35. Nxg3 Re8 36. Nxe4 dxe4 37. e6 fxe6 (37... e3! 38. Re2 a4 {wins tactically as} 39. exf7 Red8 40. Be6 Ba6! {Now is possible.}) 38. f7 Rf8 39. Bxe6 Rc5 40. Rg3? (40. Kc1 {was better} e3 41. Rg3 Re5 42. Bc4! {being the difference keeping f7 protected.}) 40... Re5 41. Bg4 e3 42. Kc1 Rxf7 43. Rxf7 Kxf7 44. Kd1 Kf6 45. Ke1 Bd5 46. a3 bxa3 47. bxa3 Be4 48. Rxe3 Bxc2 49. Kd2 Rxe3 50. Kxe3 Ke5 51. Bf3 Bf5 52. Bc6 Kd6 53. Bf3 Kc5 54. Be2 Bg6 (54... g4?? 55. Bxg4 Bxg4 56. Kd3 {Obviously being a draw, as the white king enters the corner with a known draw.}) 55. Bf1 Bh5 56. Ke4 Bd1 57. Ke3 g4 58. Ba6 Bf3 59. Bf1 Bc6 {Setting up a zugzwang.} 60. Kf4 (60. Be2 g3 {Wins trivially, as the king is forced to f2.}) 60... Kd4? {Black's play was aimed at getting access to the d4 square, but it still have to be finessed a bit.} ({Correct was first} 60... Bb5! 61. Bg2 Bd7 {as after} 62. Ke3 Bc6! {Black wins, as 63 Bf1 g3 leads to the above variation, and the pawn ending after Bxc6 also is a trivial win.}) 61. Kxg4 Kc3 62. Kf5 Kb2 63. Ke5?? {Logical looking, but the wrong direction.} ({The saving variation is} 63. Be2! Kxa3 64. Kf4 Kb4 (64... Kb2 65. Bd1!) 65. Ke3 {and now} Kc3 ({or} 65... Ka3 66. Kd2) 66. Bd1 {is just in time, securing the draw.}) 63... Kxa3 64. Kd6 Be8 65. Kc5 a4 66. Bc4 Kb2 67. Kb4 a3 68. Bd5 Bg6 69. Be6 Bb1! 70. Bd5 Ba2! 71. Be4 Bf7 72. Bb1 Bb3 {And as White is in zugzwang his fortress breaks and Dominguez resigned.} 0-1

