{[%evp 0,77,27,40,38,-4,-4,-11,-11,6,44,11,71,54,40,30,11,11,0,2,34,33,29,-24,-2,0,13,-73,-58,-21,-38,-38,21,33,43,19,33,30,32,31,31,29,61,73,68,62,79,69,69,69,61,64,86,59,70,47,60,56,56,62,36,38,42,106,107,104,137,143,143,159,170,165,170,166,187,214,221,235,245,186]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 c5 5. e3!? (5. cxd5 {is the main move, Magnus e.g. having beaten Giri last year in an excellent attacking game. But recently So has been efficient after} cxd4!? {steering the game towards a drawish ending.}) {So Magnus changes the course of event by: steering towards another drawish ending!} 5... dxc4 6. Bxc4 a6 7. O-O b5 8. Be2 Bb7 9. dxc5 {This Karjakin, by transposition, played against Magnus in the 2016 World Championship match. It was introduced by Kramnik who showed that Black's task in the ending by no means was simple.} Bxc5 ({Magnus in 2016 played} 9... Nc6!? {and drew effortlessly, but since then improvements for White have been found. And ironically it was Kramnik who introduced how Black is supposed to proceed in a later game, thus effectively neutralising his own idea!}) 10. Qxd8+ Kxd8 11. Nd2 Ke7 12. Nb3 Nbd7! {This is Kramnik's idea. Black happily gives away the pair of bishops, hoping that his central control and more active king secures the balance. Here in an earlier online game vs. So, Magnus tried 13 a5 ab8 14 xb7 Rxb7 15 d2, but here tries another twist:} 13. Rd1!? {Not threatening xd7 as ...xd7 still covers the c5 bishop, but more of a useful waiting move before declaring intentions. Black obviously has many decent moves, and Wesley chooses the most obvious, not giving White a second chance to grab the pair of bishops.} (13. Na5 Rab8 14. Nxb7 Rxb7 15. Bd2) 13... Bb6 14. a4 b4 15. a5! {This is the point. White uses the vulnerable bishop to create instability in the black queenside pawn chain.} Ba7 16. Na4 Bd5 17. Nd4 Rhb8 18. Bd2! Nc5 19. Nxc5 Bxc5 20. Rac1! {Like at move 18, White is not afraid of the isolated pawn, even despite that Black has it beautifully bloackaded with full control of the d5 square.} Bxd4 21. exd4 {At sight, it might look as if Black is perfectly fine indeed. And put the b-pawn back to b5, and indeed he would be better. But this detail very much makes the difference. As it is now, the weakness of the black queenside pawns makes the difference. Black can't play ...c8 as b4 then would fall, and having to also defend the a6-pawn means lack has no reasonable way to activate his pieces. In short: Details matter!} Kd7 22. Bf4 Rb7 23. h4! {Quite an extraordinary move, and a good one! As Black lacks active options, White's task is to improve his own position and this very much does so. Apart from trivialities like avoiding bottom-rank mates, more relevantly it leaves Black with a structural dilemma. If ...h5 the he will have weakened his kingside structure, but allowing a white h5 means h6 becomes a potential and if ...h6 then the g7-pawn will be fixed as a weakness. So tries some sort of active counterplay, but that just makes things worse.} Bb3 (23... h5) (23... h6) 24. Rd3 Ba4 25. Be5 Bb5? (25... Nd5 {immediately was a better figthing chance.}) 26. Bf3! {Black did, techically. solve the problem of the a6-pawn, but the price was losing control of the long diagonal. If 26...xd3 27 xb7 White enters the 7th rank via c7 so Black's reply is forced.} Nd5 (26... Bxd3 27. Bxb7 {[%cal Gc1c7]}) 27. Bxd5! {An impressive decision. But as Larsen stated, opposite coloured bishop endings perhaps often are drawn. But when they are winning, they are VERY winning. Black simply has zero counterplay in the following.} exd5 28. Rg3 g6 29. Rc5 Bc6 30. Rf3 f5 31. h5 {At sight Black's position might look like a fortress, but White enters via the h-file, and there is absolutely no defence against that plan.} Rb5 32. Rc1 Re8 33. hxg6 hxg6 34. Rh3 {Threatening 35 h7+ winning a piece. So Black has to defend the c6 bishop.} Re6 35. Rh7+ Kd8 36. b3 g5 37. Bc7+!? Ke8 38. Bb6 {Now the rook on b5 is completely shut out of the game!} f4 39. Rg7 {And with no reasonable moves So resigned. At best he now loses his kingside pawns, but on top of that his rook is trapped at b5, and moving his bishop allows c7 so resignation is fully in order. An impressive and instructive strategical gem by Magnus.} 1-0

