t dangerous is the energetic} 8. e4 $142 ! (8. e3 d6 9. Be2 c5 10. O-O Nc6 11. g4?! (11. Nf2 $142) 11... fxg4 (11... Qh4! $17) 12. fxg4 Nf6 13. Nf2 h6 14. e4 e5 15. d5 Ne7 16. g5 hxg5 17. Bxg5 Qe8 18. Qd3 Qg6 19. Qg3 Bd7 20. Kh1 Rf7 21. Qh4 Raf8 22. Rae1 Qh7 23. Qxh7+ Nxh7 $15 {Karjakin,S (2772)-Carlsen,M (2881) Shamkir 2014}) (8. Nf2 {Black should keep the position closed as long as possible, e.g.,} d6 (8... b6 9. e4 fxe4? (9... Nc6!? $132) 10. fxe4 Qf6 11. Ra2 Qf7 12. Nh3! $16 {[%csl Rh5][%cal Yf1d3,Ya2f2] So,W (2788)-Naroditsky,D (2633) Saint Louis 2015 For details see the notes to this game in CBM 166.}) 9. e4 Nc6 10. Bd3 Qe8 (10... Na5!?) 11. O-O e5 12. Rb1 b6 13. c5 dxc5 14. dxc5 fxe4 15. Bxe4 Bf5! 16. Qd5+ Kh8 17. Qxc6 Qxc6 18. Bxc6 Bxb1 19. g4 Nf6 20. g5 Nh5 21. Bxa8 Rxa8 22. Rd1 Bg6 23. Rd7 Rc8 24. Re7 Kg8 25. Rxe5 Re8 26. Rxe8+ Bxe8 $44 {/=, Shishkov,P (2353)-Maldonado Pacheco,J (2364) email 2017}) 8... fxe4?! (8... d6 $142 9. Bd3 Nc6 (9... f4!?)) 9. Bg5 Qe8 (9... Nf6 10. Nf2 h6 (10... exf3 11. Qxf3 h6 12. Bh4 Qe8 13. Qe3 $36 {[%cal Yf1d3,Rb1h7] gives White excellent attacking chances.}) 11. Bxf6 (11. Bh4!?) 11... Qxf6 12. Nxe4 Qf4 13. Be2 Qe3 14. Qc2 b6 15. Nf2! Nc6 16. O-O Ba6 17. Rae1 Na5 18. Bd3 Qg5 19. Re5 Qe7 20. Qe2 Nc6 21. Rh5 Qf7 22. Rh3 Ne7 23. Ng4 Qe8 {Gupta,A (2603)-Aleksandrov,A (2592) New Delhi 2020} 24. Nxh6+! gxh6 25. Rg3+ Kf7 26. f4 $18) 10. fxe4 e5 (10... Qg6 11. e5 Qe4+ 12. Kd2! g6 13. Rg1 $18 {[%cal Rf1d3]}) 11. Be2 Nf6 12. O-O d6 (12... Nxe4 13. Qc2 $32 {}) 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. Bh5!? Qe7 15. Nf2 Nc6 16. Ng4 Bxg4 17. Qxg4+ Kh8 18. Rad1 Na5 19. Qh3 Nxc4 20. Bg6 Qg7 21. Bf5 Qe7 22. Rf3 $44 {, Antonov,V (2362)-Runting,T (2350) ICCF email 2017}) 5. a3 ({As after 4...0-0, here White also has a choice between the text-move and} 5. e4 {Central expansion is played more often here and also brings White better results statistically. That said, I'm not claiming it's all clear here, after} d5 6. cxd5 exd5 7. e5 Ng8 8. Be3 (8. f4 Nge7 9. a3 Ba5 10. Be3 {is maybe a better version for White.}) 8... Nge7 9. f4 O-O 10. Nf3 Na5!? 11. Bd3 (11. Qc1 c5!? 12. dxc5 Bg4 $44) 11... Nc4 12. Qe2 (12. Bc1!?) 12... Nxe3 13. Qxe3 c5 14. O-O Bf5 15. a3 cxd4 16. Nxd4 Bc5 17. Na4 Bxd4 18. Qxd4 Nc6 19. Qe3 d4 20. Qe2 Bxd3 21. Qxd3 Qa5 22. b3 Rad8 $132 {Timofeev,A (2640)-Kovalenko,I (2611) Vladivostok 2012/ Black got excellent counterplay.}) 5... Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 b6 {[%csl Rc4] Here contrary to 4...0-0 5.a3 Black hasn't castled yet and he can not only attack the c4 faster, but also strive for 0-0-0 and hope for an easier life for his king.} 7. e4 Ba6 {Carlsen got this position also with White, but via a Sämisch with 4.a3:} (7... Na5 8. Bd3 d6 9. Nh3 (9. f4 Bb7 $132 {[%csl Re4]}) ({White has also} 9. Bg5 {, which can lead to positions from the following line or notes.}) 9... Ba6 10. Qe2 e5 11. Be3 Qd7 12. a4 Qc6 13. d5 Qd7 14. O-O Qe7 15. Nf2 Nd7 16. Rab1 O-O 17. g4 Rac8 18. Nh1 c6 19. Ng3 g6 $15 {Carlsen,M (2882)-Aronian,L (2765) Saint Louis blitz 2019/ Black has a solid position and can already fight for an advantage.}) (7... d6 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bh4 Na5 10. Bd3 g5 (10... Ba6 11. Qe2 Qd7 {is slightly more flexible, we'll check it in the next note.}) {is quite committal, but after} 11. Bf2 Ba6 12. h4 Rg8 13. hxg5 hxg5 14. Ne2 Qd7 15. e5 dxe5 16. dxe5 O-O-O! 17. Nd4 Ne8 18. c5 Bxd3 (18... Bb7!?) 19. Qxd3 Qd5 20. Qa6+ Qb7 {Black was fine and collapsed only after a series of errors} 21. Qe2 Qd5? (21... g4 $132) (21... bxc5! $15 {/}) 22. O-O g4 23. fxg4 Kb8 24. Rfd1 Qb7? 25. Nxe6 $18 {Carlsen,M (2876)-Pichot,A (2590) chess24.com blitz INT 2019}) 8. Bg5 (8. Bd3 Na5 9. Qe2 {is similar, after} d6 10. Bg5 h6 11. Bh4 Qd7 (11... g5 12. Bf2 Qd7 13. e5 Nh5 14. Nh3 dxe5 15. O-O!? O-O-O 16. c5 Bxd3 17. Qxd3 Kb8 18. Rab1 exd4 19. Qa6 Nc6 20. cxd4 Qc8 21. Qa4 Ne7 22. d5 Nxd5 23. Rfc1 Qb7 24. Qc4 Ka8 25. a4 Qc6 26. Qa6 Qb7 27. Qc4 Qc6 28. Qf1 Qb7 29. a5 $40 {Donchenko,A (2646)-Vitiugov,N (2722) Playchess.com INT 2020/ After some mutual inaccuracies White managed to build up a strong attack}) 12. a4 (12. f4 Qa4 13. e5 dxe5 14. fxe5 Nd7 15. Nf3 Bxc4 16. Nd2 Bxd3 17. Qxd3 {Polgar,Z (2400)-Browne,W (2510) New York 1986} Nxe5 $15 ({or} 17... Nf8 {[%cal Yf8g6] g6 and White must still prove his compensation.})) 12... Qc6 (12... g5!?) 13. d5 Qb7 14. Bxf6 gxf6 15. Rb1 Qc8 16. Nh3 Nb7 17. Nf4 h5 18. g4? (18. g3 $13) 18... hxg4 19. fxg4 Nc5 20. Nh5 Ke7 21. O-O Rh6 22. Qe3 Qh8 23. Rf2 Rg8 $40 {/, El Gindy,E (2431)-Aronian,L (2758) Khanty-Mansiysk 2019/ it was Black, who got to attack the enemy king.}) 8... h6 9. Bh4 Qc8 ({Magnus sidesteps the pin, but Black more often plays} 9... Na5 10. e5 ({Black doesn't have to worry about the endgame after} 10. Qa4 Qc8 11. Nh3 Qb7 12. Nf2 Qc6 13. Qxc6 dxc6 14. Bxf6 gxf6 15. Ng4 f5 16. exf5 exf5 17. Ne5 f6 18. Ng6 Rg8 19. Nh4 Rg5 20. Bd3 f4 $132 {Hoehne,E (2085)-Schludecker,E (1893) email 2010}) 10... g5 11. Bf2 Nh5 12. h4 (12. c5 Bxf1 13. Kxf1 d6 14. exd6 cxd6 15. h4 gxh4 16. Rxh4 Nf6 17. c4 dxc5 18. dxc5 Qxd1+ 19. Rxd1 Nd7 20. cxb6 axb6 21. Ne2 Rc8 $132 {[%csl Rc4] Carlsen,M (2861)-Ding,L (2805) Cupar rpd 2019/ Pressure against c4 balances the chances.}) 12... f5 {The critical position arises after} 13. hxg5 Qxg5 14. Nh3 Qg6 15. g3 {when} Qf7?! {was not ideal:} ({Possible improvements are} 15... Bxc4 {or}) (15... O-O-O!?) 16. g4 Ng7 17. Bh4 Nxc4 18. Qe2 d6 19. O-O-O Kd7 20. Bf6 Kc6 21. Bxg7 Qxg7 22. gxf5 exf5 23. Rg1 Qe7 24. d5+ Kb7 25. e6 $16 {Florian,J (2196)-Lehnhoff,H (2085) email 2016}) 10. Bd3 (10. Bxf6?! gxf6 {is a premature swap:} 11. Bd3 Na5 12. c5? (12. Qe2 $13 {/ was necessary}) 12... bxc5 13. dxc5 Bxd3 14. Qxd3 Nb3 15. Rb1 Nxc5 16. Qd4 d6 17. Qxf6 Rg8 18. g3 Qa6 19. Ne2 Qd3 20. Rd1 Qe3 21. Qd4 Qxf3 22. Rf1 Qxe4 $19 {Aguilera Lopez,A-Tolosana Esteban,E (1799) Madrid 2012}) 10... Na5 11. Qe2 d6? {A novelty, but definitely not a good one.} (11... g5 12. Bf2 Qb7 13. Rb1 O-O-O (13... d6 $142 !? $14 {/}) 14. c5 Bxd3 15. Qxd3 d6 16. cxd6 cxd6 17. Ne2 d5 18. exd5 exd5 19. Qf5+ Nd7 20. O-O Nc4 {Mazzin Juliano,J (1825)-Mafra,K (2087) Balneario Camboriu 2002} 21. Rfe1 $16 {[%csl Rc8,Rd5,Rf7] White is better, as the opponent has weaknesses on both flanks.}) ({Stronger was} 11... Qb7 $142 ! {[%cal Ye8c8]} 12. e5 ({Black not only prepares 0-0-0, but also has the sly tactical idea} 12. f4? Bxc4! {} 13. Bxc4 Qc6 $17) 12... Nh5 13. Nh3 g5 $13 {}) 12. f4! Qd7 {Taking into account move 9 this is a loss of time, but after} (12... e5 13. fxe5 dxe5 14. Nf3 $16 {Black can't retain the blockade.}) 13. Nf3? {"First activate the pieces, then advance the pawns" is a sensible rule, but like any other piece of chess wisdom, it has its exceptions and this is one of them.} ({The right way to go here was to play} 13. e5! {[%csl Gc4,Gd4,Ge5,Gf4] first. The g1 will enter the fray on the very next move, White has a big space advantage and excellent attacking chances:} Nh7 (13... g5 14. fxg5 hxg5 15. Bxg5 Nh5 16. Nh3 $16) 14. Nh3!? (14. Nf3 d5 15. cxd5 Bxd3 16. Qxd3 Qxd5 17. O-O $14) 14... d5 (14... Qc6 15. d5 exd5 16. O-O $32) 15. cxd5 Bxd3 16. Qxd3 Qxd5 17. O-O O-O 18. f5 $16 {[%cal Yh3f4] }) 13... Nh5 14. g3 (14. Bg3 {was objectively better, but it would mean a failure of White's plans and ambitions -} O-O-O $15 {is excellent for Black.}) 14... g5! {[%csl Rc4,Rd4,Re4,Rf4][%mdl 528] The pawn sacrifice is a consistent follow-up of the previous move and aims to exploit the vulnerabililty of White's overextended central pawns.} 15. fxg5 (15. Nd2 Nxf4 16. gxf4 gxh4 $17 {[%csl Re1] doesn't help, the problem is e1 has no safe place.}) 15... hxg5 16. Nxg5 (16. Bxg5? Nxg3 $19) 16... Nf4! 17. gxf4 Rxh4 $36 {The short tactical operation is over, White's position is bursting at the seams.} 18. Qf2 {Wins a tempo, but weakens the c4.} (18. Rg1 $142 ! {is much more resilient, after} O-O-O (18... Rxf4? 19. Nh3) 19. Nf3 Rxf4 20. Qe3 e5 21. dxe5 Rg4 22. Rd1 $15 {White can still fight on.}) 18... Rh8 (18... Rh6!? $17) 19. f5?! (19. Qe2 O-O-O $17 {is clearly better for Black, but opening the position helps Magnus even more.}) 19... O-O-O 20. fxe6 fxe6 21. Qf7 Rdg8 22. Qxd7+ Kxd7 23. Nf3 $8 (23. h4? Rxg5 $19) 23... Rh3!? ({Increases the pressure, but the prosaic} 23... Bxc4 $17 {/-+ was roughly equivalent.}) 24. Kf2 Rf8 25. Be2 Bxc4 26. Raf1 Rf4 ({Doesn't want to give White even a whiff of a chance after} 26... Rfxf3+ 27. Bxf3 Bxf1 28. Bg4!? Rh4 29. Kg3 $15) 27. Ke3?! {The king remains in the danger zone at his own peril.} (27. Kg2 {offers more prolonged resistance, but the  after} Bxe2 28. Kxh3 Rxf3+ 29. Rxf3 Bxf3 30. Re1 Nc4 31. Kg3 Bh5 $19 {is technically lost.}) 27... e5! 28. dxe5 dxe5 29. Rhg1 Bxe2 30. Kxe2 Rxe4+ $19 {Only now Black wins material, but the game is decided - White's reamining pawns are fatally weak and his forces lack coordination. The rest doesn't require much effort, or commentary.} 31. Kf2 Nc4 32. Rg3 Rh6! $40 {Avoiding exchanges and continuing toplay for the attack.} 33. a4 a5 34. Rfg1 (34. Kg1 Nb2 $19 {[%csl Ra4,Ga5][%cal Ga5a1]}) 34... Rf4 35. Kg2 Ne3+ 36. Kh1 Nf5! 37. Nxe5+ Ke6 38. Rg4 Rf2 {[%csl Re5,Rh2] The mating threat will cost White a piece.} 0-1

