{[%evp 0,36,25,-23,-11,-19,-19,-15,-15,-14,-13,-24,-14,-28,-38,-70,-44,-47,-47,-47,0,-33,-6,-54,-8,-6,-17,-34,-11,-34,-12,35,60,49,69,66,72,84,81]} {Playing Magnus is always a remarkable event for me, and I'm very happy I got invited to one of those Magnus' tournaments. This very win made a huge impact on chess popularization in Poland, as sport media were eager for new sensations, because most of the sports were frozen at the time due to pandemic. I gave an enormous number of interviews in almost all polish mainstream media, being lucky that there was no football coverage yet. Sometimes my win was used even a political context, as one of the heavy presidential candindates (current president) is also named Duda. At the moment Poland is divided into halves in this topic, so I won't go any further, but for sure the win did a great job for our beautiful discipline in Poland :)} 1. c4 {Mueller,Karsten} e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. e3 Bb4 5. Qc2 O-O!? {An invitation for a sharp play,} ({rather than the dull and ultra-solid} 5... Bxc3 6. Qxc3 Qe7) 6. Nd5 Re8 7. Qf5 {A nice idea thought up back in late 70's by Raymond Keene. White sets out to damage Black's kingside while Black gets an advantage in development, which often leads to sharp play.} d6 8. Nxf6+ gxf6 (8... Qxf6 9. Qxf6 gxf6 10. a3 Ba5 11. b4 Bb6 12. Bb2 $14 {is slightly better for White. Playing Black beware the following trick:} a5 13. b5 Nd8 14. d4 Bg4? (14... a4) 15. c5! dxc5 (15... Ba7 16. b6! {is an essence}) 16. dxe5 $16 {and White is close to winning already 1-0 (44) Karpov,A (2696)-Bacrot,E (2594) Cannes 2000}) 9. Qh5 (9. Qc2 {is an option.}) 9... d5 (9... e4 {is another alternative, which requires some knowledge.}) 10. a3 (10. Bd3 {is perhaps a more challenging move order for Black.} e4 (10... h6 11. cxd5 Qxd5 12. Bc2 $36) 11. cxd5 Bf8! {Clearly the strongest} (11... exd3 12. dxc6 bxc6 13. Qh4! {with b3 coming next, makes the difference.}) 12. a3 exd3 13. dxc6 bxc6 {and the same position as in the game occurs.}) 10... Bf8 11. Bd3 e4 (11... h6!? {is an additional possibility in this move order which equalizes easily, e.g.} 12. cxd5 Qxd5 13. Bb1 Be6 14. d3 f5 15. O-O e4 16. Nd4 Nxd4 17. exd4 $11 {.}) 12. cxd5 exd3 (12... Nd4!? 13. Nxd4 Re5 14. Qh4 exd3 15. Nf3 Rxd5 16. b4 Bg7 17. Bb2 Rg5!? {is a funny trick} 18. O-O Rg4 19. Qh5 Rg6 20. Qc5 Bh3 21. Ne1 {White should be better here.}) 13. dxc6 bxc6 14. b4 a5 (14... Qd5!? {is a far better version of the endgame Black later got in the game.} 15. Qxd5 $8 cxd5 16. Bb2 a5 17. O-O (17. bxa5 Rxa5 18. Bxf6 Bg7!? 19. Bxg7 Kxg7 20. O-O Bg4 $11) 17... axb4 18. axb4 Rxa1 19. Rxa1 Bxb4 20. Bxf6 c5 $11 {should be equal.}) (14... Re4 15. Bb2 Bg4 16. Qh4 {is promising for White.}) 15. Bb2 Re4?! (15... axb4! {was the simplest and the best, although perhaps Magnus wanted to get me out of the book.} 16. axb4 Rxa1+ 17. Bxa1 Re4 18. O-O (18. Nd4 {is not great in this version:} Rg4! 19. Nxc6?? Qd6 20. b5 Rg5 21. Qf3 Rxb5 22. O-O Bb7 $19) 18... Rxb4 $11 {where White has enough compensation, but Black's play shouldn't be troublesome at all.}) 16. Nd4! {My first own move, and in hindsight the best} Qd5?! {Magnus was clearly not comfortable here, as he spent what felt like ages to make this sad move, leaving himself only with 2,5 minutes left.} (16... Rg4 17. Nxc6 (17. g3?) 17... Qd7 18. b5 Rxg2 19. Qf3 Rg6 20. h4 $36 {is also extremely unpleasant for Black to play.}) (16... Bg4 17. Qh4) (16... axb4 17. Nxc6 Qd6 18. Nxb4 {is clearly better for White}) 17. Qxd5 cxd5 18. f3 $16 {Now White has a dream endgame, which is close to winning already.} Re8 (18... Re7 19. b5 a4 20. Kf2 {Here Black is not on time to attack the b5-pawn by means of Bd7-Bd6-Reb8 etc., so White is much better.}) 19. Nb5! (19. b5? a4! $14 {White has always to watch out for this little annoying move, which creates two targets on White's queenside at once.}) 19... Re7 $8 20. Bxf6 Rd7 21. Kf2 Ba6 (21... c6 22. Nc3 Ra6 23. bxa5 c5 24. Be5 f6 25. Bf4 Rxa5 26. Rhb1 $16) 22. Nd4 Bc4 23. Rhb1? {A fruit of a pathetic omitting, as I somehow missed Black's 25th move.} ({The correct} 23. Nf5! $18 {would be a simple & wise choice, aiming to put the bishop on c3, so that the entire queenside would be under control.}) 23... Ra6 24. Be5?! (24. Bh4 {is objectively better, but obviously it's an achievement for Black to expel the dark-squared bishop from the a1-h8 diagonal.}) 24... f6 25. b5 Rb6 ({I was only calculating} 25... fxe5 26. bxa6 exd4 27. a7 dxe3+ 28. dxe3 Rd8 29. Rb8 $18) ({or} 25... Bxb5 26. Bxc7 {where White is on his way to convert the advantage. Now, however, most part of White's advantage is gone}) 26. Bf4 a4 $16 {Not only I managed to lose coordination between my pieces, I also got fixed with two pernament weaknesses on a3 and b5. By this stage of the game I thought a draw is a likely outcome, as I couldn't see any way to make meaningful progress.} 27. g4 Bc5 28. h4 Kf7 29. Kg3 (29. g5) 29... Bxd4 {During the game I was wondering how I would have made progress if Black had adopted a waiting strategy? Apparently with the help of the engines it seems White can activate his rooks, generating a bunch of threats in each case, no matter how Black reacts} (29... Rb7 30. g5 Rd8 (30... Rb6 31. Kg4 Ke8 32. Rc1) 31. Kg4!? Re8 (31... Rg8 32. Kf5) 32. Nf5 Reb8 33. Rc1 Rc8 34. e4 $16) (29... Kg6 30. g5 Rg7 31. Kf2 Rd7 32. h5+ Kf7 33. Rg1 fxg5 34. Rxg5 $18 {However I think it was worth going this path, as many of White's move look bizzare to me, and I seriously doubt I would keep the position under control.}) 30. exd4 Bxb5 31. Rb4 {Mueller,Karsten: 'Bishops of opposite colours favour the attacking side Pure opposite coloured bishop endings have a high tendency to be drawn. But when other pieces, e.g., rooks are added to the mix, this favours (as it does in the middlegame) the attacking side and the endings are in no way drawish:' Mueller,Karsten: 'Ungleichfarbige Läufer begünstigen den Angreifer Reine ungleichfarbige Läuferendspiele haben eine hohe Remistendenz. Wenn aber noch weitere Figuren wie zum Beispiel Türme hinzukommen, begünstigen sie wie im Mittelspiel den Angreifer und haben mit Remis nicht viel zu tun:'} Re7? {Actually the losing mistake. Mueller,Karsten: 'After this the floodgates open.' Mueller,Karsten: 'Danach brechen die Dämme.'} (31... c6 {Mueller,Karsten: '!' Mueller,Karsten: 'was the final option to offer chances of a draw.' Mueller,Karsten: 'war die letzte Option mit praktischen Remischancen.'} 32. Re1 Rbb7 33. Rbb1 Re7 34. Rxe7+ Rxe7 35. g5 Re2 36. Kg4 $16 {is better for White, but it's difficult to assess how big his chances are.}) 32. Rab1 {Mueller,Karsten: '+-'} Bc4 (32... c6 33. Rxa4 {is what Magnus missed.}) 33. Rxb6 cxb6 34. Rxb6 $18 {The opposite-coloured bishops only make up for a drawing chances. In fact it's hard to imagine a more useless minor piece than Black's light-squared bishop here. Sensing that I was winning, I got tense, which affected on my play a bit, even though I didn't spoil my position. It's not everyday that you get a chance to beat a 3-times World Champion, even if only in an online game.} Bb3 35. Bd6 Rd7 36. f4 {With the lovely idea of f5-Be5 in mind!} f5 37. g5 (37. Be5! {was basically an instant clincher, but I refused the idea of breaking my kingside phalanx on subconscious level}) 37... Bd1 38. Bc5 (38. Be5!? {Mueller,Karsten} Bg4 39. Rh6 Kg8 40. h5 Rb7 41. g6 Rd7 42. Kh4 $18) 38... Kg7 39. Kf2 Rd8 40. Rb7+ Kg6 41. Re7 Rb8 42. Re6+ Kh5?! (42... Kg7 43. Bd6 {is terribly bad, but at least is not an immediate resigns.} Rb2 {Mueller,Karsten} 44. Be5+ Kf7 45. Rf6+ Kg8 46. Rd6 Ra2 47. Ke3 Rxa3 48. Rd8+ Kf7 49. Rd7+ Kg8 50. Rg7+ Kf8 51. Rxh7 $18) 43. Kg3 {with the unstoppable Rh6# to follow. What a beautiful moment it was! Mueller,Karsten: 'Duda's king closes the mating net.' Mueller,Karsten: 'Dudas König schließt das Mattnetz.'} 1-0

