1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. g3 Bb4+ 5. Bd2 Be7 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O c6 8. Qc2 Nbd7 9. a4!? {A highly original and virtually unexplored plan, typical for Carlsen. The structure resulting after Black's natural anwer is supposed to be completely inoffensive, but Magnus had his own views about it.} a5 {.} 10. Rc1 {Another mysterious move. White increases his pressure on a file which is not likely to open soon, as with the actual structure cxd5 can always be answered with ...exd5. Carlsen's strategic vision goes as usual much deeper than the obvious. He reckons with the fact that in order to complete his development Black will have to play ...b7-b6 and then cxd5 would force ...cxd5, opening the c-file, which he occupies in advance. His queen's knight will then be transferred to b5, a square which has become available after the unusual insertion of a2-a4 and ...a7-a5. The only question remaining open is whether Black has to rush to completie his development at all, as White does not have a clear plan in the actual structure. Other moves have been tried in practice, offering White a slight edge.} (10. Na3 Ne4 11. Be3 f5 12. Ne1 g5 13. f3 Nd6 14. Nd3 $14 {Hertneck,G (2572)-Bareev,E (2719) Germany 2002 (½-½, 61)}) (10. Rd1 {is not without a point either:} Ne4 11. Be1 f5 12. Nfd2 Ndf6 13. Nxe4 Nxe4 14. Nd2 Bd6 15. Nxe4 {½-½ Ljubojevic,L (2580)-Jussupow,A (2610) Linares 1989} fxe4 16. f3 exf3 17. exf3 $14 {[%cal Gf3f4] The usefulness of the d1-rook becomes obvious after} e5? 18. cxd5 cxd5 19. dxe5 Bxe5 20. f4 $18 {[%csl Gd5]}) 10... Ne4 $146 {An active but also committal move. After allowing the knight contact Black allows his opponent to open the centre which can be dangerous due to his undeveloped queenside.} ({In a previous game Black kept a neutral policy inviting White to show his cards.} 10... h6 11. Be1 b6 (11... Re8 12. Nbd2 {[%cal Ge2e4]}) 12. cxd5 cxd5 13. Nc3 Bb7 14. Nb5 {White has an optical advantage but Black will gradually manage to equalise due to the almost symmetrical structure:} Rc8 15. Qd1 Rxc1 16. Rxc1 Qb8 17. Bd2 Rc8 18. Bf4 Rxc1 19. Qxc1 Qc8 20. Qxc8+ Bxc8 21. Ne5 Nxe5 22. Bxe5 Ne8 23. e3 Bd7 {Sandipan,C (2608)-Meier,G (2668) Dresden 2012 (½-½, 23)}) 11. Be1 {In the similar games above White preferred transferring the bishop to e3, which looks more active. Carlsen has his own views: he intends to use his bishops on the e1-a5 and f1-a6 diagonals, putting pressure on the enemy queenside, a plan which will work miracles in the game.} f5 12. Nbd2 Bd6 13. e3 Ra7 {Preparing ...b7-b6.} (13... b6? 14. cxd5 cxd5 15. Qc6 Ba6 16. Nxe4 fxe4 17. Qxd6 exf3 18. Bh3 $18) ({The main alternative was ignoring the queenside development and launching the typical kingside attack with} 13... g5 14. Nb3 {[%csl Ga5]} ({Black may have feared the opening of the centre with} 14. Nxe4 fxe4 15. Nd2 {[%cal Gf2f3] followed by f2-f3, but} g4 16. Nb3 {[%csl Ga5]} Bc7 {[%cal Gd8f6] keeps the position more or less blocked.}) 14... g4 (14... b6? 15. cxd5 cxd5 16. Nxg5!! Qxg5 17. Bxe4 fxe4 18. Qc6) (14... f4 15. exf4 gxf4 16. Qe2 {[%cal Gb3d2]}) (14... Bc7 15. Nfd2 Qf6 16. f4 Kh8) 15. Nfd2 Ng5 {and Black has nothing to complain about.}) 14. Qd1 b6 15. cxd5 cxd5 16. Nb1! {[%csl Gb5] After having provoked the weakening of the e5-square, the knight switches to the newly created path.} Ba6 ({Black has no time to carry out a similar regrouping:} 16... Nb8 17. Na3 Na6? 18. Nb5 Ra8 19. Nxd6 Qxd6 20. Ne5 $16) 17. Na3 Qa8 {Black anticipates the exchange on b5 by defending the c6-square against the invasion of the white rooks.} ({Even though} 17... Bxa3 18. Rxa3 Rc7 {0-1 Gheorghiu,F (2530)-Jussupow,A (2590) WchT 1st Luzern 1985 (1) looks very committal, the following classical example (true, White played very passively) shows that White's dark-squared bishop can easily turn into a bad one.}) ({The problem with the game move is that after the general exchange of rooks the queen could be left hanging.} 17... Qb8 {would solve this problem but White could use the c6-square for gaining time:} 18. Nb5 Bxb5 19. axb5 Rc8 20. Qa4! (20. Bf1 {is neutralised by} Rac7) 20... Rac7 21. Rc6 Qb7 22. Bf1 Nb8 ({A necessary passive move since} 22... Rxc6 23. bxc6 Rxc6 {loses material to} 24. Ba6! Qc7 25. Bb5 $18 {[%csl Rd7]}) 23. Rxc7 Qxc7 24. b4 axb4 25. Bxb4 Nd7 26. Ne1 $14 {[%cal Ge1d3,Rd3b4,Rd3f4,Yf2f3] With hindsight, this might have been a lesser evil than in the game, though.}) 18. Nb5 Bxb5 19. axb5 Rc8 20. Bf1 {Completing a regrouping probably planned by Carlsen when he retreated with his other bishop to e1. The bishops are only apparently biting on granit as will soon be revealed.} (20. Rxc8+ Qxc8 21. b4 {is premature due to} a4 22. Rxa4 (22. Ra3 Qa8 23. Qa1 g5 $15) 22... Rxa4 23. Qxa4 Qc1 $132 {The extra pawn does not count yet and Black's activity is annoying.}) 20... Rxc1 ({With the queen on a8} 20... Rac7 {does not work due to} 21. Rxc7 Rxc7 22. b4) 21. Rxc1 Rc7 22. Rxc7 Bxc7 {Black has exchanged all the rooks under less favourable circumstances than he would have wished to. The bishop's absence from the a3-f8 diagonal allows White to play b2-b4 without exchanging bishops at once. Black will have to lose additional time to achieve that.} 23. Qa4 Qb8 (23... g5 24. b4 g4 25. Nd2 Nxd2 26. Bxd2 Qd8 27. bxa5 bxa5 28. Bd3 $14) 24. b4 axb4 25. Bxb4 {The bishop pair together with the weaknesses on e6, c6 and b6, ensure White a long term advantage.} h6 {Black eliminates the potential threat g5 (as he might wish to play ...d6 followed by ...xd6) and ensures himself against back rank problems.} (25... g5?! {weakens the position too much.} 26. Qc2 Bd6 27. Qc6 {[%csl Ge6,Gg5] The hanging g5-pawn creates irreparable problems for Black.} Ndf6 28. Bxd6 Qxd6 29. Nxg5!! $18 Qxc6 30. bxc6 {[%cal Gg5e6,Gf1a6,Gc6c8]} Nxg5 31. c7 $18) (25... Bd6 26. Bxd6 Qxd6 (26... Nxd6 27. Qc2 $16 {[%cal Gc2c6]}) 27. Qa8+ Kf7 28. Qc6 $16) 26. Qc2 Bd6 27. Qc6?! {This apparently deadly blow allows Black some surprising counterplay which Eljanov will fail to find.} (27. Bxd6! Qxd6 28. Qc6 Kf7 (28... Qxc6 29. bxc6 Ndf6 30. c7 Nd6 31. Ba6 $18) 29. Qb7 $16) 27... Ndf6 ({Missing the far from obvious} 27... Qa7! {[%csl Gf2]} 28. Bxd6 (28. Be1 Kf7 29. Nd2 $14 {may be a better try, but this obviously is playable for Black while in the game he was just lost.}) 28... Qa2! $11 29. Bg2 Qa1+ 30. Bf1 Qb2 31. Kh1 Nxf2+ (31... Qxf2? 32. Bg2 $18 {[%csl Ge1,Gf1,Gg1]}) 32. Kg2 Ne4+ 33. Kh3?! Ng5+ 34. Nxg5 hxg5 35. g4 (35. Qxd7 g4+ 36. Kh4 Qxh2+ 37. Kg5 Qh6#) 35... Qf2 $36) 28. Bxd6 Qxd6 (28... Nxd6 29. Ne5 $16) 29. Qc8+ Kh7 30. Ne5 Qe7 ({Black needs to controll f7 as after} 30... Nd2 31. Bd3 Nde4 {White gets a decisive attack with} 32. Nf7 Qe7 33. Qh8+ Kg6 34. Ne5+ Kh5 35. Be2+ $18) 31. Qc6 Ng4?! {Speeding up the defeat.} ({If} 31... Qd6 {White can create an original zugzwang with} 32. Kg2!! Qxc6 (32... Kg8 33. Qc8+ Kh7 34. Nf7 $18 {[%csl Rd6][%cal Gc8h8,Gf7e5]}) (32... Ng8 33. Nd7 $18 {[%csl Gb6]}) 33. bxc6 Ne8 34. Nd7 $18 {[%csl Gb6]}) 32. Nxg4 fxg4 33. Bd3 g6 34. Bxe4 dxe4 35. Qxb6 1-0

