1. c4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 e6 5. d4 d5 6. cxd5 exd5 7. Bb5 Bd6 8. O-O O-O 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. b3 Bg4 11. Bb2 a6 {Black has several options to achieve a playable position.} (11... Bd6 12. h3 Bh5 13. Be2 a6 14. Nh4! {A typical manoeuvre when you are fighting against the isolated pawn} Bxe2 15. Nxe2 Re8 16. Rc1 Ne4 17. Nf5 Be5 18. Bxe5 Rxe5 19. Nfd4 Qd7 20. Nf4 Rae8 21. Nxc6 bxc6 22. Qc2 c5 23. Rfd1 $14 {Kramnik,V-Polgar,J Presidents Cup Baku AZE 2010 1/2-1/2 (67)}) (11... Rc8 12. Rc1 a6 (12... Bd6 13. h3 Bh5 14. Be2 a6 15. Nh4 Bxe2 16. Nxe2 Be5 (16... Re8!? 17. Nf3 Ne4) 17. Bxe5 Nxe5 18. Qd4 Re8 19. Nf5 Rxc1 20. Rxc1 Qd7 21. Nfg3 $14 {Grachev,B-Chadaev,N TCh- RUS Loo 2013 1-0 (54)}) 13. Be2 Ba7 {leads to the game Topalov,V-Karjakin,S.} 14. h3 Bh5 15. Nh4 Bg6 16. Nxg6 hxg6 17. Bf3 d4 18. exd4 Bxd4 19. Na4 Bxb2 20. Nxb2 Nd4 21. Rxc8 Nxf3+ 22. Qxf3 Qxc8 $11 {Topalov,V-Karjakin,S Stavanger NOR Blitz 2013 0-1 (64)}) 12. Bxc6 (12. Be2 Ba7 13. Rc1 Qd7 (13... Rc8 {leads again to Topalov,V-Karjakin,S.}) 14. Na4 Ne4 15. Ne5? {It's hard to believe, but after this logical move White's position became very difficult} (15. Nd4 Bxe2 16. Nxe2 Rfe8 $11) 15... Nxe5 16. Bxe5 Bxe2 17. Qxe2 Rfe8 18. Qb2?! (18. Bb2 b5 19. Nc3 Nxf2 20. Rxf2 Bxe3 21. Qf1 Re6 22. Nd1 Bxc1 23. Bxc1 Rae8 $17) 18... b5 19. Bxg7 bxa4 20. Bh8 f6 (20... Qg4! 21. Rc7 a3 22. Qa1 Nxf2 23. Rxf2 Bxe3 $19) 21. Bxf6 a3 22. Qa1 Nxf6 23. Qxf6 d4 $17 {Lauber,A-Landa,K Bundesliga Hamburg 2012 0-1 (42)}) 12... bxc6 13. Rc1 Ba7 (13... Bd6 14. Ne2 Rc8 (14... c5!? 15. Bxf6 Qxf6 16. Qxd5 Rad8 $44) 15. Ng3 (15. Ne5!? Bxe2 16. Qxe2 Qe8 17. Nf3 $14) 15... Re8 16. Qd3 Ne4 17. Nd2?! Nxg3 18. hxg3 h5 19. Nf3 Bxf3?! 20. gxf3 Qg5 21. f4 Qg4 22. Kg2 h4 23. Rh1! hxg3 24. Rh3!! $16 {1-0 Peralta,F-Swinkels,R/Wijk aan Zee NED 2013 (31)}) 14. Ne2 {A simple but strong move. In one game White tried his knight on c5. Nothing good came of it.} (14. Na4?! Qd6 15. Rc4?! Qe6 16. Nc5 Qc8 17. Rc1 Ne4 18. b4 Bxc5 19. bxc5 f6 20. Qd4 Bxf3 21. gxf3 Ng5 $15 {0-1 Nielsen,U-Sax,G/Vejle 1994/CBM 040 (46)}) 14... Qd6 (14... Bxf3 15. gxf3) 15. Be5 Qe7 16. Ned4 (16. Rxc6?? Bxf3 17. Bxf6 Qd7 $19) 16... Bxf3?! {T. Radjabov decided to play solidly. This desire is very understandable to anyone who has seen M. Carlsen's elo. However it seems that 16...c5 was stronger.} (16... c5 17. Nc6 Qe6 18. Bxf6 (18. Nxa7? Bxf3 19. Qxf3 Qxe5 20. Nc6 Qd6 21. Na5 Qb6 $17) 18... Qxc6 19. Be7 Rfc8 20. Bxc5 Bxc5 21. b4 Qb5 22. bxc5 Rxc5 23. Rxc5 Qxc5 $11 {The position is very close to equal.}) 17. Nxf3 Rfc8 18. Qd3 a5 (18... Ne4!? 19. Bb2 a5 20. Rc2 a4 21. Rfc1 axb3 22. axb3 Qe6 $14 {White is slightly better, but it seems to me that if Black plays precisely he is out of danger.}) 19. Bxf6! Qxf6 20. Rc2 Rd8 (20... a4?! 21. b4 Qd6 22. a3 g6 23. Rfc1 Rc7 24. Nd4 Bxd4 25. Qxd4 Rac8 26. Rc5 $16) 21. Rfc1 c5 (21... d4?! 22. e4! (22. exd4 Bxd4 23. Nxd4 Rxd4 24. Qc3 g6 25. Qxc6 Qxc6 26. Rxc6 a4 $14 {the endgame is rather drawish}) 22... c5 {Black's bishop, which is responsible for the c5-pawn, is not a very active piece. Gradually White will create an attack on the kingside.}) 22. e4?! (22. Qb5! h6 (22... Bb6 23. Ne5! Qxe5 24. Qxb6 $16) 23. Qxa5 Bb6 24. Qd2 Ba5 25. Qd3 Bb4 26. a4 $16 {Black doesn't have compensation for the pawn.}) 22... Qg6! 23. Re1 dxe4! {Black has an almost equal endgame.} (23... d4?! {See the comments to the 21st move.}) 24. Qxe4 Qxe4 25. Rxe4 Rd1+ 26. Re1 Rxe1+ 27. Nxe1 Rd8 28. Kf1 (28. a4?! Rd1 29. Kf1 Rb1 30. Rc3 g6 {Black's rook is very active.}) 28... a4! 29. bxa4! {The only chance to play for a win.} Rd4 30. a5 Ra4 31. Rd2 Kf8 32. Nd3 f6 (32... c4? 33. Nb2 Rb4 (33... Rxa2 34. Nxc4 $16) 34. a3) 33. Nb2 Rxa5! (33... Rxa2? 34. Nc4 Ra4 35. Rd8+ Ke7 36. Ra8 Rxc4 37. Rxa7+ Kf8 38. Rc7! $16) 34. Nc4 Ra4 $11 35. Rc2 Ke7 36. Ke2 Ke6 37. Kd3 Kd5 38. a3 h5 39. h3 h4 40. Rc1 g6 41. Rc2! g5 42. Rc1 $22 {Black's king and rook are placed perfectly. Unfortunately he is forced to remove one of them and White will play e1.} Ra6! (42... Bb8?? 43. Nb6+) 43. Re1 Bb8 44. Re7 Bf4 (44... Be5 45. a4 Bd4 46. Re2 Be5 47. a5 Bd4 $11 {I don't see how White can make any progress}) 45. Kc3 f5? {A very serious mistake.} (45... Be5+! 46. Kb3 (46. Kd3 Bd4 47. f3 Be5 $11) 46... Kd4 47. a4 (47. Rd7+ Ke4 48. a4 Bd4) 47... Kd3 48. a5 Bd4 49. f3 Ra8 50. Re6 (50. Rb7? Ke2) 50... Rb8+ 51. Rb6 Ra8 52. Re6 $11) 46. Kb3 g4 47. a4 gxh3 48. gxh3 Rg6 49. a5 $16 Rg1 50. a6 Rb1+ 51. Kc3?! (51. Ka2! {The endgame should be winning for White} Rb4 (51... Rb8 52. a7 Kxc4 53. axb8=Q Bxb8 54. Kb2 $18) 52. Ne3+ Bxe3 53. Rxe3 Rb8 54. a7 Ra8 55. Ra3 $18) 51... Rc1+ 52. Kd3?! (52. Kb3! Rb1+ (52... Rxc4 53. Rd7+ Bd6 (53... Kc6 54. Kxc4 Kxd7 55. a7) 54. Rxd6+ Kxd6 55. Kxc4 Kc6 56. a7 $18) 53. Ka2) 52... Rd1+ 53. Ke2? (53. Kc3! {He should have changed his mind and put his king on a2} Rc1+ 54. Kb3 Rb1+ 55. Ka2) 53... Ra1 54. Nb6+ Kd6 55. Rg7 Kc6 56. Rg6+ Kb5 57. Nd5 Be5? {After this weak move Black is in trouble again. He could make a draw after 57....a2.} (57... Ra2+! 58. Kf3 Bd2! 59. Rd6 Kc4 60. Ne7 Ra3+ 61. Kg2 (61. Ke2 Bf4 62. Re6 Rxh3 63. Nxf5 Kb5 $11) 61... Ra2 62. Re6 (62. Nxf5 Be1 $11) 62... f4 63. Kf3 Kb5 64. Nd5 c4 65. Ke4 Be1! 66. Rg6 c3 67. Rg1 Ra1 68. Nxc3+ Kxa6 69. Ne2 Ra4+ 70. Kf3 Ra3+ 71. Kxf4 Bxf2 $11) 58. Rb6+ Kc4 59. Ne3+ Kc3 60. f4! Bd4 61. Nxf5 c4 62. Rc6 Rh1? (62... Bg1! {The most stubborn continuation.} 63. Ne3 (63. Nxh4?! Kb4 64. Ng6 Bd4 $13) 63... Ra2+ 64. Kf3 Kd4 65. Nxc4 Kd5 66. Ne5 Bd4 67. Rf6 (67. Rh6 Bxe5 68. Rh5 (68. fxe5 Kxe5 69. Rh5+ Kf6 70. Rh8 Kg7 71. Ra8 $11) 68... Ra3+! (68... Rxa6? 69. Rxe5+ Kd6 70. Kg4 $18) 69. Kg4 Ke6 70. Rxe5+ Kf6 71. Rh5 Kg7 72. Kxh4 Rxa6 {a theoretical draw}) 67... Bxe5 68. Rf5 Ke6! 69. Rxe5+ Kf6) 63. Nd6 $18 {Now Black's position is completely hopeless.} Rh2+ 64. Kf3 Kd3 65. Rxc4 Rxh3+ 66. Kg4 Rh1 67. Ra4 Bf2 68. Ra3+ 1-0

