1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Nd5 Nxd5 8. exd5 Ne7 9. c4 Ng6 10. Qa4 Bd7 11. Qb4 Qb8 12. h4 h5 13. Be3 (13. Bd3 {was played in MVL-Carlsen in 2019.}) 13... a6 ({After} 13... b6 14. Be2 Be7 15. g3 a6 16. Nc3 {Carlsen himself had a smooth win against Gelfand. Black pushed ...b5 now as Magnus did in the current game, but has lost a crucial tempo with ...b6.}) 14. Nc3 Be7 ({Carlsen did not dare to repeat} 14... a5 {which brought him a short victory against Caruana in the tiebreak of their World Championship match. The engine prefers White here, so surely both players knew a way for White to improve on the previous game!}) ({He did not repeat} 14... f5 {either, as he played against Van Foreest in Wijk aan Zee (2020).}) 15. g3 (15. Na4 {as in Van Foreest-Gukesh (Chess.com) may deserve attention, but here Black should probably just play 15...Qc7 in order not to lose an exchange after Nb6.}) 15... O-O! $146 {An excellent novelty! So far, only ...f5 and ...a5 had been tried.} 16. Be2 b5! (16... Bg4? 17. Bxg4 hxg4 18. h5 {would be a disaster for Black.}) 17. cxb5 axb5 18. Bxh5 Bd8! {Magnus is playing like an engine - definitely just a great preparation. Black has lost one pawn but has great compensation as the game proves.} 19. O-O ({After} 19. Bxg6 fxg6 {White's light-squares would be a disaster.}) 19... Ne7 20. Bg5 {White removes the bishop from e3 as ...Nf5 is coming.} Ba5 21. Qb3 Nf5 22. Ne2 {Had Black been able to play ...Nd4 his position would have been even better.} Bb6 (22... f6!? {(as the d2-square is still covered) looked very interesting as well, e.g.} 23. Bc1 Rc8 24. Bg6 Qb7 25. Qf3 Ne7 $17) 23. Rac1 Ra4 24. Rc6! {A great sacrifice, which Black should just have declined, for example playing 24...Bc5 or 24...Qa7.} Bxc6? 25. dxc6 Rc4 26. a4 ({After} 26. Bf3 {White has enough compensation as well. After} e4 27. Bg4 Nd4 28. Nxd4 Bxd4 29. Bd7 d5! {the position is unclear, as Black can just answer} 30. Be7 {with} Re8 {with a fine position.}) 26... Nd4? (26... d5! 27. axb5 Rc5 {threatening ...Rxb5 was a better option.}) 27. Nxd4 Bxd4 28. axb5 d5 29. Rc1! {By trading Black's active rook White is much better thanks to his queenside passed pawns.} Rxc1+ 30. Bxc1 Qb6 31. Be3 Bxe3 32. fxe3 Rd8 (32... d4 33. exd4 Qxd4+ 34. Kf1 e4 35. Qc3 Qd5 36. Be2 {and Black doesn't have enough counterplay.}) 33. Kg2 g6 34. Be2 Kg7 35. Qc3 d4 36. exd4 exd4 37. Qd3 Qa5 38. Qc2 Qb4 39. b3 Re8 40. Bc4?! (40. c7! {was winning by force:} Rc8 41. Bg4 f5 42. Bxf5!! gxf5 43. Qxf5 Rxc7 44. Qe5+ $18) 40... Re7 41. Qf2?? (41. Qd3! {would force Black to defend the d4-pawn passively.}) 41... Qc3 {Suddenly Black has enough counterplay!} 42. Qf3 Qb4?? {No, not this way!} (42... f5!! 43. b6 (43. Qxc3 dxc3 44. Bd3? c2 $19) (43. Qd5 Qc2+ 44. Kh3 Qd1! $11) 43... Qc2+ 44. Kh3 g5!! 45. hxg5 Kg6! 46. Bg8 $8 Re2 {and Black has enough counterplay!} (46... d3!?) (46... Rg7!?)) (42... Qc2+ 43. Kh3 f5!! {works as well.}) 43. Kh3? (43. Qd5! Qd2+ 44. Kh3 {was more precise: Black is paralyzed.}) 43... Qd6? {Passive moves are not the way to save that game!} (43... Qe1! {could have turned the tables:} 44. b6 d3!! 45. Bxd3? Re3 $17) 44. Qf4? (44. b6 {was just winning.}) 44... Qxf4? (44... Qc5 {was necessary.}) 45. gxf4 d3 {Black wins a piece but White's pawns are too fast.} 46. Bxd3 Re3+ 47. Kg2 Rxd3 48. b6 Rxb3 49. b7 Rb6 50. h5 gxh5 51. Kh3 Rxc6 52. b8=Q Rc5 53. Qb2+ f6 54. Kh4 {Black has no fortress, so he resigned!} 1-0

