1. Nf3 b5?! 2. e4! {The most energetic way to meet Black's extravagant move. Carlsen may have underestimated Kramnik's readiness to switch to dynamic play at such an early stage of the game.} Bb7?! ({It almost looks safer to avoid the pawn exchange with} 2... a6 3. d4 Bb7 4. Bd3 {, transposing to 1.e4 a6, as experimented in the famous game Karpov,A (2725)-Miles,A (2545) Skara 1980 EU-chT [ChessBase] (0-1, 46)}) 3. Bxb5 Bxe4 {Generally speaking, the exchange of a knight pawn for a central one should favour Black. However, the bishop is exposed in the centre and the time invested in this operation (two tempi, ...xe4-b7) is too high a price to pay. At the same time, the b5-bishop is active. For instance, it prevents an early occupation of the centre with ...d5.} 4. O-O Nf6 5. d4 e6 ({Playing for the centre with} 5... c6 6. Ba4 d5 {would leave Black desperately underdeveloped:} 7. Ne5 Qb6 8. c4 $16) 6. c4 Be7 7. Nc3 Bb7 {White is better developed and has been the first to occupy the centre. We have actually got a reversed Sokolsky (1.b4 e5 2.b2 xb4 3.xe5) with an extra tempo for White, in what is a questionable position anyway.} 8. d5 {There is no reason to refrain from this new territorial gain.} ({The slow manoeuvre} 8. Ba4 O-O 9. Bc2 {is relatively popular, but it contradicts Steinitz' teaching according to which the player having an advantage is bound to attack! Black gets a reasonable Hedgehog-type of position after} d6 10. b3 Nbd7 11. Bb2 c5 {, for instance:} 12. dxc5 Nxc5 13. Qe2 Qb6 (13... a6 14. Rad1 Qc7 15. Rfe1 Rfe8 16. Nd4 Rac8 {1/2 (22) Timoscenko,G (2536)-Tomescu,V (2453)/Padova 1999/CBM 073 ext}) 14. Rad1 Rfd8 15. Rfe1 a5 16. Ng5 h6 17. Nge4 Ncxe4 18. Nxe4 Nxe4 19. Bxe4 Bxe4 20. Qxe4 a4 $132 {1/2 (38) Iskusnyh,S (2440)-Zhelnin,V (2475)/Orel 1997/EXT 1999}) 8... O-O 9. Bf4!? $146 {It is strange that such a natural developing move is new.} ({Here, too, White usually retreats with his other bishop, as if fearing ...c6 (or what else?) True, this also is enough for an advantage, which speaks against the system of development chosen by Black.} 9. Ba4 Na6 (9... Bb4 10. Bg5 Bxc3 11. bxc3 h6 {Speelman,J (2530)-Hodgson,J/London 1985/EXT 2007 (1-0, 27)} 12. Bxf6!? Qxf6 13. Re1 $32 {It is not clear how Black could develop. Among other things, the d7-pawn needs protection.}) 10. Re1 (10. a3 Nc5 11. Bc2 a5 12. Be3 exd5 13. cxd5 $14 {Wojtkiewicz,A (2560)-Bronstein,D (2400)/Reykjavik 1994/CBM 039 ext (1-0, 44)}) 10... Bb4 11. Bg5 Nc5 12. Bc2 Bxc3 13. bxc3 d6 14. Bh4 h6 15. Nd4 exd5 16. Nf5 $16 {Eingorn,V (2475)-Semeniuk,A/Soviet Union 1984/EXT 2000 (1-0, 40)}) 9... Na6 (9... c6? {is not a threat at all:} 10. d6! cxb5 11. dxe7 Qxe7 12. Bd6 Bxf3 13. gxf3 $18) 10. Re1 Qc8 {Avoiding the pin created by g5 after ...b4. However, the last move takes the queen too far from the kingside.} 11. Nd4 Bb4 12. Bg5! Ne8 (12... Bxc3 13. Bxf6 Bxe1 (13... gxf6 14. bxc3 Nc5 15. Re3 $40) 14. Qg4 g6 15. Qf4 {[%cal Gf4h6]} Bxf2+ 16. Kxf2 Nc5 {hoping for a fork on d3 or e4.} 17. Kg1! $18 {Black has exhausted his defensive resources and can resign.}) 13. Re3 {The logical continuation, building up a decisive kingside attack.} ({The continuation suggested by the computer,} 13. Qa4!? Bxc3 14. Bxd7 $16 {looks good enough, but in a rapid game players usually play according to known patterns and rarely notice such "incidental" possibilities.}) 13... c6 {Finally, but... too late. While Black will eliminate the central white pawns, his king will get into deadly trouble.} 14. Bxa6 Bxa6 15. Qh5 f6 (15... Bxc4 16. Rh3 h6 17. Bxh6 $40) 16. Rh3! {Most of Black's pieces (the queen, the queen's rook, the bishops) are completely out of play and cannot contribute to the defence. Although White's attack implies a piece sacrifice, he will enjoy material superiority on the critical area of the board.} fxg5 17. Qxh7+ Kf7 18. Qh5+ Kg8 (18... Ke7 19. Qxg5+ Kf7 20. Qh5+ Ke7 21. Qh4+ Nf6 (21... Rf6 22. Ne4 $18) (21... Kf7 22. dxe6+ dxe6 23. Ne4 $18) 22. dxe6 dxe6 23. Re1 Kf7 24. Nf3 $18) 19. Qh7+ Kf7 20. Ne4 Bxc4 21. Qh5+ Ke7 (21... Kg8 22. Nxg5 $18) (21... g6 22. Qh7+ Ng7 23. Rf3+ $18) 22. Qxg5+ Kf7 23. Qh5+ Ke7 24. Qg5+ Kf7 25. dxe6+ dxe6 26. Nf3! {[%csl Yc4][%cal Gf3e5] An elegant regrouping move.} Kg8 27. Qh4 {Threatening g5 (either of them) followed by h8 mate.} Rxf3 28. Qh7+ (28. Qh7+ Kf8 29. Rxf3+ Ke7 30. Qh4+) 1-0

