{[%evp 0,79,25,13,29,10,10,10,10,10,42,7,20,-28,32,31,35,28,28,9,9,13,13,13,8,13,32,23,32,19,13,46,58,56,55,71,61,54,60,16,30,16,41,46,41,41,23,-26,-26,-26,-26,-32,-24,-27,-10,0,-19,-16,-35,-29,-57,-43,-43,-43,-72,-122,-147,-84,0,-95,-95,-101,143,216,166,166,29989,29990,29991,29992,29993,29994]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 Nbd7 5. Bg5 h6 6. Bh4 Be7 7. cxd5 {A move that White doesn't play that often.} (7. e3 {is the very main line.}) 7... Nxd5 8. Bxe7 Qxe7 9. e4 Nxc3 10. bxc3 O-O 11. Bd3 c5 12. O-O cxd4 13. cxd4 {The position is extremely similar to a Semi-Tarrasch (1.d4 f6 2.c4 e6 3.f3 d5 4 c3 c5 5.cxd5 xd5 etc.).} b6 (13... e5 14. d5 {would be a typical possible structure change, as it frees the c5-square for the black knight. However, although with the support of ...b6 it seems stable, it isn't necessarily: White will push a4, and whether Black allows a5 and axb6 or pushes ...a5 himself, then ...b6 will become a target.}) 14. a4 Bb7 15. a5 bxa5 16. Rxa5 Nf6 17. Re1 Rfd8 18. Qa1 Qc7 {A typical way to "protect" th a7-pawn.} 19. h3 (19. Rxa7 Rxa7 20. Qxa7 Ra8 21. Qc5 Qxc5 22. dxc5 Rc8 $11 {and Black gets his pawn back.}) 19... a6 20. Rc5 Qf4 21. Re5 Nd7 22. Ra5 Nf6 23. d5!? {Also a typical sacrifice in Semi-Tarrasch pawn structures. However, here it definitely doesn't bring White any advantage objectively.} ({According to the engine White would be slightly better playing quiet moves like} 23. g3 Qd6 24. Kg2 {but afterwards I do not see a serious plan for him.}) 23... exd5 24. e5 Ne4 25. Qd4 Rdc8! {The right rook, in order to keep the a-pawn protected!} 26. Raa1 a5 27. Rab1 Bc6 (27... Ba6!? 28. Bxa6 Rxa6 29. Qxd5 Nc3 30. Qd7 Qc4! 31. Rb7 Qe6! {would have been equal.ñ}) 28. e6!? {And a second pawn sacrifice, a bit in the same spirit as the first one: gaining initiative on the dark-squares!} fxe6 29. Ne5 Qf6 30. f3 Ng5 31. Rb6 Be8 32. Qe3 a4?! {Allowing Magnus to start a serious attack - even if Black still stands fine.} ({The prophylactic} 32... h5 {was better, avoiding Magnus' next move. It's not like playing ...h5 is very pretty, but not being able to play g4 is a serious problem for White! And Black's a-pawn will start running.}) 33. Ng4 Qd8 (33... Qc3 {would run into a pretty much forced variation, obviouly hinted by the engine:} 34. h4 a3 35. hxg5 a2 36. gxh6 Qxe1+ 37. Qxe1 a1=Q 38. h7+ Kh8 39. Qxa1 Rxa1+ 40. Kf2 {with an unclear (probably equal) position.}) 34. Rxe6 Nxe6 {Not a bad move. Instead though, the engine is showing two amusing drawing lines.} (34... Bf7 35. Nxh6+ gxh6 36. Rxh6 Kg7 37. Rb6 Kg8 38. Rh6 $11) (34... a3 35. f4 a2 36. fxg5 a1=Q 37. Rxa1 Rxa1+ 38. Kh2 Qc7+ 39. Re5 Bh5 40. Nxh6+ gxh6 41. gxh6 Rf8 42. g3 Ra2+ 43. Kg1 Ra1+ 44. Kh2 $11) 35. Qxe6+ Bf7?? (35... Kh8! {was necessary:} 36. Nxh6! (36. Qf5? Qb6+ 37. Kh1 Bg6! 38. Qxg6 Qxg6 39. Bxg6 a3 {and Black is better}) 36... Bh5! {avoiding a mate on g8, and Black is more or less out of danger.}) 36. Nxh6+! gxh6 37. Qxh6 {Suddenly Black has no defence!} Qc7 (37... Rc7 38. Re5 $18) 38. Qh7+ Kf8 39. Qh8+ Bg8 40. Qh6+ {Albeit not a fully correct game, as a whole, Magnus has shown fantastic ideas - but obviously Alireza can be disappointed, as with one or two precise moves the result could have been totally different!} 1-0

