1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 Be7 5. Bg5 h6 6. Bh4 O-O 7. e3 Nbd7 {The Orthodox Defence to the Queens Gambit was more popular in the 1910's than in the 2010's, but King Magnus can play anything he wants.} 8. Be2 {There's hardly any difference between the text} ({and the other bishop move} 8. Bd3) ({while} 8. Rc1 c5 9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Bxe7 Nxe7 {is currently considered a reliable defense, thanks to the pioneering efforts of Vladimir Kramnik. Although, Nakamura himself beat Fressinet in 2016 by going to the endgame:} 11. dxc5 Nxc5 12. Qxd8 Rxd8 13. b4 Nd7 14. Bd3) 8... dxc4 9. Bxc4 a6 10. a4 c5 11. O-O cxd4 12. Nxd4 {Hikaru goes safety first.} ({The more principled} 12. exd4 {is met by the solid defence} Nb6 13. Bb3 Bd7 14. Ne5 Bc6 {It's very hard for White to break through, while Carlsen would surely be looking to lay siege to the IQP.}) 12... Nb6 13. Be2 (13. Bb3 Nbd5 14. Nxd5 Nxd5 15. Bxe7 Nxe7 16. Bc2 Bd7 17. Qd3 g6 {Salem-Karjakin, World Rapid 2017, and Black eventually prevailed.}) 13... Nbd5 14. Nxd5 Nxd5 15. Bxe7 Qxe7 16. Qb3 b6 17. Bf3 Bb7 {There's nothing White can do in terms of playing on, and Hikaru forces total liquidation.} 18. Nf5 exf5 19. Bxd5 Bxd5 20. Qxd5 Qf6 21. Qb3 Rad8 22. Rfd1 Rxd1+ 23. Rxd1 Rd8 24. Rxd8+ Qxd8 25. g3 g6 26. h4 h5 27. Qc4 a5 28. b3 Qd1+ 29. Kg2 Qd7 30. Kf1 Qd1+ 31. Kg2 Qd7 32. Kf1 Qd1+ 33. Kg2 Qd7 1/2-1/2

