1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 g6 3. Bg2 {The Fianchetto King's Indian isn't one of White's most ambitious openings, as even the standard ...c6/...d5 setup leads to equality. However, against an enterprising Black player, it can lead to interesting chess.} Bg7 4. c4 O-O 5. d4 d6 6. O-O c5 {A first sign that we are going to get an interesting game.} (6... Nbd7 {is the Kotronias recommendation, and by and large I agree with his findings.}) (6... Nc6 {is probably the most played move, but for various reasons I am suspicious of it.}) 7. d5 (7. Nc3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Nc6!? {is the famous 'ICC gambit' favoured by Melkumyan.} 9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. Bxc6 Rb8 {Now I examine the two most-played moves.} 11. Bg2 (11. Qa4 Bh3! 12. Bg2 (12. Rd1 a5 {Threatening ...b4.} 13. a3 Nd7 $11) 12... Bxg2 13. Kxg2 Qd7! {Bringing about adequate Benko-type compensation for Black.} 14. Qxd7 Nxd7 15. Nd5!? Bxb2 16. Nxe7+ Kg7 17. Rb1 Bxc1 18. Rfxc1 Rbe8 $11) (11. b3 Bh3 12. Re1?? (12. Bd2 $44) 12... Ng4 13. Bb2 Qb6 $19) (11. Bf3 {Now the play isn't that forcing, but one interesting line leading to equality is} Ng4 12. Bg2 Bb7 13. Qc2 Bxg2 14. Kxg2 Rc8 15. b3 d5! $11) 11... Qa5 12. Qd2!? (12. Nb5 {This is most-played but probably not most critical.} Bb7 13. Bxb7 Rxb7 14. Bd2 Qa6 15. Bc3 Rc8 16. b3 d5! $11 {Black equalised quickly in Zhigalko,A-Melkumyan,H Warsaw 2014}) 12... Bb7 (12... Be6 {is also possible, and after the obligatory} 13. b3 Rxb3 14. axb3 Qxa1 15. Nb5 {then a few more well-chosen moves should neutralise whatever White has left.}) 13. Bxb7 (13. e4 Qa6 $11) 13... Rxb7 14. Rb1 Ng4 15. Qd5 Qa6 $13 {The debate over 'Benko compensation' continues.}) 7... b5 {Of course.} 8. cxb5 a6 9. bxa6 Bf5 (9... e6 10. dxe6 fxe6 {is favoured by the engine, but this structure tends not to be so great for Black due to the strength of a possible e4-push by White.}) 10. Nfd2 (10. Nh4!? {deserves attention, trying to make Black either swap bishops or give some tempi to White's e4-e5 efforts.}) 10... Nxa6 11. Nc3 (11. e4 Bg4 12. f3 Bc8 13. Nc4 $44) 11... Nb4 12. Nc4! {A very good, but risky decision.} Nc2?! (12... Bc2 13. Qd2 Bb3 14. Na3 Bxd5 15. Nxd5 Nfxd5 16. Nc4 (16. Bxd5 Nxd5 17. Qxd5 Rxa3 18. bxa3 Bxa1 $15) 16... Nc7 $44 {Black has to sacrifice an exchange in one way or another, but there seems to be sufficent compensation for it.}) 13. g4!? {Entering huge complications.} (13. Rb1! {gives White good chances of simply consolidating the pawn, e.g.} Nb4 14. e4 Bg4 15. f3 Bc8 16. a3 Ba6 17. b3 $14) 13... Nxg4 14. e4 Nxa1 15. exf5 Bxc3?! {Neither natural nor particularly good.} (15... gxf5 16. Qd3 $14) (15... Ne5 16. Nxe5 Bxe5 17. fxg6 hxg6 18. Bd2 Bxc3 19. Bxc3 Rxa2 20. f4 $13) 16. bxc3 Nf6 (16... gxf5 17. h3 Nf6 18. Bh6 $16) 17. Qe2 Re8 18. Bg5 $16 {Objectively, White is very close to winning, but now strange things begin to happen.} Qd7 19. fxg6 hxg6 20. Rxa1 Qf5 21. Bxf6? {A weird and frankly almost unintelligible decision.} ({After} 21. h4 $16 {it is difficult to imagine that the dark-squared bishop could ever be inferior to the Black knight, though of course the time pressure makes this position nevertheless somewhat difficult.}) 21... Qxf6 22. Qf3 Qh4 23. Qe4 Qf6 24. Qf3 Qh4 25. Qe4 Qf6 26. Qd3?! (26. a4 Qxc3 27. Ra3 Qc1+ 28. Bf1 $14 {would be a reasonable technical decision at this point, trying to reach some kind of endgame where the passed a-pawn can show its strength.}) 26... Reb8 27. a3 Rb3 28. Rc1 Ra4 {Now it is not totally clear who is playing on, so White forces a draw.} (28... Kg7 {followed by ...h8 might have been an intelligent way to play on White's time trouble.}) 29. Qc2 Qf4 30. Qxb3 Qxc1+ 31. Bf1 Qg5+ $11 32. Bg2 Qc1+ 33. Bf1 Qg5+ 34. Bg2 Qc1+ 1/2-1/2

