1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. Qa4 {About 40 years ago, when Black's 4-th move was still regarded as only an experiment, this -sortie was considered to be the strongest reaction and almost refutation of 4...a6. Nowadays things have changed a lot, White tries to build up some pressure without too much risk.} ({The main move is} 5. b3 {, with heavily analysed theory after} Bb4+ 6. Bd2 Be7 7. Nc3 ({and especially} 7. Bg2 {often going on far beyond the opening and even middlegame.})) ({The sharp alternative, connected with a -sacrifice, is} 5. Qc2!? Bb7 6. Bg2 c5 7. d5 exd5 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. O-O {This line has quickly developed in the past 5 years and is still highly topical. However, it hardly suits Kramnik's style, so the ex-champion adopts the more restrained old move. Lately it has become fasionable again and it occurred in quite a few high-level games.}) 5... Bb7 (5... c5 6. Bg2 ({The usual reaction, as} 6. d5 exd5 7. cxd5 Bb7 {is a rather inferior version of the aforementioned -sacrifice.}) 6... Bb7 {is only a transposition.}) ({Interesting and less explored is} 5... c6 6. Nc3 (6. Bg2 {is too soft due to} b5! 7. cxb5 cxb5 8. Qd1 Nc6 9. O-O Rc8 10. Nbd2 Be7 11. Nb3 b4 12. Re1 d5 13. Bg5 h6 14. Bxf6 Bxf6 15. e3 O-O 16. Nc5 Bb5 17. Bf1 Bxf1 18. Rxf1 e5 (18... Be7 $11) 19. dxe5 Nxe5 20. Nxe5 Rxc5 21. Nd3 Rc4 $132 {Smejkal,J-Kavalek,L/CS-ch Luhacovice/1968/}) 6... b5!? ({This positional sacrifice in the spirit of the Volga/Benko gambit is the main point, otherwise Black risks getting under positional pressure. An old example is} 6... d5 7. cxd5 exd5 8. Ne5 Bb7 9. Bg5 Be7 10. Bg2 O-O 11. O-O h6 12. Bxf6 Bxf6 13. f4 a5 14. Rad1 Qc8 15. e4 b5 16. Qc2 Na6 17. Rc1 Nc7 18. Rfd1 Qe6 19. Nxd5! Nxd5 20. exd5 cxd5 21. Qc7 Ra7 22. Qc5 $16 {Furman,S-Keres,P/URS-ch Moscow/1948/}) 7. cxb5 cxb5 8. Nxb5 Qb6 9. Nc3 Bb4 10. Bg2 O-O 11. O-O Bxc3 (11... Rc8 12. Bd2 Nc6 13. Qd1 d5 14. Na4 Qb5 15. Bxb4 Qxb4 16. Nc5 Qa5 17. Rc1 Rab8 18. b3 Ne4 19. Qe1 Nxc5 20. Qxa5 Nxa5 21. Rxc5 Nb7! $11 {1/2 Appolonov,S-Berzinsh,R/Alma-Ata 1991/TD (35)}) 12. bxc3 Bxe2 13. Re1 Bb5 14. Qb3 d5 (14... Bc6 15. c4 Na6 16. Bf4 Be4 17. Be5 d5 18. Bxf6 gxf6 19. Qe3 Nb4 20. Qh6 $40 Qd8 21. Nh4 Bxg2 22. Nxg2 Kh8 23. Nf4 Rg8 24. Rab1 Nc2? 25. Nxe6! {1-0,Skembris,S (2545)-Timman,J (2620)/Corfu m/1993/}) 15. Ne5 Nbd7 16. Be3 Rab8 17. Rab1?! (17. a4 Ba6 18. Qa3 $13) 17... Qa6 18. Qd1 Nxe5 19. dxe5 Nd7 20. Bd4 Bc4 21. Qd2 h6 $15 {Leriche,E (2319)-Gervasio,R (2310)/FRA-chT2/2004/}) 6. Bg2 c5 7. dxc5 {White tries to keep more tension in the position. Nikolic's favourite is} (7. O-O cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bxg2 9. Kxg2 Qc7 10. Rd1 a6 11. Nc3 Qb7+ (11... Be7 12. f3 O-O 13. Bf4 Qb7 14. e4 d6 15. Nde2 Rd8 16. Rd2 Nc6 17. Rad1 Ne8 18. a3 Rdc8 19. h4 h6 20. h5 Na5 21. b3 Qc7 22. e5 Rd8 23. exd6 Bxd6 24. Ne4 Bxf4 25. Nxf4 Rxd2+ 26. Rxd2 Nc6 27. b4 Ne5 28. c5 Nf6!? $132 {Browne,W-Andersson,U/Buenos Aires/1978/}) 12. f3 Be7 13. Bg5 h6 (13... Nc6 14. Bxf6 $142 !? (14. Nxc6 Qxc6 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Qxc6 dxc6 17. Ne4 Ke7 18. Nxf6 Kxf6 19. Rd6 Rhd8 20. Rad1 (20. Rxc6 Rd2 $44 {}) 20... Ke7 21. Rxd8 Rxd8 22. Rxd8 Kxd8 {1/2,Nikolic,P (2630)-Psakhis,L (2565)/ECC Eupen/1997/}) 14... gxf6 (14... Bxf6 15. Ne4 Be7 16. Nxc6 Qxc6 17. Qxc6 dxc6 18. Nd6+ $36) 15. Nxc6 Qxc6 (15... dxc6 16. Ne4! $36 (16. Rd3 O-O 17. Rad1 Rfd8 18. Rxd8+ Rxd8 19. Rxd8+ Bxd8 20. Qd1 Be7 21. Qd4 Qc7 22. Ne4 Qd8 23. Qxd8+ Bxd8 24. g4 Be7 25. f4 Kf8 26. f5 $14 {Molina,R (2322)-Bittencourt,J (2448)/BRA-ch Rio de Janeiro/2007/})) 16. Rd3 (16. Qxc6 dxc6 17. Rd3 $14) 16... Qxa4 17. Nxa4 b5 18. cxb5 axb5 19. Nb6 Ra7 {Chetverik,M (2289)-Gerstner,B (2144)/Bad Wiessee/2006/} 20. Rb3 $14) 14. Bxf6 Bxf6 15. Ne4 Be7 16. Rd3 (16. Qb3 Qc7 17. Rac1 O-O 18. f4 Nc6 19. Nf3 Rfd8 20. Qe3 Rac8 21. g4 d5 22. cxd5 exd5 23. Ng3 (23. Nd4 Qb7 $11) 23... Bc5 24. Qd2 a5 25. a3 Qe7 26. Qd3 Qf6 27. Qd2 Qe6 28. f5 Qe7 $15 {Nikolic,P (2659)-Karpov,A (2696)/Monte Carlo blindfold rpd/2000/}) 16... O-O 17. Rad1 Rc8 18. Qb3 Ra7 19. e3 Qc7 20. a3 {Hoefer,H (1960)-Skrzynski,I (1713)/corr/2000/} Nc6 $11 {}) 7... bxc5 {An important moment, which defines the character of the ensuing struggle. Taking towards the  is natural, but equally logical is the even more often played developing move} (7... Bxc5 8. Nc3 O-O 9. O-O Be7 (9... Ne4 10. Qc2 Nxc3 11. Qxc3 d5 12. Rd1 Nd7 13. Bg5 {Die korrekte Verteidigung bestand in} Be7!? (13... Qe8 14. cxd5 Bxd5 15. b4 Be7 16. Bxe7 Qxe7 17. Rac1 Rfd8 18. Nd4 Nf6 19. b5 Bxg2 20. Kxg2 Rd5 21. Nc6 Qd7 22. Qc2 Qb7 23. a4 h6 24. e4 Rc5 25. Qd3 $36 {Dautov,R (2606)-Polgar,J (2656)/Istanbul olm/2000/}) 14. Bxe7 Qxe7 15. cxd5 Bxd5 16. Rac1 Nc5 17. Ne5! (17. b4 Na4! 18. Qc2 b5 19. a3 a5 $11 {Dautov}) 17... Bxg2 18. Kxg2 a5 19. Qf3 Rac8 20. h4 (20. Nc4!? Qc7 21. Qf4 $14) 20... f6 21. Nc4 Qc7 22. Nd6 Rcd8 23. Nb5 Qe5 24. Nc3 f5 25. Qc6 Qb8 26. Rxd8 Rxd8 27. Rd1 Rxd1 28. Nxd1 Qb7 29. Qxb7 Nxb7 30. Kf3 Nd6 31. Kf4 Kf7 32. Ke5 Ke7 33. Ne3 {Nikolic,P (2652)-Istratescu,A (2561)/EU-ch Ohrid/2001/} b5!? {f7,d6=}) 10. Rd1 Na6 11. Bf4 Nc5 12. Qc2 Qc8 {A more recent attempt is} 13. Rd4!? (13. Rac1 Nce4 14. Nd4 Nxc3 15. Qxc3 a6 (15... Bxg2 16. Kxg2 a6 17. Qf3 Ra7 18. a4 Qb7 19. Qxb7 Rxb7 20. f3 Rc8 21. e4 d6 22. Ne2 Ne8 23. h4 Kf8 24. b3 Rc6 25. Rd3 Bf6 26. Rcd1 Be7 27. h5 $14 {,Ftacnik,L (2596)-Sax,G (2568)/ECC Saint Vincent/2005/}) 16. Qb3 Bxg2 17. Kxg2 Qb7+ 18. Qf3 Ne4!? ({More enterprising than} 18... Ra7 19. Qxb7 Rxb7 20. f3 Rc8 21. e4 d6 22. Ne2 Ne8 23. b3 h5 24. h4 g6 25. Rd2 Rbc7 26. Rd3 Rc5 27. a4 R5c6 28. Rcd1 Rc5 29. Kf1 Bf8 30. Nc3 Rb8 31. R1d2 Rb7 32. Ke2 $14 {Piket,J (2633)-Chuchelov,V (2518)/NED-chT/2000/ Similar to Ftacnik-Sax above, Black must patiently defend a slightly worse .}) 19. Nb3 f5 20. Nd2 (20. Kg1 Bf6 $132) 20... g5! 21. Nxe4 fxe4 22. Qg4 h5 23. Qxh5 gxf4 24. Qg6+ Kh8 25. Qh6+ Kg8 {1/2,Van Wely,L (2654)-Chuchelov,V (2603)/Bundesliga 2003/}) 13... d5 14. cxd5 (14. Rc1 Nce4 15. Nxe4 dxe4 16. Ne5 g5!? (16... Rd8 17. Rxd8+ Qxd8 18. Rd1 Qe8 19. Be3 h6 20. a3 Rc8 21. b4 Rc7 22. h4 Qc8 23. Qb3 Ba6 24. Rd4 Ne8 25. Bxe4 Bf6 {Timman,J (2591)-Jackova,J (2388)/Marianske Lazne /2009/} 26. Qd3!? Bxc4 27. Rxc4 Bxe5 $11 {/}) 17. Be3!? (17. Bxg5 Qc5 18. Bxf6 Bxf6 19. Rd7 Bc8 20. Ng4 Bg5 21. b4!? (21. Rdd1 f5 22. b4 Qxb4 23. Rb1 Qc5 24. Rb5 Qc7 25. h4 (25. f4? exf3 26. Bxf3 Ba6 27. Re5 Rad8 28. Rxd8 Rxd8 29. Kg2 Bc8 30. h4 Be7 $19 {Koneru,H (2600)-Zhao Xue (2462)/Ulan Bator/2010/}) 25... Be7 26. Bxe4 Rb8 27. Bxf5 exf5 28. Nh6+ Kh8 29. Nxf5 Bf6 $15 {/}) 21... Qf5 22. Bxe4 Qxg4 23. Rd4 f5 24. h3 Qxe4 25. Rxe4 Bxc1 26. Qxc1 (26. Rd4 Bg5 $13) 26... fxe4 27. Qg5+ Kh8 $11) 17... Qc7 18. f4 exf3 (18... Bc5 19. Qc3 gxf4 20. gxf4 Bxd4 21. Bxd4 $44 {,Turov,M (2628)-Filip,L (2470)/Rethymnon/2010/}) 19. Nxf3 h6!? $13 {/} (19... e5 20. Qf5! exd4 21. Qxg5+ Kh8 22. Bxd4 Qd8 23. Rd1 $44) (19... Bc5!?)) 14... Nxd5 15. Bg5 Bxg5 16. Nxg5 Nf6 17. Bxb7 Qxb7 18. b4 h6 19. Nf3 (19. Nxf7 Qxf7 20. bxc5 Rac8 $132) 19... Ncd7 20. Nb5 Rfc8 21. Qd2 Rc6 22. Rd1 a6 23. Nd6 Qc7 24. e4 e5 25. Rd3 Rc2 26. Qe1 a5 $132 {Aronian,L (2752)-Leko,P (2740)/Wijk aan Zee/2006/}) 8. O-O Be7 9. Nc3 O-O 10. Rd1 Qb6 ({After the main alternative} 10... d6 11. Bf4 Qb6 {White faces a choice:} 12. Rab1!? {is a more recent idea, here Black is still searching for a reliable road to equality:} ({The older move} 12. Rd2 {is more or less harmless and even gives Black more satisfactory options:} h6 (12... Nc6 13. Bxd6 Bxd6 14. Rxd6 Qxb2 15. Rb1 Qxc3 16. Rxb7 Nd4 17. Qd1 Nxf3+ 18. Bxf3 Qxc4 19. a4 $44 {gives White compensation, but no advantage.}) 13. Rad1 e5 14. Be3 Nc6 15. Qb5 Qc7 16. Qb3 a6 17. Nh4 Rfb8 18. Bxc6 Qxc6 19. f3 e4 {Onischuk,A (2664)-Eljanov,P (2687)/Foros/2008/} 20. Nf5 Bf8 21. Rxd6 $44) (12. Qb3 Rd8 13. Qxb6 axb6 14. Nb5 Ne8 15. a3 h6 16. Rac1 Nc6 17. Ne1 Na5 18. Bd2 Bxg2 19. Kxg2 Nc6 $11 {Vallejo Pons,F (2686)-Anand,V (2786)/Linares/2005/}) 12... Bc6 (12... Na6 13. a3 (13. Nb5 Rfd8!? (13... d5 14. Ne5 d4 15. Bxb7 Qxb7 16. e3 dxe3 17. Bxe3 $14 {Pelletier,Y (2525)-Alterman,B (2600)/Elista olm/1998/}) (13... Ne8 14. a3 Be4 15. Rbc1 Rd8 16. b4 Bc6 17. bxc5 Nxc5 18. Qxa7 Qxa7 19. Nxa7 Ba4 20. Re1 Nf6 21. Nd4 Rd7 22. Nac6 $16 {Zhigalko,S (2640)-Franco Alonso,A (2490)/ECC Plovdiv/2010/}) 14. Bd2 Nb4 15. a3 Bc6 16. Bxb4 (16. axb4 a6 17. Qa5 Qxa5 18. bxa5 axb5 19. b4 $13) 16... cxb4 17. Qxb4 Be4 18. Rbc1 Rab8 $11) 13... Rac8 14. Qc2 Rfd8 15. e4 Nd7 (15... Bc6 16. Qe2 h6 17. Nd5! Nxd5 (17... exd5 18. exd5 Ba4 19. b3 Bxb3 20. Rdc1 $16 {[%cal Rf3d2,Re2e7] d2}) 18. exd5 Bd7 19. dxe6 Bxe6 20. Nd2 Bf8 21. Qd3 Qc7 (21... Nc7 $142) 22. Ne4 Bg4 23. f3 Bh5 24. Bh3 $16 {Giri,A (2677)-Infante,M (2276)/Khanty-Mansiysk olm/2010/}) (15... h6 16. Qe2 Nb8 {Nikolic,P (2629)-Volke,K (2455)/Bundesliga/1999/} 17. b4 $14) 16. Qe2 h6 17. h4 (17. Nb5 Nf8 18. Qe1 Rd7 19. Rd3 Nc7 20. Nc3 Ng6 21. Be3 Qa6 22. Bf1 Ne8 23. Rdd1 Bf6 $11 {Ikonnikov,V (2527)-Volke,K (2450)/Vlissingen/2002/}) 17... Ba8 18. g4!? Nf8 19. g5 h5 20. Bg3 g6 21. Rd2 Nb8 22. Qd1 a6 23. Rd3 e5 24. Nd5 Qa7 25. Qd2 Nc6 26. Rd1 Nd4 27. Nxd4 cxd4 28. f4 $14 {Van Wely,L (2668)-Leko,P (2736)/Monte Carlo blindfold rpd/2003/}) (12... Nbd7 13. b4 cxb4 14. Qxb4! $36 (14. Rxb4 Bc6 15. Qa3 Qc5 16. Be3 Qh5 17. h3 Bxf3 18. Bxf3 Qxh3 19. Bg2 Qh5 20. Bf3 Qh3 21. Bg2 Qh5 22. Bf3 {1/2,Lautier,J (2666)-Gelfand,B (2700)/Enghien les Bains/2003/})) 13. Qa3 (13. Qc2 Rd8 14. e4 Qb7 $132) 13... Rd8 14. b4 cxb4 15. Rxb4 Qa6 16. Rb3!? (16. Qb2 Qc8 17. Nb5 Na6 18. Ra4 Qb7 19. Qb1 Nc5 20. Rb4 Be4 $36 {Lysyj,I (2638)-Carron,J (2394)/Zürich /2010/}) 16... Qxa3 17. Rxa3 h6 18. h4 Nh5 19. Be3 a6 20. g4 Nf6 21. g5 hxg5 22. hxg5 Ne8 23. g6 Rc8 24. gxf7+ Kxf7 25. c5 Nd7 26. cxd6 Bxd6 27. Rb3 $14 {Dronov,A (2617)-Mikeshin,S (2591)/corr/2007/}) 11. Bf4 Rd8 {Carlsen keeps his  on d7 and makes a useful move.} (11... d6 {-10...d6 transposes into the lines from the previous note.}) (11... Qxb2?? 12. Rab1 Qxc3 13. Rxb7 $18 {[%csl Rc3][%cal Rd1c1,Rf4d2,Rf4e5] c1,e5,d2}) 12. Rab1 {Kramnik doesn't insist on occupying the d with his  and adopts a similar set-up, as we have already mentioned above. His  supports the future b4 advance.} (12. Rd2 d6 (12... d5?! 13. cxd5 exd5 14. Ne5 Na6 15. Rad1 Nc7 16. e4 Ne6 17. Nxd5 Bxd5 18. exd5 Nxf4 19. Qxf4 Bd6 20. Qf5 $16 {Lautier,J (2672)-Macieja,B (2593)/ECC Saint Vincent/2005/}) 13. Rad1 h6 $142 (13... Ne8 14. Nb5 (14. e4 Bc6 15. Qc2 Qb7 16. Qb3?! Qxb3 17. axb3 f6! 18. Ne1 Nd7 19. Ra1 Rdb8 20. Ra3 Rb7 21. e5 Nxe5 22. Bxe5 Bxg2 23. Bxd6 Nxd6 24. Kxg2 Nf5 $15 {Gustafsson,J (2603)-Leko,P (2741)/Dortmund/2008/}) 14... Na6 15. Bg5! $146 Bxg5 16. Nxg5 Bxg2 17. Kxg2 Nac7 18. Qc2 g6 19. Nc3 Nf6 20. Qd3 Nce8 21. Qf3 Rab8 22. Nce4 Qb7 23. b3 Nxe4 24. Nxe4 Qc7 25. Qf4 e5 26. Qh4 $16 {Kramnik,V (2790)-Karjakin,S (2725)/Nice blindfold rpd/2010/}) (13... a6 14. Ng5!? Bxg2 15. Kxg2 e5 16. Be3 Qb7+ 17. f3! (17. Kg1 h6 18. Nf3 Nc6 19. Nd5 Nxd5 20. Rxd5 Nb4 21. R5d2 Nc6 22. Rd5 Nb4 23. R5d2 Nc6 {1/2,Leko,P (2751)-Bacrot,E (2728)/Nalchik/2009/}) 17... Ng4 18. Bxc5 Nd7 19. Bg1 Bxg5 20. Rxd6 Ngf6 21. h4 Bh6 22. b3 $16 {Sargissian,G (2675)-Zhigalko,A (2587)/EU-ch Rijeka/2010/}) 14. Qb5 Ne8 15. e4 g5 16. Be3 Nd7 17. h4 g4 18. Ne1 Kg7!? (18... Qxb5 19. cxb5!? (19. Nxb5 Ne5 20. b3 a6 $11) 19... Bf8 20. Nc2 Ne5 21. Na3 Rdc8 22. Bf4 Bg7 23. Bxe5 Bxe5 24. Nc4 Bd4 25. Ne2 e5 26. a4 Kf8 27. Rc2 {Navara,D (2722)-Leko,P (2724)/Khanty-Mansiysk olm/2010/} Rab8 {Navara,} 28. Na5 Ba8 $13 {/}) 19. Bf4 Bc6 20. Qxb6 axb6 21. Nc2 (21. e5 Bxg2 22. exd6 Bf6 23. Nxg2 Bxc3 24. bxc3 e5 25. Be3 Ra4 $36) 21... Ne5 22. Ne3 Nf3+ 23. Bxf3 gxf3 24. h5 Bf6 25. Ng4 Bxc3 26. bxc3 Bxe4 27. Bxh6+ Kh7 28. Bg5 Rd7 29. Bf4 f6 {Wang Yue (2732)-Bacrot,E (2716)/Nanjing/2010/} 30. Bxd6 Rad8 31. Bf4 Rxd2 32. Rxd2 Rxd2 33. Bxd2 Bd3 $11) 12... h6 (12... Na6 13. a3 (13. e4 $14 {} Nxe4? 14. Ne5 $36) 13... Bc6 14. Qc2 Qb7 15. e4 (15. Bd6!?) 15... d6 16. Ne1 Rac8 17. e5 dxe5 18. Bxe5 Nb8 $11 {Kantorik,M (2390)-Stefansson,H (2588)/Sarajevo/2010/}) (12... d5 13. cxd5 exd5 14. Ne5 $14 {is still risky for Black, although here it's more justified than with White's  on d2.}) 13. Qb5 $146 {[%mdl 8] This novelty doesn't seem too enterprising.} ({More logical seems to be} 13. a3 {[%cal Yb2b4] b4 , but Kramnik already played this earlier and Carlsen was undoubtedly prepared. However, we can only guess what exactly he had on his mind.} a5 (13... Nc6 14. Nb5 a6 15. Nd6 $14) ({A probably playable alternative is} 13... Bc6!? 14. Qc2 a5 15. e4 d6 $132) 14. Qb5 Qxb5 15. cxb5 (15. Nxb5!? {seems more promising.}) 15... d5 16. Ne5 g5 17. Be3 Nbd7 $132 18. Nc6 Bxc6 19. bxc6 Ne5 20. Na4 Nxc6 21. Bxc5 Bxc5 22. Nxc5 Rab8 23. e4 dxe4 24. Rxd8+ Rxd8 25. Nxe4 Nxe4 26. Bxe4 Nd4 27. Kg2 Nb3 $11 {Kramnik,V (2759)-Bacrot,E (2721)/Dortmund/2009/}) 13... Bc6 {Contrary to the previous note, here Black can force his opponent to swap the L. However, he had other plausible and possibly stronger moves at his disposal:} (13... d5!? {seems more to the point now,} 14. cxd5 (14. Nd2 Qxb5 15. Nxb5 Nc6 16. cxd5 Nxd5 $11) 14... Nxd5 (14... exd5 15. Nh4! {[%csl Rd5] ×d5}) 15. Ne5 (15. Qc4 Nd7!? $11) 15... g5 16. Bd2 Nc6 $13 {leads to a complex position, in which Black's piece-play compensates his shaky -structure.}) (13... Nc6 14. Ne5 (14. Qxb6 axb6 15. Bc7 Rdc8 16. Bxb6? Ra6 $17) 14... g5!? (14... d5 15. cxd5 Qxb5 16. Nxb5 Nxd5 17. Nc7! Nxf4 18. gxf4 $14) 15. Qxb6 (15. Nxc6 Bxc6 16. Bxc6 dxc6 17. Qxb6 axb6 18. Bc7 Rxd1+ 19. Rxd1 b5 $11) 15... axb6 16. Nxc6 Bxc6 17. Bc7 Rdc8 18. Bd6 Kf8 {and Black should gradually equalize.}) 14. Qxb6 (14. Qb3 d6 $11) 14... axb6 15. b3 (15. Bc7!? Rc8 16. Bxb8 (16. Bxb6? Ra6 17. Bxc5 Bxc5 18. b4 Ra3 $19) 16... Rcxb8 (16... Raxb8 17. Ne5 Bxg2 18. Kxg2 d6 19. Nf3 {can be very similar to the game.}) 17. Ne5 Bxg2 18. Kxg2 d5 19. a3 {[%csl Rb6,Rc5] is an interesting try to stabilize Black's  on the dark squares. Black should probably refrain from} dxc4 (19... d4!? 20. Nb5 $13 {/}) 20. Nb5 c3 (20... Ra4 21. Rbc1) 21. bxc3 $36 (21. Nxc3 Rb7 22. Nb5 Ra5 23. Nd6 Bxd6 24. Rxd6 b5 {and Black gradually consolidates.})) (15. Bxb8 Rdxb8 {-15.c7 leads to the same position as the line above with one move less.}) 15... g5?! {Provocative and positionally risky.} ({Carlsen was probably getting ambitious, as much simpler is} 15... Ne4 $142 ! 16. Nxe4 Bxe4 17. Rb2 Nc6 $11 (17... d5 $11 {and Black has no problems whatsoever.})) ({Even} 15... d6 {is a more solid choice.}) 16. Bxb8!? {[%mdl 32]} ({Even here Kramnik could play} 16. Bc7 Rc8 17. Bxb8 {, but he prefers the direct swap. In the long run White's main strategic aim is to achieve a position with a strong  against a passive .} (17. Bd6 Kf8 18. a4 Bxd6 19. Rxd6 Ke7 20. Rbd1 Ne8 21. R6d3 d6 $11)) 16... Raxb8 (16... Rdxb8!? 17. Ne5 Bxg2 18. Kxg2 d5 {deserves attention, leading to a position from the note to White's 15-th move, only with the extra moves b3 and g5. It's not so easy to exploit this with White, as} 19. cxd5 (19. a4 Bd6 20. Nf3 g4 { e5}) 19... Nxd5 (19... exd5 20. a4! d4 21. Nb5 $36) 20. Nxd5 exd5 21. a4 (21. Rxd5 Rxa2 22. e4 Re8 $11) 21... d4 22. e3 dxe3 23. fxe3 b5 24. Rd7 Bf8 $132 {is not too impressive.}) 17. Ne5 Bxg2 18. Kxg2 Rbc8 {[%cal Yd7d6,Yd7d5] d6,d5} 19. e4 {Black had to loses a tempo to cover the c6-square, enabling White to prevent the immediate d5.} ({He needn't worry unduly about} 19. Na4 d6 20. Nxb6 Rb8!? (20... dxe5 21. Nxc8 Rxc8 $11) 21. Nc6 (21. Nd5 Nxd5 22. Nc6 Nb4 $15) 21... Rxb6 22. Nxd8 Bxd8 23. b4 cxb4 24. c5 Rb8 25. cxd6 Nd5 $11) 19... d6 20. Nd3 ({More accurate is} 20. Nf3! g4 21. Ne1 h5 22. Rd2 $14 ({or} 22. f4!? $14 {and White retains some pressure.})) 20... Kf8?! ({Black should have used his chance to play the active} 20... d5 $142 21. e5 Ne8 22. Nb5!? $13) 21. h3 (21. Nb2!? $14 {ba4,b5}) 21... Nd7 ({Black still could have gained space with} 21... d5 $142 !?) 22. f4! {[%csl Rg5,Rh6] Another drawback of Black's 15-th move is he loosened his .} Nb8 {[%cal Yb8c6,Yc6d4,Ye7f6] c6-d4,f6 This plan is too slow and doesn't achieve the desired result.} (22... Ra8 $142 23. Rd2 $14) 23. Na4 {Forces the  back.} (23. Nb5 Na6!? {c7} (23... Nc6 24. Nb2 {[%csl Rb6,Rd6] a4,d2,bd1,×b6,d6}) (23... d5 24. exd5 exd5 25. Ne5 $36)) 23... Nd7 $8 24. Nf2 $36 Ra8 25. Rd2 Ra5 {[%cal Yb6b5] b5 Black finds a way to chase away the nagging a4} (25... Rxa4? 26. bxa4 {[%cal Rf2d1,Rd1c3] d1-c3} Ra8 27. e5 $18) 26. Nc3 Nb8 (26... Bf6 27. Nb5 d5 28. exd5 exd5 29. Rxd5!? Rxa2 30. Rbd1 $16) 27. Rbd1 (27. a4 Nc6 28. Nb5 {stops Black's idea from the game, but allows} Na7 $14) 27... Nc6 28. Nb5 gxf4 (28... Nd4 29. Nxd4 cxd4 30. a4! (30. Rxd4 gxf4 31. gxf4 Rxa2 32. e5 Bh4 $11) 30... Bf6 31. Ng4 Bg7 32. Nh2 $14 {[%cal Yh2f3,Yf3e1,Ye1d3,Yd3b4] /,f3 will force Black to play e5, killing his . White can then proceed to transfer his  to the dominant square d5 (e1-d3-b4) and prepare either b4, or opening the .}) 29. gxf4 d5!? {Although this doesn't solve Black's problems, one can understand Carlsen's liberating attempt.} (29... Nd4 {is similar to 28...d4 and might be safer , but it offers little or no active .}) 30. exd5 exd5 31. cxd5 Rxb5 (31... Nd4!? 32. Nxd4 cxd4 33. Rxd4 Rxa2 34. R4d2 Rxd2 35. Rxd2 f5! 36. Nd1 Bc5 37. Kf3 Kf7 $16 {/}) 32. dxc6 Rc8 33. Ng4 Rxc6 (33... c4 34. bxc4 Ra5 35. Ne5 $16) 34. Ne5 $36 {[%csl Rb5,Ge5] White has achieved his strategic aim and his  dominates in the . This together with the misplaced b5 and vulnerable  makes the defence very difficult.} Re6 35. Kf3 (35. Nd7+ Ke8 36. f5 Re4 (36... Rc6 37. Ne5 Rc7 (37... Rf6 38. Nc4! {[%cal Rd1e1] e1+-}) 38. Re2 $40) 37. f6 Rd4! 38. Rxd4 cxd4 39. Rxd4 Bd8 $14 {[%csl Rd7,Rf6] ×f6,d7}) 35... Ra5? $138 (35... Rb4 $142 36. Rd5!? {} (36. Rd7 b5!? $14 {c4}) (36. Rg2?! Bf6 $11 {} 37. Nd7+ Ke7 38. f5 Ree4) 36... Bf6 37. Nc4 $16) 36. Rg2 {[%cal Gg1g8,Re5d7] g,d7} (36. Rg1 f5 (36... f6 37. Rg6 $18) 37. a4! $18) 36... f6 (36... Bf6 37. Nd7+ Ke7 38. f5 $18 {wins an exchange, as here the  can't go to e4.}) (36... f5 37. Rd7 {[%cal Rd7b7] b7+-}) 37. Nc4! {Again the strategic approach, Kramnik is in no hurry to win material and tightens the screws.} (37. Rg6 Rxa2 38. Rdg1 Rxe5 39. fxe5 fxe5 40. Rxb6 $18) 37... Ra7 (37... Ra8 38. f5 Rc6 39. a4! $18 {is similar.}) 38. f5 Rc6 39. a4! $18 {[%csl Rb6,Rc5,Re7,Rf6,Rh6] All Black's  are vulnerable and his miserable  has no moves.} Rb7 (39... Ke8 40. Rg7 Rac7 41. Rh7 Bf8 42. Re1+ (42. Rxc7 Rxc7 43. Nxb6 $18) 42... Kd8 43. Rxc7 Kxc7 44. Re8 Bd6 45. Ke4 $18) 40. Rg6 h5 (40... b5 41. Na5 $18) 41. Rdg1 {[%cal Rg6g8,Rg1g7]} Ke8 (41... Kf7 42. Ne5+ $18) 42. Ne5! {The  joins the  and wraps up the game.} Rc8 43. Rg8+ Bf8 44. Ng6 Rf7 45. Rd1 $22 {The most clinical solution.} ({After} 45. Rd1 Ra8 46. Rd6 Rb8 47. Rh8 {Black is fully paralysed and can't avoid heavy material losses.}) 1-0

