1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Bb4+ 4. Bd2 Bxd2+ 5. Qxd2 d5 6. g3 c6 7. Bg2 Nbd7 8. O-O dxc4?! {An unusual move that just looks bad.} ({The solid} 8... O-O {or 8...b6 are usually played.}) 9. a4 O-O 10. a5! $146 {This simple move gives White a clear advantage, and is immediately shown by the engine. Carlsen was definitely "trying" something over-the-board, difficult to believe this was his prep!} b5 11. axb6 Qxb6 12. Rc1 Rb8 13. Na3 Ba6 (13... Qxb2 14. Qxb2 Rxb2 15. Nxc4 Rxe2? 16. Bf1 Re4 17. Nd6 Rg4 18. h3 Rg6 19. Rxc6 $18) 14. Nxc4 Bxc4 15. Rxc4 Qxb2 16. Qxb2 Rxb2 17. Bf1 Rc8 18. Rxa7 g6 19. Rc1 h5 (19... c5 20. e4 {or 20.g4!? should be lost for Black - and if Black does not manage to get rid of his weakness by playing ...c5, his position is just bad.}) 20. e4 Rd8 21. Rxc6?! {Tempting, but objectively wrong!} (21. Ng5! Rb4 22. Bc4 {was a total domination, it seems:} Nh7 (22... c5 23. e5 Nh7 24. Nxf7! Kxf7 25. dxc5 $16) 23. Nxe6! Rxc4 24. Rxc4 fxe6 25. f3 $16 {A nice picture! The black knights do not manage to create any counterplay and Black is dominated.}) 21... Nxe4 22. Rcc7 Rxf2 23. Ng5 Nxg5 24. Kxf2 Nf6 25. Ke2 Nd5 (25... Rxd4 26. h4 Re4+! {was a forced draw:} 27. Kf2 (27. Kd3 Nf3! $11) 27... Ng4+ 28. Kg2 Ne3+ 29. Kh1 Nxf1 30. hxg5 Nxg3+ 31. Kg2 Rg4 32. Rxf7 Ne4+ 33. Kf1 Nxg5 $11) 26. Rd7?! (26. Rc2 $14) 26... Rc8! 27. h4 Rc2+ 28. Kd3 Nb4+?? {A very weird decision, although it is quite unlucky that is loses!} (28... Rc3+ $11) 29. Ke3 Rc3+ 30. Kf4! Rf3+ 31. Kxg5 Kg7 ({Is} 31... Rxf1? 32. Kh6! $18 {what the World Champion overlooked?}) 32. Bh3 Nd3 33. g4! {It could be this as well!} Nf2 34. gxh5 Nxh3+ 35. Kg4 Ng1 36. hxg6 Kxg6 37. h5+ Kg7 38. Ra1 1-0

