1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 d5 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 h6 7. Bh4 c5 8. dxc5 g5 9. Bg3 Ne4 10. e3 Qa5 11. Rc1 {A rare continuation, which recently became popular.} (11. Ne2 {or}) (11. Be5 {is usually played.}) 11... Nd7?! {This leads to an endgame, clearly better for White. More appropriate alternatives:} (11... Bf5 12. Bxb8! (12. Bd3 Nd7 13. f3 Nxc3 14. bxc3 Bxd3 15. Qxd3 Bxc5 $13) 12... Rxb8 13. Bd3 O-O 14. Ne2 Rfc8 15. O-O Rxc5 16. f3 Nxc3 17. bxc3 Bxd3 18. Qxd3 Ba3 19. Rb1 Qa4 20. Nd4 Rcc8 21. f4! $40 {1-0 Gupta,A (2644)-Yu Yangyi (2626)/Zaozhuang CHN 2012/The Week in Chess 915 (73)}) (11... Nc6!? 12. Bd3! Bxc3+ (12... Nxg3 13. hxg3 d4 14. exd4 Nxd4 15. Qd2 Be6 16. a3 Bxc5 17. b4 Qxa3 18. bxc5 O-O-O 19. Nb1 Qa4 $44 {0-1 Tozer,R (2380)-Lalic,S (2405)/London 1997/CBM 058 ext (33)}) 13. bxc3 Nxc5 14. Nf3 b6 15. h4 g4 16. Ne5 Nxe5 17. Bxe5 Nxd3+ 18. Qxd3 Ba6 19. Qf5 Rg8 $13 {1/2 Lomineishvili,M (2437)-Zaiatz,E (2364)/St Petersburg 2009/CBM 129 Extra (48)}) ({There is also nothing wrong with} 11... Qxa2 {, e.g.} 12. Bb5+ Bd7 13. Bxd7+ Nxd7 14. Ne2 Nxc3 15. Nxc3 Qc4 16. Rd1 (16. h4 O-O-O) 16... Nxc5 17. f3 Bxc3+ 18. bxc3 Ne6 19. Kf2 Rc8 20. Be5 Rg8 $13 {1/2 Finegold,B (2563)-Perelshteyn,E (2522)/Chicago 2006/EXT 2007 (46)}) 12. Ne2 Ndxc5 (12... Qxa2 13. Kd1 {1/2 Priborsky,J (2344)-Gordon,S (2508)/Liverpool 2008/CBM 126 Extra} Nxc3+ 14. Nxc3 Bxc3 15. Qxc3 O-O 16. h4 $16) 13. a3 Bxc3+ 14. Nxc3 Nxc3 15. Qxc3 Qxc3+ 16. Rxc3 Ne4 17. Rc7 $16 {[%csl Gc7,Rd5]} Nxg3 (17... O-O 18. Be2 $16 (18. Be5 $143 f6 19. Bd4 Rf7)) 18. hxg3 Rb8 19. Bb5+ Kf8 20. Kd2 (20. f4!? {may be more energetic.}) 20... Kg7 21. Rhc1 Rd8?! {This move is rather senseless.} (21... Be6 $142 {[%cal Yg7f6,Yf6e5,Ye5d6]} 22. Re7 Kf6 23. Rcc7 a6 24. Be2 b5 {Of course, Black's position remains very bad.}) 22. Re7 Kf6 23. Rcc7 Rf8 24. b4?! {There was no need to move queenside pawns at the moment.} (24. Be2 $142 ! {[%cal Ye2f3,Yf3d5,Re2h5]} g4 25. Bd1 {winning the d5-pawn.}) 24... a6 25. Bd3 a5 26. bxa5 ({Why not} 26. b5 {? There was no reason to break the well-known rule that pawn exchanges are favourable for the defender.}) 26... Ra8 27. Bb5 Rxa5 28. a4 Ra8 29. Kc3 h5! {Trying to create at least some counterplay.} 30. Kb4 h4 31. Re8? {[%mdl 16384] A strange decision letting Black's pieces out.} (31. Kc5 hxg3 32. fxg3 $16 {although Black now obtains some counterplay by means of} Be6 33. Rxb7 Ke5) 31... Rxe8 32. Bxe8 {[%mdl 4096]} Be6 33. Bb5 d4!? (33... hxg3 34. fxg3 Rb8 {[%cal Yf6f5,Yf5e4,Yf5g4] was possible, too.}) 34. gxh4 gxh4 35. exd4 Bd5 36. f3 Rg8 {Black's position is straightening up like a spring.} 37. Rc2 (37. Bf1 $142 {The rook should remain active. However, White can hardly convert his extra pawn in that case, too.}) 37... Ke7 38. a5 Kd6 39. Bc4 Bxc4 40. Rxc4? (40. Kxc4 Rg5! {must lead to a draw but White has nothing better in any case} ({GM A.Morozevich fancied under time pressure that the pawn ending after} 40... Rc8+?! 41. Kd3 Rxc2?! 42. Kxc2 Kc6 {was dangerous for him due to Black's outside passed b-pawn; that was not the case; on the contrary,} 43. Kd3 Kb5 44. Ke4 Kxa5 45. Kd5! {gave him winning chances})) 40... Rxg2 41. Rc5 h3 42. Rh5 h2 {[%csl Gh2] Black is simply better in this rook endgame; fortunately for White, the limited number of pawns enables him to hold his ground.} 43. Kc4 Rc2+ 44. Kd3 Ra2 45. Ke4 f5+ 46. Kf4 Kd5 47. Rxf5+ Kxd4 48. Rh5 Rg2 49. Rh7 Kd3 50. Ke5 Ke2 51. f4 Kf1 (51... Kf3 $144) 52. f5 Ra2 53. f6 Rxa5+ 54. Ke6 Ra6+ 55. Ke7 Rxf6 56. Kxf6 Kg1 57. Ke5 h1=Q 58. Rxh1+ Kxh1 59. Kd4 1/2-1/2

