[BlackElo "2826"]
[PlyCount "147"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Bf4 Bg7 5. e3 c5 6. dxc5 Qa5 7. Qb3 {Following Ivanchuk's success with the strange 4.e3 followed by 5.b3 against Svidler, he decides to take on another sideline. 7.c1 is by far the main choice.} Na6 {Attempting to take back on c5 with the  is very logical. This gives White the option to retain one of his pawns, but at a great cost.} 8. cxd5 Nxc5 9. Qb5+ Qxb5 10. Bxb5+ Bd7 $44 {White now has his extra pawn on d5, which isn't going to fall any time soon. However you can't stop evaluating there. The pawn IS weak is Black holds e4 under control, which means it can't be reinforced easily. Furthermore Black is ahead on development, and his  on g7 can easily begin pounding on the queenside with the help of a b5 push.} 11. Rd1 Nh5 12. Bxd7+ Kxd7 13. Bg3 (13. Nf3 {doesn't look pleasant but maybe it was more important to develop than to keep the pawn structure.} Nxf4 14. exf4 {[%cal Gf3e5]} Bxc3+!? 15. bxc3 f6 16. O-O Ne4 $44 {and Black is down a pawn but not worse.}) (13. Nge2 Nxf4 14. exf4 (14. Nxf4 b5! 15. Rd2 (15. Nxb5 Rhb8 16. Nd4 Rxb2 $15) 15... b4 16. Nd1 b3 {with plentiful compensation.})) 13... b5 {White's pieces on the kingside are too far away from the queenside action, and it's hard to believe that White can keep all his pawns alive. White should be thinking about equality.} 14. d6 e6 {Black's superior development, awesome blockade of the weak d6-pawn and queenside adctivity give him more than enough compensation for the pawn.} 15. Nf3 b4 16. Nb5 Nxg3 17. hxg3 Rab8 18. Nbd4 h5 19. Rh4 f5 {Black is using his structure to try to push the white pieces back. He is in no hurry to regain material as he knows that the d6-pawn is just doomed.} 20. Ne2 e5 21. Rc4 Rhc8 22. Kf1 Ne4 23. Rc7+ {The pawn was going to fall anyway, at least this way Ivanchuk gets rid of some active pieces.} Rxc7 24. dxc7+ Kxc7 25. Nd2 Nxd2+ 26. Rxd2 {Black has finally regained his material, and it is obvious that he has emerged on top. The  is considerably better than the , which at the moment lacks any good squares. Not to mention, the structure favors Black and White's  will be out of play for a few more moves at the very least. White is forced to be passive.} a5 27. b3 a4 28. bxa4 Ra8 29. Nc1 e4 30. Ne2 Rxa4 31. Nf4 {The  has gained f4, since the e-pawn was pushed to increase the s scope. This isn't a particular big gain, though, since g5 is still in the fray.} (31. Nd4 Kb6 32. Ke1 Be5 $15 {poses the strong threat of ...c5 and ...xd4.}) 31... Bc3 32. Rc2 Kd6 33. Ke2 (33. Nxg6?? b3 34. Rxc3 bxa2 35. Rc1 Rb4! $19) 33... g5 34. Nxh5 Kd5 {Black finds himself down a pawn again, but again the compensation is very obvious. The  on h5 is out of play and he has pressure on the weak a2, not to mention that White can't make use of his majority. The only rub in the position is that it isn't clear how to break through.} 35. f3 g4 (35... Kc4 36. g4 {creates very unnecessary complications, it is much better to lock down the pawn structure at once.}) 36. Nf4+ Kc4 37. Ne6 Ra6 38. Nf4 (38. Nd4 exf3+ 39. gxf3 Rh6 {looked very troubling for White, as now he doesn't control h3, which means his g3-pawn is a prime target.} 40. fxg4 (40. Nxf5? Rh2+ 41. Kd1 Rh1+ 42. Ke2 Re1+ 43. Kf2 Kd3 {and the rook is history.}) 40... fxg4 41. Kf1 Kd3 $17 {with a strong initiative against the dangerously corralled king.}) 38... Rh6 39. fxg4 fxg4 40. Rc1 (40. Kf2 {was the best way of holding the fortress, but it still seems like Black can create dangerous threats.} Rd6 41. Kf1 Kb5 42. a3 bxa3 43. Rxc3 a2 44. Rc1 Kb4 {Black will soon pick up the rook. All that is left to be seen is if Black can penetrate the  and  defense.} 45. Ra1 Kb3 46. Rxa2 Kxa2 47. Nh5 Kb2 48. Nf4 Kc2 49. Ke2 Rd2+ 50. Ke1 {However I personally don't see how.}) 40... Ra6 (40... Rd6 41. Rc2 Kb5 42. a3 bxa3 43. Rxc3 a2 44. Ra3 Ra6 45. Rxa2 Rxa2+ {leads to the same kind of ending as before. Which I think is a draw.}) 41. Rc2 Rh6 42. Rc1 Rd6 43. Rc2 Ra6 44. Kf2 Rh6 45. Ke2 Rh1 46. Ng6 Re1+ (46... b3 47. Ne5+ Kd5 48. Rxc3 bxa2 49. Ra3 a1=Q 50. Rxa1 Rxa1 51. Nxg4 {is quite definitely drawn.}) 47. Kf2 Ra1 48. Nf4 Rb1 49. Ne6 Rd1 50. Nf4 Rd6 51. Ke2 Rc6? {After much shuffling, I think this is where Magnus missed some serious winning chances.} (51... Kb5 52. a3 (52. Kf2 Ka4 53. Kf1! (53. Ne2? Rd2 $19) 53... Rd2?! (53... Ka3 54. Ke2 Bb2 55. Rc4 Bc3 56. Rxe4 Rd2+ 57. Kf1 Kxa2 {And it may be that this is still a draw after c4! but it is not easy. White has had to find a lot of exact moves.}) 54. Rxd2 Bxd2 55. Ke2 Bc3 56. Kd1 Ka3 57. Kc2 Kxa2 58. Nd5 $11) 52... bxa3 53. Rxc3 a2 54. Rc1 Kb4 {We simply get to the same endgame we have seen previously. However at this point Black had to try to enter this endgame.}) 52. Rc1 Kb5 53. Nd5 Rc5 54. Nf4 Rc6 55. Nd5 Rc5 56. Nf4 Ka4 57. Ne6 {As opposed to the other line, the  comes back quickly into the game, and Black doesn't even win the exchange.} Rd5 58. Rc2 Ka3 59. Nd4 Rc5 60. Nb3 Rd5 61. Nd4 Bxd4 62. Rd2 {Black must surrender the exchange or he will stand much worse if not just lost after exd4.} Bc3 $11 63. Rxd5 Kxa2 64. Kd1 b3 65. Rb5 Kb2 66. Rb8 Bf6 67. Kd2 Bc3+ 68. Kd1 Bf6 69. Rf8 Bc3 70. Re8 Ka2 71. Ra8+ Kb1 72. Ra4 b2 73. Ra6 Ba5 {Because Black is Magnus. He plays cool moves.} 74. Rxa5 {Obviously Black had run out of ways to try to make progress, and there was no way that White was going to have time to pick up any pawns. Magnus had been pushing for the entire game, and he might have had chances, but was unable to finish it off at the end.} 1/2-1/2

