{[%evp 0,81,26,23,29,-3,3,3,4,10,33,33,34,19,10,14,18,17,-8,12,18,-23,-20,-32,-34,-35,-38,-54,-9,-29,-20,-41,12,-82,-68,-67,-39,-37,-39,-74,-62,-21,-16,-21,-5,-19,3,-261,-48,-106,0,0,25,25,31,119,159,130,123,109,109,82,112,112,112,24,0,9,58,78,80,0,45,-6,349,74,204,177,233,237,446,446,29997,29998]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Bd3 c5 6. Ne2 d5 7. a3 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 dxc4 9. Bxc4 Qc7 10. Ba2!? {Lately, this move has become more popular than 10.d3.} b6 11. O-O Ba6 12. Bb2 Nc6 13. Rc1!? {White wants to push c4 and d5.} Rac8 ({I believe} 13... Rfd8 {is critical, and I couldn't find what Magnus really wanted there:} 14. c4 cxd4 15. exd4 b5!? (15... Ng4 16. Ng3 Qf4 17. h3 Nf6 18. Ne2 Qh4 19. d5!? exd5 20. cxd5 Ne7 21. Re1 Nexd5 22. Nd4 Bb7 23. Nf3 Qh6 24. Ne5 Rf8 25. Nc6 $14 Bxc6 26. Rxc6 Rad8 27. Qf3 Qd2 28. Re2 Qf4? 29. Rxf6! gxf6 30. Bxd5 $18 {1-0 (49) Carlsen,M (2832)-Aronian,L (2793) Leuven 2017}) 16. d5 bxc4 17. Bxc4 (17. Bxf6 Rxd5 {isn't supposed to work for White.}) ({I've been wondering for a while whether the World Champion was willing to try this sort of crazyness:} 17. Qa4? Qa5!! 18. Qxc6 Rac8 19. Bxf6 gxf6 20. Nd4 c3 {but it is too bad for White according to the engine - even for a rapid game.}) 17... Bxc4 18. Rxc4 exd5 19. Rc2 Qd6 20. Ng3 {This position is more tricky than it looks, and White does have enough compensation, but it's hard to believe he's better.} d4 (20... Rac8!? 21. Nf5 Qe6 {looks difficult to refute to me.}) 21. Nf5 Qf4 (21... Qd5!? 22. Qd3 Ne4 23. Rxc6 Qxf5 $11) 22. Nxg7 Ne5?! (22... Rd6!?) 23. Nh5 Nf3+ 24. gxf3 Nxh5 25. Rc4! $16 {½-½ (61) Tregubov,P (2584)-Gharamian,T (2626) Chartres 2017}) 14. c4 cxd4 15. exd4 Qe7 16. d5!? {Sacrificing a pawn to open all the files, in the spirit of the position!} (16. Re1 Na5 17. c5!? bxc5 18. d5! Nxd5 19. Bxd5 exd5 20. Nf4 Qb7 21. Rb1 {was another good try.}) 16... exd5 17. Re1! Bxc4 (17... dxc4 18. Ng3 Qd7 {may have been a better way to force several exchanges, as} 19. Qf3 {is met with} Qd3 $132) 18. Ng3 Qd8 19. Bb1!? {Playing two pawns down, but with a bishop pair! That's modern chess...} (19. Bxc4 dxc4 20. Rxc4 {should be about equal.}) 19... b5! 20. Nf5 d4 21. Qd2 Be6 (21... Re8!? {looked like a safer bet to me, not to let the c1-rook invade Black's position.}) 22. Rc5!? a6 (22... Bxf5 23. Bxf5 Qd6 24. Rec1 Nd7 25. Rxb5 {would definitely offer White (at least) enough compensation.}) 23. Nxg7! Kxg7 24. Qg5+ (24. Bc1!? {would give Black an extra option of playing} Ng8 {but after} 25. Qd3 Nf6 26. Qg3+ Kh8 27. Qh4 Rg8 28. Bg5 Rxg5 29. Rxg5 {Black seems to be in trouble.}) 24... Kh8 25. Qh4 Rg8?! ({After} 25... Qe7 {the engine gives an incredible move for White to be better:} 26. Rc3!! (26. Rxc6 Rxc6 27. Bxd4?? {fails to} Bf5!! 28. f3 Re6 $19) 26... Rg8 27. Rxc6 (27. Rf3 Rg4 $132) 27... Rxc6 28. Bxd4 Bf5 29. f3! {The difference with the rook on g8 and not f8!} Qd8 30. Bxf5 $16) 26. Rxc6! Rxc6 27. Bxd4 Kg7 (27... Bf5 28. Bxf5 Rg7 29. g3 $16) 28. Qxh7+ Kf8 29. Qh6+ Ke8 30. Bxf6 Qa5 31. Qe3 {Now White is just winning.} Qb6 32. Rd1? {This imprecise move gives Black some chances to save the game.} (32. Be4!? Rd6 33. Qf3 {and Black is paralysed. Next may be e5 or just h4.}) 32... Rd6 33. Bd4 Qc6 34. Be4 Qc4 35. h3 Kd7 36. Rd2 Re8? (36... Rc8! {and although the position is highly unpleasant for Black, it isn't so clear.}) 37. Kh2 (37. Be5! {was winning at once:} Rxd2 (37... Bd5 38. Bf5+ Kc6 39. Rc2 $18) 38. Qa7+! Kd8 39. Bf6+ Kc8 40. Qb7#) 37... Bd5 (37... Rc8 38. h4 $16 {/+-}) 38. Bf5+ Be6 39. Bd3 Qa4 40. Be5! {Ths time it's obvious!} Rd5 41. Qa7+ 1-0

