{[%evp 0,71,26,14,25,19,5,0,5,17,17,19,26,17,25,-17,5,-22,-22,-4,-2,-4,-8,-11,10,-3,9,7,9,20,20,-17,-14,-13,28,-1,51,50,50,44,53,19,38,34,45,45,41,35,49,46,37,52,52,89,88,74,185,198,308,256,329,315,336,216,305,300,297,304,557,573,646,661,633,645]} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. O-O Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. c3 h6 7. Re1 O-O 8. Nbd2 a5 {The latest trend against the Italian: playing with ...a5.} 9. Nf1 Be6 {One of the many critical moves. All the moves below deserve investigation!} ({is OK for Black according to the recent games. Surely Carlsen had some idea in mind...} 9... a4 10. Bb5!? (10. h3 Ra5 11. d4 Bb6 12. b4?! (12. Ng3 exd4 13. cxd4 Nxe4! 14. Nxe4 d5 $132) 12... axb3 13. Bxb3 Ra8 14. Ng3 Re8 15. Bb2 Bd7 16. Qc2 Nh7 17. Rad1 Ng5 $13 {½-½ (38) Ding Liren (2791)-Nakamura,H (2736) chess.com 2020}) 10... a3 11. b4 Bb6 12. h3 (12. Be3!?) 12... Ne7 13. Ba4 Ng6 14. Bc2 d5 15. Bd2 (15. Ng3 dxe4 16. dxe4 c6 $11) 15... dxe4 16. dxe4 Be6 17. Bb3 Qd7 18. c4 Bd4 (18... c5! 19. b5 Rfd8 {and it seems White never manages to bring a knight to d5.}) 19. Nxd4 Qxd4 20. Qc2 Rfd8 21. Rad1 Qb6 22. Ne3 $16 {1-0 (31) Duda,J (2730)-Giri,A (2780) chess.com 2019}) (9... Ne7 10. Bb3 Ng6 11. Ng3 Re8 (11... c6 12. d4 Bb6 13. h3 Re8 14. Be3 exd4 15. Bxd4 $36 {0-1 (65) Aronian,L (2772)-Nakamura,H (2741) Kolkata 2019}) 12. h3 Be6 13. Ba4 c6 14. d4 Bb6 15. Bc2 Bc7 16. Bd2 Qe7 17. a4 Rad8 18. Bd3 Qf8 19. Qc2 Nf4 20. Bf1 Ng6 21. c4 $14 {½-½ (52) Giri,A (2764)-Nakamura,H (2736) chess24.com 2020}) (9... Re8 10. h3 Be6 11. Bb5 Bd7 12. Ng3 Bb6 13. Ba4 Ne7 14. Bc2 Ng6 15. d4 c5 16. d5 (16. dxe5!? dxe5 17. Ba4 $36) 16... c4 17. a4 Rc8 18. Rb1 Qc7 19. Nf1 Nf4 20. Be3 g5? (20... Bxe3 21. Nxe3 Qb6 $13) 21. N3d2 $16 {½-½ (40) Duda,J (2730)-Nakamura,H (2745) chess.com 2019}) 10. Bb5 Ne7 (10... Bb6 11. Ng3 Ne7 12. h3 Nd7 13. d4 $14 {1-0 (66) Ding Liren (2791)-Nakamura,H (2736) chess.com 2020}) 11. d4 exd4 12. cxd4 Bb6 13. Ng3 d5 14. e5 Ne4 15. Bd3!? (15. Be3 Nf5 16. Nxf5 Bxf5 17. Nd2 Nxd2 18. Qxd2 a4 $11 {½-½ (43) Carlsen,M (2863)-Nakamura,H (2736) chess24.com 2020}) 15... Nxg3 16. hxg3 a4 $146 (16... Bg4 {was played in the next encounter between the same players, just the day after! It may solve Black's problems:} 17. Bc2 (17. Be3!?) 17... c5 18. Be3 cxd4 (18... c4!?) 19. Bxd4 Rc8 20. Rc1 Rc4 21. Bxb6 (21. Bc3!?) 21... Qxb6 22. b3 Rc7 23. Bb1 Rfc8 24. Rxc7 Qxc7 $11 {½-½ (44) Carlsen,M (2863)-Nakamura,H (2736) chess24.com 2020}) 17. Bc2 a3!? (17... Qd7!? {followed by ...f5 or ...c5 looked more logical to me.}) 18. bxa3 {A pawn is a pawn.} (18. b4!?) 18... Bg4 19. a4 Qd7 20. Ba3 Rfe8 21. Bxe7! {A great exchange, as that knight was very important to defend Black's king.} Qxe7 22. Qd3 g6 23. Nh4 {White's position looks more pleasant, but after a move like 23...d7 Black should be more or less fine.} Qb4? (23... c5? 24. Nxg6 $18) (23... Ba5!? {followed by ...c5 also made sense.}) 24. Nxg6 Qxd4 25. Ne7+! {What a beautiful move!} Kf8 $8 (25... Rxe7? 26. Qh7+ Kf8 27. Qh8#) 26. Nxd5 Qxf2+? (26... Rxe5 27. Nxb6 Qxd3 28. Bxd3 Rxe1+ 29. Rxe1 cxb6 30. Bb5 $16) 27. Kh2 {Now White's attack is irresistible.} Rad8 (27... Qd4 28. Qh7! Qxd5 29. Bb3 Qd7 30. Rf1 Be6 31. Rad1 $18) 28. Rf1 Rxe5 (28... Qd4 29. Rxf7+ Kxf7 30. Qg6+ Kf8 31. Rf1+ $18) 29. Rxf2 Rdxd5 30. Rxf7+! Ke8 (30... Kxf7 31. Rf1+ Ke7 32. Qh7+ Kd6 33. Qg6+ Be6 34. g4 $18) 31. Qxd5!? Rxd5 32. Bg6 Kd8 33. Re1 c6 34. Rxb7 Bc7 35. Re8+ Kd7 36. Rh8 1-0

