{[%evp 0,117,26,-30,-30,-47,-54,-79,-44,-34,-55,-62,-54,-163,13,-68,-2,-30,-12,-67,-34,-40,-33,-36,-36,-44,-42,-54,-25,-41,13,-65,-75,-59,-48,-44,-44,-80,-69,-80,46,-11,60,67,117,117,126,126,129,58,58,47,58,16,53,22,41,-3,16,12,13,13,22,20,30,44,106,3,13,23,75,32,92,67,58,23,75,75,73,86,127,143,111,111,137,137,214,128,128,128,182,111,205,197,229,186,189,188,188,178,212,185,200,192,199,192,225,195,203,236,289,323,315,322,370,307,374,823,815,838]} 1. c4 e5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. O-O Nb6 7. b3!? Be6!? {One of the many possible moves, together with 7...e7, 7...d6, 7...g6.} 8. Qc2 $146 {Magnus Carlsen had more success with his tries on the two following days:} (8. Nc3 {16th of August!} Qd7 9. d4 exd4 10. Nb5 d3 11. Bb2 f6 12. Nbd4 {(definitely an opening success for White)} Nxd4 13. Nxd4 Bd5 14. Bxd5 (14. Qxd3!? $14) (14. e4!? $14) 14... Qxd5 15. Qxd3 c5 16. Qb5+ Qd7 17. Qxd7+ Kxd7 18. Nf5 Ke6 19. e4 $14 {½-½ (73) Carlsen,M (2863)-Nakamura,H (2736) chess24.com 2020}) (8. Bb2 f6 9. Nc3 Qd7 10. Qc1!? {17th of August!} Nd4?! (10... O-O-O!?) 11. Nxd4 exd4 12. Ne4 O-O-O 13. a4 d3 14. a5 Nd5 15. Nc5 Bxc5 16. Qxc5 Kb8 17. Bd4 b6 18. axb6 cxb6 19. Qa3 dxe2 20. Rfe1 Rc8 21. Rxe2 $16 {½-½ (63) Carlsen,M (2863)-Nakamura,H (2736) chess24.com 2020}) 8... Qd7 9. Rd1 O-O-O 10. Nc3 f6 (10... Nd4?! 11. Qe4 $14) 11. d4 (11. Bb2 Nd4 12. Nxd4 exd4 13. Ne4 d3 $132) 11... exd4 12. Bb2 (12. Be3 dxe3 13. Rxd7 exf2+ 14. Kf1 Rxd7 $44) 12... Bf5 (12... Kb8!?) 13. Qc1 (13. Ne4 d3 14. exd3 Kb8 $132) 13... d3! {Black's position just seems fine.} 14. exd3?! (14. e4!? Bh3 $13) 14... Bh3 15. Bh1 Bg4 16. d4 Bb4 (16... Kb8!?) 17. d5! {White should sac a pawn if he wants to be active.} (17. a3 Bxc3 18. Qxc3 Be6 $132) 17... Bxc3 18. Qxc3 Nxd5 19. Qc4 Be6 20. Nd4 Bf7? {Blundering the pawn back - bad news now that the position is open, as White has the bishop pair.} (20... Nxd4 {followed by ...b8 or ...c6 should be about equal.}) 21. Nxc6 Qxc6 22. Qg4+ Qe6 23. Qxg7 $16 Rhg8 24. Qh6 Qf5 25. Bg2 Kb8 26. Rd4!? {A nice way to reorganise White's pieces.} Rde8 27. Qd2 c6 28. b4 Qh5 29. Re1 {A typical Magnus move - exchanging pieces to win a better endgame.} ({Nevertheless,} 29. Bf1! {preventing ...e2/...e2, and threatening c1 and b5, was stronger.}) 29... Rxe1+ 30. Qxe1 Re8 31. Re4 Rxe4 32. Bxe4 Bg6 33. Bg2 Qf5 34. a3 Qd3 (34... Qb1 35. Qxb1 Bxb1 36. Bxd5 cxd5 37. Bxf6 Kc7 38. Bd4 b6 39. g4 Kc6 40. f4 Kb5 41. Kf2 Kc4 42. Ke3 {should be an easy win for White.}) (34... Kc7 {to try to enter a better version of the opposite-coloured bishops endgame was perhaps the best chance.}) 35. h4 Kc7 36. Kh2 Qb1 37. Qd2! ({In the event of} 37. Qxb1 Bxb1 38. Bxd5 cxd5 39. Bxf6 Kc6 {things would be very different than after 34...b1: the black king is very active and the white king is far away.}) 37... Be4 38. Bxf6! {A nice tactics, winning a pawn.} Bxg2 39. Kxg2 Kb6 (39... Qe4+ 40. f3 $18) 40. Bd4+ Kb5 41. Bxa7 Ka4 42. Bc5 b5 43. Qe2 (43. h5!? Kxa3 44. g4 $18) 43... Kxa3 44. Qe6 Qd3?! (44... Ka4 {was the last chance to complicate matters, but White should be winning anyway:} 45. Qxc6 Nxb4 46. Qa8+ Kb3 47. Qg8+ Ka4 48. Qg4 Qb3 49. Qd4 Qd5+ 50. Qxd5 Nxd5 51. f4 b4 52. Kf3 b3 53. Bd4 Kb4 54. Bb2 Nb6 55. Ke4 Nc4 56. Bd4 $18) 45. Qxc6 Kb3 46. Qe6 Qc4 47. Qf5 Nxb4 48. Bxb4 Kxb4 49. Qxh7 Qd5+ 50. Kh2 Ka3 51. Qa7+ Kb2 52. Qg7+ Ka2 53. Qg5 Qd3 54. Qc5 Qe2 55. h5 Qb2 56. h6 b4 57. h7 Qf6 58. Kg2 b3 59. Qa7+ 1-0

