1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 Be7 5. Bf4 O-O 6. e3 Nbd7 7. Be2 dxc4 8. O-O (8. Bxc4 {is less precise, as after} a6 9. a4 c5 10. d5? {Black would have} Nb6 $132) 8... a6!? {Nakamura's favourite move, while other players prefer 8...c5 or 8...Nb6.} 9. a4 Nd5 (9... c5 10. d5! {is supposed to give White a slight advantage.}) 10. Bg3 {Almost a novelty!} (10. Bxc4 Nxf4 11. exf4 c5 12. dxc5 Bxc5 13. Qe2 {was the game Ding Liren (2801)-Aronian,L (2775) London 2019, 1-0 (35), game which I definitely advise to see if you haven't!}) 10... c5 $146 ({The only high level game played in that position continued} 10... b6 11. Bxc4 Nxc3 12. bxc3 Bb7 13. Bd3 Bd6 {and Black was close to equality: Navara,D (2734)-Ivanchuk,V (2726) Berlin 2015, ½-½ (65).}) 11. Bxc4 cxd4 12. exd4 N7b6!? (12... N7f6 {was the alternative.}) 13. Bb3 a5 14. Ne5 Bd7 15. Ne4 Bc6?! (15... Rc8 {followed by ...Nb4 seemed quite fine for Black.}) 16. Nxc6 bxc6 17. Rc1 Nb4 18. Qe2! {Thanks to the bishop pair, White has a slight advantage. Now he manages to link his rooks.} N6d5 (18... Qxd4?? 19. Rfd1 $18) 19. Rfd1?! (19. Rfe1 {may have been a better move against possible ...f5 complications.}) 19... Re8?! (19... f5! 20. Nd6! Qd7! (20... f4 21. Qxe6+ Kh8 22. Nf7+ Rxf7 23. Qxf7 fxg3 24. hxg3 $14) 21. Nc4 $8 f4 22. Nb6 $8 Qd6 23. Nxa8 fxg3 24. hxg3 Rxa8 25. Re1 $13 {Awesome line!}) 20. Qf3 (20. h3!? {making space for the g3-bishop also made sense.}) 20... Ra7 (20... f5!? {again deserved attention.}) 21. Be5 {Magnus definitely wants Hikaru to move his f-pawn, to create a weakness on e6.} Bf8 {But Nakamura doesn't want to do it!} ({However, after} 21... f6 22. Bg3 f5 23. Nc5 Bxc5 (23... Bg5!?) 24. Rxc5 f4 25. Bxf4 Rf7 {the position looks unclear.}) 22. Qg3 f6 23. Bd6 Bxd6 24. Nxd6 {Now that these bishops are gone, White has a stable plus!} Rf8 25. Ne4!? {Keeping as many pieces as possible.} (25. Re1!? Nd3 26. Qxd3 Qxd6 27. Rc5 $14) (25. Rc5!?) 25... Qb8 26. Qf3 Re8 27. g3 Kh8 28. Nc5 Rae7 29. Bc4 e5 {A natural follow-up, although Black was solid if he didn't touch anything.} 30. dxe5 Qxe5 31. Rd2 Qg5?! (31... Nb6! {seemed more urgent than leaving the e-file. Still, after} 32. Bf1 {White is a bit better.}) 32. Nb3! {Now Black has problems with his a5-pawn.} Nb6 33. h4 Qe5 34. Re2 Qc7 35. Rxe7 Qxe7 36. Nxa5 Qe5 {The best practical chance - let's remind that this game was an Armageddon!} (36... Nxa4 37. b3 Nc5 38. Nxc6 {would be too easy for White.}) 37. Nb3? (37. Bf7! Rf8 38. Nxc6 Nxc6 39. Qxc6 {was the most direct win.}) (37. Nxc6!? {worked as well:} Qxb2 38. Rd1 Nxc4 39. Qe4!! {collecting the piece back!}) 37... Nxa4 38. Bf7 Rf8 39. Rc4 Rxf7 40. Rxb4?? {What a blunder!} (40. Re4 {(or 40.Qe4, or even 40.Qg4) was about equal. But Magnus needed to win since that game, as I've just mentioned, was an Armageddon.}) 40... Qe1+ {Now it's all over.} 41. Kh2 Qxb4 42. Qxc6 Rf8 0-1

