{[%evp 0,103,14,30,23,3,-4,6,6,10,13,2,26,-2,50,27,27,12,35,42,48,58,24,-1,-14,0,-12,-27,15,1,23,0,35,25,37,36,40,36,35,34,42,19,44,45,26,18,43,43,37,42,35,37,46,57,56,46,63,61,74,81,60,69,68,65,88,50,63,60,50,52,138,133,154,143,199,153,169,163,152,209,201,197,236,154,213,162,166,166,199,218,268,310,310,619,653,675,623,699,815,1028,1142,1152,1242,1272,1396,1568]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 Be7 5. Bf4 O-O 6. e3 Nbd7 7. Be2 dxc4 8. O-O c5 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. Bxc4 a6 11. Ng5!? b5? ({Later, at the Nations Cup, Nakamura improved on his play with} 11... h6 12. Nge4 Be7 13. a4 Nxe4 14. Nxe4 Nf6 {and eventually made a draw against Maxime Vachier-Lagrave. Still, even in that game he was suffering:} 15. Qf3! (15. Nd6 Bd7! $132) 15... Bd7 16. Nxf6+ Bxf6 17. Qxb7 Qc8 18. Qb3 Ra7 (18... Bxb2 19. Qxb2 Qxc4 20. Rfd1 Bxa4 21. Bxh6! gxh6? 22. Rd4 {could have been a fun way for the game to finish}) 19. Qa2?! {White had several good tries instead.} (19. Rac1!?) (19. Rab1!?) (19. e4!?) 19... Bxb2 20. Rab1 Bf6 21. Rfc1 Qa8 22. a5 Rc8 23. Bf1 Rxc1 24. Rxc1 Bb5 $11 {½-½ (31) Vachier Lagrave,M (2778)-Nakamura,H (2736) chess.com 2020}) 12. Bxe6! fxe6 13. Nxe6 Qe7 (13... Qe8 {isn't satisfactory for Black either:} 14. Nxf8 Bxf8 15. Bd6 Bb7 16. Bxf8 Nxf8 (16... Qxf8 17. Qd4 $16) 17. a4 b4 18. Qb3+ Ne6 19. Qxb4 Rb8 20. Qc4 (20. Qh4!?) 20... Rc8 21. Qb3 {(bringing his queen to the wrong side of the board allowed Black to take the initiative)} Rb8 22. Qc2 Ng5 23. Rae1 Bf3!! 24. Qf5 Qh5 25. h4 Qxh4 26. Qf4 Nh3+ 27. gxh3 Qxh3 28. Qxb8+ Kf7 29. Qc7+ Kg8 $11 {½-½ (36) Wojtaszek,R (2725)-Andreikin,D (2724) Jerusalem 2019}) 14. Nxf8 Qxf8 15. Ne4! {White is going to grab the bishop pair and will have a better position thanks to his fantastic centre, especially once he manages to push f3 and e4.} Bb7 (15... Nxe4? 16. Qd5+ $18) (15... Be7 16. Nd6 $16) 16. Nxc5 Qxc5 17. Rc1! Qd5 18. f3! {White gives away one of his pawns to take a huge initiative.} Qxa2 19. e4 Nf8 (19... Qxb2? 20. Rc7 $18) 20. Rf2 Rc8 21. Rxc8 Bxc8 22. Qd8 Qe6 23. Bd6 Qe8 (23... Ne8 24. Bb4 $16) 24. Qxe8 Nxe8 25. Bb4! {The b4-bishop dominates the black queenside pawns, and White is superior and not worse on the kingside.} Ne6 26. Rd2 Kf7 27. Kf2 Nf6 28. Ke3 g5 29. Rd6 Nd7 30. g3 {Just a lesson in technique. Black has two knights, but he can't do much with them.} Ne5 31. b3 h5 32. h4! gxh4 33. gxh4 Ng6 34. Be1 {Calmly.} a5 35. Rd5! {White is not in a hurry to capture the a5-pawn (at all!).} a4 36. Rxh5 Nef4 (36... axb3 37. Rxb5 $18) 37. Rg5! (37. Rxb5 Ng2+ 38. Kf2 Nxe1 39. Kxe1 Nxh4 40. Kf2 axb3 41. Rxb3 {may also win, as Black shouldn't be able to sac one of his pieces for the two white pawns, but there was no reason for White to enter such an endgame.}) 37... axb3 38. h5 Nf8 (38... b2? 39. hxg6+ Nxg6 40. Rxb5 $18) 39. Bc3 {Black has taken another pawn on the queenside, but the result remains the same: the white bishop blocks them all!} ({Of course not} 39. Kxf4?? Ne6+ $19) 39... b4 40. Bb2 N8e6 (40... N4e6 41. Rb5 $18) 41. Rf5+ Kg8 42. Rxf4! {The right choice - the opposite coloured bishops endgame is hopeless for Black.} (42. Ra5!?) 42... Nxf4 43. Kxf4 Ba6 (43... Kh7 44. Ke5 Kh6 45. f4 Kxh5 46. f5 $18) 44. Kg5 Bd3 45. Kg6 Bb5 46. f4 Be8+ 47. Kg5 Bc6 48. e5 Bd5 49. f5 Kh7 50. e6 Bc4 51. Kf6 Be2 52. Ke7 1-0

