{[%evp 0,62,30,33,33,22,19,19,19,19,26,11,12,3,7,-19,-18,-10,-13,-109,-51,-51,-37,-79,-69,-54,-46,-50,-12,-32,-22,-40,-12,-39,-41,-23,-23,-46,-46,-30,-25,-32,-29,-39,-39,-29,-29,-27,-34,-44,-31,-35,-42,-23,-51,-51,-24,-34,-37,-35,-37,-101,-21,-12,-9]} {Many feared it, some hoped for it: an Armageddon game deciding a top event. Months of chess has preluded, 4 events ending in a superfinal. 3-3 in sets, 3-3 in the final, 7th deciding set. The next 9 minutes have to define the winner. Just or not, it is well known from other sports, and defines iconic moments. Chess differs in the sense that while soccer has to do a penalty shootout, we can actually just play our normal game, only faster. Still it would be wrong just to see it as a normal chess game, or judge it by engine analysis. It is 60 000 dollars, and the year's perhaps most prestigious title at stake. 2 players sitting in different continents, in front of a computer screen:} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6!? {The move is well-known, the !? is because it took 15 seconds for the World Champion to execute it. Apparently he was unhappy with his computer setup, and like a formula-one driver decided to sacrifice valuable seconds with a "pit-stop", making the proper adjustments for optimising further performance.} 4. d3 Bc5 5. Bxc6 dxc6 6. Nbd2 Be6 {In previous games Magnus had chosen 6...0-0 but having lost the crucial game 3 earlier in the day allowing Nakamura to equal the score, he plays a more common-sense based setup.} 7. h3 {His choice against 6...0-0 but much less played after 6...e6. A "pre-move" perhaps? Trying to gain valuable seconds.} Nd7 8. Nf1 f6 9. g4 Qe7 10. Ng3 O-O-O 11. Qe2 Kb8 12. a3 Bb6 13. Be3 Nf8 14. O-O-O Ng6 15. Nh5 Bxe3+ 16. Qxe3 c5 17. Nd2 b6 18. Nb1 (18. f4 Nxf4 19. Nxf4 exf4 20. Qxf4 h5 {would leave Black in a very comfortable position, but more important, force events which very much favours the player with draw-odds.}) 18... Nh4 19. Rhg1 g5 {One could see it as a tragic situation for Nakamura. He is in a must-win situation, but Magnus has practically blocked all his pawn breaks. But that is of very little practical value in this format. White can move around practically forever, and time will become the overriding factor. In fact it is Magnus who has an incentive to speed up procedings, trying to capitalise on his positional edge.} 20. Nc3 Ng6 21. Kb1 Nf4 22. Nxf4 gxf4 23. Qe2 h5 24. f3 hxg4 25. hxg4 Rh3 26. Rg2 Rdh8 27. Rdg1 Rh1 28. b3 c4 {In some ways an Armageddon game feels closer to football than chess. The attacker has to choose to either run to the flank, or up the middle in a split-second. The choice will be made on instincts, and sometimes you score, sometimes not.... In chess we can analyse due to comptures, and can thus conclude that 28...d6 heading for d4 was the better move, but it is easy to understand Magnus' choice, getting his queen close to the opponent king feels right.} 29. dxc4 Qxa3 30. g5?? ({Trying to "box-out" with} 30. Qf1 {taking control of the first rank was the correct way.}) {But Nakamura having been patient for so long, becomes active a bit too early.} 30... fxg5?? {Staying with the football analogy, Magnus misses an open chance. In a classical chess of course it wouldnt be a problem to sit down and calculate, but again here we are talking decisions made in seconds, basically based on "guesses" When like in football some times you hit, sometimes you miss. 30...xg1+! 31 xg1 fxg5 would have practically decided the issue, the main point being that 32 xg5 g1+ 33 d1 wins for Black in numerous way, but even worse, 33...d6! 34 c1 a3+! forces a repetition.} (30... Rxg1+! 31. Rxg1 fxg5 {would have practically decided the issue, the main point being that} 32. Rxg5 Rh1+ 33. Nd1 {wins for Black in numerous way, but even worse,} Qd6! 34. Kc1 Qa3+! {forces a repetition.}) 31. Rxg5 a5 32. Qd3 {Momentum has shifted. Nothing is wrong with Black's position, but White suddenly can manoeuvre more freely. Black's problem is that to attack via the 1st rank he needs to challenge the control of the g1 square. 32...c5 achieves that, but in exchange loses the control of the b2 square, allowing the simple reply 33 xh1 xh1+ 34 b2.} Kb7 (32... Qc5 33. Rxh1 Rxh1+ 34. Kb2) 33. Rg7 {Nakamura starts making threats to the opponent's "goal", with 34 xc7 followed by 35 b5+ forking the king and queen as the obvious threat.} Rxg1+ 34. Rxg1 Rh7 35. Na4! {The computers might say 0.00 but a white plan is starting to materialise. The queen will go to c3, and the knight via b2 to d3. Instead of just having to play simple moves, Magnus has to find a concrete defence. Being behind on the clock as well, it was not at all clear who was the favourite at the time.} Qd6 36. Qc3 Bd7 37. Nb2 Rh3 38. Rd1 Qe7 39. Nd3?! {Nakamura consistently executes his plan, but perhaps like Magnus earlier the move reflects being a guess, rather than having paused to think.} (39. c5!? {would have put a lot of pressure. As d7 is hanging Black cannot take with the queen, and cxb6 followed by c4 is a threat. Black can fight with} Bb5 ({or} 39... Bg4!? {, but it is considerably easier to play White, as well as the advantage also is of an objective nature.})) {Now momentum shifts again, this time Magnus takes his chance when he gets it:} 39... Rxf3! 40. Qxe5!? (40. Nc5+ {does win an exchange, but} Qxc5 41. Qxf3 Bc6 {leaves Black with a passed pawn for the exchange, but also a very safe position, with ...a4 creating immediate counterplay against white king, should he start trying to double on the 8th rank.}) 40... Rxd3! {Being famous for his quote on not believing in fortresses, in the moment of need Magnus shows that the concrete overrules the general. This one works!} 41. Qxe7 Rxd1+ 42. Kb2 Bc6 43. e5 f3 44. Qf6 Rd2 45. e6 f2 46. e7 Re2 {The situation is clear. Black can exchange the e- and f-pawns at will, leading to a situation where White's queen has no chance to breach the defence of the bishop and rook.} 47. Qf7 Rxe7 48. Qxf2 Rg7 49. Qf4 Rg2 50. Qe5 Rg6 51. c3 Rg2+ 52. Kc1 Rg4 53. Qe6 Re4 54. Qf7 Re1+ 55. Kd2 Re4 56. b4 {White executes the only reasonable plan he has, but it is easily parried.} axb4 57. cxb4 b5! {Other moves hold too, but this makes it trivial.} 58. cxb5 Bxb5 59. Qd5+ Bc6 60. Qc5 Re6 61. Kc3 Rd6 62. b5 Bd7 63. Qe5 Bc8 64. Kc4 Rb6 65. Kc5 Kb8 66. Qe8 Rd6 67. Qf8 Rb6 {And here Nakamura offered a draw. The position is trivial for over the board chess, but for online-chess important details are in Black's favour. White's only chance left is winning on time, which in Armageddon is considered completely legitimate. Online chess allows "pre-move", meaning you can enter your move before the opponent replies, and the move, if legal, will be executed immediately, counting only as a fraction of a second on the clock. In this position Magnus can safely enter d6-b6 or vice versa. Whenever White plays b6, Black will automatically take it, and should Nakamura decide to sacrifice his queen, it will always be in a way that either it is with check, or take on b6 or d6, meaning Magnus "pre-move" is illegal. So with 50 seconds left on the clock for Magnus, Nakamura realised the impossibility of his task, and resigned himself to a draw effectively conceding the match.} 1/2-1/2

