1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. h4 c5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Na4 Nc6 7. e4 Nb6 8. d5 Ne5 9. h5 Nxa4 10. Qxa4+ Bd7 11. Qa3 Qb6 12. Qc3 Qb4 13. Qxb4 cxb4 14. f4 Ng4 15. hxg6 fxg6 16. e5 Bf5 17. Be2 Bg7 18. Bf3 O-O 19. Ne2 h5 20. Nd4 Bd7 21. Bd2 Rac8 22. Rc1 Rxc1+ 23. Bxc1 Rc8 24. Ke2 Rc4 25. Kd3 Rc5 26. Be3 Bh6 27. g3 Nxe3 28. Kxe3 a5 29. Be4 Kg7 30. Nb3 Rc8 31. Nxa5 Ra8 32. Nxb7 Rxa2 33. Nc5 Bg4 34. Nd3 g5 35. f5 h4 36. gxh4 gxh4+ 37. Kd4 1-0

[Event "Gashimov Memorial 6th"]
[Site "Shamkir"]
[Date "2019.04.08"]
[Round "8"]
[White "Karjakin, Sergey"]
[Black "Carlsen, Magnus"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "B33"]
[WhiteElo "2753"]
[BlackElo "2845"]
[PlyCount "78"]

1. e4 c5!? {Considering the tournament standings, maybe made this a somewhat surprising move. Magnus was leading the tournament by ½ a point ahead of Karjakin who then had a further 1 point to the rest of the field. Meaning for Karjakin this was almost a " must-win" situation as if the game was drawn he would need not only to score half a point more in the final round, but also to win a playoff. Even so Magnus stayed loyal to the Sveshnikov which generally does promise solidity, but as shown in the London WC match does allow one way for White to get a very complex battle:} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Nd5!? {Karjakin takes up the gauntlet and follow in Caruanas footsteps. After London Magnus won with the black pieces against Van Forest in Wijk aan Zee as well as Navara earlier in Shamkir. Magnus said after the game that he was indeed exited to see 7.Nd5 on the board as it always leads to very exiting positions. And that while the engines indeed seems to give a preference for White, people tend to underestimate the human factor and that Black's position is easier to play, as he generally tries to aim for the opponent's king, while White has to balance positional gains on the queenside with also caring about the security of his own king.} Nxd5 8. exd5 Ne7!? {The move from game 12 of the match as well as the tiebreaker and vs. Van Forest. 8...Nb8 is of equal value and was seen in game 8 & 10 as well as vs. Narvara, meaning for Karjakin it was impossible to know for sure which move to expect.} 9. c4 Ng6 10. Qa4 Bd7 11. Qb4 Bf5 {The move from game 12, later Magnus preferred 11...Qb8. Anyway this is very much a theoretical debate in progress where the status very much is unsettled. Actually a rare occurence these days that suddenly an oasis of almost untouched but very interesting positions suddenly occurs in a popular opening variation at the highest level.} 12. Qa4 Bd7 13. Qb4 Bf5 14. h4 h5 15. Bg5!? {Karjakin of course comes prepared and plays the critical move. Caruana went 15.Be3 but got into trouble before the game controversially ended in a sudden draw.} Qb8 16. Be2 a6 17. Nc3 Qc7 {In principle only this is the novelty, as 17...Nf4 18 Bf1 Be7 19 Bxe7 Kxe7 20 g3 Ng6 21 Qb6 was played in the game Houdini- Stockfish which Grischuk pointed out during his interesting live commentary during game 12 of the WC match. Magnus' idea, however, is very different and much more human. He does not want to leave his king vulnerable in the centre, on the contrary it will be hidden safely away before he launches an attack on the enemy king. That the price is the h5-pawn is of no significance. Black is playing for mate!} (17... Nf4 18. Bf1 Be7 19. Bxe7 Kxe7 20. g3 Ng6 21. Qb6 {Houdini- Stockfish.}) 18. g3 Be7 19. Be3 e4! {That's the key point. Playing ...Nf8-d7 and covering h5 before castling of course is possible, but this is so much more temptin, as the knight on e5 touches vital squares at f3 and d3 and starts making the "aggressive" h4 and g3 by White look like weaknesses.} 20. O-O O-O 21. Bxh5 Ne5 22. Be2 Qd7 {White won a pawn, but Black immediately zooms in on the sore spots in White's position, immediately threatening 23...Bg4 followed by a deadly invasion on the f3 square.} 23. Qa4 Qc8! {Obviously not allowing the exchange of queens, while still upholding the threat of ...Bg4, meaning Karjakin has to act.} 24. c5?! {Too ambitious, and now tipping the game in Black's favour.} (24. Qd1! {was the right move, controlling g4. Indeed Black is perfectly fine} Nxc4 {when} 25. Bxc4 Qxc4 26. Bd4 {leads to an equal position.}) {That is, however, the best White's position had on offer, as now things are only to get worse. Much worse.} 24... dxc5 25. Nxe4 c4!? {Magnus was happy with this move after the game. 25...b5 also was possible, forcing White to "self-pin" with 26 Qc2. But as the computer shows, after 25...c4 26 Qc2! is still the best move, so why "force" White in the right direction?} 26. Nc3 b5! {Magnus said that he saw 26...Bd3 27.Bxd3 Nf3+ 28.Kg2 Nxh4+ forcing a draw by perpetual, but that while for the tournament situation this would indeed be a good result, Black's attacking options are just too tempting to pass on.} (26... Bd3 27. Bxd3 Nf3+ 28. Kg2 Nxh4+ $11) 27. Qd1 b4! {Another forceful move. Sure it does ruin the solidity of Black's pawnstructure, but much more importantly it forces White's knight basically as far away as possible from the kingside where the game is going to be decided, as well as it allows Black's bishop access to the e4 square.} 28. Na4 Be4 29. Qd4 ({If} 29. f3 {then} Qh3! {decides.}) 29... Qf5 30. f4? {Logical, but leaving Black with a winning position.} ({The absolutely last chance was} 30. f3! {when after} Nxf3+ (30... Bxf3 31. Rae1! {and White very much fights on}) 31. Bxf3 Bxf3 32. Qf4 {White defends.}) {While now with a couple of powerful moves, Magnus shows his attacking skills.} 30... Qg6! {A great intermezzo. By attacking g3, Black forces White into unprotecting the f4 pawn.} (30... Nd3 31. g4! Qh7 32. h5 {is much less clear.}) 31. Bf2 Nd3 32. h5 Qf5! {This is the big difference, the basic point being that 33.g4 Qxf4 now is possible due to the bishop on f2 blocking the rook on f1.} (32... Nxf4 {might look tempting, but after} 33. Rae1 Qf5 34. Bg4!! {the tables are completely turned as} Qxg4 35. Rxe4 Ne2+ 36. Kg2! {wins for White.}) 33. Bg4 {Better was 33.Bxd3, still however leading to a winning position for Black after either recapture.} Qxg4 34. Qxe4 Bd6 {Of course threatening to take on f4, but while that is possible to prevent, the main theme is that Black's knight on d3 protects the e1-square meaning that his rooks can penetrate unopposed in the e-file.} 35. Qg2 Rae8! 36. Bd4 Qxh5! {Precision to the end.} (36... Re2 37. Qf3! {would force off the queens, leaving White with some hope in an indeed bad ending.}) {Now, however, 37...Re2 is a deadly threat as 38.Qf3 would then allow mate on h2.} 37. Qf3 Qg6! {Again seemingly slow, but like in move 34 Black uses the threat to the f4 pawn to gain time for his real aims.} 38. Kh1 Re4! 39. Bf2 Rfe8 {Blacks threats are numerous. 39...Nxf4, 39...Re2 or 39...Nxf2+ followed by 40...Re3. Karjakin had seen enough and resigned meaning Magnus won the Shamkir tournament with a round to spare, but also scored his 4th (!) win in a row vs. 7.Nd5. Similarities to Kasparov's dominance tournament-wise as well as his Kings Indian come to mind.} 0-1

