1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 Nbd7 {Svidler is putting his trust in a modern variation 6...bd7 which presents White with bigger challenges than the older 6...e6.} 7. Qe2 h6 8. Bh4 g6 9. f4 Qc7 {Black is abstaining from advancing the e pawn as the level of risk is much higher in a direct fight with better developed white pieces.} (9... e5 10. fxe5 dxe5 11. O-O-O (11. Nf3 Qc7 12. O-O-O b5 13. Nd5 Nxd5 14. exd5 Bd6 15. Qe3 Bb7 16. Bd3 f5?! (16... Rc8 $11) 17. Nd4 Qb6 18. Bxf5 gxf5 19. Qg3 Nf8 20. Nxf5 $18 {1-0 (38) Khader,S (2392)-Karjakin,S (2760) Riadh 2017}) 11... Qc7 12. Nd5 (12. Nb3 b5 13. Qf3 (13. g4 Be7 14. Qf3 Bb7 15. a3 Nxe4 16. Nxe4 Bxh4 17. Rxd7 Qxd7 18. Nbc5 Bg5+ 19. Kb1 Bxe4 20. Qxe4 Qd1+ 21. Ka2 O-O 22. Bd3 Qd2 $15 {0-1 (44) Savchenko,B (2550)-Sarana,A (2577) Moscow 2018}) (13. Rxd7 Nxd7 14. Nd5 Qb8 15. Bf6 Nxf6 16. Nxf6+ Ke7 17. Nd5+ Ke8 18. Nf6+ Ke7 19. Nd5+ Ke8 20. Nf6+ Ke7 {½-½ (20) Sarana,A (2613)-Khanin,S (2494) Ekaterinburg 2018}) 13... Bg7 14. Kb1 O-O 15. g4 Bb7 16. a3 Rac8 17. Bd3 Qb6 18. Be1 Qe6 19. Rg1 Nb6 20. h4 $14 {0-1 (41) Wan,Y (2500)-Cheparinov,I (2702) China 2018}) 12... Nxd5 13. exd5 Bd6 14. Nb3 b5 15. Qd2 (15. g4 h5 16. g5 Nb6 17. h3 O-O 18. Na5 Bf5 19. Bg2 e4 20. Rhf1 Bf4+ 21. Kb1 Rad8 $19 {0-1 (48) Yankelevich,L (2442)-Jakovenko,D (2710) Germany 2017}) 15... g5 (15... f5 16. g4 f4 17. Bd3 Kf7 18. Qe2 g5 19. Qe4 gxh4 20. Qg6+ Kf8 $11 {1-0 (35) Pichot,A (2552)-Asis Gargatagli,H (2529) Barcelona 2018}) 16. Be1 Nc5 17. Nxc5 Qxc5 18. h4 g4 19. Bg3 Bd7 20. Re1 f6 21. Bd3 O-O-O 22. Rhf1 Rdf8 23. Bf5 $16 {1-0 (39) Naiditsch,A (2697)-Hamdouchi,H (2580) Brest 2018}) 10. O-O-O {Carlsen most likely did not like 10.f3 because after the best 10...e5! the knight may turn out to be somewhat misplaced on f3.} (10. Nf3 Bg7?! (10... e5!) 11. O-O-O (11. e5! dxe5 12. fxe5 Ng4 13. O-O-O e6 14. Bg3 h5 15. Ne4 $16) 11... Nb6 12. e5 dxe5 13. fxe5 Nh5 14. Bf2 Be6 15. Qe4 g5 16. Nd4 Bc8 17. g3 Bxe5 18. Nc6 Bd6 19. Bxb6 Qxc6 20. Qd4 Nf6 {0-1 (29) Levrand,R (2156)-Bauer,C (2647) Zuerich 2017} 21. Rg1! Be6 22. Bg2 Qc4 23. Ne4 $16) 10... Bg7 (10... e5 11. fxe5 dxe5 12. Nb3 Bg7 13. Qc4! Qb8 14. Nc5 O-O 15. Nxd7 Nxd7 16. Nd5 b5 17. Qc6 $16 {1-0 (56) Smirnov,P (2569)-Bocharov,D (2610) Vladivostok 2016}) 11. g4 {Surprisingly enough there have been as good as no games played from this position; the players have to invent theory.} (11. Kb1 O-O 12. Nf3 e5 13. f5 b5 14. g4 b4 15. Nd5 Nxd5 16. exd5 a5 17. g5 e4 18. Nd4 hxg5? (18... Bxd4! 19. Rxd4 Ne5 20. fxg6 Bg4 21. Qe3 fxg6 $15) 19. Bxg5 $16 {1-0 (39) Melia,S (2400)-Travkina,A (2287) Riga 2017}) 11... e5 12. fxe5 Nxe5 (12... dxe5 13. Nb3 O-O $14) 13. h3 Be6 {We can safely presume, that this move was most likely part of Black's preparation. Immediate castling has the drawback of a vulnerable f6-knight that has to defend the sensitive d5-square.} (13... O-O 14. Qf2 g5 15. Bg3 b5 16. Kb1 $16) 14. Qf2 Nfd7 15. Bb5!? {The world champion must have tried to guess which line of attack will turn out to be more efficient - the text move or the unpleasant 15.d2.} (15. Qd2 O-O (15... Nc5 16. Nxe6 fxe6 17. Qxd6 Qxd6 18. Rxd6 O-O 19. b4 Ncd7 20. Ne2 $14) 16. Be7 Rfc8 17. Nxe6 fxe6 18. Qxd6 Nc5 19. Qxc5 Qxc5 20. Bxc5 Rxc5 21. Ne2 $14) 15... O-O (15... axb5?? 16. Ncxb5 Qa5 17. Nxe6 $18) 16. Bxd7 Qxd7 (16... Nxd7 17. Be7 Rfc8 18. Nxe6 fxe6 19. Bxd6 $14) 17. Nf5?! {[%mdl 32] The temptation to play a flashy attacking move proved to be too strong. The engine is not so emotional and clearly prefers the modest positional exchange of the g7-bishop with some advantage.} (17. Bf6! Rac8 (17... Bxf6 18. Qxf6 Qd8 19. Qf4 Kh7 20. Kb1 $14) 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 19. Kb1 $14) 17... gxf5 18. gxf5 Kh7 19. Rhg1 Bh8 {It makes sense to show some respect to the best player in the world even though objectively even 19...c7 with the knight retreat to g6 was good enough for keeping the balance.} (19... Qc7 20. Rg3 Ng6 21. Qg2 Rg8 22. fxg6+ fxg6 23. Rgd3 Rac8 $11) 20. Bg3 Rac8 21. Bf4 (21. fxe6 fxe6 22. Qe2 Rg8 $11) 21... Qe7! {The queen prevents aggression via h4 and prepares to create counterplay on the a1-h8 diagonal.} (21... Bc4? 22. Qh4 Bg7 23. Rxg7+ Kxg7 24. Rg1+ Ng6 25. f6+ $18) 22. fxe6 (22. Kb1 Qf6 (22... Bc4 23. Bxh6 Kxh6 24. Rxd6+ Kh7 25. f6 Bxf6 26. Rxf6 Ng6 27. h4 $16) 23. fxe6 fxe6 24. Rdf1 Ng6 25. Qg3 Nxf4 26. Rxf4 Rg8 $11) 22... fxe6 23. Qg3 Rg8 {The disappointed champion must have realised that White has not even a hint of any advantage.} 24. Qf2 Rgf8 (24... Bg7 25. Rdf1 Nc4 $11) 25. Qg3 Rg8 26. Qf2 Rgf8 1/2-1/2

