{5th round of the Shamkir Chess 2014, Vugar Gashimov's memorial. We were in different tournament situations, I was on 50 percent, Magnus was at +1 after he lost to Caruana before this game with Black. Certainly an interesting fight was ahead! It's always interesting to play against the World Champion and I have enjoyed this fight.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 {King's Indian Defence. No comments.} 5. Nge2 O-O 6. Ng3 {There are so many moves and plans in the opening phase, but annotating the game and working on chess ECO is quite different. I will not get far in theoretical debris but will show few most recent examples here:} e5 (6... h5 7. Be2 a6 (7... Nbd7 8. Be3 e5 9. d5 a5 10. a3 Nc5 11. h4 Ng4 {which is a bit similiar to our game in Nabaty-Smirin Skopje 2013, which ended in a draw.}) 8. O-O c5 9. d5 e5 {with a real mix of all the possible plans in Bologan-Mamedov, 2014 Euro Cup Rhodes, ended in a draw.}) 7. d5 a5 8. Be2 Na6 9. h4 {It's not as agressive as it looks, only if Black allows h5 it becomes a kind of tactical and offensive approach, in general it's more a positional move to play g5 after h5 and get d2-0-0-0 faster.} h5 (9... c6 10. h5 cxd5 11. cxd5 Nc5 12. Be3 Qb6 {It was played in Lutz-Gelfand, Horgen 1994. Gelfand won this game. Lutz never castled and tried a f1-d2 plan, which is going nowhere as after c4 Black may just go back to c7 threatening ...b5 later on. The Computer suggests to change directions here and go for 0-0 h6-d2 and playing on the queenside. Claims White is better but it's far from obvious for a practical game.} 13. O-O Bd7 14. h6 Bh8 15. Qd2 a4 16. f3 Rfc8 {with mutual chances!}) 10. Bg5 Qe8 (10... Nc5?! 11. Bxh5 gxh5 12. Nxh5 Ncd7 {To be honest I didn't even consider this. Too beautiful.} 13. Nxg7 Kxg7 14. f4 $36) 11. Qd2 Nc5 (11... Nh7!? 12. Be3 Qe7 (12... f5? 13. exf5 gxf5 14. Bxh5 Qd7 15. f4 {and there is no way to have counterplay here, White is too solid}) 13. Nf1 f5 14. exf5 gxf5 15. Bg5 Nxg5 16. Qxg5 Qxg5 17. hxg5 a4 {and I am sure Black has strong compensation for the pawn on the h-file.}) 12. O-O-O (12. Nb5 {Computer's suggestion that I saw during the game, as if the queen goes to d8 there are some xh5 in the air. Black can just defend the pawn with some ...d7 or other move. But...} Ncxe4 13. Nxe4 Nxe4 14. Qe3 Nxg5 15. Nxc7 Qa4 16. Nxa8 Qb4+ 17. Qd2 Qxd2+ 18. Kxd2 Ne4+ 19. Ke1 Nc5 $44 {and I would prefer to play with Black here.}) (12. f3 {is probably the most natural instead of 0-0-0, not to allow ...g4 move.} Nh7!? 13. Be3 b6 14. Bd3 f5 $132) 12... Ng4! {Important move, now White has to change his e2-bishop for the knight.} 13. Bxg4 (13. Rdf1 f6 {and White will eventually play xg4 anyway. Even though after xg4 for White here, Black has ...fxg5!? besides taking on g4 with the c8-bishop.}) (13. Qe1 f6 14. Bd2 Bh6 {is quite OK for Black.}) 13... Bxg4 14. f3 (14. Rdf1 Bd7 {and the rook is far from defending its monarch! ...b5 is coming!}) 14... Bd7 15. Be3 b6 (15... b5 16. cxb5 Bxb5 17. Bxc5 dxc5 18. Nf1 {I didnt like this position that much, g4 is coming somewhere and I am not sure how to organize direct threats on the b-line.}) 16. Kb1 Kh7 (16... f5 {was already possible here, I wanted to wait for ge2 first as it looked to me that White doesn't have any improving ideas} 17. exf5 gxf5) 17. Qc2 a4 (17... Bh6 {this is ok, but I didn't want to allow any xc5-a4 ideas} 18. Bxc5 bxc5 19. a4 {and even though I was sure that Black is ok, I didn't like to allow a4-b5 here with pressure on a5 later on.}) 18. Nge2 f5 19. exf5 gxf5 (19... Bxf5 {I thought it's about equal and gxf5 is more fighting} 20. Ne4 Nxe4 21. fxe4 Bg4 22. Rdf1 a3 {Black is even slightly better here, but draw is the most normal outcome.}) 20. Rh3 {The rook looks a bit naive on this square with f3-e3 standing, nevertheless the threat is clear g3-g5, sometimes with ideas of eleminating the g7-bishop.} Kh8! {Fighting against the g3-move, ...f4 is threatening now with ...f5 idea, White had to do something, otherwise hh1 is the only option.} 21. f4 {The most logical reaction, playing hh1 looked awful, now the rook is heading to g5 again after g3 if Black is not taking measures!} Ne4!? {Good move, preventing g3, now ...g6 is coming next and the bad position of White's pieces starts to tell, but including ...a3 was even better.} (21... a3! 22. b3 Ne4 {All the ensuing complications like in the game, are better with the a3-pawn creating new threats to White's king.}) 22. Nxe4 {Carlsen is not going to passively wait here, he prepares an exchange sacrifice with great complications where Black's weak king may become an important factor as I was already running short on time.} (22. Rf3 a3 23. b3 Qg6 24. g3 {was possible to try some kind of fortress, even though Black may slighly improve the position of his pieces and it's not sure what White wil do next.}) 22... fxe4 23. Rg3 Bg4 24. Rxg4 $8 hxg4 25. f5 Rxf5 26. Ng3 Rf8 27. Qxe4 {White has blocked all the center, h5-h6 is coming, I considered ...f4 somewhere to open up the g7-bishop, but it didn't seem to offer winning chances.} Qd7 (27... a3 {This is always good, there was no need to wait with this move, mating ideas are always there around White's king after a3.} 28. b3 Rf7! {I have tried to figure out how to bring my queen to h7, I was mostly considering f7-f6 or f7-g8-h7, but this neat way is also very nice especially having ...a3 already played!} 29. Qxg4 Qg8 30. Ne4 Qh7 {Now ...f4 is in the air, Black is just winning.} 31. Re1 (31. Kc1 Rf4!! 32. Bxf4 Qxe4 33. Qh5+ Kg8 34. Bg5 Rf8 $19) 31... Bf6 $19 {and when the a-rook joins the party, White is helpless.}) 28. a3 {White is trying to hide his king on a2 and profit from Black's king position with h5-h6.} (28. h5 a3 {then ...f6, we saw it above.}) 28... b5! {Very strong move, Magnus said on the press conference that he thought that the compensation is very strong for White, but after this move the position started to be really shaky for White.} 29. c5 (29. cxb5 Qxb5 30. Qg6 Rab8 31. Rd2 e4 32. Qh5+ Kg8 33. Qg6 Rf6 {and bf8 or ...b3, White's king is too weak now!}) 29... dxc5! {Two very important moves starting from b5!, now lines are open for Black's rooks and they developing a strong play against White's king.} 30. h5 c4 (30... Ra6 {I thought about this, but ...c4 looks much stronger to me now too after checking it back home.}) 31. h6 (31. Ka2 {This was interesting, finally revealing the idea of a3!, but White is still in trouble after} Rae8 32. h6 Bf6 {even though White have had to choose this option, his position falls apart anyway as ...h7 and ...c3 is a real threat.} 33. Nh5 Qh7 34. Qxg4 c3 35. Bc1 Qg8 $17) 31... Bf6 32. Bc5 Rf7 (32... Qh7?! {Trying to release the tension and White's pressure on Black's king wouldn't work due to} 33. Bxf8 Qxe4+ 34. Nxe4 Rxf8 35. Rf1 $11) 33. Rf1 (33. Nh5 Rg8 $19) 33... Re8! {This simple move is very important, otherwise h5 could be really dangerous, e5 is protected and Black can regroup with g5-f4 or d8.} 34. Bb4 {There is no good advice for White here, the position is already lost.} (34. Qg6 Qxd5 35. Rxf6 Qd1+ 36. Ka2 Qb3+ 37. Kb1 c3!! {It wins according to the computer, but I didn't see the exact path after} (37... Rd8 38. Rd6 Rdf8 {I saw only this idea} 39. Rf6 c3 40. Rf2 Qd1+ 41. Ka2 cxb2 {and Black is winning}) 38. Rf2 Rd8 (38... Qd1+ 39. Ka2 b4!! {This is even simplier according to the engine. As now if the bishop takes on b4, White is unable to defend against ...d8 with e3-c1!} (39... cxb2?? 40. Rxb2 Qd5+ 41. Ka1 $11 {and compared to the line with df8, the rook on f7 is hanging}) 40. Bxb4 Qb3+ 41. Kb1 Rd8 $19) 39. Be3 Rd1+ 40. Bc1 b4 $19) 34... Bg5 {I had around 1 minute here, but I was able to calculate all of my next moves and Magnus's ideas. ...c6 is a very important finesse.} 35. Nf5 {Otherwise Black plays f4 anyway.} c6! {Killing move,black is completely winning} (35... Bf4 36. Nh4 {and things are very complicated again!}) 36. Bd6 (36. d6 Ref8 $19 {the diagonal a3-f8 is blocked, Black is a rook up now.}) 36... Bf4 37. Ng7 (37. Rxf4 exf4 38. Be5+ Rxe5 39. Qxe5+ Kh7 40. Nd6 cxd5 $19) 37... Qxd6 38. Nxe8 Qxd5 {Everything is forced here.} 39. Qxd5 cxd5 40. g3 Kh7! {My first intention having few seconds, was to play ...e7 here, which was also good, but ...h7 seemed to be a more clear cut win.} (40... Re7 41. Nf6 {with few more tricks in the air as after} Bxh6 42. Rh1 (42. Nxd5 Rd7 {and I wasn't sure if it's that easy to win as b5 is too weak, even though Black is objectively winning here too}) 42... Kg7 43. Rxh6 Re6! {You have to see this move from far.}) 41. gxf4 (41. Nd6 Rd7 42. Nxb5 Bxg3 {Black has too many pawns, the d-pawn is promoted very fast.}) 41... exf4 42. Nd6 {Otherwise pawns are going forward without any obstacles.} Rf6 43. Nxb5 {It took me some time here, to understand which pawn push is better, finally it seems to make no real difference, which pawn moves leading to a win.} f3 (43... g3 44. Nd4 Kxh6 45. Kc2 Kh5 46. Nf3 Kg4 47. Ne5+ Kh3 $19) 44. Nd4 Kxh6 (44... Rf4 45. Ne6 Re4? 46. Ng5+ Kxh6 47. Nxf3 gxf3 48. Rxf3 {and I don't see how Black can win this endgame.} Kg5 49. Rf8 d4 50. Kc1 d3 51. Kd1 Re2 52. Rg8+ $11) 45. Kc2 Kg5 {The king helps to march with f- and g-pawns, White can do nothing here.} 46. Kd2 f2 47. Ne2 Rf3 48. Kc2 Kh4 49. Rh1+ (49. Nd4 Rf6 $19) 49... Rh3 50. Rf1 g3 51. Kd2 Kg4 {Carlsen resigned as the black king comes to f3 and g2. I am happy about my play in the zeitnot part and in the middlegame around move 30-40 in time scramble I was able to find all the best moves. I hope that this tournament becomes traditional and I'm very thankful to both players and organisers for the perfect tournament.} 0-1

