{[%evp 0,103,32,44,13,-24,-22,-14,0,-39,-32,-23,-10,-19,-10,-52,-7,-12,44,25,50,51,41,18,32,12,21,-4,20,0,34,-34,61,48,50,50,50,46,52,23,53,21,39,29,44,45,58,35,39,39,51,38,38,-26,38,20,63,63,48,15,11,-16,-14,-44,36,13,-5,-5,-5,-23,65,-16,15,2,1,-33,-33,-72,-72,-73,-107,-34,-25,-30,10,-13,-13,-21,-24,5,8,8,13,0,4,0,0,-20,-25,-27,-11,0,54,55,54,48]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 c6 6. e3 h6 7. Bh4 Be7 8. Bd3 (8. Qc2 O-O 9. Nf3 {Carlsen decided to avoid d3, ge2 in the Armageddon game that followed our main game. His choice of 9.f3 didn't pose any problems to Firouzja.} Re8 10. Bd3 Ne4! 11. Bxe7 Qxe7 12. Bxe4 (12. O-O Nd7 13. Rfc1 Ndf6 14. Ne5 Bf5 $13) 12... dxe4 13. Nd2 f5 14. O-O-O? {Carlsen tried to play for a win but soon ended up clearly worse.} (14. O-O Nd7 $13 {[%cal Yd7f6]} (14... Be6)) 14... Nd7 15. g4 fxg4 16. Ndxe4 Nb6 17. Ng3 Be6 $17 {1-0 (47) Carlsen,M (2863)-Firouzja,A (2728) Stavanger NOR 2020}) 8... O-O (8... Nbd7 {followed by h5 is a lot more common.}) 9. Nge2 Re8 10. O-O Nbd7 (10... Ne4?! 11. Bxe7 Nxc3 (11... Qxe7 12. Bxe4 dxe4 13. Ng3 f5 14. f3 exf3 15. Rxf3 $16) 12. Bxd8 Nxd1 13. Rfxd1 Rxd8 14. b4 $14) 11. f3 (11. Qc2 a5) 11... b5!? 12. Bf2 (12. e4?! b4 13. e5 (13. Na4? dxe4 14. fxe4 Nxe4! $19) 13... bxc3 14. exf6 Nxf6 15. bxc3 Qa5 $132 {[%cal Yc8a6,Yc6c5]}) 12... Bb7 {[#]} (12... Nb6 13. b3 (13. Ng3!?) 13... a5 14. Rc1 Bb7 15. Ng3 b4 16. Nce2 $14 {1-0 (40) Rodshtein,M (2685)-Eljanov,P (2655) Netanya 2019}) 13. Ng3 (13. e4?! b4 14. Na4?! (14. e5 bxc3 15. exf6 Bxf6 16. Nxc3 Nf8 $132 {[%csl Rd4][%cal Yf8e6]}) 14... dxe4 15. fxe4 c5! 16. e5 Ne4 $17 {Black is already in the driver's seat.}) (13. Rc1!? a6 14. Bb1 c5 15. dxc5 Bxc5 16. Nd4 Ne5 17. b3 Rc8 {Black's position seems quite solid.} (17... Qb6)) 13... b4 14. Na4 (14. Nce2 c5 15. dxc5 Bxc5 16. Nd4 a5) 14... c5 15. Nf5 {[#]} (15. b3!? Qa5 (15... Rc8 16. Rc1 cxd4 17. exd4 {I would feel slightly better with White here although objectively Black should be ok.}) 16. Rc1 Rac8 17. Nf5 Bf8 18. dxc5 Nxc5 19. Nxc5 Rxc5 $13) (15. dxc5 Bc6!? 16. Nf5 Bf8 17. Nd4 Bxa4 18. Qxa4 Nxc5 19. Qd1 Nxd3 20. Qxd3 Nd7 $13) (15. Nxc5 Nxc5 16. dxc5 Bxc5 17. Nf5 Qb6 $13) 15... Bf8?! {Firouzja hesitates and decides not to change the pawn structure by playing c4.} (15... c4!? 16. Nxe7+! (16. Bc2 Bf8 $17 {[%cal Yg7g6]}) 16... Qxe7 17. Bc2 a5 18. Re1 Qd6 $132 {Black seems to have everything under control. e4 is more or less stopped and Black will focus on trying to take advantage of the unfortunate position of the a4.}) 16. Nxc5 (16. dxc5?! Bc6!?) 16... Nxc5 17. dxc5 Bxc5 18. Rc1 Qb6 19. Qa4 {[#]} Bc8! {Black correctly kicks the knight out of f5 and transfers his bishop to d7.} 20. Nd4 Bd7 21. Qa6 (21. Qd1 a5 {is quite comfortable for Black.}) 21... Re7 (21... Rec8!?) 22. Rfd1 (22. Rc2 {Doubling the rooks on the c-file was another rather obvious plan but still Black seems to have adequare resources:} Ne8! {Black knight should move to c7 to force White's queen out of a6.} (22... Rc8? 23. Nb3 $16) 23. Rfc1 Nc7 24. Bh7+!? (24. Qxb6 axb6 {Black has everything under control.}) 24... Kxh7? (24... Kh8 25. Qd3 Na6 (25... Bxd4 26. Qxd4 (26. exd4? Ne6 $19) 26... Qxd4 27. exd4 Ne6 28. Bd3 Ree8! 29. Re1 a5 $14 {I wouldn't prefer being Black against Carlsen in this position although Black should be quite ok.}) 26. Bf5 b3! 27. Qxb3 Qxb3 28. axb3 Bxd4 29. Bxd7 Bxe3 30. Bxe3 Rxe3 $11) 25. Qd3+ Kg8 26. Rxc5 $18) 22... Ne8 {[#]} 23. Qxb6?! {It doesn't make much sense to me to take on b6 before Black commits his knight to c7.} (23. Bf1 {Making room for the white queen on d3 makes a lot of sense.} Nc7 (23... Nd6? 24. e4 (24. Rxc5!? Qxc5 25. e4 Ree8 26. Nb3 Qc6 27. Rxd5 Nb7 28. Nd4 Qc8 29. b3 $15) 24... Nc4 25. Bxc4 dxc4 26. Qxc4 $16) (23... Be6!?) {Here White has a pleasant choice:} 24. Qd3!? {Black has difficulties equalising even if White keeps the queens on the board.} (24. Qxb6 axb6 25. Nb3 Bd6 26. Bg3 Bxg3 27. hxg3 Be8 (27... Ne8 28. Ra1 (28. Rxd5 Nf6 29. Rd2 Rxe3 30. Kf2 Re5 31. Rc7 {Black remains under pressure.}) 28... Rxe3 29. Nd4 Re7 30. Nc2 $14) (27... Ba4? 28. Rd4 $18) 28. Nd4 Rxa2 29. Nf5 $44 {Black hasn't fully solved his problems yet.}) 24... a5 (24... Rc8 25. Rc2 Ne8 26. Rdc1 {looks rather awkward for Black.} Rc7 {[#]} (26... Nd6 27. Qb3) 27. e4! dxe4 28. fxe4 $16 {[%cal Yc2c5]}) 25. Kh1!? (25. Rd2 a4 26. Rdc2 Bxd4 27. Qxd4 Qxd4 28. exd4 Ne6 $14) 25... Bd6!? (25... a4?! 26. e4! {Opening the position is in White's favour.} dxe4 27. fxe4 Rae8 28. Qg3 $16) (25... Ree8) (25... Rae8 26. Qc2 Na6 27. Rd2 $16 {[%cal Yc2b3,Yc2d1,Yd2c2]}) 26. e4 (26. Qd2) 26... Qb7! (26... dxe4? 27. fxe4 $18 {[%cal Yd4f5]}) 27. Nf5 (27. exd5 Nxd5 (27... Qxd5? 28. Nc6! Bxc6 29. Qb1 $18) 28. Nb3 (28. Nf5 Bxf5 29. Qxf5 Bf4! $14) 28... Be6 29. Nc5 $14) 27... Bxf5 28. exf5 $14) 23... axb6 24. Bb1 $14 Ba4 {Black feels the need to force b3 so that White will not have this square for his knight.} (24... g6!? {Taking away from the white knight the f5 square was a decent alternative.} 25. Ne2 (25. Nb3 Bxe3 26. Bxe3 Rxe3 27. Rxd5 Be6 28. Rd4 Re2 29. Nd2 Nf6 (29... Bxa2? 30. Be4 $18 {[%cal Yg1f1]}) 30. Kf1 Re5 31. Rxb4 Rd8 {Black is active enough not to be in real trouble.}) 25... Bf5!? (25... Nc7?! 26. Nf4) (25... Bxe3 26. Bxe3 Rxe3 27. Kf2 $14) 26. Bxf5 gxf5 {Black's pawn structure is ridiculous but White fails to prove a clear advantage since his own pawns are under attack as well.} 27. Rxd5 Rxa2 28. Rd2 (28. Rb1 Rea7! $13) 28... Rxe3! 29. Rxc5 Ra1+! 30. Rc1 Rxe2 31. Rxe2 Rxc1+ 32. Re1 $11) 25. b3 (25. Nf5!? Rd7 26. b3 Bb5 27. Bg3 $14) 25... Bd7 26. Ne2 Nc7 27. Rd2 {[#]} Bc6! 28. Nd4 (28. Nf4? Nb5! $15 {[%cal Yb5c3]}) 28... Bb7 29. g4 Ne6 (29... g6!? {Stopping f5 made a lot of sense.} 30. h4 Ne6 31. Bg3 (31. h5 Nxd4 32. exd4 Bd6 $11 {[%cal Yd6f4]}) 31... Ng7!? $13 {[%cal Yh6h5]}) 30. Nf5 Rc7?! {At this point Firouzja was running low on time and this certainly made his defensive task a lot more difficult.} (30... Rd7!? {Black should have tried to make d4 work as soon as possible.} 31. h4 (31. Kg2 g6 32. Ng3 $13) (31. Rcd1 g6) 31... d4 32. Nxd4 (32. e4?! Ba6 $15) (32. exd4 Bxf3) (32. Kg2) 32... Bxd4 33. Kg2 Bxe3 34. Rxd7 Nf4+ 35. Kh2 Bxc1 36. Rxb7 Rd8 {Again Black's activity provides enough counterplay for his weak pawn structure.}) 31. h4 {[#]} Rac8?! (31... Bxe3 {Simplifying the position was Black's best chance to equalise although it is perfectly understandable why he decided against this option.} 32. Rxc7 Bxf2+ 33. Kxf2 Nxc7 34. Nd6 $36) 32. Rcd1 Bf8 33. Bg3! Rc3 (33... Rd7 34. Be5 $16) 34. Kf2 Bc5 {[#]} 35. Bd3? {Here Carlsen probably thought that his position was good anyway and that he would take advantage of Black's time pressure by playing some solid moves. d3 is a very logical move since it stops any counterplay on the 3rd rank against e3 and f3, but it appears that there is a more concrete way to secure a winning advantage.} (35. Bd6! d4 (35... Rd8 36. Bxc5 bxc5 37. Rxd5 Bxd5 38. Ne7+ Kf8 39. Nxd5 $18) (35... Ra8 36. Ne7+ Kh8 37. Nxd5 Bxd5 38. Bxc5 bxc5 39. Rxd5 $18) (35... Re8 36. Bxc5 Nxc5 37. h5 $18 {[%cal Yd2d4]}) 36. exd4! (36. Ne7+? Kh8 37. Bxc5 bxc5 38. exd4 Rxf3+ 39. Kg1 Rc7 $13) 36... Rxf3+ {If I had to guess I would say that Carlsen calculated up to this point and considered this position quite messy and searched for other moves.} 37. Kg1 Bxd6 38. Nxd6 Rg3+ 39. Kh2 Rcc3 40. Nxb7 $18) (35. Rd3!? $16 {[%cal Yg3e5]}) 35... d4 36. e4 Ra8! {[%cal Yb7a6]} 37. Bc4 {[#]} Rc2! {Very nice resource found by Firouzja with only a few minutes on the clock!} (37... d3+!? 38. Kg2 Ba6 39. Bd5 (39. Bxa6? Rxa6 $17 {[%cal Yf7f6,Ya6a7,Ya7d7]}) 39... Ra7 $13) (37... Ba6!?) 38. Bd3 (38. Be5 Raxa2 39. Ke1 $11) 38... Rcxa2 39. Be5 Rxd2+ (39... f6 40. Bd6 {[%cal Yd3c4]}) 40. Rxd2 Ra5 41. Kg3 Ba6 $11 (41... Bf8!? 42. Bd6 Bxd6+ 43. Nxd6 Ba6 44. Bxa6 Rxa6 45. Nb5 Ra8 46. Kf2 Rd8 {White should be able to make a draw without too much trouble.}) 42. Bxa6 Rxa6 43. Nxd4 Bxd4 44. Bxd4 Ra3 45. Rd3 Nxd4 46. Rxd4 Rxb3 47. Rd8+ Kh7 48. h5 Ra3 49. Rd4 Ra4 50. e5 Ra5 51. Rxb4 Rxe5 52. Rxb6 {A good result for Firouzja who handled a bad position and time pressure with remarkable maturity and posed enough problems to Carlsen to get away with half a point. On the other hand Carlsen managed to get a winning advantage from an equal position, in typical Carlsen style, but failed to get the point.} 1/2-1/2

