{[%evp 0,52,19,31,25,-11,-9,4,20,-33,-26,-19,-13,-24,3,-23,1,-19,-15,3,-12,5,6,10,20,-2,-1,-32,-21,-53,-96,-55,-58,-128,-127,-176,-207,-187,-204,-204,-204,-202,-162,-178,-166,-149,-142,-213,-168,-263,-393,-470,-353,-352,-346]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. f3 d5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 c6 {Explanation of this move - see CBM 76, annotations to the game Khenkin - Rozentalis by GM Peter Wells. However, in my opinion, its main idea is just to avoid main theory :-).} 7. a4 {A rare move.} (7. e3 {is the most normal, of course.}) (7. e4!? {is a curious pawn sacrifice frequently seen in practice, too.}) 7... Qa5 {Black did quite well in the following game:} (7... O-O 8. e3 b6 9. cxd5 cxd5 10. a5 Ba6 11. Bxa6 Nxa6 12. Qd3 Qc8 13. Ba3 Re8 14. Ne2 bxa5 15. Bd6 Nb8 16. e4 Nfd7 17. O-O Nb6 $13 {0-1 (41) Volkov,S (2485)-Rozentalis,E (2610) St Petersburg 1996}) 8. Qd2 O-O 9. e3 Re8 {Just in case anticipating possible c1-a3 and preparing ...e6-e5.} 10. Bd3 dxc4 (10... e5 11. dxe5 Rxe5 {was quite good but Black has a different plan.}) 11. Bxc4 b6 12. Bd3 {White wants to avoid the exchange of bishops but wastes a lot of time and gets a poor position.} ({The simple} 12. Ne2 Ba6 13. Bxa6 Qxa6 14. O-O {, then e3-e4 etc. was better. Black should equalize but not more.}) 12... Ba6 13. c4 c5! {[%mdl 32] Black doesn't care about d2xa5 b6xa5. He obtains the b-file and the strong b4 square for his knight in that case.} (13... Qxd2+ 14. Bxd2 c5 {was less convincing due to} 15. a5) 14. Ne2 (14. Qxa5 bxa5 15. Ne2 (15. dxc5 Nc6 {[%cal Rc6e5,Rc6b4]}) 15... Nc6 16. Ba3 Nb4 17. Bxb4 axb4 18. Kf2 {was probably a better defence although Black keeps initiative in that case, too.}) 14... Nc6! 15. Kf2 (15. Qxa5 {is too late due to} Nxa5) 15... Nb4 16. Bb2 (16. Ba3 Nxd3+ 17. Qxd3 Red8 $17 {[%csl Rc4,Rf2]}) 16... cxd4 17. Nxd4 (17. exd4 Rac8 18. Rhc1 Nfd5! {, and White is completely paralized; Black can prepare an assault on the c4 pawn (...Rc8-c7, ...Re8-c8 etc.).}) 17... Rad8 {[%cal Re6e5]} 18. Bc3 Nxd3+ 19. Qxd3 Qc5 20. Qc2 Bxc4 $19 {White has lost a pawn, and his king is unsafe.} 21. Rhd1 Nd5 22. Bd2 e5 23. Nf5 (23. Nb3 Bxb3 24. Qxb3 e4!? $19) 23... Nf4 24. Kg3 {Suicide but} (24. Kg1 {was hopeless, too.}) 24... Nd3 25. Qc3 Be6 26. Qxc5 (26. Nh4 g5) 26... Nxc5 {[%cal Re6f5,Re6b3]} 0-1

