1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. d3!? {A typical choice from Carlsen. He does not go into the usual forced lines of Spanish. On the other hand, he prepares to put long term pressure on his opponent.} b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. a4 b4 9. Nbd2 O-O 10. Re1 Na5 11. Ba2 c5 12. c3 Rb8 13. d4 cxd4?! {Not a novelty but there is no much debate about this move. It seems quite dubious to me. I believe that Karjakin had to keep the tension for a while and had to go for the usual ...c7.} (13... Qc7 14. dxe5 dxe5 15. Nc4 Rd8 16. Qe2 {and Black is just fine after b3:} b3 (16... Nxc4 17. Bxc4 bxc3 {is also ok for Black}) 17. Nxa5 Qxa5 18. Bb1 c4! 19. Qxc4 Bc5 20. h3 {½-½ (45) Cheparinov,I (2696)-Aronian,L (2763)/Sofia 2008/CBM 124 (45). And Black has enough compensation for the sacrificed pawn.}) 14. cxd4 Qc7 15. Nb3 Nxb3 16. Bxb3 Bg4?! {This does not create any real pressure on d4.} 17. Bg5 (17. h3 Bxf3 18. Qxf3 exd4 19. Bf4 {and White is just better, Carlsen's choice is also good enough.}) 17... exd4 18. Qxd4 Bxf3 19. gxf3 {[%csl Gb3,Gg5]} Nh5 20. Rac1 Qd7 21. Bxe7!? {A risky decision. White drops d2 (which could have brought him a slight but stable advantage) for a drastic sequence.} Qxe7 22. e5 Qg5+? (22... dxe5 23. Rxe5 Qf6 {was Black's only chance.}) 23. Qg4 Qxg4+ 24. fxg4 Nf4 25. Rc6! {White's initiative is decisive.} dxe5 26. Rxa6 Nd3 27. Re3 Nxb2? (27... Rbd8 28. a5 Nxb2 29. Ra7 Nd3 30. Rc7 {[%csl Gc5]} Nf4 31. a6 Ne6 32. Re7 Nd4 33. a7 $16) 28. a5 e4 29. Rxe4 Nd3 30. Rc6 Ra8 31. a6 {A blunder? No!} h5 (31... Rxa6 32. Rxa6 Nc5 33. Bxf7+ Kxf7 (33... Kh8 34. Rae6 Nxe4 35. Rxe4 $18) 34. Ra7+ $18) 32. g5 Rfc8 33. Rxc8+ Rxc8 34. g6 1-0

