[PlyCount "100"]

1. b3 {The game rarely truly begins at the first move, but with Jobava it is no great surprise. The Georgian player is exceptionally gifted, but not your standard top grandmaster. In his chess philosophy opening knowledge does not deserve any prominent spot.} g6 2. Bb2 Nf6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. d4 c5 {Carlsen is a clear favourite in the game and the world champion actively looks for early conflict.} (4... d5 5. Qd2 a5 6. f3 h5 7. e4 dxe4 8. fxe4 a4 9. bxa4 Bh6 10. Qd3 b6 11. Nf3 Ba6 12. Qd1 Bxf1 13. Rxf1 Nc6 14. d5 Na5 15. Qd4 c5 16. Qd3 O-O 17. h3 e6 18. d6 Nd7 19. g4 h4 20. e5 $14 {1-0 (48) Shengelia,D (2556)-Schreiner,P (2460) Austria 2013}) 5. e3 cxd4 6. exd4 d5 {The choice of the central structure was done in a hope, that the development of the bishop to b2 will turn out to have been slightly premature.} (6... O-O 7. Bd3 Nc6 8. Nge2 d6 9. O-O Bg4 10. f3 Bd7 11. Ne4 Nd5 12. Qd2 f5 13. Bc4 e6 14. Nxd6 Qh4 15. Nxb7 Bh6 16. f4 g5 17. g3 Qh5 18. Nc5 $18 {0-1 (37) Bancod,R (2171)-Gallegos,P (2097) ICC INT 2009}) 7. Qd2 Nc6 8. f3 h5 {In retrospect Black might have been somewhat unhappy about the missed chance to limit the scope of the light-squared bishop of White.} (8... a6!? 9. O-O-O h5 $15) 9. Bb5 Qd6 10. Nge2 Bh6 11. Qd1 (11. Qd3 a6 12. Bxc6+ Qxc6 $15) 11... Bf5 {In the games of strong players every small detail can gain great importance. It seem to have been somewhat more precise to kick the b5-bishop away with the immediate 11...a6 move.} (11... a6 12. Bxc6+ Qxc6 13. Bc1 Bg7 14. O-O Bf5 $15) 12. Bc1 Bxc1 (12... Bg7 13. Bf4 Qd7 14. O-O $11) 13. Rxc1 Rc8 14. Qd2 O-O 15. Bxc6 (15. O-O Nd8 16. Nd1 a6 17. Bd3 Bxd3 18. Qxd3 Nc6 $11) 15... bxc6 {A chess game can be presented as a sequence of more or (sometimes) less successful choices. Lesser players might have hoped to get more play after recapturing with the rook.} (15... Rxc6 16. Nd1 b5 17. O-O Rfc8 18. Ne3 Bd7 $11) 16. Na4 Rfe8 17. O-O e5 18. c3 (18. Rce1 Nd7 19. Ng3 Qf6 20. Nxf5 Qxf5 $11) 18... h4 19. dxe5 Rxe5 20. b4! {Jobava is on alert and his pawn move fixes the central structure on light squares. Black's bishop is now limited by 4 black pawns sitting on the light squares.} Rce8 (20... Nd7 21. Nd4 c5 22. Nxc5 Nxc5 23. f4 Re4 24. bxc5 Qxc5 25. Kh1 Rce8 $11) 21. Nd4 h3 22. g3 (22. Nxf5 Rxf5 23. g3 (23. gxh3? Nh5 $17) 23... Rfe5 $15) 22... Bc8 23. Nc5 Nd7 24. Nd3 {The knight exchange on d7 would have made it easier for Black to seek some activity with the timely c6-c5 advance.} (24. Nxd7 Bxd7 25. Rfe1 Rxe1+ 26. Rxe1 Rxe1+ 27. Qxe1 c5 28. bxc5 Qxc5 $15) 24... R5e7 25. Rfe1 c5 26. bxc5 (26. Rxe7 Rxe7 27. bxc5 Nxc5 28. Nf4 Ne6 29. Ndxe6 Bxe6 30. Re1 $11) 26... Nxc5 27. Nf4 (27. Rxe7 Rxe7 28. Nf4 $11) 27... Rxe1+ 28. Rxe1 Rxe1+ 29. Qxe1 Ne6 {Carlsen does not want to risk a situation in which the white knights will become too dangerous.} 30. Nfxe6 (30. Ndxe6 Bxe6 31. Qe3 Qa6 32. g4! Qxa2 33. Nxh3 a5 34. Ng5 $11) 30... Bxe6 31. Kf2 {[%mdl 4096] For a very original player like Jobava the situation on the board must have seemed a bit tedious, but he certainly had some problems judging it correctly to the last detail. Exchanging the minor pieces should have given White an equal position.} (31. Nxe6 fxe6 32. Qe3 Qa6 33. a4 $11) 31... Bd7 32. Qb1 Qc7 33. Qb3 Bc8 34. a3 (34. Qxd5 Qxc3 35. Qd8+ Kg7 36. Ne2 $11) 34... a5 35. Qb2 {White is unsure if opening of the position will be enough for a draw, so he decides to wait.} (35. Qxd5! Qxc3 36. Qd8+ Kh7 37. Ne2 Qc5+ 38. Ke1 Be6 39. Qh4+ Kg8 40. Qd8+ $11) 35... Ba6 36. Nc2 Qa7+ 37. Ne3 Bc4 38. f4 Kh7 39. g4 {The proverbial moves just before the time control are already made under some pressure.} (39. a4 Qd7 40. Qc2 Qc6 $15) 39... Qe7 40. Qc1?! (40. a4! Qe4 41. Qb8 Ba2 $15) 40... Qe4 41. f5 Qf4+ 42. Kg1 gxf5 43. gxf5 Bd3 44. Qe1 Bxf5 (44... d4 45. cxd4 Qxd4 46. Qd2 Kg7 47. Kf2 Qf4+ 48. Ke1 Bb5 $15) 45. Nxf5? {Just like tennis players chess players should also know that a very important part of the match with better opponents is inner confidence, that a loss is not the inevitable result. Jobava is trading minor pieces and the black position strikingly jumps in value.} (45. Qf2! Qg5+ 46. Qg3 Qh6 47. Qe5 Be6 $15) 45... Qxf5 46. Qg3 Kh6! {The white king is locked in on g1 and the queen endgame proves to be simply hopeless.} 47. Qb8 (47. c4 dxc4 48. Qh4+ Kg7 49. Qd4+ Qf6 50. Qxc4 Qg5+ 51. Kf2 Qg2+ 52. Ke3 Qxh2 53. Qd4+ Kg6 54. Qe4+ Kf6 55. Qh4+ Ke6 56. Qc4+ Ke7 57. Qh4+ f6 58. Qh7+ Kd6 $19) 47... Qf3 48. Qh8+ Kg6 49. Qg8+ Kf6 50. Qd8+ (50. a4 Qe3+ 51. Kf1 Qf4+ 52. Ke1 Qxh2 $19) 50... Kf5 0-1

