1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 a6 5. Qc2 e6 6. Nf3 Nbd7 7. Bd2 {White is finding it difficult to get some advantage against his opponent's flexible setup. The most popular move is still 7.b3.} (7. b3 c5 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Bd3 Bd6 10. O-O O-O 11. e4 cxd4 12. Nxd5 h6 13. Bb2 Nxd5 14. exd5 Nf6 15. Nxd4 Nxd5 16. Nf5 Bxf5 17. Bxf5 $14 {½-½ Grigorov,G (2479)-Georgiev,V (2582)/Bansko 2010 (102)}) 7... dxc4 8. a4 c5 9. Bxc4 cxd4 10. exd4 Nb6 {Nakamura plays the line also with the white pieces and is well aware of the fact that Black has some work to do before he can claim equality.} (10... Be7 11. O-O O-O 12. Rac1 (12. Rad1 Nb6 13. Bb3 Bd7 14. Bg5 Bc6 15. Ne5 Nbd5 16. Qe2 Qd6 17. Rd3 $14 {½-½ Schlosser,P (2534)-Gustafsson,J (2566)/Austria 2003}) 12... Nb6 13. Bd3 Nbd5 (13... h6 14. Qb3 Nbd5 15. Nxd5 Nxd5 16. Bc4 Bd7 17. Bxd5 exd5 18. Ne5 {The pressure of white pieces is pretty unpleasant despite the symmetrical pawn structure.} Bf6 19. Ba5! Qe8 20. Bb4 Bxa4 21. Qa3 Bxe5 22. dxe5 Bb5 23. Rfe1 $16 {½-½ Nakamura,H (2715)-Ni Hua (2665)/London 2009 (46)}) 14. Nxd5 Qxd5 15. Bg5 h6 16. Bxf6 Bxf6 17. Bh7+ Kh8 18. Be4 Qa5 19. Ne5 Bxe5 20. dxe5 Bd7 21. Qc7 Qxc7 22. Rxc7 Bxa4 $14 {½-½ Schlosser,P (2557)-Buhmann,R (2591)/Austria 2010 (30)}) 11. Bd3 $146 {Carlsen comes up with a new move and it can well be called a fresh concept. The bishop from the d3-square is eying the h7-pawn.} (11. Bb3 Be7 12. O-O O-O 13. Rac1 Bd7 14. Ne5 Rc8 15. Qd3 Bc6 16. Nxc6 Rxc6 17. a5 Na8 (17... Rd6 18. Ne2 Nbd5 19. Rc2 Qb8 20. Rfc1 $14 {½-½ Meier,G (2659)-Acs,P (2601)/Germany 2010 (59)}) 18. Rfd1 Rc8 19. d5 Nc7 20. Qg3 Nfxd5 21. Nxd5 Nxd5 22. Rxc8 Qxc8 23. Bxd5 exd5 24. Bc3 f6 25. Rxd5 Rd8 $11 {½-½ Gervasio,R (2369)-Velikov,P (2492)/Clichy 1999 (66)}) 11... Nbd5 12. Nxd5 Nxd5 {Recapturing with the black queen is not easy to punish, but White would have kept some edge.} (12... exd5 13. O-O Be7 14. Rfe1 O-O 15. Rac1 $16) (12... Qxd5!? 13. O-O Bd7 14. Bc4 Qh5 15. Ne5 Bd6 16. f4 Ng4 17. h3 Nxe5 18. fxe5 $14) 13. O-O Nb4?! {The golden rule of caution with active plans when behind in development has been violated a thousand times - even by very strong players. Can Black afford to exchange one of the white bishops?} (13... Bd6 14. Be4 (14. Rac1 Nf4 15. Bc4 O-O 16. Rfe1 (16. g3 b5! $13) 16... b5! 17. axb5 Bb7 $11) 14... Nf6 15. Rfc1 Nxe4 16. Qxe4 O-O 17. a5 f6 $14) 14. Bxb4 Bxb4 15. Bxh7! g6 (15... Bd6 16. Bd3 Bd7 (16... Bxh2+? 17. Nxh2 Qh4 18. Qc7 $18) 17. Qe2 Rc8 18. h3 $16) 16. Bxg6 fxg6 17. Qxg6+ Kf8 {The American chess star is very confident. Not every player would dare to play such a dangerous position against the top player of the FIDE rating list.} (17... Kd7 18. Qg7+ Kd6 19. Rfc1 $18) 18. Ne5 Qe7 {White is much better, so the defender has to play with caution in order to keep some counterplay.} (18... Qe8 19. Qf6+ Kg8 20. Qg5+ Kf8 (20... Kh7 21. Rfc1 Rg8 22. Rc7+ Bd7 23. Qh4+ $18) 21. Ng6+ Kg7 22. Nxh8+ Kxh8 23. Rfc1 $18) 19. Rac1 Qh7 (19... Rg8 20. Qh5 Qg7 21. g3 Bd6 22. Rfd1 Ke7 23. Rd3 $16) 20. Qg3 {It would be more difficult to find the courage for the transition into a promising endgame.} (20. h4!? Qxg6 21. Nxg6+ Kg8 22. Nxh8 Kxh8 23. Rc7 Bd6 24. Rfc1 Bxc7 25. Rxc7 b5 26. a5 $16) 20... Ke8 21. d5! {[%mdl 128] Carlsen has a clear goal - to attack the black king. The defender's pieces are poorly coordinated.} Bd6 (21... exd5 22. Ng6 Be6 23. Qe5 Qxg6 24. Qxh8+ Qg8 25. Qe5 $18) 22. Rfe1 Bd7 (22... Qg8 23. Qf3 Qh7 24. h3 $16) 23. h3 {The white queen deserves a better future than serving as a passive defender of the kingside.} Bxe5 (23... Rf8 24. dxe6 Bc6 (24... Bxe6 25. Qe3 Be7 26. Nf3 Bd7 27. Rc7 $18) 25. Rxc6! bxc6 26. Qc3 Rc8 27. Nd7 $18) 24. Rxe5 Rg8 25. Qe3 Rg6 (25... Qg6 26. g4 Rc8 27. Rxc8+ Bxc8 28. dxe6 $18) 26. dxe6 Bc6 (26... Bc8 27. Rd1 Qg7 28. Qe4 $18) 27. Rxc6! {Excellent chess understanding has enabled White to destroy the dangerous bishop without hesitation. Black will go under due to the lack of piece coordination.} bxc6 28. Qe4 Qg7 (28... Rc8 29. Rg5! Rg7 30. Qxh7 Rxh7 31. Rg8+ Ke7 32. Rxc8 $18) (28... Ke7 29. Qxc6 Rxg2+ 30. Qxg2 (30. Kxg2 Qg7+ 31. Kf1 Qxe5 32. Qxa8 $18) 30... Rg8 31. Rg5 Qb1+ 32. Kh2 Rxg5 33. Qxg5+ Kxe6 34. b4 $18) 29. Qxc6+ Ke7 30. Qc5+ Ke8 31. Qc6+ Ke7 32. Re3! {The rook retreats to a safe square, but still keeps the mobility along the 3rd rank.} Ra7 (32... Rg8 33. Qc5+ Ke8 34. Rb3 Rxe6 35. Rb8+ Kf7 36. Rb7+ $18) (32... Rd8 33. Qb7+ Ke8 34. e7 Rxg2+ 35. Qxg2 Qxg2+ 36. Kxg2 $18) 33. Qc5+ (33. Qb6!) 33... Ke8 34. Qc6+ Ke7 35. Qb6! {The white queen dominates the rook on a7, so it makes sense to sacrifice the important g2-pawn.} Rxg2+ 36. Kf1 Rg1+ 37. Ke2 Ra8 38. Qb7+ Kd6 (38... Kf8 39. Qxa8+ $18) (38... Kf6 39. Qxa8 $18) 39. Rd3+ Kxe6 (39... Kc5 40. b4+ Kc4 41. Qe4+ Qd4 42. Qxd4#) (39... Ke5 40. Qxa8 $18) 40. Qc6+ {The black king ends its life in the centre hunted by the two unrestrained white major pieces.} (40. Qc6+ Kf5 41. Rd5+ Qe5+ 42. Rxe5+ Kxe5 43. Qxa8 $18) 1-0

