{[%evp 0,90,20,20,20,20,29,29,66,41,47,44,26,20,19,28,29,13,13,13,16,15,32,22,22,19,24,4,26,-12,-7,-2,15,3,3,19,19,19,19,0,28,28,28,28,28,-53,-58,-52,-51,-44,-68,-81,-64,-64,-55,-54,-61,-85,-106,-106,-46,-67,-46,-46,-46,-44,-65,-65,-65,-65,-26,-41,-35,-35,-38,-140,-140,-143,-143,-333,-267,-272,-272,-479,-542,-554,-600,-1097,-1126,-1146,-1146,-1146,-1189]} {Every now and then Magnu successfully applies some of the 'overtures' from my personal repertoire. I recall his wonderful Dutch Stonewall versus Anand, or the recent white victories in the Nimzo-Indian with 4.a3 and the Grünfeld 5.d2!? etc. Today I have a happy opportunity to comment on his play with black pieces in my favorite French Defence.} 1. e4 e6 {Magnus himself considered ''the French Defence to be a second-rate opening at best''. Nevertheless, he often used it, even in his decisive games.} 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 Be7!? {A modern interpretation of the Classical System. Black actually refrains from the standard queenside action (7...a6 or 7...cxd4) in favour of play in the centre with ...b6 and ...f6.} 8. a3 {Not a very logical move, Black still has not advanced ...a6/...b5. But most importantly, with this tempo White greatly reduces his attacking potential.} ({The main theoretical direction is} 8. Qd2 {also played by Carlsen on both sides:} O-O ({Black can still switch the game to a flexible and trendy hybrid with} 8... a6!? {I advise to watch the successful current online games of Caruana and Kramnik}) (8... b6 {is premature} 9. Bd3 (9. Bb5! {is the strongest idea, intending 0-0-0 and f4-f5}) 9... O-O (9... f6!) 10. O-O (10. h4!?) 10... f6 11. exf6 Nxf6 $132 {1-0 (44) Carlsen,M (2837)-Morozevich,A (2770) Astana 2012}) 9. Be2 ({today} 9. dxc5!? {become the main answer}) (9. Bd3 f6!) (9. g3 a6 10. Ne2 b5 $132 {1-0 (27) Carlsen,M (2765)-Jimenez Capilla,J (2029) Madrid 2008}) 9... a6 10. O-O b5 11. Kh1 Qc7 12. a3 Bb7 13. Rad1 Rac8 14. Qe1 cxd4 15. Nxd4 Nxd4 16. Bxd4 Bc5 17. Qh4? (17. Qf2 $142) 17... Bxd4 18. Rxd4 f6! $17 {0-1 (45) Karjakin,S (2720)-Carlsen,M (2810) Wijk aan Zee 2010}) 8... O-O 9. Qd2 {A rather inconsistent decision for Ivanchuk, to return to the usual paths. However, in the French structures, you never know the exact recipe.} b6!? {Coincides with the theme of Black's modern set-up.} 10. g3 ({The stem game already showed that Black is in perfect order:} 10. Bd3 f6!? 11. exf6 Nxf6 $132 {0-1 (49) Macieja,B (2470)-Morozevich,A (2590) St Petersburg 1997}) 10... Kh8 {For example, now Black is losing an important tempo,} ({although it seems logical in the event of} 10... f6 11. Bh3 Kh8!? $13) 11. h4! {Finally an attacking move!} f6 12. exf6 ({More critical was} 12. O-O-O!? {keeping the tension in the centre.}) 12... Nxf6 13. Bh3 {This manoeuvre does not make much sense.} ({Still} 13. O-O-O!? {is the best idea:} Ne4? (13... c4 14. Ne5 $13 {½-½ (28) Sutkalenko,D (2253)-Williams,J (2122) ICCF email 2013}) 14. Nxe4 dxe4 15. Bb5 $18 {1-0 (48) Vocaturo,D (2579)-Rombaldoni,A (2513) Condino 2014}) 13... Bd7 14. Rd1 cxd4 15. Nxd4 e5 ({Now Black had a chance with} 15... Na5! $36 {as in case of} 16. Nxe6 Qc8! {, according to the engine Black is winning, but it is difficult to assess the position even for the best players in the world.}) 16. Nxc6 Bxc6 17. fxe5 d4 (17... Nh5!?) 18. exf6 Bxf6 19. O-O dxc3!? 20. Qxd8 Raxd8 21. Rxd8 Rxd8 22. b3? {From here on the Norwegian GM proceeds to outplay the tournament leader in this double-bishops ending. Typical Carlsen.} (22. bxc3! $11 {definitively eliminates the danger of Black's c-pawn.}) 22... Re8! 23. Bf2 Be4 24. Re1 Re7 25. Rc1 h5 26. Bg2 Bf5 27. Bf3 g6 28. a4 Kh7 29. b4 g5? {Inaccuracy is inherent in important games.} ({Black could have obtained a technical win using a typical roundabout with the bishop:} 29... Bg7! 30. a5 bxa5 31. bxa5 {and now} Bh6 {with the idea} 32. Re1 Rxe1+ 33. Bxe1 Bd2! $19 {etc.}) 30. a5! g4 31. axb6? (31. Bd5 $11 {defending with b3.}) 31... axb6? {It is well known that Magnus likes to make the most practical decisions at the board.} ({It was principled to accept the sacrifice} 31... gxf3 32. bxa7 Be4 {that only at first glance seems complicated, but should be won for Black.}) 32. Bc6 Re2 33. Bxb6 Be5 34. b5 Kg7 35. Ba5 Rxc2 36. Rxc2 Bxc2 37. Kf2? (37. b6 {would have probably held the position.}) 37... Bd3? {This already looks like a game of ping-pong.} (37... Bg6! $19) 38. Ke3 Bg6 39. Be4? {The last mistake.} ({Once again Ivanchuk could have tried to defend with} 39. b6) 39... c2 40. Bd2 Bxg3 41. Bxg6 Kxg6 42. Kd3 Bf2 43. Kxc2 g3 44. Bf4 g2 45. Bh2 Kf5 {Nicely played by the amazing Magnus. A game to study, especially the sharp ending!} 0-1

