[PlyCount "127"]

{[%evp 0,28,19,28,20,30,49,35,35,28,30,11,22,34,54,25,18,10,10,-9,-8,-5,-32,-29,-35,-40,-30,-21,-25,-19,-16] This is the fourth game of the match in a preliminary stage of the tournament. First 3 games were drawn, with Svidler defending some pretty shaky positions.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. Bf4 Bg7 4. e3 O-O 5. Be2 c5 6. O-O Qb6 7. Nbd2 d5 (7... Qxb2 {was surprisingly possible, but nobody does that in rapid, of course -} 8. Nc4 Qb5!) 8. Qc1 {a bit passive. After} (8. dxc5 {White would have more chances to fight for initiative, for instance} Qxb2 9. Be5 Qa3 10. c4 Qxc5 11. Nb3 Qc6 12. Rc1) 8... Nc6 9. c3 Nh5! 10. Bg5 h6 11. Bh4 g5 12. Bg3 Nxg3 13. hxg3 {White played the opening as if he were playing with the black pieces. A similar strategy was often employed by Gata Kamsky.} e5 {Not the only way to continue.} (13... Bf5 14. Nb3 c4 15. Nbd2 Rac8 16. b3 cxb3 17. axb3 a6 18. b4 {[%cal Gd2b3] White keeps his fair share of chances here.}) 14. Nxe5 Nxe5 15. dxe5 Bxe5 16. e4 dxe4 17. Nc4!? {Magnus starts a very brave attempt at complicating matters.} (17. Nxe4 Bf5 18. Nd2 Rad8 19. Nc4 Qc7 $11 {is much more simple.}) 17... Qe6 (17... Qc7 18. Qe3 Bf5 19. Rad1 Rad8 20. Nxe5 Qxe5 21. f4 {the point} gxf4 22. gxf4 Qg7 {with a mess}) 18. Qe3 Bg7?! {Maybe slightly inaccurate.} ({After} 18... Bc7! {Black controls d6, and feels much safer.} 19. Qxc5 Qc6! 20. Qxc6 bxc6 {and only Black could be better here, though White should be able to hold, of course.} 21. g4 Be6 22. Nd2 f5 23. gxf5 Bxf5) 19. Qxc5 (19. Rad1!? {Not a bad idea, actually.} b6 20. Nd6 Qe5 21. Nxc8 Raxc8 22. Rd7 Rc7 (22... Qe6 23. Rxa7) 23. Rfd1 {and White has a lasting compensation for the pawn.}) 19... b6 (19... Qc6!? {was maybe simpler:} 20. Qxc6 bxc6 21. Rfd1 Be6 {probably leads to equality after} 22. Na5 Rab8 23. Rd2 Rb6 ({or} 23... Rxb2 24. Rxb2 Bxc3 25. Rab1 Bxa5 $44)) 20. Qa3 f5?! {Very natural, but does create weaknesses and ignores the development a bit too much.} ({After} 20... Qc6 21. Rad1 Be6 22. Ne3 Rfd8 {Black keeps his king very safe and has a full share of chances:} 23. Qa6 (23. Rxd8+ Rxd8 24. Qxa7 Bf8 25. Rd1 Ra8 26. Bb5 Rxa7 27. Bxc6 Rxa2) 23... f5 24. g4 f4 25. Nf5 Kh8) 21. Rad1 {Now Black has to solve some serious tactical problems.} Bd7 {Maybe not the best.} (21... Qg6 22. g4!? (22. Rd6 Be6 23. Ne3 Kh8 24. Bc4 Rf6 {Black hangs on in this slightly awkward manner.} 25. Qb3 Re8 26. Bxe6 Rfxe6 27. Rxe6 Rxe6 28. Qd5 f4 29. Nf5 Re5 30. Qd8+ Re8 31. Qd7 Bf8 $13) 22... fxg4 23. Rd6 Qe8 24. g3 Kh8 25. Ne3 Be6 26. Qb4 Bg8 27. Bc4 $44) (21... f4 22. Nd6 (22. Rd6 Qe7 23. Nxb6 axb6 24. Bc4+ Be6! 25. Rxe6 Qxa3 26. bxa3 Kh8 $14) 22... Kh8 23. gxf4 Rxf4 (23... gxf4 24. Nxe4! Bb7 25. Bf3! $16) 24. Bc4 Qe7 25. Bd5 Rb8 26. Bxe4 $14) (21... Kh8 22. Rd6 Qf7 23. Nxb6 $16) 22. Rd6 (22. Ne3! {was already very strong:} Kh8 23. g4 (23. Rd6!? Qe8 {transposes, see below.}) 23... f4 24. Nf5 Be5 (24... Rxf5 25. gxf5 Qxf5 26. Qe7 $16) 25. Bc4 Qe8 26. Bd5 $16) 22... Qe8 (22... Qe7 23. Ne3 (23. Nxb6 {is too cheap:} axb6 24. Bc4+ Be6 25. Rxe6 Qxa3 $14) 23... Rf6 24. Rfd1 {White keeps quite some pressure.}) 23. Bh5?! {Not the best, but finding the right line would be tough under any circumctances.} (23. Ne3! Kh8 (23... Be6 24. Rxe6!) (23... f4 24. Nd5 f3 25. gxf3 exf3 26. Bc4 Kh8 27. Ne7 $16) 24. Nd5 Qf7 25. Bh5! {Now!} Qxh5 26. Rxd7 Rf7 27. Qa4 Rxd7 28. Qxd7 Rf8 29. Ne7! Qf7 30. Rd1 f4 31. gxf4 gxf4 32. Qd6 Kh7 33. Re1 $16) (23. Rfd1 {allows some play:} Bb5 (23... Be6 24. Ne3 Kh8) 24. R6d5 e3) 23... Qe7 (23... Qxh5 24. Rxd7 Rf7 25. Rfd1 f4 26. gxf4 gxf4 27. Qb3 Rxd7 28. Rxd7 Kh8 29. Qd1 {is still a bit better for White.}) 24. Ne3 (24. Rfd1 Be6 25. Qa4 Rad8 26. Qc6 Rxd6 27. Rxd6 Rf6 {doesn't feel clear}) 24... Be6 25. Bd1! {Undoubtedly, the point behind the plan with Bh5. Now Black has a choice.} (25. g4 f4 26. Nf5 Bxf5 27. gxf5 Rad8 28. Rfd1 Rxd6 29. Rxd6 g4! 30. Bxg4 Qh4) 25... f4 {This leads to what looks like a draw, but things are very deceptive here...} (25... Rae8!? {looks wrong, but in fact very solid.} 26. Bb3 (26. Rxe6 Qxa3 27. bxa3 Rxe6 28. Bb3 Kf7 29. Nxf5 Bxc3 {White really has nothing to gain out of the pin, and even has to be careful.} 30. Nxh6+ Ke7 31. Bxe6 Kxe6) 26... Bxb3 27. Qxb3+ Kh8 28. Rd5 f4 29. Nf5 Qe6 30. g4 e3 {looks very complex.}) (25... Kh8 26. Bb3! f4 27. Nd5 (27. gxf4 gxf4 28. Rxe6 Qxa3 29. bxa3 fxe3 30. fxe3 Rac8!) 27... Qf7 28. Nxf4 Bxb3 29. Ng6+ Kg8 30. Qxb3 Qxb3 31. axb3 Rfe8 32. f4 {Here White keeps some winning chances though.}) 26. Rxe6! (26. gxf4 gxf4 27. Rxe6 Qxa3 28. bxa3 fxe3 29. fxe3 {leaves less pawns on the board and probably favors Black.}) 26... Qxa3 27. bxa3 fxe3 28. fxe3 Rxf1+ 29. Kxf1 {This endgame looks like it shouldn't be too difficult, but Magnus is especially dangerous in these positions. The rest of the game is deeply impressive.} Kf8 (29... Bxc3 30. Bb3 Kg7 31. Rxe4 $14) (29... Rd8!? 30. Bb3 Kf8 {This type of finesse is beyond humans.} 31. Ke2 (31. Rxe4 Re8!) 31... Bxc3 32. Rxe4 Rd2+ 33. Kf3 h5 34. g4 h4 35. Re6 Rb2 36. Rg6 Be1 {The brilliant point of Black's counterplay.} 37. Rxg5 Rf2+ 38. Ke4 Rxg2) 30. Ke2 Rc8 31. Bb3 Rxc3 32. Rxe4 Rc5?! {Natural, but not the best.} (32... Rc6! {A great idea, playing for the domination of white rook. Now even Magnus would find it difficult to generate chances.} 33. g4 (33. Kf3 Bb2 34. a4 h5 35. g4 h4) (33. Ba4 Rc3 34. Re8+ Kf7 35. Ra8 Rxa3 $11) 33... Bb2! 34. a4 a5 {[%csl Ge4] a unique picture, with a "trapped" rook in the center of the board.}) 33. Re6! Re5 {More decisive measures were called for.} (33... h5! {[%cal Gg5g4]} 34. Kd3 (34. g4 hxg4 35. Re4 Bb2 36. a4 Re5 37. Rxg4 Bc1) 34... g4 35. Ke4 Bb2 {[%cal Gc5c7,Gc7e7] with chances for counterplay.}) 34. Rd6 (34. Rc6 Rc5 35. Rd6 {has a point, too.}) 34... Ke7 {Svidler plans on holding the game by staying solid, but perhaps he should have grabbed the chance for some activity.} (34... g4! {[%csl Gg3][%cal Gh6h5]}) 35. Rg6 Kf8 (35... Bf8!? {with the same theme, trying to limit White's rook activity, was the best defence.} 36. a4 (36. g4 Kd7) 36... Kd7 37. Kd3 {[%cal Ge3e4,Gb3d5] but still tough, of course.}) 36. g4! {Now the World Champion turns into a well-tuned machine.} Rc5 37. a4! {We'll see soon enough why.} Re5 38. Kd3 Rc5 39. Bc4 Re5 40. Rc6 Rc5 41. Rd6 Ke7 42. Rg6 Kf8 43. e4! {Actually, zugzwang.} Rc7 44. a5! bxa5 45. Ra6 Ke7? {A loss of focus, but Svidler was badly pressed on time.} ({After the better} 45... Rd7+ 46. Bd5 Rc7 47. Rxa5 Bb2 {Black still has some chances, but his task is not enviable.}) 46. e5! {Magnus is very focused and grabs his chance.} Kf8 (46... a4 47. Bd5 Kf8 48. Ke4 {doesn't change much.}) 47. Rxa5 Ke7 48. Bb3 Rb7 49. Ke4 Bf8 50. Kf5 Rc7 51. Ra6 Ke8 52. Kg6 Rb7 53. Re6+ {A slight slip.} (53. e6! Rc7 54. Ra5 {[%cal Ga5f5] was game over.}) 53... Kd8? (53... Be7 {and there's still a bit of work to be done.} 54. Bd5 (54. Kxh6 Rb6!) 54... Rb4 55. Bc6+ Kf8 56. Kxh6 Rxg4 57. Kg6 Rb4) 54. Rf6 Be7 55. Kxh6! Rb5 56. Rf5 Rb4 57. e6 Rxg4 58. Rd5+ Ke8 59. Kg6 Rf4 60. Ra5 Kf8 61. Rxa7 Rf6+ 62. Kh7 Bd6 63. Rf7+ Rxf7+ 64. exf7 {One of those games that, despite visual simplicity, have a lot of content, especially for the theme of choosing between varying defensive formations under pressure. At the end of this game Magnus still had about 10 minutes, having started with 15. I can't think of many players in history capable of this level at this speed. Very, very impressive stuff.} 1-0

