1. Nf3 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Nf6 {[%emt 0:00:00]} 2. c4 {[%emt 0:00:00]} e6 {[%emt 0:00:01]} 3. b3 {[%emt 0:00:09]} d5 {[%emt 0:00:01]} 4. Bb2 {[%emt 0:00:03]} Be7 {[%emt 0:00:02]} 5. e3 {[%emt 0:00:03] Sparing me another annotation of a b3 Catalan.} O-O {[%emt 0:00:16]} 6. Nc3 {[%emt 0:00:03]} c5 {[%emt 0:00:03]} (6... b6! $11 {would have been more in keeping with the logic of Anish's main game today- avoiding potentially awkward hanging-pawns formations.}) 7. cxd5 {[%emt 0:00:07] Just as theorised in the notes to the main game, this is the way White should play. Obviously d4 is fine immediately, but why not make Black play with a central structure he doesn't like?} Nxd5 {[%emt 0:00:01]} 8. Nxd5 {[%emt 0:00:09]} exd5 {[%emt 0:00:03]} (8... Qxd5 9. Bc4 Qd6 10. O-O Nc6 $11 {still looks fine for Black, if a bit passive.}) 9. d4 {[%emt 0:00:04]} Qa5+ {[%emt 0:00:10] Technically the novelty.} (9... Nc6 10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. a3 Bg4 12. Be2 a5 13. O-O Qd6 14. Qd3 Bh5 15. Rfd1 Rfd8 16. Nh4 Bxe2 17. Qxe2 Qe6 18. Nf3 $14 {Votava,J-Froewis,G Austria 2016}) 10. Qd2 {[%emt 0:00:03]} Qxd2+ {[%emt 0:00:03]} 11. Kxd2 {[%emt 0:00:04]} Nc6 {[%emt 0:00:00]} 12. dxc5 {[%emt 0:00:12]} Bxc5 {[%emt 0:00:02]} 13. Bb5 {[%emt 0:00:02] So the position is still fine for Black, but White is taking no risks, and can choose to play against the IQP 'as is' or maybe with some kind of xc6, fc1, d4-c5 thing. Ideal for blitz.} Bb4+ {[%emt 0:00:15]} 14. Ke2 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Be6 {[%emt 0:00:12]} (14... Ne7 $14 {might not have been a terrible idea under the circumstances.}) 15. Rac1 {[%emt 0:00:10]} Rac8 {[%emt 0:00:01]} 16. Rhd1 {[%emt 0:00:12]} (16. Bxc6 bxc6 17. Ne5 {was a useful tactical chance: Black can either sacrifice the pawn or allow} c5 18. Nd3 {but in either case he is definitely suffering.}) 16... Be7 {[%emt 0:00:20]} 17. h3 {[%emt 0:00:16]} a6 {[%emt 0:00:06]} 18. Bd3 {[%emt 0:00:01]} Nb4 {[%emt 0:00:01]} 19. Bb1 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Rxc1 {[%emt 0:00:03]} 20. Rxc1 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Rc8 {[%emt 0:00:01]} 21. Rd1 {[%emt 0:00:13]} Nc6 {[%emt 0:00:02]} 22. g4 {[%emt 0:00:07] Thinking about f5, and also restricting Black's kingside pawns, allowing for a future f4-f5 thrust.} h6 {[%emt 0:00:07]} 23. Nd4 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Nxd4+ {[%emt 0:00:06]} 24. Bxd4 {[%emt 0:00:01]} Ba3 {[%emt 0:00:10]} 25. f4 {[%emt 0:00:21] Like that.} f6 {[%emt 0:00:09]} 26. Bg6 {[%emt 0:00:07]} (26. e4 dxe4 27. Bxe4 $14 {looked quite convincing, even though it liquidates the most immediate target of White's pressure.}) 26... Kf8 {[%emt 0:00:05]} 27. Kf3 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Ke7 {[%emt 0:00:09]} 28. h4 {[%emt 0:00:06]} (28. b4!? {was worth noticing, but after} Bxb4 29. Rb1 a5 30. a3 Bd6 31. Rxb7+ Rc7 32. Rb8 Rc8 33. Rb5 a4 $11 {it all just fizzles out a bit.}) 28... Bb4 {[%emt 0:00:16]} 29. Bd3 {[%emt 0:00:21]} Bd7 {[%emt 0:00:21]} 30. e4 {[%emt 0:00:37] Eventually White had to make this break, though he has spent enough time in the process that Black is probably objectively fine again.} Bc3?! {[%emt 0:00:26]} (30... dxe4+ 31. Bxe4 Bc6 32. h5 Bxe4+ 33. Kxe4 Ke6! $11 (33... Rc2? 34. Kf5 $36)) 31. Bf2 {[%emt 0:00:12]} Bc6 {[%emt 0:00:07]} 32. exd5 {[%emt 0:00:01]} Bxd5+ {[%emt 0:00:01]} 33. Be4 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Bxe4+ {[%emt 0:00:04]} 34. Kxe4 {[%emt 0:00:01]} Ke6?! {[%emt 0:00:11] Just like the previous note, except Black's pieces are now out of sync.} (34... Bb4 35. Be3 (35. Kf5 Rc2 36. Bd4 Kf7! $11) 35... Ke6 36. a4 $14 {Black has not completely equalised here, but it's better than the game.}) 35. f5+ {[%emt 0:00:11]} Ke7 {[%emt 0:00:01]} 36. Rc1 {[%emt 0:00:02] Forcing a favourable bishop endgame.} Rc6? {[%emt 0:00:21] Definitely losing and almost certainly the decisive mistake.} (36... Kd7 37. Kd3 Bb4 38. Rxc8 Kxc8 39. Kc4 Bd6 40. Kd5 Kd7 41. a4 $16 {might also be winning, or it might not - but White can play with various ideas, like d4 and then g5, or c5 at the right moment. I suspect Black has just enough answers.}) 37. Kd3 {[%emt 0:00:07]} Bb4 {[%emt 0:00:21]} 38. Rxc6 {[%emt 0:00:02]} bxc6 {[%emt 0:00:01]} 39. Kc4 {[%emt 0:00:01]} Bd6 {[%emt 0:00:03]} 40. Bc5 {[%emt 0:00:45]} Kd7 {[%emt 0:00:00]} 41. h5 {[%emt 0:00:16]} Bf4 {[%emt 0:00:05]} (41... Kc7 42. Bxd6+ Kxd6 43. b4 $18) 42. Bf8 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Ke8 {[%emt 0:00:04] The last idea is to try and trap this bishop, but White can just go back and pick a better time for the incursion.} 43. Bc5 {[%emt 0:00:12]} Kd7 {[%emt 0:00:03]} (43... Bc7 {was another place to try and defend from.} 44. Kb4 Bd8 (44... a5+ 45. Ka4 Bd8 46. b4 axb4 47. Kxb4 Bc7 48. a4 $18) 45. Bd6 Bb6 46. a4 {However, it is now zugwang. Black has to play ...a5, and then the game is almost over.} a5+ 47. Kc4 Be3 48. b4 axb4 49. Kxb4 {White can walk the pawn to a6 and then play c5.}) 44. Kb4 {[%emt 0:00:09]} Bd2+ {[%emt 0:00:09]} 45. Ka4 {[%emt 0:00:01]} Kc7 {[%emt 0:00:12]} 46. b4 {[%emt 0:00:07] The black king is now caught trying to do two jobs at the same time.} Bf4 {[%emt 0:00:43]} 47. Bf8 {[%emt 0:00:05]} Kb6 {[%emt 0:00:07]} ({of course, after} 47... Kd7 48. Ka5 Ke8 {White is not obliged to take on g7, but can conserve his bishop with} 49. Bc5 $18) 48. Bxg7 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Bg5 {[%emt 0:00:01]} 49. Bf8 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Bf4 {[%emt 0:00:04]} 50. Be7 {[%emt 0:00:04]} Bg5 {[%emt 0:00:07]} 51. Kb3 {[%emt 0:00:08] Heading back to win with e3.} (51. Bd8+ Kb7 52. Ka5 Bh4 53. Be7 Bg5 54. Bc5 Bf4 55. Bd4 Bg5 56. a3 $18 {is another zugzwang idea to win.}) 51... Kc7 {[%emt 0:00:04]} 52. Kc4 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Kd7 {[%emt 0:00:08]} 53. Bc5 {[%emt 0:00:01]} Kc7 {[%emt 0:00:03]} 54. Kd3 {[%emt 0:00:11]} Kd7 {[%emt 0:00:19]} 55. Be3 {[%emt 0:00:02]} 1-0

