1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. h3 Na6 7. Be3 {In my opinion, this move is less accurate than} (7. Bg5) 7... e5 8. d5 c6 9. g4 Nc5 10. Nd2 a5 11. a3 Nfd7 (11... a4 {was possible right now as it was hardly favourable for White to capture this pawn at the price of exchanging his dark-squared bishop and weakening the dark squares in his camp (especially after g2-g4):} 12. Bxc5 dxc5 13. dxc6?! (13. Nxa4 $142 Qa5 14. Nc3 Bh6 $44) 13... bxc6 14. Nxa4 Bh6 15. Nc3 Rb8 16. Rb1 Qa5 17. Bd3 Rd8 18. Qc2 Be6 19. Nf3 Bf4 $17 {0-1 Muse,M (2474)-Levacic,P (2336)/Medulin 2002/CBM 090 ext (42)}) 12. Rg1 {A surprising decision. White allows ...a5-a4.} ({In the event of} 12. b4 axb4 13. axb4 Rxa1 14. Qxa1 Na6 15. Qa3 c5 16. bxc5 Naxc5 {breaking up Black's queenside defence is not an easy task.}) 12... a4 {White's queenside is now paralysed but he's got a plan: long castling followed by a kingside pawn attack.} 13. Qc2 Nb6 14. O-O-O Bd7 15. Kb1 cxd5? {What looks like a small inaccuracy turns out to be a grave positional error.} (15... Rc8 $142) 16. cxd5 (16. exd5 f5 $132) 16... Rc8 17. Bb5! {[%mdl 32] That's the point. White trades bishops, weakening the light squares in Black's camp and his a4-pawn.} Bxb5 18. Nxb5 Qd7 19. Nc3 Bf6 (19... Rc7 20. h4 Rfc8 21. h5 {, and Black can't make further progress.}) 20. g5 Bd8 {This bishop is aiming for a5 but where can the b6-knight be moved to?} 21. h4 Na8 (21... Nb3 22. Nxb3 axb3 23. Qxb3 Nc4 {(S.Zagrebelny)} 24. Nb5 Nxe3 25. fxe3 {hardly gives Black any real compensation for the missing pawn.}) 22. Bxc5 Rxc5 23. Qxa4 $16 {/+- White has won a pawn and his pieces are also better positioned. The rest is a matter of technique.} Qc8 24. Rc1 Nb6 25. Qd1 Qh3 26. Qf3 Qd7 (26... Qxh4?? 27. Rg4 $18 {[%cal Rc1h1]}) (26... Qxf3 27. Nxf3 f5 {was relatively better.}) 27. Qd3 Kg7 28. Rc2 f6 {The last attempt to create counterplay.} 29. gxf6+ Rxf6 30. h5! {White has accurately calculated the consequences of this move.} Rxf2 31. hxg6 h6 (31... hxg6 32. Rxg6+ Kxg6 33. Qg3+ Bg5 34. Qxf2 $18) 32. Nd1! {It is essential for White to swap his passive c2 rook for Black's most active piece - the f2.} (32. Nf3! {was equally good, with the same idea.}) 32... Rxc2 (32... Rf8 33. Ne3 $18) (32... Rf6 33. Rxc5 dxc5 34. Ne3 Rxg6 35. Nf5+ Kh7 36. Rxg6 Kxg6 37. Nf3 $18 {}) 33. Nxf2! (33. Qxc2 $143 Rh2 34. Ne3 $140 Bg5) 33... Rc8 34. Ng4 Bg5 35. Nf3 Nc4 36. Nxg5 hxg5 37. Ne3 (37. Qh3! {(pointed out by many commentators) was winning on the spot:} Rh8 38. Qxh8+ Kxh8 39. Rh1+ $18) 37... Nxe3 38. Qxe3 Qa4 39. Qxg5 Qxe4+ 40. Ka1 Re8 (40... Rc7 41. Qd8 $18) 41. Rc1! (41. Rc1 Qf4 42. Rc7+ Kg8 43. Qh5 $18) 1-0

