1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 c5 5. d4 cxd4 6. Nxd4 O-O 7. Nc3 Qc7 {This provocative continuation is the second popular move in this position after} (7... Nc6) 8. Nd5!? (8. b3 d5!? {is Black's main idea seen in a number of games.}) 8... Qxc4 (8... Nxd5 9. cxd5 Qa5+ 10. Bd2 Qb6 11. Bc3 {leads to a quiet position with a small edge for White. The text move is sharper but not enough for equality either.}) 9. Nxe7+ Kh8 10. Nxc8 (10. O-O $143 Nc6 11. Ndxc6 (11. Nxc8 Raxc8 {[%cal Yf8e8]}) 11... dxc6 12. Nxc8 Raxc8 13. b3 Qc3 14. Bd2 Qe5 15. Qe1 Rfe8 16. e3 Ne4 17. Rc1 Rcd8 $132 {1/2 (38) Horvath,P (2458)-Horvath,C (2565) Hungary 2005}) 10... Rxc8 11. O-O Nc6 {Black's development advantage doesn't fully compensate for his damaged pawn structure.} 12. Be3 $146 {A simple developing move, enough for keeping a tiny plus. Alternatives:} (12. Nxc6?! dxc6 13. b3 Qc3 14. Bd2 Qe5 15. Rc1 Rd8 16. Rc2 Ne4 $11 {1/2 (60) Khurtsidze,N (2418)-Lomineishvili,M (2396) Tbilisi 2005}) (12. e3 Nxd4 13. exd4 d5 14. Be3 Rc6 15. Bf3 Rac8 16. Qb3 Qxb3 17. axb3 a6 18. Rfc1 Rxc1+ 19. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 20. Bxc1 Ne4 21. Be3 f5 $11 {1/2 (28) Sargissian,G (2602)-Radjabov,T (2667) Moscow 2005}) (12. Nb3!? Re8 13. e3 a5 14. Bd2 a4 15. Rc1 Qb5 16. Nc5 $36 (16. Nd4?! Nxd4 17. exd4 Qxb2 18. Rc2 Qxd4 19. Qc1 Ne4 $15 {1/2 (37) Wang,H (2710)-Aronian,L (2797) Beijing 2014})) (12. Nc2 Re8 (12... Ne4 13. Ne3 Qe6 14. Nd5 f5 15. Be3 Qe5 16. Nc3 Nxc3 17. bxc3 Rd8 18. Rb1 b6 19. c4 Rac8 20. Qa4 $16 {1-0 (34) Borovikov,V (2595)-Iskusnyh,S (2472) Pardubice 2005}) 13. Ne3 Qe6 14. Nd5 Nxd5 15. Bxd5 Qxe2 16. Bxf7 Re7 17. Qxe2 (17. Bd5 Nd4 18. Be3 Qxb2 19. Rb1 Ne2+ 20. Kg2 Nc3 21. Rxb2 Nxd1 22. Rxb7 Nxe3+ 23. fxe3 Rxe3 24. Rxd7 $14 {1/2 (32) Azaladze,S (2499)-Saric,S (2400) Novi Sad 2014}) 17... Rxe2 18. Rb1 Rf8 19. Bc4 Rexf2 20. Rxf2 Bd4 21. Bf4 g5 22. Kg2 Bxf2 23. Bxg5 Bd4 24. Rd1 Bxb2 25. Rxd7 Ne5 {1/2 (25) Wojtaszek,R (2569)-Fressinet,L (2638) Warsaw 2005} 26. Rd8! $14) 12... Ng4 {Black must deprive White of his bishop pair.} 13. Nc2 Nxe3 14. Nxe3 Qe6 15. Nd5 Bxb2 16. Rb1 Be5 17. Rxb7 $14 {As a result of the opening, White has got a small but clear advantage due to his strong d5 knight and the weakness of Black's d7 pawn.} Rab8 18. Rxb8 Rxb8 19. e3 Rb2 20. a4 Ne7 (20... Nb4 {was more active, and in case of} 21. Nf4 Qe7) 21. Nf4 {Black is now forced to trade his bishop, and the position simplifies even further. That gives White more chances than} (21. Nxe7 Qxe7 22. Qd3) 21... Bxf4 22. exf4 {[%cal Ge1e8,Rd1d4] White now wins a pawn as he is threatening Qd1-d4+.} Qb6 (22... Kg8 23. Qd4 {[%cal Rd4b2,Rd4a7]} Qb6 24. Qxd7) 23. a5 ({After} 23. Qxd7 Qc5 {White has hardly anything better than} 24. a5) 23... Qc5 24. Qxd7 Ra2 25. Qd3 Kg7 ({Black can recapture the pawn:} 25... Rxa5 {but after} 26. Qe2 {[%cal Yf1d1] White keeps pressure despite Black's passed pawn. He controls central files, and his king is safer. Instead, Black decides to prevent White's activation allowing White to keep his extra pawn.}) 26. a6 h5 27. Bb7 Nf5 28. Qe4 Nd6 29. Qb1 Rd2 30. Qa1+ Kg8 31. Bg2 Nf5 32. Qe5 Qxe5?! {A strange decision to undouble White's pawns and liquidate into a technical endgame.} (32... Qb6 {was consistent although after} 33. Qc3! Rb2 34. Bd5 {White activates his bishop and increases his winning chances.}) 33. fxe5 {[%mdl 4096]} Re2 (33... Ra2 34. Bb7 Re2 (34... h4!?) 35. f4 (35. Rd1 $143 Rxe5 36. Rd8+ Kg7 37. Ra8 Nd6 38. Rxa7 Re7) 35... Ne3 36. Rc1 {, and White should win anyway.}) (33... h4!? 34. Rb1 Nd4 35. gxh4 Ne2+ 36. Kh1 Nd4 {was probably the best chance to get counterplay.}) 34. Rb1 Rxe5 35. Rb7 Re1+ ({Black will be unable to keep defense on the 7th rank after} 35... Re7 36. Bh3 {as the pawn endgame after} Kf8 37. Bxf5 gxf5 38. Rxe7 Kxe7 39. Kg2 {is lost, e.g.} Ke6 (39... Kd6 40. Kh3 Kc6 41. Kh4 Kb6 42. Kxh5 Kxa6 43. h4 Kb5 44. Kg5 a5 45. h5 a4 46. h6 a3 47. h7 f6+ $8 48. Kxf5 a2 49. h8=Q a1=Q 50. Qxf6) 40. Kf3 Ke5 41. h3 f4 (41... Ke6 42. Kf4 Kf6 43. h4 $22) 42. gxf4+ Kf5 43. Ke3 {etc.}) 36. Bf1 h4 37. Rxa7 h3 38. Rd7 Ra1 39. g4 (39. a7 $142 Kg7 40. g4 Nh4 41. f4 Nf3+ 42. Kf2 Nxh2 43. Bxh3 $18) 39... Nh4 40. Rd3 Ng2 41. Rxh3 Nf4 42. Rf3 g5 43. Rb3 {Black resigned although White still needed to work a while to convert his material advantage.} 1-0

