{[%evp 0,77,26,26,26,-11,-11,-9,16,11,11,11,11,2,20,-3,7,7,7,5,5,16,16,16,16,13,19,7,29,25,25,9,6,9,0,0,0,0,27,22,29,0,31,15,27,-15,-15,-15,-15,-56,-64,-69,-71,-56,-57,-57,-79,-63,-63,-82,-53,-87,-76,-106,-106,-106,-117,-138,-138,-681,-630,-887,-919,-919,-883,-868,-872,-894,-913,-913]} 1. d4 ({At the very beginning a disclaimer - some of the games, quoted in the notes, in fact went} 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. d4 d5 4. Nc3 a6 {Such transpositions are altogether typical for closed games.}) 1... d5 ({Also Black can toy with the move order. In the tournament Carlsen already played the aforementioned slightly different version of our line with} 1... Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 a6 {After} 5. c5 (5. cxd5 {is the main move - see below.}) ({We'll use this chance to check some lesser alternatives here.} 5. Bg5 {might in fact be quite risky due to} dxc4 6. a4 (6. e4?! b5 7. e5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Nxg5 hxg5 10. Bxg5 Nbd7 {is similar to the Botvinnik System of the Semi-Slav, but here Black has played ...a6 instead of ...c6, which is in his favour:} 11. exf6 (11. Qf3 Rb8 12. exf6 (12. Ne4 Nxe4 13. Bxd8 Bb7 14. Bxc7 Bb4+ 15. Ke2 Rc8 $19) 12... Bb7 13. Qe3 Nxf6 14. d5? (14. h4 Be7 $15) 14... Nxd5 15. Qd4 Qxg5 16. Qxh8 Nb4 $19 {, Izzat,K (2487)-Illingworth,M (2494) Cammeray 2018}) (11. Be2 Bb7 12. Bf3 {also runs into tactics} Nxe5! 13. Bxb7 (13. dxe5 Qxd1+ 14. Rxd1 Bxf3 $17) 13... Nd3+ 14. Kf1 Bh6!? 15. Bxf6 Qxf6 16. Ne4 Qxd4 17. Bxa8 O-O (17... f5 $142 !? 18. Qh5+ Kd7 19. Qf7+ Kd8 20. Qxe6 fxe4 21. Qd5+ Qxd5 22. Bxd5 Rf8 23. f3 e3 $36) 18. Qf3 Qxb2 (18... Rxa8!?) 19. Nf6+ Kg7 20. Nh5+ Kg6 21. Rd1 Rxa8 22. g4 Qd4 23. h4 Rh8 $44 {Vostrotin,V (2390)-Glazman,M (2547) email 2006}) 11... Bb7 {[%csl Gb7][%cal Ga8h1] gives the b7 the beautiful long  - White is struggling for equality here:} 12. Be2 (12. d5?! Nc5 13. dxe6 (13. Be2 Bxd5 14. Nxd5 Qxd5 15. Qxd5 exd5 16. O-O-O O-O-O 17. Be3 Bh6 18. f4 Ne4 19. g4 d4 20. Bxd4 Bxf4+ 21. Kc2 c5 22. Bc3 Rxd1 23. Bxd1 Nf2 $19 {Gunina,V (2427)-Grachev,B (2619) Moscow rpd 2021}) 13... Nxe6 14. Qxd8+ Rxd8 15. Be3 (15. Bd2 Rxd2 (15... Bc5!? $17) (15... Bh6 $17) 16. Kxd2 Bh6+ 17. Ke1 Nd4 18. Rd1 c5 19. f3 Be3 $44 {Chakravarthi,R (2415)-Kapnisis,S (2464) Anogia 2018}) 15... Nd4 16. O-O-O Nf5 17. Bd2 Bh6 $36 18. f4? Rxd2! 19. Re1+ Kf8 20. Kxd2 Bxf4+ 21. Kc2 Ne3+ 22. Kb1 b4 23. Rxe3 Bxe3 $19 {Kornev,A (2507)-Frolyanov,D (2616) Moscow 2011}) 12... b4 13. Na4 Bxg2 14. Rg1 Bd5 15. Rc1 Nxf6 16. Bxc4 Rg8 17. Qb3 Qd7 18. Bxd5 (18. h4 $142 $15) 18... Qxd5 19. Qxd5 Nxd5 20. Ke2 Rh8 21. Rh1 f6 22. Be3 Rh4 23. Rcg1 Kf7 $17 {[%csl Rd4,Rf2,Rh2] Hebden,M (2460)-Xu,Y (2518) Reykjavik 2017/ Black had a more compact pawn-structure and duly converted his advantage.}) 6... Nc6 7. e3 Na5 8. Ne5 c5 ({Deserving attention is} 8... Bb4 {} 9. Nxc4 Qd5! 10. Bxf6 Nxc4 11. Qb3 Nxe3 12. Qxb4 Nc2+ 13. Kd2 Nxb4 14. Nxd5 Nxd5 15. Bxg7 Rg8 $15) 9. Nxc4 cxd4 10. Qxd4 Qxd4 11. exd4 Nxc4 12. Bxc4 Be7 13. Bf4 Bd7 14. d5 {quickly led to a sterile endgame after} Rc8 15. dxe6 Rxc4 16. exd7+ Kxd7 17. O-O-O+ Kc6 18. Be5 Rd8 19. Rxd8 Bxd8 20. b3 Rc5 21. Bd4 Rf5 22. f3 Bb6 $11 {Topalov,V (2740)-Ding,L (2805) Abidjan blitz 2019}) (5. e3 {avoids playing a real gambit, but locking in the c1 is too soft and can't promise White an advantage:} c5 ({Fully playable is also} 5... dxc4 6. Bxc4 b5 {, followed by b7 - Black gets a good version of the QG Accepted.}) 6. cxd5 exd5 7. g3 (7. Be2 Nc6 8. O-O Bd6 9. dxc5 Bxc5 $132 {- D32 is a fairly well-known theoretical position - a standard tabia, in which the isolani promises Black typical piece counterplay.}) 7... Nc6 8. Bg2 c4!? 9. Ne5 Bb4 10. Bd2 O-O 11. O-O Re8 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. b3 Bxc3 14. Bxc3 Ne4 15. Rc1 a5 16. bxc4 Ba6 17. f3 Nxc3 18. Rxc3 Bxc4 19. Rf2 Qg5 20. Qc1 h5 $132 {Carlsen,M (2862)-Dubov,D (2702) chess24.com Airthings rpd 2020}) 5... b6 6. cxb6 cxb6 7. Bf4 Bd6 8. Bg3 Bb7 9. e3 Nc6 10. Bd3 O-O 11. Rc1 Rc8 12. Bh4 Be7 13. O-O Ne4 14. Bxe7 Nxe7 15. Ne2 Rxc1 16. Qxc1 Qd6 17. Qb1 Rc8 18. a3 a5 19. Ng3 Nf6 20. Ne5 g6 21. Rc1 Rc7 22. Rc2 Nd7 23. f4 Nf6 $11 {Radjabov,T (2765)-Carlsen,M (2847) chess24.com rpd 2021/ White had only a symbolic plus, which quicky evaporated. However, Magnus against Hikaru had already lost a Nimzo-Indian with 4.c2, so he chooses a different move order.}) 2. c4 e6 ({Even in rapid games, choosing the best opening for the given situation is great alchemy. Despite his success in game 4, Carlsen (understandably) refrains from} 2... c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 Bg4 5. h3 Bxf3 6. Qxf3 e6 7. Bd3 Nbd7 8. O-O Bd6 9. Nc3 O-O 10. Rd1 Re8 11. Qe2 Qe7 12. Bd2 dxc4 13. Bxc4 e5 14. dxe5 Qxe5 15. g3 b5 16. Bb3 a5 17. Bc2 Qe6 18. Kg2 a4 19. a3 Ne5 20. Bb1 Qe7 21. Be1 Rad8 22. Ba2 Ned7 23. Qc2 Be5 24. Rac1 Nc5 25. Ne2 Rxd1 26. Rxd1 $16 {Nakamura,H (2736)-Carlsen,M (2847) chess24.com rpd 2021}) 3. Nc3 (3. Nf3 a6 {is another possible modification of our line. After} 4. c5 ({As indicated,} 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nc3 {is the main line, with which we will deal later on.}) {More enterprising is} 4... b6 ({Black has a choice:} 4... Nc6 5. Bf4 b6!? (5... Nge7?! 6. Nc3 Ng6 7. Be3! b6 8. cxb6 cxb6 9. h4! Bd6 10. h5 Nge7 11. h6 g6 12. Bg5 O-O 13. Bf6 b5 14. e3 Bd7 15. Bd3 Rc8 16. a4 b4 17. Ne2 Qb6 18. Nc1 Rc7 19. Nb3 $16 {Alekhine,A-Rubinstein,A The Hague 1921 This historical game is in the Megabase with Alekhine's notes.}) 6. cxb6 cxb6 7. e3 Bd6 8. Nbd2 Nge7 9. Rc1 Bb7 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. Bd3 Rc8 12. a3 h6 13. O-O O-O 14. Qe2 a5 15. Ba6 Bxa6 16. Qxa6 Ra8 17. Qd3 Rfc8 18. Rc3 Nd8 19. Rfc1 Rxc3 20. Rxc3 Rc8 21. Nb1 Nb7 22. h4 Rxc3 23. Nxc3 Qc7 24. g3 Nd6 $11 {Mamedyarov,S (2764)-Sanal,V (2581) Playchess.com rpd 2020}) 5. cxb6 c5!? (5... cxb6 6. Bf4 {leads to a similar position, as in the previous note.}) 6. Nc3 (6. Qb3 Bb7 7. Nc3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 {Studer,N (2494)-Horvath,D (2419) Bad Ragaz 2019} Nd7! 9. Na4 Ngf6 {[%cal Yf6e4]} 10. Bg5 Nc5 11. Nxc5 Bxc5 $11 {/}) 6... Nd7 (6... Qxb6?! 7. Na4 $16) 7. Na4 (7. e4 dxe4 8. Nxe4 Bb7 9. Bd3 Qxb6 10. dxc5 Nxc5 11. Nxc5 Bxc5 12. O-O Nf6 13. b4 {Paravyan,D (2653)-Fedoseev,V (2678) Lichess.org blitz 2020} Bd6 14. Be3 Qd8 $11) 7... cxd4 (7... c4? 8. Bd2 $16 {Bogoljubow,E-Alekhine,A WCh Germany 1934}) 8. Qxd4 {Sapunov,E (2461)-Lavrik,D (2425) Chess.com blitz 2020} Ne7 9. e4 Nc6 10. Qe3 Nb4 $11 {Nimzowitsch}) 3... a6!? {Introduced into practice around 1899 by Dawid Janowski. However, his results with his own brainchild were far from impressive, moreover his ideas might have been unjustly underestimated due to his subsequent heavy losses in two matches against Lasker. In any case, when I started playing chess in the 1970s, this was just a mere sideline, considered inferior and dubious. Instead of proceeding with his development, Black makes an inconspicuous pawn move - how can this be good? Fast forward to the present and we see elite GMs, even the current World Champion playing the line quite regularly. Janowski is possibly smiling somewhere on the cloud :), for us it remains to figure out: "What is different?" I will try to explain my take. Firstly, chess in general has changed substantially and become far more concrete, than even in the relatively not so distant past. In the Orthodox QG Declined, one of White's main methods of fighting for the initiative are lines with the exchange on d5.} ({We have dozens of thousands of games with} 3... Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Be7 (5... c6 6. e3 Nbd7 {is similar}) ({However a valid question is - can't Black play} 5... Be6 6. e3 Nbd7 {, saving a tempo on a6 and developing in the same manner, as in the 5.f3 line (note to White's 5th move)? The difference is here the  is still on g1 and the plan} 7. Bd3 Bd6 8. Nge2 {is pretty dangerous:} O-O 9. Nf4 c5 10. dxc5 Nxc5 11. O-O h6 12. Bh4 Rc8 13. Rc1 Be5 14. Bb1 Bxf4 15. exf4 d4 16. Nb5 Qb6 17. Nxd4 Rfd8 18. Bxf6 gxf6 19. Qc2 Rxd4 20. Qh7+ Kf8 21. f5 Bd7 22. Rfe1 $18 {Dragnev,V (2485)-Repka,C (2534) Le Castella 2018})) ({or} 3... Be7 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bf4 c6 6. e3 Bf5 7. g4 {1-0 Riumin,N-Kan,I Moscow International-03 1936 (8) In both cases White, who voluntarily enters the so-called Carlsbad structure, can fight for an opening edge, which is also born out by statistics - he scores around 60% here. Kasparov himself, who liked swapping on d5, wrote that the Carlsbad promises White a positional initiative. Is it really so and can't Black do something against it, perhaps by deploying his pieces more actively, than in the aforementioned lines? The cunning 3...a6!? is both noncomittal and potentially useful (is a parallel with the Najdorf completely out of place?). Moreover, contrary to the more standard moves 3...f6 and 3...e7 it practically forces White to play cxd5 if he wants to put Black under any pressure. That said and done, the defence must still be prepared for White's main strategic plans in the Carlsbad. Against the queenside minority attack (b4, a4, b5), Black's 3rd move obviously slows down White's play. Countering possible central expansion (f3, e4, connected with the Nge2 development), or outright kingside agression (f4, g4 - this is a valid plan not only in connection with 0-0-0, as we see in the historical game quoted below) is more difficult. Black will try to achieve this mainly by developing his f8 more actively to d6 and leaving the e-file open. In the following notes we will also see other, less usual ideas - a timely c5, developing the b8 to c6, deploying the g8 to e7 to fight for the b1-h7 diagonal with a timely f5 etc. Conversely White will try to create positons/situations, in which... a6 turns out to be just a waste of time. Is it really possible? - the jury is still out on this one...}) 4. cxd5 {As we have already said above, this is not only almost forced, but also the most consistent move - striving to exploit 3...a6!?, White opens up the position. The alternatives are less testing:} (4. c5 e5!? 5. dxe5 (5. e3 e4 (5... Nc6 $142 6. a3 exd4 7. exd4 g6!? $132 {[%csl Rd4][%cal Yf8g7,Yg8e7,Ye7f5] /=}) 6. b4? (6. f3! $36) 6... Nf6 7. Nge2 h5 8. h4 g6 9. Nf4 Bh6 10. Qb3 c6 11. a4 O-O 12. Ra2 Bg4 $17 13. Be2 Nbd7 14. Bxg4 Nxg4 15. f3 Bxf4 16. exf4 exf3 17. gxf3 Re8+ 18. Re2 Nh6 19. Rxe8+ Qxe8+ 20. Kf2 Nf5 21. Ne2 Qxe2+ {0-1, Belov,V (2598)-Grachev,B (2614) Moscow rpd 2019}) 5... d4 6. Na4 (6. Ne4 Qd5 7. Ng3 {Vecsey,Z-Treybal,K Prague 1931} Nc6 $36) 6... Nc6 7. Nf3 Bg4 8. Qb3 Qd7 9. Bd2 O-O-O 10. O-O-O Nge7 $13 11. Ng5?! {Jobava,B (2603)-Grachev,B (2612) Chess.com blitz 2020} Nxe5 $17) (4. e4 dxe4 5. Nxe4 Bb4+ 6. Bd2?! (6. Nc3 c5 7. a3 Ba5 $132) 6... Qxd4 {and again the difference between c6 (Slav gambit) and a6 is in Black's favour:} 7. Bxb4 Qxe4+ 8. Be2 Nc6 $17 {with a healthy extra pawn.}) ({After} 4. e3 Nf6 {White will likely continue} 5. Nf3 {, transposing into a position we have already mentioned above in the note to 1...d5.}) 4... exd5 5. Bf4 {White doesn't commit his g1 and plays a straighforward developing move.} ({The most popular move in this position is} 5. Nf3 {Natural development, knights before bishops - already Lasker said that, right? However, as with many such rules, the exceptions are numerous and here Black seems to hold his own:} {Not surprisingly, Black's main reaction is to develop a piece -} Nf6 ({Carlsen at least 3 times played the rather provocative} 5... h6 {, Black equalised after} 6. Bf4 Nf6 7. e3 Bd6 8. Bxd6 Qxd6 9. Qc2 (9. Qb3!? O-O 10. Bd3 {might be more promising}) 9... O-O 10. Bd3 Re8 11. h3 Be6 12. O-O Nbd7 13. a4 Rac8 14. Rfc1 c5 15. dxc5 Nxc5 16. Nd4 Bd7 17. Qd1 Rc7 18. Bc2 Rec8 19. Nde2 Ne6 20. Bb3 Bc6 21. Nd4 Qb4 22. Nce2 Bd7 23. Rxc7 Rxc7 24. Qe1 Qxe1+ 25. Rxe1 Nc5 26. Rc1 a5 $11 {Shankland,S (2725)-Carlsen,M (2835) Wijk aan Zee 2019/ For more details see the notes to this game in CBM 188 by D.Fernandez.}) (5... c6 6. Bf4 ({The energetic} 6. e4 dxe4 7. Ng5 Be7 8. Bc4 {is not too dangerous. With some engine preparation Black may dare to accept the sacrifice:} Bxg5!? (8... Nh6 9. Ngxe4 Nf5 10. d5 O-O 11. Bf4 cxd5 12. Nxd5 Nc6 13. O-O Be6 14. Re1 Bxd5!? 15. Bxd5 Bb4 16. Nc3 Nfd4 17. Be4 Re8 18. Re3 g6 19. h4 Ne6 20. Qxd8 Raxd8 21. Bxc6 bxc6 $11 {Mamedyarov,S (2772)-Vallejo Pons,F (2710) Moscow 2017/led only to simplification and equality}) 9. Qh5 Be7 10. Qxf7+ Kd7 11. Na4 (11. Be6+ Kc7 12. Bf4+ Kb6 13. d5 Bxe6 14. Qxe6 Bg5 15. dxc6 Nxc6 16. Nd5+ Ka7 17. Bc7 Qf8 18. Bb6+ Kb8 19. Bc7+ Ka7 20. Bb6+ Kb8 21. Bc7+ Ka7 {½, Korobov,A (2679)-Mamedov,R (2645) Douglas 2019}) 11... Qa5+ 12. Bd2 Qxa4 13. b3 Qa3 14. Qxg7 Nf6 15. Qxh8 Qd6 16. g3 b5 17. Bf4 Qb4+ 18. Bd2 Qd6 19. Bf4 Qb4+ 20. Bd2 {½, Anton Guijarro,D (2688)-Vallejo Pons,F (2710) Linares 2020}) 6... Bd6 7. Bxd6 Qxd6 8. e4!? (8. e3 Ne7 ({or} 8... Nf6!? {is roughly equal})) {is more direct, but} 8... dxe4 (8... Ne7 9. e5 $14) 9. Nxe4 Qe7 10. Qe2 Nh6! (10... Be6 11. Nc5 Nf6 12. Nxe6 $14) 11. Nd6+ Kd8 12. Nxc8 Qb4+ 13. Qd2 Re8+ 14. Ne5 Qxd2+ 15. Kxd2 Kxc8 16. Bc4 (16. Re1 Kc7 17. Bd3 Nd7 18. Nxd7 {Kienel,N (2162)-Amann,H (2411) email 2017} Kxd7 19. Re3 Rxe3 20. fxe3 g6 $14 {/= is pleasant for White, but still far from a tangible advantage.}) 16... f6 17. Nf7 Nxf7 18. Bxf7 Rd8! 19. Rae1 Rxd4+ 20. Kc3 c5 21. Re8+ Rd8 22. Rxd8+ Kxd8 23. Re1 b5 24. Re8+ Kc7 25. Rh8 Ra7 26. Rxh7 Kd6 27. Rxg7 Nc6 28. Rh7 Re7 $132 {Navara,D (2742)-Hammer,J (2651) Novy Bor 2016/ gave Black sufficient play to hold the endgame.}) 6. Bg5 ({Soft is} 6. Bf4 Bd6 7. Bg3 ({Totally harmless is} 7. Bxd6 Qxd6 8. e3 Bf5 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Nbd7 11. O-O O-O 12. Qc2 Rfe8 $11 {½, Rausis,I (2651)-Jurcik,M (2451) Czech Republic 2019}) 7... O-O 8. e3 (8. Qc2 Nc6! 9. a3 Ne7 $11 {and the  gets to f5 anyway.}) 8... Bf5 9. Qb3 Bxg3 10. hxg3 Nc6 11. Nh4 (11. Be2 Qd6 12. O-O Ne7 13. Ne5 h5 14. Rfc1 Ng4 15. Na4 b6 16. Qc3 Rfc8 17. Nd3 Bd7 18. b3 Ng6 19. Qb4 Qf6 20. Nc3 Qf5 21. a4 a5 22. Qa3 Nf6 23. Ra2?! (23. Nb5! $13) 23... Qg5 24. Ne1 c5 25. Nf3 Qh6 26. dxc5 Rxc5 27. Nb5 Rac8 28. Rxc5 Rxc5 29. b4 axb4 30. Qxb4 Ne4 31. Bf1 h4 $40 {Karjakin,S (2783)-Adams,M (2761) Shamkir 2017 For more details see the notes to this game in CBM 178 by Quintiliano.}) 11... Bg4 12. Bd3 Re8 13. O-O Rb8 14. Rac1 Ne7 15. Qc2 c6 16. Rfe1 Qd6 17. a3 Ng6 18. Nf3 Ne7 19. Ne5 g6 20. Nxg4 Nxg4 21. Be2 Nf6 $11 {Anton Guijarro,D (2650)-Adams,M (2751) Caleta 2017}) 6... Be6 {and now:} {Straightforward development} 7. e3 (7. Qb3?! Nbd7! 8. Qxb7 Rb8 9. Qxa6 Rxb2 {is playing with fire:} 10. a3 (10. Bc1 Rb6 11. Qd3 Bb4 12. Bd2 O-O (12... Qa8 13. e3? (13. g3 Ne4 14. Bg2 $13) 13... Ne4 14. Be2 Bf5 15. Nh4 Nxd2 16. Qxd2 Rc6 17. Rc1 Rxc3! $19 {Nesterov,A (2482)-Meskovs,N (2558) St Petersburg 2019} {} 18. Rxc3 Qa5) 13. e3 (13. g3 g6! $40 {[%cal Re6f5] White can hardly solve his problems with development here.}) 13... Qa8 $142 ! (13... g6?! 14. Qc2 {is unconvincing.}) (13... c5 14. Na4 {Pogromsky,M (2411)-Putilov,V (2181) Nizhnij Tagil 2012} (14. Be2!?) 14... Ne4!? 15. Nxb6 Nxd2 16. Nxd2 Nxb6 17. Rb1 Ba5 18. Qb5 Nc4 19. Bxc4 dxc4 20. Qxc5 Qa8 21. d5 Bf5 $44 22. O-O! Bxd2 23. Qxc4 $15) 14. Be2 g6 $36) (10. Rc1? Bb4 11. Bd2 Qb8 12. Qd3 O-O 13. g3 Ne4 14. Bg2 Bg4 $19 {Megalios,K (2416)-Halkias,S (2552) Skopje 2019}) 10... Be7 (10... Bd6 11. e4 dxe4 12. Nxe4 O-O $32 {Black with his lead in development almost always has great compensation...}) 11. g3 O-O (11... Rb3!?) 12. Bg2 Ne4 (12... Rb3 13. Qa5 c5 $44) 13. Bxe7 Qxe7 14. Nd1 Rb3 15. Qc6 Rxa3 16. Rxa3 Qxa3 17. O-O Rc8 18. Ne3 Nc3 19. Re1 h6 20. g4 Qb2 21. Kf1 $11 {Chatalbashev,B (2531)-Lobron,E (2512) Germany 2019/ White can be glad he survived the opening without serious damage.}) ({Voluntarily swapping the  with} 7. Bxf6 {is not too strategically desirable, but in connection with} Qxf6 8. Qb3 {it's another direct attempt to refute Black's setup:} {All this led Black to} Ra7 {A motif from various lines of the Slav - Black avoids weakening his queenside and hopes he will be able coordinate his forces and reanimate the a7 in time:} ({Black's initial reaction was to parry the xb7 threat with} 8... b5? {, but this runs into the surprising computer-fuelled (the engines show the right way very quickly) refutation} 9. a4! b4 (9... Nc6 10. axb5 Nxd4 11. Nxd4 Qxd4 12. bxa6 $16) 10. Nxd5 Qd8 11. e4 c6 12. Qe3! (12. Bc4?! cxd5 13. exd5 Bc8 14. d6 Bxd6 15. Bxf7+ Kf8 $15 {and in comparison with the main line, White has only vague compensation for the piece.}) 12... cxd5 13. exd5 Qxd5 14. Rc1 {[%cal Yc1c8,Rf1c4,Rd4d5,Yf1d3,Yd3e4] Inserting 9.a4! was crucial, otherwise Black would have b4+ at this point. It's amazing how it all fits - the main threat is c4 and d5, but in connection with ideas such as c8 and d3-e4 Black is almost helpless despite his extra :} {The most resilient is} Be7 $142 (14... Qd8 15. d5! Qxd5 16. Bc4 Qd6 17. O-O Nd7 (17... Be7 18. Rfd1 Qxd1+ 19. Rxd1 Bxc4 20. Qe4 $18 {[%cal Re4c4,Re4a8]} (20. Nd4!? $18 {[%cal Rd4f5]})) 18. Ng5 $40 {[%csl Re8] and Black's  can't survive, e.g.} Be7 19. Bxe6 fxe6 20. Qf3! {[%cal Rf3f7,Rf3a8]} Bxg5 21. Qxa8+ Bd8 22. Rfd1 $18) (14... Kd8 {also doesn't help -} 15. Bd3 {[%cal Rd3e4]} Ra7 (15... Bf5 16. Qg5+ $18) (15... f5 16. Ng5 Kd7 17. O-O $32 { White is attacking with all his pieces against a , trapped in the centre - the game is decided.}) 16. Be4 Qb3 (16... Qd7 17. d5 $18) 17. Qg5+ Re7 (17... f6 18. Qa5+ Ke8 19. Qb6 $18 Re7 20. Qxb8+ Kf7 21. O-O Qxa4 22. Bc6 Qa2 23. Rfe1 Rg8 24. d5 {1-0, Checa,N (2495)-Korley,K (2453) Charlotte 2018/ This game seems to be the premiere of the piece sac idea.}) 18. Qe5 Rd7 19. Qxb8+ Ke7 20. O-O g6 21. d5 Bg7 22. Qf4 Bxd5 23. Bxd5 Qxd5 24. Rfe1+ Kf8 25. Qxb4+ {1-0, Suleymanli,A (2429)-Rozum,I (2593) Budva 2019}) 15. Bd3 Qd7 (15... Ra7 16. Be4 (16. Bc4 Qxc4 17. Rxc4 Bxc4 18. b3 Bxb3 (18... Bd5 19. Qe5 {[%cal Re5d5,Re5b8]}) 19. Qxb3 O-O $16) 16... Qb3 (16... Qd7 17. d5 Bxd5 18. Ne5! $18) (16... Qd8 17. d5 Bxd5 18. Bxd5 Rd7 (18... Rc7 19. Bxf7+ Kxf7 20. Qf4+ $18) 19. Bxf7+ Kxf7 20. Ne5+ Ke8 21. Nxd7 Nxd7 22. O-O Rf8 23. Rfe1 Rf7 24. Rc6 Kf8 {Golecki,J (2172)-Grochol,K (2030) Szklarska Poreba 2020} 25. Qc1 Nc5 26. Rxc5 Bxc5 27. Qxc5+ $18) 17. Rc8+ Bd8 18. Rxb8 $16) (15... Qd8 16. Be4 Ra7 17. d5 $18 {-15...a7}) (1