e4 dxe4 14. Nxe4 Qf4+ 15. Kb1 O-O $132) 10... Bf5 11. Bxf5 Nxf5 12. O-O-O Nd7 13. e4 Ng7!? $14 (13... dxe4 14. Nxe4 {shows the difference between f3 and ge2, White has the initiative after} Qe7 15. d5 $36)) 10. f3 Bh5 11. Nge2 Nd7 12. O-O-O {and now:} {The immediate} Bg6!? {also deserves attention, after} (12... Qh6 13. Nf4 Bg6 14. Bxg6 ({Helps Black,} 14. Qf2!? {seems more dangerous}) 14... Nxg6 15. Nxg6 hxg6 16. Kb1 Nb6 17. e4 O-O-O 18. Qb3 Nc4 19. Ka1 Qg5 20. g3 Kb8 21. f4 Qg4 22. Na4 Ka7 $13 {/, Dragun,K (2594)-Rakhmanov,A (2650) Moscow 2016}) 13. e4 dxe4 14. fxe4 {Zhao,X (2508)-Batsiashvili,N (2480) Germany 2017} {Black can play} c5!? 15. e5 Bxd3 16. exd6 (16. Qxd3 Qh6+) 16... Bxc2 17. Kxc2 Nf5 $13) 6. e3 Bd6 7. Bxd6 {This time it's White, who makes a logical move. However, it doesn't cause Black too many problems, so we should check the alternatives more closely:} ({After} 7. Bg3 O-O 8. Bd3 Re8 9. Qc2 {Black got counterplay with the direct} Nc6!? {[%cal Rc6d4,Rc6b4]} (9... Bg4?! 10. Bh4! h6 11. h3 $36) (9... h6!? 10. Rc1?! {Bryzgalin,K (2449)-Liyanage,R (2181) Chess.com blitz 2020} Bxg3 11. hxg3 c5! $36) 10. Be2 Nb4 11. Qb3 c5 12. Bxd6 Qxd6 13. dxc5 Qxc5 14. Nf3 Be6 15. Nd4 Nc6 16. Rd1 Nxd4 17. Rxd4 Ne4 18. Bf3 Rac8 19. O-O Nxc3 20. bxc3 b5 21. Rc1 Red8 22. Qd1 Rd7 23. h4 Qa3 $11 {/, Nakamura,H (2736)-Le,Q (2709) chess24.com rpd 2021}) ({The critical response is} 7. Bg5 $142 ! {- White avoids the swap even at the cost of a tempo. After} Be6 8. Bd3 {Black must tread with care:} (8. Bxf6 Qxf6 9. Qb3 Ra7 10. Nf3 {can't be too dangerous for Black - he is a tempo up on one of the 5.f3 f6 6.g5 e6 7.xf6 xf6 8.b3 a7 lines, analysed above. After} O-O 11. h4 Nd7 12. Qc2 c6 13. Bd3 {Andreikin,D (2725)-Grandelius,N (2663) Lichess.org blitz 2021} h6 14. Ng5 Rc8 $132 ({or} 14... Qe7 $13 {Black is fine})) {The most often played move is} 8... Nbd7 {and now:} (8... O-O 9. Nge2 h6 10. Bf4 (10. Bh4!? $14 {is more natural and ambitious}) 10... c5!? 11. Bxd6 Qxd6 12. dxc5 Qxc5 13. O-O Nc6 14. Rc1 Qd6 15. Bb1 Rad8 16. Qc2 d4 17. Rcd1 Qe5 18. exd4 Nxd4 19. Nxd4 Rxd4 20. Rfe1 Qc5 21. Rxd4 Qxd4 22. Rd1 Qc5 23. h3 b5 $11 {Holt,C (2549)-Tari,A (2639) Chess.com blitz 2021}) (8... h6 9. Bh4 g5!? 10. Bg3 h5 11. Bxd6 Qxd6 12. h4 gxh4! 13. Nf3 h3 14. gxh3 Nbd7 15. Qc2 Rg8 16. O-O-O O-O-O 17. Kb1 Nb6 18. Ne2 Kb8 19. Nf4 Nbd7 20. Rhg1 Rc8 21. Rxg8 Rxg8 22. Rc1 Rc8 23. Qd1 c5 $14 {/=, Dragnev,V (2508)-Jurcik,M (2451) Batumi 2018/ With careful play Black held a slightly inferior position, but his whole concept is not without risk.}) {Even more energetic is} 9. f4!? {[%csl Re6]} ({We won't analyse} 9. Nf3 {, as here Black is a tempo up on the fully satisfactory 5.f3 lines (White's  went to g5 in 2 moves). Instead we will check 2 stronger moves.}) (9. Nge2 h6 10. Bh4 (10. Bf4 Qe7 11. O-O O-O 12. Qb3 c5 13. Bxd6 Qxd6 14. dxc5 Nxc5 15. Qa3 Rfd8 16. Rfd1 Ng4 17. Ng3 h5 18. Be2 h4 19. Bxg4 hxg3 20. hxg3 Bxg4 21. Rxd5 Qxd5 22. Nxd5 Rxd5 23. e4 Re5 24. f3 Bd7 {Holt,C (2537)-Gordievsky,D (2607) Chess.com blitz 2017} 25. f4 Rxe4!? 26. Qxc5 Re2 $44 {}) 10... Qe7 ({After} 10... O-O {Black will have to think twice about g5 and White can play like Woktaszek in the game below.}) 11. h3!? ({The kingside expansion requires patience, Black got an excellent position after} 11. f3 c5 12. O-O O-O 13. Kh1 Rfe8 14. f4 Nb6 15. Ng3 cxd4 16. exd4 Qd8 17. Qf3 Be7 18. f5 Bd7 19. Rae1 Bc6 20. Re2 Qd6 21. Rfe1 Bf8 22. Nh5 Nxh5 23. Qxh5 Rxe2 24. Qxe2 Re8 $15 {Suleymanli,A (2520)-Niemann,H (2508) Niksic 2021}) 11... g5!? ({After} 11... c6 12. Qc2 Rc8 13. O-O Nb6 14. Rae1 O-O {White is fuly prepared for} 15. f4! $36 c5 16. Ng3 Qd8 17. Nh5 Be7 18. Bxf6 Bxf6 19. Nxf6+ Qxf6 20. dxc5 Rxc5 21. Qf2 Rcc8 22. e4! dxe4 23. Nxe4 Qd8 24. Rd1 Bd5 25. Bb1 Qc7 26. Nf6+! gxf6 27. Qg3+ Kh8 28. Qh4 Rfe8 29. Qxh6+ Kg8 30. Bh7+ Kh8 31. Bf5+ Kg8 32. Rd3 Qc5+ 33. Kh2 Re3 34. b4 {1-0, Wojtaszek,R (2737)-Lupulescu,C (2620) Germany 2018/ A model attack by White!}) ({Black can also postpone the sortie with} 11... Nb6 12. Qc2 g5 13. Bg3 Bxg3 14. Nxg3 O-O-O $132) 12. Bg3 Nh5 (12... Bxg3 $142 ! 13. Nxg3 O-O-O $13) 13. Bxd6 Qxd6 14. Qc2 O-O-O 15. O-O-O?! (15. b4!? $36 {is more aggressive, White's attacking chances seem more realistic.}) 15... Kb8 16. Kb1 f5!? $11 {Levin,E (2543)-Usmanov,V (2464) St Petersburg 2018}) 9... h6 (9... Nb6?! 10. Nf3 c5 11. O-O O-O 12. Ne5 c4 13. Bc2 Be7 14. Qf3 Ne8 15. Bxe7 Qxe7 16. f5 Bd7 17. Nxd5 $18 {Xu,Y (2554)-Kiolbasa,O (2255) Tornelo INT 2021}) 10. Bh4 g5 (10... Nb6 11. Nf3 Bg4 12. h3 Bxf3 13. Qxf3 Be7 {gets rid off the e6, but White still has a pull after} 14. O-O-O $36 {Afanasiev,V (2263)-Jurcik,M (2488) Riga 2019}) 11. fxg5 hxg5 ({Other moves are no better:} 11... Nh7 12. Qc2 hxg5 13. Bg3 Ndf8 14. e4 $36) (11... Ng4 12. Nf3 Be7 13. Qe2 hxg5 14. Bg3 Nh6 15. O-O-O c6 16. e4 dxe4 17. Bxe4 $40 {Xu,Y (2562)-Batsiashvili,N (2433) Changsha 2019}) 12. Bxg5 Rxh2 (12... Bxh2 13. Nf3 (13. Nce2 {or}) (13. Nh3!? $36 {promise White more}) 13... Bg3+ 14. Kd2 Rg8 15. Qb3 (15. Bh7 Rg7 $13 (15... Rf8 16. e4 dxe4 17. Nxe4 Bd6 18. d5 Bxd5 19. Nxd6+ cxd6 20. Qa4 Bc6 {Falkevall,A (2209)-Carlsson,L (2258) Helsingborg 2021} 21. Rae1+ Ne5 22. Qd4 Bxf3 23. gxf3 Qa5+ 24. Kc2 $18)) 15... Qe7 16. Qxb7 Rb8 17. Qxa6 Rxb2+ 18. Kc1 Qb4 19. Nd1 Rxg2 20. a3 Qd6 21. Qc8+ Ke7 22. Qxg8 Qc6+ 23. Kb1 Qb6+ {½, Palliser,R (2411)-Ris,R (2457) England 2019}) 13. Rxh2 Bxh2 14. Nge2 Bd6 ({Loses a tempo, but} 14... Qe7 15. g3 O-O-O 16. Kd2 {[%csl Rh2] cuts off the h2}) 15. Nf4 c5 (15... Bg4 {Xu,Y (2478)-Nie,X (2348) Shenyang 2019} 16. Be2! Bxf4 17. exf4 {} Qe7 18. Nxd5 Bxe2 19. Qd2! $18) 16. Nh5 cxd4 17. exd4 Qe7? (17... Bg4 18. Qxg4 Nxg4 19. Bxd8 Rxd8 20. Nxd5 $16 {gives White an endgame with an extra pawn. However, now the pin will cost Black even more dearly.}) 18. Kf1! O-O-O 19. Nxf6 Rh8 (19... Nxf6 20. Qf3) 20. Qc2 Kd8 21. Nfe4 f6 22. Nxd6 Qxd6 23. Be3 Qg3 24. Bg1 $19 {Yu,Y (2762)-Fedoseev,V (2707) Danzhou 2018/ White proceeded to convert his extra piece. To put it shortly - after 7.g5! Black hasn't yet been able to demonstrate full equality.}) ({Balck equalises after} 7. Qf3 Bxf4 ({Contrary to the previous note, White's  is not yet on d3 and Black's  may become the target of a bayonet attack after} 7... Bg4 8. Qg3 Bxf4 9. Qxf4 Bh5 10. Qe5+ Kf8 11. f3!? $14 {[%cal Yg2g4,Yh2h4]}) 8. Qxf4 Nbd7 (8... O-O?! 9. Bd3 c6 10. Nge2 Nbd7 11. g4 Re8 12. g5 Nh5 {Krush,I (2429)-Khademalsharieh,S (2494) Chess.com blitz 2020} 13. Bxh7+! Kxh7 14. Qxf7) 9. Bd3 (9. Qg5?! O-O 10. Nxd5 c5! $32 { } 11. Nxf6+ Nxf6 12. Qxc5 Ne4 13. Qb4 Qh4 14. g3 Nxg3! 15. fxg3 Qe4 $17) 9... Nf8!? 10. Nge2 Ne6 11. Qf3 O-O 12. h4 c5 13. Rd1 cxd4 14. exd4 Re8 15. O-O Nf8 (15... Qd6 $11) 16. Qg3 Nh5 17. Qg5 Nf6 18. Nf4 Ne6 19. Nxe6 Bxe6 20. Rfe1 Qb6 21. Qd2 Bg4 $11 {Krush,I (2429)-Khademalsharieh,S (2494) Chess.com blitz 2020}) 7... Qxd6 8. Bd3 Bg4 {[%mdl 32] With the risk of again repeating myself, I'll yet again point out Black in this line strives to effectively use, or at least exchange the c8. This is abundantly clear to an excellent strategist like Carlsen, thus the immediate sortie, which is rather risky.} ({Fully playable is the more standard} 8... O-O 9. Nge2 (9. Qc2 {can be only a transposition, but Black can also change the character of the position with} c5 10. dxc5 Qxc5 11. Nge2 Nc6 12. Rd1 Rd8 13. O-O Bg4 14. h3 Bh5 15. Qa4 {Gelfand,B (2737)-Nisipeanu,L (2672) Heraklion 2017} Ne5 $132) 9... Re8 (9... Bg4!?) 10. Qc2 (10. O-O Bg4 11. Qc2 Bxe2 12. Nxe2 c6 13. a3 Nbd7 {gives White nothing at all. Although the position is still about equal, Black's play is far easier, as White doesn't have a concrete plan - none of White's typical Carlsbad strategies is effective here. After} 14. h3 g6 15. Nc3 Nh5 16. Be2 Ng7 17. Rfe1 f5 18. Bf3 Re7 19. h4 Rae8 20. Ne2 h6 21. Rad1 g5 22. Ng3 Rf8 23. hxg5 hxg5 $36 {Puzanov,V (2283)-Kavc,A (2206) email 2017/ White was gradually overrun on the kingside.}) 10... Nc6 (10... b6!? 11. O-O c5 {is also interesting, the suspect} 12. Rfd1? (12. dxc5 $142 bxc5 $13) 12... c4 13. Bf5 Nc6 14. Bxc8 Raxc8 15. Qd2 b5 16. Ng3 h5 17. Qe2 g6 18. Qf3 Kg7 19. Nce2 h4 20. Nf1 b4 21. Rac1 a5 {Molina - Svane, Chess.com blic 2020.  Black gains space and a significant advantage.} 22. Nf4 a4 $17 {, Molina,R (2434)-Svane,R (2613) Chess.com blitz 2020/ Black had more space and an advantage on both flanks.}) 11. a3 Bg4 12. O-O Bh5 13. Rac1 Bg6 14. Bxg6 hxg6 15. Na4 Re7 16. Nc5 Nd8 17. h4 c6 18. Nf4 Nh5 19. Nxh5 gxh5 20. b4 Ne6 21. Qf5 g6 $11 {Tregubov,P (2592)-Nisipeanu,L (2674) Germany 2018}) 9. Qb3 Nc6 10. h3 (10. Qxb7 Rb8 11. Qxa6 Nb4 (11... O-O!?) 12. Qa4+ (12. Qxd6 Nxd3+ 13. Kd2 cxd6! 14. Kxd3 Bf5+ $36 {[%csl Rh1] and even the endgame is dangerous for White.}) 12... Ke7 13. Be2 Bxe2 14. Ngxe2 (14. Kxe2 Ra8 15. Qd1 Qa6+ 16. Kf3 Ne4 17. Nh3 Qf6+ $11 {is at least a perpetual.}) 14... Nd3+ 15. Kf1 Nxb2 16. Qc2 Nc4 $44 {[%csl Rf1,Rh1][%cal Yb8b2,Yf6g4] gives Black good compensation, Magnus possibly analysed something like this at home.}) ({A computer suggestion is} 10. Rc1!? {, for example} O-O (10... Bh5? {already allows} 11. Qxb7) 11. h3 Bh5 12. g4 Nb4 13. Be2 Bg6 14. Nf3 $14 {The  is aiming for e5 and here it's not so simple for Black to coordinate his forces.}) 10... Bh5 11. Nge2 $146 {A novelty.} ({Hikaru simply continues his development instead of} 11. g4 Bg6 12. O-O-O (12. Bxg6 hxg6 13. g5 Ne4 14. Qxd5 Qxd5 15. Nxd5 O-O-O $11 (15... Rd8!? $13 16. Nxc7+?! Ke7 {[%csl Rc7]})) 12... O-O-O 13. Bxg6 hxg6 14. Nge2 g5 15. Ng3 g6 16. Kb1 {The problems began only after} Rh7? ({Black could be satisfied after the circumspect} 16... Kb8 $11) 17. e4! dxe4 18. Ncxe4 Nxe4 19. Nxe4 Qf4 20. Rhe1 $36 Nxd4? 21. Rxd4! Rxd4 22. Nc5 b6 23. Re8+ Rd8 24. Qd5! {Yakubboev,N (2591)-Tristan,L (2535) Chess.com blitz 2020}) 11... Bxe2 12. Nxe2 O-O {The opening is almost over, the position resembles Puzanov-Kavc, quoted above. Once again Black is well placed to counter any typical Carlsbad plans. Moreover Nakamura will soon have to make a difficult decision - White can't remain passive, but where is he going to play?} 13. Rc1 ({Hikaru is not excited with the double-edged} 13. g4 $13 {, but with the text move he loses the option to play 0-0-0.}) 13... Nd8 {Black must parry the threat xb7, but this won't significantly disrupt the the coordination of his forces.} 14. Qa3 Qd7 {The first display of ambition.} (14... Qxa3 15. bxa3 Ne6 $11 {is fine, but Black wants to play actively on the kingside and for this he needs  on the board.}) 15. O-O g6 {Another sign Black wants to avoid simplification.} ({However, here} 15... Ne6?! {is objectively weaker due to} 16. Bf5 $14 {[%cal Yf5e6,Ye2f4]}) 16. Nf4 Re8 17. Bc2 c6 18. Nd3 Ne6 19. f4 {So White will play on the kingside. Or will he really, is this an active, or defensive move, aimed against the possible g5?} ({Nakamura probably considered} 19. Nc5 $11 {too simple and tame.}) ({However,} 19. b4 Ng5 20. Rfe1 {was less comittal, than the text-move.}) 19... Ng7 20. f5?! {Consistent, but too ambitious - this advance first and foremost weakens White's position.} ({After} 20. Ne5 Qc7 21. Qc3 $13 {the  heads for the , the e5 stands strong and the position is roughly and dynamically equal.}) 20... g5! (20... Nxf5 21. Nc5 Qc8 22. Bxf5 gxf5 23. Rc2 $44 {is maybe not better for White, but he has tangible compensation. Carlsen's reaction is both safer and objectively better.}) (20... Rxe3? 21. fxg6 $18) 21. Ne5 Qc7 22. h4? {A further and even more serious weakening.} (22. Qc3 {or}) (22. Rf3 {is met by} Re7 {Black prepares the swap, or direct elimination of the centralized  (xe5) and is slightly better, but the game is far from decided.}) 22... g4! {Taking the important f3-square from the .} 23. Qc3 (23. Rf4 {gives Black a pleasant choice between} h5 $17 ({and} 23... Rxe5!? $44 {/})) 23... Ngh5 {[%cal Rh5g3]} 24. Qe1 {Prevents g3, but now Black realises his main strategic motif.} ({After the desperate} 24. e4 Qb6! 25. Kh2 Rad8 {[%csl Re5] White's position is falling apart, for example} 26. Qe3 dxe4 27. Bb3 Rxe5! 28. Qg5+ Kf8 29. dxe5 Qc7 30. Rc5 Rd5! 31. Bxd5 Qxe5+ $19) 24... Rxe5! {[%mdl 544] A strong positional exchange sacrifice. Petrosian probably wouldn't hesitate long to chop off the opponent's most active piece, neither did Magnus...} 25. dxe5 Qxe5 $17 {[%csl Re3,Rf5,Rg3,Rh4] /-+ Black has a pawn for the exchange, but above all he controls all the important squares on the kingside. White has numerous weaknesses and no effective counterplay, so Magnus can gradually increase his pressure without any hurry or haste.} 26. Qc3 Qg3 27. Qe1 Qd6 28. Qf2 ({After} 28. e4 Re8 29. Qe3 {Black can among others play} Qg3!? (29... Ng3 $19) 30. Qxg3 Nxg3 31. Rfe1 Nfxe4 32. Bxe4 Nxe4 $19 {[%csl Rf5][%cal Yg8g7,Yg7f6,Ye8e5] Material is already equal, but the f5 is doomed - Black's position is technically winning.}) 28... Re8 29. Rcd1 Qe5 30. Rd4 c5 (30... Qxe3?! 31. Qxe3 Rxe3 32. Kf2 $17 {is less convincing, as the f5 remains protected.}) 31. Rd2 Ng3 32. Rfd1 Kf8! {Playing it nice and slow. Carlsen improves his position, protecting the e8 makes e4 a real threat.} ({Winning material immediately with} 32... Qxe3?! 33. Qxe3 Rxe3 34. Rxd5 Nxd5 35. Rxd5 $15) ({or} 32... Nfe4?! 33. Bxe4 Nxe4 34. Rxd5 Nxf2 35. Rxe5 Rxe5 36. Kxf2 $44 {would give White drawing chances. Even the engine approves of the text and already considers Black's win a matter of technique.}) 33. Rd3 ({More resilient was} 33. Qe1) 33... Nfe4 (33... Nge4!? 34. Qf4 Qxb2 $19 {is even more convincing.}) 34. Qe1 ({A better practical chance was} 34. Rxd5 {, but after the precise} Qf6!? ({Magnus would have maybe preferred the technical} 34... Qxb2 35. Bxe4 Qxf2+ 36. Kxf2 Nxe4+ 37. Ke1 Nc3 $19 {and the  is won for Black.}) 35. Bxe4 Qxh4 36. Qc2 Nxe4 37. Rxc5 Kg7! (37... g3? 38. Rc8 Qh2+ 39. Kf1 Qh1+ 40. Ke2 Qxg2+ 41. Kd3 $13 {nie je jasné}) 38. f6+ (38. Rc8 Re5! $19) 38... Kh6 39. Rf5 g3 40. Rdd5 Nxf6! $19 {Black wins.}) 34... Qf6 {Now all is much simpler and attack breaks through.} 35. Rxd5 Qxh4 36. Bxe4 Qh1+ 37. Kf2 Nxe4+ 38. Ke2 Qxg2+ 39. Kd3 {White resigned without waiting for Black's move,} ({The quickest and most forcing way is} 39. Kd3 c4+ (39... b5 $19) (39... Qxb2 $19) 40. Kxc4 Qc2+ 41. Kb4 Qxb2+ 42. Ka5 b6+! 43. Kxa6 Qa3+ 44. Kxb6 Rb8+ 45. Kc7 Qa7+ 46. Kc6 Qb7#) 0-1

