{The world champion's return to open tournaments indeed had a rough start, but by round 5 he certainly had found his pace, and on Christmas Eve he created excellent entertainment for all those kids waiting for the evening to come. And for all other chess-lovers:} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. f3!? {A move that has been very much in the limelight over the last couple of years, but somehow the interest seems to have faded a bit, for no apparent reason except fashion.} d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nb6 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Be3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. O-O-O f5 (9... Qd6 {has been established as the main line, and is what Caruana played against Magnus in Norway Chess 2014. But Li, stays true to his favourite choice.}) 10. e5 Nb4 11. Nh3 Qe8!? {Here however Li springs his surprise, just before Magnus would had a chance to show how he thought to proceed after Li's usual move 11..a5. The text move has the queen not only the keeping an eye on the f7-square, attacking a2, but also ideas like ...a4 exchanging of an important defender of both the queenside and the d5-square. Magnus, almost in Grischuk like fashion, here had a very long think, trying to grasp the subleties of Li's novelty.} 12. Kb1 a5 {Black switches back to this move, the point of 12.b1 being that 12...f7 could now just be answered by 13.g5! and 13...xa2 no longer is check.  In a way this position is a "mini zugzwang", in the sense that White would love to play f4 and h4 with a complete grip, but Black's concept with ...e8 is, that f4 can be answered strongly by ...g5! as h5 is protected. Thus Black do not want to play ...e6, as this exactly allows f4 and h4, so both players try to bide their time, while making small improvements to their position.} 13. Be2 c6 14. Rc1 Kh8?! {A logical move, seen often in the Leningrad Dutch freeing the g8-square for the black bishop, but with hindsight it is easy to see that the king is very vulnerable in the h-file.} (14... Be6 {seems preferable straight away, even if} 15. Ng5!? {still seems to give White a promising position.}) 15. Ka1! {One could make the same argument here in reverse, that the white king is vulnerable due to the attack in the a-file, but there is a major difference. Partly in that there is no white pawn on b3, and thus the direct ...a4 is harmless, but especially as it becomes relevant in the game that ...xa2 is no longer check.} Be6 16. Nf4 ({Without the inclusion of ...h8 a1} 16. Ng5 {was the move, here however it leads to} Nc4! {as} 17. Qe1?? {now fails to} Nxe3 {when} 18. Nxe6 {is met by a deadly fork on c2, due to the king now being on a1.}) 16... Qf7 17. h4! {Exploiting the situation to the maximum attacking the black king and not caring about an unimportant pawn.} Bxa2?! {A very principled move.} (17... Rfd8 {still seemed very decent, as the threat of 18...xe5 slows White down somewhat.} {Probably} 18. Nh3!? {is the correct way forward, preparing 19.h5, and if} ({, and} 18. Nxe6 Qxe6 19. f4 (19. h5 g5) 19... h5! {leaves the position too blocked for White's liking.}) ({The straightforward} 18. h5 g5 19. Ng6+ Kg8 {leads nowhere}) 18... h5 {by Black, then} ({and if} 18... Bxe5 {then} 19. Ng5! {followed by 20.h5 gives a very dangerous attack for the sacrificed pawn.}) 19. g4!) 18. h5 Kg8 {Of course it can be seen as admitting that move 14 was wrong, but Black will claim he won the a2-pawn, and that White has to play energetically to prove Black's plan wrong:} 19. hxg6 hxg6 20. g4 Bb3! {Neccesary,} ({as} 20... a4 {is answered by the simple} 21. gxf5 a3 22. b3! {when Black's attack comes much too late, despite winning the white queenside pawns. But now however should the black pawn reach a3, his threats are deadly, so Magnus has to act decisively.}) 21. Bd1! {White wants to get his queen to h2 as soon as possible, creating mating threats against the black king, but in this way he maximises the pressure on Black's position, exploiting the fact that the bishop on b3 is vulnerable, which is more than Black's position can bear. Another move actually has a lot of logic too:} ({As White wants to get his queen to h2, and also slow down Black's attack,} 21. Ba6!? {comes to mind, and indeed the computers after some time shows White is completely winning!}) 21... a4 22. Qh2 Rfd8 23. Qh7+ Kf8 24. d5 {A good move, attacking the knight on b6, and if a knight takes on d5, allowing e6.} ({However} 24. gxf5!? {might be a simpler win, allthough "simple" might not be very accurate, the point is that after} gxf5 (24... a3 {also does not work, as} 25. Bxb3 Qxb3 26. Ne6+! {is just in time, due to} Ke8 27. Qg8+! Kd7 28. Nc5+ {winning the queen on b3.}) {the counter-intuitive} 25. Bxb3! axb3+ 26. Kb1 {is completely winning for White, as his attack comes first. The main point is} Ra5 27. Rcg1 Rda8 {and now} 28. Qh8+! {exploits the g-file which has been opened, and wins on the spot.}) 24... Nc4!? {Setting up an unstoppable threat of ...a3. White's only chance it getting to the black king first, which requires very accurate calculation.} 25. Nxg6+! {Reversing the order would be fatal.} ({After} 25. e6?? a3! {Black gives mate.}) 25... Ke8 26. e6 a3!? {Very close to being brilliant.} 27. exf7+ Kd7 {It looks like the black king has escaped, and being a queen down seems less important, than the speed of the mating attack. But:} 28. Ne5+!! {Destabilising the knight on c4 is the key, as without it, Black has no forced mate.} ({That White is in grave danger is illustrated by} 28. bxa3?? Rxa3+ 29. Kb1 Bc2+!! {which forces mate.} 30. Bxc2 (30. Rxc2 Rb3+! 31. Kc1 Nd3# {being the point.}) 30... Ra1+!! 31. Kxa1 Bxc3+ 32. Kb1 Na3#) 28... Bxe5 ({As} 28... Kc7 29. Nxc4 {puts an end to the black attack,}) ({or} 28... Nxe5 29. Bxb3 {as well, Black has no choice.}) 29. Qxf5+ Kc7 30. Qxe5+!! {Forcing the c4 to leave its post. As 30...c8 31.e6+ is curtains, Black has to take.} Nxe5 31. Bxb3 {The bishop on b3, did end up hanging! Materially Black is not in a hopeless situation, as he now wins an exchange, but the passed pawn on f7 makes White's edge decisive.} axb2+ 32. Kxb2 Nbd3+ 33. Kb1 Nxc1 34. Rxc1 Kc8 ({If} 34... Nxf7 {then} 35. dxc6 {threatening on f7, as well as b5+ wins.}) 35. dxc6 bxc6 36. f4 {And Black resigned. Magnus' trademark indeed is unique technical performances making him compared to Capablance, but in this game he shows he also has the ability to play like Tal.} 1-0

