1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. Qc2 Bb4+!? {An interesting transposition to the Catalan, avoiding the dangerous lines with ...c5 and d4-d5.} 6. Bd2 Be7 7. Bg2 c6 8. O-O (8. c5!? {has scored quite well in practice and is logical, too, from the structural point of view.} O-O 9. Na3 Bb7 (9... d5 10. cxd6 Qxd6 11. O-O {[%cal Ga6e2,Gd2f4]} Rc8 12. Rfe1 Bb7 13. Nc4 $16 {Shulman,Y (2632)-Friedel,J (2516)/Ledyard 2009/CBM 129 Extra (1-0, 31)}) 10. b4 a5 11. Nc4 Nd5 12. Rb1 axb4 13. Nxb6 Nxb6 14. Rxb4 Ra6 15. Qb3 d5 16. Rxb6 Rxb6 17. cxb6 c5 18. O-O c4 19. Qb2 Nc6 20. a4 $16 {Topalov,V (2791)-Zhigalko,S (2592)/Dresden 2008/CBM 128 (1-0, 40)}) 8... d5 9. Ne5 Nfd7 ({Delaying this knight retreat offers White additional possibilities:} 9... O-O 10. Rd1 Nfd7 (10... Qc8 11. Nc3 Nbd7 12. Rac1 Nxe5 13. dxe5 Nd7 14. cxd5 cxd5 15. Bf4 g5 16. Bxd5! {Ponomariov,R (2734)-Kramnik,V (2790) Dortmund 2010 CBM 138 [Ponomariov] (1-0, 36)}) 11. cxd5 cxd5 12. e4! {This is the difference! The e-pawn is not pinned any more.} (12. Bf4 Nxe5 13. dxe5 {transposes}) 12... Nf6 (12... Nxe5 13. exd5! $36 {Dzagnidze,N (2551)-Skripchenko,A (2460) Antakya 2010 CBM 140 [Marin,M] (0-1, 29)}) 13. Nc3 Bb7 14. exd5 Nxd5 15. Rac1 (15. Qb3 Nd7 16. Nxd5 exd5 17. Bxd5 Bxd5 18. Qxd5 Nxe5 19. Qxe5 Bf6 20. Qf5 Qxd4 21. Bc3 Qc5 22. Qxc5 bxc5 23. Bxf6 gxf6 24. Rac1 Rab8 25. b3 $16 {Llanes Hurtado,M (2446)-Guliev,L (2454) Santa Cruz de la Palma 2007 (1-0, 45)}) 15... a6 16. Qb3 Nxc3 17. Bxc3 Bxg2 18. Kxg2 Qd5+ 19. Qxd5 exd5 20. Bd2 $16 {Gupta,A (2590)-Zeng,C (2377) Chennai 2011 (1-0, 45)}) 10. cxd5 cxd5 11. Bf4 Nxe5 12. dxe5 O-O 13. Rd1 {This position has proven quite viable for Black. It is quite possible that Nakamura did not expect this course of events at all, since the novelty he produced one move later hardly changes the evaluation.} Bb7 (13... Qd7 {is also playable.} 14. Nc3 Rc8 (14... Nc6? 15. Bxd5! exd5 16. Nxd5 $16 {Bogdanovski,V (2435)-Mitkov,N (2492)/Gevgelija 2002/CBM 087 ext (1-0, 41)}) 15. Rac1 Bb7 (15... Nc6 16. Qa4 Bb7 17. h4 a6 18. Nxd5 exd5 19. Bxd5 {Banusz,T (2517)-Bokros,A (2479)/Szentgotthard 2010/CBM 135 (1/2, 62)} Bf8!?) 16. h4 Nc6 17. Qd2 Rd8 18. e3 Rac8 19. Qe2 Qe8 20. h5 h6 21. Qg4 Kh8 $11 {Prudnikova,S (2407)-Ioseliani,N (2491)/Bled 2002/EXT 2003 (1/2, 43)}) ({But} 13... g5?! {is obviously premature, since the e5-pawn is not attacked yet.} 14. Be3 Nd7 15. Bd4 Rc8 16. Nc3 Qc7 17. e4 $36 {Koneru,H (2603)-Thejkumar,M (2414)/Mumbai 2008/CBM 124 Extra (1-0, 27)}) 14. Nd2 $146 {Not that great a square for the knight.} ({Black did not face big problems after} 14. Nc3 {either:} Nc6 15. h4 (15. Nxd5?! exd5 16. Bxd5 Qe8 17. Qe4 Rd8 18. Rac1 Ba8 $17 {Feller,S (2424)-Palac,M (2582)/Cap d'Agde 2006/EXT 2007 (0-1, 45)}) 15... Rc8 {The most logical developing move.} (15... h6 {An unnecessary weakening.} 16. e4 d4 17. Qe2 Qb8 18. Nb5 Rd8 19. Qg4 Kf8?! (19... g6 20. Rac1 Nxe5 $13) 20. Rac1 a6 (20... Nxe5?! 21. Qh5! Bf6 22. Nc7 $16) 21. Nd6 $16 {Razuvaev,Y (2560)-Nikolenko,O (2475)/Pardubice 1993/TD (1-0, 34)}) 16. Rac1 (16. e4 d4 17. Qe2 Qe8 18. Nb5 Bc5 19. a3 Rd8 20. b4 Be7 21. Qb2 d3 22. Bg5 $13 {Rahman,Z (2538)-Roktim,B (2389)/Dhaka 2005/CBM 105 ext (1/2, 64)}) 16... a6 17. Qb1 Qe8 18. Rc2 (18. Na4 Bd8 19. b3 Bc7 20. Be3 Nxe5 21. Bxb6 Bxb6 {1/2 Damljanovic,B (2585)-Roiz,M (2605)/Valjevo 2007/CBM 119}) 18... b5 19. a3 b4 20. axb4 Nxb4 21. Rcc1 Bc5 22. Na2 Nxa2 23. Qxa2 Qb5 24. e3 a5 $36 {Lastin,A (2590)-Tkachiev,V (2605)/Sochi 2005/CBM 107 (1/2, 42)}) 14... Nc6 (14... Na6!? 15. e4 Rc8 16. Qb1 Nb4 17. exd5 Bxd5 18. Be4 Bxe4 19. Nxe4 Qe8 20. Nc3 (20. Nd6 $13) 20... Qc6 21. a3 Na6 22. Ne4 Nc5 23. Nxc5 Bxc5 $11 {Iskusnyh,S (2455)-Lastin,A (2611)/Samara 2011/ (1/2, 35)}) 15. Nf3?! {Underestimating the force of the next move.} (15. h4 $142 {Carlsen} Rc8 $36) 15... g5! {I cannot help remembering another occasion in which a plan based on ...g7-g5 yielded Carlsen a great win. It was the Hedgehog from Kramnik,V (2799)-Carlsen,M (2733) Wijk aan Zee 2008 CBM 122 [Marin,M] (0-1, 57)} 16. Be3 g4 {Well, here everything is simpler: Black wins a pawn. However, one should also pay attention to his stability in the centre, ensured precisely by the far advanced pawn.} 17. Nd4 Nxe5 $15 18. Bh6 Re8 19. e4 Bc5 20. Nb3 Rc8 21. Nxc5 Rxc5 22. Qa4 Bc6 23. Qd4 Qf6 $17 24. Bf4 dxe4 25. Bxe4 Nf3+!! {If my endgame analysis is correct, this is a brilliant move, leading almost by force to a won position, despite the presence of opposite-coloured bishops..} (25... Bxe4 26. Qxe4 Nf3+ 27. Kg2 h5 $17 {would have been a reasonable alternative.}) 26. Bxf3 Qxd4 27. Rxd4 Bxf3 28. Rd7 Rd5 29. Rxd5 (29. Rxa7 Red8 $19) 29... exd5 30. Be3 Re4 31. Re1 d4 32. Bd2 Rxe1+ {(!) The same comment applies to this move.} (32... Be2 $17) 33. Bxe1 Be2! {Caging the enemy king.} 34. f4 {White cannot do without this freeing move, which allows Black to create a second far advanced passed pawn.} (34. Bd2 Kg7 35. Kg2 Kg6 36. h3 h5 37. hxg4 hxg4 38. Bf4 (38. f4 gxf3+ 39. Kf2 Kf5 40. Bf4 Ke4 41. Bb8 a6 42. Ba7 b5 43. Bb8 Bc4 44. a3 Bd5 45. Bf4 Kd3 46. Be5 Kc4 47. Bf6 d3 48. Ke3 Be4 $19) 38... Kf5 39. Bh6 Ke4 40. Bg7 b5 41. Bf6 a6 42. Bg7 Bc4 {Just as in the game, it is important to clear the way for the king. With the actual structure, the space for invasion is too narrow.} 43. a3 (43. b3 Bd3 44. Bf6 Bb1 45. a3 Bc2 46. b4 Bd3 47. Bg7 Be2 48. Bf6 Kd3 49. Bg7 Kc4 $19) 43... Be2 44. Bf6 Kd3 45. Be5 Kc4 46. Bf6 d3 47. Bc3 Kb3 $19) 34... gxf3 35. Bf2 d3 36. Be1 Kg7 37. Kf2 Kf6 38. Ke3 Kf5 39. h3 {Another important decision.} ({Keeping the pawn on h2 would not save him, either:} 39. Bc3 Kg4 40. Kf2 Kh3 41. Kg1 Bd1 42. Bd2 ({The attempt to keep the bishop prisoner with} 42. b3 {fails to} Bc2 43. Kf2 Bb1 44. a3 Ba2 45. b4 Bd5 {The bishop is right in time to defend f3.} 46. Kg1 b5 47. Bd2 Be4 {We will see a lot of this setup, with two pawns close to the promotion square and defended by the bishop.} 48. Bc3 Kg4 $19) 42... Ba4 (42... Bc2 {is less effective now:} 43. Kf2 Bb1?! 44. a3 Kxh2 45. Kxf3 {White cannot be sure of a draw at all, but Black's winning plan is less obvious here.}) 43. Bc3 Bc6 44. Bd2 Bd5 45. b3 a5 46. Be1 (46. Be3 b5 47. Bd2 b4 48. Be3 Bxb3!) 46... Bc6 (46... a4?! 47. bxa4 Bxa2 48. a5 b5 49. a6 Bd5 50. a7 Kg4 51. Kf2 {White can hold all the enemy pawns, while the black bishop is partly restricted.}) 47. Bd2 a4! {Weakening the light squares in order to prepare the king's invasion.} 48. bxa4 Bxa4 49. Kf2 Bc6 50. Kg1 b5 51. Be3 Bd5 52. a3 Be4 53. Bd2 Kg4 54. Kf2 Kf5 55. h3 Ke6 56. Be3 Kd5 57. Ke1 Kc4 58. Kd2 Kb3 59. Bc5 h5 $22 $19 60. Kd1 b4! {×b4} 61. axb4 Kc3 $19) 39... h5 40. Bd2 Bf1 41. Be1 ({Here is another possible ending with the same familiar winning method:} 41. h4 Kg4 42. Kf2 (42. Be1 Be2 {...f5-f4}) 42... Be2 43. Bf4 Bd1 44. Ke3 Bc2 45. Kf2 Bb1 46. a3 Ba2 47. Bc1 Bd5 48. Bf4 b5 49. Bd2 a6 50. Bc3 Be4 51. Bd2 Kf5 52. Ke3 Ke6 53. b3 {Hoping to create a barrier, but it will not last for long.} Kf5 54. a4 Kg4 55. axb5 axb5 56. Kf2 Bd5 57. b4 Be4 {A familiar setup.} 58. Be3 Kf5 {Now, everything is ready for the invasion.} 59. Bf4 Ke6 60. Bd2 Kd5 61. Ke3 Kc4 62. Kf2 Kb3 63. Ke3 Kc2 64. Be1 f2 $19) 41... Bxh3 42. Kxd3 ({The most fantastic win arises after} 42. Kxf3 Bg4+ 43. Ke3 Be2 44. Bc3 Kg4 45. Be1 f6 {Playing for zugzwang.} 46. Bf2 f5 47. b3 (47. Be1 f4+ 48. gxf4 h4 49. f5 h3 50. f6 h2 51. f7 h1=Q 52. f8=Q Qxe1 $19) 47... b5 (47... f4+ 48. gxf4 h4 49. f5!) 48. a3 a6 49. b4 {The tempi have been exhausted, but now the queenside configuration is lethal for the white king.} f4+! 50. gxf4 h4 51. f5 h3 52. f6 d2! 53. Kxd2 (53. Kxe2 h2 54. f7 d1=Q+!) 53... h2 (53... Bc4 54. Bg1 Kg3 55. Bb6 h2 56. Bc7+ Kg2 57. Bxh2 Kxh2 58. Kc3 $11) 54. f7 h1=Q 55. f8=Q Qd1+ 56. Kc3 Qd3+ 57. Kb2 Qd2+ $19 {with mate to follow soon. Quite an unexpected finish!!}) 42... Bf1+ 43. Ke3 Kg4 44. Kf2 Bb5 45. Bc3 Bc6 46. Be5 b5 47. Bb8 a6 48. Bc7 f5 49. b3 Bd5 50. Bd6 f4 51. gxf4 (51. Bxf4 h4 $19) 51... h4 52. f5 Kxf5 53. Ke3 Kg4 54. Kf2 h3 55. Ke3 Be4 56. Kf2 Bb1 {The already known plan of widening the area for infiltration.} 57. a3 Ba2 58. b4 Bf7! ({Black's plan is familiar to us: with two pawns close to promoting and sustained by the bishop, Black keeps both white pieces busy and can quietly march in with the king.} 58... Bf7 59. Bc7 Bh5 60. Bb8 Kf5 61. Bc7 Ke4 62. Bd6 Kd3 63. Bb8 (63. Kg3? h2) ({The black bishop's placement is explained by the following variation:} 63. Bh2 Kc2 64. Kg3 Bg4 65. Bg1 Kb3 {Once again this setup. Black's kingside group remains untouchable. The bishop on g2 would be less effective, because Black could not re-activate it if necessary. Besides, Nakamura has kept the f1-square defended, so there was no way to get it there.}) 63... Kc3 64. Kg1 Kb3 65. Bc7 Kxa3 66. Ba5 Bg4 $22 $19) 0-1

