1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d5 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. O-O Nb6 8. Nc3 Nc6 9. d5 Na5 10. e4 c6 11. Bf4 Nac4 12. Qe2 Bg4 13. h3 Bxf3 14. Bxf3 cxd5 15. exd5 {A fairly common Grünfeld structure.} Re8 (15... Nd6 {Carlsen-Wei Yi, 2016}) 16. Rac1 e5? {An unfortunate idea. Helping White's f3 into the game is not recommended.} 17. dxe6 Rxe6 18. Qc2 g5 19. Rfd1 Qe7 20. Bd2 Rd8 ({The only way to justify g6-g5 seemed} 20... Nxd2 21. Qxd2 {but in a few moves,} Re5 22. Kg2 Rf8 23. b3 f5 24. a4 a5 {the game would turn into a typical Carlsen endgame. I wouldn't rate Black's chances of survival too optimistically.} 25. Qd6 $16) 21. Nd5! Nxd5 22. Qxc4 Red6 23. Re1 Qf6 24. Qg4 h6 25. Rc8! Bf8 26. Bxd5 ({Magnus didn't seem to trust} 26. Re8 Rxc8 27. Qxc8 Qxf3 28. Rxf8+ Kg7 29. Rh8 Rg6 {although White stands better after} 30. Rg8+ Kh7 31. Re8 Qd1+ 32. Be1 {as Black's problems with his king seem permanent.}) 26... Rxd5 27. Bc3 Qg6 (27... Qd6 $142) 28. Re8? (28. Qf3 Rxc8 29. Qxd5 {is still much better for White.}) 28... Rxe8? {Veselin was really low on time.} ({He missed his only chance:} 28... Rd1+ 29. Kh2 Rxe8 30. Rxe8 Qd3! {as} 31. Qb4 Rh1+! {draws by perpetual.}) 29. Rxe8 Qb1+ 30. Kh2 Qf1 31. Be1 Kg7 32. Qf3 1-0

