{[%evp 0,178,28,10,41,41,41,41,50,36,38,33,73,31,31,33,31,-3,-3,-28,-15,-11,0,-30,-12,-37,-18,-26,6,-18,11,-19,-19,-19,-4,-14,-13,-20,-28,-26,37,76,70,42,46,-8,-3,-61,-67,-83,-67,-59,-48,-52,-47,-50,-46,-45,-35,-41,-25,-14,-20,-42,-51,-51,-34,-28,-32,-26,-25,-34,-12,-10,-18,-19,-20,-17,-4,-13,0,-33,-35,-30,-12,-19,-24,-23,-35,-32,-36,-30,-46,-61,-35,-30,-37,-15,-21,-22,0,-16,-6,-42,-54,-99,-69,-111,-106,-170,-168,-169,-169,-169,-169,-179,-187,-180,-180,-183,-153,-199,-199,-255,-249,-241,-216,-239,-230,-239,-233,-234,-260,-260,-288,-326,-347,-410,-480,-517,-331,-325,-327,-448,-399,-399,-329,-338,-353,-352,-349,-346,-346,-349,-349,-349,-374,-389,-411,-403,-405,-405,-376,-383,-387,-388,-387,-388,-255,-192,-184,-183,-157,-507,-527,-537,-537,-537,-557,-567,-567]} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Magnus favourite opening of late, obviously played in the World Championship but actually starting already from the 2018 Sinquefield Cup. There MVL played 3.Nc3, but since then the focus has been on first 3.Bb5 and then the popular 7.Nd5 in the Sveshnikov where Caruana earlier in the tournament continued his theoretical discusion vs. Magnus.} 3. Nc3 {Svidler copies MVL, staying away from the most principal battles.} e5 4. Bc4 Be7!? {Absolutely the standard move, but in St. Louis Magnus choose the more surprising 4...g6, but here reverts to the standard.} 5. d3 d6 6. Nd2!? {Kasparov's favourite move, aiming for the control of d5. Later attention switched to 6.0-0 Nf6 7.Ng5 leading to a much sharper battle, but the positional approach both has logic and venom.} Nf6 {The modest 6...Bg5 was the choice in the early encounters, but modern theory has started to focus on this natural development move.} 7. Nf1 Nd7!? {This, however, is much less played, but does follow in the footsteps of both Kramnik and Gelfand and was also employed twice by Lenic in his 2917 World Cup match vs. Magnus' French second Laurent Fressinet. The idea is to exchange White's strong knight on d5, and while obviously this also could be done from f6, and thus by logic Black just throws away 2 tempi, the point is doing it on Black's terms, trying to force White to take on b6.} 8. Nd5 (8. Ne3 {was Fressinet and Kasparov's choice, and while White does keep a nominal edge, neither managed to break down the solidity of Black's position.}) 8... Nb6 9. Nxb6!? axb6 10. c3 {This is the point of White's play, creating a somewhat akward double pawn, and now not allowing 10.Ne3 b5! removing the weakness due to 11.Bxb5 Qa5+! losing a piece.} (10. Ne3 b5! 11. Bxb5 Qa5+! {[%cal Ga5e1,Ga5b5]}) 10... O-O 11. Ne3 Bg5 12. O-O {Here Kramnik took on e3 followed by ...Qe7 and Be6 equalising, and the engines verify similar thoughts, suggesting 12...Be6. But how do you actually play for a win?} Kh8!? {At least posing the question if ...f5 next is a "threat" as well as generally safeguarding the king.} 13. a3?! (13. Nd5 {now ould have been safer as after} Bxc1 14. Qxc1 f5 15. exf5 Bxf5 16. Qe3 {White does keep a pleasant position with b4 or f4 next.}) {Svidler however indicates that ...f5 does not worry him, and Magnus follows with maybe you should be?} 13... f5! 14. Nxf5 Bxc1 15. Rxc1 Bxf5 16. exf5 d5! {White got bishop vs knight, but black has the f-file and more pawns in the centre.} 17. Ba2 {Svidler plays ambitiously eyeing the d5-pawn as a potential weakness.} ({but safer would be the liquidating} 17. Bb5!? Rxf5 18. Bxc6 {simplifying with a likely draw.}) {Not the battle focuses around if White's bishop on a2 will be in or out of play:} 17... Rxf5 18. Qg4 Rf6 19. f4?! {The wish to exchange as well as annulate the pressure in the f-line is understandable, but the ensuing tactics turns out (very) well for Black. Common sense chess with 19.Rce1 Qd6 20.Qd1 brings some harmony into White's positions, and while his aggressive prospects are not huge, he does have a solid position. After the game move, however, not so much.} (19. Rce1 Qd6 20. Qd1) 19... exf4 20. Qg5 {A neccesary intermediate move} ({as} 20. Rxf4? {loses material immediately to} Ne5 21. Qg3 Rxf4 22. Qxf4 Nxd3) 20... Qf8! {While the early phase of this game of Magnus has seen standard moves combined with a bit of provocation, the rest is another serious matter, developing a strong attack and finishing it off in style. Keeping the f4 pawn is crucial in his attacking concept, and readmitting the bishop on a2 into the game of much lesser importance, as the game will show its just shooting into thin air.} 21. Qxd5 {21.Bxd5 rerouting the bishop to e4 would have been ideal strategically, but unfortunately drops a pieces due to 21...Rf5!} (21. Bxd5 Rf5!) 21... Rd8! 22. Qf3?! {Logical and consequent, but giving Black everything he wants.} (22. Qg5 h6 23. Qh5 Rxd3 24. Rce1 {is indeed a pawn down, but with decent compensation with good chances of a draw.}) 22... Ne5 23. Qe4!? {Svidler maybe pinned his hopes on this move, kicking the knight and thus not allowing ...f3 which would be lethal after the direct 23.Qxb7.} (23. Qxb7 f3!) 23... Ng4! {Magnus is in the zone, both mentally but also with the knight invading next on e3 where it becomes the fix-point of the black attack.} (23... Nxd3 {would leave Black a pawn up but the attack would then evaporate.}) 24. Rce1 Ne3 25. Rf2 Re8! 26. Qxb7 g5! {First a pawn sac and then followed up by this key move. The knight on e3 and the pawn on f4 completely block White's rooks, and the bishop on a2 basically is on an empty diagonal as Black's attack will happen on the dark squares. White's position might look sufficiently solid, but the march of the g-pawn drastically changes that. White'ss position is actually so bad that there is nothing he can do, and Svidler decides to literally do that, going back and forth:} 27. Rfe2?! {I guess should be criticised on principle, but I cannot offer any reasonable alternative.} g4 28. Rf2 Qh6! {Simply threatening ...g3 and then mate on h2.} 29. Qc7 {X-raying the g3 square.} Ref8! {Renewing the threat of... g3, with hxg3 Ng4+! now being the followup, and there is no longer a rook hanging on e8 against which White can do absolutely nothing.} 30. h3!? {One might think that Svidler decided to be a good sport and allow a very spectacular finish, but actually his position is just dead lost.} ({The computer wants tragic moves like} 30. Qe5 g3 31. hxg3 Ng4 32. Qxf6+) ({or} 30. g3 fxg3 31. Qxg3 {allowing} Rxf2 {just to illustrate how desperate the situation actually is.}) 30... gxh3 31. g3 fxg3 32. Rxf6!? {The idea is that after 32...Qxf6 33 Qxg3 white does fight on, but also that after:} h2+! 33. Kh1 g2# {We have a mate that just speak for itself in its beauty:} 0-1

