1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. b3 Bb7 6. Bg2 Bb4+ 7. Bd2 c5 8. O-O O-O 9. Bc3 Na6 10. a3 (10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. e3 Be7 12. Nbd2 Nc5 13. Rc1 Qc7 14. Qe2 Rac8 15. Rfd1 Rfd8 16. Be5 d6 17. Bb2 a6 18. Nd4 Bxg2 19. Kxg2 Qb7+ 20. f3 d5 $11 {1-0 Ponomariov,R (2705)-Grischuk,A (2715)/Moscow 2007/EXT 2008 (33)}) (10. Bb2 {is played more often, e.g.} cxd4 11. Qxd4 (11. a3 Be7 12. b4 Rc8 13. Qxd4 Nb8 14. Nbd2 d6 15. Rfd1 Nbd7 16. Rac1 Rc7 17. Ne1 Qa8 18. e4 Rfc8 19. Nd3 a6 20. Qe3 h6 21. h3 b5 $132 {1/2 Pankratov,A (2530)-Olofson,D (2515)/ICCF 2002/ICCF Telechess CBM 112 (43)}) (11. Nxd4 Bxg2 12. Kxg2 Qc7 13. Nf3 Be7 14. Nc3 Nc5 15. Qd4 Qb7 16. Rfd1 Rfd8 17. Rac1 Rac8 18. Kg1 d6 19. Qe3 a6 20. Nd4 Bf8 $11 {1/2 Aronian,L (2808)-Carlsen,M (2815)/Monte Carlo 2011/CBM 142 (37)}) 11... Be7 (11... Bc5!? 12. Qf4 d5) 12. Nc3 Nc5 13. Rfd1 d6 14. Rac1 a6 15. Qe3 Qc7 16. b4 Ncd7 $132 {0-1 Olafsson,H (2535)-Hjartarson,J (2595)/Vestmannaeyjar 1994/EXT 1997 (65)}) 10... Bxc3 11. Nxc3 cxd4 12. Nxd4 (12. Qxd4 Nc5 13. Nd2 Bxg2 14. Kxg2 d5 15. cxd5 exd5 16. Rfd1 Re8 17. Rac1 Qd7 18. b4 Ne6 19. Qd3 d4 $132 {1-0 Neverov,V (2535)-Kiselev,S (2470)/Pardubice 1993/TD (48)}) 12... Bxg2 13. Kxg2 Nc5 14. Rc1 Qc7 15. b4 Nb7 16. Ncb5 Qb8 17. Qd3 Rc8 18. Rfd1 h6 19. f3 a6 20. Nc3 Nd6 21. Nb1 Nde8 22. Nd2 Qb7 23. c5 {Black's position is very solid and can resist such attempts to break it up.} (23. N2b3 d6 {leads to a normal hedgehog.}) 23... bxc5?! (23... a5!? {, immediately opening up the a-file and activating the rook, may be more accurate.}) 24. bxc5 Qa7 (24... Rab8 $142) 25. N2b3 {[%csl Rd4] The wrong arrangement of the knights!} (25. N4b3 $142 ! a5 26. a4 Nd5 27. Nc4! {, and Black faces some problems}) 25... Nd5 26. c6 dxc6?! {Obvious but not good. Black had a chance to trade his passive rook:} (26... Rxc6! 27. Rxc6 (27. Nxc6 Ne3+ 28. Kg1 (28. Kh1 Nxd1 {[%cal Rd1f2]}) 28... dxc6 {regaining material with a good position}) 27... dxc6 28. Nxc6 Ne3+ 29. Kg1 Qb6 30. Rc1 Nf5+ {with sufficient counterplay.}) 27. e4 {Of course, after this pawn sacrifice White will have compensation due to his more active pieces but it is not enough for any advantage.} (27. Nxc6 Ne3+ 28. Kh1 Nxd1 29. Nxa7 Rxc1! 30. Kg2 Rc3 31. Qxd1 Rxa7 32. Qd8 Rxb3 33. Qxe8+ Kh7 $15) (27. Na5 $142 {setting up the knight on c6 and creating a kind of domination:} c5 (27... Ne7 28. Naxc6 Nxc6 29. Nxc6 $14 {[%csl Gc6]}) 28. Ndc6 c4 (28... Qb6 29. e4) 29. Rxc4 Ne3+ 30. Kg1 Rd8! 31. Qxd8 Nxd1+ 32. Qd4! Qxd4+ 33. Rxd4 Nc3 34. Kf2 $14) {However, Black's inaccurate play enables White to create a similar domination later on.} 27... Ne7 28. Nc5 Nf6 29. Ndb3 Ng6 30. Qc3 Nf8 (30... a5 $142 31. a4 Qc7) 31. Na5 Rab8 32. a4 Kh8 33. Rb1?! {Why exchange Black's crumpled pieces?!} (33. Rd6! $36) 33... Rb6 34. Rxb6 Qxb6 35. Nab7 (35. Nab3!? Rb8 36. Rd6) 35... Rb8 36. Nd6 Kg8 37. Rd2 a5 38. Nd3 N8d7 39. Nc4 Qa6 40. Rc2 (40. Nxa5 Ra8 41. Nxc6 Qxa4) 40... c5 41. Rb2 Rb7 42. Rxb7 Qxb7 43. Qb2 Qa6 44. Qb5 Qxb5 45. axb5 {[%mdl 4096]} a4 {The exchanges have been favourable for Black, who must now reach a draw without problems.} 46. Ndb2? {[%mdl 8192] White loses his sense of danger and misses a strong tactical blow!} ({A logical outcome of the game could be something like} 46. b6 a3 47. Nc1 (47. Nxa3 Nxb6 48. Nxc5 $11) 47... Kf8 48. b7 Ke7 49. e5 Nd5 50. Nxa3 Nb4 51. Nb5 Nc6 52. f4 Kd8 53. Kf3 Nd4+ 54. Nxd4 cxd4 55. Ke4 Kc7 56. Kxd4 Kxb7 $11) 46... Nb6! 47. Kf2 (47. Nxb6? {is impossible due to} a3 {, and Black's pawn queens, which means that Black now blockades White's passed pawn and keeps his strong extra pawn.}) 47... Nfd7 48. Ke3 Kf8 49. f4 f5 50. exf5 exf5 51. Kd3 Ke7 52. Na3 Nf6! 53. Nbc4 Nxc4 54. Nxc4 Ne4 55. Ne5?! {Totally upset, White doesn't offer serious resistance.} (55. Kc2 Nd6! 56. b6 Kd7 57. Na5 {, setting up a barrier, was much more stubborn.}) 55... Kd8 56. g4 fxg4 57. Nxg4 Nd6 58. b6 Kc8 59. Ne3 Kb7 60. Nd5 Kc6 0-1

