{[%evp 0,141,19,31,14,-14,-11,0,0,6,43,66,55,20,15,4,22,24,11,11,35,6,7,-10,-13,5,0,1,-12,-21,9,1,-9,-23,-23,-122,65,83,83,86,61,95,90,105,90,104,97,105,112,95,109,105,105,106,110,102,111,77,111,93,100,98,81,71,108,109,90,90,70,61,51,86,99,72,60,84,113,85,84,92,93,81,88,68,66,55,47,55,55,55,67,43,66,45,62,47,68,54,74,76,171,102,137,135,135,152,153,179,197,193,271,271,277,277,266,256,256,243,256,211,320,298,301,255,269,259,309,256,298,308,302,310,329,313,313,278,276,287,271,235,295,587,587,587]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 dxc4 {Duda stays loyal to his tournament preparation, the Vienna had netted him 2 effortless draws in the tournament already.} 5. e4 Bb4 6. Bxc4!? {In the first round Vidit played the traditional 6 Bg5 whereafter Duda surprised with 6...b5!? 7.a4 c5. Magnus follows Duda's game with Mamedyarov, where White played the now popular pure pawn sacrifice, aiming for quick development.} Nxe4 7. O-O Nf6!? (7... Bxc3 8. bxc3 Nxc3 {gives Black even 2 extra pawns, but the white initiative becomes overwhelming. Danish IM Steffen Pedersen and Swedish GM Tiger Hillarp Pedersen showed me this idea way before it became popular, and I managed to win a blitz game agasnt Van Wely in the German Team Championship before forgetting about it, and being completely lost against Pedersen himself in an blindfold event in Denmark 2007. Won by Magnus himself, as it was organised in order to give him training for his first appearance in the Melody Amber Tournament.} 9. Qb3 Nd5 10. Ba3 Nc6 11. Rfe1 Nce7 12. Bxe7 Nxe7 13. d5 exd5 14. Bxd5 O-O 15. Bxf7+! Rxf7 16. Rad1 Qf8 17. Ng5 {is the basic tactical point, and my game against Pedersen from 2007.}) 8. Qa4+ Nc6 9. Ne5 Rb8 {Obviously the line has evolved, and Black found more sensible solutions than the early greedy attempts. 9...Rb8 is Caruana's choice against Aronian from the Norway Chess tournament 2018, but obviously part of the American's preparation for the candidates tournament. Funnily it was already played by Magnus' second Laurent Fressinet in 2010, whose loss had nothing to do with his position after the opening.} 10. Rd1!? {Aronian took on c6, and Halkias played 10.d5. Magnus plays a less direct approach, but blitzed out the following moves obviously still in his preparation. Duda knew the risks. Playing Caruana's favourite line, which Magnus obviously must have prepared, with hindsight might seem like a bad idea, but the young Pole showed numerous times in Wijk that he is not afraid of a challenge.} O-O 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Qxa7 {Quite a typical scenario in modern chess: White threatened a blitz attack by sacrificing a pawn, Black choose a solid continuation, and now suddenly White changes gears, now playing for a better pawn structure and a potentially passed a-pawn.} Bd7 {Threatening a "perpetual" against White's queen with ...Ra8/...Rb8.} 13. Qa4 c5 14. Qc2 {This is why White included 10.Rd1 0-0, he needed the rook to protect the d4-pawn.} cxd4 15. Rxd4 Bc5?! {Magnus mentioned in a video interview after the tournament that he recalled asking some of his second trying to sell him on this concept and he asked why can black not just go 15...Bc5 followed by 16...h6? Somebody switched on the engine, and said 17.Bxh6 and 18.Qd2 is +1. But no one told me what this +1 consisted of.} 16. Rh4!? {It was notheworthy that the Norwegian supercomputer "sesse" preferred 16.Rd1, aiming for more positional methods.} h6? {Played quickly, but walking straight into preparation. Or sort of preparation at least:} 17. Bxh6! gxh6 18. Qd2 {Blacks problem is that h6 cannot be protected, and losing it basically leads to mate on h8.} Nh7! {Or 18...Ne8 leading to the same thing.} (18... h5 19. Qg5+ Kh8 20. Ne4! {also causes the game to end immediately, so Black has to praise himself lucky things are actually not worse than they are.}) 19. Qxh6 Bxf2+! {Black sacrifices the bishop to force an exchange of queens. White cannot decline with 20.Kh1 as the bishop on f2 also threatens the white rook on h4.} 20. Kxf2 Qf6+ 21. Qxf6 Rxb2+! {Not only did Black have a saving check forcing off the queens, but he even manages to pick up a pawn as well with this intermezzo equalling the material balance.} 22. Kg1 Nxf6 23. Rf1! {Maybe this position does not look as much, but White's initiative still is very threatening. As Magnus knew the machines evaluated the sacrifice on h6 as advantageous for White, and he (correctly) saw no better option than this, it meant he could enter this seemingly innocent position with confidence.} Kg7!? {Duda realized his problems, and went for a practical solution.} (23... Ne8 24. Rf3 f5 25. Rg3+ Ng7 {is the computer's preferred line, but showing a considerable initiative for White after} 26. Ne2 {followed by 27.Nf4 or 27.Nd4.}) 24. Rf3 {Black's problem is the safety of his king. Rg3+ basically mating is the immediate threat.} Rg8 25. Rhf4! {Blacks last move has a dual purpose of providing an escape route via f8 for the king, as well as then attacking g2 with the rook. White, however, is just in time to start his own attack in the f-line and Duda is now forced to give up material.} Bc6 26. Rxf6! ({If} 26. Rg3+? Kf8 27. Rxf6 {Black has} Rxg3 28. hxg3 Rxg2+ {followed by ...Rc2 winning back the piece.}) 26... Bxf3 27. Rxf3 Rd8 {White has 2 minor pieces for the rook, but the reduced remaining material as well as Black's activity makes it a kind of 50-50 situation if Black can manage the draw or not.} 28. Rf2 Rb4 29. Bb5?! {Too ambitious. More stable was 29.Bb3, but Magnus underestimated Black's strong resource at move 30.} Ra8 30. a4 c6! 31. Be2 ({Due to} 31. Bxc6 Rc8! {winning a piece, Duda now manages to push White's bishop away from b5, meaning the knight is forces to stay at c3 to defend the pawn at a4.}) 31... Ra5 32. Rf3 f5 33. Kf2 Rc5 34. Bd1 {Defending the pawn on a4, and thus releasing the knight on c3 of its defensive duties.} e5?! {Logical, advancing the pawn exploiting that 35.Rxf5 can be met by 35...Rc3. However, it does weaken the f5 pawn, meaning Black will have to advance it further and thus allow White's knight to permanently settle on f4.} 35. Ne2 Kf6 36. Ra3 Rb1 37. Ke1! {A good retreat, defending the d1 bishop and not fearing 37...Rcd5 due to 38 Nc3! forking the black rooks.} e4 38. g3! ({Not falling for the trick} 38. a5? Rbb5 39. a6 Ra5! {winning the white a-pawn.}) 38... Ke5 39. h4 Ra5 40. h5 c5 41. Kd2 c4 42. Kc2 Rb8 43. Kc3 Rb1 44. Kc2 Rb8 45. Kc1 Rb6 46. Bc2 Rd6 47. Nf4 {White's pieces start landing on their ideal squares, even so breaking Black's resistance is not at all easy.} Rc5 48. Re3 Kd4 49. Re1 Rh6?! {Here Duda did not realize it was a crucial moment of the game, and just played a common sense move.} ({He had his chance, as} 49... Kc3! {would ensure counterplay sufficient for serious drawing chances as after} 50. Ne2+ Kb4 {Black makes it much more difficult for White to improve his position than in the game.}) 50. Kd2! {A very strong move either luring Black's pawn to c3, meaning b3 becomes available for the white bishop, or as in the game, White's king gets to c3.} Re5 51. Ne2+ Kc5 52. Rh1! {Covering the h5-pawn and thus getting ready for Kc3.} Re8 (52... Kb4 53. Ke3! {intending Kd4 and Rb1+ becomes decisive.}) 53. Kc3 Rg8 54. Rb1! Rxh5 55. Bxe4! Re8 56. Nf4! {A fascinating tactical sequence, despite the limited amount of material. Its end result is just the elimination of the kingside pawns.} Rg5 57. Rb5+ Kd6 58. Bxf5 Rxg3+ 59. Kd4!? {Taking on c4 was also possible, but Magnus' idea with the previous moves was aiming exactly for this position: material has been reduced even further, but Black's king suddenly surprisingly finds itself under a devastating attack!} Rg1 60. Rb6+ Ke7! {The good thing from Black's perspective is that he avoids getting mated. The problem is that the king ends up too far away from a8.} 61. Ng6+ Kf7 62. Ne5+ Kg8 63. Nxc4 {Materialistic, at last.} Ra8 64. a5 Ra1 65. Rb5 Ra7 66. Be4 Rc7 67. Rf5 Kg7 68. Bc2 Rc1 69. Kc3 Rf7 70. Rxf7+! Kxf7 {An interesting observation was being made by the spectators following the game in the live chat. This ending is generally a draw as proved by Tablebases. Here, however, the black king is not close enough to a7, and with his next move Magnus shuts down any black hope of stopping the a-pawn with his rook from a1:} 71. Na3! {Now White's a-pawn queens trivially, and not wanting the world champion to end the game by showing his skills mating with knight and bishop, Duda resigned.} 1-0

