1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 Bf5 5. Nc3 e6 6. Nh4 Bg6 (6... Be4) ({and} 6... Bg4 {are also playable, but the move in the text considered as the most solid one.}) 7. Nxg6 hxg6 8. Bd3 {In this position White can play almost every legal move, the choice of Magnus is just one of the options.} (8. Bd2 Nbd7 9. a3 Be7 10. Qc2 O-O 11. Be2 dxc4 12. Bxc4 c5 13. dxc5 Nxc5 14. O-O Rc8 15. Rfd1 Qc7 16. Rac1 Rfd8 17. Be2 Nce4 18. Be1 Nxc3 19. Qxc3 Rxd1 20. Bxd1 Qxc3 21. Rxc3 Rxc3 22. Bxc3 {/= ½-½ Carlsen,M (2693)-Anand,V (2786)/Dortmund 2007/CBM 119 (39)}) 8... Nbd7 (8... c5 9. Qb3 Qd7 10. cxd5 exd5 11. dxc5 Bxc5 12. Qb5 (12. O-O!? Nc6 (12... Bd6 13. f4 $14) 13. Rd1 $14) 12... Qxb5 13. Nxb5 Kd7 14. O-O Nc6 15. Rd1 Ke7 16. Bd2 Bb4 17. Nc3 Rac8 18. Rac1 Rhd8 19. a3 Bd6 20. Ne2 Ne5 {½-½ Kramnik,V (2744)-Gelfand,B (2724)/Saint Vincent 2005/CBM 110}) 9. O-O Bd6 10. h3 {So far the players have been following their previous exciting encounter from the Tal Memorial.} dxc4 11. Bxc4 O-O {Boris is the first to deviate.} (11... Nb6 12. Bb3 e5 13. Qc2 Qe7 14. Bd2 O-O-O 15. d5 (15. Nb5 {might be an improvement for White trying to eliminate the dangerous bishop.}) 15... e4! 16. dxc6 Qe5 17. f4 exf3 18. Rxf3 Ng4! 19. cxb7+ Kb8 20. hxg4 Rh1+ 21. Kf2 Rxa1 22. Ne2 $13 {1-0 Carlsen,M (2826)-Gelfand,B (2744)/Moscow 2011/EXT 2012 (38)}) 12. Qc2 Qe7 {In general, the position is very close to equal. In the long term White hopes to benefit from the pair of bishops. Since none of Black's forces have advanced beyond the sixth rank, the second player needs more space. For this purpose he may choose between two main ideas: ..e6-e5 or ...c6-c5 to be carried out at an appropriate moment.} 13. Rd1 Rac8 14. Bd2 (14. e4?! {is met by} e5 {and Black gets good play on the dark squares.}) 14... Nb6 15. Bf1 (15. Bb3 c5 (15... e5?? 16. Qxg6 $18) 16. dxc5 Rxc5 17. Rac1 Rd8 18. Be1 g5 19. Qe2 a6 {Black has a satisfactory position.}) 15... e5 (15... c5 16. dxc5 Rxc5 17. Qb3 {keeping slight, but stable pressure.}) 16. dxe5 Bxe5 (16... Qxe5 {is less accurate.} {After} 17. g3 {later on the queen might get kicked away by f2-f4.}) 17. Rac1 Rcd8 18. Be1 {Now massive exchanges along the d-file will follow. Both sides have no grounds to avoid them, otherwise the opponent will seize the central line for free.} Rxd1 19. Rxd1 Rd8 20. Rxd8+ Qxd8 21. g3 (21. Na4 Nxa4 22. Qxa4 a6 (22... Bxb2 23. Qxa7 Qd7 24. Qa8+ Kh7 25. Qa4 Kg8 26. Qb3 Be5 27. a4 $14 {[%cal Gf1c4]}) 23. Be2 Qd7 {Black is slightly worse, but has a solid position.}) 21... Qe7 22. Bg2 Bd6 23. Bd2 Qe6 24. b3 Nbd5 25. Ne2! (25. Nxd5 cxd5! 26. Bc3 Ne4 27. Bd4 a6 {Black doesn't have any problems. His pieces are active enough and he covers all the squares on the c-file against White's queen invasion.}) 25... Nb4 26. Qb1 Qf5! 27. e4 Qc5 28. Nf4?! {I don't think that this is a good provocation.} (28. Be3 Qa5 29. Qd1 Bf8 30. Nc1 (30. a4 Bc5 $11) 30... Nxa2 31. Nxa2 Qxa2 32. e5 Nd5! 33. Bxd5 cxd5 34. Qxd5 b6 35. e6 Qb1+ 36. Kg2 Qf5 37. exf7+ Qxf7 $11) 28... g5! {Why not?} 29. Ne2 g4 {This pawn is annoying.} 30. h4 (30. Be3 Qa5 31. Qd1 Bf8 $132) 30... Nd7? {It's logical to transfer the knight to e5, but White can now exploit the moment when Black has two pieces on the d-file. So far Boris has played well, but this mistake ruins all his efforts.} (30... Be5! 31. Be3 Qa5 32. Nc1 Qd8 {[%cal Gd8d1]} 33. Ne2 Qa5 {The position is equal. If White pushes too hard, then after} 34. a4 Qd8 35. Bxa7?! Qd2 36. Bf1 Nc2 {[%csl Re4] Black seizes the initiative.}) 31. Be3 Qc2 (31... Qa5 32. Qd1 {[%csl Rd6,Rd7] is the point.} Qc7 33. a3 Na6 34. b4 {This position is extremely unpleasant for Black, perhaps just bad. The knight on a6 is misplaced, the g4 is a target and the manoeuvre e2-d4-f5 is in the air.}) 32. Qxc2 Nxc2 33. Bxa7 {It's a free pawn!} Ba3 (33... Bc5 34. Bxc5 Nxc5 {was an interesting practical chance. Black's knights are very active, though after} 35. Nc1 {The extra pawn will tell sooner or later.}) (33... b6 34. e5 $18 {[%csl Rc6]}) 34. Nc3 Bb2 35. Nd1 (35. Na4?! {is less accurate:} Bd4 36. Bxd4 Nxd4) 35... Bc1 36. Bf1 Nb4 (36... Ne5 37. Be2 f6 38. Bb8 Nf3+ (38... Nd4? 39. Bxe5 Nxe2+ 40. Kf1 fxe5 41. Kxe2 $18) 39. Kg2 Ncd4 40. Bc4+ Kf8 41. Ne3 b5 42. Bd6+ Ke8 43. Bg8 $18) 37. a4 Nf6 38. e5 Nd7 39. Bd4 Nc2 40. Bc3 Nc5 41. Be2 {[%csl Rg4]} Ne4 (41... Nxb3 42. Bxg4 $18) 42. Bxg4 Nxc3 43. Nxc3 Bb2 44. e6! fxe6 45. Bxe6+ Kf8 46. Ne4 {Two extra pawns promise White an easy win.} Nd4 47. Ng5 Ke7 48. Bg8 Kf8 49. Bc4 Ke7 50. Kg2 b5 51. Bg8 Kf8 52. a5! {[%csl Ga5]} 1-0

