1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 O-O 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 d5 7. cxd5 Ne4 8. Qc2 exd5 9. Bf4 {This line was examined in CBM 132, annotations to Morozevich - Alekseev.} Nc6 ({Everyone (apparently except Vladimir Kramnik) would like to see what Magnus Carlsen had prepared against} 9... Bf5 10. Qxc7 Qxc7 11. Bxc7 Rc8 12. Bf4 Nc6! (12... Rc2 $143 ?! 13. f3 Nf6 14. g4 Bg6 15. h4 Rxb2 16. h5 Bc2 17. e3 Ba4 18. Rh2! $16 {1-0 Morozevich,A (2751)-Alekseev,E (2714)/Biel 2009/CBM 132/[Krasenkow] (34)}) {Was it an improvement on the following game between two top female players? :} 13. f3 (13. Rd1 $143 Na5 14. g4 Bd7 15. f3 Nf6 16. Nh3 Ba4 17. Rd3 Rc2 $36 {0-1 Wiley,T (2287)-Balogh,C (2595)/Arinsal 2009/CBM 131 Extra (21)}) 13... Nf6 14. g4 Bc2 (14... Bd7?! 15. e3 Na5 16. Bd3 $16) (14... Nxd4!? 15. Rd1! Ne6 16. gxf5 Nxf4 17. e3 N4h5 18. Ne2 Rc2 19. Rd2 Rac8 20. Nc3! {consolidating his position and keeping some positional advantage.}) (14... Bg6!? {[%cal Yc6a5,Ya5b3] looks, however, very strong}) 15. e3 Na5 16. b4 (16. Kf2!? Bg6 17. Re1 Rc2+ 18. Re2 Rac8 19. Kg3!?) 16... Nb3 17. Ra2 Nc1 18. Rb2 Nxg4! 19. e4!? (19. h4 Nf6 $36) 19... Nd3+ 20. Bxd3 Bxd3 21. Kd2?? (21. e5 Rc4! $36) 21... Nf2 22. Be3 Bf1! 23. Bxf2 Bg2 $19 {0-1 Zhu Chen (2488)-Xu Yuhua (2485)/Nanjing CHN 2009/The Week in Chess 779 (40)}) 10. e3 Re8 $146 {[%mdl 8]} ({Kramnik doesn't repeat a promising line played by Vishy Anand:} 10... g5!? 11. Bg3 f5 12. O-O-O (12. Nf3!? f4 13. exf4 g4 14. Ne5 Nxd4 $13) 12... f4 13. f3 Nd6?! (13... fxg3! 14. fxe4 Rf2 $36) 14. exf4 gxf4 15. Bf2 Bf5 16. Qc5 Ne7 17. Ne2 Bg6 $13 {0-1 Karpov,A (2619)-Anand,V (2788)/Corsica FRA 2009/The Week in Chess 782 (32)}) {In the game Black gets a good position, too; it is unclear why Carlsen played such a line, which looks rather risky for White.} 11. Nf3 g5! 12. Bg3 g4 13. Ne5 Nxe5 14. Bxe5 (14. dxe5!? Bf5 15. Bd3 Bg6 (15... c5 16. f3 gxf3 17. gxf3 c4 18. Bxe4 dxe4 19. Rg1 $13) 16. Bxe4 (16. Bf4 c5!) 16... Bxe4 17. Qe2 Qg5 $11) 14... c5 15. Bd3 Bf5 (15... Qa5+? {is premature as after} 16. b4! cxb4 17. O-O bxa3 18. f3 {White completes his development and obtains a strong attack.}) 16. Qe2 (16. O-O c4 17. Bxe4 Bxe4 {[%cal Ye8e6,Ye6b6,Ye6h6] is favourable for Black (M.Notkin).}) ({Or} 16. Bxe4 Bxe4 17. Qxc5 Bxg2 18. Rg1 Bf3 $17 {[%csl Re1]}) 16... f6 17. Bxe4 (17. Bf4? Qa5+ 18. Kf1 cxd4 $19) (17. f3 {leads to a bad endgame for White:} gxf3 18. Qxf3 c4! 19. Bxe4 (19. Qxf5 cxd3 $19 {[%csl Gd3,Ge4]}) 19... Bxe4 20. Qxf6 Qxf6 21. Bxf6 Re6 $17 {[%csl Rb2][%cal Ye6b6] according to IM Maxim Notkin.}) 17... Bxe4 18. Bg3 Qa5+ 19. Qd2 Qxd2+ {A disputable decision. White's drawing chances are more realistic in the endgame, due to the opposite-coloured bishops, but Kramnik rejected} (19... Qa6 {in view of} 20. f3 (20. Qe2? Qxe2+ 21. Kxe2 cxd4) 20... gxf3 21. gxf3 Bxf3 22. Rf1 {e.g.} Bh5 (22... Bg4 23. Rf4 Bh5 24. Kf2) 23. Rf5!? (23. Rf4 cxd4 24. Rxd4 Rac8!? $40) 23... Rxe3+!? (23... Bg6 24. Rxd5) 24. Qxe3 Re8 25. Be5! (25. Qxe8+ Bxe8 {[%cal Ra6e6]} 26. Rf2 cxd4 {looks very bad for White}) 25... cxd4 26. Qg3+ Bg6 27. O-O-O! fxe5 ({or} 27... Qe2 28. Bxd4 Qe4 29. Qd3! Bxf5 30. Qxe4 Bxe4 31. Kd2) 28. Rxe5 Rc8+ 29. Kd2 {, and White holds on, keeping chances to survive.}) 20. Kxd2 {[%mdl 4096]} c4 {[%cal Ye8e6,Ye6b6]} ({After} 20... cxd4 21. exd4 Bxg2 22. Rhe1 {M.Notkin} Be4 23. Rac1 {White activates his rooks and creates sufficient counterplay; of course, the exchange of all rooks will lead to a dead draw.}) 21. f3! {White must urgently create counterplay; otherwise Black's queenside pawn advantage will decide the game.} gxf3 22. gxf3 Bg6 (22... Bxf3?! 23. Rhf1 $11) 23. h4 {[%cal Yh4h5]} Bh5 24. Ke2 Re6 25. Be1 Kf7 26. Kf2 Rae8 27. Bd2 Rb6 28. Bc3 Rb3 {[%cal Yb7b5,Ya7a5,Yb5b4] Black's plan is double-edged as his rook can find itself out of play.} 29. a4! {Preventing 29...b5} b6 {[%cal Ya7a6,Yb6b5]} 30. Rhe1 {[%cal Ye3e4]} Bg6 (30... a6 31. e4 b5 32. axb5 axb5 33. Ra7+ Re7 34. Ra3 Rxa3 35. bxa3 $11) 31. e4! {Still!} dxe4 32. d5 a6? (32... exf3 $142 33. Rxe8 Kxe8 34. Kxf3 $11) 33. fxe4? {Missing an excellent chance:} (33. h5 $142 ! Bxh5 ({or} 33... Bf5 34. Re3! {[%cal Ya1e1,Yf3e4]}) 34. Rxe4 Rxe4 35. fxe4 {[%csl Rb3] and the initiative passes to White - see annotation to move 28!}) 33... Bxe4 34. d6 f5 35. Rg1 Ke6 36. Rg7 Kxd6 37. Rxh7 {White's passed h5 pawn should guarantee him a draw.} Bd3 38. Kf3 Re4 39. h5 Rh4 40. Rg1 Be4+ 41. Ke3 Rh3+ 42. Kf4 Rf3+ 43. Kg5 b5 44. axb5 axb5 45. Kf6 (45. Ra1!? Rfxc3 46. bxc3 Rxc3 {doesn't change much.}) 45... Rbxc3 46. bxc3 Rxc3 47. Rg8 Rh3 48. Rc8 c3 49. Rhc7 (49. Rhh8!? Bc6 50. Rh6! {[%cal Rf6f5] was simpler, with a forced draw::} Kd7 51. Rhh8 Kd6 52. Rh6 {as} Bd7? {doesn't work due to} 53. Kf7+ Ke5 54. Rc5+ Kd4 55. Rc7 {, and White wins a piece (M.Notkin).}) 49... c2 50. Kg5 Rh2 (50... Rh1 51. Rxc2 Bxc2 52. Rxc2 $11) 51. Rc3?! (51. Rc5! b4 52. Rc4 b3 53. Kf6 Kd5 54. R8c5+ Kd6 55. Rc8 $11 {M.Notkin}) 51... Ke6 (51... Ke5 $142 {but White apparently has a draw anyway. In many variations he achieves the endgame  vs. +:} 52. R3c5+ Kd4 53. Rxb5 Rg2+ (53... Bd3 54. Rb4+ Ke3 55. Rb3 Rg2+ 56. Kf6 f4 {is just a transposition of moves}) 54. Kf6 (54. Kf4?? Rg4#) 54... f4 55. Rb4+ Ke3 56. Rb3+ Bd3 (56... Kd2 $143 57. Ke5) 57. Rcc3 Rd2 (57... Ke2 58. Rb2 Kd2 59. Rbxc2+ Bxc2 60. Rf3 Rg4 61. Ke5 $11) 58. h6 Ke2! (58... f3 59. h7 f2 60. h8=Q f1=Q+ 61. Ke7 $11) 59. Kg5! f3 60. h7 f2 (60... Bxh7 61. Re3+ Kd1 62. Rxf3! $11) 61. h8=Q f1=Q 62. Qe5+ Kd1 63. Rxc2!! {Parrying mating threats!} Qg2+ 64. Kh4! Qh1+ (64... Qf2+ 65. Qg3!? {forcing the exchange of queens}) 65. Kg5! Qg2+ 66. Kh4 Bxc2 67. Qa1+ Ke2 68. Qe5+ Be4 (68... Kf1 69. Qf4+ Kg1 70. Rg3) 69. Rg3 $11) 52. Re8+ Kf7 53. Rb8 Rg2+ (53... Rh1 54. Rc7+ Ke6 55. Rb6+ Kd5 56. Rxb5+ Kd4 57. Rbc5 $11 {M.Notkin}) 54. Kf4 Ke6 55. Re8+ Kd7 56. Rb8 Ke6 (56... Rf2+!? {is very hard to assess but apparently White holds his ground after} 57. Kg5!? (57. Ke5 $143 Rf3 58. Rc5 Re3 59. Kf4 Re2) 57... Rf3 58. Rc5 Rf1 59. Rbc8 {etc.}) 57. Re8+ Kf6 58. Rf8+ Ke6 1/2-1/2

