{[%evp 0,50,30,6,40,31,42,32,15,-17,40,52,52,47,47,47,52,13,9,-4,124,3,47,32,89,38,48,4,3,7,77,7,7,0,71,15,117,30,30,-79,-30,0,-68,-59,-63,-63,-54,-118,-49,-184,-141,-570,-644]} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 h6 5. Nc3 {Not a rare option, but much less popular than 5.0-0 and 5.c3.} Bb4 6. O-O Bxc3 7. bxc3 O-O 8. Bb3 d6 (8... d5 {right away was Dubov's choice in his next game against the same Magnus Carlsen!}) 9. Nd2 $146 ({The only existing game went} 9. Re1 Re8 10. h3 Na5 11. Nh2 d5 12. Bxd5 Nxd5 13. exd5 Qxd5 14. Qh5 Nc6 {[½-½ (47) Seul,G (2436)-Fridman,D (2539) Essen 2000] and White would have had a small pressure had he played} 15. Re3!? $36 {; obviously, there were many options for Black to deviate from that game if he wants to.}) 9... d5 {Dubov surely considered this move to be better with no white knight putting pressure on e5.} (9... Be6!? {or 9...Bg4!? were possible as well.}) 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Ne4 Na5 12. Bxd5!? {Magnus decides to go for forcing tactics!} ({The natural} 12. Re1 {was offering White a small advantage according to the engine.}) 12... Qxd5 13. Bxh6 f5 $8 14. c4 Qc6 (14... Nxc4 15. Nc3 Qa5 16. dxc4 Qxc3 17. Qd5+ {looks dangerous for Black, even if the engine sort of holds the position.}) 15. Bd2 fxe4 16. Bxa5 Qg6 17. Qe2 (17. f3! $14) 17... Rf4 18. Bd2? {A very weird decision, missing the most obvious possible move!} (18. f3! {and White was still better.}) 18... Bg4 19. f3 $8 {Otherwise ...f3 would have led to a quick mate.} Bxf3 20. Qf2 Rg4 21. g3 Rf8 22. Rae1 Rf6! 23. dxe4 Qh5! {[%csl Rg3]} 24. Re3 Bxe4 25. Qe2? {A huge blunder in a bad position.} ({After} 25. Qe1 {White was much worse, not to say lost, but the game wasn't over.}) 25... Rxg3+! 0-1

