{[%evp 0,49,58,35,26,-11,3,4,22,16,7,11,18,13,32,24,25,31,20,18,29,29,34,36,52,44,24,28,59,59,59,52,66,69,69,26,20,36,44,11,20,21,58,50,49,44,357,381,1149,474,494,336]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Bb7 5. Bg2 Bb4+ 6. Bd2 c5 7. Bxb4 cxb4 8. O-O O-O 9. Nbd2 a5?! ({Black usually starts with d6 in order not to allow White to push his e-pawn to e5:} 9... d6 10. Re1 Nbd7 11. e4 e5 $132) 10. Re1 d6 11. e4 Nfd7 (11... Nbd7 12. e5 $14) 12. Qe2!? {A natural move, and also the engine's sugestion. Several other games went 12.c2.} (12. a3 Na6 {doesn't bring White anything and justifies Black having kept the knight on b8.}) 12... e5 (12... Na6 13. e5 dxe5 14. Nxe5 Bxg2 15. Kxg2 Nxe5 16. Qxe5 $14 {½-½ (17) Lemos,D (2538)-Shirov,A (2698) Buenos Aires ARG 2012}) 13. Rad1 Nc6 14. Nf1! {White won a precious tempo in the complications and is already clearly better.} Qf6 (14... Qc7 15. Ne3 $16 {1-0 (26) Karavade,E (2386)-Zherebukh,Y (2541) Coubertin 2009}) 15. Ne3 (15. dxe5!? Ndxe5 16. Nh4 {with an overwhelming advantage.}) 15... Nxd4 16. Nxd4 exd4 17. e5 (17. Nf5 {was very good as well, but the games move is a safe path to a clear advantage.}) 17... Qxe5 18. Bxb7 Ra7 19. Qg4 Nf6 20. Qxd4 Rxb7 21. Qxd6 Qxb2 (21... Qxd6 22. Rxd6 {would lead to a very bad and passive endgame that Magnus didn't seem to want, for good reason!}) 22. Rd2 Qc3? {And a serious blunder that loses the game, but Black was in huge trouble anyway.} (22... Qa3 23. Ng4! {and it becmes difficult to find moves for Black! Look at the queen on a3!}) 23. Nd1! {The double threat xc3 and xf8+ wins the game.} Re8 24. Rxe8+ Nxe8 25. Nxc3 1-0

