1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 g6 5. Nf3 Bg7 6. Bd3 O-O 7. O-O Bg4 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 e6 10. Rd1 Nbd7 11. Bd2 Re8 12. Rac1 {This system was examined in CBM 91, annotations to Hebden - Meenakshi by GM L.Ftacnik.} (12. Be1 {is also played.}) 12... Rc8 (12... Qe7 13. Qe2 (13. cxd5 exd5 14. Qg3 Rac8 15. Be1 Nf8 16. b4 Ne6 17. Na4 b6 18. Qf3 Qd7 19. Nc3 b5 20. Nb1 Qd6 $11 {1-0 Veresov,G-Ragozin,V/Moscow 1944/URS-ch (57)}) (13. Qg3 dxc4 14. Bxc4 e5 15. Bb3 Rad8 16. Qh4 Nb6 17. dxe5 Qxe5 18. Qb4 Bf8 19. Qf4 Qxf4 20. exf4 Nfd5 $11 {1/2 Kuzubov,Y (2582)-Zugic,I (2477)/Calvia 2007/CBM 120 ext (56)}) 13... dxc4 (13... Rad8 14. Bb1 (14. Be1 a6 15. a3 e5 16. dxe5 Nxe5 17. cxd5 Nxd3 18. Rxd3 Nxd5 19. Nxd5 Rxd5 20. Rxd5 cxd5 21. Qc2 d4 {½-½ Simagin,V-Ignatiev,F/Moscow 1962/ (29)}) 14... Nf8 15. Be1 Rd7 16. a3 dxc4 17. Qxc4 Nd5 18. Qf1 Bh6 19. Rd3 f5 20. Rcd1 Red8 21. Bd2 Rc7 22. Qe2 {1-0 Lisitsin,G-Ragozin,V/Moscow 1948/ (57)}) 14. Bxc4 e5 15. d5 cxd5 16. Bxd5 e4?! (16... Nb6) 17. Bxb7 Rab8 18. Bc6?! (18. Nd5 $14) 18... Rxb2 $11 {1/2 Hebden,M (2534)-Meenakshi,S (2266)/Torquay 2002/CBM 091/[Ftacnik] (43)}) (12... dxc4 13. Bxc4 e5 14. Bb3 exd4 15. exd4 Nb6 16. Bg5 Qd6 17. Bf4 Qd7 18. Be5 $14 {1-0 Ivanov,S (2528)-Hasangatin,R (2458)/St Petersburg 1999/CBM 071/[Ftacnik] (44)}) (12... a6 13. Qe2?! (13. Be1) 13... e5 14. dxe5 Nxe5 15. cxd5 Nxd5 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Bb1 Qb5 18. Qxb5 axb5 19. b3 Red8 20. Bb4 Bf8 21. Bc3 Bg7 22. Bb4 Bf8 23. Bc3 Bg7 24. Bb4 {1/2 Lushenkov,M (2368)-Chuprikov,D (2377)/Sochi 2006/EXT 2007}) 13. Be1 Qe7 14. Qe2 a6! {[%mdl 32]} (14... e5 {is a typical advance in such positions but now it was not so good due to} 15. dxe5 Nxe5 (15... Qxe5 16. cxd5 cxd5 (16... Nxd5 17. Nxd5 cxd5 18. Bb5) 17. Bb5 $14) 16. cxd5 Nxd5 (16... Nxd3 17. Rxd3 cxd5 18. Nxd5 Nxd5 19. Rxc8 Rxc8 20. Rxd5) (16... cxd5 17. Bc2! {[%csl Rd5][%cal Yc2b3,Ye2b5]}) 17. Nxd5 cxd5 18. Bb5 Rxc1 19. Rxc1 $14) 15. b3 Rcd8 {Black decides to wait.} (15... e5!? {was now quite appropriate:} 16. dxe5 Nxe5 17. cxd5 cxd5 $11 {Black's acive pieces compensate for his isolated pawn.}) 16. Qc2 Rc8 17. Bf1 Bf8 18. Qb1 Rcd8 19. g3 {The transfer of this bishop to g2 (to control the d5 square and therefore prevent ...e6-e5) is quite typical in this structure.} Bg7 20. Bg2 h5 21. Ne2 h4?! {It was probably better to continue waiting:} (21... Bh6) 22. g4 e5 23. Ba5! Rc8 24. dxe5 Nxe5 25. Bc3 (25. cxd5! Nxd5 (25... cxd5 26. Nf4) 26. e4 Nc7 27. f4 $14 {[%csl Ge4,Gf4,Gg4]}) 25... dxc4?! (25... Red8! 26. Ba5! Re8 27. cxd5) 26. bxc4 Qc7 ({After} 26... b5 27. cxb5 cxb5 28. Bb4 {the position opens up and White's bishop pair become very strong but the text move allows him to fix Black's weakness on b7.}) 27. c5 $16 {[%csl Rb7,Gc5]} Rcd8? {Loses material without enough counterplay.} (27... Nd5 28. Bd4 $16) 28. Rxd8 Rxd8 29. Bxe5 Qxe5 30. Qxb7 Rd2 31. Nd4 Ne4 32. Nxc6 Qb2 33. Ne7+ Kf8 (33... Kh7 34. Qxb2 Bxb2 35. Bxe4 Bxc1 36. c6 $18) 34. Qa8+ Kxe7 35. Qxe4+ Qe5 36. c6 Rd8 37. c7 Rc8 38. Qb7 Qe6 39. Qxc8 (39. Qxc8 Qxc8 40. Bb7 $18) 1-0

