1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 e6 5. Nf3 Nbd7 6. Qc2 Bd6 7. g4 Nxg4 8. Rg1 Qf6 9. Rxg4 Qxf3 10. Rxg7 Nf6 11. h3 {This move (keeping the h2 pawn and preventing ...f6-g4) was played by Aronian against Morozevich (see CBM 117, with GM M.Marin's annotations). Below you will see other practical examples.} (11. Rg5 {is the main theoretical line - see my survey in CBM 127.}) 11... Qf5?! $146 {Hardly a good novelty. In the endgame White keeps a small but clear edge due to his better pawn structure.} (11... e5?! 12. dxe5 Bxe5 13. Rg5! {[%cal Rg5e5,Rf1g2]} Nd7 14. cxd5 cxd5 15. Rf5 (15. Bd2 d4 16. Bg2 Qf6 17. exd4 Bxd4 18. O-O-O $16 {1-0 Chytilek,R (2320)-Franta,J/Litomysl 1996/EXT 1998 (25)}) 15... Qh1 16. Bd2 d4 17. exd4 Bxd4 18. O-O-O O-O 19. Bh6 Re8 20. Rg5+ Kh8 21. Rxd4 Qxf1+ 22. Rd1 Re1 23. Bg7+ Kg8 24. Be5+ {1-0 Tisdall,J (2492)-Sidselrud,S (2150)/Norway 2002/EXT 2003}) (11... h6!? {, preventing g7-g5, looks quite good for Black:} 12. Bd2 (12. Qe2!? Qxe2+ (12... Qf5!?) 13. Bxe2 Kf8 14. Rg1 dxc4 15. e4 Bb4 16. f3 b5 17. Kf2 a6 18. Bf4 Bb7 19. Rac1 Nd7 20. b3 cxb3 21. axb3 $44 {1-0 Le Quang,L (2542)-Neelotpal,D (2485)/Cebu City 2007/CBM 120 ext (38)}) 12... e5 (12... Bd7 13. Qb3 Ke7 14. c5 Bc7 15. Qxb7 $14 {1-0 Le Quang,L (2583)-Garnelis,J (2188)/Vung Tau 2008/EXT 2009 (37)}) 13. Rg3 (13. cxd5 exd4 14. exd4 Kf8 15. Rg1 Bf5 16. Qb3 Re8+ 17. Be3 Rg8 $132 {1/2 Siebrecht,S (2451)-Haba,P (2546)/Kallithea 2008/CBM 127 (37)}) 13... Qh5 14. cxd5 exd4 15. Ne4 Nxe4 16. Qxe4+ Qe5 17. Qxe5+ Bxe5 18. Rf3 cxd5 19. Bb5+ Ke7 20. exd4 Bd6 21. Kf1 Be6 $11 {1/2 Aronian,L (2744)-Morozevich,A (2741)/Morelia/Linares 2007/CBM 117/[Marin,M] (29)}) (11... Kf8!? 12. Rg5 (12. Rg1 $143 Rg8 13. Rxg8+ Kxg8 14. Bd2 dxc4 15. Bxc4 e5 16. dxe5 Bxe5 17. Bf1 Bf5 $17 {0-1 Splichal,S (2167)-Michenka,J (2351)/Karvina 2003/EXT 2005 (41)}) 12... Qh1 {needs testing}) 12. Qxf5 exf5 13. cxd5 cxd5 (13... Nxd5!? 14. Nxd5 cxd5 15. Bd2 $14) 14. Nb5 Bb4+ 15. Bd2 Bxd2+ 16. Kxd2 Ke7 17. Bd3 Be6 (17... Bd7!?) 18. Nc7!? {A good idea - to exchange the theoretically "bad" e6 bishop, in fact, the main defender of the black pawns, using the opportunity that Black can't recapture with his f7 pawn.} Rag8 19. Nxe6 Kxe6 20. Rxg8 Nxg8?! {[%mdl 4096] Too "subtle".} ({Any beginner would play the simple} 20... Rxg8 {, and he would be right:} 21. Rc1 Rg2 22. Ke2 Rh2 23. Kf3! (23. Rc7 Rxh3 24. Rxb7 Rh2 {[%cal Rf6g4]}) 23... Rxh3+ 24. Kg2 Rh5 25. Rc7 Rg5+ 26. Kf3 Ng4 {[%cal Rg4h2] - Black's counterplay must be strong enough to survive.}) 21. Ke2 (21. Rc1 Ne7 22. Rc7 Rb8 {[%cal Ye6d6]}) 21... Ne7 22. Kf3! (22. Rc1 Rc8 23. Rxc8 Nxc8 24. Kf3 {was probably insufficient for the victory. GM M.Carlsen decides to keep rooks on the board.}) 22... Rc8 23. a4! Rc7 24. a5 h6 25. h4 Kf6 26. h5 Nc8 27. Kf4 Nd6 28. Rg1 Rc8 29. f3 Ke6 {Black is completely passive.} 30. Rg7 Rh8 31. Bc2! {The "two weaknesses", attacked by the stronger side, should be as far from each other as possible, then it is more difficult for the defender to hold both; therefore - h6 and d5, rather than f5.} Rc8 32. Bb3 Rh8 33. Rg1 Rc8 34. Rg7 Rh8 35. Rg2 Rc8 36. Rg1 $22 {The black knight must prevent e3-e4, the rook can't leave the 8th rank in view of g8-h8, and if it leaves the c-file, White plays c1 threatening c5 and c7; finally, pawn moves create new weaknesses.} Ne8! {The lesser evil.} (36... Rb8 37. Rc1 Rc8 38. Rc5! Rxc5 39. dxc5 Nc4 40. e4 fxe4 41. Bxc4 dxc4 42. Kxe4 $18) (36... Nc4 37. Bxc4 dxc4 (37... Rxc4 38. Rg7) 38. Ra1 {[%cal Ya1a4,Ya4b4]}) (36... b6 37. axb6 axb6 38. Ra1 $16) 37. e4 fxe4 38. fxe4 Nf6 39. e5! Ne4! (39... Nxh5+ 40. Ke3 {[%cal Rg1h1]}) ({The endgame after} 39... Rg8 40. Rxg8 Nxg8 {is very difficult for Black:} 41. Bd1 Ne7 42. Bg4+ f5 43. Bh3 $22 b6 (43... Nc6 44. Bxf5+ Ke7 45. Bc8 Nxa5 46. Kf5) 44. a6 b5 45. Bf1 b4 (45... Nc6 46. Bxb5 Nxd4 47. Bd3 $22 $16) 46. Bd3 Nc6 47. Bxf5+ Ke7 48. Ke3 {and White should gradually win.}) 40. Ke3 b6?! {Black unnecessarily opens up the a-file for his opponent. "Don't move pawns on the side where you are weaker!"} (40... Ng5 $142 $14 41. Rf1 $140 Ne4) 41. axb6 axb6 42. Kd3 (42. Bd1!? Rc4 (42... Ra8 43. Bc2 Ng5 44. Rf1) 43. Bg4+! (43. Rg8 Rb4 $132) 43... f5 (43... Ke7 44. Bf3 Rb4 45. Bxe4 dxe4 46. Rg2 $16) 44. exf6+ Kxf6 45. Bf3 {[%cal Rg1g6]} Kf5 46. Bxe4+ dxe4 47. Rf1+ Ke6 48. Kxe4 Rc2 49. d5+ Ke7 50. b4 {with a promising rook ending.}) 42... Nf2+ 43. Ke2 Ne4 44. Ke3 f6?! (44... Ng5!? {was still possible although White could now invade along the a-file:} 45. Ra1 f6! 46. exf6 Ne4 47. f7! Rf8 48. Ra6 Rxf7 49. Rxb6+ Nd6 {with good drawing chances (S.Zagrebelny).}) 45. Rg6 Rc1 46. Rxh6? (46. exf6! Re1+ 47. Kd3 Nxf6 48. Bxd5+ Kf5 (48... Kxd5 49. Rxf6 b5 50. Rb6 $18) 49. Bf3 {(I.Ganiev)} Rb1 50. Kc3 (50. Kc2 $143 Rf1 51. Be2 Rf2) 50... Re1 51. Kc4 {and White should gradually win}) 46... Rh1 47. Bc2 Rh3+? {Obvious but wrong!} (47... Re1+! 48. Kd3 (48. Kf4 Rf1+ 49. Kg4 Rg1+) 48... Rh1 {and White can't make any progress (S.Zagrebelny).}) 48. Kf4! Rh4+ 49. Kf3 {When a rook and a knight are jumping around your king, you must sometimes display real acrobacy!} Nd2+ 50. Ke2? {This move seriously complicates White's task.} (50. Kg3 $142 ! Rxd4 51. Rxf6+ Kxe5 52. Rf5+ Ke6 53. h6 Rc4 (53... Ne4+ 54. Bxe4 dxe4 55. h7 Rd8 56. Rh5 Rh8 57. Kf4 $18) 54. h7 Rxc2 55. Re5+! (55. Rf8 $143 Ne4+ 56. Kg4 Rh2 57. h8=Q Rxh8 58. Rxh8 Kd6) (55. h8=Q $143 Ne4+! 56. Kf4 Rf2+ 57. Ke3 Kxf5) 55... Kxe5 56. h8=Q+ Kd6 57. Qf8+ Kc7 58. Qf7+ Kb8 59. Qxd5 Nf1+ 60. Kh4 {- with his pieces scattered all over the board, Black can't organize successful defence.}) 50... Rh2+ 51. Kd1 Nc4 52. Rxf6+ Ke7 53. Bg6 Rd2+ 54. Kc1 (54. Ke1!? Rxd4 55. h6 Rh4 (55... Nxe5 $143 56. Rxb6 Rh4 (56... Nxg6 57. Rxg6 Rh4 58. Rb6) 57. Bf5) 56. Bf5 d4 57. Re6+ (57. Rc6 d3!) 57... Kf7 58. b3 Ne3 59. Rf6+ Ke7 60. Bc8 Rh2 61. Rd6 $16) 54... Rxd4 55. b3! (55. h6 Rh4 56. Bf5 {is now impossible due to} Rh1+ 57. Kc2 Ne3+ 58. Kd3 Nxf5 59. Rxf5 Rxh6 $11) 55... Nxe5 56. Rxb6 Rh4 ({The rook endgame after} 56... Nxg6 57. hxg6 {should be winning for White.}) 57. Bf5! Nf3? (57... Rh1+ 58. Kc2 (58. Kd2 Nc4+!! 59. bxc4 Rxh5 $11 {S.Zagrebelny}) 58... Nf3 {[%cal Rf3d4]} 59. Bg4 ({or} 59. Rb7+ Kf6 60. Bg6 Ne5) 59... Rh2+ 60. Kc3 Ng5 {and White can hardly win.}) 58. h6! {The pawn finally moves forward!} Nd4 59. h7! Nxf5 60. Rb8 Nd4 61. Kb2! Kd6 62. h8=Q Rxh8 63. Rxh8 {This is a technically winning endgame but some work still has to be done.} Kc5 64. Rh5 Nc6 65. Rh4 Nb4 66. Ka3 {White must disorganise Black's defence. First, he forces the advance of the black d-pawn, then he must try to push his own pawn at an appropriate moment.} d4 (66... Nc2+ 67. Ka4 d4 68. Rh5+) 67. Rh5+ Nd5 68. Kb2 Kc6 69. Ka3 Kc5 70. Rh4 Nb4 71. Rh8 Nc6? {This makes White's task much easier.} (71... Nd5 72. Rd8! $18 {[%cal Ya3b2]}) (71... Nc2+ $142 72. Kb2 Nb4 73. Rc8+ Nc6 ({or} 73... Kd5 74. Ka3) 74. Ka3 Kd5 {It is still difficult for White to reach victory; e.g.} 75. b4? ({first he must play} 75. Rc7 Kd6 76. Rh7 {etc.}) {is premature and leads to a draw after} 75... d3 76. Rh8 d2 77. Rh1 Kc4) 72. Rh5+ Kd6 73. b4 {Now, with the black king cut off, everything is over.} d3 (73... Ne5 74. Kb3 Kd5 75. b5 $18) 74. Rh3 Ne5 75. Kb3 d2 76. Kc2 Nc6 77. Rh4 1-0

