{The 2016 and 2018 WC-match, and the 2017&18 blitz all would look average from a player of Magnus caliber, was it not for the final day. Each time at a very critical situation, the champion delivered the performance needed with a total score of the overwhelming 21½/25 equalling 86 %! Next in line was Wang Hao:} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 g6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 5. d3 Bg7 6. h3 {As in game 1 of the recent WC-match, Wang plays the most common move, thus not trying to follow Caruana's thought of improving with 6.0-0 in order to avoid Black's upcoming knight manoeuvre.} Nf6 7. Nc3 Nd7 8. Be3 e5 9. Qd2 {Caruana omitted this typical inclusion, but Wang follows the main line.} h6 10. O-O b6!? ({Much less common than the standard} 10... Qe7 {but similar to game 1 from London.}) 11. a3 Nf8 12. b4 Ne6 13. bxc5 f5 {Due to the inclusion of Qd2/h6 Black now threatens ...f4 winning a piece. His play is very ambitious, having the pair of bishops, trying to seize space in the centre and on the kingside. The price is a serious lack in development and risks of a quick white counterattack.} 14. exf5 gxf5 15. Qe1 ({The computer suggests ideas like} 15. Rae1 f4 16. Nxe5 {, assessing the position as equal.}) 15... O-O 16. Rb1 e4!? {A good idea, but with a tactical issue.} (16... bxc5 {simply regaining the pawn while keeping Black's trumps was strong, but somewhat unimaginative.}) 17. dxe4 f4 18. Rd1? {Logical, but wrong.} ({Actually the rook served a much more inportant function on b1, as} 18. cxb6! {was possible due to} fxe3 19. b7! {regaining the piece with an edge. Black can play 18...Rf7!? still with a messy position, and decent chances especially with this being a blitz game, but objectively White would be better.}) (18. Bc1 Ng5 19. Qe2!? {also looks interesting exploiting the fact that 20.Qc4+ is possible after 19...Bxc3, but the computers suggests the interesting} Nxh3+ (19... Bxc3 20. Qc4+) 20. gxh3 Qd7 {with a strong attack!}) 18... Qe7 19. Bd4 Nxd4 20. Nxd4 Ba6 {Both players holds big trumps. Black has sacrificed 2 pawns, but has the pair of bishops, and aims at winning the exchange on f1. White needs to consolidate, also, the f5-square looks inviting for his knight. Wang, however, sees a tactical possibility, but missed Black's reply to it!} 21. Nxc6? Qxc5 22. Nd5 Rae8! {By controlling the e7 square, Black threatens the knight on c6 as its no longer indirectly defended due to the fork on e7, meaning it has to retreat when Black safely can take the exchange on f1.} ({White's idea was} 22... Bxf1 23. Nde7+ Kh7 24. e5! {when suddenly the b1-h7 diagonal gives him a huge attack as} Qxc2 {can be replied to by} 25. Rd2!) 23. Ncb4 Bxf1 24. Kxf1 Qc4+ {To make things even worse for White, not only did 22...Rae8! defend, it also attacked the e4 pawn.} 25. Qe2 Rxe4 26. Qxc4 Rxc4 {Black has an exchange and a easily winning ending:} 27. Ke2 a5 28. Nd3 Rxc2+ 29. Kf3 Ra2 30. Nxb6 Rxa3 31. Nc4 Rc3 32. Nxa5 Rd8 33. Ke2 Rd5 34. Nb7 Bf8 35. Rd2 Rb3 36. Nbc5 Bxc5 0-1

