{[%evp 0,92,24,26,26,12,70,32,46,15,47,16,63,29,56,34,41,22,73,1,28,7,22,21,21,32,32,30,28,24,17,-79,-65,-60,-64,-72,-15,-20,5,0,7,-10,-6,-11,2,-60,45,31,141,15,8,-13,-35,-85,-121,-179,86,-199,-205,-280,-277,-279,-291,-312,-196,-157,-90,-108,-108,-120,-120,-120,-93,-172,-159,-153,-138,-153,-62,-143,-123,-180,-192,-149,-130,-168,-182,-209,-295,-469,-469,-655,-749,-749,-749]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 c6 3. h3!? {A funny way to prepare Bf4, as the move h3 is useful against future ...Nh5 ideas. By delaying Bf4, White also delays a possible ...Qb6 to attack the b2-pawn.} d6 4. Bf4 Nbd7 ({In the event of} 4... Qb6 {White could sac a pawn with} 5. Nbd2 {and e2-e4. Note a logical answer if the pawn was on d7 would be ...d5, but to play ...d6 and then ...d5 makes little sense. Another advantage of 3.h3!}) 5. e3 g6 6. Be2 Bg7 7. O-O O-O 8. c4 ({Older games went} 8. Nbd2 {- definitely more efficient against 8...Ne4, but also less aggressive!}) 8... Ne4 9. Qc2 e5! $146 {An excellent novelty!} (9... f5 {was always played so far, but Black almost always ended up having a "bad Dutch" position.}) 10. Bh2 ({Black would be happy to sac a pawn with} 10. Qxe4?! Nf6 11. Qc2 exf4 12. exf4 Nh5 $36) (10. dxe5 dxe5 11. Qxe4? exf4 {is even worse as b2 is hanging.}) 10... Ng5 11. Nbd2 Nxf3+ 12. Nxf3 Re8 {With the move 9...e5 Black managed not to weaken anything.} 13. Rfd1 Qe7 14. Rac1 exd4 15. exd4!? {Difficult to say if Magnus missed Dubov's next move winning an exchange to deliverately sacced it - although knowing Magnus, option 2 is a bit more likely!} (15. Nxd4 {would be safe but Black would be fine after} Be5! {neutralizing the h2-bishop.} (15... a5 16. Nf3 Ne5 {is possible as well.})) 15... Bh6 (15... Qxe2? 16. Re1 $18) 16. Bd3 Bxc1 17. Qxc1 Qf6 {According to the engine, this move is unprecise. Later, f6 is rather a square for the f7-pawn to consolidate the dark squares.} (17... Nb6!? {with the idea of ...Be6 made a lot of sense. White has compensation, but he isn't better!}) 18. Bf4! Nf8 (18... d5 {- preventing White's 20th move - was better, it seems. It isn't a logical move against Bf4 systems, but somehow the last moves changed the nature of the position and now White's bishop is trying to change his diagonal. After 19.Ne5 or 19.Nh2 White has compensation for equality.}) 19. Bg5 Qh8 20. d5! {With this move, White paralyzes a bit Black's pieces, and his initiative starts to be good enough to play for an advantage. The main explication is that is gains space, and that the d6-pawn will remain a weakness.} cxd5 21. cxd5 Nd7 22. Bb5 Qg7 ({Black probably didn't play} 22... a6 {due to} 23. Bxd7 Bxd7 24. Qf4 {threatening Bf6 and Qxd6; however, here Black can equalize in an elegant maneer:} f6! 25. Bxf6 Rf8 26. Qxd6 Qxf6 27. Qxd7 Rf7 $13) 23. Bh6 Qf6 24. Rd4? {Magnus is right to play for a win, but he chose the wrong move.} (24. Qc7! {was pausing great problems:} Qd8 (24... Qe7 25. Qc3! f6 26. Re1 $18) 25. Qxd6 (25. Qc3!? f6 26. Nd4 {is pretty annoying as well}) 25... Ne5! (25... Nf6 26. Qb4 Bd7 27. Bxd7 Nxd7 28. d6 $16) 26. Qb4!? Nxf3+ 27. gxf3 Re5 28. Qd4 f6 29. Bf4 $16) 24... a6 25. Rf4!? ({Again, Magnus refuses to make a draw with} 25. Bg5 Qg7 26. Bh6 {and perpetual check... on the queen!}) 25... Qe7 26. Bxd7 Bxd7 27. Bg5 Rac8! (27... Qe2 28. Qc7 {should be problematic for Black.}) 28. Qa1? (28. Qxc8 {would lead to a dry equality. Thus Magnus found another way to continue the game - but this time took a wrong decision.}) 28... Qe2 29. b3 Rc2 30. Bh4 Rb2 ({There was nothing wrong with} 30... Rxa2 {as} 31. Qf6 {is answered with} Qd1+ 32. Kh2 Qxd5 $19) 31. Kh2 {A fun way to paralyze the White queen.} Qc2 (31... h6 {was good as well.}) 32. Rc4 Qb1 {Dubov is right about that - without queens on the board, he's definitely better.} 33. Qxb1 Rxb1 34. Rc7 Bf5 35. Bf6 h6 36. Nd2 (36. Rxb7 Be4 37. Nd4 Bxd5 $17) 36... Rd1 37. Nc4 Rxd5 38. Rxb7 Be6 39. Ra7 Rd1 40. Nb2? (40. Rxa6 {was necessarg to keep some chances.}) 40... Rd2 41. Kg3 g5! {Now it's all over. White became passve the the black king has nothing to fear any more.} 42. Rxa6 Rc8 43. h4 gxh4+ 44. Kf3 Rcc2 45. Na4 Rxf2+ 46. Ke3 Rxf6 0-1

