1. Nf3 b6!? {After 1.d4 g6 and 1.e4 c6 in the previous rounds, Carlsen goes for another non-standard opening.} (1... b5 2. e4 Bb7 3. Bxb5!? Bxe4 4. O-O Nf6 5. d4 e6 6. c4 Be7 7. Nc3 Bb7 8. d5 O-O 9. Bf4 Na6 10. Re1 Qc8 11. Nd4 Bb4 12. Bg5 Ne8 13. Re3 (13. Qa4! $16) 13... c6 (13... e5!? $14) 14. Bxa6 Bxa6 15. Qh5 f6 16. Rh3 fxg5 17. Qxh7+ Kf7 18. Qh5+ Kg8 19. Qh7+ Kf7 20. Ne4 Bxc4 21. Qh5+ Ke7 22. Qxg5+ Kf7 23. Qh5+ Ke7 24. Qg5+ Kf7 25. dxe6+ dxe6 26. Nf3 Kg8 27. Qh4 Rxf3 28. Qh7+ {1-0, Kramnik,V (2791)-Carlsen,M (2823) Moscow rpd 2011 This is certainly a game Carlsen isn't willing to cherish - check the notes by Marin in CBM 144. Therefore this time he chooses a more modest version of the queenside fianchetto.}) 2. e4 {The most principled reaction, which attempts to profit from Black's chosen move order.} (2. d4 Bb7 3. c4 e6 {might transpose into a Queen's Indian, while}) (2. c4 Bb7 3. Nc3 Nf6 {could still lead to a Hedgehog English as well.}) ({Surprisingly, the most often played move in this position is} 2. g3 {, which hardly attempts to refute Black's setup:} Bb7 3. Bg2 {After} e6 ({An interesting independent option is} 3... e5!? 4. c4 (4. d3 g6 {can be similar.}) 4... g6 5. O-O (5. d4 exd4 6. Qxd4 Nf6 7. Nc3 Bg7 8. O-O Nc6 9. Qe3+ Qe7 10. Qxe7+ Kxe7 11. Bg5 Na5 12. Nd2 Bxg2 13. Kxg2 Ke6 14. Rac1 h6 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Nd5 Bxb2 17. Nxc7+ Kd6 18. Nxa8 Bxc1 19. c5+ Ke7 20. Rxc1 Rxa8 21. cxb6 axb6 22. Rc3 Nc6 $11 {Topalov,V (2801)-Aronian,L (2752) Monte Carlo rpd 2006}) 5... Bg7 6. Nc3 Ne7 7. e4 c5 8. a3 O-O 9. d3 h6 10. b4 d6 11. Rb1 Nd7 12. Nd5 Kh7 13. Nh4 Rb8 14. Bh3 Bc8 15. Ng2 Nf6 16. Bxc8 Rxc8 17. bxc5 bxc5 18. Nxe7 Qxe7 19. Qa4 Rc7 20. Qa6 Rfc8 21. Bd2 Rc6 22. Qa4 h5 23. Rb5 Bh6 $11 {Shchekachev,A (2555)-Degraeve,J (2551) Saint-Quentin 2015}) 4. O-O Nf6 {play can again transpose into the aforementioned openings, with an extra option being a King's Indian Attack setup} 5. d3!? d5 6. Nbd2 Be7 7. e4) 2... Bb7 3. Nc3 e6 4. d4 Bb4 ({Attemtpting a Hippo with} 4... g6 {allows the unpleasant} 5. Bg5!? Be7 6. Be3 d6 7. Bd3 Nd7 8. Qd2 a6 9. O-O-O b5 10. Kb1 Nb6 11. h4 Nf6 12. e5 Nfd5 13. Ne4 h5 14. Bg5 $16 {Kramnik,V (2801)-McShane,L (2706) Moscow blitz 2012}) (4... Nf6 5. e5 (5. Bd3 Bb4 {transposes to the game}) 5... Ne4 6. Nxe4 (6. Bd3 Bb4 {see below}) 6... Bxe4 7. Bg5 (7. Bd3!? Bxd3 8. Qxd3 {}) 7... Be7 8. Bxe7 Qxe7 9. Nd2 Bb7 10. Qg4 f5 11. Qh5+ g6 12. Qh6 Nc6 13. c3 O-O-O 14. f4 Rdg8 15. Nf3 Nd8 16. Ng5 Qg7 17. Qxg7 Rxg7 18. h4 Rhg8 19. Kf2 h6 20. Nf3 Nf7 21. Rh2 g5 22. hxg5 hxg5 $36 {Zezulkin,J (2530)-Stocek,J (2537) CZE 2001}) 5. Bd3 Nf6 (5... Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 d6 7. O-O Nd7 8. Re1 (8. Bg5 Ne7 (8... f6 9. Be3 Qe7 10. Nd2 Qf7 11. c4 Ne7 12. Nb3 O-O 13. f3 f5 14. Qe2 Qg6 15. Nd2 Nf6 16. exf5 exf5 17. Rae1 Rae8 18. Qd1 Qh5 19. c3 Ng6 20. Qc2 Bc8 21. Re2 Re7 22. Rfe1 Rfe8 23. Nf1 {Alekseev,E (2636)-Rozum,I (2590) Sochi blitz 2016} f4! $11) 9. Nd2 h6 10. Qh5 O-O 11. Bh4 Qe8 12. f4 f6 13. Qxe8 Raxe8 14. a4 a5 15. Bc4 d5 16. Bb5 Bc6 17. Bxc6 Nxc6 18. f5 exf5 19. exd5 Ne7 20. c4 f4! 21. Bf2 Nf5 $132 {/, Kramnik,V (2784)-Ivanchuk,V (2731) Tromsoe 2013}) 8... Ne7 9. a4 a5 10. e5! Bxf3?! (10... dxe5 11. Nxe5 Nxe5 12. Rxe5 $14) 11. Qxf3 dxe5 12. dxe5 Ng6 13. Ba3 c5 14. Rad1 Ra7 15. Bb5 Qc8 16. Qd3 Qc7 17. Qe4 Qc8 18. f4 O-O 19. Rd6 Ne7 20. Red1 Nd5 21. Qf3 Nb8 22. c4 $18 {Sergejev,R (2349)-Psakhis,L (2598) ICC blitz 2001}) (5... Ne7 6. O-O O-O 7. Ne2! f5 8. Ng3 fxe4 9. Nxe4 Nf5 10. c3 Bd6 11. Nxd6 cxd6 12. Re1 Na6 13. d5 (13. Ng5! $36) 13... Nc5 $132 14. c4 Qf6 15. Bc2 b5! $13 {Hansen,E (2573)-Antipov,M (2569) Berlin blitz 2015}) 6. Qe2 {The most natural move, but White has other alternatives.} (6. Nd2!? {is tricky, the  aims directly for g4 or h5. White should get at least a slight initiative:} O-O $142 (6... d5 7. e5 Nfd7 (7... Ne4 8. Qg4 Kf8 (8... Nxc3 9. Qxg7 Rf8 10. a3 $16) 9. Ncxe4!? (9. Bxe4 dxe4 10. Ncxe4 h5 11. Qf4 Bxd2+ 12. Nxd2 Nc6 (12... Bxg2 13. Rg1 Bb7 14. c3 Nc6 15. b3 Ne7 16. Ba3 Rg8 17. Rg5 g6 18. O-O-O $16 {Saint Amour