8 12. Rfe1 Qh5 13. Ne5 Qxe2 14. Rxe2 Nd5 15. Bd2 Nxe5 16. Rxe5 O-O 17. c4 (17. Rh5 h6 18. f3 Rfd8 19. Rh4 Ne7 20. Kf2 f6 21. Bc4 Kf7 22. Re1 Rd6 23. Kg1 Nf5 24. Rh5 g5 $15 {[%csl Rh5] bubble bridge,h (2501)-celestica,h (2528) Engine Room 2012}) 17... Nf6 18. f3 c5 $132) 10... Qh5 11. Bf4 {Continuing his development and retaining the tension.} ({The text move is more natural than} 11. Nd2 Qxe2 12. Bxe2 O-O 13. a4 e5 14. d5 c6 15. dxc6 Bxc6 16. a5 Nc5 17. Re1 Rfd8 18. Nb3 Nfd7 19. Nxc5 Nxc5 20. Be3 f6 21. Reb1 Rab8 22. axb6 axb6 23. Bf1 Rb7 24. Rb2 Kf7 25. Rab1 Rdb8 26. f3 g5 27. h3 h5 28. Be2 Ke6 $11 {goga2,h (2486)-donmez,h (2630) Engine Room 2012}) 11... Rc8 (11... c5 $143 12. d5 $36) 12. a4 (12. d5 O-O!? (12... Nc5 $132) 13. dxe6 Bxf3 14. exf7+ (14. gxf3 Nc5 15. e7 Rfe8 $15) 14... Rxf7 15. Qxf3 Qxf3 16. gxf3 Nh5 $11) ({Moradiabadi suggests} 12. Ne5!? {While White is doing OK in the endgame after} Qxe2 13. Bxe2 Nxe5 14. Bxe5 O-O {[%cal Yf8d8,Yc7c5]} 15. f3 ({, or} 15. a4!? $11 {/ , simplification has also helped Black and he can get his counterplay going.})) 12... a5 13. Rab1 $146 {[%mdl 8]} (13. Nd2 Qxe2 14. Bxe2 O-O 15. Rfe1 h6 16. f3 Rfd8 17. Be3 Nf8 18. c3 Ng6 19. Rab1 Ne7 20. Bf2 Ba6 21. Bd1 g6 22. Rb2 Re8 23. h4 Nh5 24. Bc2 Kg7 25. Kh2 Rcd8 26. g3 Nc6 27. Be4 Ne7 28. Bb1 Nc6 29. Ba2 Nf6 30. g4 e5 $132 {alerich,h (2561)-omriomri (2495) Engine Room 2012}) ({Another way to simplify the position is the aforementioned} 13. Ne5) ({Eljanov decides to keep the tension, retain the queens and make h5 feel uncomfortable. However, the text move has a drawback in making the a4 more vulnerable.} 13. Rfe1 $13 {deserves attention.}) 13... O-O 14. Rb5 c5 {[%cal Rb7c6]} 15. dxc5 {White can't retain the rook on its outpost, so he changes the character of the position.} (15. Bd6 {is roughly equal:} Rfd8 (15... Bc6 16. Bxf8 Bxb5 (16... Kxf8!? 17. Rbb1 Bxf3 18. Qxf3 Qxf3 19. gxf3 cxd4 $44) 17. Bxc5 Bxc4 18. Bxc4 bxc5 $11) 16. dxc5 Nxc5 17. Bxc5 Rxc5 18. Rxb6 Bc6 $44 {/=}) (15. c3 Bc6 16. Rfb1!? Bxb5 (16... e5!? 17. dxe5 Qg4 $13) 17. Rxb5 $44 {is an engine suggestion (!), but not many GMs would sacrifice an exchange out of the blue...}) 15... Rxc5 {A step in the wrong direction.} 16. Bd6?! ({still holds the balance, e.g.} 16. Ne5 Qxe2 17. Bxe2 Ba6 18. Rxc5 Nxc5 19. Rb1 Nfd7 (19... Nxa4 20. Bf3 Rc8 21. Nc6 Kf8 22. Bd6+ Ke8 23. c5 Nd7! 24. Ra1 Naxc5 25. Na7 Rd8 26. Bc7 e5 27. Bc6 Bb7 $11) 20. Nxd7 Nxd7 21. Be3 $11) 16... Rxb5 17. cxb5 (17. axb5 $142 !? Rc8 $15 {is very comfortable for Black, but the a5-pawn still still isn't playing a major role and at least White has no weakness on a4.}) 17... Rc8 {[%csl Gd5]} 18. c4 {[%csl Ra4,Rc4] Covers d5, but now both c4 and a4 are potentially vulnerable.} Nc5 19. Bc2 Nce4 {Putting the bishop on an unprotected square is a more serious, tactical mistake.} 20. Bf4? ({He had to play} 20. Bg3 $8 {White's position is unpleasant, but he can fight after} Ng5 (20... Nxg3 21. hxg3 g6 $15) 21. Bd1 Nxf3+ 22. gxf3 Nd7 $15 {} 23. Qd2 Nc5 24. Qd6 Bxf3 25. Bxf3 Qxf3 26. Qxb6 h5 $40) 20... Nc3 21. Qd3 Qg4 (21... Bxf3 $142 ! 22. gxf3 (22. Qxf3?? Ne2+) (22. Qxc3 Nd5 23. Qxf3 (23. Qd2 Qg4 24. Bg3 Qxc4 25. Bd3 Qc3 26. Qxc3 (26. Bf4 Bxg2! 27. Kxg2 Qxd3 $19) 26... Rxc3 $19) 23... Qxf3 24. gxf3 Nxf4) 22... Qg6+! 23. Qxg6 (23. Kh1 Qxd3 24. Bxd3 Nxa4 $17) 23... hxg6 {[%cal Rc8c4,Rc3e2]} 24. Be3 Rxc4 25. Bd3 Rxa4 26. Bxb6 Nxb5 (26... Nd7!? 27. Be3 Nd5) 27. Bxb5 Rb4 28. Bxa5 Rxb5 $17) 22. Be5? {The final mistake, after which Eljanov doesn't get back into the game.} (22. Qxc3 {I suspect Carlsen would have preferred the simple} Qxf4 (22... Nd5 {is more complex:} 23. Bxh7+ (23. Ne5 Qxg2+ 24. Kxg2 Nxf4+ 25. Kg3 Ne2+ 26. Kh3 Nxc3 27. Rc1 f6 28. Nd7 Ne2 $19) (23. h3 Nxc3! (23... Qxf4 24. Qd3 $15) (23... Nxf4 24. hxg4 Ne2+ 25. Kh2 Nxc3 26. Ne5 $132 {[%csl Rb6]}) 24. hxg4 Ne2+ 25. Kh2 Nxf4 26. Ne5 (26. Rd1 Bxf3 27. gxf3 Kf8 $17) 26... f6 27. Nd7 Rxc4 28. Bb3 Rb4 29. g3 Nd5 30. Bxd5 exd5 31. Nxb6 d4 $17) 23... Kh8 24. h3 $8 Nxf4 (24... Qxf4 25. Qe5 Rxc4 26. Bd3 $15) 25. hxg4 Ne2+ 26. Kh2 Nxc3 27. Rc1 (27. Bc2 Bxf3 28. gxf3 Rxc4 $17) 27... Ne2 28. Re1 Bxf3 29. gxf3 Nd4 30. Be4 Rxc4 31. Ra1 g5 $17) 23. Nd2 Ng4 24. g3 Qe5! 25. Qxe5 Nxe5 26. Rd1 g5 {Black has a clear positional plus, but material still remains equal and White can plod on.} 27. Bb3 $17) 22... Qxc4 23. Qxc4 Rxc4 $19 {[%cal Rc3e2] Carlsen has an extra pawn and his pieces dominate the board - the game is decided.} 24. Bd3 (24. Bb3 Rb4 25. Bxc3 Rxb3 $19) 24... Rc8 25. Ra1 (25. Bxc3 Rxc3 $19 {is hopeless, but even retaining more pieces doesn't help to create counterchances.}) 25... Nfd5 26. Nd2 f6 27. Bd6 (27. Bxc3 Rxc3 28. Bf1 {allows White to suffer longer, but the outcome is in no doubt after} Kf8 29. Ne4 Rc7) 27... Nb4 28. Bc4 Bd5 29. Bf1 Nba2! {[%csl Ra4] White loses another pawn and any compensation is still non-existent.} 0-1

