{As was true of another young player yesterday, Carlsen opted to take a rest against his opponent - World Champion Anand. Reinvigorated today, the Norwegian brought his A-game to the A section, in a demolition worthy of the world's top-ranked, A-grade player.} 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 a6 5. Qc2 g6 6. Bd3 Be6 $146 {leaving well-tested lines early.} 7. b3 Bg7 8. Nge2 c5 9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Nxd5 Bxd5 11. e4 Be6 12. Qxc5 Nc6 13. Be3 Rc8 14. Qa3 O-O 15. Rd1 Nxd4 (15... a5 {with the idea of providing an outpost for the knight, facilitating an exchange for White's light-squared bishop, may have been better.}) 16. Bxd4 Bxd4 17. Bb1 Bc5 18. b4 Qxd1+ {with this move, things go from bad to worse.} (18... Bxf2+ {was to be much preferred.}) 19. Kxd1 Bxf2 20. Nf4 Bc4 21. Bd3 Rfd8 22. Ke2 Bxd3+ 23. Kxf2 Rc2+ 24. Kf3 Bc4 25. Rc1 Rxa2 26. Qc3 b5 27. Ra1 Rdd2 28. Rxa2 Rxa2 29. h4 h5 30. g4 hxg4+ 31. Kxg4 f6 32. e5 Kf7 33. exf6 exf6 34. Qe3 Rc2 35. Qa7+ {With Black's position in tatters, Ivanchuk mericfully put himself out of his own misery.} 1-0

