1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 a6!? {An out of form (and out of luck) Carlsen playing Black against his own second and good friend, Jon Ludwig Hammer in the last round. An unenviable situation for the Champion. It is therefore not a surprise that Carlsen chooses to play something slightly different...} 4. Bg5 f6?! {[%csl Re6][%cal Rg8f6,Ga2g8] ...but this is a real surprise. Whatever the engines say, this move would be criticised by any book on openng fundamentals. It weakens the a2-g8, takes away an important square from the g8- and also creates a target on e6.} (4... Be7 {is normal.}) 5. Bd2 dxc4 6. e3 Nc6 (6... b5 7. a4 Bb7 8. axb5 axb5 9. Rxa8 Bxa8 10. b3 $14 {[%csl Rb5,Re6,Re8,Rf6][%cal Gf7f6] White will win back the pawn. The weakening move ...f6 will matter sooner or later.}) 7. Bxc4 {[%csl Re6]} Bd6 8. e4 (8. Nc3!? Nge7 9. O-O O-O 10. Ne4 $14) 8... Nge7 9. O-O O-O 10. Qb3 Kh8 (10... Qd7 11. a4 $14 {[%csl Rc8,Re6,Rg8] White has the space. Black has the weakness and awkwardly placed pieces.}) 11. Bxe6 Bxh2+ 12. Kxh2 (12. Nxh2? Nxd4 $17) 12... Qd6+ 13. Kh1 ({Hammer was probably worried about some intermediate checks that could spoil his calculations somewhere after} 13. Kg1 {[%cal Gc6d4,Gd4f3,Gc6e5,Ge5f3,Gf3g1] but concrete variations do not show anything to worry about, for example} Bxe6 14. d5 Bg8 (14... Nxd5 15. exd5 Bxd5 16. Bf4 $16) 15. Qa3 $14) 13... Bxe6 14. d5 Bg8 (14... Nxd5 15. exd5 Bxd5 16. Bf4 $16) 15. Qa3! {Forcing a pleasant endgame.} Qxa3 16. Nxa3 Na7 (16... Nb8 {[%csl Ra8][%cal Gb8d7,Gc7c6] controls the d7-square and therefore makes ...c6 a viable option to consider. But it holds up the  on a8, giving us the pros and cons.} 17. Bb4 Re8 18. Rac1 c6 19. Rfe1 $14 ({The point of ...b8 is that} 19. d6 {[%csl Rd7][%cal Gb8d7] is a move that Black need not worry about.} Nc8 $15) (19. Nc4 Nc8)) (16... Ne5? 17. Nxe5 fxe5 18. f3 $16 {[%csl Rc7,Re5]}) 17. Bb4 Rfe8 18. Rac1 Rac8 (18... c6?! 19. d6 $14 {[%csl Ra7][%cal Rb8d7]}) (18... Ng6!? 19. Rxc7 (19. Rfe1 c6 20. Nc4 Nf4 21. d6 (21. Nb6? Rad8 $132) 21... Nd3 $132) 19... Rxe4 20. Bc5 (20. Rxb7? Bxd5 $17) 20... Bxd5 21. Rd1 Bg8 (21... Bxa2 22. Bxa7 Rxa7 23. Rd8+ Bg8 24. Nc4 {[%cal Gc4d6]} h5 (24... h6 25. Nd6 Re7 26. Rcc8 $16) 25. Nd6 $18 {[%cal Gc7c5,Gc5h5,Gc7c8,Gd8g8]}) 22. Bxa7 Rxa7 23. Rd8 $36 {[%csl Ra7,Rg8][%cal Gc7a7,Gc7h7,Gd8a8,Gd8h8]}) 19. Nd4! f5 20. f3 (20. Bxe7?! Rxe7 21. exf5 (21. Nxf5 Rxe4 $15) 21... Rd8 $15 {[%csl Rd5]}) 20... fxe4 21. fxe4 $14 {[%csl Rc7,Gd5,Ge4] The central pawns take away a lot of important squares from Black pieces and White has annoying pressure on c7.} Ng6 22. Nf5?! (22. Rfe1 $142 {[%csl Gd5,Ge4] maintaining the strong central control.} Nf4 {[%cal Gf4d3]} 23. Nf5! {[%csl Rg7][%cal Gb4c3,Gc3g7]} Nd3 24. Bc3 Bf7 25. Nxg7 Kg8 26. Nxe8 Rxe8 27. Bd4 {[%csl Ra7]} Nxe1 28. Rxe1 $14 {[%csl Ra7,Rc7,Gd5,Ge4]}) 22... Rxe4 23. Bc3 Bxd5?! ({On any other day and in any other tournament Carlsen would have found} 23... h6 $142 24. Bxg7+ Kh7 25. Bd4 (25. Bxh6 Bxd5 $132) 25... b6 26. d6 c5 $13) (23... Ne5? 24. Rce1 $18) 24. Bxg7+ Kg8 25. Bd4! {[%csl Ra7][%cal Gf5h6,Gd4a7] Carlsen admitted to having missed this move. That is real bad form.} Rxd4 26. Nxd4 Bxa2 27. b3 Rd8 ({Black's best chance was to try and fight after} 27... c5 28. Rf2 cxd4 29. Rxc8+ Nxc8 30. Rxa2 {and it is not that easy for White to convert this position into a win.} Nf8 31. Nc4 $14 {and a long battle awaits.} ({White cannot really take the  on d4. For example} 31. Rd2 Ne6 32. Nc2?! Nd6! 33. Nxd4? Ne4 $17 {[%csl Rd1,Rd3,Re2][%cal Ge4g3,Ge4f2]})) 28. Ne6 Rd2 ({Black should have pushed the  away with} 28... Rd6 $142 $16) 29. Rc3 (29. Rxc7 Bxb3 30. Rg7+ Kh8 31. Rff7 Bxe6 32. Rxh7+ Kg8 33. Rhg7+ Kh8 34. Rxb7 Bd5 (34... Nb5 35. Nxb5 axb5 36. Rxg6 $16) 35. Rh7+ Kg8 36. Rbg7+ Kf8 37. Rxg6 $18) 29... Re2 (29... Nc6 30. Rcf3 {[%cal Gf3f8]} h5 31. Rf7 Nce5 32. Rg7+ Kh8 33. Nc4 $18) 30. Nf4 (30. Rf6 $18 {[%cal Gc3f3,Gf6f8] was simpler, forcing Black to give up another exchange to avoid the threatened mate with cf3 followed by f8.}) 30... Rb2 31. Nxg6 hxg6 32. Rxc7 Rxb3? (32... Bxb3 33. Rf6 $18 (33. Rxb7 Nb5 34. Rb1 Rxb1+ 35. Nxb1 $16)) ({A better fighting chance was} 32... Nc6 33. Re1 (33. Rf6!? Rxb3 34. Rxg6+ Kf8 35. Nc2 $16 {[%csl Gg2]}) (33. Rxb7 Rxb3 34. Rxb3 Bxb3 35. Rf6 Ne5 36. Rxa6 $16) 33... Kf8 34. Nc4 Rxb3 35. Nd6 {[%cal Ge1e8]} Rb1 36. Rxb1 Bxb1 37. Nxb7 $16 {[%cal Gb7c5,Gc5a6,Gc5e6]}) 33. Rd1 {[%cal Gd1d8]} Nc6 34. Rdd7 {[%cal Gc7c8] A fine victory for Jon Ludwig Hammer who kept up the pressure all through the game. From Carlsen's viewpoint, bad luck, bad form, the pressure of the home crowd and a final round against his own Second, everything went wrong for the Champion in this tournament. He is mortal, after all. The next event will be a test of his character and it will be no surprise if he comes out fighting with a vengeance.} 1-0

