1. e4 {Marin,M} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 Ng4 {Modern chess sometimes sports most illogical ideas. Black is moving the only developed piece with a tall ambition to spoil the planned English attack set up of the opponent.} 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Bg3 Bg7 10. h3 Nf6 11. Qf3 Qb6 12. O-O-O Nc6 13. Nxc6 Qxc6 14. Be2 Qc5 15. Rhe1 {White is still looking for the way of "refuting" Black's provocative concept. The general mobilaization of the pieces towards the center can hardly spoil anything.} g4 {Marin,M: 'Played partly in accordance with Nimzovitsch' postulate that any mobile pawn formation that does not advance is likely to become a weakness. However, in this case it would be desirable to play ...h5 and ...g4, since the operation carried out in the game leaves the kingside weakened anyway.'} (15... Nd7 16. Nd5 Ne5 17. Qb3 b5 18. f4 Nc4 19. Bxc4 bxc4 20. Qf3 $16 {1-0 Rytshagov,M (2490)-Kulaots,K (2510)/Estonia 2003 (30)}) (15... Be6 16. e5 dxe5 17. Bd3 e4 18. Nxe4 Nxe4 19. Qxe4 O-O-O 20. a3 Rd5 21. Qb4 Rhd8 22. Qxc5+ Rxc5 $11 {½-½ Lutz,C (2640)-Polgar,J (2715)/Budapest 2003 (25)}) 16. hxg4 Bxg4 17. Qd3 Be6 {Carlsen must take care about his pieces, as the careless exchange of the bishops would seriously enhance White's chances.} (17... Bxe2?! 18. Nxe2 Ng4 19. Qf3 Rg8 20. Kb1 Bf6 21. c3 $16) 18. e5 {'!' Marin,M.} (18. Bf3 Ng4 19. Nd5 Ne5 20. Qb3 Nxf3 21. gxf3 (21. Qxf3 h5 $14) 21... b5 22. Bh4 Ra7 23. Rg1 $14) 18... dxe5 19. Bf3 e4! {A fine defensive move and a true proverbial spanner in the works of otherwise perfectly coordinated Leko's forces.} (19... O-O 20. Rxe5 Qb6 21. Na4 Qb4 22. Nc5 Bxa2 23. Bxb7 $16) 20. Nxe4 Nxe4 21. Rxe4 (21. Bxe4 O-O 22. c3 Rac8 23. Kb1 $14) 21... O-O {Marin,M: 'The black king's position looks unsafe, but in fact his bishops are targetting the enemy king, while the white bishop concentrate on an almost empty wing. '} 22. Qe3 {The Hungarian super grandmaster is a perfectionist in the openings, but not your typical courageous attacker in most positions. Yet even he must have been intrigued by the dangerous exchange sacrifice on e6 with very interesting chances.} (22. Rxe6!? {Marin,M: 'deserves serious attention, but does not seem to be in Leko's cautious style.'} fxe6 23. Qb3 Kh8 24. Qxe6 Rac8 25. Be4 (25. c3 Bxc3 26. Qxh6+ Kg8 27. Bd5+ Qxd5 28. Qxf8+ Kxf8 29. Rxd5 Be5+ 30. Kb1 Bxg3 31. fxg3 Kf7 $11) 25... Qg5+ 26. Kb1 Rcd8 $13) 22... Rac8 23. Qxc5 (23. c3 Qxe3+ 24. Rxe3 b5 25. a3 $14) 23... Rxc5 24. Bg4?! {The meek exchange of the bishops is kissing all chances of playing for an advantage good bye. It must have been preferable to keep the active pair of bishops shooting against Black's queenside.} (24. Ra4 Rb5 25. c3 Bf6 $14) 24... Bxg4 25. Rxg4 f5 26. Rb4 (26. Rg6 f4 27. Bh4 Rfc8 28. c3 Kf7 29. Rb6 Rh5 30. g3 Rc7 $11) 26... b5 27. a4 Rfc8 {Carlsen has made his name as one of the players with best technique in the world. The position is objectively equal, but he is doing his best to put pressure on his opponent.} (27... Kf7 28. c3 Be5 $11) 28. c3 Rc4 29. Rxc4 Rxc4 30. axb5 axb5 31. Rd8+ (31. Rd5 f4 32. Bh2 b4 33. Kd2 bxc3+ 34. bxc3 $11) 31... Kh7! {It takes an open mind to admit, that sometimes even the passive looking retreat can be stronger than automatic centralization.} (31... Kf7 32. Rd5! b4 33. Rxf5+ Kg6 34. Rf4 Rxf4 35. Bxf4 bxc3 36. b4 Kf5 37. Be3 h5 38. Kc2 Ke4 39. b5 Kd5 40. Kd3 $14) 32. Rd7 Rg4! 33. Bb8 (33. Rxe7 f4 34. Bh2 Rh4 35. Bg1 Rg4 36. f3 Rxg2 37. Bd4 h5 38. Rf7 Kg8 39. Rf5 Bxd4 40. cxd4 $11) 33... Rxg2 34. Rxe7 Kg6 35. Bg3 (35. Re6+ Kf7 36. Rb6 Rxf2 37. Rxb5 Kg6 38. Rb6+ Kg5 39. Rb7 Bf6 $15) 35... h5 36. Kd1? {[%mdl 4096] On the highest chess level there is only one true way to win the game - put pressure against your opponent! The best and brighest are sometimes crumbling under the unpleasant strain.} (36. Rb7 f4 (36... Bh6+ 37. Kd1 f4 38. Rb6+ Kh7 39. Rb7+ $11) 37. Bxf4 Rxf2 38. Rb6+ Bf6 39. Bg3 Rf5 40. Bh4 Rf4 41. Bg3 $11) 36... Bf6 37. Re6 Kf7 38. Re3? {Bad as Leko's position has become it might not have been beyond hope. This nervous move is surely a losing mistake.} (38. Re2! h4 39. Bc7 h3 $17) 38... h4 39. Bc7 Rxf2 40. b3 Rf1+ (40... f4 41. Rd3 Ke6 42. Ke1 Rc2 43. Bxf4 Rxc3 $19) 41. Ke2 Rc1 42. c4 {The good advice became a commodity, that White can not value enough. Parting with the second pawn looks like an act of a pure desperation...} (42. Kd2 Bg5 43. Kxc1 Bxe3+ $19) (42. Ba5 Kg6 43. Rf3 Ra1 44. Bb6 Ra3 45. c4 h3 $19) 42... bxc4 43. bxc4 Rxc4 44. Bb8 Rc2+ 45. Kf1 Kg6 46. Re2 Rc8 47. Bd6 Rc6 48. Be7 {We all know, that the rook endgame with f- and h-pawn might be defendable for the weaker side. Unfortunately not in the case, when the king is cut on the first rank.} (48. Bb8 h3 49. Kf2 Kg5 $19) 48... Bxe7 49. Rxe7 Rc2! 50. Re3 Kg5 51. Kg1 Kg4 52. Ra3 f4 53. Ra8 Rd2 54. Ra7 Kg3 55. Rg7+ Kf3 56. Ra7 Rd1+ 57. Kh2 Ke2 58. Kh3 f3 59. Ra2+ (59. Re7+ Kf1 60. Kxh4 f2 $19) 59... Rd2 {White has decided, that admitting defeat is not going to hurt him at all.} (59... Rd2 60. Ra8 f2 61. Re8+ Kf1 62. Kh2 Re2 63. Ra8 Re4 64. Kh3 Ke2 65. Ra2+ Kf3 66. Ra3+ Re3 67. Ra1 f1=Q+ 68. Rxf1+ Ke2+ $19) 0-1

