1. e4 {[%emt 0:00:00]} c5 {[%emt 0:00:06]} 2. Nf3 {[%emt 0:01:09]} e6 {[%emt 0:00:12]} 3. d4 {[%emt 0:02:08]} cxd4 {[%emt 0:00:17]} 4. Nxd4 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Nc6 {[%emt 0:00:10]} 5. Nc3 {[%emt 0:00:12]} Qc7 {[%emt 0:00:31] Another pull-no-punches opening choice from Matlakov, who in his Black games against the other 2 world champions in this event (yep, quite a tough draw!) chose innovative systems in the Italian and Ruy Lopez involving knights moving to the edge. Maybe this is something of a habit for him, as in this game the c6-knight soon makes its way to a5...} 6. Be3 {[%emt 0:01:25]} a6 {[%emt 0:00:30]} (6... Nf6 7. f4 Bb4!? {is an interesting attempt to make do without ...a6 for at least a few moves:} 8. Nb5 Qa5 9. e5 Nd5 10. Bd2 Nxc3 11. Bxc3 O-O 12. Qd2 a6 (12... f6!? $132) 13. Bxb4 Qxb4 14. Qxb4 Nxb4 15. Nd4 $132 {1/2-1/2 (15) Motylev,A (2656)-Svidler,P (2751) Tallinn 2016}) 7. Qd2 {[%emt 0:01:04]} Nf6 {[%emt 0:00:53]} 8. f4 {[%emt 0:00:24]} (8. O-O-O Be7 (8... Bb4 9. f3 {is the absolute main line:} Ne5 (9... Na5 {is also a thing here, but White must be slightly better in the ending after a3}) 10. Nb3 b5 (10... d6 11. Bd4 $16 {was the unfortunate opening outcome (for Black) of one game from the 2016 British Championships}) 11. Qf2!? {Main is 11.e1 but this is an interesting new idea.} Be7?! 12. g4 d6 13. Rg1 Rb8 14. g5 $14 {Dominguez Perez,L-Vachier Lagrave,M Berlin 2015}) 9. f3 h5 10. Nxc6 bxc6 11. Bf4 e5 12. Bg5 a5 13. Bc4 a4 14. a3 d6 15. f4 O-O 16. Rhf1 Bg4 17. Rde1 Rfd8 18. Qe3 Rab8 19. fxe5 dxe5 20. Bxf6 Bxf6 21. h3 Rd4 22. hxg4 Rxc4 23. gxh5 Be7 24. Rf3 Rd4 25. Nd1 Qb6 26. h6 Qd8 27. Rg3 g6 28. Rf1 Rb7 29. Nf2 Bh4 30. Rg4 Bxf2 31. Qxf2 Rbd7 32. Qf3 Kh7 33. Kb1 Qe7 34. Rg3 Qh4 35. Rg4 Qe7 36. Rg3 Qd6 37. Rc1 Rd1 {Grischuk,A (2783)-Cori Tello,J (2641) Tbilisi 2017 ½-½}) 8... Bb4 {[%emt 0:02:08]} 9. Bd3 {[%emt 0:00:41]} Na5 {[%emt 0:00:36]} (9... Ne7!? {tries to play ...d5 under favourable circumstances, but just ends up looking slow after the same move} 10. a3 $14) 10. a3 {[%emt 0:00:31]} Bxc3 {[%emt 0:00:32]} 11. Qxc3 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Qxc3+ {[%emt 0:00:18]} 12. bxc3 {[%emt 0:00:04]} d5 {[%emt 0:00:07]} 13. exd5 {[%emt 0:00:05]} (13. e5 Ne4 14. Nb3 Nc4 $11 {led to an interesting game with a sidelined White knight:} 15. Bxc4 dxc4 16. Na5 Bd7 17. Bd4 Rc8 18. Nxb7 Bc6 19. Na5 Bd5 $44 {Volokitin,A-Giri,A Germany 2014}) 13... exd5 {[%emt 0:01:03]} 14. Nb3 {[%emt 0:00:09]} Nc4 {[%emt 0:00:47]} 15. Bd4 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Ne4 {[%emt 0:04:13]} 16. Nc5 {[%emt 0:01:07]} Nxc5 {[%emt 0:25:42]} 17. Bxc5 {[%emt 0:00:05]} Bd7 {[%emt 0:00:25] The position is similar in essence to Mamedyarov-Carlsen from yesterday - the only difference being that Carlsen held nearly effortlessly there, but Matlakov is not able to do the same today.} 18. O-O-O {[%emt 0:02:34]} O-O-O {[%emt 0:01:38]} 19. Bxc4 {[%emt 0:00:53]} dxc4 {[%emt 0:00:24]} 20. Bb6 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Rde8 {[%emt 0:01:07]} 21. Rd4 {[%emt 0:00:11]} Re6 {[%emt 0:07:55] Dropping a healthy pawn by force.} (21... Bb5!? 22. Rhd1 Kb8 {looks a bit passive, but avoids going in for hard-to-calculate forced lines. Black's idea is ...c8-c6.}) 22. Rxc4+ {[%emt 0:00:24]} Rc6 {[%emt 0:00:48]} (22... Kb8 23. Rd1!? Bb5 24. Rb4 Rxb6 25. c4 $14 {is unlikely to be much more pleasant, since Black now ends up with doubled pawns himself.}) 23. Rxc6+ {[%emt 0:00:44]} Bxc6 {[%emt 0:00:26]} 24. Rd1 {[%emt 0:00:25]} Bxg2 {[%emt 0:18:10]} 25. Rg1 {[%emt 0:00:14]} Be4 {[%emt 0:12:19]} 26. Rxg7 {[%emt 0:00:44]} Bg6 {[%emt 0:00:18]} 27. a4 {[%emt 0:12:05]} (27. f5 {immediately was also possible, and Black's drawing chances may not be as good as they immediately seem.}) 27... Rf8 {[%emt 0:05:24]} 28. Kb2 {[%emt 0:04:57]} Kd7 {[%emt 0:02:12]} (28... h5 {could also have been played, since it is not clear, especially given the further course of the game, whether having the king out helps or hinders Black's drawing efforts.}) 29. f5 {[%emt 0:00:34]} Bxf5 {[%emt 0:00:17]} 30. Bc5 {[%emt 0:00:02]} Rc8 {[%emt 0:00:20]} 31. Rxf7+ {[%emt 0:00:07]} Ke6 {[%emt 0:00:06]} 32. Re7+ {[%emt 0:00:03]} Kf6 {[%emt 0:00:17]} 33. Bb4 $14 {[%emt 0:00:53] Now White has a healthy nibble, and he coaxes ...a5.} a5 {[%emt 0:09:19]} (33... b5 34. axb5 axb5 35. Rb7 Re8 36. Rxb5 Re2 $14 {was a marginally better version of the same thing.}) 34. Ba3 {[%emt 0:00:21]} Rc4 {[%emt 0:00:43]} 35. Rxb7 {[%emt 0:00:41]} Rxa4 {[%emt 0:00:12]} 36. Ra7 {[%emt 0:05:14]} Re4 {[%emt 0:09:40]} 37. Rxa5 {[%emt 0:00:11]} Re2 {[%emt 0:00:05]} 38. Bd6 {[%emt 0:00:05]} Bxc2 {[%emt 0:02:20]} 39. c4 $16 {[%emt 0:00:55] Eventually Carlsen had to be able to swap one of his doubled pawns for another pawn. The position is now completely thankless - the best Black can hope for is not even a straight draw, but to have to defend rook and bishop against rook!} Ke6 {[%emt 0:02:00]} 40. Ra6 {[%emt 0:00:00]} Bf5+ {[%emt 0:05:14]} 41. Kc3 {[%emt 0:01:48]} Be4 {[%emt 0:11:24]} 42. Kd4 {[%emt 0:06:19]} Kf5 {[%emt 0:02:03]} 43. Ra5+ {[%emt 0:00:29]} Kg4 {[%emt 0:03:26]} 44. c5 {[%emt 0:05:32]} Bf3 {[%emt 0:05:04]} 45. Ra7 {[%emt 0:01:20]} h6 {[%emt 0:04:19]} 46. Rh7 {[%emt 0:03:14]} Re4+ {[%emt 0:04:33]} 47. Kd3 {[%emt 0:01:05]} Re6 {[%emt 0:00:06]} 48. Kc4 {[%emt 0:00:11]} Bc6 {[%emt 0:02:48]} 49. Rc7 {[%emt 0:00:51]} Bh1 {[%emt 0:00:47]} 50. Kb5 {[%emt 0:01:16]} h5 {[%emt 0:02:17]} (50... Re1 $16 {keeps the game going a bit better.}) 51. Rg7+ {[%emt 0:01:38]} Kh4?! {[%emt 0:01:02] Walking into all kinds of mating motifs.} (51... Kf5 52. Rg1 Bf3 53. Rf1 {might have been a line Black was afraid of, but he survives by defending with the rook:} Re3 $16) 52. Rg1 {[%emt 0:16:52]} Ba8 {[%emt 0:01:23]} 53. Kb6 {[%emt 0:06:28]} Re2 {[%emt 0:01:29]} 54. Kc7 {[%emt 0:01:04]} Bd5 {[%emt 0:03:57]} 55. Rg3 {[%emt 0:03:30]} Bh1 {[%emt 0:00:35]} (55... Rxh2 56. Rd3 {is a nice small tactic.}) 56. Rc3 {[%emt 0:07:56]} Kg4 {[%emt 0:03:34]} 57. c6 {[%emt 0:01:07] So White has managed everything he needs to and he will win the bishop without the need for a h-pawn swap. In a game Sowray-Harvey, Bedford 2013, the weaker side continued even after the win of a similar piece, but ultimately even after some inspired resistance was unable to save half a point.} 1-0

