1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 Bc5 5. Nb3 Bb6 6. Nc3 Nf6 7. Qe2 O-O {A rare move, but once played by Anand! To be honest, I just don't like this line for Black.} (7... d6 {is more common.}) 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bh4! $146 (9. h4 d6 10. f3 hxg5 11. hxg5 Ng4 12. fxg4 Qxg5 13. Qf3 Bxg4 14. Qg3 Ne5 15. Be2 f5 16. exf5 Qxf5 17. O-O-O Bxe2 18. Nxe2 Qf2 19. Qh2 Qxe2 20. Qh8+ Kf7 21. Rdf1+ Bf2 22. Qh7 Ke6 23. Kb1 Qg4 {0-1 Rublevsky,S (2649)-Anand,V (2781)/Bastia 2004}) 9... a5 10. a4 Nd4 {Not forced, but attractive. But I am not sure that other continuations are easier to play.} ({I checked the following line briefly:} 10... Re8 11. O-O-O d6 12. f3!? Be6 13. g4 Bxb3 (13... Ne5 14. Nd4 $16) 14. cxb3 Nd4 15. Qg2!? Nxb3+ 16. Kb1 Nd4 17. Bc4 c6 18. g5 hxg5 19. Bxg5 {nad and Black will face a decisive attack.}) 11. Qd3 Nxb3 12. cxb3 Re8 13. O-O-O {From there Etienne started to spend a lot of time for each move. But here I already prefer White even if Black's position shouldn't be a desaster at all.} d6 14. Qc2! {White wants to go c4, and at the same time allow some combinations with e5.} Bd7?! {I guess the idea for this move is to wait for c4 and go ...e6, without playing ...c6, weaknessing the d6-pawn. That's why 15.b1!? could be clever. But it looks like 15.c4 is almost winning by force.} (14... c6 15. Bc4 Qe7 {ought to be a better defense. Somehow, I feel like White should be at least slightly better. But things are far from easy, for instance:} 16. Rhe1 Be6 17. Nd5!? (17. f3 Bxc4 18. bxc4 Qe6 {followed by ...d7, and Black should have no problem.}) 17... cxd5 18. exd5 g5! 19. Bg3 Nxd5 20. Rxd5 Bc5 21. Rxc5 dxc5 22. Rxe6 fxe6 23. Qg6+ Qg7 24. Bxe6+ Rxe6 25. Qxe6+ Kh7 $11) 15. Bc4 Be6 16. Rhe1 Qe7 17. e5! dxe5 18. Rxe5 {White is threatening d5. From now Etienne play kind of all best defensive moves. But it is not enough to hold this tough position against Carlsen.} Qf8 $8 19. Bxf6 gxf6 20. Re2 $16 Qg7 21. Bxe6! Rxe6 22. Rxe6 fxe6 23. Rd3 Kh8 24. Rg3 Qh7 25. Qd2 Bc5 $8 26. Ne4 Be7 27. Rh3 Kg7 (27... Rd8 28. Rxh6 Rxd2 29. Rxh7+ Kxh7 30. Kxd2 $18) 28. Qd7 $18 Kf7 29. Ng5+!? (29. Nxf6!? Kxf6 30. Rf3+ $18) 29... fxg5 30. Rf3+ Kg8 31. Qxe6+ Kh8 32. Rf7 Bd6 33. Rxh7+ Kxh7 34. Qf7+ Kh8 35. g3 Ra6 36. Kb1 Bb4 37. f4 gxf4 38. gxf4 1-0

