1. c4 g6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2 O-O 6. Bg5 c5 7. d5 e6 8. Qd2 exd5 9. exd5 Qb6 10. Nf3 Bf5 11. Nh4! Ne4 12. Nxe4 Bxe4 13. f3 Qxb2 14. Rc1 {This line of the Averbakh System was popular a couple of decades ago. It is quite risky for Black, who has won the b2-pawn but is considerably behind in development.} Bf5 {Alternatives:} (14... Bf6?! 15. Qxb2 Bxb2 16. fxe4 Bxc1 17. Bxc1 Re8 18. Nf3 Rxe4 19. Ng5 $16 {[%cal Yc1f4] 1-0 (42) Korotylev,A (2445)-Filippov,V (2455) Moscow 1995}) (14... Qxd2+ 15. Kxd2 Bxd5 {A brilliant but insufficient idea of the late GM Leonid Yurtaev. White has enough time to consolidate his position.} (15... f6 16. Bf4 g5 17. Bxd6 gxh4 18. Bxf8 Kxf8 19. fxe4 Bh6+ 20. Kc3 Bxc1 21. Rxc1 Ke7 22. Rf1 Nd7 23. Rf4 Rg8 24. Bf1 Ne5 25. Rxh4 $16 {1-0 (46) Freise,H-Klein,M Koenigsfeld 1969}) (15... Bf5 16. Nxf5 gxf5 17. Rb1 b6 18. Bd3 Nd7 19. Be7 Rfe8 20. Bxd6 Ne5 21. Rhe1 $16 {1-0 (44) Kachiani Gersinska,K (2448)-Berezina,I (2304) Istanbul 2000}) 16. cxd5 Re8 (16... Nd7 $142 17. g3 Nb6 18. Ng2 $14) 17. Rb1! (17. Bf4 Bf6 18. g3 (18. Bg3 a6 19. f4 Nd7 20. Bg4 Nb6 21. Bf3 Na4 22. Bf2 b5 23. g4 b4 $132 {0-1 (63) Zaichik,G (2420)-Yurtaev,L (2360) Volgodonsk 1983}) 18... g5 19. Bxd6 gxh4 20. Bc7 Nd7 $11 (20... a6 21. Rxc5 Nd7 22. Rc4 Be5 23. gxh4 Nf6 24. Rg1+ Kf8 25. Rg5 Bxc7 26. Rxc7 Rad8 27. Bc4 h6 28. Rf5 Re7 29. Rxe7 Kxe7 30. Kc3 $14 {1-0 (45) Sorin,A (2460)-Peralta,F (2360) Buenos Aires 1998}) 21. Bb5?! {1-0 (46) Grivas,E (2360)-Kotronias,V (2430) Athens 1986} a6! 22. Bxd7 Re7 $15) (17. Rhe1 a6 18. Bd3 (18. Bf4 Bf6 19. g3 g5 20. Bxd6 gxh4 21. Bxc5 Nd7 22. Ba3 hxg3 23. hxg3 Re5 24. Bc4 b5 25. Bb3 a5 $36 {1/2 (41) Lerner,K (2495)-Yurtaev,L (2360) Tallinn 1983}) 18... Nd7 19. a4 h6 20. Be3 Nb6 21. Nxg6! fxg6 22. a5 $14 {1/2 (31) Legky,N-Yurtaev,L Soviet Union 1984}) 17... Nd7 (17... Re5 18. f4! Rxd5+ 19. Kc1 Nc6 20. Rxb7 Rd4 21. Nf3 $16 {1-0 (34) Legky,N (2365)-Monin,N Tallinn 1985}) 18. Bb5 Re5 19. Bxd7 Rxg5 20. Rxb7 Rxd5+ 21. Kc2 $16 {1-0 (32) Lputian,S (2530)-Yurtaev,L (2435) Volgograd 1985}) (14... h6! 15. Bxh6 Qxd2+ 16. Bxd2 Bf6 17. g3 Bxh4 (17... g5 18. fxe4 gxh4 19. Bf4 Nd7 20. Bxd6 Rfe8 21. Bd3 Be5 22. Bxe5 Nxe5 23. Ke2 $14 {1/2 (39) Polugaevsky,L-Gufeld,E Tallinn 1965}) 18. gxh4 Bf5 19. Bf4 Rd8 20. Rg1 (20. h5 gxh5 (20... Na6 $142 $14) 21. Rg1+ Kh7 22. Rg5 Bg6 23. Kd2 Na6 24. Rcg1 Rd7 25. Bd3! Rg8 26. h4 Nb4 27. Bb1 Na6 28. Rxh5+ {1-0 (28) Bekker Jensen,S (2403)-Nordenbaek,J (2262) Denmark 2000}) 20... Na6 21. Kd2 Nc7 22. a4 Ne8 23. h5 Ng7 24. hxg6 fxg6 25. Rc3 Kf7 26. a5 Rd7 27. Bd1 Rh8 28. Ba4 $14 {1-0 (48) Boleslavsky,I-Balendo,E Minsk 1970}) 15. Nxf5 gxf5 16. Qxb2 Bxb2 17. Rb1 $146 {[%mdl 8] This novelty is quite obvious. In the previous game White played strangely:} (17. Rc2?! Be5 18. f4 Bd4 19. Bd3 Re8+ 20. Re2 Rxe2+ 21. Kxe2 Nd7 22. Bxf5 Re8+ 23. Kd2 Nb6 24. Bd3 Kg7 $132 {0-1 (45) Bukhman,E-Nezhmetdinov,R Daugavpils 1973}) 17... Bc3+ 18. Kd1 {[%cal Rb1b7,Re2d3,Rg5e7]} Re8 (18... b6 19. Bd3 $16 (19. Be7 Re8 20. Bxd6 Nc6!!)) 19. Rxb7 Na6 20. a3 Rab8 21. Rxb8 Nxb8 (21... Rxb8 22. Kc2 Be5 (22... Rb2+ 23. Kxc3 Rxe2 24. Bf4!) 23. Bc1! $16) 22. Bd3 $16 {White has the bishop pair, and Black has weaknesses on f5 and d6. Such positions are peanuts for GM Magnus Carlsen.} Nd7 23. Kc2 Bd4 24. Rb1 Nb6 25. Bf4 Be5 26. Re1 Kg7 27. Bg3 Re7 28. f4 Bf6 29. Rxe7 Bxe7 30. Be1 h5 31. g3 Bf6 32. Kb3 Kg6 33. h3 {[%cal Rg3g4]} 1-0

