{[%evp 0,61,80,53,81,84,88,86,93,56,65,64,94,87,110,88,106,77,60,92,48,60,65,33,58,-13,-13,-25,48,-11,-4,-19,60,-1,88,57,92,96,96,28,48,63,48,17,48,-87,77,77,206,188,296,114,342,198,417,425,476,454,444,300,786,781,29993,29994]} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e6 7. f3 b5 8. Qd2 Nbd7 9. g4 h6 10. O-O-O Ne5?! {'!?' Ponomariov played this three times in 2009. Black's idea is playing for ...b4 quickly and in case of ce2 c4, while a4 could have been met by ...d7. Nowadays the modern engines immediately show the disadvantage of this move. The knight move is equally revolutionary as Topalov's pet idea of 10...b5-b4. Black is moving the knight, that is already developed and prepares real counterplay.} (10... Bb7 {is the most popular continuation,}) ({and} 10... b4 {is the most fashionable one.}) 11. Qe1 {'N' A logical reply, which avoids the ...c4 tempo in case of ...b4 ce2. White has yet to find an idea, that would put the daring concept of the opponent under pressure. Carlsen comes with a new move, but it's merit might be modest.} (11. a3 {was one of among Ponomariov games, which is a completely acceptable reaction:} Nfd7 (11... Bb7 12. Kb1 (12. h4 Rc8 13. Rg1 Qa5 (13... g6 14. g5 hxg5 15. hxg5 Nfd7 16. f4 Nc4 17. Bxc4 Rxc4 18. f5 Nc5 19. fxe6 Nxe6 20. Nxe6 fxe6 21. Bd4 $16 {1-0 Bologan,V (2669)-Dobrov,V (2487)/chessassistantclub.com INT 2004 (30)}) 14. g5 hxg5 15. hxg5 Nfd7 16. g6 Nxg6 17. Nxe6 fxe6 18. Rxg6 Kf7 19. Rg3 Nf6 20. Bd3 e5 21. Qf2 Rxc3 22. Bb6 Qa4 23. bxc3 Qxa3+ 24. Kd2 $11 {½-½ Shirov,A (2714)-Guliyev,N (2526)/Port Barcares 2005 (42)}) 12... Rc8 13. Be2 Nfd7 14. f4 Nc4 15. Bxc4 Rxc4 16. Rhe1 Be7 17. Bf2 O-O 18. h4 Nc5 19. g5 h5 20. b3 Rxc3 21. Qxc3 Nxe4 22. Qe3 Qa5 $17 {0-1 Grischuk,A (2719)-Belov,V (2543)/playchess.com INT 2004 (54)}) 12. Qf2 Qc7 13. Rg1 g5 14. h4 Rg8 15. Kb1 Rb8 16. Na2 Be7 17. Rh1 Nb6 $13 {with a complicated middlegame, Dominguez Perez,L (2719)-Ponomariov,R (2739) Moscow blitz 2009.}) (11. f4! {is the strongest continuation to attack immediately and ignore the  on g4.} Nexg4 12. e5 (12. Bg1 b4 13. Na4 Nxe4 14. Qe1 d5 15. f5 Bd6 16. fxe6 Bxe6 17. Nb6 Qxb6 18. Nxe6 Qa5 19. Nxg7+ Kf8 $17 {1-0 Azarov,S (2544)-Najer,E (2608)/playchess.com INT 2004 (26)}) 12... b4 ({if} 12... dxe5 13. fxe5 Nxe5 14. Qg2! Bd7 15. Bf4! $18 {crushing the black position.}) 13. exf6 bxc3 14. Qxc3 Nxf6 (14... Nxe3!? 15. Qxe3 Qb6 16. Bc4 $16) 15. Bg2 $16 {with a promising position for White, Morozevich,A (2750)-Ponomariov,R (2739) Moscow blitz 2009.}) (11. g5 hxg5 12. Bxg5 Bb7 13. Rg1 Qa5 14. a3 Rc8 15. Kb1 Rxc3 16. Qxc3 Qxc3 17. bxc3 Rxh2 18. Bf4 Rh5 19. Rg5 Rxg5 20. Bxg5 $11 {0-1 Motylev,A (2651)-Grischuk,A (2704)/Moscow 2004 (34)}) (11. Bd3 Bb7 12. Rhe1 (12. a3 d5 13. exd5 Nxd5 14. Nxd5 Bxd5 15. Bf4 Nxf3 16. Nxf3 Bxf3 17. Qe3 Bxh1 18. Be4 Bxe4 19. Rxd8+ Rxd8 20. Qxe4 Rd5 $15 {0-1 Erenburg,S (2585)-Volokitin,A (2662)/Mainz 2006 (74)}) 12... b4 13. Nce2 d5 14. exd5 Nxd5 15. Kb1 Bc5 16. Bg1 Qb6 17. f4 Nxg4 18. Nxe6 fxe6 19. Bg6+ Kd7 20. Bxc5 Qxc5 21. Nd4 Kc8 $17 {0-1 Landa,K (2600)-Najer,E (2653)/Sochi 2006 (29)}) 11... Qc7 {Another typical move in this line. The  goes out of the d-file and is useful on c7.} (11... Bb7 12. h4 $40) (11... b4 12. Nce2 d5 13. Nf4 $40) 12. h4 {The straightforward continuation, but this move allows good play for Black on the queenside. Careful players would surely consider the modest a3 idea, that complicates Black's counterplay for some time.} (12. a3! {would be more accurate to avoid ...b4,} Rb8 (12... Bb7 {'' ''} 13. Kb1 $16 {followed by h4 with the typical kingside's attack.}) 13. h4 b4 14. axb4 Rxb4 15. Be2 $16 {and White is ready for the attack by g5 then f4.}) 12... b4 13. Nce2 (13. Na4!? {was also possible, although it involves huge complications:} Bd7 (13... Qb7?! 14. b3 Bd7 {''} 15. Nb2 d5 (15... a5 16. Rg1 $16) 16. g5 hxg5 17. exd5 Nxd5 18. Bxg5 $36) (13... d5!?) 14. Qxb4 Rb8 15. Qc3 Qb7 16. Qa5 Nxf3! 17. Nxf3 Qxe4 $132 {with a double-edged fight.}) 13... Nc4 (13... d5! {probably gives Black a good game, but might have appeared too risky for Ponomariov. For example:} 14. Bf4 (14. g5 hxg5 15. exd5 Nxd5 16. Bxg5 Bb7 $13 {'='}) 14... Bb7 15. Bg2 (15. Kb1 dxe4 16. fxe4 Bd6 17. Rg1 Rd8 $15) 15... dxe4 16. fxe4 Nfxg4 17. Bh3 $44 {with a complicated middlegame.}) 14. Nf4 {The typical attempt to crush through on e6.} (14. Bf2 {was the alternative to keep the important dark-squared , however} e5! (14... Be7 15. Ng3 $40) (14... d5 15. exd5 Nxd5 16. Ng3 $36 {''}) 15. Nf5 Be6 $132 {is also leading to a sharp position.} (15... Bxf5 16. gxf5 a5 $13)) 14... Nxe3 ({The greedy} 14... e5?! {could have been met by} 15. Nd5 Nxd5 16. exd5 exd4 17. Bxd4+ Be7 18. Bxg7 Rg8 19. Bxh6 $16 {'' and White is obviously better.}) 15. Qxe3 Qb6?! {After this move everything works out wonderfully for White. Ponomariov has an ambition of his own and the queen moves hopes to complicate opponent's life with pressure along the dark squares.} (15... Be7 {was important, when White has to remove one of the s from the centre because of the ...e5 threat.} 16. Nd3 a5 (16... Qb6!?) 17. Bh3 e5 (17... Nd7 18. g5 $14) 18. Nb5 (18. Nf5 Bxf5 19. gxf5 O-O 20. Rhg1 Kh8 $15) 18... Qb7 $132 {with an unclear fight.}) (15... Bd7 16. Be2 e5 17. Nd5 Nxd5 18. exd5 Be7 19. Nf5 Bxf5 20. gxf5 Bf6 $11) 16. Bc4! {Now the  enters the game, and a blow, lethal or not, on the e6-square becomes almost inevitable.} Qc5?! {'?' Another dubious move with the , but it is harder to play with Black. Your humble annotator is amazed by the level of neglect both players will display when dealing with hidden issue of time. Black is repeatedly playing with the queen in a situation, when his general development could use some attention.} (16... Be7? {is hopeless in view of} 17. g5 $18) ({and} 16... e5? {could have been met by} 17. Bxf7+! Kxf7 18. Qb3+ {This is the point!} Ke8 19. Ng6 exd4 (19... Rh7?! 20. Ne6 $18 Be7 21. g5 $18) 20. Nxh8 $16 {and White will soon be both positionally and materially better.} Ra7 21. Ng6 $16) (16... Nd7! {was the only chance of a defence,} 17. Nfxe6! (17. Qb3 Nc5) (17. Qe2 Ne5 18. Bb3 Bd7 $14) 17... fxe6 18. Bxe6 Bb7 19. Qf4! (19. Qb3 O-O-O $13) 19... O-O-O 20. Qf7 $36 {and even though Black has managed to castle, he is still under great pressure.}) 17. Qb3! {White protects his  and continues to pressure e6. Now Black is defenceless against the capture on e6.} d5?! {Another error, but Black's position is already bad.} (17... Ra7 18. Bxe6 fxe6 19. Nfxe6 Bxe6 20. Nxe6 Qb5 21. Nf4 $18 {''}) (17... Qe5 18. Ndxe6 Bxe6 19. Nxe6 fxe6 20. Bxe6 $16 {This position might not be objectively losing for Black, but it is very difficult to defence for him in a practical game.}) 18. exd5 Bd6 19. Nfxe6! {One of the winning moves. In higher sense an obvious move. Carlsen has to use his initiative and the sacrifice of a piece for three pawns destroys the e6 stronghold in Scheveningen/Najdorf Sicilian. What is more it creates a dangerous intruder of his own on the same most critical square!} (19. Ndxe6! {was also strong,} fxe6 (19... Bxe6 20. Nd3 Qe3+ 21. Kb1 $18) 20. Ng6! $18 {and White is completely winning.} (20. Nd3 Qa5 21. dxe6 Bb7 $13)) 19... fxe6 20. dxe6 Be7 ({In case of} 20... Bb7 {Black could have been met by} 21. e7! {The e- is like a battering ram.} Bd5 22. Bxd5 Qxd5 23. Qa4+ Kf7 24. Nf5 Bf4+ 25. Kb1 Qxf3 26. Rhf1 Qxg4 27. Qb3+ Kg6 28. Rg1 $18 {and White wins (Carlsen).}) (20... Kf8 21. Nf5 Be7 22. Nxe7 Qxe7 23. g5 Nh5 24. Qe3 $18) 21. Qd3 (21. Nf5 $18 O-O (21... Bb7 22. Nxg7+ Kf8 23. Nf5 $18) 22. Nxe7+ Qxe7 23. g5 Ne8 24. gxh6 Kh8 $16) ({and} 21. g5 {are also winning.} Nh5 (21... hxg5 22. Qd3! $18) 22. Bd5 Ra7 23. Bc6+ Kf8 24. Qd3 Nf4 25. Qe4 $18) 21... O-O 22. Bb3? {Carlsen really wanted to keep his . The next moves by both players are strange crimes against the logic of attack and defence. White's advantage is based partly on the fact, that Black is badly behind in mobilizing his pieces. Peaceful retreat of the bishop throws away the lion share of the winning advantage!} ({Continuing the attack with} 22. g5! {would be the best reply,} Nh5 (22... hxg5 23. hxg5 Qxg5+ 24. Kb1 Nh5 25. Nc6 $18) 23. gxh6 Nf4 24. Qe4 Qxc4 25. hxg7 Rf6 (25... Rd8 26. Qxf4 Bxe6 27. Qh6 Qxa2 28. Qh8+ Kf7 29. Qh7 Kf6 30. g8=N+ Rxg8 31. Rhe1 $18) 26. Qxa8 $18 {and White is winning.}) 22... Rd8? {Ponomariov wanted to prevent White's main idea, g6 and f5, but there is also another strong attacking idea here. Ponomariov was probably programmed to be beaten badly on the given day. An independent chess mind would otherwise surely concentrate on a developing move with the bishop, that would more or less complete the development and nearly equalize the chances.} ({Instead,} 22... Bb7 {'!' would giving some real resistance,} 23. Qg6 ({The tempting} 23. g5? {would be bad now, because of} Nh5 24. gxh6 Nf4 25. Qe3 g6 $17 {and Black stops the white attack, his position is suddenly better.} (25... gxh6 26. Rhg1+ Kh8 27. Rg4 Qc7 $11)) (23. Nf5 Rad8 24. Qe2 (24. Qxd8? Rxd8 25. Rxd8+ Kh7 26. Nxe7 Bxf3 $17) 24... Kh7 $11) 23... Nd5 ({After} 23... Ne8? {Carlsen shows a brilliant analysis:} 24. g5 hxg5 25. c3!! {Frees the c2-square for the .} gxh4 (25... Rf6 26. Qf7+!! {is the beautiful idea} Rxf7 27. exf7+ Kh7 28. Bc2+ g6 29. hxg5+ Kg7 30. Ne6+ Kxf7 31. Nxc5 $18) 26. Rdg1 $18 {followed by c2 with a decisive attack.}) 24. Nf5 Rxf5 25. gxf5 Kh8! $44 {and White is materially better, but Black now enjoys good piece play.}) 23. g5! Nh7 (23... hxg5 24. Qg6 $18 ({or} 24. hxg5 Qxg5+ 25. Kb1 $18 {and Black is defenceless against the threat of dg1, with inevitable disaster on g7.}) 24... g4 25. Nf5 Rxd1+ 26. Rxd1 Bf8 27. e7+ Kh8 28. exf8=Q+ Qxf8 29. Rd8 $18) (23... Kh8 24. gxf6 Bxf6 25. Qf5 Qb6 26. Bd5 $18) 24. gxh6 {White has already a winning attack.} Qh5 (24... gxh6 25. Rhg1+ Kh8 26. Qg6 Qe5 27. Nf5 Rxd1+ 28. Kxd1 Bb7 29. Nxe7 $18) (24... Qxd4 25. Qxd4 Rxd4 26. Rxd4 gxh6 27. Rg1+ Kf8 28. Rf4+ $18) 25. Qe4! {The most efficient. The black pieces are not coordinated, and soon they will all be hanging.} Qxh6+ 26. Kb1 Ra7 {The pieces of the defender are badly scatered on the board with no common purpose. The final execution of White's attack will be brutal and impressive.} (26... Rxd4 27. Rxd4 Rb8 28. Qe5 Ra8 29. Qd5 Rb8 30. Qd8+! Nf8 (30... Bxd8 31. Rxd8+ Nf8 32. e7+ $18) 31. Qxe7 $18) 27. Nf5 Rxd1+ 28. Rxd1 Qf6 29. Rd7 {'!' A smart choice, but Chess beauty on the highest level and a modern form of small combination!} (29. Rd8+ $18) ({and} 29. Rg1 $18 {are also clearly winning for White.}) 29... Bxd7 30. exd7+ Kf8 {The choice for the defender is not always so deeply rotten as in this moment. At least both ways of going down have undeniable charm...} (30... Kh8 31. Nxe7 (31. Qd5 $18) 31... Rxd7 32. Qa8+ Nf8 33. Qxf8+! Qxf8 34. Ng6+ Kh7 35. Nxf8+ $18) 31. Qd5 {and Black resigned. What a nice victory by Carlsen in attacking style!} 1-0

