1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. a3 Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 Nc6 {A rare continuation (with the idea of ...e6-e5), to which White replies with a genuine novelty on move 6!} 6. e4!? $146 {[%mdl 8] Practice had seen:} (6. Bg5 e5!? 7. e3 h6 8. Bh4 Qe7 9. Nf3 d6 10. Be2 g5 11. Bg3 Ne4 12. Rc1 f5 {(..) 1/2 Stahlberg,G-Porat,Y/Warsaw 1935/EXT 2004}) (6. Nf3 d6!? 7. Nd2 e5 8. e4?! exd4 9. cxd4 Nxd4 10. Bb2 Ne6 11. Bd3 b6 12. O-O Bb7 13. Re1 O-O $15 {1/2 Kuzminykh,E-Bronstein,D/Leningrad 1970/EXT 2001 (25)}) (6. e3!? e5 7. Bd3 O-O {leads to a position from the 5...0-0 system}) (6. f3 e5 (6... d6 7. e4 O-O {leads to the 5...0-0 system}) (6... b6 7. e4 Ba6 {leads to a popular position from the 5...b6 system (6.f3 Ba6 7.e4 Nc6).}) 7. e4 d6 8. Be3 b6 9. Bd3 Na5 10. Ne2 Ba6 11. Ng3 h6 (11... O-O $142 {- 5...0-0}) 12. O-O Qd7 13. Qe2 O-O-O?! 14. Nf5! Rh7 15. c5! $16 {1-0 Lazarev,E-Romantchouk,J/Ternopol 1964/EXT 1999 (22)}) 6... Nxe4 7. Qg4 f5 (7... Nf6 8. Qxg7 Rg8 9. Qh6 e5!? {needs testing.}) 8. Qxg7 Qf6 9. Qxf6 Nxf6 {This position presents a typical positional conflict: White has a bishop pair with an asymmetrical pawn structure (which should favour him) but his own pawn chain is damaged (doubled pawns). Such positions are very hard to evaluate; usually the better player prevails.} 10. Nf3 b6 11. d5!? {A curious pawn sacrifice, which Black, however, rejects.} Na5 (11... exd5 12. cxd5 Nxd5 13. Bd3!? (13. Bc4 Nce7) 13... O-O 14. Bc4 Ne7 15. Bg5 Kg7 16. O-O-O $44) 12. Nd4 Kf7 13. dxe6+ dxe6 14. Bf4 Ba6 {Black keeps the tension. He had a good chance to simplify the game:} (14... e5! 15. Bxe5 Re8 16. f4 Ng4 17. O-O-O (17. Be2 Nxe5 18. fxe5 Rxe5 19. O-O c5! 20. Bf3 Rb8 21. Nb5 Kf6 22. Nxa7 Bb7 $15) (17. Nf3 Nc6 $15) 17... Nf2! (17... Nxe5 18. fxe5 Rxe5 19. Bd3 $36 {[%csl Rf7]}) 18. Be2 Nxh1 19. Bh5+ Kf8 20. Rxh1 Bd7! (20... Re7 $143 21. Bf3 Bb7 22. Bxb7 Nxb7 23. Nxf5 $44) 21. Bxe8 Rxe8 $15 {/=}) 15. Nf3 Ne4 16. Ne5+ Kf6 17. f3 (17. h4!? Nxc3 18. Rh3 Ne4 19. Re3 $44 {[%cal Yg2g4]}) 17... Nd6 18. O-O-O Rhd8?! {Again avoiding drawish lines like} (18... Naxc4 19. Nxc4 (19. Rxd6 Nxe5! 20. Bxe5+ Kxe5 21. Rc6 Bxf1 22. Rxf1 Kf6 23. Re1 Rae8 24. Rxc7 Re7 25. Rc6 h5 $11) 19... Bxc4 (19... Nxc4 20. Rd4 e5 21. Bxc4 Bxc4 22. Rxc4 exf4 $11) 20. Rxd6! Bxf1 21. Rd7 Bb5 22. Rxc7 Rac8 $11) (18... Ndxc4?! 19. Bxc4 Nxc4 20. Rhe1 Nd6 21. Nc6 $44) (18... Bxc4?! 19. Rxd6 cxd6 20. Nxc4 Nxc4 21. Bxc4 Rac8 22. Bb3 Rxc3+ 23. Kb2 Rd3 24. Rc1 $132) ({To complicate the game,} 18... Rhg8!? {was probably more appropriate; if} 19. h4 {then} Bxc4 {gains in strength.}) ({Or the mysterious} 18... Rac8!? 19. c5 Bxf1 20. Rhxf1 Nb3+ 21. Kc2 Nxc5 22. Rfe1 $44) 19. h4! {[%mdl 160] Now White obtains pressure on the kingside.} Nf7 20. Nd7+ Kg7 (20... Ke7? 21. Bg5+! Nxg5 22. hxg5 $16) 21. Rh3! (21. g4!? Bxc4 22. Bh3 $36) 21... Kh8! 22. Bg5?! (22. Bxc7 $142 Rdc8 23. Bf4 Nxc4 24. Rd4! $16 {[%cal Yh3g3]} (24. Rg3 e5! 25. Nxe5 Nfxe5 26. Bxe5+ Nxe5 27. Bxa6 Rxc3+ $11) 24... e5 $140 ? 25. Bxc4! Bxc4 26. Nxe5 $18) 22... Nxg5 23. hxg5 Kg7 (23... Bxc4 24. g6 (24. Nf6 Rxd1+ 25. Kxd1 Kg7 26. Rxh7+ Kg6) (24. Bxc4!? Nxc4 25. f4 Kg7 26. Rh6 $16 {-23...Kg7}) 24... h6! 25. Bxc4 Nxc4 26. Rxh6+ Kg7 27. Rh7+ Kxg6 28. Rdh1 $40) 24. Rh6! Bxc4 25. Bxc4 Nxc4 26. Rdh1 (26. f4!?) 26... Rh8 27. f4?! (27. Rxe6 Rae8 28. Rc6 Re7 (28... Nxa3 29. Nf6 $16 Re7 $140 30. Nd5 Rd7 31. Nxc7 $18 {[%cal Rc1b2]}) 29. Nf6 Ne5 30. Nh5+ Kf8 31. Rf6+! Rf7 32. Rh6 $16 {[%cal Yh5f4]}) 27... c5?! (27... Rad8 $142 28. Nf6 c5 (28... Rd3? 29. Rxh7+ Rxh7 30. Rxh7+ Kg6 31. Rh6+ Kf7 32. Nh5 {[%cal Rg5g6]} Rxc3+ 33. Kb1 Rb3+ 34. Kc2 $18) 29. Rxh7+ $140 ({maybe it is better not to hurry:} 29. a4) 29... Rxh7 30. Rxh7+ Kg6 31. Rh6+ Kf7 32. Nh5 Rg8! {- Black holds on.}) 28. Rxe6 Rae8 29. Rxh7+ (29. Rxe8!? {was also good:} Rxe8 30. Nf6 Re3 (30... Rh8 31. Rh6 {etc.}) 31. Rxh7+ Kg6 32. Rh6+ Kf7 33. Nh5 Kg8 34. Rg6+ (34. g6 Re1+ 35. Kc2 Re6! {-that's the point!}) 34... Kf8 35. Rf6+ Kg8 (35... Ke7 36. g6) 36. g6 {[%cal Rg6g7]} Re1+ $8 37. Kc2 Rh1 38. Ng3 Ne3+ 39. Kd3 Nd5 40. Rc6! Rd1+ 41. Kc4 Ne3+ 42. Kb5 $18) 29... Rxh7 30. Rxe8 {[%mdl 4096]} Kf7 (30... Nxa3 31. Ne5 {[%cal Yg2g4]}) (30... Rh1+ 31. Kc2 Nxa3+ 32. Kb3 Nb1 33. Re7+ $18) 31. Ra8 Rh1+ 32. Kc2 a5 (32... Nxa3+ 33. Kb3 Nb5 34. Ne5+ Kg7 (34... Ke7 35. g6 Kf6 36. Rf8+ Kg7 37. Rf7+ Kh8 38. g4! $18) 35. g4! fxg4 36. f5 {, and White's connected passed pawns decide the game.}) (32... Ke6 33. Ne5! Nxe5 34. Re8+ $18 {e.g.} Kd5 35. Rxe5+ Kc4 36. g4! fxg4 37. g6 Rh8 38. g7 Rg8 39. Rg5 b5 40. Kd2 a5 41. Rxg4 Kd5 42. Kd3 $18 {}) 33. Ra7 (33. Kd3 $142 ! b5 (33... Nb2+ 34. Ke2 Nc4 35. Rf8+ Ke7 36. g6 $18) 34. Ra7) 33... Nxa3+ 34. Kd2! Rh2? (34... Nb5 35. Rb7 Nd6 {was the last chance. A possible line:} 36. Ne5+ Kg8 37. Rxb6 Ne4+ 38. Kd3 Rd1+ (38... Rc1 39. Kc4 {[%cal Yc4d5,Yd5e6]} a4 40. Rb8+ Kg7 41. Rb7+ Kg8 42. Ra7 Nxc3 43. g3 $22 Kf8 (43... a3 44. Nd3 Rc2 45. Kb3) 44. Ng6+ Ke8 45. Nh4 Ne4+ 46. Kd5 Nxg3 47. Rxa4 $18) (38... a4 39. Kc4) 39. Kc4 Nd6+ 40. Kxc5 Ne4+ 41. Kb5 Nxc3+ (41... Rb1+ 42. Kxa5 Rxb6 43. Kxb6 Nxc3 44. Kc6 $18) 42. Kc6 Ne2 43. Rb8+ Kg7 44. Rb7+ Kg8 45. Ng6 Rd4 46. Ne7+ Kf8 47. g6 Rb4 48. Ra7 Nxf4 49. g3! Ne6 50. Nd5 {, and White should win.}) 35. Nxb6+ Kg6 36. Rxa5 Rxg2+ 37. Kd1 Nb1 (37... Nc2 38. Nd5) 38. Rxc5 {, and White converted his material advantage without major problems:} Nd2 39. Nd5 Ne4 40. Rc6+ Kf7 41. Ne3 Rg3 42. Ke2 Ke8 43. Re6+ Kf7 44. Re5 Nxc3+ 45. Kf2 Rh3 46. Rxf5+ Kg6 47. Rf6+ Kg7 48. Nf5+ Kg8 49. Kg2 Rd3 50. Rd6 Ne2 51. Rg6+ Kh8 52. Rh6+ Kg8 53. Ne7+ Kf7 54. Ng6 Kg7 55. Kf2 Nc3 56. Ne7 Ne4+ 57. Ke2 Ra3 58. Nf5+ Kg8 59. Re6 Nc3+ 60. Kf3 Nd5+ 61. Kg4 Ra1 62. Re5 Rg1+ 63. Kf3 Rf1+ 64. Kg2 1-0

