1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 {This is the continuation of the theoretical duel between the same players initiated at Sofia 2009.} 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 Bg7 11. Bd3 Ne7 12. Nxe7 Qxe7 13. c4 f5 14. O-O O-O 15. Qh5 Rb8 16. exf5 e4 17. Rae1 Bb7 18. Qg4 Rfe8 19. cxb5 d5 20. bxa6 Bc6 21. b3 Kh8 22. Nc2 Bc3!? $146 {The bishop occupies a dominating position and this will be characteristic for Black's position in general. Despite his huge material advantage, White will have little breathing space at his disposal and will find no active plan at all.} ({In the previous game, Shirov,A (2745)-Carlsen,M (2770)/Sofia 2009/CBM 131/[Rogozenco] (1-0, 30),} 22... Be5 {was played. It should be mentioned that in the Sofia game, the novelty also belonged to Carlsen: 21...h8 instead of the previously played 21...d7.}) 23. Qh3 Qf6 24. Be2 d4 {Black is not interested in trading his active bishop for the passive white rooks.} 25. Bc4 Rg8 26. g3 Rbe8 27. Rd1 e3 {All the pieces were placed on the best squares and time has come for pawn breaks.} 28. fxe3 dxe3 29. Be2 Re4 30. Qg2 Re7 {White has no reasonable way to avoid the repetition.} 31. Qh3 Re4 32. Qg2 Re7 33. Qh3 1/2-1/2

