1. Nf3 {Bu chooses the most solid way to start a game.} e6 2. c4 d5 {Many strong players did not like Magnus' opening choice. According to them, he should have gone for something non-symmetrical and much more non-theoretical.} 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 c6 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Qc2 {Now we are in the main line of the Meran. Although it is well known for being a complex opening, White has many ways to go for a very safe and uncomplicated position.} b6 (6... Bd6 {is the main line.}) 7. Be2 Bb7 8. O-O Bd6 9. e4 Nxe4 10. Nxe4 dxe4 11. Qxe4 Nf6 12. Qc2 {White is threatening c5.} c5! {A good move by Magnus who has activated his light squared bishop, which is more often than not a problem in this opening.} 13. Qa4+ Nd7 14. Rd1 O-O?! (14... Qc7! $15 {Magnus was not on the top of his game. Otherwise he would have surely chosen this move.}) 15. Ne5! $16 {Once Bu Xiangzhi made this move, he became very comfortable about his position.} (15. dxc5 Nxc5 $15) 15... Nf6 16. dxc5 bxc5 17. Nd7! {A move which Magnus most probably missed.} Ne4 18. Rxd6! (18. Nxf8? Bxh2+ 19. Kxh2 Qh4+ 20. Kg1 Qxf2+ 21. Kh2 Nd2 $19) 18... Nxd6 19. Nxf8 Qxf8 20. Be3?! (20. Bd3 $14 {A highly pleasant position for White, who not only has the bishop pair, but also a superior pawn structure. This move stops f5-d4.}) 20... Nf5! $11 21. Rd1 Nxe3 22. fxe3 {Although the position is equal now, Black is in big trouble because he is in a must win situation and with every exchange, his chances diminishing.} h6 23. Rd7 Be4 24. Rxa7 Rb8 25. b3 Qd8 26. Qd7 Qf6 27. Rc7 Qa1+ 28. Kf2 Kh7 {Magnus gets desperate. But how else is he going to create his chances?} 29. Qxf7 Qb2 30. Qf4 (30. Qxe6 Rf8+ 31. Rf7 $16) 30... Bd3 31. Rxg7+! {Of course, Bu Xiangzhi had seen this from quite a distance.} Kxg7 32. Qc7+ Kg6 33. Qg3+ {There is no way to get out of the perpetual.} 1/2-1/2

