1. e4 e5 2. Bc4 Nf6 3. d3 Nc6 4. Nf3 Be7 5. O-O O-O 6. Bb3!? {A comparatively rare variation.} ({The main line goes} 6. Re1 d6 7. a4) 6... d6 {I may be wrong, but after this slow move both players were on their own, without previous home preparation.} ({The alternative is} 6... d5 {in the spirit of the Marshall attack:} 7. exd5 Nxd5 8. h3 a5 9. a4 (9. a3 Nd4 {Radjabov,T (2740)-Ponomariov,R (2733) Medias 2010 CBM 137 [Rogozenco,D] (½-½, 30)}) 9... Nd4 {Karjakin,S (2723)-Gelfand,B (2758) Khanty-Mansiysk 2009 CBM 134 [Gelfand,B] (0-1, 33)}) 7. c3 Be6 {A natural developing move, but in the long run the bishop may be exposed to the threat d3-d4-d5.} ({Playing in the spirit of the Chigorin Ruy Lopez looks entirely viable:} 7... Na5 8. Bc2 c5 9. d4 (9. h3 Nc6 10. Re1 Qc7 11. Nbd2 Be6 12. Nf1 Nd7 13. Ng5 Bxg5 14. Bxg5 d5 $132 {Bortnyk,O (2588)-Wang,H (2712) Abu Dhabi 2016 (0-1, 23)}) 9... cxd4 10. cxd4 Nc6 11. d5 Nb4 12. Bb3 a5 13. a3 Na6 14. Bc2 Bd7 15. Bd2 a4 16. Re1 b5 $11 {Tiviakov,S (2639)-Nikolic,P (2601) Leiden 2015 (0-1, 48)}) 8. Re1 (8. Bxe6 fxe6 9. Qb3 Qc8 {stops the white initiative for instance} 10. Ng5?! Nd8) 8... Qd7 9. Nbd2 Rab8!? $146 {A mysterious move. At first I thought it was aimed at defending b7, but it looks more probable that it was a way of preparing ..b7-b5 in one of the lines below.} ({After the natural} 9... Rad8 {Black has to reckon with} 10. d4 $146 exd4 11. cxd4 Bg4 ({The discussion about the b7-pawn does not make sense. After} 11... Bxb3 12. Qxb3 d5 13. e5 Ne4 14. Nxe4 dxe4 15. Rxe4 Qd5 {with just some compensation, the rook is needed on d8, of course.}) 12. Ba4! $14) (9... Bxb3 10. Qxb3 (10. axb3 a5!?) 10... Nd8 $146 {[%cal Gc7c6,Gd8e6] looks like the most flexible way of freeing Black's position. For instance} 11. d4 exd4 12. cxd4 d5 13. e5 Nh5 14. g3 c6 {The control on the light squares offers Black good manoeuvring possibilities.}) ({What is not very clear to me is why did Black prefer the game move over} 9... a6 {with similar ideas.}) 10. Bc2 (10. d4 {runs into} exd4 11. cxd4 Bg4 12. h3 (12. Ba4 b5!) 12... Bh5 {and White does not have adequate ways of maintaining his central pawns intact.} 13. Qc2 (13. g4 Nxg4 14. hxg4 Qxg4+ 15. Kh1 Nxd4 16. Re3 Qf4 17. Kg2 Bg5 18. Rd3 Qg4+ 19. Kf1 Qh3+ 20. Kg1 Bxd2 21. Nxd4 Qxd3 22. Qxd2 Qxe4 $19) 13... Na5 $11) (10. Nf1 {looks like a constructive alternative.}) 10... d5!? {Very provocative and eventually successful!} 11. h3 ({I assume Carlsen considered that after the imemdiate pawn grabbing} 11. exd5 Bxd5 12. Nxe5 ({Actually I find Black's position a bit artificial after} 12. h3 Bd6 13. Nf1) 12... Nxe5 13. Rxe5 Bd6 14. Re1 Rbe8 {offers Black too much initiative, partly because of the lack of defence of the g4-square. Here are a couple of lines sustaining this point of view.} 15. Nf1 (15. Nc4 Qg4! $19) 15... Qg4 16. Qxg4 Bxh2+! 17. Kh1 (17. Nxh2 Rxe1+ 18. Nf1 Nxg4 $19) (17. Kxh2 Nxg4+ 18. Kg3 Rxe1 $19) 17... Nxg4 18. Rxe8 Rxe8 19. f3 Bd6 20. fxg4 Re2 $44 21. Ba4!! {[%csl Gc6]} (21. Bb3 Bc6! 22. d4 Be4!) 21... Rxg2 22. Ne3 Rh2+ 23. Kg1 Rh1+ 24. Kf2 Rh2+ {with a perpetual.}) 11... h6 {Preventing g5 and reckoning with the fact that after h2-h3 Black gets attacking chances in another way than in the lines above.} 12. exd5 {Picking up the gauntlet. Being a world champion creates certain obligations.} Nxd5 13. Nxe5 Nxe5 14. Rxe5 Bd6 15. Re1 Bxh3! {Due to his advance in development, Black gets full compensation for the sacrifced piece.} 16. gxh3 Qxh3 17. Nf1 {The most consistent move.} (17. Qf3 {would have allowed (and forced!) a draw by perpetual:} Bh2+ 18. Kh1 Bg3+) (17. Nf3? {runs into problems:} Rbe8 18. Bd2 (18. d4 Rxe1+ 19. Qxe1 Qxf3) (18. Rxe8 Rxe8 19. d4 Re6 $19 {[%cal Gf7f5,Ge6g6]}) 18... Rxe1+ 19. Bxe1 Nf4 $19) 17... Rbe8 {Black's play flows naturally, in a similar way to the Marshall attack main lines. For White, there are small choices on each step and the correct answer is not always obvious. This slightly speaks against Carlsen's decision to allow the sacrifice.} 18. d4 (18. Bb3 c6 19. Bd2 Re6 20. Rxe6 fxe6 21. Qe1 Rf6 22. Qe4 Kf7 $44) 18... f5 19. Bb3 c6 20. f4 Kh7 (20... g5!? {, immedoately putting pressure on f4, may have been even stronger:} 21. Re2 ({The point is that} 21. fxg5? {clears the fourth rank for the rook:} Re4 $19) 21... Kg7 22. Rf2 Nxf4 23. Bxf4 Bxf4 24. Qf3 Qxf3 25. Rxf3 Re7 26. Rd1 h5 {Black has only two pawns for the piece but the compact kingside mass is threatening.}) {Black has a strong attack for the piece and White faces the threat on f4. Carlsen decides on parrying it in a simplistic way.} 21. Bxd5? {This bishop was important because it now only was ready taking on d5 when really needed but also defended the queen.} (21. Re2! {was the only way of staying out of troubles. Black would have retained compensation for the piece, even though not necessarily entirely adequate.} Nxf4 (21... Rxe2 22. Qxe2 Rf6 23. Bxd5 cxd5 24. Nh2 Rg6+ 25. Kh1 Rg4 26. Bd2 Bxf4 27. Bxf4 Rxf4 28. Rf1 $44) (21... Qg4+ 22. Rg2 $16 {[%cal Gb3d1]}) 22. Rh2 Qg4+ 23. Qxg4 fxg4 $44 {/}) 21... cxd5 22. Re3 (22. Re2 {does not work anymore:} Qg4+ {[%csl Gd1]} 23. Kf2 g5 24. fxg5 Re4 $40) 22... Rxe3 23. Bxe3 g5 $19 {Being incompletely developed, White is helpless now against the combined attack of Black's pieces and pawns.} 24. Kf2 (24. fxg5 f4 25. Qc2+ Rf5 {[%cal Gf4f3]} (25... Kh8 26. Qg6) 26. Bf2 f3 27. Be3 Kh8 28. c4 Qg4+ 29. Kh1 Qh4+ 30. Kg1 f2+ 31. Bxf2 Rxg5+) 24... gxf4 25. Qf3 (25. Bd2 Qh4+ 26. Kf3 Rg8 $19) 25... fxe3+ 26. Nxe3 Qh2+ 27. Kf1 Rg8 {Black is attacking with equal material already.} 28. Qxf5+ Rg6! ({The only way to avoid the repetition. If} 28... Kh8 29. Qf6+ Rg7 30. Nf5 $11 {it is Black already who needs to look for a perpetual.}) 29. Ke1 h5?! {This could have spoiled the win, as it is by one tempo too slow.} ({Black should have unpinned the rook at once. He would escape from perpetual with a curious manoeuvre:} 29... Kg7! 30. Qd7+ Kh8 31. Qd8+ Kh7 32. Qd7+ Rg7 33. Qf5+ Kh8 34. Qc8+ Rg8 $19) 30. Kd1? ({Missing his chance with:} 30. Rd1! {Good or bad, White needs to complete his development. Even great players tend to forget simple things under the pressure.} Kh6 31. Rd2 Rg1+ 32. Nf1 Qh4+ 33. Rf2 {and White is entirely safe already.}) 30... Kh6 31. Nc2 h4 {The rest is agony. There is no perpetual, the white king is in danger and the h-pawn unstoppable.} 32. Ne1 h3 33. Nf3 Qg2 34. Ne1 Qg4+ 35. Qxg4 Rxg4 36. Nf3 Rg1+!? (36... Rg1+ 37. Nxg1 h2) 0-1

