1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Nd5 Be7 10. Bxf6 Bxf6 11. c3 Bg5 12. Nc2 Ne7 {This line became the main alternative for Black to the classical 12...0-0 and 12...b8. Its theory has developed very quickly in the past 4-5 years. Carlsen and even Jakovenko with the black pieces have used it already.} 13. Ncb4 ({Another possibility is to include the moves h4/h6, which has advantages and drawbacks for White. Here is the most recent example:} 13. h4 Bh6 14. a4 bxa4 15. Ncb4 O-O 16. Qxa4 Nxd5 17. Nxd5 a5 18. Bb5 (18. Bc4 {offers Black counterplay due to the weaknesses created on the kingside by the move 13.h4:} Kh8 19. g3 f5 20. exf5 Bxf5 21. O-O Be4 $132) 18... Be6 19. Bc6 Rb8 20. b4 axb4 21. cxb4 Kh8 ({The main alternative is} 21... Bxd5 22. Bxd5 Qb6) 22. b5 Bxd5 23. Bxd5 Qb6 24. O-O ({In the event of} 24. Bc6 {Black has the nice transfer of the bishop to a better diagonal:} Be3! 25. O-O (25. fxe3 Qxe3+ 26. Kd1 Qd3+ 27. Ke1 Qe3+ {is a draw}) 25... Bd4 26. Ra2 f5 27. exf5 Rxf5 28. g3 Rbf8 {and thanks to the pressure on f2 Black has nothing to fear, Grischuk,A (2719)-Illescas Cordoba,M (2604)/Dresden 2008}) 24... Qxb5 25. Qxb5 Rxb5 26. Ra6 $146 ({In Bacrot,E (2705)-Eljanov,P (2720)/Elista 2008 White took another pawn, but it led to the same result:} 26. Bxf7 g5 27. hxg5 Bxg5 28. Be6 Rb4 29. Bf5 d5 30. Ra5 dxe4 31. Rxe5 Bf4 32. Rxe4 Rxe4 33. Bxe4 Rc8 34. Bf3 Rc1 35. Bd1 Kg7 {and White cannot unpin the bishop without exchanging the rooks, which leads to a completely drawish position.}) 26... f6 27. Rxd6 Rc5 28. h5 (28. Rd7 Rc1 29. Rxc1 Bxc1 {looks slightly unpleasant for Black, but he simply waits and the position is a draw.}) 28... Rc7 29. g3 g6 30. Kg2 Kg7 {With the king on g7 (instead of being cut on h8) Black doesn't have even the slightest problem to achieve a draw.} 31. Kh3 Bc1 32. f4 exf4 33. gxf4 g5 34. fxg5 Bxg5 35. e5 Re7 36. e6 f5 37. Bc6 Kh6 38. Bf3 {½-½ Shirov,A-Radjabov,T/Bazna 2009}) 13... O-O 14. a4 bxa4 15. Qxa4 (15. Rxa4 a5 16. Nxe7+ (16. Bb5 {is a different story here and if Black wishes, he can force complete equality:} Bd7 17. Nxe7+ (17. Bxd7 axb4!) 17... Bxe7 18. Nc6 (18. Bxd7 axb4 $11) 18... Qe8 19. Qd5 Be6 20. Qd3 Bd7 21. Nxe7+ Qxe7 $11) 16... Qxe7 17. Bc4 Bd7 18. Nd5 Qe8 19. Ra2 Bd8 20. O-O Rc8 21. Bb3 Rb8 $11 {0-1 Smeets,J (2550)-Carlsen,M (2625)/Wijk aan Zee 2006 (44)}) 15... Nxd5 (15... a5 16. Bb5 Nxd5 17. Nxd5 Be6 18. Bc6 Rb8 19. Ra2 Qc8 20. O-O Bd8 21. b4 Kh8 22. Rd2 Qa6 23. b5 $16 {1-0 Kamsky,G (2690)-Carlsen,M (2570)/Khanty Mansiysk 2005 (31)}) 16. Nxd5 Bd7 (16... a5 17. Bb5 {leads to the above mentioned game Kamsky-Carlsen, see 15...a5.}) 17. Qc2 {Keeping the queen in the centre looks like the optimal decision.} (17. Qa2 a5 18. Bd3 (18. Bc4 a4 19. O-O Rb8 20. Rfd1 Kh8 $14 {/=}) 18... Bc6 19. O-O Qb8 20. Bc4 Kh8 21. b3 f5 22. exf5 {½-½ Anand,V (2799)-Carlsen,M (2733)/Linares 2008}) (17. Qa5 Qb8 18. b4 Bd8 19. Qa2 a5 20. Bc4 Bc6 $132 {½-½ Berg,E (2586)-Carlsen,M (2690)/Faaborg 2007 (60)}) 17... a5 18. Bc4 {I believe this is the critical position of the line 12...e7. Although it is very close to equal, in fact White has a small plus, since he can play on without any major risk. His plan is to combine pressure on the d-file with play on the queenside (b2-b4, or pressure on the a-file first). In each case Black still must find a way to arrange optimally all his pieces (and first of all to find a job for his dark-squared bishop, which, being on the diagonal c1-h6, can easily be out of any queenside action).} Rc8 $146 (18... a4 19. O-O Qa5 20. Nb4 (20. Qe2 Qc5 21. Ra3 Kh8 22. Rfa1 Rac8 23. Rxa4!? Bxa4 24. Rxa4 f5 25. Bd3 Ra8 26. Rxa8 Rxa8 27. g3 $44 {1-0 Sjugirov,S (2395)-Gladyszev,O (2485)/Lipetsk 2007 (62)}) 20... Qc5 21. Bd5 Ra7 22. Nd3 Qb6 23. Rfd1 Kh8 24. c4 Qb8 25. Qc3 Rc8 26. c5!? (26. Bxf7 Bg4 27. Bd5 Bxd1 28. Rxd1 $13) 26... Bb5 27. c6 $13 {0-1 Korneev,O (2657)-Jakovenko,D (2671)/Pamplona 2006 (67)}) (18... Kh8 19. O-O f5 20. exf5 Bxf5 21. Bd3 $14) (18... Qb8 19. O-O Rc8 20. Qe2 Qb7 21. b3 Be6 22. Ra2 Bd8 23. Rfa1 Kf8 24. h3 Qc6 25. Bb5 Qb7 26. Bc4 Qc6 27. Bb5 $14 {½-½ Lukin,A (2423)-Shariyazdanov,A (2594)/St Petersburg 2001}) (18... Bc6 19. O-O Qb8 20. Ra3 Qb7 21. Rfa1 (21. b3!?) 21... a4 22. Re1 Ra5 23. Qe2 Bd8 24. Raa1 g6 25. Kh1 Kg7 26. Rad1 a3 27. b4 $14 {½-½ Korneev,O (2613)-Cherniaev,A (2504)/Catalan Bay 2003 (45)}) (18... Rb8 19. Qe2 a4 20. O-O Qc8 21. Nb4 Bd8 22. Rfd1 Bb6 23. Nd5 Qd8 24. Nxb6 Rxb6 {½-½ Illescas Cordoba,M (2598)-San Segundo Carrillo,P (2536)/Ayamonte 2007}) 19. Qe2 Kh8 20. O-O f5 21. exf5 Bxf5 22. Bd3! Be6 (22... e4? 23. Bxe4 Re8 24. f3 $16) 23. Be4 Rb8 24. Qc2 Bg8 ({Perhaps Black must accept an inferior position by playing immediately} 24... Bxd5 25. Bxd5 Qb6 26. b3 Qc5 $14 {since now after} 27. Be4 {he has} d5! 28. Bxh7 e4 29. Bg6 Rb6 30. Bh5 Bf6 $44 {But instead of 27.e4 better is 27.ad1, with advantage for White.}) 25. b3! $14 {Jakovenko shows that although Carlsen completed his plan (f7-f5), he is still far from complete equality. White is slowly improving, while Black doesn't have any counterplay. Exchanging on d5 would lose completely Black's control over the light squares and protecting h7 will involve creating further weaknesses.} (25. Rfd1 Bxd5! 26. Rxd5 ({or} 26. Bxd5 Qb6 $11) 26... Qb6 $11 {[%csl Rb2,Rf2][%cal Gf8f2,Gg5e3] and Black equalizes in view of the double attack (xf2 followed by Ae3 is also a threat).}) 25... Rb5 26. c4 Rb8 27. Nc3? {Letting it slip away. Jakovenko plans 28.d5, but he misses that Black can avoid the exchange of light-squared bishops.} ({After the correct} 27. Rfd1 {Black starts to have real problems, since it is not clear what to do. The queen from d8 cannot move because it must protect the pawn on a5, the bishop on g8 must protect h7. The rooks and the bishop on g5 don't have any meaningful moves either. White can play next d3 and then either double on the d-file or play h3 or c3. In any case after 27.fd1 Carlsen's position would have been quite unpleasant. For instance} Bxd5 28. Rxd5 Qb6 {loses a pawn after} 29. Rb5 Qd4 30. Rd1) 27... Qb6! {Immediately exploiting the drawback of White's previous move.} 28. Rab1 h6! 29. Bd5 Bh7 {The point. White cannot exchange the bishops. As the result of 27.c3 Black has succeeded in relocating his queen.} 30. Be4 Bg8 31. g3 (31. Nd5 Qc5 {is a different story now. The queen is much better placed on c5 than on d8. Additionally, Black has the plan d8-b6, equalizing completely.}) 31... Qc5 32. Kg2 Bd8 {Now the position is equal.} 33. Rbd1 Qb4 34. Rb1 Bb6 35. Nd5 Bxd5 36. Bxd5 Bd4 {White's only chance to set at least some problems is to place the queen on e4 and the bishop on c2 or d3, but he doesn't have time to achieve a good version of such a rearrangement.} 37. Be4 Qc3 {Black understandably wants to force the exchange of queens, but Jakovenko finds a way to avoid it.} (37... Rf6 $11) 38. Qe2! (38. Qxc3 Bxc3 $11) 38... Rf6! (38... Rxb3 {requres from Black after} 39. Qg4 Rxb1 40. Qg6 {to find some computer-study-like ways to save himself, which is of course completely unnecessary for him.}) 39. Bd3 Qb4! {A clever decision. Carlsen realized that the plan with c3 led Black nowhere and he rightly brings the queen back.} (39... Rxb3? 40. Rxb3 Qxb3 41. Qe4 g6 42. Rb1 $36) 40. h4 (40. Qe4 Qb7 $11) (40. f3 Qb7 $11) 40... Qb7+ 41. Be4 Qf7 $11 42. Bd5 Qg6 43. Be4 (43. h5 Qg5 $132 {[%csl Rf2,Rh5][%cal Gb8f8]}) 43... Qf7 44. Bd5 Qg6 45. Be4 1/2-1/2

