1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 e6 4. O-O Nge7 5. c3 a6 6. Ba4 b5 7. Bc2 Bb7 8. Qe2 d5 9. e5 d4 10. Be4 Qb6 {The queen move aspires to improve on the undesirable score, that used to be achieved by the seemingly more logical knight move.} (10... Nd5 11. d3 Be7 (11... dxc3 12. Nxc3 (12. bxc3 Be7 13. c4 Nb6 14. a3 Na4 15. Bd2 O-O 16. Nc3 Nxc3 17. Bxc3 Qd7 18. Rfb1 $14 {½-½ Svidler,P (2713)-Grischuk,A (2701)/Togliatti 2003 (41)}) 12... Nxc3 (12... Be7 13. Rd1 O-O 14. d4 Nxc3 15. bxc3 cxd4 16. cxd4 Na5 17. Bxb7 Nxb7 18. h4 Na5 19. Bg5 $11 {½-½ Rublevsky,S (2645)-Eljanov,P (2639)/Saint Vincent 2005 (72)}) (12... Qd7 13. Rd1 Be7 14. d4 Nxd4 15. Nxd4 cxd4 16. Nxd5 exd5 17. Rxd4 O-O 18. Bd3 Rfe8 19. Bd2 g6 20. Rg4 $11 {½-½ Rublevsky,S (2644)-Moiseenko,A (2597)/Moscow 2003 (42)}) 13. bxc3 Be7 14. d4 cxd4 15. cxd4 Qd7 (15... Rb8 16. Rd1 Nb4 17. Bd2 Nd5 18. Qd3 g6 19. Bh6 $11 {0-1 Hracek,Z (2613)-Krasenkow,M (2661)/Ostrava 2007 (47)}) 16. a4 bxa4 17. Rxa4 O-O 18. Bd2 a5 19. Rfa1 h6 20. h4 Ba6 21. Qe3 $11 {1-0 Morozevich,A (2742)-Van Wely,L (2697)/Monte Carlo 2002 (81)}) 12. c4! {The key idea indicating, that the central location of the knight has also darker sides.} bxc4 (12... Nb6 13. Bf4 b4 (13... Qd7 14. Na3 b4 15. Nc2 O-O-O 16. a3 a5 17. axb4 axb4 18. Ng5 Rdf8 19. Qh5 Qe8 20. Bf3 $16 {1-0 Gaehler,M (2223)-Martinovic,S (2400)/Szeged 2007 (45)}) 14. Nbd2 Kd7 15. Nb3 a5 16. a3 a4 17. axb4 axb3 18. b5 Qc7 19. bxc6+ Bxc6 20. Nd2 $16 {1-0 Burnett,R (2437)-Nguyen Anh Dung (2496)/Budapest 2000 (52)}) 13. dxc4 Nb6 14. Ne1 Qc7 (14... Rb8 15. Nd2 Qc7 16. Nd3 a5 17. f4 Nb4 18. Nxb4 axb4 19. a4 Bxe4 20. Nxe4 O-O $14 {1-0 Fedorchuk,S (2592)-Ejsmont,K (2348)/Ustron 2006 (40)}) 15. Nd2 (15. b3 Nd7 16. f4 O-O-O 17. Nd3 Rdg8 18. Nd2 h6 19. Bf3 g5 20. Ne4 Nb4 21. Nxb4 cxb4 $11 {1-0 Tolstikh,N (2405)-Michalek,J (2300)/Pardubice 1995 (32)}) 15... Nd7 16. Nd3 Nb4 17. Nf3 Bxe4 18. Qxe4 Qc6 19. Qe2 Nxd3 20. Qxd3 $11 {1-0 Gu Xiaobing (2339)-Yu,L (2328)/Lishui 2009 (71)}) 11. d3 (11. cxd4 Nxd4 12. Nxd4 cxd4 13. d3 Bxe4 14. Qxe4 Rc8 15. Nd2 Nc6 16. Nf3 Be7 17. Qg4 Kf8 18. Bd2 $16 {1-0 Cabrilo,G (2484)-Andrijevic,M (2402)/Sozina 2004 (47)}) 11... Rd8 12. a4 Nd5 {Once again this logical move might not be the most promising. In the future players may explore the intricacies of the dynamic solution with the exchange on the c3-square.} (12... dxc3!? 13. Nxc3 (13. bxc3 b4 14. Re1 (14. Nbd2 Nd5 $11) 14... b3 15. Nbd2 Na5 $13) 13... b4 14. a5 Qc7 15. Na4 Nxa5 16. Qc2 c4 17. Bxb7 b3 18. Qb1 Nxb7 19. dxc4 Qxc4 20. Be3 $14) 13. axb5 axb5 14. cxd4 (14. Bd2 Be7 15. Na3 O-O 16. c4 bxc4 17. Nxc4 Qc7 18. Ng5 $14) 14... cxd4 (14... Nxd4 15. Nxd4 cxd4 16. Bd2 (16. Nd2 Be7 17. Nb3 O-O 18. Bd2 Nb4 19. Rfc1 $14) 16... Rc8 17. Ba5 Qc6 18. Nd2 Qc2 19. Qg4 $14) 15. Nbd2 Nf4 (15... Be7 16. Nb3 h6 17. Bd2 b4 18. Rfc1 O-O 19. g3 $14) 16. Qd1 Nb4? {[%mdl 32] Radjabov neglects one of the main principles of strategy and jumps right into the conflict zone before finishing his own development.} (16... Nd5 17. Nb3 Be7 18. Bd2 Ndb4 (18... b4 19. Qe2 h6 $14) 19. Qe2 O-O 20. Ng5 (20. Rfc1 h6 21. g3 $14) 20... g6 21. Nxh7 Kxh7 22. Qh5+ Kg8 23. Bxg6 fxg6 24. Qxg6+ $11 {½-½ Pahud,C (2185)-Carron,J (2300)/Lausanne 2003 (29)}) (16... Ng6 17. Bxg6 (17. Re1 Bb4 18. Qb3 Nge7 $11) 17... hxg6 18. Ne4 Be7 19. Bg5 O-O 20. Qe2 $14) 17. Nb3! Bxe4 18. dxe4 Nfd3 (18... Ng6 19. Nfxd4 Nc6 20. Be3 Ncxe5 21. Ra5 $16) 19. Bg5 Rc8 {The necessity of the rook retreat does not bode well for Black's strategy.} (19... Be7 20. Bxe7 Kxe7 21. Qd2 Ke8 (21... h6 22. Nc1 $18) 22. Ra3 $16) 20. Nfxd4 Nxb2 (20... h6 21. Bd2 Nxb2 22. Qg4 $16) 21. Qe2 (21. Qh5 Nc4 22. Rfd1 h6 (22... Nxe5 23. Ra5 Nbc6 24. Rxb5 Qa6 25. Ra1 Ba3 26. Nc5 $18) (22... Na6) 23. Qh3 Na6 24. Be3 $14) 21... Nc4 22. Rfc1 Bc5 {It is not a big surprise, that despite the seemingly firm build up on the queenside the black pieces actually suffer from shaky legs.} (22... Be7 23. Nxb5! Qxb5 (23... Bxg5 24. Rxc4 $18) 24. Bxe7 Kxe7 25. Ra7+ Ke8 26. Ra5! Qb6 27. Rxc4 $16) 23. Nxb5 (23. Nxc5! Qxc5 24. Be3 Nc6 (24... Qxe5 25. Rcb1 Qd6 (25... Nxe3 26. fxe3 $18) 26. Nxb5 Qd3 27. Qg4 Nxe3 28. Qxg7 Rf8 29. Rxb4 Nc2 30. Nc7+ Kd7 31. Nxe6 $18) 25. Nxb5 Qxb5 26. Qxc4 Qxc4 27. Rxc4 O-O 28. Rac1 $16) 23... O-O? {Radjabov does not usually excel in the defence. This might be the reason why he missed the most stubborn way to resist Carlsen's activity after his relatively soft move.} (23... Qxb5 24. Ra5! Qxa5 25. Nxa5 Nxa5 26. Qb5+ Nac6 27. Rxc5 $18) (23... Bxf2+! 24. Qxf2 Qxb5 25. Nc5!? (25. Qd4 O-O 26. Be7 Rfe8 27. Bxb4 Qxb4 28. Na5 Qxa5 29. Rxa5 Nxa5 30. Rxc8 Rxc8 $16) (25. Qe2 O-O 26. Nd4 Qxe5! (26... Qc5 27. Be3 Rfd8 28. Nf5 Qf8 29. Rxc4 exf5 30. exf5 $16) 27. Rxc4 Rxc4 28. Qxc4 Qxg5 29. Qxb4 Qe3+ 30. Kh1 Qxe4 $16) 25... O-O! (25... h6 26. Be7!! Nxe5 (26... Kxe7 27. Ra7+ Kd8 28. Rxc4 $18) 27. Bd6 Nc4 28. Nxe6 fxe6 29. Ra7 Qb6 30. Bxb4 $18) 26. Be7 Rfe8 27. Bd6 Nxd6 28. exd6 Nd3! 29. Nxd3 Qxd3 30. e5 (30. Rd1 Ra8! 31. e5 Rxa1 32. Rxa1 Qc3 $14) 30... Rxc1+ 31. Rxc1 f6 $14 {Black would have kept excellent chances to hold the game.}) 24. Nxc5 Nxe5 (24... Rxc5 25. Be7 Qxb5 26. Bxc5 Qxc5 27. Qxc4 $18) 25. Be7 {The catastrophe is complete, Radjabov will lose loads of material and every right to continue with his resistance.} (25. Be7 Rfe8 26. Nd6 Rxc5 (26... Rxe7 27. Nxc8 $18) 27. Rxc5 Qxc5 28. Nxe8 Qxe7 29. Ra8 $18) 1-0

