1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Qc2 Bd6 7. g4 h6 8. Bd2 dxc4! {GM Lars Schandorff extensively deals with this system in his survey (CBM 125). And indeed, Black obtains a good position here.} 9. Bxc4 b5 10. Be2 Bb7 11. e4 Be7 12. g5 hxg5 13. Nxg5 b4 {Or else:} (13... Rc8 14. Rg1 Nf8 15. e5 b4 16. Na4 N6d7 17. Ne4 c5 18. Nexc5 Bxc5 19. Nxc5 Nxc5 20. dxc5 Qd4 21. Be3 Qxe5 22. Bb5+ Ke7 23. O-O-O $36 {1-0 Harika,D (2455)-Van den Bersselaar,J (2164)/Gibraltar 2008/CBM 122 Extra (26)}) (13... Kf8 14. Rg1 b4 15. Na4 c5 16. e5 Ne8 17. Nxf7! Kxf7 18. Qg6+ Kf8 19. Qxe6 cxd4 20. Qf5+ Ndf6 21. exf6 Nxf6 22. Nc5 Bd5 23. Rc1 $36 {1-0 Petrosian,T (2616)-Pruess,D (2425)/Las Vegas 2008/CBM 124 Extra (33)}) (13... Qb6 14. O-O-O Qxd4 15. Be3 Qb4 (15... Qe5 16. f4 Qb8 17. Rhg1 (17. Kb1! $36) 17... Bc5 18. Qd2 Qc7 19. Bxc5 Nxc5 20. e5 b4? (20... Nd5) 21. exf6 bxc3 22. Qd4! gxf6 23. Nh7 cxb2+ 24. Kb1 Nd7 25. Qxd7+ Qxd7 26. Nxf6+ Ke7 27. Nxd7 $18 {1-0 Miedema,R (2208)-Ris,R (2377)/Venlo 2007/EXT 2009 (39)}) 16. f4 a6 (16... Qa5 17. f5 e5 18. Rhg1 Bc5 19. Bxc5 Nxc5 20. Kb1 $36 Ncd7? 21. Qb3 Rf8 22. Ne6! fxe6 23. Bh5+ Kd8 24. fxe6 Nxh5 25. Rxd7+ Kc8 26. Qd1 Rh8 27. Qd6 $18 {1-0 Nielsen,P (2653)-Smeets,J (2518)/Warsaw 2005/CBM 108 (33)}) 17. f5 exf5 18. e5 Nxe5 19. Qxf5 Neg4 20. Bxg4 Qxg4 21. Qe5 Nd7 22. Rxd7 Qxd7 23. Rd1 Qg4 24. Qxg7 Rf8 25. Nce4 $44 {1/2 Nyback,T (2575)-Smeets,J (2525)/Germany 2007/CBM 118 (67)}) (13... c5!? {deserves serious attention:} 14. dxc5 (14. d5 $142 !? {, which needs practical tests}) 14... b4! {better than} (14... Nxc5 15. Bxb5+ Nfd7 (15... Kf8 16. O-O-O $36) 16. Rg1 a6 17. Be2 (17. e5 axb5! (17... Bxg5 18. Bxg5 Qc7 19. Bxd7+ Qxd7 20. Rd1 Qc7 21. Rd6 Rxh2 {1-0 Czakon,J (2484)-Savchenko,S (2508)/Montpellier 2006/CBM 113 ext (40)}) 18. Nxf7 Qb8! 19. Nxh8 Nxe5! $36 {[%csl Re1]} 20. O-O-O $140 Rxa2!! 21. Nxa2 Be4 22. Qc3 Ned3+ 23. Kb1 Nb4+ 24. Ka1 Nc2+ 25. Kb1 Nd4+ $19) 17... Ne5 18. Rd1 Ncd3+?! (18... Qc7 $13) 19. Kf1 Nxf2? (19... Rxh2 20. Be3 Bxg5 21. Rxg5 Qxg5 22. Bxg5 Rxf2+ 23. Kg1 Nf3+ 24. Bxf3 Rxc2 25. Rxd3 Rxb2 $14) 20. Kxf2 Bc5+ 21. Kg2 Qf6 22. Rgf1 Qh6 23. Bf4 Be3 24. Bxe5 Qxg5+ 25. Bg3 {1-0 Nyback,T (2634)-Kojima,S (2272)/Beijing 2008/CBM 126 Extra}) 15. Na4 Rh4! 16. c6 Rc8! 17. Bb5 (17. cxd7+ Nxd7) 17... a6 18. cxd7+ Nxd7 19. Bxd7+ Qxd7 20. Qb3 Qb5 21. Nc3 bxc3 22. bxc3 f6 23. Qxb5+ axb5 24. Nf3 Rxe4+ $19 {0-1 Epiney,F (2382)-Bubir,S (2398)/IECG 2007/CBM 125/[Schandorff] (41)}) 14. Na4 c5! {Even now this move is correct. Before the present encounter it had only been played in a computer-assisted game in a Freestyle event.} (14... Qa5 15. Rg1 c5 16. e5 cxd4 17. exf6 Bxf6 18. Rc1 (18. a3!) 18... Rxh2? (18... Rc8! $13) 19. Qc7 Qxc7 20. Rxc7 Bc8 21. Bxb4 $18 {1-0 Watson,J (2400)-Pozarek,S (2185)/Philadelphia 1993/EXT 1999 (43)}) (14... Rc8 15. e5 Nd5 16. Nxe6 fxe6 17. Qg6+ Kf8 18. Rg1 Bf6 19. exf6 Qxf6 20. Qg3 $16 e5 21. dxe5 Qxe5? 22. Nc5! Qxg3 23. Nxd7+ Ke8 24. Rxg3 Kxd7 25. Rxg7+ $18 {1-0 Jankovic,A (2525)-Sriram,J (2408)/Kavala 2006/CBM 113 ext (31)}) (14... Qc7 15. e5 (15. O-O-O!?) 15... Nd5 16. Rg1 Bxg5 17. Bxg5 Rxh2 18. Bg4 c5 19. Rc1 Kf8 (19... c4 20. Bxe6!) 20. Nxc5 Nxc5 21. Qxc5+ Qxc5 22. Rxc5 $14 {1-0 Nyback,T (2585)-Jaracz,P (2490)/Dresden 2007/CBM 118 (48)}) 15. Nxc5 (15. dxc5?! Rh4! {- see above 13...c5.}) 15... Nxc5 16. dxc5 Rc8 17. Qa4+ (17. Bxb4 Nd7 18. Bb5 O-O 19. Bxd7 Qxd7 20. h4 f6 21. Nf3 Qb5 22. Bc3 Rxc5 23. O-O-O e5 24. Rde1 Rc7 $44 {1/2 "Rybusia"-"Badbishop1"/playchess.com 2007 (64)}) 17... Bc6 18. Bb5 Bxb5 19. Qxb5+ Qd7 20. Qxd7+ Nxd7 21. Ke2 Rh4 22. Rac1 Rxc5 23. Rxc5 Bxc5 $15 {[%mdl 4096] So, Black has recaptured the sacrificed pawn and obtained a slightly better endgame due to the weakness of White's h- and a-pawns.} 24. f4?! (24. f3 {was probably more accurate (I.Ganiev).}) 24... e5! 25. h3 exf4 26. Nf3 Rh6 $17 {[%csl Ra2,Re4,Rh3]} 27. Bxf4 Re6 (27... Rg6!?) 28. Nd2 Bd4 29. Rb1 (29. a3 Bxb2 30. axb4 Bc3 {loses a pawn anyway}) 29... Ra6 30. a3!? (30. Kd3!? Bf6) 30... bxa3 31. bxa3 Rxa3 32. Nc4 Ra2+ 33. Kd3 Bf2 34. Bd6 {White has given up a pawn but activated his pieces, and it is not easy for Black to convert his material advantage.} Nb6 (34... a5 35. Rb7 Nc5+ 36. Bxc5 Bxc5 37. Ne5 $132) 35. Bb4 Nxc4 36. Kxc4 Bb6 37. Kd5 Rg2 38. Rb3 Rg5+ 39. e5 Bc7? {Exchanging the bishops is a wrong decision. Rook endgames are famous for their drawish tendencies, and this one is no exception.} (39... Rg1 $142 $17 {I.Ganiev}) 40. Bd6 Bxd6 41. Kxd6 Rg6+ 42. Kd5 Rb6 43. Rc3 Kd7 44. Rf3 Ke7 45. Rc3 Rb7 (45... Rb5+ 46. Kd4 Kd7 47. Rg3! g6 48. Rf3 Ke7 49. Rc3 {doesn't change much}) 46. Rc6 g6 47. Ra6 {[%csl Ga6]} Kf8 (47... Rc7 48. Ra1 Kd7 49. Ra6 Kc8 {is senseless due to} 50. e6 fxe6+ 51. Rxe6 Rg7 52. Ke5 {winning the g6 pawn with a draw.}) 48. Ke4 Kg7 49. Kf4 Re7 50. Ra5 Kh6 51. Kg4 a6 {Black has no other way to make progress but the resulting ending is theoretically drawn.} 52. Rxa6 {There is no need for an engine-driver to look for new ways like} (52. Kf4 {Amazingly, Magnus Carlsen's train finally derails anyway!}) 52... Rxe5 53. Rb6 Re3 54. Ra6 f5+ 55. Kh4 Re4+ 56. Kg3 Kg5 57. Ra3 Re2 58. Kf3 Rh2 59. Kg3 Rb2 60. Ra8 (60. Rc3 {was good enough, e.g.} f4+ 61. Kf3 Rh2 62. Rc5+ Kh4 63. Kxf4 Rf2+ (63... Rxh3 64. Rg5!) 64. Ke3 Rf6 (64... Rb2 65. Rc6) 65. Ra5 Kxh3 66. Rg5 Kh4 67. Rg1 g5 68. Rh1+ $11) 60... Rb3+ 61. Kg2 Kf4 62. Ra4+ Ke3 63. h4 (63. Kg3!?) 63... Rb6 (63... f4 64. Ra6 $11) 64. Kg3 Rd6 65. Rf4 Rd1 66. Rf3+ Ke4 67. Rf4+ Ke5 68. Ra4 f4+ 69. Kg2 Rd2+ 70. Kf3 Rd3+ 71. Kg2 Rg3+ 72. Kh2 (72. Kf2? Rh3 73. Ra5+ Kf6 74. Ra6+ Kg7) 72... Kf5 73. Ra6 Re3 74. Ra5+ Re5 75. Ra2! {This is the so-called Vaisser system of defence (applied by GM Anatoly Vaisser in one of his games).} Kg4 (75... Re6 76. Rg2 f3 77. Rg5+ Kf4 78. Kg1 Ra6 79. Kf2 {and Black can't make progress.}) 76. Rg2+! Kh5 77. Kh3 {Black can't attack the h4 pawn and protect his g6 pawn at the same time!} Ra5 78. Rg1 Ra3+ 79. Kh2 Ra6 80. Kh3 Ra5 81. Rg2 Rc5 82. Rg1 f3 {The last attempt.} 83. Kg3?! (83. Rf1! Rf5 (83... Rc3 {doesn't change anything}) 84. Rf2 Rf4 85. Kg3 Rf8 86. Kh3 {was much simpler}) 83... Rf5 84. Rf1?? {[%mdl 8192] A terrible mistake. Losing concentration at the end of the game is the main problem for Magnus (see also his game vs Radjabov).} (84. Kf2! {was now the only way to a draw:} Rf6 85. Rh1 Kg4 86. Rg1+ Kxh4 87. Rh1+ Kg4 (87... Kg5 88. Rg1+ Kh6 89. Rh1+ Kg7 90. Rh3) 88. Rg1+ Kf4 89. Rg3 Ke4 90. Rg4+ Ke5 91. Rg5+ Ke6 92. Rg3 $11) 84... f2 $22 {and White must let the black king forward.} 85. Kh3 Rf3+ 86. Kg2 Kg4 87. h5 {Desperation.} Rg3+! 88. Kh2 (88. Kxf2 Rf3+ 89. Kg2 Rxf1 90. Kxf1 Kxh5 $19) 88... Kf3! 89. Ra1 Rg2+! 90. Kh1 (90. Kh3 Rg1) 90... Rg5 91. Ra3+ Kf4 92. Ra4+ Kg3 93. Ra3+ Kh4 (93... Kh4 94. Ra1 Rg1+ 95. Rxg1 fxg1=Q+ 96. Kxg1 Kxh5 $19) 0-1

