1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 d5 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 h6 7. Bxf6 Qxf6 8. Nf3 O-O 9. e3 Bf5 (9... Re8 10. a3 Bxc3+ 11. Qxc3 c6 12. Be2 Bg4 13. O-O Nd7 14. b4 a6 15. a4 Rac8 16. Rfc1 Qd6 17. Qb2 Nb6 18. b5 Bxf3 19. Bxf3 Nc4 20. Qb1 Nd2 21. Qd3 Nxf3+ 22. gxf3 axb5 23. axb5 c5 $11 {1/2 Rychagov,A (2532)-Belozerov,A (2549)/chessassistantclub.com INT 2004/EXT 2006 (70)}) 10. Bd3 (10. Qb3 Qb6 11. Be2 c6 12. O-O Bd6 13. Na4 Qxb3 14. axb3 Nd7 15. Rfc1 Rfe8 16. Nc5 Nxc5 17. dxc5 Bc7 18. Nd4 Bd7 19. b4 a6 $11 {1/2 Petran,P (2485)-Ravia,M/Ajka 1992/TD (28)}) 10... Bxd3 11. Qxd3 c6 (11... Nd7 12. O-O c6 {may be a more accurate order of moves.}) 12. O-O (12. Ne5!? Re8 (12... Qe6! {[%cal Yb8d7]}) 13. O-O Bd6 14. f4 Na6 15. a3 Nc7 16. Rf3 a5 17. Kh1 Rad8 18. Rg1 c5 19. Qf1 c4 20. g4 Qe7 21. g5 $40 {1-0 Papadakis,M (2045)-Dobrev,N (2374)/Dos Hermanas 2003/EXT 2004 (57)}) 12... Nd7 13. a3 (13. Rfc1 a5 14. Na4 Rfb8 15. Rab1 Bd6 16. Qc2 Qe7 17. Ne1 Rf8 18. Nd3 f5 19. a3 f4 20. exf4 Bxf4 21. Re1 Qg5 22. Nac5 Nxc5 23. Nxc5 Rf7 24. Re2 Bd6 25. Rbe1 Bxc5 26. Qxc5 Qf4 27. g3 Qf6 28. f4 Raf8 29. Re5 Qg6 30. Kh1 Qg4 $14 {1/2 Haik,A (2425)-Pomar Salamanca,A (2430)/Buenos Aires 1978/EXT 2000}) 13... Bxc3 (13... Bd6!? 14. e4 Qg6 15. Rad1 (15. Nh4 {[%cal Yh4f5]} Qg4 (15... Qh5 16. Qh3) 16. Nf5 dxe4 17. Qxe4 Qxe4 18. Nxe4 Bc7 $11) 15... dxe4 16. Nxe4 Rfe8 17. Rfe1 Nb6 18. Nc5 Qxd3 19. Nxd3 Rad8 20. Kf1 Rxe1+ 21. Rxe1 Kf8 22. h3 f6 23. Nd2 Kf7 24. Nb3 Bf8 25. Rc1 {1/2 Donner,J (2470)-Smyslov,V (2620)/Amsterdam 1954/EXT 2001}) 14. Qxc3 Nb6 {[%cal Yb6c8,Yc8d6,Yb6c4,Yc4d6] The Karlsbad structure with only knights on the board is considered comfortable for Black. The present game doesn't change that evaluation but sheds light on some niceties.} 15. Ne5 Qf5 16. Rfc1 f6 17. Nd3 Nc4 18. Nc5 Nd6 {[%csl Gd6]} 19. Qc2 Qxc2 20. Rxc2 Rae8 21. g3 g5 {This move creates an object of attack for White (h2-h4).} (21... h5!? $11) 22. Kg2 Kg7 23. a4 Re7 24. Rh1 Kg6 25. Rc3 h5 26. h4 Rh8 (26... g4!? $11 {The transfer of White's knight to f4 is harmless.}) 27. Rcc1 (27. hxg5!? fxg5 28. Nd3) 27... Rhh7 28. Rh2 Nf5?! (28... g4 {was still possible, with a completely dead position.}) 29. hxg5 fxg5 30. Nd3 g4 {Now the situation is completely different: the white knight has got the e5-square, and Black's h5-pawn is weak.} 31. Rch1 Kg5 32. Nf4 Ng7 33. b4! a6 34. b5 axb5 35. axb5 Rh6 36. Ra1 Nf5 37. Ra8 h4!? (37... Rg7 $14 {is possible but the text move gives White a seemingly attractive possibility.}) 38. gxh4+ (38. Rg8+ Rg7 39. Rxg7+ Nxg7 40. Rxh4 Rxh4 41. gxh4+ Kxh4 42. bxc6 bxc6 43. Ng6+ Kg5 44. Ne5 {is hardly enough for a victory, e.g. after} Nh5 45. Nxc6 Kf5) 38... Nxh4+ 39. Rxh4!? {A "brilliant move" but Black miraculously holds his ground. Therefore} (39. Kf1 $142 $14) 39... Kxh4! (39... Rxh4 40. Rg8+ Kf5 41. Ng6 Rhh7 42. Nxe7+ Rxe7 43. bxc6 bxc6 44. Kg3 $16) 40. Rg8 {[%cal Rf2f3]} (40. bxc6 bxc6 41. Rg8 Ra7) 40... Rf7 41. f3 (41. bxc6 Rxc6! (41... bxc6? 42. f3 $18) 42. Ng6+ Kh5 43. Ne5 Rf5 44. Nxc6 bxc6 $11) 41... Rxf4 42. exf4 {[%mdl 4096]} cxb5! (42... gxf3+ 43. Kxf3 {[%csl Rh4] should be lost for Black.}) 43. Rxg4+ (43. fxg4 Ra6! 44. f5 (44. Kf3 Ra3+ 45. Ke2 b4) 44... b4 45. Kf3 Ra1! 46. Rh8+ Kg5 47. Rh5+ Kf6 48. Rh6+ Kg5 49. Rg6+ Kh4 50. Kf4 Rf1+ 51. Ke5 b5! 52. Rb6 Kxg4 53. f6 Kg5 $11) 43... Kh5 44. Rg5+ Kh4 45. Rg7! (45. Rxd5?! Rb6!) 45... Kh5 46. Rxb7 Ra6 47. Rxb5 Kh4! 48. Rb2 (48. Rxd5 Ra2+ 49. Kf1 Kg3 50. f5 Kxf3 51. Ke1 Ke4 $11) (48. Kf2 Ra2+ 49. Ke3 Ra3+ 50. Ke2 Ra2+ 51. Kd3 Ra3+ 52. Kc2 (52. Kd2 Kg3 53. f5 Kxf3 $11) 52... Rxf3 53. Rxd5 Kg4 54. Rd8 Kf5! 55. d5 Ke4 56. d6 Kd5 $11) 48... Ra4 49. Rd2 Kh5 {Despite two extra pawns, White can't strengthen his position.} 50. Kg3 Rb4 51. Rd1 Ra4 52. Kh3 Ra3 53. Rh1 Rd3 54. Kg3+ Kg6 55. Rh4 Rd2 56. Rg4+ Kf6 57. f5 1/2-1/2

