1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. Bg5 dxc4 6. e4 b5 7. e5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Nxg5 hxg5 10. Bxg5 Nbd7 11. exf6 Bb7 12. g3 c5 13. d5 Qb6 14. Bg2 O-O-O 15. O-O b4 16. Na4 Qb5 17. a3 exd5 18. axb4 cxb4 19. Qg4 {An old move in this well-known position. Other options:} (19. Qd4) (19. Re1 {- see Topalov-Shirov in this issue}) (19. Be3 {(the most popular move)}) (19. Bf4 {(introduced recently, with excellent results for White)}) (19. h4) 19... d4 {Positions arising in the Botvinnik system are usually crazy and hard to assess. Black tries to clarify the position a little bit. Otherwise White would eventually create a strong attack on the black king:} (19... Bd6 20. Be3 Kc7 21. Bd4 (21. Rfd1?! Rdg8 22. Qf5 Rg6 23. Bd4 Rhh6 {0-1 Lhost,G-Roussel,C/Bethune 2005/CBM 107 ext (39)}) 21... Rhe8 (21... Ne5!?) 22. b3 Nc5 23. Nxc5 Bxc5 24. Qf4+ Kb6 25. Be5 (25. Ra4! a5 26. Rfa1 $16) 25... Rxe5 26. Qxe5 c3 27. Rfe1 Rd6 28. g4 d4 29. Bxb7 Kxb7 30. Qf5 $16 {½-½ Zhou Jianchao-Zhang Pengxiang/Wuxi 2006/CBM 113 ext (35)}) (19... Bh6 20. Rfd1 (20. Rfe1 Rdg8 21. h4 Bxg5 22. hxg5 d4 23. Qxd4 Bxg2 24. Kxg2 Rxg5 25. Re4 Qf5 26. Qxc4+ Kd8 27. Re8+ {0-1 Babula,V-Kanovsky,D/Luhacovice 2003/CBM 094}) 20... Rhg8 (20... Rde8 21. b3 c3 22. Bxh6 Rxh6 23. Rd4 a5 24. Bxd5 Re6 25. Bxe6 fxe6 26. Rxd7 Kxd7 27. Rd1+ {1-0 Badjarani,I-Peyrou Olya,M/Iran 1997/EXT 2003}) 21. h4 Bxg5 22. hxg5 Bc6 23. Rd4 Kb7 24. b3 $16 {0-1 Dias,A-Pelletier,Y/Matinhos 1994/EXT 1997 (52)}) (19... Rg8 20. h4 Bd6 21. Qd4 Be5 22. Qxa7 Kc7 23. Bh3 Nxf6 24. Rfe1 Ra8 25. Qe3 Ne4 26. Bf4 f6 27. Bxe5+ fxe5 28. Qh6 $36 {1-0 Burgess,G-Adnoy,H/Gausdal 2002/CBM 086 ext (36)}) 20. Bxb7+ Kxb7 (20... Qxb7 21. Qxd4 Bd6 22. Qxd6 Rxh2 23. Kxh2 Rh8+ 24. Bh4 $18 {1-0 Ortiz,E-Stein,J/Monterey Park 2004/EXT 2009 (27)}) 21. Qe4+ Qc6 22. Qxd4 Bd6 23. Rfe1 Rde8 24. Re3!? $146 {[%mdl 8] An attempt at an improvement. Previous games saw:} (24. Rxe8 Rxe8 25. Be3 Bb8 (25... Nb6 26. Nxb6 axb6 27. Rd1 Be5 28. Qd2 c3 29. bxc3 bxc3 30. Qa2 Re6 31. Rb1 Qe8 32. Qd5+ Kc7 33. Ra1 Qc6 34. Ra7+ Kb8 35. Qxc6 Rxc6 36. Rxf7 Bxf6 $44 {½-½ Williams,L-Wilson,J/Hastings 1995/EXT 1998 (40)}) 26. Qxd7+ Qxd7 27. Nc5+ Kc8 28. Nxd7 Kxd7 29. Ra4 (29. Bd2!?) 29... Bd6!? (29... a5 30. Rxa5 Be5 31. Bc5 b3 32. Ba3 Ke6 33. f4 Bd4+ 34. Kf1 Rb8 $44 {0-1 Zarubin,O-Andrianov,N/Severodonetsk 1982/EXT 1999 (42)}) 30. Rxa7+ Kc6 31. Ra6+ Kd5 32. Kf1 b3 33. Ra5+ Kc6 34. Ra6+ {½-½ Beliavsky,A-Smeets,J/Amsterdam 2007/CBM 120 (35)} (34. Bd4 Bb4 35. Ra1 Rd8 36. Be5 Rd2 $44)) 24... Ne5! (24... Rxe3?! 25. Bxe3 {is poor for Black as his rook is on h8 and not on e8 as in the previous variation.}) 25. h4 (25. Rc1!?) 25... Re6 {Sometimes chess is very easy: if you don't know what to do - centralize your pieces! For White it is more difficult to find a guiding light.} 26. Bf4 (26. Qd1!? Rhe8 $140 27. Qh5) 26... Rhe8 27. Bxe5 Rxe5 28. Rxe5?! (28. Rae1!? Rxe3 29. Rxe3 Rxe3 30. fxe3 Bf8 31. Qd8 Qxa4 32. Qxf8 Qd1+ $11) 28... Rxe5 29. Qg4?! {White decides to advance his h-pawn at any cost.} (29. Rd1 $142 Bc7 {[%csl Ra4]} 30. b3 cxb3 31. Qxb4+ Rb5 $15) 29... Ra5 30. h5 Rxa4 31. Rxa4 Qxa4 32. h6 Qc6! {Consolidation.} 33. Qh5?! (33. h7? c3 34. bxc3 bxc3 35. h8=Q c2 $19) (33. Qg8 $142 ! {[%cal Rh6h7]} Be5! 34. Qxf7+ Ka6 35. Qe7 Bxf6 36. Qxb4 Qb5 37. Qd6+ Qb6 $17) 33... c3?! {Of course, something like} (33... a5!! 34. Qxf7+ Ka6 $17 {(with the idea of preventing f7-a2+ after ...c4-c3) can hardly be found by a human. White had little chances to survive in that case, e.g.} 35. h7 (35. Qg6 c3 36. bxc3 bxc3 37. Qc2 Bf8 38. h7 Qxf6) 35... c3 36. bxc3 bxc3 37. h8=Q c2 38. Qh6 c1=Q+ 39. Qxc1 Qxc1+ 40. Kh2! (40. Kg2 Qc6+ 41. Kg1 Be5) 40... Qc2 41. Kg1 Qb1+ 42. Kh2 Qe4! $17) 34. bxc3 bxc3 35. Qxf7+ Bc7?! {This leads to a draw. Black could have kept winning chances after} (35... Ka6 36. Qa2+ Kb5 37. h7 (37. Qc2 Bb4!) 37... c2 38. Qb2+ Bb4 39. Qc1 Ba3 40. Qf1+ Ka4 41. h8=Q c1=Q {etc.}) ({or} 35... Kb6 36. Qb3+ Ka5 {etc.}) 36. Qb3+ Bb6 37. Qf7+ {Black now can't escape checks.} Bc7 (37... Qc7 38. Qd5+ Ka6 (38... Kb8?? 39. f7 Qxg3+ 40. Qg2 Bxf2+ 41. Kh1 $18) 39. Qa2+ Ba5 40. Qe6+ $11) (37... Ka6 38. Qa2+ Ba5 39. Qe2+ Kb6 40. Qe3+ Kb7 41. Qe7+ Bc7 42. Qb4+ $11) 38. Qb3+ Bb6 39. Qf7+ Bc7 1/2-1/2

