{[%mdl 2]} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 Bg7 11. Bd3 Ne7 12. Nxe7 Qxe7 13. c4 f5 14. O-O O-O 15. Qh5 {This used to be the main variation in the line 10...g7. However, in the past decade 15.h5 has been almost completely overshadowed by 15.f3.} Rb8 16. exf5 e4 17. Rae1 Bb7 18. Qg4 Rfe8 19. cxb5 d5 20. bxa6 Bc6 21. b3 {A rare move, which after this game might bring new life into the entire 15.h5 variation. Shirov is not afraid to give his opponent attacking possibilities. Such a materialistic approach needs to be supported by deep home analysis.} (21. f3 {is considered to lead to equality after} h5) (21. a7 Rxb2 {is also not problematic for Black.}) 21... Kh8 $146 {Although this plan didn't work in the present game, it is difficult to believe that White's queenside pawns are worth more than Black's attacking prospects on the kingside and the strong pawn centre.} (21... h5 22. Qg3 Kf8 23. Nb1?! Be5 24. f4 Bf6 25. Qf2?! Qb4! 26. a3 Qd4 $17 {0-1 Koch,J (2453)-San Emeterio Cabanes,J (2392)/Le Port Marly 2009 (38)}) (21... Bd7 22. a7 Rb7 23. Nc4! Rd8 24. Ne3 exd3 25. Nxd5 Qd6 26. Qg5? (26. Qh4 Bxf5 (26... Bb5 27. f6 $18) 27. Ne7+ Rxe7 28. Qxe7 $14) 26... f6 27. Qg4 Bc6 $17 {0-1 Fier,A (2526)-Cordova,E (2477)/Buenos Aires 2007 (43)}) (21... Qxa3? 22. f6 Qf8 23. Be2 $18) 22. Nc2 Be5 23. Be2 d4 ({Not} 23... Rg8 24. Qh5 Rg5 25. Qh6 f6 26. Rd1 {[%cal Gc2d4,Gc2e3]} Qc5 27. Ne3 Rbg8 28. Rc1 Qb6 29. g3 $16 {/+-}) 24. Bc4 Rg8 25. Qh3 (25. Qh5 {can lead to the same position as in the game:} Rg7 ({Besides, Black has an additional possibility} 25... e3 26. g3 Qd6 27. Qh4 Ba8 $44) 26. g3 Rbg8 27. Qh6 (27. a3 e3 28. Nxe3 dxe3 29. Rxe3 Qf6 $13)) 25... Rg7 ({A reasonable alternative is} 25... e3 {Then after} 26. g3 Qd6 (26... Qc5 27. Qh4 $16) {the strongest for White is} 27. Rd1! (27. Bxf7 Rg7 28. Bc4 exf2+ 29. Kxf2 d3 $36) (27. Nxe3 dxe3 28. Rxe3 Bd4 $13) 27... exf2+ 28. Kxf2 Be4 29. Ne1! {Black has compensation, but it is not clear whether it's enough.}) 26. g3 Rbg8 27. Qh6 {The critical position for the evaluation of 21...h8.} Qc7? {Up till now Carlsen played all logical moves, but now he goes wrong. The point is that the sacrifices on g3 don't work out for Black and therefore 27...c7 is useless. Now Shirov can transfer the knight to d5 with tempo, decisively improving White's position.} ({After} 27... Ba8 {Black has visible compensation, especially from the "human" point of view. Nevertheless with accurate "computer" moves White can defend and remain with slightly better prospects.} 28. Rd1 (28. a3 {[%cal Gc2b4]} Rg5!? (28... e3 29. Nxe3! dxe3 30. Qxe3 Re8 31. f4 Bf6 32. Qxe7 Rxe7 33. Rxe7 Bxe7 34. b4 $18) (28... Qc5 $44) 29. Rd1 (29. Qh3 e3 $40 30. f4 $140 Rg4 31. fxe5 Rh4! $19) (29. f4 exf3 30. Rf2 (30. Kf2? Rxg3 $19) 30... Qc7 31. Kf1 (31. Kh1? Bxg3 32. Re8 Bf4 33. Qf6+ Rg7 34. Rxg8+ Kxg8 35. Qxd4 Rg2 $19) 31... Bxg3 32. Re8 Rxe8 (32... Bf4 33. Qf6+ Rg7 34. Rxg8+ Kxg8 35. Qxd4 $16) 33. Qxg5 Bxf2 34. Qf6+ Kg8 35. Qg5+ $11) 29... e3 $40) 28... Qc5!? (28... e3 29. Nxe3! dxe3 30. f6! (30. Qxe3 Bxg3 31. hxg3 Rxg3+ 32. Qxg3 Qh4 33. Bd5 $11) 30... Bxf6 31. Qxe3 Qc7 32. Bd5 Qa5 33. Bb7 Bxb7 34. axb7 Qc7 35. Qe4 Rb8 36. Qf5 Rg6 37. Rd7 Qc6 38. Rxf7 Rxb7 39. Rxb7 Qxb7 $14) 29. Rfe1 ({Worse is} 29. Rc1 e3 30. Ne1 Bd6 $44 {[%cal Gc5f5]}) 29... e3 30. Kf1! {The best defence against the sacrifice on g3.} (30. Nxe3 dxe3 31. Qxe3 Bxg3! 32. Qxc5 Bd6+ 33. Kf1 Bxc5 $15) 30... Rg5 (30... Bxg3 31. hxg3 Rxg3 32. fxg3 Qxf5+ {doesn't work in view of} 33. Qf4 $18) 31. Nxe3 dxe3 32. Rd8! e2+ 33. Rxe2 Rxd8 34. Qxg5 Rd1+ 35. Re1 Rxe1+ 36. Kxe1 Qd4 37. Qe3 $14) 28. Nb4 {Immediately exploiting the fact that the b4-square is no longer controlled by the opponent's queen. As the result Black loses the e4-pawn and the long diagonal remains closed. Suddenly White is easily winning.} Ba8 (28... Bxg3 {is answered by the cool} 29. fxg3 Rxg3+ 30. Kh1 {and it's over for Black.}) 29. Nd5 Qd8 (29... Qd7 30. Rxe4 Qxf5 31. Qh4 d3 32. Re3 $18) (29... Bxd5 30. Bxd5 e3 {creates no threats at all, since the control over diagonal a8-h1 secures White's defence against any threats on the g-file.} 31. Qc6 $18) 30. Rxe4 (30. Rxe4 f6 31. Rxe5 fxe5 32. Nf6 Rf8 33. Nh5 Ra7 34. f6 $18 {[%cal Gh6g7]}) 1-0

