1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 Nc6 8. Qd2 O-O 9. Bc4 {The first major crossroads, when White has two main alternatives - the text-move and} (9. O-O-O {Here the most principled reaction is} d5 {, the position after} 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Bd4 {also arose in Baku:} Nxc3!? (12... e5 {is the most usual move, but this seems to be a plausible alternative.}) 13. Qxc3 Bh6+ 14. Be3 Bxe3+ 15. Qxe3 Qb6 {, po} 16. Qxb6 ({The  swap is too soft, White can try to fight for an advantage only with} 16. Qc3 {, or}) (16. Qxe7 {, but even here it's far from simple:} Be6 17. Qa3 Rad8 18. Bd3 Rd5 $44 {}) 16... axb6 17. a3 Be6 18. Bd3 Kg7 19. Rhe1 Rfd8 20. Be4 Rxd1+ 21. Rxd1 Rc8 22. a4 f5 23. Bd3 Ra8 24. b3 Kf6 25. Kd2 Bd5 26. c4 Bf7 27. Kc3 c5 28. Bc2 Be8 29. Ra1 Ke5 30. Re1+ Kd6 31. Rd1+ Kc7 32. Re1 $11 {Bacrot,E (2705)-Grischuk,A (2716)/Baku/2008/}) 9... Bd7 10. Bb3 ({Radjabov's move avoids the Chinese Dragon with} 10. O-O-O Rb8!? {, which has gained some popularity lately.} (10... Rc8) ({or} 10... Ne5 {is the main line.})) (10. h4 {was once considered the most accurate move-order. White first opens the h and plays 0-0-0 only later, when it suits him. However, once} h5 {became the main response, this nuance lost its meaning - it all boils down to a simple transposition.}) 10... Rc8 ({White's move order allows} 10... Nxd4 11. Bxd4 b5 12. h4 (12. a4!?) 12... a5 13. a4 bxa4 14. Nxa4 e5 $132 ({or} 14... Be6!? {with sharp positions. However, the character of the struggle is somewhat different and White often strives to exploit the ×. As the Dragon's main characteristic is opposite-side castling and mutual attacks against the enemy , the text-move is more popular.})) 11. h4 ({Another theoretically important moment.} 11. O-O-O Ne5 ({In the side-line} 11... Nxd4 12. Bxd4 b5 13. Nd5 Nxd5 14. Bxg7 Kxg7 15. exd5 {Black's best chance to generate sufficient  is the pawn-sacrifice} a5 16. a3 b4! 17. axb4 axb4 18. Qxb4 Kg8 $13) 12. Kb1 {is an approach, which has caused Black problems. Recently he has shown some ideas how to solve them:} {Another less committal, but plausible alternative was chosen by Carlsen 2 rounds later:} Re8!? ({The most consistent is} 12... Nc4 13. Bxc4 Rxc4 14. g4 b5 15. b3 {with the interesting sacrificial idea} b4! (15... Rc5? 16. Ne6 fxe6 17. Bxc5 dxc5 18. e5 $16) (15... Rc8 16. Ndxb5 Qa5 17. a4 $14 {/}) 16. bxc4 bxc3 17. Qxc3 Qc7 18. g5 Nh5 19. Kc1 (19. Rd3 Rc8 20. Qd2 Qxc4 21. Nb3 {Haznedaroglu,K (2440)-Sluka,R (2354)/Olomouc/2007/} Bc3 $44) 19... Rc8 20. Qd3 Be5 21. h4 Rb8!? $44 (21... Nf4 22. Bxf4 Bxf4+ 23. Kb2 Be5 24. c3 Qa5 25. Rb1 Qa4 26. Ka1 a5 27. Rb3 $16 {Socko,B (2630)-Stocek,J (2572)/Bundesliga/2006/})) 13. h4 (13. Bh6 Bxh6 14. Qxh6 Rxc3 15. bxc3 a5! $36 {and White does best to bail out with} 16. a4 Qb6 17. h4 Bxa4 18. h5 Nc6 19. hxg6 Bxb3 20. gxh7+ Kh8 21. cxb3 Nxd4 22. cxd4 Qxb3+ 23. Ka1 {1/2, Karjakin,S (2686)-Ivanchuk,V (2729)/Foros/2007/}) 13... h5 14. Bh6 Nc4 15. Bxc4 Rxc4 16. Bxg7 Kxg7 17. Nd5 (17. g4 hxg4 18. h5 Rh8 19. hxg6 fxg6 20. f4 e5 21. Nde2 Rxh1 22. Rxh1 Nxe4 23. Nxe4 Rxe4 24. fxe5 {looks dangerous, but after} Rxe5 $13 ({or} 24... Qe8!? {White doesn't seem to have any clear road to an advantage.})) 17... e5!? {Once the dark-squared  have been exchanged, Black can afford even this Najdorf move.} 18. Nxf6 Qxf6 19. Nb3! (19. Ne2 Rc6 {is harmless, as Black's major pieces are harmoniously placed.} 20. Nc3 Be6 21. Nd5 Bxd5 22. Qxd5 Qf4! 23. Qd2 Qxd2 24. Rxd2 f5 25. Re1 Kf6 26. c3 Ke6 27. Kc2 fxe4 28. Rxe4 Rec8 29. Kd3 b5 30. a4 a6 $11 {Karjakin,S (2732)-Carlsen,M (2765)/Baku/2008/}) 19... Re6 (19... Rc6? 20. Na5 $16) (19... Be6 20. Qxd6 Rec8 21. c3 b5 $14 {is a better way to sacrifice a pawn, but even here Black hardly has full compensation.}) 20. Qa5 (20. Qe3 $142 b6 21. Nc1!? $11 {[%csl Re6][%cal Yc1e2,Ye2c3] /,e2-c3,צe6 is a better chance to profit from the misplaced  and fight for an edge.}) 20... Ra4 21. Qc3 Qd8 22. Rd2 Qb6 23. Nc1 Bb5 24. b3 Ra6 25. Rhd1 Qd8 26. g3 Rc6 27. Qe3 Qb6 28. Qxb6 Rxb6 29. Ka1 f5 $11 {Ponomariov,R (2684)-Tiviakov,S (2599)/FIDE WCh Moscow/2001/}) 11... h5 12. O-O-O Ne5 13. Bg5 (13. Kb1 Re8 {-11.0-0-0}) (13. Bh6 Nc4 (13... Bxh6 $143 14. Qxh6 Rxc3 {[%csl Rg6] doesn't have a good reputation, contrary to Karjakin-Ivanchuk above, Black has problems with ×g6, for example in the line} 15. bxc3 Qc7 16. Kb1 a5 17. f4 Nfg4 18. Qg5 a4 19. fxe5 axb3 20. cxb3 Nf2 21. e6! $16 {/+-}) 14. Bxc4 Rxc4 15. Bxg7 Kxg7 $13 {leads to a similar position, as in the above note - there we had the extra moves b1 and e8. The main difference is that after} 16. g4 hxg4 17. h5 Rh8 18. hxg6 fxg6 19. f4 e5 20. Nde2 (20. fxe5 dxe5 21. Nde2 Rh3! 22. Rhf1 Rf3 23. Rh1 Rh3 $11) 20... Rxh1 21. Rxh1 Nxe4 22. Nxe4 Rxe4 23. fxe5 {Black can play even} Rxe2! 24. Qxe2 (24. Qh6+ Kf7 25. Qh7+ Ke6 26. Qxg6+ Kxe5 27. Rh5+ Kf4 28. Qxd6+ Kf3 29. Qd5+ Kf4 30. Qd6+ Kf3 $11) 24... Qg5+ $13 {/=}) 13... Rc5 {[%csl Gc5]} ({Introduced by Sosonko in 1977, the  on the 5-th rank helps out both in the defence and the . This quickly became the main line, replacing} 13... Nc4 14. Qe2 Na5 15. Kb1 $36) 14. Kb1!? {Useful prophylaxis, Black has learned to deal quite well with the direct assault} (14. g4 hxg4 15. h5 (15. f4 Nc4 16. Qe2 (16. Qd3 Qc8 $13 (16... b5!?)) 16... Qc8 17. Bxf6 (17. f5?! Nxb2 18. Kxb2 Rxc3 19. fxg6 Rxb3+ 20. axb3 fxg6 $36 {can be better only for Black.}) 17... Bxf6 18. Bxc4 (18. Nd5?! Rxd5 19. exd5 b5 20. h5 g5 21. fxg5 Bxg5+ 22. Kb1 f5 $17 {Ivanchuk,V (2740)-Topalov,V (2670)/Belgrade/1995/}) 18... Rxc4 19. Nd5 Re8 20. e5 Bg7 21. h5 Rc5! $15 {Murariu,A (2347)-Sebe_Vodislav,R (2253)/ROM-chT/2003/}) 15... Nxh5 16. Nd5 Re8 17. Rxh5 (17. f4 Nc4 18. Qd3 b5 19. f5 Rxd5 20. exd5 Bxd4 21. fxg6 Bxb2+ 22. Kb1 Ng7!? $15 23. Bxc4 bxc4 24. Qe4 fxg6 (24... Bf6? 25. Rh8+! Kxh8 26. Qh1+ $18) 25. Kxb2 Qb6+ 26. Ka1 Bf5 27. Qxc4 Rc8 $36 {Brkic,A (2570)-Babic,Z (2034)/Bizovac/2007/}) 17... gxh5 18. Qh2 Rxd5 19. Bxd5 Qb6! 20. Qxh5 e6 21. Bb3 gxf3 22. Bh6 Bxh6+ 23. Qxh6 f2! $13 {} 24. Qf6 Bc6 25. Nf5 (25. Rh1? f1=Q+ $19) 25... exf5 26. Qg5+ Kf8 27. Qh6+ Kg8 $11) 14... Re8! {Even Black does better to think about the defence first, the text-move overprotects e7.} ({After} 14... b5 15. g4 hxg4?! (15... a5 $142) 16. h5 Nxh5 17. Nd5 Re8 18. Rxh5 gxh5 19. Qh2 Rxd5 20. Bxd5 Qb6 {[%csl Rb5] Black's  is limited by the b5 and White has} 21. Nf5! $40 {1-0 Hakki,I (2428)-Abu Sufian,S (2276) Asia-ch Calcutta 2001 (7)}) 15. g4?! {Radjabov confessed after the game he got confused and mixed up the sharp lines. However, proving an advantage for White is not easy anyway:} (15. Rhe1 Qa5 16. f4 (16. a3 Qa6! (16... b5?! 17. Bxf6! exf6 18. Nde2 Rc6 19. Nd5 Qxd2 20. Rxd2 Nc4 21. Bxc4 bxc4 22. Red1 f5 23. exf5 Bxf5 24. Nd4 $14 {[%csl Rc4,Rd6] /,ק,Anand,V (2725)-Kasparov,G (2795)/PCA WCh New York/1995/}) 17. f4 Nc4 $15) 16... Nc4 17. Bxc4 Rxc4 18. Bxf6 (18. Nb3 Qa6 19. e5 Ng4 20. exd6 Bxc3! 21. bxc3 e6 $44 {} 22. f5 gxf5 23. Bf6 f4 24. Be5 Qb5 25. Bxf4 Rec8 26. g3 Rxc3 27. Qe2 Qc6 28. Rd2 e5! $19 {Brooke,R (2187)-Horwitz,D (2241)/corr/2002/}) 18... Bxf6 19. Nd5 Qxd2 20. Nxf6+ exf6 21. Rxd2 f5 $11) ({A better chance is} 15. Bh6 $142 !? Nc4 (15... Bh8?! 16. g4 Qa5 17. g5 Nh7 18. f4 Nc4 19. Qd3 Na3+ 20. Kc1 e6 {Almasi,Z (2631)-Golubev,M (2536)/Bundesliga/2003/} 21. f5!? $40) 16. Bxc4 Rxc4 17. Bxg7 Kxg7 18. Nd5 $11 {/ and we have again reached the position we had in the note to move 11 with one extra move (White played g5-h6 and Black c5xc4).}) 15... hxg4 16. h5 {White again plays the most aggressive move.} (16. f4 Nc4 17. Qe2!? (17. Bxc4 Rxc4 18. Bxf6 Bxf6 19. e5 dxe5 20. fxe5 Bxe5 21. Nde2 Bxc3!? (21... Bd6 22. h5 g5 $15) 22. Nxc3 Rxc3 23. Qxc3 Qc8 $15) {Black can try to deviate earier with} 17... Na5!? (17... Qc8 18. f5 Na3+ (18... gxf5?! 19. exf5 Ne5 20. Bxf6 exf6 21. Ne4 Nf3 {Slaby,J (2363)-Mikrut,D (2369)/Poraj/2003/} 22. Bxf7+! Kxf7 23. Nxd6+ Kf8 24. Qg2 $16) 19. bxa3 Rxc3 20. fxg6 Rxb3+ 21. axb3 fxg6 22. Qd3 Nh5 23. Rhf1 Be5 24. Ne2 g3 25. Nf4 Nxf4 (25... Bg4? 26. Nxg6 Bxd1 27. Qxd1 g2 28. Rg1 Bg7 29. Qxh5 Qc3 30. e5 dxe5 31. Bh6 Bxh6 32. Rxg2 {1-0, Motylev,A (2666)-Carlsson,P (2514)/EU-ch Plovdiv/2008/}) 26. Bxf4 g2 27. Rf2 Rf8 $44) 18. e5?! Nxb3 19. Nxb3 Rxc3 20. bxc3 Nh5 21. Rxd6 Qc8 22. Rxd7 Qxd7 23. Rd1 Qc8 24. Qd3 {Vadla,Z (2240)-Kutuzovic,B (2420)/Zadar/1997/} Kh7! {[%cal Rf7f6] f6}) 16... Nxh5 17. Rxh5 {White burns his boats.} (17. f4 $142 Nc4 (17... Nc6!? 18. e5 Qc8 $13 {Rogozenko}) 18. Qd3 b5 19. f5 Qb6 $13 (19... Qc8!?) {/,} 20. Nd5?! Rxd5 21. exd5 Qxd4 22. Qxd4 Bxd4 23. Rxd4 Bxf5 $36) (17. Bh6 e6! $15 {leads nowhere for White.}) (17. Nd5 Rxd5! (17... Nc4!? $15) 18. Bxd5 Qb6 $36 {is excellent for Black according to Golubev. Here we again see how postponing b5 helps to activate the d8.}) 17... gxh5 18. Qh2 Ng6 {A rather modest move, but it's not the only one.} ({After} 18... Nxf3 {there is no immediate refutation in sight, but Black is the one, who risks more:} 19. Nxf3 gxf3 20. Qxh5 e6 21. Nd5 f6!? (21... Qa5 22. Bh6! Rxd5 $8 23. exd5 Bxh6 24. Qxh6 f2 25. c3 Qb5 26. Bc2 {gives White at least a draw without any risk.}) 22. Bh6 (22. Be3 f2!? $13 (22... Rxd5 23. exd5 e5 {Mainka,R (2450)-Komljenovic,D (2440)/Bad Mergentheim/1988/} 24. Rg1 $36) (22... Rb5 23. Bh6 (23. Rg1 exd5 24. Rxg7+ Kxg7 25. Bh6+ Kh8 26. Bf4+ Kg7 $11) 23... Re7 24. Rh1 Qe8 25. Nxe7+ Qxe7 26. Qg6 $44)) 22... Re7 $8 $13) ({Radajbov mentioned the enterprising alternative} 18... Qb6!? 19. Qxh5 e6 $15) (18... Qa5!? $15 {is also interesting.}) 19. Qxh5 Qa5!? $146 {[%mdl 8]} (19... Rxg5 20. Qxg5 e6 21. Qxg4 Qf6 22. Nce2 $13 (22. Qg3 Qe5 23. Qxe5 Bxe5 24. Nce2 Kg7 25. Rg1 Kf6 26. c3 Rh8 $36 {Glimbrant,T (2270)-Pavlovic,M (2495)/Barbera del Valles/1994/})) ({Carlsen's novelty is a safer choice, than} 19... e6 20. Nf5! Qa5! 21. Rxd6 (21. Rh1 Rxf5 22. exf5 Qxf5 23. fxg4 Qe5 $15) 21... Bc6 $13) 20. f4! (20. Qxg6? e6 $19) (20. Nd5 Rxd5 (20... e6 21. Rh1 exd5 22. Qh7+ Kf8 23. Bh6 Bxh6 24. Qxh6+ Ke7 25. Qg5+ f6!? (25... Kf8 26. Qh6+ $11) 26. Qxg6 dxe4 $15) 21. Bxd5 e6 22. Qxg4 Ne5 23. Qg3 exd5 24. Bf6 Ng6 25. Bxg7 Kxg7 26. Nf5+ Kf6!? 27. Rxd5 Qxd5 28. exd5 Bxf5 $15) 20... Rxg5 (20... Rxc3 21. bxc3 Qxc3 22. Bxf7+ (22. f5 Ne5 {[%cal Rc3h3] h3} 23. Rh1 Qxd4 24. Qh7+ Kf8 25. Bh6 Ng6 $8 26. Bxg7+ Qxg7 27. fxg6 Rc8 28. Bxf7 Qxh7 29. Rxh7 e6 30. Rh4 Kg7 31. Rxg4 Rc4 $15) 22... Kxf7 23. f5 $11 {gives White at least a draw.}) 21. fxg5 e6 22. Nf5? {[%mdl 64] White goes for an ambitious tactical line, which favours his opponent.} (22. Qxg4 $142 $11 {/ is perhaps slightly more pleasant for Black, but nothing more than that.}) 22... exf5 23. Qxg6 Be6 24. Qh5 (24. Bxe6 Rxe6 25. Qxf5 Bxc3! 26. bxc3 Qxf5 27. exf5 Re4 $17 (27... Re5 28. Rg1 Rxf5 29. Rxg4 Kg7 $15)) 24... fxe4 25. Rf1 (25. Nd5 e3! 26. g6 Kf8 27. gxf7 Bxf7 (27... Re7 28. Qg5 Bxd5 29. Rf1! Qd8 $8 30. Qxd5 e2 31. Re1 $13) 28. Rf1 Qe1+! 29. Rxe1 Bxh5 30. Rf1+ Bf7 31. Nxe3 Rxe3 32. Rxf7+ Ke8 $17 {[%csl Gg4] g4, Carlsen}) 25... Qe5! (25... Bxb3 26. axb3 Re7 27. Qxg4 $15 {/}) 26. Rxf7 Bxb3 (26... Rf8 $142 !? $17 {/-+ is probably even better, as it forces further simplification.}) 27. axb3 g3 28. Ka2?! (28. Rxb7!? Rf8 29. Qe2 Rf2! $17 {[%csl Ge4,Gg3] } (29... Qxg5 30. Nxe4 Qe5 31. Rxg7+ Qxg7 32. Qg2 $15)) 28... Rf8 (28... g2? 29. Rf2! {} g1=Q 30. Qf7+ Kh7 31. Qh5+ $11) 29. Rxf8+ Kxf8 30. Qg4 e3 31. g6 e2? {[%mdl 8192]} (31... Ke7 32. Qf3 e2! 33. Qxb7+ (33. Qxe2 Qxe2 34. Nxe2 g2 35. c3 Be5 $19) 33... Kf6 34. Nxe2 Qxe2 35. Qf7+ Kg5 36. Qxg7 g2 37. Qxa7 Kxg6 $19) (31... Bf6!? $19) 32. Qf3+? {[%mdl 8192]} ({Mutual errors are typical for such a tactically demanding game. Radjabov repays the favour, after} 32. Qd7! Kg8 (32... Qe7 33. Qc8+ Qe8 34. Qf5+ Kg8 35. Qd5+ Kh8 36. Qh1+ Kg8 37. Qd5+ $11) 33. Qc8+ Bf8 34. Qxb7 Be7 35. Qh1 (35. Nxe2 Qxe2 36. Qd5+ Kg7 37. Qf7+ Kh6 38. g7 g2 39. g8=Q Qa6+ 40. Kb1 Qf1+ $11) 35... Kf8 36. Qh7 Ke8 37. Nxe2 Qa5+ 38. Kb1 Qe1+ 39. Ka2 (39. Nc1 g2 40. g7 Qxc1+ 41. Kxc1 g1=Q+ 42. Kd2 Qf2+ $11) 39... Qa5+ $11) 32... Ke8 $19 33. Qf7+ Kd8 34. Qg8+ Kd7 35. Qf7+ Qe7 36. Qf5+ (36. Nxe2 Qxf7 37. gxf7 g2 $19 {[%cal Yd7e7] e7}) 36... Kd8 37. Qa5+ b6 38. Qd5 e1=Q 39. Qa8+ Kd7 40. Qb7+ Ke8 (40... Ke8 41. Qa8+ (41. Qc6+ Kf8 42. Qf3+ Kg8) 41... Qd8 42. Qc6+ Kf8 43. Qf3+ Qf6 $19) 0-1

