1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. Nf3 dxe5 5. Nxe5 c6 {Most likely Carlsen's opening choice came as a surprise to Topalov, although Magnus has already used this system before.} 6. Bd3 ({The natural looking} 6. c4 {is inaccurate. After} Nb4 {Black suddenly develops an initiative, threatening to take on d4 followed by the check on c2. Therefore White must place the knight on a3, after which Black easily achieves comfortable play:} 7. Be3 Bf5 8. Na3 (8. Nd3 e5! $36) 8... Nd7 $11) ({Besides 6.d3, other attempts to fight for the advantage are} 6. Be2) ({and} 6. Bc4) 6... Nd7 {This is the main idea of Black's setup: to exchange the exposed knight and achieve a very solid position.} 7. Nxd7 {In my opinion this is harmless for Black.} ({When he was 13-years old, Carlsen lost against} 7. O-O {which is actually the strongest continuation:} Nxe5 (7... g6 {is the alternative}) 8. dxe5 Nb4 (8... g6 $142 $14 {/=}) 9. Be4 Qxd1 10. Rxd1 f5 11. a3 Na6 12. Bf3 g6 13. Nd2 Be6 14. Be2 Nc7 15. Nf3 Bd5 16. Nd4 Bg7 17. c4 Bf7 18. f4 $14 {Sutovsky,E (2639)-Carlsen,M (2385)/Rethymnon 2003.}) (7. Nf3 N7f6 8. h3 Nb4! 9. Bc4 Bf5 10. Bb3 a5! $11) 7... Bxd7 8. O-O g6 9. Nd2 ({Somewhat better is} 9. Re1 Bg7 10. c3 O-O 11. Bg5 {although Black hardly has any opening problems.}) 9... Bg7 10. Nf3 O-O 11. Re1 ({More cautious is} 11. h3 $11) 11... Bg4! 12. c3 c5! {Carlsen's fight for the initiative is justified: Black leads in development and uses his bishops to create pressure on White's centre.} 13. Be4 ({Although after} 13. dxc5 {Black has} Nxc3! 14. bxc3 Bxc3 {White is only slightly worse:} 15. Bh6 Bxe1 (15... Re8? 16. Re3 $18) (15... Bxa1? 16. Qxa1 $18) 16. Qxe1 Qxd3 17. Ne5 (17. Qe5? f6 18. Qxe7 Rf7) 17... Qd4 18. c6 (18. Bxf8 Kxf8 19. Nxg4 Qxg4 $17) 18... bxc6 19. Nxc6 Qf6 20. Nxe7+ Kh8 21. Nd5 Qd4 22. Bxf8 Rxf8 23. Ne3 Be6 24. Rd1 $15 {/=}) 13... cxd4 14. cxd4 e6 {A dream position for Black, who has perfectly placed pieces against the isolated pawn.} 15. Qb3?! ({After} 15. h3 Bxf3 16. Bxf3 Qb6 17. Bxd5 exd5 18. Be3 {White has better chances to achieve a draw. For instance:} Qxb2 19. Qd3 Qb6 20. Rab1 Qc7 21. Rb5 Rfd8 22. Qb3 {regaining the pawn.}) 15... Bxf3! {Very concrete. Black wins the central pawn.} 16. Bxf3 Bxd4 17. Bxd5 Qxd5 18. Qxd5 exd5 19. Rd1 Bg7 $17 20. Kf1 (20. Rxd5 {does not restore material equality:} Rfd8 21. Rxd8+ Rxd8 22. Be3 (22. Kf1 Rd1+ 23. Ke2 Rh1 {is just bad for White}) 22... b6 23. Rb1 Bxb2 {with a healthy pawn up.}) 20... Rfd8 21. Bg5 Rd7 22. Rd2 h6 23. Be3 d4 24. Rd3 Rc8 25. Bd2 Rc2 {Black is a pawn up and has more active pieces. Carlsen converts it convincingly into a full point.} 26. Rb1 Re7 27. a4 f5 28. b3 Rec7 (28... Kf7 29. Rc1 Rxc1+ 30. Bxc1 Ke6 $17 {/-+}) 29. Be1 Kf7 30. Rd2 Rc1 31. Rxc1 Rxc1 32. Ke2 Rb1! 33. Rd3 Ke6 34. h4 Kd5 $19 35. Bd2 Ke4 36. Rg3 f4 37. Rd3 Be5 38. f3+ Kd5 39. Be1 Bd6 40. Bd2 g5 41. hxg5 hxg5 42. Be1 g4 43. fxg4 Ke4 44. g5 Ke5 0-1

