1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Be7 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bf4 c6 6. Qc2 Bd6 7. Bxd6 Qxd6 8. e3 Ne7 {A detailed review of this line is given in annotations to Onischuk - Kovacs in this issue.} 9. Bd3 b6 10. Nf3 Ba6 11. O-O Bxd3 12. Qxd3 Nd7 (12... O-O $142 13. e4 dxe4 14. Nxe4 Qd5) 13. e4! {[%mdl 32] This is now extremely effective.} O-O ({The point is that after} 13... dxe4 14. Nxe4 Qd5 {(or elswhere)} (14... Qe6 15. Qa3 O-O? 16. Nfg5 $18) 15. Qa3! {Black will not be able to castle! Therefore Dmitry Jakovenko decides to let the e4-pawn move forward.}) 14. e5 $14 {White now has an edge due to his space advantage.} Qe6 15. Rae1 Rfe8 (15... f6!?) 16. Nh4! {[%cal Yf2f4,Yf4f5]} Ng6 (16... f5!? {It would take White a lot of time to prepare g2-g4.}) 17. Nxg6 Qxg6 18. Qd2 Nf8 (18... f5 {[%cal Yd7f8]}) 19. f4 Qf5? (19... f5 {was possible here, too. Instead, Black blocks White's f-pawn with his queen, which proves ineffective.}) 20. Nd1 f6 21. Ne3 Qd7 22. Qd3 {[%cal Re3f5,Rf5d6]} fxe5 {Black finds a way to parry White's threat but White now obtains a crushing pawn avalanche. One of Philidor's model games had a similar course.} 23. dxe5 Ne6 24. f5 Nc5 25. Qd4 {[%cal Ye5e6,Ye3g4,Yf5f6]} Ne4 {Desperation, rather than a blunder. White now wins a pawn and then converts his material advantage.} 26. Nxd5! {[%mdl 64]} Qxd5 (26... cxd5 27. Rxe4 $18) 27. Qxe4 Rad8 28. e6 (28. f6!?) 28... Qxe4 29. Rxe4 Rd6 (29... Rd2 30. b4! (30. g4 $143 Rxb2 31. g5 Rb5 32. h4 Kf8 33. f6 Rd5) 30... Rxa2 31. Rc1 Rc8 32. Rec4 $18) 30. g4 Kf8 31. g5 Ke7 32. Kg2 Rd5 33. Kg3 Kd6 34. h4 c5 35. f6 gxf6 36. gxf6 Rd3+ 37. Kh2 Rd2+ 38. Kh1 1-0

