1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. e3 {This second main continuation has lately been somewhat neglected in the light of all the excitement after} (5. Bg5 h6 6. Bh4 ({In Foros 2008, Carlsen was twice successful with} 6. Bxf6 Qxf6 7. e3 {, but his wins had little to do with the opening battle.}) 6... dxc4 7. e4 g5 8. Bg3 b5 {After his loss to Anand in Morelia 2008 (see CBM 123), Carlsen has avoided this sacrificial line with White (the exception being one rapid game against Van Wely).}) 5... Nbd7 6. Bd3 dxc4 7. Bxc4 b5 8. Bd3 Bb7 9. a3 {Currently a topical sideline.} ({The main moves are 9.e4 and especially} 9. O-O {and now:} {The older} a6 {still seems a safer choice:} (9... b4 10. Ne4 Nxe4 11. Bxe4 Bd6 12. a3 bxa3 13. b3 Nf6 14. Nd2 Qc7 15. Bf3 Bxh2+ 16. Kh1 Bd6 17. Nc4 Be7 18. Bxa3 O-O 19. Bc5!? $146 (19. Bxe7 Qxe7 20. Ra5 Rfd8 21. Kg1 c5! 22. Rxc5 Ne4 23. Bxe4 Bxe4 24. Qg4 Bd3 25. Ra1 Rac8 26. Raa5 Rb8 27. Qd1 Be4 28. Qa1 Rb7 $11 {/, Topalov,V (2813)-Kramnik,V (2743)/WCh Elista/2006/}) 19... Rfd8 (19... Bxc5 $142 20. dxc5 Rfd8 {} 21. Nd6 Nd7 $132) 20. b4 Bxc5 21. bxc5 a5 22. Re1 Ba6 23. Nb6 Rab8 24. Rxa5 Bb5 25. Qa1 Nd5 (25... Bd3!? $14) 26. Ra7 Rb7 27. Rxb7 Qxb7 28. Qa5 {Aronian,L (2737)-Grischuk,A (2728)/Sochi/2008/} Rb8! $14 (28... Nxb6 29. Qxb5 $16)) 10. e4 c5 11. d5 c4 12. Bc2 Qc7 13. dxe6 fxe6 14. Nd4 Nc5 15. Be3 e5 16. Nf5 (16. Nf3 Be7 17. Ng5 O-O! $44 {0-1 Kasimdzhanov,R (2678)-Kasparov,G (2804) Linares 22nd 2005 (9) is one of Kasparov's last contributions to opening theory - see}) {Another option is} 16... g6!? (16... Ncxe4 17. Nxe4 Nxe4 18. Qg4!? Nf6 19. Qh3 g6 20. Bg5 gxf5 21. Bxf6 Rg8 22. Rfe1! Rxg2+ (22... Qc6 23. Rxe5+ Kf7 24. Be4 Qxf6 25. Rxf5 Bxe4 26. Rxf6+ Kxf6 27. Qh4+ Kf5 28. Qxh7+ Rg6 29. Re1 Rd8 30. Qh5+ Rg5 31. Qf7+ {1-0, Akopian,V (2700)-Ippolito,D (2397)/Gibraltar op/2007/}) 23. Kf1 Rxf2+! (23... Rg6? 24. Rxe5+ Kf7 25. Qxh7+ {1-0, Grigoryan,A (2509)-Soozankar,A (2253)/Urmia/2008/}) 24. Kxf2 Qb6+ 25. Ke2 Qxf6 26. Bxf5 $13 {is risky, but probably playable for Black.}) 17. Nh6 (17. Bg5 Ncxe4 18. Nxe4 Nxe4 19. Bxe4 Bxe4 20. Bf6 gxf5 21. Bxh8 Bd6 $44 {}) 17... Bg7 18. Qf3 Ne6 19. Qh3 Bc8 20. Qh4 (20. Qg3 $142) 20... Qe7 21. Rfe1 Nd5 22. Qxe7+ Nxe7 23. Nd5 Bb7 24. Ng4 Nd4 25. Bd1 Nxd5 26. exd5 O-O-O 27. Bg5 Rxd5 $17 {Morozevich,A (2758)-Anand,V (2792)/WCh Mexico City/2007/}) 9... b4 (9... Bd6 10. O-O O-O 11. Qc2 h6 12. e4 e5 13. dxe5 Nxe5 14. Nxe5 Bxe5 15. h3 a6 16. Be3 c5 17. Bxc5 Re8 18. Rad1 Qc7 19. Nd5 Nxd5 20. exd5 Bxb2 21. d6 Qc6 22. f3 Rec8 23. Bh7+ Kh8 24. Be4 Qxc5+ 25. Qxc5 Rxc5 26. Bxb7 Rd8 27. Rfe1 Be5 28. d7 $16 {[%csl Gd7] d7, Topalov,V (2777)-Ivanchuk,V (2781)/Bilbao/2008/}) (9... a6 10. b4 a5 11. Rb1 axb4 12. axb4 Nd5 13. Nxd5 exd5 14. O-O ({or even} 14. e4!?) 14... Bd6 15. Qc2 $14 {is somewhat better for White, therefore the text move is the most usual reaction, considered to be the main line.} (15. e4 $14)) 10. Ne4 {This continuation brought new life into the whole line.} (10. axb4 Bxb4 11. O-O c5 12. Na2 a5 13. Nxb4 axb4 14. Rxa8 Qxa8 15. dxc5 O-O $11 {is equal and sterile.}) 10... Nxe4 ({The text move is a more solid alternative, than} 10... c5 11. Nxf6+ gxf6 12. O-O Qb6 13. axb4 cxd4 14. Nxd4 Bxb4 15. Qa4!? (15. Bd2 Rg8 16. f3 Rg5 17. Bxb4 Qxb4 18. Rf2 Rc5 19. Qd2 Qb6 20. b4 Rc7 21. Nb5 Rcc8 22. Bf1 Ke7 23. Qd4 a6 24. Rd2 Bd5 25. Qxb6 Nxb6 26. Nd4 Bc4 $11 {Moskalenko,V (2520)-Dreev,A (2650)/Alushta/1994/}) 15... a5 16. Bb5 O-O-O 17. Bc6 Nc5 18. Bxb7+ Kxb7 19. Qc2 e5 20. Nf3 Rc8 21. Bd2 Rhg8 22. Bxb4 Qxb4 23. Qf5 Rg6 24. Rfc1 a4 25. Rc3 Qg4 26. Qxg4 Rxg4 27. Rac1 $14 {Mammen,H-Stuart,A/corr/1996/}) 11. Bxe4 bxa3 {Now Black hardly plays anything else.} ({The popularity of 10.e4 started with the following game:} 11... Qc7 12. axb4! Bxb4+ 13. Bd2 Bxd2+ 14. Nxd2 c5 15. Qc2 Qb6 16. dxc5 Qxc5 17. Qa4 Rb8 18. O-O O-O 19. Qxd7 Rfd8 20. Bxh7+ Kxh7? (20... Kf8! 21. Qa4 Rxd2 $44) 21. Qxf7 Rxd2 22. Ra4 Qg5 23. g3 e5 24. Rh4+ Qxh4 25. gxh4 Rd6 26. h5 Be4 27. Qe7 Rbb6 28. Qxe5 Re6 29. Qf4 {1-0, Gelfand,B (2670)-Anand,V (2725)/Biel izt/1993/}) 12. O-O {This  sacrifice in the interests of development replaced the former alternative} (12. bxa3 Bd6 {Even the attempt to breathe life into the position with} 13. Nd2!? {is hardly sufficient after} (13. O-O O-O 14. Bb2 Rb8 15. Qc2 c5 16. Bxb7 Rxb7 17. dxc5 Bxc5 18. Rfd1 Qe7 19. a4 {1/2, Karpov,A (2735)-Anand,V (2770)/WCh Lausanne/1998/CBM 063}) (13. Bd2 Rb8 14. O-O (14. Qa4 c5 15. Bxb7 Rxb7 16. dxc5 Bxc5 17. Ba5 Qb8 18. Rd1 O-O {1/2, Karpov,A (2775)-Kramnik,V (2730)/Dortmund/1995/}) 14... O-O 15. Bb4 c5 16. dxc5 Bxc5 17. Bxb7 Rxb7 18. Nd4 Qb6 19. Bxc5 Qxc5 20. Qd2 Qd5 21. Qc3 h6 22. Rac1 Ne5 23. h3 Rfb8 24. Qc5 Qe4 25. Qc2 Qd5 26. Qa4 Qe4 27. Qc2 Qd5 {1/2, Karpov,A (2770)-Kramnik,V (2775)/Monte Carlo rpd/1996/}) 13... Qc7 14. Nc4 Be7 15. O-O O-O 16. Rb1 Rab8!? (16... Nf6 17. Bf3 Ba6 18. Qc2 Rac8 19. e4 Nd7 20. Be2 c5 21. d5 exd5 22. exd5 Bxc4 23. Bxc4 Nb6 24. Rb3 Bf6 25. Be3 Be5 26. g3 $14 {Beliavsky,A (2654)-Galkin,A (2587)/FIDE WCh New Delhi/2000/}) 17. Qa4 Nb6 18. Qa5 Ba8 19. Nxb6 axb6 $11 {Beliavsky}) 12... Nf6 (12... Bd6 13. b3 (13. b4 Nf6 14. Bd3 a5!? 15. bxa5 Qxa5 16. Ne5 O-O 17. Nc4 Qc3 18. Bxa3 Bxa3 19. Rb1 Ba6 20. Rb3 Qxc4 21. Rxa3 Qd5 22. Rxa6 Rxa6 23. Bxa6 c5 24. dxc5 {1/2, Tomashevsky,E (2646)-Jakovenko,D (2710)/RUS-ch Moscow/2007/}) 13... Nf6 {transposes into the 9.0-0 b4 line above.} (13... Qe7 14. Qc2 $14)) (12... axb2 13. Bxb2 Nf6 (13... Be7 $143 14. Qc2 h6 15. Bxc6 Rc8 16. d5 exd5 17. Bxd7+ Qxd7 18. Qb3 d4 19. Rxa7 Bd5 20. Qb6 $16 {Najer,E (2616)-Potkin,V (2470)/Ubeda/2001/}) 14. Bd3 {-12...f6.}) 13. Bd3 axb2 14. Bxb2 a5 {Introduced into practice by Kramnik, the alternatives were:} (14... Be7 15. Qb1 Rb8 (15... O-O 16. Ba3 $44) 16. Rxa7 Qb6 17. Qa2 O-O 18. Rb1 Ra8 19. Rxa8 Rxa8 20. Qc4 Ba6 21. Qc2 Bxd3 22. Qxd3 Qb5 23. Ne5 Rb8 24. Qxb5 Rxb5 25. Nc4 c5 {1/2, Ivanchuk,V (2695)-Kramnik,V (2725)/New York rpd/1994/ Black gradually overcame his problems and fully equalized, nevertheless in Mexico 2007 he sought a safer alternative.}) (14... Bb4 15. Qb3! a5 16. Ba3 Qb6 (16... Qe7 17. Rfb1 $14) 17. Ne5 O-O 18. Nc4 Qc7 19. Bxb4 axb4 20. Qxb4 Rfd8 21. Ne5 g6 22. Rxa8 Rxa8 23. Rc1 Nd5 24. Qb2 Ra4 25. h3 Rb4 26. Qa3 Qb6 27. Be4 Rb3 28. Qd6 Qc7 29. Qc5 $16 {1-0 Moiseenko,A (2632)-Huzman,A (2589)/Montreal/2008/}) 15. d5!? $146 {[%csl Re8][%cal Ga1h8][%mdl 640] A stunning novelty, White sacrifices another  to open the  a1-h8 and create more space for a direct  against the  in the . A blow against a heavily protected point is usually very effective - if not otherwise, than at the very least psychologically. In the following course of the game Aronian initially defends quite well, but later practically inexplicably falls to pieces.} (15. Qa4 Bb4 16. Ba3 Nd5 17. e4 (17. Be4!? {Marin}) 17... Nb6 18. Qb3 Qe7 19. Rab1 Bxa3 20. Qxb6 Bb4 21. Ne1 O-O 22. Nc2 Rfd8 {is too tame - White regains the , but his  evaporates.} 23. Nxb4 axb4 24. Qxb4 Qxb4 25. Rxb4 Ba6 26. Bxa6 $11 {1/2, Gelfand,B (2733)-Kramnik,V (2769)/WCh Mexico City/2007/}) ({Interesting, but not quite clear is} 15. e4!? Bb4 16. Qc2 Nd7 17. Rfd1 (17. d5 cxd5 18. Bxg7 Rg8 19. Bd4 Rc8 $132) 17... Rc8 18. Qb1 O-O 19. d5 cxd5 20. exd5 Nf6? (20... Bxd5! 21. Bxh7+ Kh8 $13) 21. dxe6 Qe7 22. Ng5 h6 23. Bh7+ Kh8 24. Bxf6 Qxf6 25. Nxf7+ Rxf7 26. exf7 Qxf7 27. Be4 $18 {Moiseenko,A (2632)-Illescas Cordoba,M (2603)/ESP-chT Cala Mayor/2008/ This game was played only a few days earlier, than ours.}) ({As yet untested, but plausible altrnatives are} 15. Qb3!? Rb8 (15... Bb4 16. Ba3 {transposes into Moiseenko-Huzman above.}) 16. Bc3 Ba6 17. Qd1 Bxd3 18. Qxd3 $32) ({or} 15. Ne5 $44) 15... Nxd5 {Black must take the .} (15... cxd5 $143 ? 16. Bb5+ Nd7 17. Ne5 Bc8 18. Rc1 (18. Qh5 g6 19. Qf3 $18) 18... Bd6 19. Rxc8 $18) (15... Qxd5 16. Qc2 (16. Qb1 $44) (16. Ne5 $44) 16... Qc5 17. Qe2 Be7 (17... Qh5 18. Bxf6 gxf6 19. Qb2 $36) 18. Bd4 Qd5 19. Rfd1 $32) (15... exd5 16. Nd4!? {[%csl Rc6][%cal Yd4f5,Yd1a4] f5,a4,×c6} Bb4 (16... Be7 17. Nf5 {} O-O? 18. Nxg7! Kxg7 19. Qg4+ Kh8 20. Bxf6+ Bxf6 21. Qf5 $18) 17. Nf5 g6 18. Nh6 $44) 16. Ne5 Nf6 {Aronian prevents a  sortie to h5, but invests another tempo.} (16... Bb4 17. Qh5 Qc7 18. Rfc1 $44 (18. Nf3!? Bc3 19. Bxc3 Nxc3 20. Qc5 Nd5 21. Rfb1 $44)) (16... Nb4 17. Qh5 Qc7 18. Rfd1 $44) (16... Qc7!? $13) 17. Qa4 $32 Bb4 (17... Bd6!? {is an attempt to stabilize the position by returning both extra s:} 18. Ng4!? $13 (18. Nxc6 Qd7 19. Nxa5 (19. Bb5 O-O 20. Rfc1 Rfc8 $11) 19... Qxa4 20. Rxa4 Bd5 (20... Ke7 21. Bxf6+ Kxf6 (21... gxf6 22. Rb1 Bd5 23. e4 Bc7 24. exd5 Rxa5 25. Rxa5 Bxa5 26. Rb7+ $36) 22. Nxb7 Bxh2+ 23. Kxh2 Rxa4 $14 {/=}) {} 21. Bxf6 gxf6 22. e4 Bc6 $11)) 18. Nxc6 Bxc6 $8 (18... Qxd3? 19. Ne5+ $18) (18... Qd7? 19. Bb5 Ba6 20. Bxa6 Rxa6 21. Nxb4 $18) 19. Qxc6+ Ke7 20. Rfd1 {White has regained one  and Black's  has remained in the . However, Black's  are finally connected and this should suffice to gradually contain White's .} Rc8 21. Qf3 (21. Qa6 Qd6 22. Qb7+ (22. Qxd6+ Bxd6 23. Rxa5 Ra8 $11) 22... Qc7 {[%cal Yh8d8] hd8= only helps Black.}) 21... Qb6 (21... Qc7 22. Ba6 Rcd8 23. Rdc1 Qb8 24. Rc6 $36) 22. Bd4 Qb8 (22... Qc6!? 23. Qg3 Rhd8 {f8=} 24. Qxg7? Rxd4! 25. exd4 Rg8 $19) 23. Ba6 Rcd8 24. Bb7!? {Carlsen wants to keep maximum tension in the position and spurns immediately simplifying alternatives. However, objectively even his choice doesn't promise that much.} (24. Rdc1 e5 25. Bc5+ Bxc5 26. Rxc5 Rhe8 27. Rcxa5 Kf8 $11) (24. Rab1 Rd6!? {} 25. Bc5 Bxc5 26. Rb7+ Nd7 27. Rxb8 Rxb8 $11) 24... h5 {[%csl Gb7,Gd4,Re7,Gg1] Black starts to crack up. Perhaps Aronian cherished higher ambitions with his extra , but White has suffcient long-term compensation with his  and safer .} ({Definitely safer was} 24... e5 $142 !? 25. Bb6 (25. Bb2 Rxd1+ 26. Rxd1 Rd8 27. Rxd8 Kxd8 $11) 25... Rd6! 26. Rxd6 Qxd6 27. Bxa5 Bxa5 (27... Rb8 $11) 28. Rxa5 Qb4 29. Rxe5+ $8 (29. Ra1? e4 $17) 29... Kd6 30. Rd5+ Nxd5 31. Qxd5+ Ke7 (31... Kc7 32. Ba6 $11) 32. Qe5+ Kf8 33. Qb8+ Ke7 34. Qe5+ $11) 25. h3 ({The luft is useful, but more forceful was} 25. Rab1!? Ng4 26. g3 f6 27. h3 Ne5 28. Qg2 $44 {/}) 25... h4?! {Pointless, as the  still can't be activated via h5. Black still had} (25... e5 $142 $11 {This is similar to 24...e5 and although h3 is more useful than h5, he should be able to hold the balance.}) 26. Rab1 $36 e5 (26... Rd6 27. Rdc1! {[%cal Rd4e5] e5}) 27. Rxb4! {[%mdl 64] Time has come for tactical solutions.} axb4? {This loses by force.} (27... exd4 28. Rbxd4 $16 {or}) (27... Rxd4 28. Rbxd4 exd4 29. exd4 $16 {[%csl Re7] ע leads to a difficult position due to Black's exposed �, but it was definitely more resilient.}) 28. Bc5+ Ke6 $8 (28... Ke8 29. Bc6+ Rd7 (29... Nd7 30. Qf5 Qc7 31. Qg5 $18) 30. Rxd7! Nxd7 31. Qf5 Qc7 32. Qg5 f6 33. Qxg7 $18) 29. Ra1! $18 Rd6 (29... Rd2 30. Ra6+ (30. Ra8 $18) 30... Kd7 31. Bxb4! {} e4 32. Qf5+ Kd8 (32... Ke8 33. Re6+!) 33. Qa5+ Ke8 34. Qb5+ Rd7 35. Bc6 $18) 30. Bxd6 Kxd6 (30... Qxd6 31. Ra6 $18) 31. Qc6+ Ke7 32. Ra8 {This nets White a decisive material advantage.} Qd6 33. Qxd6+ (33. Rxh8 $18) 33... Kxd6 34. Rxh8 b3 35. Ba6 Nd7 (35... e4 36. Rb8) 36. Rxh4 Nc5 (36... Nc5 37. Bc4 $18 (37. Be2 $18)) 1-0

