1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. b3 d5 6. Bg2 Bb4+ 7. Bd2 Bd6 {Quite a rare system. Black doesn't want to lock the e-file by playing 7...e7. However, White is not obliged to release the pressure.} 8. Nc3! {8. cxd5 is much more popular, but the text move poses more problems.} (8. cxd5 exd5 9. O-O O-O 10. Nc3 Nbd7 {leads to a long strategic fight. This position can also arise after 6.cxd5 and gives Black satisfactory results.}) 8... O-O ({In the preceding game Vishy was able to secure a stable edge:} 8... c6 9. O-O O-O 10. e4 Nxe4 11. Nxe4 dxe4 12. Ng5 Be7 13. Nxe4 Nd7 14. Bf4 Nf6 15. Qc2 Rc8 16. Rfd1 $14 {1-0 Anand,V (2770)-Granda Zuniga,J (2630)/Madrid 1998/CBM 065/[Avrukh,B] (39)}) 9. O-O dxc4?! $146 {A novelty in a rather rare position. This new approach is not successful, since White's pressure along the h1-a8 diagonal is too strong.} ({Black cannot avoid an inferior position after} 9... Nbd7 {either:} 10. e4 Nxe4 11. Nxe4 dxe4 12. Ng5 Nf6 (12... Be7 13. Nxe4 Bb7 14. Qc2 $14 {½-½ Pinheiro,J (2288)-Spraggett,K (2501)/Lisbon 2000/CBM 079 ext (14)}) 13. Nxe4 Nxe4 14. Bxe4 Rb8 15. Qc2 $14) (9... c6 {leads to the above mentioned game of Anand.}) 10. Ne5 c6 (10... Bxe5 {didn't work in view of:} 11. dxe5 Nd5 12. Nxd5! exd5 13. Bb4 c5 14. Bxc5 bxc5 15. Bxd5 Nc6 16. Bxc6 $16 {and White is just a pawn up.}) 11. bxc4! {The most ambitious way: White keeps the knight on e5, and it delays Black's development.} ({The quiet alternative} 11. Nxc4 Be7 12. e3 Qc7 13. Rc1 Nbd7 14. Re1 Rad8 15. a4 $14 {also leads to a safe advantage.}) 11... Qc7 ({Grabbing a pawn by means of:} 11... Bxe5 12. dxe5 Ng4 13. Bf4 Qxd1 14. Rfxd1 Bxc4 15. h3 Nh6 16. g4 $16 {is not recommended, since almost all Black's pieces are out of play.}) 12. Bf4 {This natural move seems inaccurate, since Black may exploit the vulnerable position of White's bishop and ease the pressure.} ({A stronger move was:} 12. f4! Bb7 13. e4 Rd8 14. Qc2 $16 {with a clear advantage due to a strong pawn centre.}) 12... Bb7 ({Correctly avoiding} 12... Nbd7 13. Nxc6 Bxf4 14. gxf4 Qxf4 15. e3 Qd6 16. Nb5 Bxb5 17. cxb5 $16) ({But the immediate} 12... Nh5!? {comes into consideration, since after} 13. Be3 Bxe5 14. dxe5 Qxe5 15. Qb3 Nd7 16. Bxc6 Rad8 $14 {Black's position is not that bad.}) 13. Rc1 Nh5 ({Again avoiding a nice tactical trap:} 13... Nbd7 14. Nb5! cxb5 15. cxb5 Nc5 (15... Qb8 16. Nxd7 Nxd7 17. Bxd6 Qxd6 18. Bxb7 $18) 16. dxc5 bxc5 17. Bxb7 Rad8 18. Bc6 Bxe5 19. Bxe5 Qxe5 20. Qa4 $16) 14. Ne4?! {Exchanging the strong f4 would definitely ease Simen's task.} ({Much stronger was} 14. Be3 Bxe5 15. dxe5 Qxe5 16. c5 $36 {with a powerful initiative for the sacrificed pawn.}) 14... Nxf4 {The most natural response.} ({However, it was possible to delay it by means of} 14... c5!? 15. d5 (15. Nxd6 Bxg2 16. Kxg2 Nxf4+ 17. gxf4 Qxd6 $11) (15. Qd3 f6 16. dxc5 Nxf4! (16... Bxe5?! 17. Bxe5 Qxe5 (17... fxe5 18. Ng5 Nf6 19. Nxe6 Qe7 20. Nxf8 Kxf8 21. Bxb7 Qxb7 22. Rfd1 $36) 18. Ng5 fxg5 19. Bxb7 $14) 17. gxf4 Be7 18. Nf3 bxc5 $11) 15... Nxf4 16. gxf4 Bxe5 17. fxe5 Qxe5 18. f4 Qc7 $132) 15. gxf4 c5 16. Qd3 {Now Magnus has an unpleasant threat, so Black has to simplify.} Bxe5 ({Of course, not} 16... cxd4? 17. Ng5 g6 18. Qh3 h5 19. Bxb7 Qxb7 20. Nxg6 fxg6 21. Qxe6+ $18) 17. Ng5 (17. dxe5 Nc6 18. Rfd1 Rad8 19. Nd6 Nb4 20. Qa3 Bxg2 21. Kxg2 f6 $132) 17... g6 18. Qh3 h5 19. dxe5 Nc6! {Simen is on top!} ({Correctly refusing} 19... Bxg2?! 20. Qxg2 Nc6 21. Ne4 Rfd8 22. Qg5 $40 {and White develops a strong attack.}) 20. Ne4 (20. e3 Na5 21. Bf3 Kg7 22. Qg2 Rad8 $11) 20... Nd4 21. Nf6+ Kg7 {The king is somewhat exposed, but White's attacking potential is not great.} 22. Rfe1 Nf5 {Freeing a a way for the rooks.} ({After} 22... Rh8 23. e3 Nf5 24. Rc3 Rad8 25. e4 Nd4 26. Bf1 Kf8 27. Qh4 $14 {Black's position is solid, but rather passive.}) 23. Rc3 Rh8 (23... Rfd8!? 24. e4 Nd4 25. Rg3 Rh8 26. Qh4 $14) 24. Rd1 Rad8 25. Rcd3 Rd4?! {A serious inaccuracy. Provoking e2-e3 is not a good idea, since that is an important prophylactic move.} ({Instead, Simen should have opted for:} 25... Nd4 26. e3 (26. Kf1 Ba6 27. Rc1 Bb7) 26... Ne2+ 27. Kf1 Nc3 28. Rxd8 (28. R1d2 Nb1! 29. Rxd8 Rxd8 30. Rxd8 Bxg2+ 31. Qxg2 Qxd8 $11) 28... Rxd8 29. Rxd8 Bxg2+ 30. Kxg2 Qxd8 31. Qf3 Nxa2 32. Qb7 Nb4 33. Qxa7 Nc2 $132) ({Or} 25... Rxd3 26. Qxd3 Bxg2 27. Kxg2 Qc6+ 28. Kg1 Qa4 $132) 26. e3 Rxd3 27. Rxd3 Bxg2 ({Too passive is} 27... Bc8 28. Be4 b5 29. cxb5 Qa5 30. Bc6 $36) 28. Qxg2 Rd8 29. Kf1 ({It was possible to grab a pawn:} 29. Nxh5+!? Kf8 30. Rxd8+ Qxd8 31. Nf6 a6 32. Qf3 b5 $14 {In the resulting position Black doesn't have sufficient compensation for the pawn, but it would be very difficult for White to make any progress.}) 29... Kf8 (29... Rxd3?? 30. Ne8+) 30. Rxd8+ Qxd8 31. Ke2 {Just in time! White is covering all the squares along the d-file, so Black is doomed to a passive defence.} Qc8! (31... Ne7 32. Qb7 $16) 32. a3 {A useful prophylactic move - the pawn is somewhat less vulnerable now.} Ke7 (32... Ne7 33. Qg5 Ng8 34. Ne4 Kg7 35. Nd6 Qa6 36. Qd8 Qxa3 37. h3 $36) 33. h3 Kd8 34. Qe4 Kc7 {Simen decides to keep the king on the q-side.} ({A waiting strategy was possible as well:} 34... Ke7!? 35. Qd3 Qc6 36. Kd2 Qb7 {and Black has good drawish chances.}) 35. Nh7 Qe8 36. Ng5 {Keeping an eye on the weak f7.} b5?! {The first step in a wrong direction - there was no need to change the character of the position.} ({After the correct} 36... Qd7! 37. Ke1 (37. Qa8 Qa4 $132) 37... a6 38. Ke2 Kb8 {Black's position seems very solid.}) 37. Qd3 (37. cxb5!? Qxb5+ 38. Qd3 Qb2+ 39. Kf3 Qb7+ 40. Ne4 $14) 37... a6 38. Kd2 {White has all the time in the world, since Black has no active possibilities.} Kb6 39. Kc3 Qe7 40. Ne4 {Now the b5 is a new target for White.} Qc7 (40... Qe8 41. Kb3 Qc8 42. Nc3 $14) 41. Kb3 Kc6 (41... Qe7 42. Nc3 Qe8 $14) 42. Nc3 bxc4+? {This leads to a very difficult position.} ({Simen's task was tough, but it looks like after} 42... Qa5! 43. Qe4+ Kb6 {Black's position is still tenable. For instance,} 44. Qa8 bxc4+ 45. Kb2 Kc7! 46. Qa7+ Kd8 $11 47. Qxf7 $140 ?? Qb6+ 48. Kc1 Qb3 $19) 43. Qxc4 Qb6+ 44. Kc2 {Now Black cannot avoid an unpleasant penetration of White's queen.} Qa5 45. Qe4+ Kc7 46. Qa8! $16 c4? {The decisive mistake, which leads to loss of material.} ({The last chance was} 46... Kd7 47. Qb7+ Ke8 48. Qb8+ Kd7 49. Kb2 Qc7 50. Qxc7+ Kxc7 51. Ne4 Kc6 52. Kb3 Kd5 53. Ng5 Nh6 54. Kc3 $16) 47. Qf8! Kd7 ({It was possible to provide more resistance, but after} 47... Nh6! 48. Qd6+! (48. Qxh6 Qxa3 49. Qg7 Qb3+ 50. Kd2 Qb2+ 51. Kd1 Qxc3 52. Qxf7+ Kd8 $16) 48... Kc8 49. Ne4 Qa4+ 50. Kd2 Qa5+ 51. Ke2 Nf5 52. Qc6+ Kd8 53. Nd6 Nxd6 54. exd6 c3 55. Qb7 Qb5+ 56. Qxb5 axb5 57. e4 $18 {White wins anyway.}) 48. Ne4 Qa4+ 49. Kc1 $18 {The f5 is restricted, so Black lacks any counterplay.} Qc6 50. Qxf7+ Kc8 51. Nd6+ (51. Ng5 $18 {was also good enough.}) 51... Kd8 ({The move} 51... Nxd6 {offers no hope:} 52. Qxe6+ Kb7 53. Qe7+ Ka8 54. exd6 $18) 52. Qf8+ Kc7 53. Qc8+ Kb6 54. Qb8+ {One more technical achievement of World Champion!} ({Black resigns in view of} 54. Qb8+ Kc5 55. Qb4+ Kd5 56. Qd2+ Kc5 57. Qa5+ $18) 1-0

