1. b3!? {The Nimzowitsch-Larsen opening has seen a surge in popularity recently, especially due to a popular online course by Adhiban dubbing his followers as "1.b3 cowboys". As a Dane, I'll stick to its original name, as well as to point out that while Magnus did score an impressive 4.5/5 points with it in the World Rapid and blitz, Larsen did actually100% with 6/6 in Teeside 1972! What's the point of the modest looking move? According to the engines it immediately spurns any hope of an white advantage, but at least drops the evaluation to only 0.00. And that's exactly the point. It gives white a position that is not worse, and encourages black to not play symetrical but instead start playing in the centre. It is for confident players believing they are better than their opponents. I recall asking Larsen about a typical position, telling him: " but you are not objectively better here", getting the reply: "No, but then I will have to beat him by playing better!"} e5 {The most logical reply, iccupying the centre and blocking the bishops diagonal at the same time.} 2. Bb2 Nc6 3. e3 Nf6 4. Nf3!? {50 years ago, Larsen used 4 Bb5 to beat fellow Scandinavian Andersson, but later it has been established that after 4...Bd6! black is perfectly fine, the point being that 5 Na3 is met strongly by 5...Na5!} Bd6!? {Abdusattorov copies the concept, but now at least white to not have a bishop on the unnatural b5 square. 4...e4 is principled, and Magnus later faced it and won in an important blitz game vs, the later bronze winner Matirosyan. Black gains some time for development, but at least white has managed to re-open the diagonal for his b2 bishop.} 5. c4 O-O 6. d3 Re8 7. a3 a5 8. Be2 Bf8 9. O-O d5 10. cxd5 Nxd5 11. Nbd2 {A typical reversed sicilian has arisen, and "typical" meaning that what has gotten an ideal setup, as while in the nirmal sicilians ahving an extra tempo, white can fight for the initiative in several agressive ways, but here Abdusattorov had to settle for just making standard moves achieving a solid position. But from the reversed dragon we know that even the extra tempo for white, and having avoided the sharper tries, does not neccesarily at all mean that white is better.} f6 12. Qc2 Bf5 (12... Be6 {was played in Fischer- Andersson Siegen 1970 (!). Abdusattorov chooses a different location for his bishop.}) 13. Rfe1 Bg6 {With colors reversed the plan would be Ne4, Rac1, and a later d4, equalizing comfortably, but now, actually having the white pieces, makes that seem less ambitious. The following concept was seen in the brilliant Fischer- Andersson mentioned above. There Fisch had to make prepatory moves like Kh1 and Rg1, due to the g4 square being covered, but here Magnus can execute the plan right away:} 14. g4!? {Objectively this should get a ?, but the concept being played by both Fischer and Carlsen, and praised by Kasparov in his book, somehow overrules. And indeed the main problem is a "random tactic"m as black is better after 14...a4! The point is that 15 b4 Nxb4 16 axb4 Nxb4 gives black 3 pawns for the piece under very favourable circumstances as now the pawn being on g4, really hurts whites position. 15 bxa4 then is the better choice, but after 15...Nb6! black regains the pawn, and white does not really have a good way to easily regroup. Again with the pawn on g4 feeling misplaced.} Qd7 (14... a4! 15. b4 (15. bxa4 Nb6!) 15... Ndxb4 16. axb4 Nxb4) 15. Kh1 Rad8?! {An obvious "logical" move, but a bad one! Which is why the white setup is so attractive, due to the logical flow of the game, will help it in his preferred direction. However modern computers indicate a much stronger plan for black. 15...h6! stopping whites immediate intentions on the lingside, and after 16 Rg1 Nd8 17 h4 Nf7 18 Rg2 Ra6! black has not only consolidated his kingside in a harmonius manner, but the rook from a6 does not conly defend via the 6-rank, but also intends 19...Rc6 when the knight starts entering at c3. If anything it is black who is better, but mainly it becomes a 2-way complex struggle.} (15... h6! 16. Rg1 Nd8 17. h4 Nf7 18. Rg2 Ra6! {[%cal Ga6c6,Gc6c2]}) 16. Ne4 Kh8 17. Rad1 Bf7 18. Rg1 {Abdusattorovs main problem is that his position is "perfect". All his pieces are on ideal squares, yet nothing concrete can be done. And if he just waits, white will keep making useful moves improving his own position. A strange paradox pointed out by Mihai Suba excactly covering these kind of Scheveningen style and hedgehog positions.} Nb6 19. Rg3 {It is interesting how AI treats certain chess positions. Here, without any hesitation it prefers 19 g5 f5 20 g6! Bxg6 21 Neg5.} (19. g5 f5 20. g6! Bxg6 21. Neg5 {There is no immediate reward, neither is white winning, hardly even considerably better, but it simply prefers the long term attacking chances, even being a pawn down. Nh4 might be next, doubling in the g-line etc. It is easy to see the attraction, yet it is a full pawn, and no immediate deceisive threats.}) 19... a4!? {Not neccesarily a good move, but a very human one. Black needs to generate counterplay, and cant just sit and wait.} 20. bxa4 Na5 {At least now Magnus faces directs threats and has to react. Bu apparently there is a strong element of bluff in it, as both 21 g5 Bb3 22 Qc1 Bxd1 23 Bxd1! as well as 21 Qb1 Nxa4 22 g5 f5 23 g6! is better for white. However, Magnus doesn't immediately manage to make the neccesary mental shift, and keeps regrouping:} 21. Rdg1?! (21. g5 Bb3 22. Qc1 Bxd1 23. Bxd1!) (21. Qb1 Nxa4 22. g5 f5 23. g6!) 21... Bd5?! {here however slows Abdusattorov slows down, and thus misses his chance:} (21... Nxa4! {was the move, the big difference being that after} 22. g5 f5 23. g6 Bb3 {is available, ensure that the kingside remains closed, while black made considerable progress on the queenside.}) 22. g5 f5 23. Nc3 {Not a bad moce, white is still better, but a brilliant one existed. 23 Nf6!! wins, as after 23...gxf6 24 gxf6 black has no reasonable way to defend against whites plan of e4 closing the long diagonal, and then Nxe5 becomes unstoppable. 24...Qc6 is met by 25 Qb1! and after 25...Qxf6 26 e4 it is striking that while whites attack perhaps seems a bit slow, the computers already says +3 despite white just being a piece down at present!} (23. Nf6!! gxf6 24. gxf6 Qc6 25. Qb1! Qxf6 26. e4) 23... Bc6 24. e4 Nxa4 25. Nxa4 Bxa4 26. Qc3 Nc6 {Abdusattorov got the pawn back, and his positional trhumps are obvious. It is the crucial stage of the game: Either Magnus finds the exact attacking moves , else black is just better:} 27. g6! {Brilliant! Even if the brilliance is somewhat hidden. The intention is obvious: White vacates the g5 square, with 28 Ng5 being the threat. 27...h6 does not prevent it, as after 28 Ng5! hxg5 29 Rh3+ Kg8 white plays 30 d4!! This threatens not only 31 Bc4+ but also 31 Rh8+ Kxh8 32 Qh3+ mating next move on h7 which is more than what the black position can take. If 27...f4 there are several solutions, but 28 Rh3(!) Qxh3 29 Ng5 Qd7 30 Nf7+ Kg8 31 Qc4 is themost extraordinary, with both 31...Re6 32 Bg4! and 31... Qe6 32 gxh7+! either queening or forking the queen with a check on g5 next winning immediately. Abdusattorov chooses the toughest defence available:} Nd4!? (27... h6 28. Ng5 hxg5 29. Rh3+ Kg8 30. d4!! {[%cal Gh3h8,Gc3h3]}) (27... f4 28. Rh3! Qxh3 29. Ng5 Qd7 30. Nf7+ Kg8 31. Qc4 Qe6 (31... Re6 32. Bg4) 32. gxh7+!) 28. Ng5! {No time for hesitation, details like defending the bishop hanging on e2 there is no time for. In principle 28...Re7 was technically blacks best reply, even though white is winning after the interesting 29 Bh5! securing access to the f7 square. But of course Abdusattotov takes the offered piece:} Nxe2 29. Nf7+?! {A much cleaner, and more beautifull win woud have been 29 Qc4! as after 29...Nxg3+ 30 Rxg3 despite being a rook down, whites threats are unstoppable. perhaps Magnus feared 30...Re7 covering the f7 square stopping the knight check, but 31 gxh7! sets up the threat of 32 Qg8 mate, which can only be stopped if black gives up severe amounts of material.} (29. Qc4! Nxg3+ 30. Rxg3 Re7 31. gxh7! {[%cal Gc4g8]}) 29... Qxf7 30. gxf7 Nxc3 31. fxe8=Q Bxe8? {A strange mistake, as this leaves the e5 pawn hanging. 31...Rxe8 32 Bxc3 Bc6 still put up resistance. But both playrers had little time left at this stage.} (31... Rxe8 32. Bxc3 Bc6) 32. Bxc3 Bg6 33. exf5 Bxf5 34. Bxe5 Bxd3 35. Rxg7! {And as after 35...Bxg7 white can choose between 36 Bxg7+ Kg8 37 Bf6+ or simply just 36 Rxg7 both winning instantly, Abdusattorov resigned.} (35. Rxg7 Bxg7 36. Bxg7+ (36. Rxg7) 36... Kg8 37. Bf6+) 1-0

