1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 {The Najdorf Defence - or actually Attack. One of the most popular openings in the world.} 6. Be2 {A calm reply, which was the favourite move of, among others, Anatoly Karpov.} e6 {choosing a Scheveningen type of position. Anther popular move is e5.} 7. O-O Be7 8. a4 Nc6 9. Be3 O-O 10. f4 Qc7 11. Kh1 Re8 12. Bf3 {Now we are at a very known position, which occured in about 2000 games and was even played in the famous match Karpov-Kasparov.} Bf8 {One of main moves, the bishop goes away and Black is preparing e5 at some point.} (12... Rb8 {is another main move here. One which was played more often.}) 13. Qd2 {The standard manoeuvre: the queen goes to f2.} ({Another typical queen manoeuver beginning with} 13. Qe1 {is not dangerous now as after} Nxd4 14. Bxd4 e5 {Black gets good play.}) 13... Rb8 (13... Na5 {is other move. Black wants to activate his knight and bring it to c4.} 14. b3 {not allowing c4.} (14. Qf2 {leads to a very sharp game after} Nc4 15. Bc1 e5 16. Nde2 d5!) 14... Bd7 {with a complicated battle.}) 14. Qf2 e5 {The standard move, which was part of Black's plan, beginning with the move 12...f8.} (14... Na5? {would be a typical blunder} 15. Nxe6! Bxe6 16. Bb6 {winning back the piece, and White is a pawn up.}) 15. fxe5!? $146 {The novelty, which probably was prepared by Magnus.} (15. Nde2 {is the main move here.} b5 16. axb5 axb5 {with an unclear position.}) 15... dxe5 (15... Nxe5 {was of course an alternative.} 16. Bg5 (16. Be2 {allows} Neg4 17. Bxg4 Nxg4 18. Qf4 Nxe3 19. Qxe3 Qc5 {and with two bishops Black will not have any problems I think.}) 16... Nfg4 17. Qg1 Nxf3!? {is tricky, but good I think.} (17... Be6 18. Be2 {keeping the bishop. And the deeper I analyzed this position, the more I liked it. I think White is better here.}) 18. gxf3 Qc5! {attacking the guy on g5.} 19. Nd5 Re5 20. Bd2 Rxd5 21. exd5 Ne5 {with some compensation for an exchange. The position is totally unclear.}) 16. Nb3 Nb4 {The standard move in these positions. But it might not be the best.} (16... Be6 {was a great alternative. I actually prefer this move to the text.} 17. Bb6 (17. Rad1 Nb4 18. Bb6 Qc8 {now we see the point of ...e6! Black is totally fine.}) 17... Qc8 18. Nd5 (18. a5!?) 18... Bxd5 19. exd5 Nb4 20. c3 e4 21. cxb4 exf3 22. Qxf3 Qd7 23. Bd4 Qxd5 24. Bxf6 Qxf3 25. Rxf3 gxf6 {with equality. The line was of course not forced.}) 17. Ba7 {It is useful to force the rook back to a8 before playing b6.} Ra8 18. Bb6 Qe7 (18... Qc6 {is not satisfactory either.} 19. Na5 Qe6 20. Rad1 {and White keeps a clear advantage. The bishop on c8 is probably wanting to go back to move 16... and play 16...e6!.}) 19. Rad1 {Good move: White develops his rook. Always useful!} Be6 {The first really inaccurate move.} (19... Bg4! {A very strong move, which probably keeps equality.} 20. Bxg4 (20. Bc5 Qc7 21. Bxf8 Kxf8! {and Black is fine.}) 20... Nxg4 21. Qe2 (21. Qg3 Nf6 22. Bc5 Qe6 23. Bxf8 Kxf8! {and Black has no problems.}) 21... Nf6 22. a5 {with an unclear game, but in my opinion Black is not worse.}) 20. Nd5 {Yes! Creating a dangerous passed pawn, which will be really dangerous with the support of the nice bishop on b6.} Bxd5 21. exd5 e4 22. d6 Qe6?! {The idea of Grischuk was to exchange queens. He will succeed, but the resulting endgame is very bad for him.} (22... Qe5 {was stronger.} 23. d7 Nxd7 24. Rxd7 exf3 25. Qxf3 f6 26. Nd4 Qe4 27. Rxb7 Rac8 28. c3 (28. Qxe4 Rxe4 29. g3 Nxc2 30. Rc1 Re1+ 31. Rxe1 Nxe1 32. Ra7 Nd3 33. Rxa6 Nxb2 34. a5 Nc4 35. Kg2 Re8 {and even though White's pawn looks dangerous, Black probably can hold.}) 28... Nd3 29. Ba7 Qxf3 30. gxf3 g6 31. Kg1 {and White is a pawn up, but Black still has some chances, as White's pieces are not so well coordinated.}) 23. Nc5 Qf5 24. Be2 Qxf2 25. Rxf2 Nbd5 (25... Nfd5 {was bad} 26. d7 Reb8 27. Nxb7! Rxb7 28. d8=Q Rxd8 29. Bxd8 Ne3 30. Rd4 {and White is just an exchange up.}) 26. a5 {This very natural and strong looking move was a bit inaccurate.} (26. Rxf6! {was the right move} Nxf6 (26... Nxb6 {and now, just} 27. Rff1 {and Black is hopeless.}) 27. a5 Rec8 28. b4! {just pushing it, with a big advantage for White.} Rc6 {The only move to force White to play d7.} 29. d7 Nxd7 30. Rxd7 {and White is almost winning. If not winning.}) 26... Nxb6 27. axb6 Rab8? {Mistake.} (27... Rec8! {was very strong and could give Black some chances.} 28. b4! (28. Nxb7 Rxc2 29. g3 {the only move, otherwise e3 comes.} Nd7 30. b4 Rb2 31. Nc5 Nxb6 32. Nxe4 Rxb4 {and Black is at least OK.}) 28... Rc6 29. Nxb7 Rxb6 30. d7 Rxb7 31. d8=Q Rxd8 32. Rxd8 Rxb4 33. Kg1 {and White is better, for example:} e3 (33... Rb6 34. Rc8! g6 35. Bc4 {with pressure on f7.}) 34. Rf5 Rb1+ 35. Rf1 Rb2 36. Bd3 {and e1-e3 to follow, with advantage for White.}) 28. Rxf6! {Not difficult, but nice.} gxf6 29. Nd7 {The point is the fork: f6 and b8 are hanging.} f5 30. c4 {White is simply improving his position. Black cannot save himself anymore.} a5 (30... Bg7 {does not help.} 31. Nxb8 Rxb8 32. c5 Bf6 33. Bxa6 {and White wins, just like in the game.}) 31. c5 Bg7 32. Nxb8 Rxb8 33. Ba6!! {A brilliant move, which wins on the spot!} Bf6 34. Bxb7 Rxb7 35. c6 Rxb6 36. Rc1 Bxb2 37. d7 1-0

