1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Bf4 Bg7 5. e3 O-O 6. Rc1 Be6!? {A rare but playable move, instead of the normal continuations} (6... c5) (6... c6) (6... dxc4) 7. c5?! {This is probably too ambitious. White will hardly be able to hold this structure.} (7. Nf3 dxc4 {leads to a popular D92 line}) (7. Qb3 {is the main alternative to the text move:} b6 8. Nf3 c5!? (8... c6 $143 9. Ng5 dxc4 10. Bxc4 Bxc4 11. Qxc4 Nd5 12. Nxd5 cxd5 13. Qb3 e6 14. O-O Nd7 15. Nf3 Nf6 16. Qa4 $14 {1-0 Farago,I (2510)-Groszpeter,A (2375)/Budapest 1979/MCD (58)}) (8... Nc6?! 9. Qa4! (9. cxd5 $143 Nxd5 10. Nxd5 Bxd5 11. Qa4 e5! 12. Bg5 f6 $13 {0-1 Soln,P (2280)-Jelen,I (2400)/Vrhnika 1995/EXT 1998 (33)}) 9... Na5 10. cxd5 Nxd5 11. Nxd5 Qxd5 12. b4 Bd7 13. Qa3 Nc6 14. Bc4 $16) 9. dxc5 (9. Rd1 dxc4 10. Bxc4 Bxc4 11. Qxc4 cxd4 12. Nxd4 Qc8 13. Qe2 Nc6 $11 {1/2 Bhat,V (2481)-Kudrin,S (2566)/Tulsa 2008/CBM 123 Extra (19)}) (9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Nxd5 Bxd5 11. Bc4 Bxf3! 12. gxf3 Nc6 $132) 9... Nbd7!? 10. c6 (10. cxb6 Nc5 11. Qb4 axb6 $44) 10... Nc5 11. Qd1 (11. c7 Qc8 12. Qb4 dxc4 13. Bxc4 Nh5 $132) (11. Qb4 Rc8 $132) 11... dxc4 12. Qxd8 Rfxd8 13. Nd4 Nd5 14. Nxe6 Nxe6 15. Nxd5 Rxd5 16. Rxc4 Bxb2 $10 {Vainerman,I (2385)-Serebro,A/USSR 1987/Inf 45}) 7... c6 (7... Nh5!? 8. Bg5 b6 9. b4 a5 10. a3 axb4 11. axb4 bxc5 12. bxc5 h6 13. Bh4 f5 14. Bd3 Nf6 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. f4 c6 17. Nf3 Nd7 $13 {1/2 Van der Werf,M (2389)-Negi,P (2526)/Wijk aan Zee 2008/CBM 122 (56)}) 8. Bd3 (8. h3 b6 9. b4 Ne4 10. Nf3 a5 11. Na4 Nd7? (11... b5 $142 12. Nb6 Ra7 $13) 12. b5! bxc5? (12... cxb5 13. Bxb5 bxc5 14. dxc5 $16) 13. bxc6 g5 14. Nxg5 Nxg5 15. Bxg5 Nf6 16. Nxc5 Qd6 17. Bb5 {1-0 Timoscenko,G (2494)-Michalik,P (2330)/Banska Stiavnica 2006/CBM 113 ext}) 8... Bg4!? {Black starts preparing ...e7-e5.} (8... b6 9. b4 a5 {is another plan.}) 9. Qc2 (9. Nge2 {looks more logical.}) 9... Nfd7!? 10. Bxb8?! (10. h3 e5! 11. Bxe5 (11. dxe5 Be6 12. Nf3 Nxc5 $11) 11... Nxe5 12. hxg4 (12. dxe5 Be6 13. f4 Nd7 14. Nce2 f6 15. f5! gxf5 16. Nd4 Re8 $44) 12... Nxg4 13. Be2 $11 {S.Zagrebelny}) 10... Nxb8! (10... Rxb8 11. h3 Be6 $11) 11. h3 Bc8 12. f4 (12. Nf3 Nd7 {[%cal Ye7e5]}) 12... b6 13. Na4 (13. b4 a5 14. a3 axb4 15. axb4 Na6! 16. Bxa6 (16. Rb1 bxc5 17. bxc5 Qa5 $17 {[%cal Ra6c5,Ra6b4]}) (16. Qb2 bxc5 17. bxc5 Qa5 18. Ra1 Nxc5! 19. Rxa5 Nxd3+ 20. Kd2 Nxb2 21. Rxa8 Nc4+ $17) 16... Bxa6 17. Nf3 bxc5 18. bxc5 f6 $15 {[%cal Ye7e5]}) 13... e5! {[%mdl 512] In Kasparov's style! Black opens up the position against the opponent's uncastled king.} 14. dxe5 (14. fxe5 f6 15. e6 Re8 $15 {[%csl Re3]}) 14... f6 15. exf6 Qxf6 16. Nf3 (16. cxb6!? axb6 17. a3 (17. Nxb6 Rxa2) (17. Nf3 b5 18. Nc3 Qe7 19. Kf2 Na6 $36) 17... b5 18. Nb6 Ra7 19. Nxc8 Rxc8 $36 {[%cal Ya7e7,Yc8e8]}) 16... Qe7 17. Kf2 b5 18. Nc3 Na6 19. Qd2 (19. Nd1 Nb4 20. Qd2 Nxd3+ 21. Qxd3 Bf5 22. Qd2 Be4 {[%cal Rg6g5]} 23. h4 Rae8 $17) (19. Bxb5 Bf5! $17) 19... Nxc5 $17 {Black has recaptured the pawn and has a lot of advantages: a bishop pair, weakness of the white king and the e3-pawn.} 20. Bb1 Kh8 (20... Bb7 {[%cal Ya8e8]}) (20... Bd7) 21. b4?! {[%csl Rc4] Additionally weakening the c4-square.} (21. Ne2!? {[%cal Ye2d4]}) 21... Nb7 22. Ne2 Nd6! 23. Ned4 (23. Rxc6 Nc4 24. Qd3 a5! $17 {[%csl Rc6]}) 23... Nc4 24. Qd3 Bd7 25. h4 {This counterattack is White's only chance.} Rae8 26. Rce1 (26. h5 Nxe3 27. hxg6 Rxf4 28. Rxh7+ Kg8 $17 {S.Zagrebelny}) 26... Qxb4 27. h5 Nd6 (27... Bf5!? 28. Qb3 Qd6 $17) 28. hxg6 Ne4+ 29. Kg1 h6 30. Rc1 Qb2 31. Qc2 Qa3 ({Simpler is} 31... Qxc2 32. Bxc2 Ng3 33. Rh4 c5 {with an overwhelming position.}) 32. Nb3 c5 33. Re1 Bf5 34. Nh4 c4 35. Nxf5 Rxf5 36. Nd2 Rxf4! 37. Qd1! (37. exf4?? Bd4+ $19) 37... Rf6 (37... Rff8!? {was probably a bit more accurate:} 38. Nxe4 dxe4 39. Qd7 (39. Rh5 a6) 39... Re7 40. Qxb5 Qc3 41. Re2 Qf6 $19) 38. Nxe4 dxe4 39. Qd7 {The white queen (not exchanged by Black on move 31) becomes extremely active.} Qe7?! (39... Re7! 40. Qxb5 Qc3 41. Qb8+ Rf8 42. Qg3 Qd2 {S.Zagrebelny} 43. Rf1 Rxf1+ 44. Kxf1 Re8 {[%cal Rd2d1]} 45. Kg1 Be5! $19) 40. Qxb5 Qe6? {The 40th move!} (40... Rxg6 {was still winning a pawn and eventually the game as} 41. Qxc4? {led to a mating attack for Black:} Rxg2+ 42. Kxg2 Qg5+ 43. Kf2 (43. Kh3 Be5 44. Rhg1 Qh5+ 45. Kg2 Qf3#) 43... Rf8+ 44. Ke2 Qg2+ 45. Kd1 Rd8+ $19 {S.Zagrebelny}) 41. Rh4 {White now keeps the material balance and equalizes.} Rf5 42. Qa4 {[%cal Rh4e4,Ra4a7]} Ref8 43. Rxe4 Qxg6 44. Re8 Rxe8 45. Bxf5 Qf7 46. Qd7 Qxd7 47. Bxd7 Rd8 48. Rd1 c3 49. Ba4 Rxd1+ 50. Bxd1 Be5 51. Kf1 Kg7 52. Ke2 h5 53. Kd3 h4 54. Bf3 Kf6 55. Kc2 Ke6 56. Kd3 Kf6 57. Kc2 Ke6 58. Kd3 1/2-1/2

