1. d4 e6 2. c4 Bb4+ 3. Bd2 Bxd2+ 4. Qxd2 f5 {An unusual way of transposing from an accelerated Bogo-Indian to a Dutch Defence. Had White captured with the knight on d2, Black would have had better possibilities of setting up the Dutch dark-squared plan with ...c6 and ...e5, because White would have had no pressure in the centre at all.} 5. g3 Nf6 6. Bg2 O-O 7. Nf3 d6 8. O-O Qe7 9. Nc3 c6!? {A relatively new plan. Instead of playing on the dark squares, Black transposes to a Stonewall, which may seem paradoxical given the fact that the dark-squared bishops have been exchanged. The subtlety justifying such an approach is that White has developed his queen's knight on a square far away from Black's main weakness, e5. Of course, he can initiate the typical manoeuvre e3, e2-f4-d3, but this is time consuming, allowing Black catching up in development. I was surprised to notice how important a single tempo can be in what looks like a "slow" position. More about it later.} ({It has long been established that Black's slight delay in development makes the plan of opening the centre quite risky. Here is a classic example:} 9... Nc6 10. Rfe1 e5 11. Nd5 Qd8 12. dxe5 dxe5 13. Rad1 e4 14. Ng5 $36 {Botvinnik,M-Ilyin Zhenevsky,A/Leningrad 1930/EXT 99 (1-0, 39)}) 10. Rfe1 ({Carlsen had played this line before:} 10. Rad1 d5 11. Qc2 Nbd7 12. Rfe1 ({As proved by the main game, defending the pawn with} 12. b3 {may allow a later freeing operation based on ...dxc4, bxc4 ...c5.} b6 13. cxd5 {After b2-b3, which prevents the actication of the queen with a4, this does not look too scary.} cxd5 14. Qb2 Ba6 {Since the white queen has no access to the a-file the bishops stands well here. One may ask, why should this bishop be "worse" than its rival?} 15. Rd2 Rac8 16. Rc1 Rc7 17. Rdc2 Rfc8 18. Nd1 Qd6 $11 {Haik,A (2475)-Ivkov,B (2525)/Cannes 1986/MCL (1/2, 41). Black has no problems at all.}) 12... Ne4 (12... dxc4?! {offers White a strong initiative after} 13. e4 f4 14. Ne2 $14 {To be compared with the similar line given in a later note, in which the queen rook would be on a1 still.}) 13. e3 Nd6 14. c5 Nf7 {Black is ready to open the centre with ...e5. Although this will leave White with the control of d4, the kingside initiative should not be neglected.} 15. b4 e5 16. dxe5 Nfxe5 17. Nd4 Nf6 18. Na4 Kh8 19. Qb3 Bd7 20. a3 Rae8 21. Nb2 g5 22. f4 Ng6 23. Nd3 gxf4 24. exf4 Ne4 25. Nf3 Qf6 26. Nde5 Nxe5 27. Nxe5 Bc8 28. Qe3 h5 $132 {Maiwald,J (2508)-Carlsen,M (2786)/Germany 2008/CBM 127 Extra (0-1, 38). It is curious that computers give White a clear advantage in this position. I would be rather worried by Black's attack, without seeing a clear queenside plan to compensate for it.}) (10. Rae1 d5 11. Ne5 Nbd7 12. f4 {This brutal way of occupying the e5-square is not too promising.} Ne4 13. Nxe4 dxe4 14. Nxd7 Bxd7 15. c5 Be8 {The position has been stabilised, Black development is entirely satisfactory and once again one would ask: whose bishop is better?} 16. e3 Bh5 17. h3 Rad8 18. Qf2 g5 {Displaying some ambitions already.} 19. fxg5 Qxg5 (19... e5!? 20. dxe5 Qxc5 $132) 20. Qf4 Qg7 21. Rf2 Bg6 22. Ref1 Rfe8 23. Qg5 Qe7 24. Qxe7 Rxe7 25. g4 fxg4 26. hxg4 e5 $132 {Gyimesi,Z (2592)-Glek,I (2512)/Germany 2009/CBM 128 Extra (1-0, 39). Black later lost only as a consequence of an endgame blunder.}) 10... d5 11. e3 {Preparing e2-f4-d3.} (11. Qc2 Ne4 (11... Nbd7!?) 12. Rab1 (12. e3 Bd7?! {This is too slow, allowing White to regroup in time. He should have chosen Carlsen's plan from the previous game.} 13. Ne2 Be8 14. Nf4 Nd7 15. Nd3 Ndf6 16. Nfe5 $16 {Kharitonov,A (2585)-Ovetchkin,R (2475)/Moscow 1998/CBM 063/[Tyomkin,D] (1-0, 44). White is in complete control of the position.}) 12... Nd7 13. b4?! {This attacking move only creates weaknesses.} Nd6! 14. cxd5?! (14. c5!?) 14... cxd5 15. Ne5 Kh8! (15... Nxe5 16. dxe5 Nc4? 17. Nxd5! $18) 16. f4 (16. Bxd5 exd5 17. Nxd5 Qd8 18. Nc7 Nb6 19. Nxa8 Nxa8 $132) 16... Nxe5 17. dxe5 Nc4 18. Qd3 Bd7 19. e4 {This not only fails to shake Black's stability, but also clears an important diagonal for the "bad" bishop.} fxe4 20. Nxe4 Be8 21. Nc5 Bg6 22. Be4 $8 b6 (22... Nxe5 23. fxe5 dxe4 $15) 23. Bxg6 bxc5 24. Bxh7 g5 $132 {Nikolaidis,K (2308)-Glek,I (2553)/Korinthos 2000/EXT 2001 (0-1, 36)}) 11... Nbd7 12. b3 $146 {This kind of queenside tension offers Black additional ideas for counterplay.} ({Quite possibly, Giri did not like} 12. Qc2 {because of} dxc4!? $146 (12... b6?! 13. cxd5! {With the d1-a4 diagonal open, this move is strong.} cxd5 14. Rac1 Bb7 15. Qa4 a6 16. Ne2 Rfc8 17. Nf4 Ne4 18. Nd3 g5 19. Nfe5 Ndf6 20. Qb3 Qd6 21. Red1 g4 22. Nf4 $16 {with complete domination on dark squares, Krizsany,L (2430)-Hardicsay,P (2395)/Hungary 1996/EXT 2000 (1-0, 32)}) 13. e4 ({The attempt to retrieve the pawn releases the pressure in the centre, allowing Black adequate counterplay:} 13. Nd2 Nb6 14. Bf1 e5 15. Nxc4 Nxc4 16. Bxc4+ Be6 17. Bxe6+ Qxe6 18. dxe5 Qxe5 19. Qb3+ Rf7 20. Rad1 Re8 $11) 13... f4!? 14. Ne2 (14. gxf4 Nh5 $36) 14... fxg3 15. hxg3 b5 16. b3 {White has obvious compensation for the pawn, but apparently not more than that. We have to continue the line for many more moves in order to illustrate one possible difference if compared to the position with the extra tempo ad1 mentioned above.} Ba6 17. bxc4 bxc4 18. Nf4 e5 19. dxe5 Nxe5 20. Nxe5 Qxe5 21. Bf1 Kh8 22. Bxc4 Bxc4 23. Qxc4 {White has retrieved the pawn and has the more compact structure. Black should hurry with his counterplay.} Ng4! 24. Qxc6 Nxf2! 25. Kxf2 {In the aforementioned line, White would win with 25.d5!, which is not available now.} g5 26. Qd5 Qg7 $13) (12. Qe2 Ne4 {leaves White without the possibility of continuing his knight maneouvre.}) 12... b6 13. Ne2 Bb7 14. Nf4 {White has completed his regrouping, but Black has a harmonious dveelopment and can open play to his favour.} dxc4!? (14... Ne4 15. Qb2 c5 $132 {is another possible continuation.}) 15. bxc4 c5 $132 16. Ng5?! {This over-optimistic move hands over the initiative.} Bxg2 17. Kxg2 e5 18. Nfe6 (18. dxe5 Nxe5 $15 {[%cal Ge7b7]}) 18... Ne4! 19. Nxe4 Qxe6 20. Ng5?! {Losing a pawn without compensation.} (20. d5 Qh6 21. Nc3 e4 $15 {The weakness of the f3-square is more important than that of the e6-square for two main reasons. First, a knight on f3 would be closer to the king. Secondly, the e6-knight can be removed with an exchange sacrifice.} 22. Ne2 Ne5 23. Qc3 Rae8 24. Nf4 g5 25. Ne6 Rxe6 26. dxe6 Qxe6 {The c4-pawn is in danger and the kingside attack remains a permanent danger.}) 20... Qxc4 21. dxc5 (21. dxe5 Nxe5 22. Rac1 Rad8! $19) 21... Nxc5 22. Rec1 Qd3 $17 {Black has parried any infiltration of White's pieces and converting the pawn remains a matter of technique.} 23. Qxd3 Nxd3 24. Rc7 h6 25. Nf3 Rf7 26. Rc3 Rd7 27. a4 Rad8 28. Rb1 Rd5 29. Rc7 R8d7 30. Rxd7 Rxd7 31. Rb5 Rd6 32. a5 e4 33. Nd4 g6 34. Rb1 Kf7 35. h3 Ke7 36. Nb5 Rd5 37. axb6 axb6 38. Nd4 Rd6 39. Nb5 Rf6 40. f3 exf3+ 41. Kxf3 Ne5+ 42. Ke2 Nd7 43. Nd4 Rd6 44. g4 fxg4 45. hxg4 Ne5 46. Nb5 Rf6 47. Rb4 Rf2+ 48. Kxf2 Nd3+ 49. Kf3 Nxb4 50. Kf4 Kf6 51. Nc3 Nd3+ 52. Ke4 Nc5+ 53. Kf4 g5+ 54. Kf3 Ke5 55. Nb5 Ne4 56. Na7 Nd2+ 57. Ke2 Nc4 58. Nc6+ Ke4 59. Ne7 Nxe3 60. Nc8 Nxg4 61. Nxb6 Ne5 62. Kf2 h5 63. Kg3 h4+ 64. Kh3 Kf3 65. Nc8 Nd3 66. Nd6 Nf4+ 67. Kh2 g4 68. Nf5 g3+ 69. Kh1 g2+ 70. Kh2 Kf2 0-1

