1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Bg4 4. Nf3 ({Carlsen does not feel prepared for the unclear complications arising after} 4. f3 Bf5 5. Bb5+ Nbd7 6. c4 e6 {, see for instance Finkel,A (2467)-Gershon,A (2502)/Ramat Aviv/Modiin 2000/CBM 081/[Finkel,A] (1-0, 34)}) 4... Nxd5 5. h3 {After this move, the queen will get on an active position, although in some lines it will also stand in the way of its own bishops.} (5. Be2 {looks more flexible, but also less ambitious, see for instance Anand,V (2720)-Kamsky,G (2695)/Sanghi Nagar 1994/CBM 042/[Ftacnik] (0-1, 75)}) 5... Bxf3 6. Qxf3 c6 7. Be2 {Definitely, this does not look like a harmonious way of developing, with the bishop behind the queen. However, the alternatives tend to offer less.} (7. Bd3 g6 8. O-O Bg7 {more or less forces the passive} 9. c3 {, when Black's position is relatively safe after} O-O) (7. Bc4 g6 {leaves the d5-knight stable, at least temporarily.}) (7. c3 {consolidates the centre, but delays the development.} Nd7 (7... g6 {also looks OK}) 8. Nd2 e5 $132 {Szabo,K (2489)-Varga,Z (2481)/Budapest 2009/CBM 132 Extra (0-1, 51)}) 7... g6 8. O-O Bg7 9. Rd1 {This active way of defending the d4-pawn would not have been possible with the bishop on d3...} O-O 10. c4 {...while this move would been physically prevented by c4.} Nc7! $146 {The only way to make the pressure against the d4-pawn effective. Among others, Black intends to break up the centre with a well timed ...e5. In the event of a pawn exchange, this would lead to a structure which has been discussed in the notes to the game Caruana-Tiviakov from the same tournament.} (10... Nb6?! {allows the natural development with} 11. Nc3! e5 ({With the knight on b6,} 11... Bxd4? {leads to trouble:} 12. c5! Nc8 13. Bh6 Re8 14. Bc4 $18) 12. d5 $16 {Cayford,T-Hickmann,H/USA-ch1 sf corr 1972/Corr Nr. 1 (1-0, 20)}) 11. Qe4! {Although moving with the queen for the second time before having even touched the queenside pieces looks unnatural, this is the best way to restore the coordination: the e2-bishop enjoys maximum mobility now. In concrete terms, White not only defends d4, but also prevents ...e5. The deviation from the natural way of developing is enabled by White's significant space advantage and mighty bishop pair.} ({The generally desirable} 11. Nc3 {is less effective than in the aforementioned game.} Bxd4 12. Bh6 e5!? 13. Bxf8 Qxf8 $44 {[%cal Gc7e6,Gb8d7] Black is in total control of the dark squares and has excellent compensation for the exchange.}) (11. Be3 {has the abstract defect of developing the bishop before the knight, leaving the b2-square vulnerable:} e5 12. d5 (12. dxe5 Qe7 13. Nc3 Nd7 {looks OK for Black.}) 12... e4! (12... cxd5 13. cxd5 e4 14. Qxe4 Bxb2 15. d6 Bxa1 16. Qxb7 $16) 13. Qxe4 Bxb2 14. dxc6 Nxc6 15. Rxd8 Raxd8 16. Na3 {The only way to keep fighting for the initiative.} Bxa1 17. Qb1! Be5 18. Qxb7 Nd4 19. Bf1 Nce6 $13) 11... f5!? {This extravagant idea is the only way to dislodge the queen from its central location while maintaining the pressure against the d4-pawn. However, this specific way of carrying it out may be inaccurate.} ({After} 11... Ne6?! 12. Be3 {it is already impossible to play ...f5-f4 under favourable circumstances:} Qc8 (12... Qd6 13. Nc3) 13. Nc3 f5 14. Qd3 f4 15. Bg4 h5 (15... fxe3 16. Qxe3 Rf5 17. d5) 16. Bxe6+ Qxe6 17. d5 $36) ({Black should probably have inserted the developing moves} 11... Nd7 12. Nc3 {The difference is that in some lines the queen's transfer to b3 is prevented.} f5 13. Qe3 (13. Qh4 Ne6 14. d5 Bf6 15. Qg3 Nd4 $13) 13... Qe8 {[%cal Ge7e5]} 14. f4 {(The same idea as in the game)} Rd8 15. Qf2 (15. b3 c5 $132) 15... Nf6 16. Be3 b5 $132 {[%csl Gd5,Ge4][%cal Gb5c4,Gb5b4]}) 12. Qe3 {White has to obstruct his other bishop.} (12. Qh4 {fails to keep the d4-square well defended:} Ne6 13. d5 (13. Bh6? Nxd4 $17) (13. Be3 Bf6) (13. Nc3 Bf6 14. Qg3 Nxd4) 13... Bf6 14. Qg3 Nd4 $132) 12... Kh8 {There was no absolute need for this prophylactic move.} (12... Nd7 {looks better. White can deviate from the aforementioned line with} 13. Qb3 Rb8 14. Nc3 $14 {followed by f4 and, possibly, c5.}) 13. Nc3 ({Here, too} 13. Qb3!? {comes into consideration, especially since Black does not have ...b8 at his disposal.}) 13... Nd7 14. b3 {White is just a few steps away from restoring the absolute harmony of his position with b2, d2, ad1.} (14. d5 {is premature:} Qe8 15. Qg3 e5 16. Bg5 ({After} 16. d6 Ne6 {[%csl Gc5] White cannot connect his pawns with c5.}) 16... Qf7 $13 {White's central pawn looks threatening, but the force of Black's duo should not be underestimated.}) 14... Qe8 15. f4! {Short may have underestimated this move during the planning of the previous phase of the game. Suddenly, Black is left without adequate counterplay.} (15. Bb2 e5 16. d5 e4 $132) (15. Ba3 Rf7 16. Rd2 e5 $132) 15... Qf7 16. Bb2 Rad8 17. Bf3 Bf6 18. Rd2 Qg7 19. Rad1 g5 {The only way to question White's strategic supremacy, but this creates new weaknesses as well.} 20. Ne2! {[%cal Ge2g3]} Rg8 21. Kf1!? {Preventively evacuating the dark squares. The king would have been less safe on h1 because of the possible ...g4.} h6 22. Ng3 Qg6 23. Qd3?! {Allowing Black unexpected counterplay.} (23. Nh5 g4 24. hxg4 fxg4 25. Nxf6 $16) 23... gxf4 24. Nh5 Nc5 25. Qc2 Ne4 26. Re2 (26. Bxe4 Qxh5 27. Bxf5 f3 $132) 26... Ng3+ 27. Nxg3 fxg3 $13 {White retains the more compact position, but is a pawn down and his king may become vulnerable.} 28. d5 cxd5 29. cxd5 Bxb2 30. Qxb2+ Qg7 31. Qc1 Rd7 32. Rde1 Rf8 33. Re5 Nb5 34. Qc5 Nd6 35. Qxa7 Ne4 36. Qd4 e6 37. Rd1 exd5 38. Rxd5 Rxd5 39. Qxg7+ Kxg7 40. Rxd5 Kf6 41. Bxe4 fxe4 42. Ke2 Ke6 43. Rd4 Rf2+ 44. Ke3 Rxg2 45. Rxe4+ Kd5 46. Rd4+ Ke5 47. Re4+ 1/2-1/2

