1. e4 Nc6 {Carlsen's opening surprises do not... surprise anybody anymore. Usually, it is a about a rare move or line in the early phase of the game, but it seems that he also intends trying out all the openings, one by one! Apparently, he has never played this opening before.} 2. Nf3 ({We can only speculate about what he had in mind after} 2. d4 {Possibly the "main line"} d5 3. e5 Bf5 {with some sort of Advance Caro Kann structure.}) 2... d6 3. d4 Nf6 {Black's intention is to transpose to a classical Pirc, where due to the early commitment of the queen's knight he will have to play ...g4, but this is one of Black's main weapons anyway.} 4. d5 {With both central pawns under pressure, this is the only way of transposing to some sort of King's Indian, where the pawn will stand on c4 instead of c2.} ({It is worth mentioning that Carlsen had twice played} 4. Nc3 Bg4 5. d5 ({If} 5. Be2 g6 6. O-O Bg7 {play is not far from transposing to a genuine Pirc.}) 5... Nb8 (5... Bxf3 {is premature:} 6. Qxf3 Ne5 7. Qg3 c6 8. f4 Ned7 9. dxc6 bxc6 10. e5 Nd5 11. exd6 exd6 12. Nxd5 cxd5 13. Bd2 $16 {Carlsen,M (2385)-Kiik,K (2439) Rethymno 2003 (1-0, 27)}) 6. h3 Bxf3 7. Qxf3 Nbd7 (7... g6 {may be more flexible, leaving the d7-square available for the king's knight.}) 8. g4 {A logical way to try taking advantage of the unusual move order.} g6 9. Be3 Bg7 10. O-O-O c5 11. Qe2 (11. Qg2 {may have slowed down a bit the queenside counter-attack.} b5 12. Bxb5!?) 11... b5 12. g5 (12. Qxb5 Rb8 {may be less dangerous than it seems, but in blitz this is not an easy decision to take.}) (12. Nxb5 Nxe4) 12... Nh5 13. Nxb5 Rb8 14. c3 Qa5 15. a4 Nb6 (15... O-O 16. Qc2 Nb6 $11) 16. Nc7+ Kd8 17. Qa6 Qxa4 (17... Bxc3!? 18. Qxa5 Bxa5 19. Na6 Rc8 20. Bb5) 18. Bb5 Qxa6 19. Nxa6 Rb7 20. Bc6 $16 {Carlsen,M (2851)-Petrosian,T (2611) chess.com INT (blitz) 2016 (1-0, 34)}) (4. Bb5 a6 (4... Nxe4? 5. d5 a6 6. Bd3 {wins a piece}) (4... Bd7!? 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 {or 6...g4}) 5. Ba4 (5. Bxc6+ bxc6 6. Qe2) 5... b5 6. Bb3 Nxe4? (6... Bg4) (6... Bb7) (6... Na5) 7. Bd5 {1-0 De Jong,T (2258)-Lindeman,J (1985) Utrecht 2017}) 4... Nb8 5. Bd3 g6 ({In view of the line examined below it makes sense to play} 5... Bg4 {at once. But probably Carlsen thought it would be better avoiding the same kind of treatment as in his games with White, based on an early h2-h3.}) 6. O-O Bg7 7. c4 O-O 8. Nc3 (8. h3!? e5? {This transposes to a King's Indian with two tempi less for Black.} (8... Na6 {is more flexible:} 9. Nc3 Nc5 10. Bc2 a5) 9. Nc3 Na6 10. a3 Nh5 11. Re1 Qe8 12. b4 h6 13. c5 f5 14. Bd2 Nf6 15. exf5 gxf5 16. Nh4 e4 17. c6 bxc6 18. Bxa6 Bxa6 19. Nxf5 cxd5 20. Bxh6 Bxh6 21. Nxh6+ Kh7 22. Ng4 $18 {Tabatabaei,M (2509)-Maghsoodloo,P (2584) Teheran 2017 (½-½, 59)}) 8... Bg4 9. Be2 ({After castling short White does not get much with} 9. h3 Bxf3 10. Qxf3 Nbd7 11. Qe2 c6 12. Bd2 a5 13. b3 Nc5 14. Bc2 e6 (14... Re8 {[%cal Ge7e6]}) 15. dxe6 (15. Rad1!?) 15... Nxe6 16. Rad1 Nd7 17. Qd3 {Poniaev,R (2265)-Nikolaev,N (2411) Dagomys 2010 (1-0, 53)} a4! 18. Nxa4 (18. Qxd6 axb3 19. axb3 Nd4 20. Rc1 Be5 21. Qb4 b6 $17 {[%csl Gb4]}) 18... Ndc5 19. Nxc5 Nxc5 20. Qg3 Rxa2 21. Bb1 Rb2 22. b4 Ne6 $132) 9... Nfd7 {In the first phase, Black consolidates his control on the dark squares. Later he will break on the light squares. Quite logical and consistent. White has more space but cannot create threats easily.} 10. Bg5 a5 11. Nd2 Bxe2 12. Qxe2 c6 13. Kh1 a4 14. a3 {0-1 Botvinnik,M-Geller,E Budapest Maroczy Memorial 1952 (2) Frankly, I am very much against such self-blocking moves if they do not lead to something concrete, for instance winning the a4-pawn. The first time I was puzzled by such a move was when I saw the game} (14. Rac1 {is a more flexible way of neutralising the effects of ...a4-a3.} Na6 15. f4 (15. Nxa4 Nb4) 15... Nac5 16. Nf3 Re8 {[%cal Gd8a5,Ge7e6] Maybe White wanted to prevent the immediate ...a6, but freezing the queenside offers Black comfortable play.}) 14... Nc5 15. f4 Nbd7 16. Nf3 Qc7 17. Rad1 (17. Bxe7? Rfe8 18. Bh4 Bxc3 19. bxc3 Nxe4 20. Qc2 cxd5 21. cxd5 Qxc3 $17) {Later Carlsen will change the rooks' placement.} 17... Rae8 {[%cal Ge7e6]} ({He might have refrained from} 17... Rfe8 {due to} 18. e5 f6 (18... dxe5 19. fxe5 Nxe5 20. Bf4 f6 21. dxc6 bxc6 22. Ne4 Nxe4 23. Qxe4 $44) 19. e6 fxg5 20. exd7 Qxd7 21. Nxg5 {when it would be of course better having the other rook on f8. But even this looks acceptable for Black after} e6) 18. f5 {The only way to fight against the planned ...e7-e6, turning the e4-pawn vulnerable. But now the weakness of the e5-square increases Black's stability and manoeuvring possibilities, while White does not have real attacking chances due to his strategic problems on other sectors of the board.} Qa5 (18... e6?! 19. dxe6 fxe6 20. fxg6 hxg6 21. Be3 $14 {[%csl Gg6][%cal Gf3g5]}) 19. Na2 {Heading for b4 but weakening e4.} Nf6 20. Nd2 Ncd7 21. Nf3 Nc5 22. Nd2 Ncd7 23. Nf3 Rc8 {Carlsen of course plays on. Black's position is too pleasant to agree to a draw by repetition.} 24. Nb4 Rfe8 {SLightly careless.} (24... cxd5 25. cxd5 Rc5 $132) 25. Bd2 ({Missing a good chance for activity.} 25. e5! Nxe5 (25... dxe5 26. dxc6 bxc6 27. Bxf6 Nxf6 28. Nxe5 $16 {The c6-pawn is hanging but there is also a surprising kingside threat:} Qb6?! 29. Nxf7! Kxf7 30. Qe6+ Kf8 31. fxg6 hxg6 32. Nd3 $18 {[%cal Gd3e5]}) 26. Nxe5 dxe5 27. d6 $16 {Black is suddenly hanging.}) 25... Qc7 26. dxc6 {White could not stand the pressure anymore.} (26. Bc3 cxd5 27. cxd5 Qc4 28. Rfe1 Qxe2 29. Rxe2 Nc5 30. e5 Nfe4 $15) 26... bxc6 27. Bc3 e5 $11 {Blck has stabilised the centre with good play, but White is not without chances either due to the pressure along the f-file.} (27... e6 28. fxg6 hxg6 29. e5 $36) 28. Nd2 (28. fxe6 Rxe6 $17) (28. fxg6 hxg6 {[%csl Gd4][%cal Gd7c5,Gc5e6] clears the queen's knight's path to d4.} 29. Ng5 Nc5 30. Qe1 Nh7) 28... Nc5 29. Rf3 Nh5 30. g3 Bh6 31. Rdf1 Qe7 32. Nd3 Nxd3 {After this natural exchange Black loses stability in the centre.} (32... Nd7!? {[%cal Gh5f6,Gd6d5]}) 33. Rxd3 Nf6 34. Rdf3 Rf8 35. c5 {The idea is right, but White could have prepared it} (35. Nb1!? $14 {[%csl Ga4][%cal Gc3d2,Gb1c3] This not only prepares the regrouping illustrated with arrows but also removes the knight from the attacked square, making the pressure on e5 real.} Rb8 36. c5 d5 37. exd5 Nxd5 38. Qxe5 $16) 35... d5 36. exd5 cxd5 37. fxg6 (37. c6!? {wins an exchange but with good compensation for Black.} g5!? (37... Rxc6 38. Bb4 {forces} Rd6 {due to the pressure on the f-file but Black seems to be doing well.} 39. Rd3 Rfd8) 38. Bb4 (38. Qxe5 Qxe5 39. Bxe5 Rxc6 40. Bxf6 Rxf6 41. Rd3 Rb6 {is also playable for Black.}) 38... Qe8 39. Bxf8 Kxf8 40. Rc3 g4 $44) 37... fxg6 38. Qxe5 Qxe5 39. Bxe5 Nd7 40. Rxf8+ Bxf8 {Black's endgame is slightly more pleasant already due to the queenside configuration. But a draw is the most probable result.} 41. Bd4 (41. Bd6 $11) 41... Nxc5 42. Rc1 Bh6 43. Bxc5 (43. Rxc5 Rxc5 44. Bxc5 Bxd2 45. Kg2 $11) 43... Bxd2 44. Rd1 Rxc5 45. Rxd2 Kf7 46. Rd4 Ke6 47. Rxa4 Rc2 48. Kg1 Rxb2 49. Rh4 Rb7 (49... h5 50. g4 hxg4 51. Rxg4 Kf5 52. Rd4 Ke5 53. Rg4 d4 54. Kf1 d3 55. Ke1 $11) 50. Kf2 Kd6 51. Ke3 Kc5 52. Kd3 Rb3+ 53. Kc2 Rxa3 54. Rxh7 Kc4 55. Rc7+ Kd4 56. Rc6 Ra2+ 57. Kb3 Rxh2 58. Rxg6 Kd3 59. g4 d4 60. g5 Rg2 61. Rh6 Ke2 (61... Rxg5 62. Rh3+ {is similar to the game.}) 62. Rh1 Rg3+ 63. Kb2 Rxg5 64. Rh2+ Kd3 65. Rh3+ Kc4 66. Kc2 Rg2+ 67. Kd1 Rb2 68. Rg3 Rh2 69. Rf3 Rh1+ 70. Kd2 Rh2+ 71. Kd1 Rh1+ 72. Kd2 Rh2+ 1/2-1/2

