1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Nf3 Nb6 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. e3 O-O 9. O-O Re8 10. Re1 a5 11. Qd2?! {A very strange move. I still believe that it was a "lapsus manus": Carlsen intended to play the theoretical} (11. Qe2) 11... e5 12. d5 Nb4 13. e4 c6 14. a3 cxd5! {Quite an obvious refutation of White's play.} 15. axb4 axb4 16. Rxa8 bxc3 17. bxc3 Nxa8 18. exd5?! (18. Qxd5 $142 Nb6 (18... Qxd5 19. exd5 Nb6 20. Nd2 {[%cal Yc3c4]}) 19. Qa5 {although Black, controlling the crucial c4-square, keeps a small edge after} Qf6 20. Bg5 Qc6 {[%csl Gc4]}) 18... Nb6 {[%csl Rd5]} 19. Rd1?! (19. d6 Re6 (19... Nc4!?)) (19. c4!? Nxc4 20. Qb4 Nd6 21. Be3 {getting some activity for the missing pawn although Black is obviously better.}) 19... e4 20. Ng5?? {[%mdl 8192] Just blundering a piece in a poor position.} (20. Ne1 Bg4 21. f3 e3 22. Qe2 Bd7 23. Bxe3 (23. c4 Nxc4! 24. Qxc4 e2 25. Rd2 Qb6+ 26. Kh1 Qf2 $19) 23... Ba4 {[%cal Yb6d5]} 24. Rd2 Bxc3 $19) (20. Nh4 h6 $19 {[%cal Rg6g5]}) ({It was relatively better (but hopeless enough) just to give up the d5-pawn:} 20. Nd4 Qxd5 $19) 20... e3 21. Qb2 Qxg5 22. Bxe3 (22. Qxb6 e2 23. Re1 Qxc1 24. Rxc1 e1=Q+ 25. Rxe1 Rxe1+ 26. Bf1 Bh3 $19) 22... Qg4 (22... Qg4 23. f3 Nc4) 0-1

