1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Bb7 5. Bg2 Be7 6. O-O O-O 7. Nc3 Ne4 8. Bd2 Bf6 9. Rc1 c5 {The most common. Provoking the d4-d5 advance would make the f6 more powerful, but it would also lock in the other bishop for a while.} ({In the following important encounter White was able to get some advantage after:} 9... Nxd2 10. Qxd2 d6 11. d5 e5 12. e4 Nd7 13. h4 a5 14. Bh3 Nc5 15. Kg2 Bc8 16. Bxc8 Qxc8 17. Qe2 Be7 18. Nd2 $14 {Tomashevsky,E (2731)-Ponomariov,R (2709) Baku AZE 2016}) 10. d5 exd5 11. cxd5 Nxd2 12. Nxd2 d6 13. Nde4 Be7 {This retreat looks somewhat passive, but Black wants to keep both bishops on the board.} ({The more ambitious} 13... Be5!? {looks perfectly playable too:} 14. f4 (14. e3 Ba6 15. Re1 g6 16. f4 Bg7 17. g4 b5 $132 {Marin,M (2549)-Brunello,S (2468) Edinburgh 2007}) 14... Bd4+ 15. Kh1 Ba6 16. Qd2 Qe7 17. g4 Nd7 18. Rfe1 g6 19. g5 Bg7 $132 {Gelfand,B (2703)-Almasi,Z (2644) Monte Carlo 2002}) 14. f4 f5 {This move is very committal - the e6-spot is permanently exposed now.} ({There was also the interesting} 14... Nd7!? 15. g4 a6 16. a4 Nf6! (16... Re8?! 17. g5 Bf8 18. Kh1 $14 {Karpov,A (2725)-Salov,V (2660) Linares 1993}) 17. Nf2 b5 $132) 15. Nf2 Ba6 $146 {This innovation hardly can be considered successful.} ({The previously played} 15... Na6 {looks more natural and offers Black adequate counterplay:} 16. Re1 Nc7 17. e4 (17. a3?! Bf6 18. Qd2 Qd7 $15 {Malakhatko,V (2546)-Lutz,B (2292) Basel 2011}) 17... fxe4 18. Nfxe4 Bc8 $132) 16. a4 {This natural restricting move yields Black an important tempo for consolidating.} ({A more energetic approach would be} 16. Re1! b5 17. Qc2 Nd7 18. e4 b4 19. Ncd1 fxe4 20. Bxe4 Nf6 21. Bf5 $36 {and Black would suffer from the vulnerablity of his light squares.}) 16... Bf6 17. Re1 Nd7 18. e3 ({For some reasons, Magnus deviates from the tempting} 18. e4!? Bd4 19. exf5 Rxf5 20. Bh3 Rf8 21. Ne4 Nf6 22. Ng5 $14) 18... Rb8 ({After} 18... g6 19. e4 Bd4 20. exf5 gxf5 21. Qc2 $14 {the weakness of the e6-spot starts to tell.}) 19. Qc2 (19. Bh3!?) 19... g6 20. Nb5 ({Now Black is better prepared to meet} 20. e4 Bd4 21. Kh1 (21. exf5 g5! $132) 21... Bxf2 22. Qxf2 fxe4 23. Nxe4 Ne5 $132) 20... Bxb5 21. axb5 Bg7! {An excellent prophylactic move - now the f5 is over-protected.} (21... Re8 22. Ra1 Qc7 23. e4 fxe4 24. Nxe4 Bd4+ 25. Kh1 Nf6 26. Ng5 $36) 22. Bh3 ({The immediate} 22. e4 Bd4 23. exf5 {also can be met with the same approach as in the game:} g5! (23... Rxf5 24. Bh3 Rf8 25. Kh1 $14) 24. Re4 gxf4 25. Rxf4 Qe8 26. Kh1 Nf6 $132 {with double-edged play.}) ({However,} 22. Ra1!? {deserved serious attention:} Rb7 23. e4 {Now, when Black's rook is tied down to protecting the a7, this advance becomes more effective.} Bd4 24. Kh1 Bxf2 25. Qxf2 fxe4 26. Rxe4 Nf6 27. Ree1 $14) 22... Kh8 23. e4 g5! {GM Cordova manages to make use of having the dark-squared bishop - now neither king is safe.} 24. fxg5 ({Black's tactical idea was based on the following line:} 24. Bxf5 gxf4 25. gxf4 Qh4! 26. Bxd7 Bd4 27. Bf5 Rxf5 28. exf5 Bxf2+ 29. Qxf2 Rg8+ 30. Kf1 Qh3+ 31. Ke2 Rg2 $11) 24... f4 ({Another possibility was} 24... Ne5 25. Qe2 fxe4 26. Nxe4 Nf3+ 27. Kg2 Nxg5! ({The ''greedy''} 27... Nxe1+?! 28. Rxe1 Qe7 29. Be6 $14 {leads to a passive position.}) 28. Nxg5 Qxg5 $11) 25. Bf5 fxg3 26. Nh3 {White was able to keep the g5, so the position remains quite sharp.} gxh2+ 27. Kh1 {As often happens, the h2 only makes White's monarch safer!} ({Correctly refuting} 27. Qxh2?! Be5 28. Qg2 Rg8 29. Kh1 Nf8 $15 {and White's king is in a real danger.}) 27... Be5 28. Qe2 Rf7 (28... Qe7 29. Qg4 Rf7 30. g6 Rg7 31. Ng5 Nf6 32. Qh4 $13) 29. Rc2 Rg7 (29... Nf8 30. Qh5 Rg7 31. Rf1 Qe8 32. Qh6 $13) 30. Rf1 {Both sides have time for mobilising their forces now.} Qe8 31. Qd3 Nf8 32. Rg2 Ng6 33. Bxg6 Qxg6 {Keeping the pawn on h7 looks safest for a human eye.} ({However, there was nothing wrong with} 33... hxg6 {either:} 34. Nf2 Qe7 35. Ng4 Qxg5 36. Rf3 Qc1+ 37. Rf1 Qg5 $11) 34. Nf4 Bxf4 35. Rxf4 Qh5 ({Black would also obtain sufficient counterplay after} 35... Rf7 36. Rxf7 Qxf7 37. e5 Qf4 38. e6 Rf8 $11) 36. Qc3 Kg8 {This move isn't that bad,} ({but} 36... Qd1+ 37. Kxh2 Qh5+ 38. Kg1 Qd1+ {was a more convincing way of securing a draw:} 39. Rf1 Qd4+ 40. Qxd4 cxd4 41. Rd1 Rf8 42. Rxd4 Rf4 43. Rc4 Re7 $11) 37. Qf6 Rf7? {Letting White pin the f7 is a bad decision.} ({The correct move was} 37... Qg6 38. Kxh2 Qxf6 39. Rxf6 Re8 40. Rg4 h5 41. Rgf4 Rxg5 42. Rxd6 Rge5 $11) 38. Qe6 ({Another winning way is less obvious:} 38. Qh6 Qd1+ 39. Kxh2 Rxf4 40. g6 $18) 38... Rf8 39. Rgf2? {Such mistakes in the winning positions are untypical for Magnus!} ({The correct} 39. Rf5 {would have put Black in a hopeless situation:} Qd1+ 40. Kxh2 Qh5+ 41. Kg3 Qg6 42. Rgf2 Qxe6 43. dxe6 Rxf5 44. exf5 $18) 39... c4? {GM Cordova doesn't stand the pressure till the end and loses on the spot.} ({At the same time, after} 39... Kg7 40. Qh6+ (40. Qxd6 Rxf4 41. Rxf4 Qd1+ 42. Kxh2 Qd2+ 43. Kg3 Qe3+ $11) 40... Qxh6 41. gxh6+ Kg6 42. Rxf7 Rxf7 43. Rxf7 Kxf7 44. Kxh2 Kf6 45. Kg3 Kg5 46. b3 Kxh6 47. e5 Kg7 48. e6 Kf6 {the resulting endgame seems drawish. For instance,} 49. Kg4 Kg6 50. Kf3 h5 51. Kf2 Kf6 52. Kg3 Kg7 53. Kh3 Kf6 $11) 40. g6! Qd1+ (40... Qxg6 41. Rg2 $18) 41. Rf1 1-0

