1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Qb3 dxc4 5. Qxc4 Bg7 6. e4 O-O 7. Be2 Nfd7 8. Be3 Nb6 9. Qd3 {A relatively new move, first applied by Kramnik against Dominguez several months ago - see CBM 128.} f5 (9... Nc6 10. Rd1 f5 11. Nf3 f4 12. Bc1 Bg4 13. e5 Qd7 14. Ne4 Rad8 15. Nc5 Qc8 16. Qc3 e6?! (16... Nd5 17. Qb3 b6 18. Ba6 Qb8 19. Ne4 Na5 20. Qa4 c5 $13) 17. h3 Bh5?! (17... Bxf3 18. Bxf3 Nxd4! 19. Bxb7 Qb8 20. Be4 Bxe5 21. Nxe6 $13) 18. O-O Nd5 19. Qb3 Na5 20. Qc2 Nc6 21. Ng5! $14 {[%csl Re6] 1/2 Kramnik,V (2772)-Dominguez Perez,L (2719)/Dresden 2008/CBM 128/[Krasenkow] (45)}) 10. Rd1 f4 11. Bc1 e5 12. d5 c6 13. Nf3 cxd5 14. Nxd5 Nxd5 15. Qb3!? $146 {[%mdl 8]} (15. Qxd5+ Qxd5 16. exd5 Bf5 17. O-O Nd7 18. Rfe1 a6 19. Bd2 Rac8 20. Ng5 Rc2 21. Ne6 Rfc8 22. Nxg7 Kxg7 23. Bc3 Kf6 24. Bf3 b5 $13 {1/2 Jakovenko,D (2737)-Mamedyarov,S (2731)/Elista RUS 2008/The Week in Chess 737 (57)}) 15... Kh8 (15... Be6!? 16. exd5 Bf7 17. Qxb7 Nd7 $44 {S.Zagrebelny Of course, this line needs further analysis.}) 16. Bc4 Nc6 17. Bxd5 Qe7 (17... Qb6 {doesn't change much:} 18. h3 Nd4 19. Nxd4 exd4 {[%cal Yg7e5]} 20. Bd2 $14 {The black bishop will be strong on e5 but White's d5 bishop is even stronger!}) 18. h3 (18. Bd2 Bg4 19. Bxc6 bxc6 20. Bb4 c5 21. Bc3 Rae8 22. h3 Bc8 23. Kd2 Rd8+ 24. Kc1 Rd4!? 25. Bxd4 exd4 26. Rhe1 Be6 27. Qa3 Rd8 28. Re2 Qc7 29. e5 Qb6 30. Ng5 Bd5 31. e6 Re8 $44 {1/2 Aleksandrov,A (2636)-Zinchenko,Y (2527)/Moscow RUS 2009/The Week in Chess 744 (44)}) 18... Nb4 (18... Nd4 19. Nxd4 exd4 20. Bd2 a5 21. Rc1 $14 {[%csl Gb3,Gd5][%cal Gc1c7]}) 19. Bc4?! {A tempo was more important than this bishop!} (19. O-O $142 ! Nxd5 $140 20. exd5 {[%csl Gd5,Re5]} e4 21. Rfe1 Re8 22. d6 Qd8 23. Nh2 $18 {[%cal Rc1f4,Rd6d7]}) 19... b5!? 20. Bxb5?! (20. Bd2! a5 (20... bxc4 21. Qxb4 Qxb4 22. Bxb4 Re8 23. O-O $16 {[%csl Re5]}) 21. Bxb4 Qxb4+ 22. Qxb4 axb4 23. Bd5 $14) 20... Nxa2 (20... Be6!? 21. Bc4 Bxc4 22. Qxc4 Rac8 $44) 21. Bd2 (21. Qxa2 Qb4+) 21... Rb8 22. Qa4 Qb7 23. O-O Qxb5 24. Qxa2 Qxb2 25. Qxa7 Qb7 $11 {Black has solved all his opening problems. But equal doesn't quite mean drawn! Several minor inaccuracies - and the Cuban GM loses this game...} 26. Qc5 Be6 27. Qd6 Bb3 28. Rb1 Qxe4 29. Bc3 Rbd8 (29... Qd5 $11 {R.Sherbakov}) 30. Qa3 Bc2?! {This bishop will be out of play and hanging.} (30... Bd5 $11) 31. Rb5 Qa4?! (31... Qc6 $142 32. Rxe5! (32. Qb2 Rc8 33. Bxe5 Bxe5 34. Rxe5 Qc3!) (32. Rc5 Qa4 33. Qb2 Rb8) 32... Ra8 33. Qb2 Be4 34. Nd4 Qb7 35. Rb5 $36 {- analysis by IM M.Notkin.}) 32. Qb2 Bd3? (32... Be4 33. Bxe5 Qa7! 34. Ra1 Qe7 35. Bxg7+ Qxg7 36. Ne5 $16) 33. Rb7! $18 {[%mdl 128] Suddenly White obtains a killing attack.} Qc2 (33... Bxf1 34. Bxe5 Rd7 35. Bxg7+ Kg8 36. Rxd7 Qxd7 37. Bxf8 $18 {M.Notkin}) (33... Rd7 34. Nxe5 Rxb7 (34... Bxe5 35. Bxe5+ Kg8 36. Rxd7 Qxd7 37. Rc1 $18) 35. Qxb7 Qa6 36. Qe7 {[%cal Re5g6]} Kg8 37. Nd7 Rf7 38. Qd8+ Bf8 39. Nxf8 Rxf8 40. Qd5+ Rf7 41. Ra1 $18) 34. Qb4 Rfe8 35. Re1 (35. Bxe5! Bxe5 36. Nxe5 Rxe5 37. Qxf4 Rf5 38. Qh6 $18) 35... Be2 36. Nxe5 Bxe5 37. Bxe5+ Rxe5 38. Qxf4 Qf5 39. Qh6 1-0

