1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 Nc6 8. Qd2 O-O 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. O-O-O Rb8 11. Bb3 Na5 (11... Ne5 12. f4 Neg4 13. h3 Nxe3 14. Qxe3 $14) 12. Bh6 {The Chinese Dragon (10...b8) is quickly developing and already has its own theory. Dominguez goes for the latest fashion, where Black was having troubles lately.} (12. g4 b5 13. Bh6 {is best answered with} Bxh6 14. Qxh6 {transposing to the game.}) (12. Kb1 b5 13. h4 Nc4 14. Bxc4 bxc4 {is no problem for Black.}) 12... Bxh6 13. Qxh6 b5 14. g4 {Designed to prevent the quick advance e7-e5. Other moves allow Black to combine conveniently the advances ...e5 and ...b4.} (14. h4 e5 15. Nde2 b4 {has been tested multiple times in practice and the assessment is that White has no advantage.}) 14... Nxb3+ (14... e5? {runs into} 15. Nf5! $16) 15. Nxb3 b4 16. Nd5 Nxd5 17. exd5 Rb6! {Most likely this game will change the evaluation of the entire line from better for White into perfectly acceptable for Black. The idea behind 17...b6 is to protect the d6=pawn, then play e7-e5 and recapture with the f-pawn on e6.} (17... Ba4 18. Nd4 Qa5 19. h4 $16 {½-½ Kritz,L (2609)-Jianu,V (2531)/Plovdiv 2008 (51)}) 18. Rhe1 $146 {A logical novelty: anticipating the advance of the e-pawn White brings his rook to the central file. Unfortunately for the Cuban Grandmaster he will soon fail to do the same with his queen, which will remain out of play on h6.} ({The only available game went} 18. h4 e5 19. dxe6 fxe6 20. Rd3 ({In the event of Khalifman's suggestion} 20. Qe3 {Black easily equalizes with} Qf6 21. Nc5 (21. Rh3 Qf4 $15) 21... dxc5 22. Rxd7 Ra6 (22... Qxf3 23. Qxf3 Rxf3 24. Rxa7 Rf7 25. Ra5 $14 {/=}) 23. Qh6 Qf4+ 24. Qxf4 Rxf4 25. Rd8+ Kg7 $11) 20... Bb5 21. Re3 e5 22. h5 g5 23. Qe6+ Kh8 24. Nd2 Bd7 25. Qd5 $13 {1-0 Maslak,K (2521)-Porat,M (2137)/Pardubice 2008 (55)}) 18... e5 19. dxe6 fxe6 20. Re3 {The course of the game shows that leaving the queen on h6 is a little too ambitious.} (20. f4?! Qf6 21. Rd4 a5 $36) ({The main battlefield is the centre and therefore bringing back the queen comes into consideration. After} 20. Qd2 {the position would have remained double-edged. If} Rxf3?! {then} 21. Nd4 Rf6 22. g5 Rf7 23. Nxe6 $36) 20... Rf7 {Just in case, Carlsen takes control over the 7th file, protecting once more the pawn on h7.} 21. Nd2 {Provoking the advance of the d-pawn, which would weaken dark squares.} d5 {The most natural answer.} ({However, an interesting possibility was} 21... Ra6 {trying to exploit the temporary vulnerability of White's king. Dominguez had to find} 22. Kb1 ({worse is} 22. Ne4 d5 23. Ng5 Re7 24. Kb1 Qa5 25. a3 Qc5 $17) 22... Bc6 (22... Qa5 23. a3 $13) 23. Rxe6 Bd5 24. Re3 Bxa2+ 25. Kc1 Bd5 26. Ne4 Ra1+ 27. Kd2 Rxd1+ 28. Kxd1 {and White probably keeps the balance.}) 22. Nb3 {White has achieved his goal, but it turns out that he cannot make full use of the dark-squared weaknesses in Black's camp. The problem is that he must keep the knight to protect his king. Moreover, in order to control squares d4 and e5 White needs his queen too.} Qc7 23. Kb1 (23. h4!?) ({After} 23. Nd4 {Black attacks the a2-pawn:} Ra6 (23... e5? 24. Rde1!) 24. Kb1 Qa5 (24... Qc4!?) 25. a3 Rb6) 23... Rb8 24. Rde1?! {[%csl Rd4] Not sensing the danger. Dominguez wants to create pressure on the e-file, but he loses control over the d4-square. White has problems in the centre because besides controlling the dark squares he must also protect the weak pawns c2 and f3. His queen on h6 is basically doing nothing.} ({Now} 24. Nd4 {is met by} e5 $36) ({Correct was} 24. Qh4 Rbf8 25. Qf2 Rf4 26. Qe2 {and Black is only marginally better.}) 24... Rc8 25. R1e2 (25. Nd4 Rf4 26. Rd3 e5 $19) 25... Qb6 26. h4 {The battle in the centre is lost and there is no choice left.} (26. Rd2 Rcf8 27. Nd4 Rf4 $17) 26... d4 27. Re5 (27. Rd3 Bb5 $19) 27... d3! 28. cxd3 Rxf3 29. d4? (29. h5? Rxd3 30. Re1 Qf2 31. R5e2 Qxe2! 32. Rxe2 Rd1+ 33. Qc1 (33. Nc1 Bb5 $19 {[%cal Gb5d3]}) 33... Rcxc1+ 34. Nxc1 gxh5 35. gxh5 Rd5 $19) ({Again it was necessary to return with the queen} 29. Qd2 {although after} Bb5 30. R5e3 Rcf8 {There is no question about Black's advantage.}) 29... Bb5 {Including the bishop in the attack decides immediately.} 30. R2e3 ({Other moves lose quicker:} 30. Re1 Rxb3! 31. axb3 Bd3+ 32. Ka2 Qa6+) ({Or} 30. Rxe6 Qxe6 31. Rxe6 Bd3+ 32. Ka1 Rf1+) 30... Bd3+ 31. Ka1 Qxd4 {Nice, but not the most precise.} (31... Rf2! {wins by force:} 32. Re1 (32. h5 Qxd4 $19) (32. Rxe6 Qxe6 33. Rxe6 Rf1+) 32... Rxb2! 33. Kxb2 Rc2+ 34. Ka1 Qc6! {[%cal Gc6c3]} 35. Rc5 (35. Nc5 Qg2 $19) 35... Rxa2+ 36. Kxa2 Qa4+ 37. Kb2 Qa3#) (31... Rf1+? {on the other hand throws the advantage away} 32. Re1) 32. Rxe6 (32. Nxd4? Rc1#) 32... Rf1+ 33. Re1 Qxg4 34. Rxf1 Qxe6 35. Nc5 Qe2 36. Rc1 Bf5 $17 {/-+} 37. Qf4 a5 38. h5 Qe7 39. Qc4+ ({With little time both players missed that after} 39. hxg6! {Black cannot take the knight:} Rxc5? {due to} (39... hxg6 40. Qc4+ {and here} Be6 {is not as strong as in the game:} 41. Qd4 Qg5? 42. Rh1!) 40. gxh7+ Kxh7 41. Qh2+ Kg8 (41... Bh3 $8 42. Qxh3+ Kg7 43. Rg1+ Rg5 44. Rh1 {and Black still needs a lot of precision to defend}) 42. Qg1+ $18 {Therefore instead of 39...c5 Black should play 39...hxg6 40.c4+ e6 but then after 41.d4 White still can put up a stubborn resistance, since, in opposition to the game, the open h-file makes a big difference and 41...g5 is not so good due to 42.h1 with White's counterplay.}) 39... Be6 40. Qc2 (40. Qd4 Qg5 $19) 40... Qg5 41. hxg6 hxg6 $19 {The time-trouble is over and Black is completely winning thanks to the pin on the c-file.} 42. a3 bxa3 43. Qc3 axb2+ (43... Qd5 44. b3 a4 $19) 44. Kxb2 Qd5 45. Rc2 a4 46. Ka1 a3 47. Qe3 Bf7 48. Qc3 g5 49. Qe3 Re8 50. Qc3 Re2 51. Nb3 (51. Rxe2 Qd1+) 51... Rxc2 52. Qxc2 Qe5+ 53. Kb1 Kg7 54. Qd2 Bxb3 0-1

