1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 d5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 Ne4 7. Qc2 c5 8. dxc5 Nc6 9. cxd5 exd5 10. e3 Qa5+ {Very modern opening with early imbalance. Black is ahead in development, but should he wait White would develop his pieces and use two mighty bishops.} (10... Bf5 11. Bd3 Ne5 (11... Qg5 12. f3 Qh4+ 13. Kf1 Ng3+ 14. hxg3 Bxd3+ 15. Qxd3 Qxh1 16. b4 O-O 17. Bb2 Rfe8 18. Kf2 Qh6 19. Ne2 Ne5 {1/2 Kasimdzhanov,R (2683)-Aronian,L (2750)/Mainz 2007 (35)}) 12. Nf3 Nxd3+ 13. Qxd3 Ng3 14. Qb5+ (14. e4 Nxe4 15. O-O O-O 16. Qd4 Qf6 17. Qxf6 Nxf6 18. Nd4 Bg6 19. Bf4 Rfe8 20. f3 Nd7 21. Bd6 $14 {1/2 Kasimdzhanov,R (2682)-Asrian,K (2634)/France 2007 (44)}) 14... Bd7 15. Qxb7 Nxh1 16. c6 Be6 17. c7 Qc8 18. Qc6+ Bd7 19. Qd6 a5 20. Bd2 Ra6 21. Qe5+ Kf8 22. Rc1 f6 23. Qxd5 $16 {1/2 Morozevich,A (2755)-Adams,M (2729)/Moscow 2007 (51)}) (10... Qf6 11. f3 Qh4+ 12. g3 Nxg3 13. Qf2 Nf5 14. Qxh4 Nxh4 15. b4 a6 16. Kf2 Ne5 17. Bb2 f6 18. Rd1 Be6 19. Ne2 Bf7 20. Rg1 Nc4 21. Bc1 $16 {1-0 Carlsen,M (2733)-Polgar,J (2707)/Wijk aan Zee 2008 (52)}) 11. b4!? Nxb4 12. axb4 Qxa1 13. Bb5+ Kf8 14. Ne2 a6 15. Bd3 Bd7 16. f3 Ba4 17. Qb2 Qxb2 18. Bxb2 Ng5 19. Nd4 {The very first move deviating from the games of Kasimdzhanov. White plays with compensation for the exchange, but no advantage.} (19. Kd2 Rc8 20. Ra1 Bd7 21. Nf4 Bc6 22. Bf5 Re8 23. Ne2 Ne6 24. h4 h5 25. Be5 Rh6 26. Bd6+ Kg8 27. Bd3 {1/2 Kasimdzhanov,R (2690)-Karpov,A (2670)/Moscow 2007 (31)}) 19... Bd7 (19... Ne6 20. Nf5 Bb5 (20... a5 21. b5 Nxc5 22. Bxg7+ Ke8 23. Bxh8 Nxd3+ 24. Kd2 Kd7 $11) 21. Ke2 Bxd3+ 22. Kxd3 f6 $15) 20. Kf2 (20. Nf5 Bxf5 21. Bxf5 Ne6 22. Kf2 Ke7 $15) 20... f6 21. Ra1 Ke7 22. Bc2! {Carlsen is a fighter and he hopes for more than just equalizing lines.} (22. h4 Nf7 23. e4 Ne5 24. Bc2 dxe4 25. Bxe4 Nc6) (22. Re1 $11) 22... Rhd8 (22... Nf7 23. Bb3 Bc6 24. Nf5+ Kf8 25. f4 $36) (22... g6 23. Re1 Ne6 24. e4 $14) 23. h4 Nf7 (23... Ne6 24. Nf5+ Kf8 25. Nd6 Rdb8 $13) 24. Bxh7 Rh8 25. Bc2 Rxh4 26. Bb3 (26. e4 dxe4 27. Bxe4 Rb8) (26. b5 Ne5 (26... Rxd4 27. exd4 Bxb5 28. Re1+ Kf8 $11) 27. Bc3 g5 $15) 26... Rh5? {[%mdl 2048] Adams shows the ambition of his own, but it comes at the wrong moment and displaces the rook.} (26... Ne5! 27. Bc3 (27. Rd1 a5 $15) 27... Nc6 28. Bxd5 Nxd4 29. exd4 Bc6 30. Re1+ Kf8 31. Bb3 Rd8 32. d5 Bxd5 33. Rd1 Bxb3 34. Rxd8+ Ke7 $11) 27. Ne2! Bc6 (27... Rah8 28. Nf4 R5h6 29. Nxd5+ Kf8 30. Nb6 Bc6 31. Rd1 Rh1 32. Rd2 $36) 28. Nf4 Rg5 29. b5! Bxb5 30. Nxd5+ Kf8 (30... Kd8 31. Nb6 Rb8 32. Bxf7 $18) (30... Rxd5 31. Bxd5 Rd8 32. e4 Bc6 33. Ba3 $14) 31. Nc7 Rd8 32. Ne6+ Ke8? {For a bit mysterious reasons Adams makes a big step in the wrong direction, the natural e7 was much better.} (32... Ke7 33. Nxd8 Nxd8 34. g4 $14) 33. Bd4 Bd7 34. Nxd8 Nxd8 35. Rh1 Ke7 {The defender admitts his mistake, but it is too late.} (35... Ne6 36. f4 Rf5 37. Bxe6 Bxe6 38. Rh8+ Kd7 39. g4 Rd5 40. f5 Bf7 41. Rf8 Be8 42. Rg8 $16) 36. e4 (36. f4 Rg6 37. Bd5 Rh6 $14) 36... Ne6 37. Be3 Re5 38. Rh7 (38. Bd5 Bc6 39. f4 Bxd5 40. fxe5 Bxe4 41. exf6+ gxf6 $16) 38... Kf8 39. Bc2 Bc6 (39... Nxc5 40. f4 Nxe4+ 41. Kf3 $16) 40. f4 Bxe4?! {The tempting move in the fight for the best survival chances, objectively better was to take with the rook.} (40... Rxe4 41. Bxe4 Bxe4 42. Rh8+ Kf7 43. g4 $16) 41. Rh8+ Ke7 42. Rb8! (42. fxe5 Bxc2 43. exf6+ gxf6 44. Rb8 Be4 $16) 42... Nd8 43. fxe5 Bxc2 44. exf6+ gxf6 45. Bd4 f5 46. Be3! {Carlsen plays with technique, that is truly exceptional for such a young player. White's position is already winning.} (46. Bf6+ Kxf6 47. Rxd8 Ke5 48. g3 Be4 $16) 46... Kd7 47. Bg5 Ne6 48. Rxb7+ Kc8 (48... Kc6 49. Rb6+ Kd5 50. Rd6+ Ke5 51. Rd2 $18) 49. Rb2 Be4 50. Be7! (50. Be3 f4 51. Bd4 $16) 50... Nf4 51. Ra2! Kd7 (51... Kb7 52. g3 $18) (51... Nd3+ 52. Kg3 Kb7 53. Bd6 $18) 52. Bd6 Nxg2 53. Rxa6 {With minimal number of pawns White will win due to the better coordination of his pieces.} f4 54. Ra4 Bc6 55. Ra7+ Ke6 56. Ra6 1-0

