[WhiteElo "2848"]
[BlackElo "2644"]
[PlyCount "75"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Qxd4!? {The Hungarian variation, that should never be underestimated. Either a quick development or a Hedgehog-like setup could be White's weapons here} a6 (4... Nc6 {the main line, naturally} 5. Bb5 Bd7 6. Bxc6 Bxc6 {and now White follows with c3-g5-0-0-0 or c4 straight away, with a rich battle either way.}) 5. h3!? {A setup with e3, e2, c4, c3 is sought by White. A decision that proved to be very good, since Black's troubles started already after his 15th move.} Nc6 6. Qe3 g6 7. c4 Bg7 8. Be2 Nf6 9. Nc3 O-O 10. O-O Nd7 11. Rb1! {The expansion with b4 is always a good plan, and when Black plays ...a5 to stop that (and also secure c5 for his ), White gets the b5-square for the c3-.} a5 (11... b6 {was another way to fight, b7, c8 coming. Play could go like this:} 12. b4 Bb7 13. Rd1 Rc8 14. Na4!? $14 {and the b6- will be slightly harassed.}) (11... Qb6!? {Possibly an interesting novelty, forcing an early -exchange, that should work in Black's favour.} 12. Nd5 Qxe3 13. Bxe3 Nf6 {And I feel Black is ok, with natural developing moves coming}) 12. b3 Nc5 13. Bb2 f5 {This is part of Black's plan - if he doesn't try this, White is just building up an advantage with fd1, b5, and a3-b4 later.} 14. exf5 Bxf5 15. Rbd1 $14 {This is the kind of position where Black has to be careful, since central files are open and both black s are still there. Highly typical of the Accelerated Dragon's Maroczy like positions. Personally, I like White's chances in these structures. It just feels like Black's position is highly unstable and moves like a3, g5, d5, b5 must be watched all the time.} a4 16. Ba3! Qa5 (16... Bxc3?! {a highly concrete decision, that should be considered and could go like this:} 17. Qxc3 Ne4 18. Qe3! (18. Qc1 {would also be decent} axb3 19. axb3 Rxa3 20. Qxa3 Nc3 {Even here, White claims a small edge with a simple move like 21.fe1, because of Black's unstable central pawns and his open } 21. Rfe1 $14) 18... axb3 19. Bb2 {ideas like this should be considered, because it feels like 16...xc3 should backfire somehow. Now g4 is a threat and in the event of 19...xa2, then simply 20.b3, with huge compensation} Rxa2 20. Qxb3 $16) 17. Nb5 (17. b4!? {was attractive, but was probably not leading to much:} Nxb4 18. Bxb4 Qxb4 19. Nd5 Qa5 20. Nxe7+ Kh8 21. Nxf5 gxf5 $14 {Keeping the pieces in a tense position with 17.b5 is always more interesting than doing this. Of course, 18...f6! may change the whole evaluation and induce White to choose this line instead.}) 17... axb3 18. axb3 Qxa3 {White was claiming a small, comfortable advantage already, but this looks drastic. I mean, Black is far from stable, and he's not even going to take on b3. There will be some piece play, indeed. But it doesn't look right.} (18... Rf6! {This is what I'm talking about - if Black wants to claim a good position, he has to do it in a concrete fashion, which probably was the thought process that occurred to Gawain, but he overestimated the -sacrifice and didn't consider this odd-looking  activation (I would hardly take this move seriously while analysing during a game)} 19. Bxc5 (19. b4 {leads to a crazy position as well:} Nxb4 20. Bxb4 Qxb4 21. Nc7 Qa5 22. Nxa8 Re6! 23. Qd2 Bc3 24. Qd5 Be4 $44 {and Black's strong piece positioning (and a ) surely compensate for the exchange}) (19. Ng5 h6 20. g4 {would be another direct try to refute Black's idea} hxg5 21. gxf5 Rxf5 22. Bxc5 Re5 $13 {same idea, on the 5th rank now :)}) 19... Re6! {The point, now the e2- becomes a target} 20. Qc1 Rxe2 21. Bd4 Nxd4 22. Nfxd4 Re5 $13) 19. Nxa3 Rxa3 20. Nd2 $16 {Slowly White coordinates so as to exchange some pieces, when Black's problems will become bigger and bigger} Bd4 21. Qg3 Be5?! (21... Kg7!? {a small -improvement was possible, maintaining some positional pressure.}) 22. f4 {This 'weakness' also helps White to gain some space.} Bf6 23. Bg4 Nd4 24. Kh1 Bc2 25. Rde1 Kh8 26. Re3 h5 27. b4! h4 (27... Nd3! 28. Bd1! {disturbing completely Black's harmony} h4! (28... Nf5 29. Qxg6 Nxe3 30. Bxc2 Nxc2 31. Ne4 $18 {and the material advantage becomes a deadly attack}) 29. Qxg6 Nf2+ 30. Rxf2 Bxg6 31. Rxa3 {also good for White, but perhaps the most stubborn idea.}) 28. Qf2 Nd3 29. Qg1 $18 {Now everything is hanging and e4 is a very strong threat.} Nf5 30. Bxf5 gxf5 31. Nf3 Rc3 32. c5 Bb3 33. Ne1 Bd4 34. Nxd3 dxc5 (34... Rxd3 {wouldn't change anything:} 35. Rxd3 Bxg1 36. Rxb3 Bd4 37. cxd6 exd6 38. Rd3 $18) 35. Qf2 Rf7 36. Rc1 cxb4 37. Rxc3 bxc3 38. Qe1 1-0

