1. e4 {[%emt 0:00:07]} e5 {[%emt 0:00:10]} 2. Nf3 {[%emt 0:00:39]} Nc6 {[%emt 0:00:09]} 3. Nc3 {[%emt 0:00:51]} Nf6 {[%emt 0:00:25]} 4. d4 {[%emt 0:00:07]} exd4 {[%emt 0:00:22]} 5. Nxd4 {[%emt 0:00:05]} Bb4 {[%emt 0:00:19]} 6. Nxc6 {[%emt 0:00:13]} bxc6 {[%emt 0:00:03]} 7. Bd3 {[%emt 0:00:16]} d5 {[%emt 0:01:36]} 8. exd5 {[%emt 0:00:15]} O-O {[%emt 0:00:10]} 9. O-O {[%emt 0:00:03]} cxd5 {[%emt 0:00:07]} 10. Bg5 {[%emt 0:01:31]} c6 {[%emt 0:00:14]} 11. Qf3 {[%emt 0:00:39] It is interesting how these things change. In 2008 this line was taught to me from the White side by Ukrainian-Israeli GM Boris Alterman; shortly after becoming an IM (in 2011) I viewed it as primarily a drawing weapon and now I think it's just a way to get a long and strategically messy game which the better player will probably win. That is in fact what occurred.} Bd6 {[%emt 0:02:48]} (11... Rb8 12. h3 {probably brings White a little bit closer to his dream of a4, b3, c4, f4 and ad1.}) (11... Be7 {is more played and is most solid, but the doubled f-pawns are not something to get that scared about.}) 12. Rae1 {[%emt 0:01:50] Possibly preparation, but in any case quite logical to take the e5-square from Black's bishop.} (12. h3 {is main, and now a tactic worth knowing is} Rb8 13. b3 Be5 14. Bf4!? {although it doesn't necessarily promise White anything after} Bxc3 15. Bxb8 Bxa1 16. Rxa1 Bg4 17. hxg4 Qxb8 $11 {Morozevich,A-Kramnik,V Moscow 2013. However, we were not destined to see another bishop-vs- knight-and-solid-d5-centre draw this round.}) 12... Rb8 {[%emt 0:06:40]} 13. b3 {[%emt 0:02:24] Keeping equality.} (13. Na4 h6! $15) 13... a5 {[%emt 0:13:26]} (13... Bb4 {tries to take advantage of White's tactically vulnerable pieces. There could follow:} 14. Qg3 Bd7 {and I assume Carlsen saw this and steered clear due to the drawing line} 15. Qh4! h6 16. Bxh6 $11) 14. h3 {[%emt 0:05:11]} h6 {[%emt 0:05:20]} 15. Bxf6 {[%emt 0:00:12]} Qxf6 {[%emt 0:00:03]} 16. Qxf6 {[%emt 0:00:07]} gxf6 {[%emt 0:00:19]} 17. Ne2 {[%emt 0:00:07]} c5 {[%emt 0:08:27]} 18. Ng3 {[%emt 0:02:40]} Rd8 {[%emt 0:00:40]} 19. Nf5 {[%emt 0:02:17] So White has emerged with a marginally more comfortable position from the opening, but Black isn't giving up on trying to win just yet.} Bf8! {[%emt 0:03:03] Bishops of the same colour always offer more imbalance, and in this case White needs to be concerned about the ...c4 push too.} (19... Bxf5 20. Bxf5 a4 21. g3 axb3 22. axb3 Be5 $11 {would be a reasonably solid way to make a draw.}) 20. Ne7+ {[%emt 0:14:20]} Bxe7 {[%emt 0:00:03]} 21. Rxe7 {[%emt 0:00:06]} Be6 {[%emt 0:01:18]} 22. Rd1 {[%emt 0:06:01]} (22. Ra7! {As always, White should exchange precisely one pair of rooks, and this poses Black some significant problems.} Ra8 (22... c4 23. Be2 {is inconvenient for Black as he doesn't obtain enough in the event of ...c4, but} Ra8 {is also bad:} 24. Rxa8 Rxa8 25. f4 $14) 23. Rxa8 Rxa8 24. f4 (24. g4 h5 $11) 24... f5! (24... Kf8 25. Kf2 Ke7 26. g4 $14 {leads to the same problem as in Giri-Hou yesterday, and a Rubinstein game about 100 years ago: the isolated rook's pawn is often a major weakness.}) 25. g4 fxg4 26. f5 Bd7 27. hxg4 Re8 $11) 22... c4 {[%emt 0:09:45]} 23. Be2 {[%emt 0:20:23]} a4 {[%emt 0:00:23] Now Black is having some fun: possibly Adhiban missed from afar that ...xc2 can pin a bishop to a c7-rook a few moves into certain lines.} 24. bxa4 {[%emt 0:01:35]} Bf5?! {[%emt 0:03:32] Either an inaccuracy or a slight gamble. Either way, it makes the game look a bit less 'smooth' given that it is a win in the end, but also increased the likelihood of that being the result.} (24... Kf8 25. Rc7 Rb2 $132 {was for instance one reasonable continuation for Black.}) 25. Bf3? {[%emt 0:20:41] Missing the accidental chance.} (25. c3! Ra8 (25... d4 26. Bxc4 dxc3 27. Rxd8+ Rxd8 28. Bb3 $14) 26. Rc7! {The point is that White can use the pin on the d-file, and for this reason Black is unable to take on a4. For instance:} Be6 27. f4 f5 28. Rd2 $14) 25... d4! $15 {[%emt 0:01:40]} 26. a5? {[%emt 0:08:52]} (26. Re2 Rb2 27. Red2 {is the engine suggestion but following} d3!? {it is also a bit depressing, e.g.} 28. cxd3 (28. c3 Rb6 29. a5 Ra6 $15) 28... c3 29. Rxb2 cxb2 30. Rb1 Rb8 31. Be4 Be6 32. a5 Bxa2 33. Rxb2 Rxb2 34. a6 Rb1+ 35. Kh2 Ra1 36. a7 Bd5 37. Bxd5 Rxa7 $15 {and the endgame should be held, especially at this level: bishop goes to f5 and is supported by g4, for instance, but it requires a certain appetite for suffering to go in for this.}) 26... Bxc2 $19 {[%emt 0:01:45]} 27. Rc1 {[%emt 0:00:36]} Rb1 {[%emt 0:06:42]} 28. Rxb1 {[%emt 0:09:46]} Bxb1 {[%emt 0:00:03]} 29. Rc7 {[%emt 0:03:43]} c3 {[%emt 0:00:49] The engine takes a while to realise, but White is toast.} 30. Bd1 {[%emt 0:02:52]} (30. Kf1 Re8 {snares the a5-pawn in a reasonably funny way.}) 30... Ra8 {[%emt 0:02:33]} 31. Rc5 {[%emt 0:00:56]} Bxa2 {[%emt 0:00:47]} 32. Bc2 {[%emt 0:00:16]} Be6 {[%emt 0:01:47]} 33. Kf1 {[%emt 0:02:06]} Rc8 {[%emt 0:00:59]} 34. Rxc8+ {[%emt 0:00:10]} Bxc8 {[%emt 0:00:02]} 35. Ke2 {[%emt 0:03:28]} Ba6+ {[%emt 0:00:03]} 36. Kf3 {[%emt 0:00:11]} d3 {[%emt 0:00:19] A very well played game!} 0-1

