1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. Bc4 e6 7. N1e2 b5!? {An unusual approach, L'Ami is trying to seize early control over some useful squares on the queenside. However such an approach is not without its drawbacks - serious weaknesses are being created on the queenside.} (7... Bd6 {is perhaps more prudent.} 8. Nf4 Bxf4 9. Bxf4 Nf6 10. O-O O-O 11. c3 Nbd7 12. Qe2 Re8 13. Rad1 a5 14. Rfe1 a4 15. Bd3 Bxd3 16. Qxd3 Qa5 17. c4 Qa6 18. Qc2 Rad8 19. h3 Nb6 20. b3 Nbd7 21. Rd2 Ra8 22. Nf1 Qb6 23. Bd6 axb3 24. axb3 c5 25. dxc5 Nxc5 26. Bxc5 Qxc5 27. Ne3 h5 28. Red1 Qb4 29. Qb1 b5 30. Rd4 Rab8 31. Qd3 bxc4 32. bxc4 Rec8 33. Rd6 Qc5 34. Qd4 Qxd4 35. R6xd4 Kf8 36. Kf1 Ke7 37. g4 Rc5 38. f4 hxg4 39. hxg4 g5 40. f5 Re5 41. Re1 Re4 42. Rxe4 Nxe4 43. fxe6 fxe6 44. Ke2 Kd6 45. Ra1 Rb2+ 46. Ke1 Kc5 47. Rd1 Rb3 48. Nf1 Kxc4 49. Rd8 Kc5 50. Rd7 Ra3 51. Rd8 Rh3 52. Rd7 Rb3 53. Rd8 Rb2 54. Ne3 Rh2 55. Rd7 Ra2 56. Rd8 Nf6 57. Rf8 Nd7 58. Rd8 Ne5 59. Kf1 Nf3 60. Nc2 Nh2+ 61. Kg1 Nf3+ 62. Kf1 e5 63. Ne3 e4 64. Rd5+ Kb4 65. Rd8 Rh2 66. Rd7 Kc3 67. Nd1+ Kc2 68. Ne3+ Kc1 69. Rd1+ Kb2 70. Rd7 Kb3 71. Rc7 Nd4 72. Rc8 Rh3 73. Nd5 Rf3+ 74. Kg2 Rd3 75. Kf2 Ne6 76. Ne3 Nf4 77. Re8 Nh3+ 78. Ke2 Nf4+ 79. Kf2 Rd2+ 80. Kf1 Rd4 81. Rc8 Ra4 82. Kf2 Ra2+ 83. Kf1 Nd3 84. Nd5 Rf2+ 85. Kg1 Rd2 86. Ne3 Re2 87. Nd5 Nf4 88. Ne7 e3 89. Nf5 Rg2+ 90. Kh1 e2 91. Nd4+ Kb4 92. Nc2+ Kb3 93. Nd4+ Kb4 94. Nc2+ Kb5 95. Nd4+ Kb6 96. Rc1 Rf2 97. Kg1 Nh3+ {0-1 Campora,D (2505)-Dreev,A (2690)/Dos Hermanas 2003/CBM 095}) 8. Bb3 Bd6 9. Nf4 Bxf4 {Black decides that the bishop on g6 is a more useful piece to retain than the one on d6, but the dark-squared weaknesses incurred with this exchange, are potentially highly significant in the long-run.  I recently wrote an openings report on a similar line, and observed that in such positions when Black has exchanged the knight on f4, White has an enduring advantage due to the two bishops. I don't think that the judgement is any different here, and Carlsen is like no other at nursing small advantages.  Personally because of this I would rather retain the dark-squared bishop in this opening. If White exchanges on g6, then the h-file is opened and Black will have some counterplay.} (9... Nf6 10. h4 Qc7 11. Qf3 Nd5! {would have been less compliant.}) 10. Bxf4 Nf6 11. O-O O-O 12. c4! {With this highly practical and somewhat counter-intuitive decision, Carlsen shows very deep understanding of the position. He opens up another front - the c-file, along which he will stage a lengthy war of attrition against the c6-pawn.  Watching the game online at the time, I observed that most of the watching kibitzers seemed taken aback by this. Doesn't this just leave White with a isolated d-pawn?  But sometimes to gain something we have to give something in return. First we have to ask what logic brought Carlsen to make this move. If White doesn't play c4, how can he make progress? It becomes clear with some analysis, that without the aid of a pawn break, it is not easy for White to get anywhere. Mere piece play alone is unlikely to make much imprint in the rock that is the black position. White needs a plan!} (12. c3 Nbd7 13. a4 Qb6 14. a5 Qa6 15. Re1 c5! {would be a very typical line, where Black is very well-placed, and it is not clear what White is doing. Therefore White must give Black a target - the pawn on d4 - in order to gain one himself, the backward weakling on c6.}) 12... bxc4 13. Bxc4 Qb6 ({If Black is to defend this line in the future, then} 13... Nd5 {would be a possible starting point.} {It is entirely possible that L'Ami rejected this due to} 14. Bxd5! cxd5 (14... Qxd5 15. h4 h6 16. h5 Bh7 17. Qg4 {gives White a useful initiative.}) (14... exd5 {just looks ugly - Black has not resolved the problem of his backward c-pawn.}) 15. Rc1 {when White has a useful majority on the queenside - and any attempt to stoke up trouble by Black is likely to lead to disaster, for example} Qa5? 16. h4! h6 17. h5 Bh7 18. Qg4 {with a very strong attack.}) 14. Qd2 Rd8 15. Rfd1 Nbd7 16. Rac1 Nd5 ({The problem now for Black is the attempt to rid himself of this weakling pawn on c6, can run into problems.} 16... c5 17. a4! (17. dxc5 Nxc5 18. Qe3 {is also not bad. Next move White will play b3, emphasising his structural advantage.} h6 19. b3) 17... cxd4 18. a5! {and the queen on b6 has run out of useful squares.}) (16... e5 17. Be3 Ng4 18. Bg5! Ndf6 19. h3 Rxd4 20. Qe2 Nh6 21. Qxe5 {and Black falls apart.}) 17. Bd6 N7f6 18. Bc5 Qb8 19. f3 {Now White just had a bind, which fully justifies Carlsen's brave decision on move 12.} h6 20. Ne2 Nd7 21. Ba3 e5 22. b3! {Delaying a decision is probably best.} ({A more forcing move was} 22. Bxd5 cxd5 23. Nc3 {but after} Qb7 24. dxe5 Nxe5 25. Nxd5 Nd3! {Black is not without counterplay for his pawn. More importantly, White would lose control over the position, which is certainly not in Carlsen's style.}) 22... Qb6 23. Nc3 N7f6?! {But this turns out to be rather inaccurate. In fact the knight was better placed on d7 than where it eventually lands, on d5, where it looks good but does little.} ({Therefore} 23... Nxc3! 24. Qxc3 Re8! {was indicated, where Black threatens to gain serious counterplay with ..e4.} 25. Kh1 e4 26. fxe4 Bxe4 {and with the knight coming to f6, Black has co-ordinated his forces and solved most of his problems.}) 24. Bc5 Qc7 25. dxe5 Qxe5 26. Bd4 Qe7 27. Re1 Qd6 28. Qf2 Nxc3 29. Bxc3 Nd5 30. Be5 Qa3 31. h4 ({It is obvious that White has a strategic advantage, but one of the hardest situations in chess is when you have a number of promising continuations. A possibly even stronger alternative was to crank up the pressure with} 31. f4!? Re8 32. Qg3 Qc5+ 33. Kh1 Qf8 34. Be2 Re6 35. Bg4 f5 36. Bf3 {when Black is really creaking.}) 31... f6 32. Bd4 Kh7 33. Bxd5 Rxd5 34. Rxc6 Qb4 35. Rc4 Qd6 36. Bc5 Qd8 37. Kh2 a6 38. Be7 Qb8+ 39. Qg3 Qxg3+ 40. Kxg3 {Despite the pawn deficit, Black has serious drawing chances in this ending. In fact I would wager that against 99 percent of grandmasters, L'Ami would gain a draw without any real difficulty.  However against Carlsen of course, it is a different story altogether. At this point, he's just getting going! Such is his reputation in endings, that any kind of disadvantage can normally be viewed as hopeless!   Also the psychological pressure of having to play accurate move after accurate move, to defend a worse ending against someone who has boundless energy and confidence, is in incredible shape, and is happy to grind away forever, should not be underestimated.} Ra7 41. Rc6 a5 42. Bc5 Rad7 43. Be3 R7d6 44. Rc4 Re6 45. Kf2 Rde5 46. Rc3 Be8 47. Bd2 a4 48. Rxe5 Rxe5 49. b4 Bb5?! {A step in the wrong direction, Black plays too passively. In order to increase his chances of a draw, it's essential to exchange as many pawns on the kingside as possible. This is because that as long as the kingside remains intact, White has the opportunity of creating an attack over there, to try and distract and break the blockade that Black has on the queenside.} (49... g5! {would have gained some vital space.} 50. Rc7+ Kg6 51. g4 h5 52. Bc3 Re6 53. hxg5 fxg5 54. gxh5+ Kxh5 {is starting to look quite drawish.}) 50. Be3 h5 51. Rc7 Kg6 52. Ra7 Rd5 53. Kg3 Rd3 54. Bc5 Ra3? {Now however, L'Ami switches to active defence at just the wrong moment. Knowing when to defend passively in the ending, and when to defend actively, is one of the most difficult skills a chessplayer can master, if it is at all possible.} ({It was hard to commit to complete passivity with} 54... Rd7 {but after} 55. Ra5 Rb7 56. Kf4 Kf7 57. g4 hxg4 58. fxg4 Ke6 {it is not so obvious how White makes progress.}) 55. Bf8 Rxa2 56. Kf4 Kh7 57. Rxg7+ Kh8 58. Kf5! {It is amazing that even so late in the tournament, and in the sixth hour of play, Carlsen still manages to beso accurate. Yet again he finds the narrow path to victory. The guy is a machine, he just doesn't seem to get tired!} Rc2 59. Kxf6 {White's rampaging king, and the pawns he can gather on the kingside, is more relevant than the black a-pawn, which is easily controlled.} a3 60. Ra7!? ({The "dance of death" with the bishop would also have won -} 60. Bc5! a2 61. Bd4 Rd2 62. Be5 Re2 63. Ba1 Re1 64. Bc3 Rc1 65. Be5 Re1 66. Ra7! {and White wins, as the black rook isn't hitting g2 anymore.}) 60... a2 (60... Rxg2 61. Rxa3 $18) 61. g3 Rc6+ 62. Kg5 Kg8 (62... Ra6 {would not have saved Black either -} 63. Bg7+ Kg8 64. Rxa6 Bxa6 65. Bd4 Be2 66. f4 (66. Kxh5 Bxf3+ 67. Kg5 Bc6 68. Kf6 {also looks winning}) 66... Kf7 67. f5 Ke8 68. b5 Bxb5 69. Kxh5 {and the white pawns are overwhelming.}) 63. Bc5 Ba6 64. Bd4 Rd6 65. Ba1 {The h5 is dropping, so Black had seen enough.} 1-0

