{[%evp 0,85,58,5,13,-13,38,45,75,77,60,45,67,59,59,55,66,22,78,70,70,90,90,90,94,94,92,88,88,54,84,55,55,55,56,36,58,58,68,70,96,97,97,89,99,77,104,121,138,102,102,73,108,104,104,108,122,116,144,109,121,144,153,153,145,114,151,130,266,237,266,204,297,297,297,297,315,378,512,1477,1477,1994,29991,29992,29993,29994,29995,29996]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. Qb3 dxc4 6. Qxc4 O-O 7. e4 Nc6 8. Be2 e5 9. d5 Nd4 10. Nxd4 exd4 11. Qxd4 c6 12. d6!? $146 {[#] This move was a novelty at that time, and players yet to figure out how to react.The 4.Nf3 and 5.Qb3 had not been played by Carlsen before this game. It is fair to assume that Dominguez was not ready for this turn of events. This line got huge popularity after this game.Perhaps, there is a room for further debates.} (12. Qd1 Re8 13. O-O Nxe4 14. Nxe4 Rxe4 15. dxc6 Rd4 $8 16. Qb3 bxc6 17. Bc4 Qe8 18. Bg5 Re4! $11 {1/2-1/2 (22) Eingorn,V (2525)-Kuzmin,G (2520) Kharkov 1985}) (12. Qc4 {Today, this move is considered the main line.} b5 {Is the modern approach. Leads to pawn down endgame.} (12... cxd5 {Old approach.} 13. exd5 Bf5 14. O-O Rc8 15. Qb3 Ne4 16. Nxe4 Bxe4 17. Bf3 Bxf3 18. Qxf3 Rc5 19. Rd1 Re8 20. Be3 (20. d6 $16 {1-0 (35) Gyimesi,Z (2624)-Okhotnik,V (2440) Hungary 2005}) 20... Rc2 21. Rac1 Rxc1 22. Bxc1! $18 (22. Rxc1?! Bxb2 23. Rb1 Qa5 $14 {1-0 (35) Nilsen,J (2397)-Merkesvik,S (2230) Fagernes 2016})) 13. Qxc6 Bd7 14. Qd6 Re8 15. Bg5 (15. e5 Ng4 16. e6 fxe6 17. h3 Ne5 18. dxe6 Bxe6 19. Qxd8 Raxd8 20. Bxb5 Bc4 21. Bxe8 Nd3+ 22. Kf1 Bxc3 23. bxc3 Nxc1+ 24. Kg1 Ne2+ 25. Kh2 Rxe8 26. Rhe1 $11 {1/2-1/2 (36) Yu,Y (2763)-Vachier Lagrave,M (2774) Khanty Mansiysk 2019}) 15... b4 (15... Rb8!? $146 16. a3 a5 17. O-O b4 18. axb4 axb4 19. Nd1 Nxe4 20. Bxd8 Nxd6 21. Bc7 Rb7 22. Bxd6 Rxe2 23. Ra8+ Re8 24. Rxe8+ Bxe8 25. Re1 Bd7 26. Re4 (26. Be5!? {Could be an interesting try. Black needs to find several good moves.} Bxe5 27. Rxe5 Rc7 28. Kf1 Rc1 29. Ke2 Ba4 30. Ne3 Rb1 31. Nc4 Bb5 32. Kd3 f6! 33. Re6 b3 34. Kd4 Bxc4 35. Kxc4 Rxb2 36. Rxf6 Rd2 37. Rb6 b2 38. Kc3 Rxd5 39. Rxb2 {Almost draw.}) 26... Ba4 27. b3 f5 28. Rxb4 Rxb4 29. Bxb4 Bxb3 30. Nc3 Bxc3 31. Bxc3 Bxd5 {1/2-1/2 (31) Giri,A (2783) -Mamedyarov,S (2817) Wijk aan Zee 2019 CBM 188 [Fernandez,D]}) 16. Qxb4 Nxd5 17. Nxd5 Qxg5 18. O-O Rab8 19. Qd6 Bh3 20. Nf4 Bg4 21. Bxg4 Be5 22. Qd7 Qxf4 23. g3 Qf6 24. Rad1 h5 25. Be2 Re7 26. Qd5 Rxb2 27. Rd2 Rxd2 28. Qxd2 {Black is pawn down but MVL knows how to hold it. He has gone to this line several times. Objectively, it should be draw. ½-½ (59) Aronian,L (2799)-Vachier Lagrave,M (2804) Tbilisi 2017}) 12... Nd5?! {This move does not equalise fully.} (12... Re8 {The latest high-level game continued:} 13. Qd3 b5! {This is the hardest move, probably Dominguez did not see during the game. The idea is that immediately Re6 did not work.} (13... Re6 14. Bf4 {[%csl Gh5][%cal Ge2h5,Gf4d6] The light squared bishop defends h5. So Black cannot push away the bishop on f4.}) 14. f3 (14. a3 Be6 15. Be3 (15. f3 Bc4 16. Qd1 Re6! $146 (16... Bxe2 17. Nxe2 Re6 18. Qc2 Qxd6 19. O-O Nd7 20. Be3 $14 {1-0 (33) Nguyen, N (2630)-Raghunandan,K (2412) Hanoi 2018}) 17. Bxc4 bxc4 18. Qe2 Rxd6 {Black does not have any problem. White is the side that needs to be careful.} 19. Qxc4? Nxe4! 20. Nxe4 Rd1+ 21. Ke2 Rxh1 22. Bg5 Rxa1 23. Bxd8 Rxd8 24. Ng5 Rf8 $15) 15... Bc4 16. Qd2 Bxe2 17. Kxe2 Nxe4 18. Nxe4 Rxe4 19. Rhd1 Re5 {[%cal Ge5d5]} 20. Rac1 Rd5 21. Qc2 Rxd6 22. Rxd6 Qxd6 23. Qxc6 Qxc6 24. Rxc6 Bxb2 25. Ra6 $11) 14... Re6 15. Be3 (15. Bf4? Nh5 $17) 15... Rxd6 16. Qc2 Nh5 17. Rd1 $11 (17. O-O Bd4 18. Bxd4 Rxd4 $15 {0-1 (53) Bosiocic,M (2591)-Ragger, M (2701) Austria 2018})) 13. Qd3 Nxc3 14. bxc3 Qf6 15. Bb2 Rd8 16. Rd1 Qe6? {This was an actual mistake.} (16... Qe5! {Does not allow f4.} 17. O-O Qc5 {[%cal Gc5g1]} 18. Bc1 (18. Kh1 Be5 19. f4 Rxd6 20. Qf3 Rxd1 21. Bxd1 Bf6 22. f5 $40 {White's attack demands accurate play from Black. Objectively, Black should hold.}) 18... Be5 19. Be3 Qxd6 20. Qxd6 Rxd6 21. f4 Rxd1 22. Rxd1 Bf6 23. c4 Be6 24. g4 {White has pressure.}) 17. f4! {Stops Be5} Qxa2 18. Rd2 Qa5 (18... a5!? {Black should have tried to create counterplay by marching the pawn forward. Although the position is bad here too.} 19. O-O a4 20. h4 a3 21. Ba1 Qb3 22. h5 a2 23. Qe3 $16 {[%cal Gf4f5]}) 19. Qe3 Bd7 (19... Qb6 20. Qf2 {White should trade queens on f2. Check 20.Kf2 variation.} (20. Kf2 Qxe3+ 21. Kxe3 a5 22. e5 f6! {[%cal Gf6e5,Gg7h6,Gh6d2] Things get complicated.}) 20... Be6 21. h4 Qxf2+ (21... h5 22. g4 Qxf2+ 23. Kxf2 hxg4 24. h5 $18) 22. Kxf2 a5 23. Rc1 $16 {[%cal Gb2a3]}) 20. Kf2 Re8 21. Ra1 Qd8 22. c4! {White trades his passive bishop. The advantage is overwhelming.} Bxb2 23. Rxb2 $18 b6 24. Bf3 Qh4+ 25. Kg1 Qf6 26. Qd2 g5 27. g3! {Keeping pawn on f4 is important.} gxf4 28. gxf4 Kh8 29. Kh1 Rg8 30. e5 Qh4 31. Qd4 Rg7 32. Rg2 Rag8 {It is time for the final blow.} 33. e6! Bxe6 34. Rxg7 Rxg7 35. Bxc6 f6 36. d7 Bxd7 {The rest is not interesting although the final tactic is worth seeing.} 37. Bxd7 Re7 38. Be6! Rxe6 39. Qd8+ Kg7 40. Rg1+ Kf7 41. Qg8+ Ke7 42. Rg7+ Kd6 43. Qf8+ 1-0

