1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 O-O 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 b6 7. Bg5 Bb7 8. e3 d6 9. Ne2 Nbd7 10. Qc2 c5 11. Rd1 cxd4 {Black demonstrates the most accurate order of moves.} (11... Qc7 12. Nc3 cxd4 13. Rxd4 a6 {- see CBM 121, game Morozevich-Beliavsky.}) 12. Rxd4 h6! 13. Bh4 Qc7 14. Nc3 d5 {This is now more appropriate than without ...h7-h6 g5-h4.} 15. Bg3 (15. cxd5 Nxd5! (15... Bxd5 $143 16. f3) {as now} 16. Bd3 {doesn't attack the h7-pawn.}) 15... e5 16. cxd5 Bxd5! $146 {[%mdl 8] This is stronger than} (16... Nxd5 17. Rc4 Qb8 (17... Qd6!?) 18. Be2 N7f6 19. O-O Ba6 20. Nxd5 Nxd5 21. Bf3 Rd8 22. Rd4 Bxf1 23. Rxd5 Ba6 24. Bxe5 Qc8 25. Rxd8+ Qxd8 26. Bxa8 Qxa8 {½-½ Bareev,E-Grischuk,A/Khanty-Mansiysk 2007/CBM 122 (72) - Black survived in this endgame.}) 17. Be2 Rac8 {Black plays safely but apparently it was quite possible to take the pawn:} (17... Bxg2 18. Rg1 Bd5 (18... Qb7!?) 19. Rh4 $44 Rfd8 20. Rxh6 $140 Be4!) 18. e4 Bc6 19. f3 Nh5 20. Rd2 Nxg3 21. hxg3 Nf6 $11 22. O-O Qe7 23. Rfd1 Bd7 24. Ba6 Rc7 25. Qd3 Be6 26. Qd6 Bb3 27. Nb5 Qxd6 28. Rxd6 Bxd1 (28... Rc5!? 29. R1d2 Rc1+ 30. Kh2 Bc4 $132) 29. Nxc7 Ba4 30. Rd3 Rb8 31. b3 Bd7 32. Nb5 Bxb5 33. Bxb5 Rc8 34. Ba6 Rc1+ 35. Kf2 Kf8 36. Rd8+ Ke7 37. Ra8 Rc7 38. g4 h5 39. gxh5 Nxh5 40. Bc4 Nf4 41. g3 1/2-1/2

