1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e5 7. Nf3 Be7 8. Bc4 O-O 9. O-O Be6 10. Bb3 Nc6 11. Qe2 Na5 12. Rfd1 Nxb3 13. cxb3 {Recapturing with the c-pawn is not desirable, but Carlsen believes in the advantages it carries. Opening of the c-file invites activity by White's rook, also the knight move to d5 will gain in impact.} Nd7 (13... Qe8 14. Ne1 (14. Bg5 Nh5 15. Nd2 f5 16. Bxe7 {1/2 Movsesian,S (2632)-Babula,V (2585)/Czechia 2006}) 14... Ng4 (14... h6 15. Nc2 Qd7 16. Nb4 Rfc8 17. f3 Bd8 18. Rd3 a5 19. Nbd5 Nxd5 20. Nxd5 a4 21. bxa4 Rxa4 22. b3 {1-0 Carlsen,M (2675)-Nunn,J (2617)/Amsterdam 2006 (45)}) 15. Nc2 f5 16. f3 Nxe3 17. Nxe3 fxe4 18. Nxe4 Rd8 19. Nd5 Qf7 20. Qd3 Bxd5 21. Qxd5 Qxd5 22. Rxd5 Kf7 23. Rad1 Ke6 $11 {0-1 Carlsen,M (2698)-Karjakin,S (2672)/Cap d'Agde 2006 (50)}) 14. Nd2 Qc7 15. Rac1 Rfc8 16. Nd5 Qd8 {Ivanchuk is much too strong to fall for the simple trick of the black rook being hit by the e3-bishop later in the tactical line.} (16... Qxc1? 17. Nxe7+ Kf8 18. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 19. Nf1 Rxf1+ 20. Kxf1 Kxe7 21. Qc2 Kd8 22. f4 $16) 17. Rxc8 (17. Nxe7+ Qxe7 18. Nc4 Rc6 19. Na5 Rxc1 20. Rxc1 h6 21. Nxb7 Nf6 22. Na5 Nxe4 $11) 17... Rxc8 18. Nxe7+ Qxe7 19. Nc4 Bxc4 {Black was surely confident, that he could hold the position even though White will now have reason to fight for more.} (19... Rc6 20. Na5 Rc7 21. Qd2 Nf8 22. Qb4 Rd7 23. f3 $14) 20. bxc4 Nc5 21. b3 (21. f3 Ne6 22. g3 Rc6 $14) 21... Rc6 (21... Nxe4? 22. Qg4 $18) 22. Qc2 h6 23. g3 Qc7 24. Kg2 (24. Bxc5 Rxc5 25. Qd2 Rc6 26. Qd5 $14) 24... Nd7 25. Qd3 Nf6 26. a4 Nd7 27. h3 (27. b4 Rxc4 28. Bc5 b5 $11) 27... Kf8? {The king has decided to help with the support of the vulnerable d6-pawn, but the move has a serious flaw.} (27... Qa5 28. Qe2 Qb4 29. Rd3 Nc5 30. Bxc5 Qxc5 $14) 28. Bc1?! {[%mdl 64] The last truly exciting moment of this game has passed without either playersreally noticing. Carlsen misses a golden chance to create serious problems with the b-pawn advance.} (28. b4!? Rxc4 (28... Nf6?! 29. b5 Rxc4 30. Bb6 Qc8 31. Qxd6+ Kg8 32. Qe7 Qe6 33. Rd8+ Kh7 34. Qxe6 fxe6 35. bxa6 bxa6 36. a5 Nxe4 37. Ra8 $16) (28... a5 29. c5 axb4 30. cxd6 Qa5 31. Qb3 Ra6 32. Bd2 Qxa4 33. Rc1 $16) 29. Bc5 Rxc5 30. bxc5 Nxc5 31. Qxd6+ Qxd6 32. Rxd6 Nxa4 33. Rd5 f6 (33... Nc3? 34. Rxe5 f6 35. Rc5 Nxe4 36. Rc8+ Ke7 37. Rc7+ $16) 34. Rd8+ Ke7 35. Rg8 Kf7 36. Rc8 b6 37. Rc7+ Kg6 (37... Kf8 38. Rb7 Nc5 (38... b5 39. Kf3 $16) 39. Rxb6 Nxe4 40. Rxa6 $16) 38. f4 exf4 39. gxf4 Nc5 40. Kf3 a5 41. Rc6 $16) 28... Nc5 29. Qf3 Qb6 30. Ba3 Qxb3 31. Qxb3 Nxb3 32. Bxd6+ Kg8 33. Bxe5 Rxc4 34. Rd8+ Kh7 35. Rd7 Rxe4 36. f4 {White's pieces are active enough to secure draw.} Kg8 (36... Kg6 37. Rxb7 Nc5 38. Rb6+ f6 39. Bd6 Nxa4 40. Rxa6 $11) 37. Rd8+ Kh7 38. Rd7 Kg8 39. Rd8+ Kh7 1/2-1/2

