1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Nf3 Be7 6. Bc4 {This is a somewhat unusual square for the bishop. Normally, 6.d3 follows automatically.} Ngf6 {Nigel must be familiar with the position, as once he was on the white side, even though the game ended in a quick draw.} 7. Nxf6+ (7. Qe2 O-O 8. Nxf6+ Bxf6 9. Be3 c5 10. O-O-O Qa5 11. Qd2 Qxd2+ 12. Rxd2 Nb6 13. Be2 {½-½ Short,N (2615)-Spassky,B (2620)/Solingen 1986/TD}) 7... Nxf6 8. O-O O-O 9. Ne5 {This move is directed to preventing the fianchetto by b7-b6, followed by c8-b7.} (9. Qe2 b6 10. Bg5 Bb7 11. Rad1 Nd5 {has appeared in practice numerous times. Black has very little to worry about.}) 9... c5 10. dxc5 Qxd1?! {I don't think Black should give up the fight over the d-file.} (10... Bxc5 {Here White can avoid the exchange of queens, but it shouldn't be a source of worry for Black.} 11. Qe2 Nd5! (11... Qc7 12. Bf4 $36) 12. Rd1 Qc7 13. Bxd5 exd5 14. Rxd5 Be6 15. Rd1 Rae8 $44) 11. Rxd1 Bxc5 12. Be2! $14 {The bishop will be ready to go to f3, pressing on Black's queenside.} Ne4 {[%csl Rf2]} 13. Nd3 Be7 14. Be3 h6? {Under normal circumstances this would be a useful move, but here it's a loss of a vital tempi. In fact, this is already the last critical moment in the game.} (14... Bd7? 15. Bf3 f5 (15... Bc6? 16. Ne5 f5 17. Nxc6 bxc6 18. Bxe4 fxe4 19. Rd4 $18) 16. Bxe4 fxe4 17. Nc5 Bxc5 18. Rxd7 $16) (14... f6? 15. a4 e5 16. Bf3 Bf5 17. a5 a6 18. Ra4 Nd6 19. Bd5+ Kh8 20. c4 $16) (14... b6! {was called for.} 15. Ne5 (15. a4 Bb7 16. a5 Rfc8 17. axb6 axb6 18. Rxa8 Bxa8 $14) 15... Bd6 16. Nc4 (16. Bf3 Bxe5 17. Bxe4 Rb8 {is not much for White.}) 16... Bc7 17. Na3 (17. Bf3 Bb7 18. Rd7 Rac8 $11) 17... Be5 18. Bd4 Bxd4 19. Rxd4 Bb7 20. Rad1 $14 {After 14...b6 in all lines Black's position is slightly worse, but with accurate defence he should be able to hold.}) 15. a4! {From now on Black won't be able to complete his development without losing material.} a5 (15... b6 16. a5 {is no longer satisfactory for Black.}) 16. g3 {A somewhat surprising quiet move, which doesn't spoil anything, though. Black doesn't have a useful move.} (16. Ne5 {was far more obvious with the same idea which Magnus realised one move later.}) 16... Rd8 17. Ne5 Nf6 18. Nc4 Rxd1+ (18... Nd5 19. Nb6 Nxb6 20. Bxb6 {and Black is paralysed.}) 19. Rxd1 Nd5 20. Nb6 Nxe3 21. fxe3 Rb8 {The elimination of the bishop didn't make Black's life easier. His queenside pieces are still stuck.} 22. Kf2 e5 ({The alternatives lead to a very similar position:} 22... f6 23. Rd4 {[%csl Rc8][%cal Gd4c4]} e5 24. Rd5) (22... g6 23. Rd4 e5 24. Rd5) 23. Rd5 Be6 24. Rxa5 $18 Rd8 25. Bd3 {Black had to give up the important pawn in order to complete his development. Now the game enters into the technical phase, where White's pawn majority on the queenside, three against one, decides matters.} Bf6 26. e4! {Killing Black's hopes of opening some diagonals.} Rd4 27. Rb5 (27. Ra7 Rb4 28. Rxb7 Rxb2 29. a5 {was even easier.}) 27... g6 28. a5 Kg7 29. Ke2 Bg4+ 30. Kf2 Be6 31. Kf3 h5 32. Nd5 Bd8 33. b4 {Slowly, but surely, White's pawns march forward.} Bc8 34. Ke2 Bg4+ 35. Ke1 f5 36. Rxb7+ Kh6 37. Ne3 Bf3 38. a6 fxe4 39. Be2 Bg5 40. a7 1-0

