1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Qc2 Bd6 7. b3 O-O 8. Be2 b6 9. O-O Bb7 10. Bb2 Re8 11. Rad1 Qe7 12. Rfe1 Rac8 ({For some reasons, Topalov sticks to this continuation, that had led him to defeat in the second play-off game against Kramnik. From analytical point of view, the variation might be OK, but it will again prove as an "unlucky" choice. The main alternative is} 12... Rad8) 13. e4 (13. Bd3!? {Kramnik,V (2743)-Topalov,V (2813)/Elista 2006/CBM 115/[Marin,M] (1-0, 45)}) 13... Nxe4 14. Nxe4 dxe4 15. Qxe4 Bb4 16. Rf1 Ba3 $146 ({Previously, Black played this generally desirable move only after forcing the white queen define its intentions.} 16... Nf6 17. Qh4 Ba3 18. Ba1 c5 19. Ne5 cxd4 20. Rxd4 Rcd8 21. Rf4 Nd7 22. Qg3 Nxe5 23. Bxe5 f5 24. Bh5 Rf8 $13 {Genov,P (2485)-Rmus,A (2310)/Sutomore 2004/CBM 102 ext (0-1, 33)}) 17. Bxa3 Qxa3 18. Qc2 Nf6 19. c5!? {This structure is typical for the Catalan opening, but with a very significant difference: by immediately exchanging his passive bishop, Black basically solves his opening problems.} Ba6 20. Bxa6 Qxa6 21. Ne5 Nd5 {Black's control of this central square entirely compensates for White's advantage of development.} 22. Nc4 Red8 23. a3 Rc7 24. Qc1 Ne7 25. Rd2 Nf5 26. Rfd1 b5 27. Ne5 Qc8 28. Rd3 f6 29. Nf3 Rcd7 30. Qf4 Rd5 31. Qe4 Qd7 32. R3d2 {The d4-pawn is not in real danger, but its relative weakness restricts White's possibilities. If the game was played between less ambitious opponents, the most probable result would have been a draw.} h5 33. h4 a5 34. Kf1 Re8 35. Re1 Kf7 36. Red1 Nh6 37. Re1 Re7 38. Qf4 Rf5 39. Qd6 Qc8 ({This looks like an attempt to play for a win, but also a bit like playing with fire.} 39... Qxd6 40. cxd6 Rd7 41. Rc1 Rxd6 42. Rdc2 {would have led to approximate equality.}) 40. Rde2?! ({Carlsen misses the possibility of a thematic pawn break:} 40. d5!? {followed by d4, when Black's army would have been rather poorly coordinated.} Rxd5 (40... cxd5 41. Nd4 Re5 42. Rxe5 fxe5 43. Nc6 Rc7 44. Nxe5+ Kg8 45. b4 $16) 41. Rxd5 cxd5 42. Nd4 {Black's whole queeside is in danger, while the central mass of pawns cannot be advanced easily. Another source of danger is the strong c-pawn, sustained by practically all White's pieces.} b4 43. axb4 (43. a4 $16) 43... axb4 44. c6 $16) 40... Rd5 41. Qf4 Nf5 42. Re4 Rd8 $11 43. g4?! {With light-squared bishops on board, this would have been a decissive attack already, because White's bishop would have largely contributed to creating decissive threats. The way it is, Black has reliable stability on light squares, ensuring him against immediate dangers. In the long run, White faces the risk of getting a ruined pawn structure, which makes his decision quite questionable.} hxg4 44. Qxg4 Qd7!? ({An ambitious approach. Instead of preventing White's planned attack, Black starts a powerful counterplay on light squares. He could have immediately taken the h-pawn under control with} 44... Rh8 {and if} 45. h5 {then} Qd7 (45... Rh6? {unexpectedely offers White a crushing attack after} 46. Qxf5! exf5 47. Rxe7+ Kf8 48. Nh4! $18)) 45. h5 Kg8 ({Here, too,} 45... Rh8 {could have been played.}) 46. Kg2 Qd5 {The queen's planned incursion to b3 is very unpleasant for White, who's attack has not really started yet.} 47. Qg6 Rf8 ({As pointed out by Jacob Aagaard,} 47... Qxb3 {was entirely possible, not so much for the sake of the pawn, but with the intention of submitting White's arrierguard to some unpleasant pressure, for instance} 48. h6 (48. Rg4 Kf8! {Aagaard}) 48... Qd3 49. Rg4? Rxd4!! $19 {Aagaard. The point is that} 50. Nxd4 {loses the queen to} Nh4+) 48. h6 Qd8? (48... Nxh6? 49. Rh4 Nf5 (49... Kh8 50. Reh1 $18) 50. Rh5! $18 {followed by h1.}) (48... Qxb3!?) 49. d5!? ({Better later than never! White intends to distract the enemy queen from the defence of the kingside. The immediate} 49. Rg4?! {would have been adequately met by} Qe8) ({At the same time,} 49. Rxe6?! Qd5! {would have left White with problems of coordination because of the threat ...h4+.}) 49... Qxd5 50. Rg4 $40 Qd7 ({This regrouping will fail to absolve Black from problems, but} 50... Qxb3 {was bad, too.} 51. Rh1 Ref7 ({This move is more or less necesaary because in case of the more natural} 51... Qd5? {White has a fantastic combination at his disposal:} 52. Qh7+!! Kxh7 53. hxg7+ {followed by mate.}) 52. Rh5 {White has put up enormous pressure against the enemy king's position. Black is lost, for instance} Qc2 (52... b4 53. hxg7 Rxg7 54. Qh7+ Kf7 55. Ne5+! fxe5 56. Rxf5+ $18) 53. hxg7 Ne3+ (53... Rxg7 54. Qh7+ Kf7 55. Rxg7+ Nxg7 56. Qxc2 $18) 54. Kh3 Qxg6 55. gxf8=Q+ Kxf8 56. Rxg6 $18) 51. Rh1 Qe8 52. hxg7 Rxg7 53. Qh7+ Kf7 54. Rxg7+ Nxg7 55. Rh6 ({When attacking, the natural tendency is to move the pieces "forward". However, chess geometry is special. Here,} 55. Nd2 {would have been the start of the knights marching forward to d6. Black would have faced a difficult defensive task.}) 55... Qd8 56. Qg6+ Kg8 57. Qh7+ Kf7 58. Qg6+ Kg8 59. Rh7 Qd7 60. Nd2 {The same comment as after the 49th move applies here. It seems that the young Norvegian prodigee is able to learn from his own mistakes at breathtaking speed....} f5 61. Nf3 Rf6 62. Qxf6 Kxh7 63. Ng5+ Kg8 64. Qg6 ({The position looks so joyless for Black, that Topalov resigned. He probably saw that he has no perpetual after} 64. Qg6 Qd5+ 65. f3 {but obviously missed that after} e5 {the queen's access to g8 is opened. After} 66. Qh7+ Kf8 67. Qh8+ Qg8 {White has nothing better than take a draw by perpetual with} 68. Nh7+) 1-0

