1. d4 d6 2. Nf3 g6 3. c4 Bg7 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. e4 O-O 6. h3 Na6 (6... e5 {is the most popular main line.}) 7. Be3 c5 (7... e5 {is the other variation.}) 8. Be2 (8. d5 {is more common. However 8.e2 must not be worse.}) 8... cxd4 9. Nxd4 Nc5 10. Qc2 b6 11. O-O (11. b4!? {is an interesting choice. White doesn't want to allow ...b7 to attack the e4-pawn.} Ne6 12. Nb3 (12. Rd1!?) ({The careless} 12. O-O?! {could have been met by} Ng4! 13. hxg4 ({or} 13. Nxe6 Nxe3 $15) 13... Nxd4 $36 {and Black looks nicely placed.}) 12... a5 13. bxa5 bxa5 14. a4 Nd7 15. Rd1 Ba6 16. O-O Rc8 17. Nd5 Nec5 $132 {Granda Zuniga-Maze, Sitges 2015, and Black looks fine.}) 11... Bb7 12. Bf3 Rc8 13. Rad1 {The natural developing move.} (13. b4!? {was a good alternative again,} Na6 (13... Ncd7?! 14. e5!) (13... Ne6 14. Nxe6 fxe6 15. Qb3 {and White's structure is better, his position looks more tempting.}) 14. Qb3 Qc7 {Now the c4-pawn is indefensible, so White has to find tactical counterplay.} 15. Rac1! Qxc4 ({If Black doesn't capture the pawn with} 15... Qb8 16. Nd5 $36 {and White has gained some space, his position is powerful.}) 16. e5 Qxb3 17. axb3! Bxf3 18. exf6 Bxf6 19. gxf3 Nxb4 {and Black has three pawns for the knight, but after} 20. Rfd1 $14 {White is slightly better.}) 13... Qc7 14. b3 Qb8 15. Rfe1 Rfe8 16. Bc1 a6 17. Qe2 e6 {Black has followed a natural plan and he wants to play ...d5 at a good moment.} 18. Bb2 Rcd8 (18... d5!? {was an interesting option to break in the centre,} 19. exd5 exd5 20. Qc2 dxc4 21. bxc4 $11 {with an equal position. Probably Carlsen wanted to keep the tension.}) 19. Qc2 Qa8 20. Qe2 {White doesn't find any plans, he is trying to hold the position.} h5 (20... d5!? {was possible again,} 21. exd5 exd5 22. Qf1 (22. Qc2? {is bad now, as} Rxe1+ 23. Rxe1 dxc4 $17 {and the d4-knight is hanging.}) 22... dxc4 23. Qxc4 $11 {and they exchanged the central pawns.}) 21. Ba1 Ncd7 (21... d5 {is similar to the analysis of the 18th and 20th moves.}) 22. g3 (22. Nc2 {was more solid, however} Ne5 {and Black takes the f3-bishop.}) 22... d5! {The typical break-through in the hedgehog structure.} 23. Bg2 {It is a cool-blooded reply, now Black has a lot of tempting possibilities.} e5 ({Probably} 23... dxe4!? {was the most precise,} 24. Nxe4 Nxe4 25. Bxe4 Bxe4 26. Qxe4 Qxe4 27. Rxe4 Ne5 28. Re3 Rd6 $15 {Black has a comfortable endgame.}) 24. Nc2 dxe4 (24... d4 25. Nd5 {gives good counterplay for White.}) 25. Nxe4 Nxe4 26. Bxe4 Bxe4 27. Qxe4 Qxe4 28. Rxe4 Nc5 29. Ree1 Nd3 30. Re3 e4 31. Bxg7 Kxg7 32. Ne1 {White would like to exchange for a  endgame, which gives the best chance of a draw.} ({In case of} 32. f3 Nc5 33. Rxd8 Rxd8 34. b4 Ne6 35. fxe4 Ng5 36. h4 Rd2! $15 {and White has to fight to equalise.}) 32... Nc5 $15 {Black's position is more tempting, but with White's accurate play he will hold the endgame.} 33. Rxd8 Rxd8 34. b4! {The best practical chance.} Nd3 35. Rxe4 Nxb4 36. a3 Nc6 37. Nc2 Rd1+ 38. Kg2 a5 39. Ne3 Ra1 40. Nd5 Rxa3 41. Nxb6 {It is good to simplify the position for White, because he has more chance to equalise.} Rc3 42. Re8 Rb3 43. c5 Rc3 44. Na4 Rc4 45. Rc8! Ne5 46. Nb6 Rc2 47. Nd5 {White is active, so probably Black can't avoid the exchange of the a- and c-pawns.} a4 48. Ne3 Rc3 49. Nd5 Rc1 50. Ra8 Rxc5 51. Ne3 {Black can't protect the a4-pawn.} Nc4 52. Rxa4 Nxe3+ 53. fxe3 Rc2+ 54. Kf3 g5 55. Ra5 f6 56. e4 Rc3+ 57. Kf2 Rc2+ 58. Kf3 Rc3+ 59. Kf2 {Nice defending technique by White!} 1/2-1/2

