1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Nf3 b6 5. Bg5 Bb7 6. Nd2 h6 7. Bh4 Nc6 8. a3 Bxc3 9. bxc3 Qe7 {The knight from the square can be relocated later and Carlsen sees no need to lose time for d6-bd7.} 10. e3 g5 11. Bg3 d6 12. h4 {The first independent move can not achieve much as the second player is already somewhat ahead in the development.} (12. Be2 O-O-O 13. Rb1 e5 14. d5 Na5 15. e4 Nd7 16. h4 Kb8 17. h5 Nc5 18. Qc2 Bc8 19. Nf1 Rhf8 20. Ne3 Nxe4 21. Qxe4 f5 22. Qc2 f4 23. O-O e4 24. Qd2 fxg3 25. fxg3 Qe5 $17 {1-0 Pelletier,Y (2607)-Perunovic,M (2420)/Plovdiv 2008 (49)}) 12... O-O-O 13. Be2 Nd7 (13... Na5!? 14. f3 (14. Bf3 Nd7 15. Bxb7+ Kxb7 16. Qf3+ c6 $11) 14... g4 15. Qc2 Rdg8 16. Bf2 gxf3 17. gxf3 Ng4 18. Bg1 f5 $15) 14. Nb3 f5 15. Qc2 Rhf8 16. f3 (16. hxg5 hxg5 17. O-O-O Kb8 $13) 16... e5 17. hxg5 hxg5 18. O-O-O {The real start of the middle game in a fairly even position. White will try to control the open h-file, Black will hope for action on the queenside.} (18. d5!? Ncb8 19. e4 f4 20. Bf2 Na6 $11) 18... Rde8 19. Bd3 (19. d5 Ncb8 20. Bf2 $11) 19... Qf7 20. Rh6 Ba6 21. Nd2 (21. d5 Ne7 22. Rdh1 $11) 21... exd4 22. exd4 Na5 23. Rdh1! Re7 (23... Nxc4? 24. Qa4 Nxd2 25. Qxa6+ Kb8 26. Kxd2 Qa2+ 27. Bc2 $18) (23... Bxc4?! 24. Nxc4 Nxc4 25. Qa4 b5 (25... Re3 26. Rh7 $18) 26. Qxb5 Ncb6 27. c4 $16) 24. Qa4 {Bacrot is not finding courage to play the principal move h7, as he probably feared the strength of enemy's counterplay with two rooks.} (24. Rh7 Qxh7 (24... Qf6 25. Rxe7 Qxe7 26. Bxf5 Bxc4 27. Qe4 Qxe4 28. fxe4 $16) 25. Rxh7 Rxh7 26. Bxf5 Rh1+ 27. Kb2 Nxc4+ (27... Rg1 28. Bh3 g4!? 29. fxg4 Re8 30. g5 Nxc4+ (30... Re2 31. Bf4 Nxc4+ 32. Kb3 Na5+ 33. Ka4 $16) 31. Nxc4 Bxc4 32. Bf2 Rf1 33. Bg4 b5 34. Bg3 Kb8 $13) 28. Nxc4 Bxc4 29. Qe4 Rxf5! 30. Qxf5 Rg1 31. Qh3 Bf1 32. Qg4 Rxg2+ 33. Kc1 Bc4 34. Qxg5 b5 $13) 24... Re3 25. Rh7 Qe8 26. Qc2 (26. Kc2? Rxd3 27. Kxd3 Nc5+ $19) 26... Nf6 27. R7h6 (27. Bxf5+? Kb8 28. R7h6 Nxc4 $17) 27... Kb7 28. Bf2 Rxd3!? {[%mdl 32] Carlsen is growing into a great follower of Tigran Petrosian in his love for positional exchange sacrifices.} (28... Re7 29. d5 $11) 29. Qxd3 Nd5 30. Re1 (30. g3 Qa4 $13) 30... Qa4 31. Qc2 Qxa3+ 32. Qb2 Qxb2+ 33. Kxb2 Nxc4+ 34. Nxc4 (34. Kc2 g4 $15) 34... Bxc4 {Black can be satisfied with two pawns and excellent control of many squares.} 35. Be3 (35. Rh7 Rg8 $15) 35... g4 36. fxg4?! (36. Bd2 gxf3 37. gxf3 Rg8 $15) 36... fxg4 37. Rg6? a5? {The upcoming time control has seriously influenced the quality of play of both players. The mutual blunder ignores the fact, that Bacrot could have lost the piece.} (37... Re8! 38. Bd2 Rxe1 39. Bxe1 Nf4 40. Rxg4 Nd3+ 41. Ka3 Nxe1 $19) 38. Rxg4 a4 39. Rg3 (39. Ka3 b5 40. g3 Nxc3 $17) 39... a3+ 40. Kxa3? (40. Ka1 b5 41. Rf3 Rxf3 42. gxf3 Nxc3 $15) 40... Ra8+ 41. Kb2 Ra2+ 42. Kc1 Nxc3 {The excitement is over; White will lose the exchange and the ending should be winning for Black.} 43. Bd2 (43. Bf4 Ne2+ 44. Rxe2 Rxe2 45. Rg7 Kc6 $17) 43... Ne2+ 44. Rxe2 Bxe2 45. Rg7 Ra4 46. Bc3 (46. Be3 b5 47. g4 b4 $17) 46... b5 47. g4 Bf3 (47... b4 48. Bd2 Bc4 $17) 48. g5 b4 49. Bb2 (49. Bd2 Bd5 $17) 49... Ra5 {Magnus is able to stop the dangerous g-pawn and the white bishop b2 will remain a misserable piece.} 50. Kc2 (50. g6 Be4 51. Rg8 Rg5 $19) 50... Bd5 51. Re7 (51. g6 Bc4 52. Re7 Rg5 53. g7 Rg2+ 54. Kc1 Bd5 $19) 51... Bc4 52. Bc1 Rf5 53. Bd2 Rf2 54. g6 Rg2 55. g7 Kc6 56. Re8 (56. Kc1 b3 $19) 56... Bf7 57. Rb8 (57. Re7 Bg8 $19) 57... b3+ 58. Kc3 Rxg7 59. Kb2 Bc4 60. Kc3 Kd5 {Finally all black pieces can work together, White is lost.} 61. Rb7 Rg3+ 62. Kb2 Kxd4 (62... Kxd4 63. Rxc7 Rg2 64. Kc1 b2+ $19) 0-1

