1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+ Nd7 {The knight move has the reputation of being a fighting line, but Black is falling behind in development.} 4. d4 a6 5. Bxd7+ Bxd7 6. dxc5 dxc5 7. Nc3 e6 8. Bf4 {Ni Hua is clearly ahead in creating the play, but Black has no real weaknesses.} (8. Ne5 Ne7 (8... Qc7 9. Qxd7+ (9. Nxd7 Rd8 (9... O-O-O!? 10. Qf3 Rxd7 11. Bf4 Bd6 $11) 10. Qf3 Rxd7 11. Bf4 Qc6 12. O-O Nf6 13. Rfd1 Be7 14. Rxd7 Nxd7 15. Qg3 O-O 16. Rd1 Rc8 17. Bd6 $14 {½-½ Unzicker,W (2535)-Najdorf,M (2520)/Nice 1974 (39)}) 9... Qxd7 10. Nxd7 Kxd7 11. Bf4 Kc6 12. O-O-O Nf6 13. f3 Be7 14. Ne2 Rad8 15. Rxd8 Rxd8 16. Rd1 Nd7 17. b3 h5 18. c3 g5 19. Be3 Rg8 {0-1 Romero Holmes,A (2544)-Bologan,V (2673)/Pamplona 2003 (61)}) 9. Qh5 Ng6 10. Nxg6 fxg6 11. Qe5 Qf6 12. Bf4 Be7 {½-½ Zvjaginsev,V (2650)-Bu Xiangzhi (2620)/Moscow 2004}) 8... Ne7 9. Ne5 (9. Qe2 Ng6 10. Bg3 Be7 11. h4 h5 12. O-O-O b5 13. e5 b4 14. Ne4 Qa5 15. Kb1 Qb5 16. Rd3 c4 17. Nd6+ Bxd6 18. Rxd6 b3 19. Nd4 {½-½ Hracek,Z (2595)-Lutz,C (2596)/Mallorca 2004}) 9... Ng6 {The defender has no time for niceties as White is ready to strike.} (9... Bb5? 10. Qh5 g6 11. Qf3 Nc6 12. Nxc6 Bxc6 13. Be5 Rg8 14. Rd1 Qg5 15. Bf6 Qh5 16. g4 Qh6 17. g5 (17. Nd5 exd5 18. exd5 Ba4 19. d6 Bxd6 20. Rxd6 $18) 17... Qh4 18. Qg3 Qxg3 19. hxg3 $16 {½-½ Adams,M (2729)-Bu Xiangzhi (2708)/Yerevan 2008 (64)}) 10. Qh5 (10. Bg5 Qc7 11. Nxd7 Qxd7 12. Qxd7+ Kxd7 13. Be3 Bd6 14. g3 Kc6 15. O-O-O Rad8 16. Rhe1 b5 17. Nb1 Bc7 18. Nd2 {½-½ Riff,J (2428)-Cossin,S (2395)/Rosny sous Bois 2004 (39)}) 10... Bc6 {Carlsen must play well to keep his chances for equality alive. A good alternative seem to be the queen foray to h4.} (10... Nxe5 11. Bxe5 Bc6 12. O-O Qa5 13. Rad1 c4 14. Qg5 Qc5 15. Rfe1 f6 16. Bxf6 Qxg5 17. Bxg5 Bb4 18. a3 Bxc3 19. bxc3 h6 20. Bf4 $16 {1-0 Brkic,A (2544)-Feletar,D (2377)/Sibenik 2009 (52)}) (10... Qh4!? 11. Qxh4 Nxh4 12. O-O Rd8 13. Nxd7 Rxd7 14. Be3 $14) 11. Bg3 (11. Nxg6 fxg6 12. Qg4 Qf6) (11. Nxc6 Nxf4 12. Nxd8 Nxh5 13. Nxb7 Rb8 14. Na5 Rxb2 15. O-O-O Rb8 $14) 11... Nxe5 (11... Be7 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. f4 Qb8 14. O-O-O Nxf4 15. Qg4 g5 16. h4 $14) 12. Bxe5 c4 13. O-O (13. f4!? b5 14. a3 Qd7 15. Rf1 O-O-O 16. Rd1 Qb7 17. Rxd8+ Kxd8 18. Rf2 $14) 13... Qa5 14. Qg5 h6 15. Qg3 {White is losing momentum and opts for the second best move. More challenging for the opponent would have been the retreat to f4.} (15. Qf4!? h5 (15... f6 16. Bxf6 (16. Bc7 Qg5 17. Qf3 Bb4 $11) 16... gxf6 17. Qxf6 Rh7 18. Qxe6+ Re7 19. Qg6+ $16) 16. Rad1 f6 17. Bc7 Qg5 $11) 15... f6 16. Qg6+ (16. Bc7 Qg5 17. Qf3 Bb4 18. Qe2 Kf7 $11) 16... Ke7 17. Bf4 (17. Bd4 Qg5 18. Qxg5 hxg5 19. Rad1 $11) 17... Be8 18. Qg3 Kf7 19. Rad1 Bc6 20. Rd2 e5! {Carlsen has a very good feel for the position and is ready to fight for the advantage at the first suitable moment.} (20... Be7 21. Rfd1 Rac8 22. Bd6 Bxd6 23. Rxd6 Rhd8 $11) 21. Be3 Bb4 22. f4 Rhe8 23. f5 Bc5 {Not even the player heading for the top spot in the rating list has found the courage to play provocative solution of grabbing the pawn on c3.} (23... Bxc3!? 24. bxc3 Qxc3 25. Qg6+ Kg8 26. Bxh6 Re7 27. Qg4 Qb2 $15) 24. Rfd1 Rad8 25. Rxd8 Bxe3+ (25... Rxd8?? 26. Qg6+ Kg8 27. Rxd8+ Qxd8 28. Bxc5 $18) 26. Qxe3 Rxd8 27. Rxd8 Qxd8 28. Kf2 Qd6 {[%mdl 4096] For most mortals the chances would seem fairly even, but Magnus is envisaging a pawn advance on the queenside.} (28... Qd4 29. Kf3 (29. Qxd4 exd4 30. Ne2 Bxe4 31. Nxd4 Ke7 $15) 29... Qd6 $11) 29. a3 a5 30. Kf3 (30. Ke2 Qd4 31. Kf3 $11) 30... Kg8 (30... b5 31. Qe2 (31. Qa7+? Kg8 32. Qxa5 Qd2 $17) 31... Qd4 32. Qe3 Qd7 33. Ke2 b4 $15) 31. g3? {Ni Hua can not afford waiting tactics as Black is making progress with his plan.} (31. Ke2! Kf8 (31... b5 32. Qb6 $14) 32. Qb6 Qd4 33. Qxa5 Bxe4 34. Qa8+ Kf7 35. Qc8 $15) 31... b5! 32. Ke2 (32. Qb6?? Bxe4+ $19) 32... b4 33. axb4 axb4 34. Nd1 {The choice of retreats for the knight was not exactly encouraging; White probably played the best move.} (34. Nd5? Bxd5 35. Qd2 c3 36. bxc3 Bc4+ $19) (34. Na2!? Bb7 35. c3 Qa6 36. Nxb4 Qa1 37. Nd5 Qh1 $17) 34... Ba4 (34... Qd4 35. c3 Qxe4 (35... bxc3 36. Nxc3 Qd6 37. Qd2 $11) 36. Qxe4 Bxe4 37. cxb4 Bxf5 38. b5 Kf7 39. b6 Be4 40. Ne3 Ke6 41. Nxc4 $11) 35. b3? {The opening of the position is certainly in Black's favour as his bishop is stronger than the knight.} (35. Qd2! Qd4 36. Qxd4 exd4 37. Kd2 Bb5 38. c3 dxc3+ 39. bxc3 b3 40. Ke3 Be8 41. Kd4 Bf7 $15) 35... cxb3 36. cxb3 Qa6+ (36... Bb5+ 37. Ke1 Qc6 38. Kd2 Qa8 39. Qc5 Qa2+ $17) 37. Kd2 Bb5 38. Qc5 Qa2+ 39. Qc2?! {The last move before the time control deserves some criticism, since White will not be able to stop the pesky enemy pieces.} (39. Kc1 Qe2 (39... Be2 40. Ne3 Kh7 41. Nd5 Qa1+ 42. Kc2 Qd1+ (42... Bd1+ 43. Kd3 Bxb3 44. Qxb4 Qf1+ $17)) 40. Qxb4 Bd3 41. Qd2 Qxe4 42. g4 Bf1 $17) 39... Qa7! 40. Qc8+ Kh7 41. Kc1 Qa1+ 42. Kc2 Qd4 {The triumph of Black's strategy, the white position will fall apart.} 0-1

