[BlackElo "2861"]
[PlyCount "132"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. c3 {Is this a psychologyc joke? Did Hou Yifan hope to make Carlsen feel uncomfortable by using his own weapon against him? A double edged experiment...} Nf6 4. d4 exd4 5. e5 Nd5 {With a bit of imagination, we can notice some similarities with the Alapin Sicilian.} (5... Ne4 6. Qe2 f5 7. exf6 d5 $13 {De Mondragon,J-Palciauskas,V/USA 1972/Corr Nr. 1 (0-1, 25)}) 6. Bc4 (6. cxd4 Bb4+ {Kanmazalp,O (2379)-Atalik,S (2608)/Golden Sands 2012/CBM 149/[Atalik,S] (0-1, 41)}) 6... Nb6 7. Bb3 d5 (7... d6 {looks quite allright, too:} 8. O-O (8. Ng5 Nxe5 9. cxd4 h6) 8... Be7 (8... dxe5 9. Ng5) 9. exd6 Qxd6 10. Nxd4 Nxd4 11. cxd4 Be6 12. Bxe6 Qxe6 13. Re1 {Strategically, Black is doing great, and the pressure along the e-file allows White only to avoid getting the worse of it.} Qd7 14. Qe2 Nd5 15. Nc3 Nxc3 16. bxc3 O-O! 17. Rb1 (17. Qxe7? Rae8 $19) 17... b6 18. Bf4 Bd6 {1/2 Sermek,D (2495)-Mikhalchishin,A (2520)/Portoroz 1993/CBM 037}) 8. cxd4 Bg4 {An interesting position and I would say no less consistent than those arising from more popular openings. White has the centre, Black intends to undermine it. Just playing in according to the basic rules. Maybe the Ponziani should be tried more often?} 9. Be3 (9. Nc3 Be7 10. h3 Bh5 (10... Bxf3 11. gxf3 Qd7 12. f4 g6) 11. Be3 Qd7 {Emma,J-Pilnik,H/Buenos Aires 1972/EXT 99 (0-1, 43)}) 9... f6 $146 ({It is hard to say whether this pawn break should be played with or without the intermedioate check on b4:} 9... Bb4+ 10. Nc3 f6 11. h3 Bh5 12. g4 Bg6 13. O-O Qd7 $13 {Barrenechea Bahamonde,G (2261)-Maiorov,N (2574)/Paleochora 2012/ (0-1, 39). Carlsen probably thought that the bishop on f8 is more active than the knight on b1. This way, the developing moves from the previous game should favour White abstratly speaking.}) 10. O-O Qd7 11. h3 Bh5 12. e6!? {A bit too optimistic, I assume.} ({White should have kept developing:} 12. Re1 O-O-O 13. Nc3 Kb8 14. Rc1 {with chances to retain some pressure.}) 12... Qd6! (12... Qxe6 13. Ng5 fxg5 (13... Bxd1 14. Nxe6 Bxb3 15. Nxc7+ Kd7 16. Nxa8 $16) 14. Qxh5+ g6 15. Qxg5 $36) 13. Nc3 O-O-O 14. a4 a5 15. Nb5 Qxe6 16. Bf4 Rd7 17. Re1 Qf7 18. Rc1 Bb4 19. Re3 g5 20. Bh2 Nc4 21. Bxc4 dxc4 $15 {Black has stabilized, ha san extra pawn, and even if he loses it somehow, his position is better strategically.} 22. Qc2 (22. g4 Bg6 23. Nd2 Bd3 $15) 22... Bxf3 {I would thin twice before making his concession. Apparently, Carlsen was confident in his ability to maintain the advantage during the process of simplifications.} ({I would prefer} 22... Bg6 23. Qxc4 Qxc4 24. Rxc4 Bf7 25. Rc2 Rhd8 $17 {[%cal Gh7h5]}) 23. Rxf3 Nxd4 24. Nxd4 Rxd4 25. Be5 {Could he have missed this, as a result of premature relaxing? Sounds almost like a blasphemie, but 2800+ are human, too.} Rd2 26. Qf5+ Qd7 27. Qxf6 Re8 28. Bg3 Rd4 29. Rf1 g4 30. hxg4 Rxg4 31. Rf4 h5 32. Rxg4 hxg4 33. Rc1 c3 34. bxc3 Bc5 {Despite having given away the bishops pair and the extra pawn, Black maintains the better chances because of his safer king.} 35. Qg5 b6 36. c4 Re4 37. Qg6 Re2 38. Kf1 Qd2 39. Qxg4+ Kb8 40. Qxe2 Qxc1+ 41. Qe1 Qxc4+ 42. Qe2 Qxa4 43. Be5 Bd4 44. Bxd4 Qxd4 45. g4 Qd5 46. Qe8+ (46. f4 Qh1+ 47. Kf2 Qh2+ 48. Kf3 Qxe2+ 49. Kxe2 Kc8 {Three connected pawns can put the enemy king in zugzwang, while two pwns cannot do the same.} 50. Kd3 Kd7 51. Kc4 c5 52. Kb5 Ke7 53. Ka4 c4 54. Ka3 b5 55. Kb2 a4 {and so on.}) 46... Kb7 47. Qa4 b5 48. Qxa5 Qd1+ 49. Qe1 Qxg4 50. Qe5 Qc4+ 51. Kg2 Qc6+ 52. Kf1 b4 53. f4 b3 54. f5 Ka6 55. Qa1+ Kb6 56. Qh8 Qc1+ 57. Kg2 Qc2+ 58. Kh1 b2 59. Qb8+ Ka5 60. Qa7+ Kb4 61. Qb7+ Ka3 62. Qf3+ Qb3 63. Qa8+ Qa4 64. Qf3+ Ka2 65. Qd5+ Ka1 66. Qe5 c5 0-1

