1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 {Not sure whether I should consider this a surprise or not. However, when I first saw the first three moves I thought the world champion was actually opting for a King's Indian Defence!} 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. g3!? {An interesting line!} (5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. g3 {is also very interesting!}) 5... dxc4 6. Qa4+ Nfd7! {The best move but very well-known!} 7. Qxc4 Nb6 8. Qd3 O-O 9. Bf4 (9. Bg2 Nc6 $15) 9... Nc6 10. Rd1?! {I'm not a fan of this move. I understand that White is trying to protect d4 with d1 but that costs him too many tempi. Instead, I think White should have tried to get his king to safety as soon as possible.} (10. Bg2 Nxd4 11. Nxd4 e5! (11... Qxd4 12. Qxd4 Bxd4 13. Nb5 Bxb2 14. Rb1 a6 15. Rxb2 axb5 16. Rxb5 Bd7 17. Re5 Bc6 18. Bxc6 bxc6 19. Rxe7 Nd5 20. Re4 Rfe8 21. Rxe8+ Rxe8 22. Kd2 Nxf4 23. gxf4 Re4 24. e3 Ra4 25. Ra1 Kf8 26. Kc3 Ke7 27. Kb3 Ra5 28. Rc1 Rb5+ 29. Kc3 Rc5+ 30. Kd2 Rxc1 31. Kxc1 Kf6 32. Kd2 g5 33. fxg5+ Kxg5 34. f4+ Kg4 35. Kd3 Kf3 36. f5 f6 37. a4 h5 38. a5 h4 39. a6 h3 40. e4 Kg2 41. Ke2 Kxh2 42. Kf2 c5 43. e5 c4 44. exf6 c3 45. f7 c2 46. f8=Q c1=Q {1/2-1/2 (46) Bruedigam,M (2312)-Lagunow,A (2441) Germany 2013}) 12. Bxe5 (12. Ndb5 Qxd3 13. exd3 exf4 14. Nxc7 Rb8 15. O-O fxg3 16. hxg3 Rd8 $17 {1/2-1/2 (59) Huebner, R (2595)-Topalov,V (2750) Dortmund 1996}) 12... Bxe5 13. Rd1 Qe7 14. O-O c6 $15) 10... Bf5 11. Qd2 Bg4 12. Bg2? {This is too much.} (12. Ne5 Nxe5 13. Bxe5 Bxe5 14. dxe5 Qxd2+ 15. Rxd2 Nc4 16. Rd4 Nxe5 17. Re4 f6 18. Bg2 {may give White some chances.}) 12... Bxf3 13. Bxf3 Nxd4 14. Bxb7 Nc4 15. Qc1 Rb8 16. b3 Rxb7 17. bxc4 c5 18. O-O Rb4 {You know things are going really bad when every natural move for Black poses serious problems for White.} 19. Bh6 Bxh6 20. Qxh6 Rxc4 21. Ne4 Nxe2+ 22. Kg2 Qa8 23. f3 Rxe4! {Strong and forced otherwise g5 was a threat.} 24. fxe4 Qxe4+ 25. Kf2 Nd4 26. Qe3 Qd5 27. Rd2 e5 {The game is over. The rest is just an agony.} 28. Re1 Re8 29. Qe4 Qe6 {No exchange this time.} 30. Re3 Kg7 31. Rb2 c4 32. g4 Qf6+ 33. Ke1 Qg5 34. Kf2 Re6 35. Ke1 h5 36. h3 Rf6 37. Kd2 Rf4 38. Qxe5+ {I am sure Meier had seen the end but decided to give the joy of geometry to the spectators.} Qxe5 39. Rxe5 Nf3+ {Fork number 1.} 40. Ke3 Nxe5 41. Kxf4 Nd3+ {And fork number 2 decides the game!} 0-1

