[Event "Biel GM"]
[Site "Biel"]
[Date "2007.08.02"]
[Round "9"]
[White "Carlsen, Magnus"]
[Black "Radjabov, Teimour"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B07"]
[WhiteElo "2710"]
[BlackElo "2746"]
[PlyCount "55"]

{[%evp 0,55,20,28,79,84,71,83,94,27,53,9,43,47,34,37,37,46,37,13,-6,-10,-10,-68,-58,-50,-41,-48,-48,-61,-41,-34,-34,-29,2,-28,9,7,13,13,13,63,81,59,81,56,211,211,372,612,612,434,662,662,553,585,747,735] It's quite natural that such a game between two young and promising rivals is important for both of us. Despite being a few years younger, 16 years old Magnus Carlsen (and recently above 2700) is going to show his class.} 1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 {Back in his youth, Radjabov was a really ambitious player. He was known to be a King's Indian specialist, and I am by no means surprised that he is trying to drag Magnus into a slightly less theoretical and clear middlegame. In that sense, the Philidor Defence looked like a pretty decent choice.} 4. Nge2 Nbd7 5. g3 {At first sight, it looks like the future World Champions decide to go for a quiet choice. But having a stable position will simply enable him to launch a stronger attack. Let's see how the opening developed} c6 6. Bg2 (6. a4 {Of course, it is the other option, stopping Black's idea, but it's also quite compromising and weakening in the queenside, and it's rarely Magnus's type.} Be7 7. Bg2 O-O 8. O-O Re8 9. h3 Bf8 10. d5 cxd5 11. exd5 h5 12. Be3 b6 13. Qd2 Ba6 $14 {0-1 (39) Ghaem Maghami,E (2596)-Movsesian,S (2658) Berlin 2015}) 6... b5!? {Radjabov immediately asks his opponent to make choices, in that case, to allow ...b4 or not.} 7. a3 (7. O-O b4 {This is maybe not necessary immediately, but it's already worth considering it.} 8. Na4 c5!? $132) 7... Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. h3 a5 {Black needs two more moves to be able to continue the advance with ...b4, so in the meantime, Carlsen should find a solution for his knight. And it's like he was already thinking about it on the last move!} 10. g4!? Ba6 11. Ng3 b4?! (11... exd4! {This was certainly more critical. Radjabov has an occasion to disrupt White's harmony and he doesn't take it.} 12. Nce2!? (12. Qxd4 b4! 13. Nce2 bxa3 14. Rxa3 d5 $15 {[%cal Ra3e3,Re7c5] In opposition to the game, bringing the rook to the active square e3 is impossible, so it has to get back}) 12... c5 13. Nf5! {Maybe something like this, intending to have dangerous play on the kingside was White's idea.} g6! (13... Re8? 14. g5 Nh5 15. Nexd4 $16 {[%cal Rd1h5]}) 14. Nxe7+ Qxe7 15. Bh6 Rfe8 16. Ng3 b4 17. Re1 $44 {And it's true White has good compensation, but the position remains balanced, and at leat Black has an extra pawn.}) 12. Nce2 bxa3 13. Rxa3 d5 {[%cal Re7a3] This tempo gained on the rook seems to show that Black will have good possibilities to open the centre at his convenience, and most of the time the position is going to be fine for Black.} 14. Re3! {But this rook lift toward the centre is as strong as it is unusual!} dxe4 (14... exd4? 15. Nxd4 $16 {[%cal Rd4c6]}) (14... Re8 15. Re1! Qc7 {Waiting and reinforcing the position was another approach.} 16. dxe5! Nxe5 17. g5 Nxe4 (17... Nfd7 18. exd5 $18) 18. Nxe4 dxe4 19. Rxe4 Rad8 20. Nd4 $16 {[%cal Re4e5] And it suddenly becomes obvious that the rook is great on e4.}) 15. Re1! {For tactical reasons, it would have been a mistake to take back immediately, but there is no rush.} (15. Nxe4 Nd5 16. Rg3 exd4 $17) 15... Qc7?! {I think that the unusual continuation of the game made Radjabov lose the track of the game.} (15... Re8 {This was somehow stronger.} 16. g5 Nd5 17. Rxe4 Bxg5 18. dxe5 Bxc1 19. Qxc1 Rxe5 20. Rxe5 Nxe5 21. Nd4 $16 {[%cal Re1e5,Rd4c6]}) 16. Nf5! Bd8?! 17. g5 Nd5 18. Rxe4 {White's pieces are so much more active than his opponent's that the position is simply already winning for him.} f6 (18... exd4 {This would only help White bringing pieces toward the kingside, and it looks like a deadly attack} 19. Nexd4 $18 {[%cal Rd1h5,Re4h4]}) 19. Neg3 g6 (19... Kh8? {Bringing the king to a "safer" place seems reasonable, but it's actually helping White's attack to develop.} 20. g6! hxg6 21. Rh4+ Kg8 22. Qg4! gxf5 23. Nxf5 {It's impossible to stop this attack.} Rf7 24. Qg6 $18 {[%cal Rg6h7,Rh7h8]}) 20. Nh6+ Kg7 21. dxe5 fxg5 {Radjabov understands that the pawn on g5 is decisive for the attack. The problem is that the e-pawn also proves to be too strong!} (21... fxe5 22. c4! N5b6 23. b3 $18) 22. e6! {Very powerful. Carlsen doesn't try to save the knight, he tries to open the files for his rooks.} Kxh6 23. e7! {From now on, Radjabov will try to escape, but Carlsen remains precise.} Qb6 (23... Nxe7? 24. Rxe7 Bxe7 25. Rxe7 Rad8 26. Qd4 $18 {[%cal Rd4g7,Rd4h4]}) 24. exf8=Q+ Nxf8 25. c4! Nf4 26. Qd6 Kg7 27. Bxf4 gxf4 28. Re7+ {Radjabov eventually gives up. Impressive game by Carlsen, showing all game long his willingness, not only to win, but to destroy his opponent.} (28. Re7+ {A mate was soon going to follow:} Bxe7 29. Rxe7+ Kg8 (29... Kh6 30. Qxf4+ g5 31. Qf6+ Ng6 32. Qg7#) 30. Qf6 $18) 1-0

