1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. d4 Nbd7 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 Be7 7. e3 c6 8. Qc2 O-O 9. Bd3 Re8 10. h3 Ne4?! {A pawn sacrifice for the bishop pair is quite popular in modern chess - maybe the influence of Rybka? But this one is too odd. White gets a strong centre and a solid position.} 11. Bxe4 dxe4 12. Nxe4 Qa5+ 13. Kf1 Bf8 {A logical but too passive move.} (13... Qf5 14. Bxe7 Rxe7 15. Nfd2 Nb6 16. Rc1 Nd5 17. Kg1 Nb4 18. Qb3 a5 19. Nd6 Qd3 20. Nxc8 Rxc8 21. Nc4 Qxb3 22. axb3 b5 23. Nd6 Rd8 24. Nf5 Re6 25. g4 $16 {1/2 Van Wely,L (2655)-Piket,J (2630)/Groningen 1997/CBM 063/[ChessBase] (52)}) (13... Nf8?! {has been seen in practice, too:} 14. Bxe7 Rxe7 15. Kg1! Qf5 16. Nfd2 $16 {1/2 Vybiral,Z (2206)-Vevera,R (1966)/Klatovy 2005/CBM 107 ext (27)}) 14. Bf4? {White wrongly ignores the pin, after which Black obtains excellent counterplay.} (14. Kg1 Qf5! (14... Nb6 15. Nc5 Nd5 16. a3 h6 17. Bh4 $16 {1-0 Vybiral,Z (2245)-Lacina,A (2250)/Prague 2001/EXT 2003 (55)}) 15. Nfd2 Bb4 16. g4 Qg6 17. f4 Bxd2 18. f5 Qxg5 19. Nxg5 Bxe3+ 20. Kg2 Bxg5 $13) (14. Nc3! {is good enough to keep a clear advantage.} Nb6 (14... b5 15. Kg1 Nb6 {1-0 Videki,S (2385)-Robert,A (2225)/Biel 1990/EXT 1997 (37)} 16. Ne4! Bf5 $140 17. Nf6+! $18) (14... Bd6 15. e4 Nf8 16. g3 f6 17. Be3 Be6 18. a3 Rad8 19. Kg2 Bb8 20. b4 $16 {1-0 Sedlacek,O (2445)-McLeod,S/ICCF Email 2002/ICCF Telechess CBM 100 (53)}) (14... Bb4 15. e4 Nb6 16. Kg1 Be6 17. a3 Bd6 18. e5 Bc7 19. Bd2 h6 20. Kh2 Rad8 21. Rhe1 $16 {1-0 Klein,E-Sarabia Utrilla,J/ICCF WS 2006/ICCF CBM 132 (55)}) 15. g4 (15. Bf4!? Nd5 (15... Bf5 16. e4 Bxe4 17. Nxe4 Qf5 18. Nfg5 Kh8 19. Nf6! {1-0 Khenkin,I (2609)-Glaser,S (1946)/Bad Homburg 2008/CBM 124 Extra}) 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 $16) 15... h6 16. Bh4 Nc4 17. e4 b5 18. Kg2 Bb7 19. Rhe1 $16 {1-0 Lazarev,V (2445)-Mai,I (2185)/Meisdorf 1996/EXT 1997 (48)}) 14... Qf5 15. Nfd2 Nb6! {Even stronger than} (15... c5!? 16. g4 Qg6 (16... Qd5 17. Qb3 Qxb3 18. axb3 cxd4 19. exd4 Nb6 $44) 17. Rc1 (17. d5?! b5! $36) 17... cxd4 18. exd4 Nb6 $44) 16. Rc1 (16. Qb1 Nd5 17. g4 Qg6 18. Be5 f6 19. Bd6 f5 20. Bxf8 fxe4 21. Ba3 b6 $40 {[%cal Yc8a6,Ya6d3,Ya6e2]}) 16... Nd5 17. g4 Qg6 18. Nd6 (18. Be5 f6 (18... Nb4 19. Qb1 f6 20. Bg3 Be6 21. b3) 19. Bd6 (19. Bg3 h5! $36) 19... Bxd6 20. Nxd6 Qxc2 21. Rxc2 Rxe3! 22. Rxc6 Rd3 23. Rxc8+ Rxc8 24. Nxc8 Rxd2 25. Kg2 Rxb2 $15) 18... Rd8! 19. Qxg6? (19. Nxc8 $142 Nxf4 20. exf4 Qxc2 (20... Rxd4 21. f5 Qh6 22. Nb3) 21. Rxc2 {[%csl Gc2]} Rxd4 22. Nxa7 Rxa7 23. b3 {and White should not lose.}) 19... hxg6 (19... fxg6! {was even stronger, according to V.Yemelin. Black could have made use of the f-file.}) 20. Nxc8 Nxf4! 21. exf4 Rxd4 22. Nb3 Rb4! $17 23. Kg2 (23. Nxa7? Rxa7 {is bad for White; therefore Black keeps a queenside pawn advantage, in addition to the advantage of his bishop over the white knight in this open position; meanwhile, White's f4 pawn is doomed anyway.}) 23... Rxc8 24. Rhd1 Rxf4 25. Rd7 Rb4 26. Rc2 Re8 27. Rcd2 Be7!? {Black concludes that White's invasion to the 7th rank is not too dangerous as the g7 pawn will be safely protected.} (27... Re5!? {[%csl Rb3] was also possible:} 28. Rc7 (28. Rd8 f5) 28... Rd5 29. Rxd5 (29. Re2 f5 30. g5 f4! 31. h4 f3+ 32. Kxf3 Rf5+ 33. Kg2 Rxh4 34. Rxb7 Rxg5+ 35. Kf3 Rh3+ $17) 29... cxd5 30. Rd7 Rb5 $17 {[%cal Ya7a5]}) 28. Rc7 Bf6 29. Rdd7 b6! (29... Re2 30. Nc1! Rc2 31. Nd3 Rd4 32. Rxb7 Rd2 33. Nc5 Rxd7 34. Nxd7 Bxb2 35. Nc5 $132 {[%cal Rc5e4]}) (29... a6 30. Rxb7 (30. Rxf7 b6 {[%cal Yc6c5]}) 30... Rxb7 31. Rxb7 Re2 32. g5! Bxb2 (32... Bxg5 33. Nd4 $132) 33. Rb6 $132) 30. Rxa7 g5! 31. Rd2 (31. Rxf7 Re2 32. Nc1! Rd2 33. Rfd7 Rxd7 34. Rxd7 Rxb2 35. Nd3 Re2 $17 {[%cal Yc6c5]}) 31... c5 32. Ra6 (32. Rb7 c4 33. Nd4 Rd8 34. a3 Ra4 $19) (32. Rc2 c4 $140 ({but what will White do after, say,} 32... g6 {?}) 33. Nd2 b5 34. Rb7!) 32... c4?! {Black unnecessarily forces the game.} (32... Kh7!?) 33. Nd4 Bxd4 (33... Rd8!? {but after} 34. a3! {[%csl Rb4]} Rb5 35. Nxb5 Rxd2 36. Rxb6 Rxb2 37. a4 Ra2 38. Rc6 (38. Ra6 $143 ?! Be5) 38... Rxa4 39. Nd6 c3 40. Kf3 {Black's victory is doubtful.}) 34. Rxd4 Rxb2 {[%mdl 4096]} 35. Rd7 (35. Rxc4 Ree2 36. Kg3 Rxf2 37. Rxb6 Rg2+ 38. Kf3 Rbf2+ 39. Ke4 (39. Ke3 Rxa2) 39... Rf4+ 40. Kd5 Rxc4 41. Kxc4 Rxa2 42. Rb3 Rd2 {should be lost for White.}) 35... c3 36. Raa7 Rf8 37. Rdc7 (37. Rac7 $143 c2 38. a4 Ra2 39. Re7 b5! 40. axb5 Ra7!! 41. b6 c1=Q 42. bxa7 Qf4 {and Black should win, e.g.} 43. Rb7 g6 44. Rb6 Kg7 45. Rbb7 Rc8 46. Rbd7 Qf6 47. Rb7 Qc6+ 48. Kg3 Qd6+ 49. Kg2 Qd5+ 50. Kg3 (50. Kh2 Qf3) 50... Rc3+ 51. Kh2 Ra3 $19) 37... c2 38. Kg3? {A big mistake as the a2-pawn will now be lost.} (38. a4! g6 39. a5 (39. Kh2 Rb8 40. Kg2 Ra2! $19 {[%cal Yb6b5]}) 39... bxa5 40. Rxa5 Rd8 41. Rxg5 ({but not} 41. Raa7? Rd1 42. Ra8+ Kg7 43. Rac8 Rd2 $19 {[%cal Rd2f2]}) 41... Rd2 {[%cal Rd2f2]} 42. Kg3! {and there is apparently no win for Black, e.g.} Kg7 (42... Rxf2 43. Rb5! Rg2+ 44. Kf3 Ra2 45. Ra5 Rxa5 46. Kxg2 Ra2 47. Kf3 Kg7 48. g5! $11) (42... Rb3+ 43. Kg2 (43. f3 Rdd3 44. Rxc2 Rxf3+ 45. Kg2 Rxh3 $17) 43... Kg7 44. h4!) 43. Rgc5 (43. h4!? Rxf2 44. h5 Kf6 45. Rgc5 gxh5 46. gxh5 Kg7 47. R5c6 {etc.}) 43... Rxf2 44. Rb7 (44. h4!?) 44... Ra2 45. Ra7 Rg2+! 46. Kf3 Rxa7 47. Kxg2 Ra2 48. Kf3 Kf6 {- that's why Black has played 42...g7 but...} 49. h4! {[%cal Yh4h5] with sufficient counterplay} ({not, however,} 49. g5+? Ke6 50. Ke3 Kd6 51. Rc8 Kd5 52. Kd2 Ra3 53. Rc7 Rxh3 54. Rxf7 Rh2+ 55. Kc1 Rg2 56. Rf6 Rxg5 57. Kxc2 Ke5 $19 {})) 38... Rd8 39. Rxf7? (39. Kg2 {was much more stubborn:} Kh7 40. Rc3 Rd2 41. Rxf7 Kg8! 42. Rff3 (42. Ra7 Rxf2+ 43. Kg3 Rd2 44. Rc8+ Kh7 45. Kf3 b5 $19 {V.Yemelin}) (42. Rf5? Rd1) 42... Rxa2 43. Rc8+ Kh7 44. Rf7 Rd8 (44... Ra8!?) 45. Rc3 (45. Rcc7 Ra7!! 46. Rxg7+ Kh6 47. Rge7 Rd6 $19) 45... Rd1 46. Rfc7 Rd2 {[%cal Rd2f2]} 47. Rf7 Kg8! 48. Rff3 b5! 49. Rc8+ Kh7 50. Rf7 Ra8! ({not} 50... Rd8 51. Rcc7 Ra7? {now, due to} 52. Rxa7! c1=Q 53. Rxg7+ Kh6 54. Rgc7! Qd2 55. Ra6+ Rd6 56. Rd7!! Qd5+ 57. Kg1) 51. Rcc7 Kh8! {etc.} 52. Rxg7 $140 c1=Q) 39... Rd3+ 40. Kg2 c1=Q {The black king escapes to d8.} 0-1

