1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. c4 c5 4. d5 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nc3 g6 7. g3 Bg7 8. Bg2 O-O 9. O-O a6 10. a4 Re8 11. Bf4 Ne4 {I had this early knight move in my opening notebook (paper notebook, I mean) about 20 years ago. I remember that I was attracted by the activity displayed by the black rook along the 4th rank, but did not bother about remaining underdeveloped. Nowadays I would think twice before playing this line.} 12. Nxe4 Rxe4 13. Nd2 Rb4 14. b3!? {A rare move. According to my old notebook, it was first suggested by Bozidar Ivanovic.} ({The main line continues} 14. Ne4 {Apparently threatening to win a crucial pawn.} h6! {Black's whole play is based on this modest looking move.} 15. Ra2! {Suddenly, it becomes clear that capturing the d6 pawn would lose material to echo variations:} (15. Nxd6 Rxf4 16. Nxc8 Rd4 {or}) (15. Bxd6 Rxe4 16. Bxb8 Rd4) ({These lines are a good illustration of the fourth rank activity of the black rook. My initial preparation relied on an older game by Tal:} 15. Bd2 Rxb2 16. Qc1 Rxd2 17. Qxd2 f5 18. Nc3 {and now I wanted to improve over Savon-Tal USSR 1970, where Black saved himself by a miracle, with the developing move} Nd7 {Black has a dangerous queenside majority and a strong bishop to compensate for the exchange; besides, White's rook don't have too many active possibilities yet. 15.a2 was the novelty prepared for use against me by Adorjan.}) 15... f5 16. Nc3 Qf6 {I only once repeated the line in recent times. The game went:} 17. Qc1 {Before retreating the bishop to d2 in order to bother the rook, White defends the b2 pawn without loss of tempo, causing at the same time a further weakening of the black kingside.} (17. e4 Nd7 18. exf5 gxf5 19. Bd2 Qf7 20. b3 Nf6 {I put maximum pressure on the central pawn, in order to prepare the spectacular escape of the rook.} 21. Ne2 Re4! 22. Bc3 ({After} 22. Bxe4 fxe4 {Black would get a strong centre after eliminating the d5-pawn, as well as excellent attacking chances on the weakened light squares around the white king.}) 22... Re7 23. Nf4 {White has regrouped his minor pieces in an optimal way, but he will still have to lose some time in order to nullify the pressure along the e-file, thus allowing Black to maintain the balance.} Bd7 24. Re1 Rae8 25. Rxe7 {and White offered a draw in the game Adorjan-Marin, Szirak Izt 1987/CBM 003. A logical continuation would have been} Rxe7 26. Re2 Rxe2 27. Qxe2 Nh5 {A key move, exchanging White's active knight.} 28. Bd2 Nxf4 29. Bxf4 Qf6 30. a5 {with even chances. When annotating the game for the New in Chess Yearbook 8, I became less convinced that Adorjan's 17.e4 was the best move. 17.c1, as played by Akesson, seemed much more dangerous, but in my notes I gave an apparently promising continuation for Black...}) 17... g5 18. Bd2 {The only thing I remembered during the game was that this was the critical continuation and that I had written something about it. White intends to retreat the knight to d1 and win the exchange in a reasonable form.} Bd7!? {One of the most paradoxical move I've ever played. As if it wasn't enough that Black is undeveloped, he seemed to volunturily block the access of the knight and rook to life. How can a player ever consider such a move? I had, of course, started with the natural continuations.} ({I saw rather quickly that} 18... b5? {loses to} 19. Nxb5! {because of the pin along the c-file. (I was rather confused when I found out that in 1987 I had missed this tactical blow and recommended 18...b5 as the best move with the continuation}) (18... Nd7 {looks like the most natural continuation, but after} 19. Nd1 {the main problem is that White will capture on b4 only after the black knight will move away from d7, thus failing to occupy the c5-square, made available by ...cxb4. At the same time, maintaining the knight on d7 would leave the rest of the queen side undeveloped. We shall soon see that the move actually played in the game is a much better way of coordinating the pieces.}) 19. Qc2 {One obvious aim of Black's last move was to delay d1 for a moment because of the hanging a4-pawn. However, the idea is much deeper than that, as will become clear after the next move.} a5! {After having taken the b5 square under control, Black can safely play this move, preparing to develop the knight to a6.} 20. Nd1 ({My opponent avoids a small trap:} 20. Nb5?! Bxb5 21. Bxb4? axb4! {when Black wins material.}) 20... Na6 21. Bc3 Qf7 22. Ra1 {White has made all the preparatory moves and seems to be ready to capture the rook. On the other hand, Black has completed his development and is ready to recapture on b4 with the c-pawn, clearing the c5 square for the knight, an improved version of the idea behind the natural 18...d7 (instead of 18...d7). I considered my position to be slightly better already.} f4 {It is important to deny the access of the white knight to c4 via e3. For the first time in the game, my opponent sank into a prolonged thought. He gradually understood that "winning" the exchange wouldn't favor him and played the strong} 23. Be4 {taking under control the weakened light diagonal and preventing the annoying ...f5.} ({I had hoped for a variation such as} 23. Bxb4 cxb4 24. Rc1 Nc5 25. b3 Bf5 26. Qa2 Re8 {when White is completely paralyzed. For instance,} 27. Re1? {with the idea of defending the e2 pawn and preparing b2-c4, would lose instantly to} Nd3!) 23... Rf8 24. Bg6 Qe7 25. Re1 Be5 $36 {Black prepares to drive the enemy bishop back with g7, f6, thus preparing a massive kingside attack, Akesson,J (2333)-Marin,M (2531)/Gothenburg 2004/CBM 099 (½-½, 93)}) 14... Rxf4 $146 {This move has never been played before, but was part of my preparation, too.} (14... Nd7 15. Ra2 (15. Bxd6! $14) 15... Ne5 16. Bxe5 Bxe5 17. Nc4 Qe7 18. a5 Bd7 19. Qc2 Bb5 $11 {Hausner,I (2435)-Sikora Lerch,J (2415)/Frenstat 1982/MCD (1-0, 56)}) 15. gxf4 Bxa1 16. Qxa1 {Black has managed to somewhat simplify the position, but this does not solve the problem of his delay in development. Just look at the position! All his pieces (except for the king) find themselves on their initial squares, while White has brought all his pieces into play already (well, maybe with the exception of his rook). However, if Black manages to develop somehow, he would get a good position, mainly because of his more flexible pawn structure.} Nd7 17. Ne4! {It is important to prevent Black from placing his knight on f6.} ({My old analysis goes} 17. Nc4 Nf6 18. a5 Qe7 {with a reasonable position for Black. My main argument was that a massive pawn attack in the centre, which generally is a logical consequence of installing a knight on c4, is not without risk because it would weaken the f4-square:} 19. e4 Nh5! $132) 17... Qe7 18. Rc1 {Systematic improvement of piece placement.} b6 {A safe, but somewhat passive way to complete the development. The bishop will have nothing to do on b7.} (18... f5?! 19. Ng5 Nf6 20. Rc3 $36 {[%csl Ye6]}) 19. Rc3 Nf6 20. Re3 Nxe4 21. Bxe4 Qd8 22. Rg3 {White concentrates all his forces against the enemy kingside. However, the g6-square is solidly defended and Black gets some time to continue his development.} Qh4?! {The idea of this move was probably to force e3 and cut the rook off from the rest of the board.} ({I believe that Black should have hurried to neutralize White's pressure along the long dark diagonal with} 22... f5 23. Bd3 Ra7 {[%cal Ya7f7,Gd8f6] This manoeuvre might make the whole variation playable.}) 23. e3 Qe7 24. Bd3 {The bishop is well placed here. It inhibits ...b5 and prevents the natural development based on ...d7 and ...e8, by keeping the a6-pawn under observation.} Bb7 25. e4 Re8 26. f5 $14 {White has strong pressure now and it is not easy for Black to free himself.} Qh4 (26... Qe5 {would have led to similar positions as in the game.}) 27. h3 Bc8 28. Qc3 Qf4 29. Qf6 Qe5 30. Qxe5 Rxe5 ({Maybe} 30... dxe5 $14 {would have offered better chances to avoid being crushed by White's central pawns.}) 31. f4 Re8 32. f6! $16 {Black is very passive. He cannot do anything against White's systematic strengthening of his position.} Bd7 33. Re3 b5 34. axb5 axb5 35. e5 Kf8 36. Kf2 Kg8 37. Be2 Kf8 38. h4 Kg8 39. Ke1 Kf8 40. Kd2 h6 41. Bd3 h5 42. e6 fxe6 43. Bxg6 exd5 44. Bxe8 Bxe8 45. Re6 c4 46. Rxd6 cxb3 47. Kc3 Kf7 48. Kxb3 Kg6 49. Kb4 Kf5 50. Kc5 Bf7 51. Kxb5 Be6 52. Kc5 Kxf6 53. Kd4 Kf5 54. Ke3 Bf7 55. Kf3 Be6 56. Ra6 Bf7 57. Ra8 Kf6 58. Ra6+ Kf5 59. Ra1 Bg6 60. Rg1 Kf6 61. Ke3 Bf7 62. Rg5 Ke6 63. Kd4 Kf6 64. Kc5 Ke7 65. Rg7 Kf6 66. Rxf7+ 1-0

