1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 d5 3. c4 c6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. a4 Bf5 6. Ne5 Nbd7 7. Nxc4 Nb6 8. Ne5 a5 ({Exchanging a pair of knights is one of the main strategical ideas for Black in this system. However, trying to do so immediately is a mistake:} 8... Nfd7 9. e4 Nxe5 (9... Bg6 10. a5 (10. Nxg6 hxg6 11. a5 Nc8 12. Qb3 $16) 10... Nxe5 (10... Nc8 11. a6 $18) 11. axb6 Nd7 12. Rxa7 Nxb6 13. Rxb7 e6 14. Be3 $16) 10. exf5 Ned7 (10... Nec4 11. a5 $18) 11. a5 $16 Nc8 (11... Nd5 $142 12. Nxd5 cxd5 13. Qb3!? $16) 12. a6! b6 (12... bxa6 $142 13. Bxa6 $16 {/+-}) 13. d5! $18 {1-0 Fridman,D (2562)-Maze,S (2525)/Marseille 2006 (24)}) ({Also bad is} 8... Nbd7?! 9. Qb3 Nxe5 10. dxe5 Ng4 11. Qxb7 Nxe5 12. f4 (12. Bf4 Rb8 13. Qxa7 Rxb2 14. Rd1 Nd7 15. e4 e5 16. Bc1! Rc2 17. exf5 Rxc3 18. Qb7 $16) 12... Ng6 (12... Ng4 13. Qxc6+ Bd7 14. Qf3 $16) 13. e4 Bd7? (13... Qc8 $8 14. Qxc8+ (14. Ba6 Bd7 15. a5 e5! 16. f5 Qxb7 17. Bxb7 Rb8 18. a6 Nf4 $132 {[%cal Gf4d3,Gf4g2,Gf8c5]}) 14... Bxc8 15. Be3 e6 (15... e5 16. f5 $14) 16. O-O-O $14) 14. f5 Ne5 15. Bf4 f6 16. Bxe5 fxe5 17. Rd1 $18 {1-0 Kasparov,G (2805)-Timman,J (2635)/Riga 1995 (22)}) 9. g3 ({Alternatives are} 9. f3) ({and lately the fashionable} 9. Bg5) 9... Nfd7 (9... e6 10. Bg2 Bb4 11. O-O O-O 12. e3 h6 13. Qe2 Bh7 14. Rd1 Nfd7 $14 {/=}) 10. Nxd7 (10. Nd3 e5!? (10... Bxd3 11. Qxd3 e6 (11... e5!?) 12. Bg2 Be7 13. O-O O-O 14. Rd1 $14 {1-0 Cech,P (2404)-Holemar,D (2380)/Brno 2004 (30)}) (10... Nd5 11. Bg2 Nb4 12. e4 Bg4 13. f3 $16 {1-0 Blagojevic,D (2450)-Kirov,N (2435)/Cetinje 1996 (40)}) 11. dxe5 Nc5 12. e4 (12. Nxc5 Qxd1+ 13. Nxd1 Bxc5 $44) 12... Be6 13. Nxc5 Bxc5 14. Qxd8+ Rxd8 $44) (10. Nf3 e6 11. Bg2 Bb4 12. O-O O-O 13. e4 Bg4 $11 {½-½ Komarov,D (2566)-Berkes,F (2602)/France 2004 (22)}) 10... Qxd7 (10... Nxd7 11. Bg2 e6 12. O-O Bb4 13. e4 Bg6 14. f4!? h6 15. f5 $36 {1-0 Gyimesi,Z (2553)-Shaw,J (2465)/Cappelle la Grande 2004 (53)}) 11. e4 (11. h3 e6 (11... e5!?) 12. e4 Bg6 13. Bg2 Bb4 14. O-O O-O $11) 11... Bh3 ({Worse is} 11... Bg4 12. f3 Bh3 13. Bxh3 Qxh3 14. Qb3 Ra6 (14... Qg2 15. Rf1 Ra6 16. d5 e5 17. dxe6 $18) 15. Be3 Qg2? (15... e6 16. Kf2 $16) 16. O-O-O Qxf3 17. Rhe1 $18 {1-0 Nielsen,P (2636)-Timman,J (2616)/Malmo 2002 (23)}) 12. Bxh3 ({After} 12. Be2 {Black achieved excellent play in the game in which 11...h3 was played for the first time:} e6 13. Be3 Qc7 (13... Bb4 14. d5 Nc8 15. dxc6 Qxd1+ 16. Rxd1 bxc6 $11) 14. d5 exd5 15. Qd4 Nc4 16. exd5 (16. Bxc4 dxc4 17. Qxc4 O-O-O $17) 16... Nxe3 17. Qxe3+ Be7 {½-½ Bu Xiangzhi (2640)-Sokolov,I (2676)/Turin 2006 (48)}) 12... Qxh3 13. Qb3 Ra6 {This relatively new; the theoretical position is considered to offer Black reasonable chances. Aronian's next novelty is a good attempt to improve, but the position remains rather double-edged anyway.} 14. Bf4! $146 ({Previously} 14. Be3 {was played:} e6 15. d5 Bb4 16. dxe6 Qxe6 17. Qxe6+ fxe6 18. Bxb6 Rxb6 19. O-O-O Bc5 20. f4 Ke7 {0-1 Gyimesi,Z (2611)-Bu Xiangzhi (2644)/Bundesliga 2007 (82), with an edge for Black.}) 14... e6 15. Be5!? {Before playing c7 White would like to provoke some weaknesses in opponent's pawn formation.} (15. Bc7 {brings nothing in view of} Bb4) 15... Bb4 ({After} 15... f6 16. Bc7 Bb4 17. O-O-O O-O {strong is} 18. Ne2! {pointing out drawbacks of 15...f6.} (18. Kb1 Rf7) (18. Na2 c5 19. Nxb4 c4!? 20. Qa3 axb4 21. Qxb4 Nxa4 $132 {[%csl Rc1]}) (18. d5 cxd5 19. exd5 Nxd5 (19... Nd7!?) 20. Nxd5 exd5 21. Qxd5+ Qe6 $11 (21... Kh8 22. Kb1 $13))) 16. Bxg7 Rg8 17. Be5 Qg2 18. O-O-O Bxc3 19. Qxc3 (19. bxc3 Qxe4 20. c4 Qe2 $11) 19... Qxe4 20. Rhe1 Qd5 ({In case of} 20... Qf5 {White can consider} 21. d5!? {with initiative:} cxd5 (21... Nxd5 22. Rxd5 exd5 (22... cxd5 23. Qc8+ Ke7 24. Qxb7+ $18) 23. Qc5! {and White wins}) 22. Qc7 (22. Rxd5!? Nxd5 (22... exd5? 23. Bd6+ $18) 23. Qc8+ Ke7 24. Qxb7+ Kf8 25. Qxa6 Qxf2 26. Qd6+ Ke8 27. Rd1 $13) 22... Qg5+ 23. Kb1 Qe7 (23... Qd8 24. Qc2 $44 {[%csl Rh7][%cal Gf2f4,Gf4f5]}) 24. Qb8+ Qd8 25. Qxb7 Qc8 26. Qxc8+ Nxc8 27. Rc1 $14) 21. b3 Qd7 22. Qc2 Qe7 {Continuing the same courageous strategy started with 15...b4. Carlsen sacrifices the pawn on h7.} ({More to the point looks to me} 22... Rg6 23. h4 h5 {with a complicated position:} 24. Qe2 (24. Kb2 $14 {/}) 24... Qe7 25. Kb2 Qb4 {[%cal Gb6a4]} 26. Ra1 Rg4 $13) 23. Qxh7 Rg6 (23... Qa3+ 24. Kd2 Rg6 (24... Qa2+ 25. Qc2 $16) 25. Re3! Qb2+ $140 26. Ke1 $16) 24. Re3!? {It turns out that White can defend conveniently even with his queen on h7.} (24. Kb2 Qb4 $132) ({Aronian must have came to the conclusion that in spite of his extra pawn White's winning chances are not so high after} 24. Qh8+ Qf8 (24... Kd7 25. d5! cxd5 ({or} 25... Nxd5 26. Qb8 $40) 26. Qb8 $40) 25. Qxf8+ Kxf8) 24... Nd5 (24... Qa3+ 25. Kb1 $16) 25. Rf3 Qf8 26. h4 ({Interesting was the unexpected blow} 26. Bd6 {although it is not clear if this is better than the game:} Rg7 (26... Qxd6? 27. Qxf7+ Kd8 28. Qxg6 $18) (26... Rh6 27. Rxf7! Qxf7 28. Qxh6 Qf3 29. Qxe6+ Kd8 30. Qg6 Qxb3 31. Bc5 $16) 27. Qh6 (27. Bxf8 Rxh7 {[%csl Rf8,Rh2]}) 27... Qg8 {[%cal Gg7h7]} 28. h4 $14) 26... Rh6 (26... Rb6 27. Kb2 $14) 27. Qc2 Ra8 {So White won a pawn, but making progress is extremely difficult. Black has a powerful knight on d5 and always keeps counterplay possibilities against White's king. In my opinion the position is objectively just slightly better for White. In practice it might be even easier to play with Black, who must fight against White's ideas, whereas White still must search for those ideas.} 28. Qd2 Rh5 29. Kb2 Qg8 30. Re1 Qg6 31. Bd6 Rd8 32. Ba3 Qg4 {Both sides are maneuvering, trying to improve. Essentially there are no changes in the position.} 33. Rd3 Rf5 34. Re5 Kd7 35. Qe2 Rg8 {These moves must have been made when both opponents where short of time. It is not easy to figure out who will benefit from the exchange of queens.} 36. Qe1 {Aronian is trying to exploit his opponent's pawn on a5, but Carlsen succeeds in keeping counterplay without making the move ...b6.} ({Apparently more logical is to play the endgame after} 36. Qxg4 Rxg4 {but how to make progress still remains an open question. On the other hand here White at least won't have to worry about a possible attack against his king.} 37. Rxf5 exf5 $44 {[%cal Gf5f4]}) 36... Rg6 37. Re2 Rf3 38. Rxf3 Qxf3 39. Rc2 Qf6 40. Qxa5 Qxd4+ 41. Ka2 Rf6 42. f4 {[%csl Rg3] Unlike his opponent, Aronian doesn't have any problems advancing his pawns. But f2-f4 weakens the g3-pawn and this factor is immediately exploited by Carlsen.} ({Some chances for advantage were kept by} 42. Bc5 Qe4 43. Rd2 Rf3 44. Qa7 Kc7 45. Be3! $14 {[%cal Ga7d4] and bad is} Nxe3?? 46. Qa5+ b6 47. Qa7+ Kc8 48. Qa8+ Kc7 49. Qd8+ Kb7 50. Rd7+ Ka6 51. Qa8#) (42. Qa8 Nc3+ $11) 42... Rg6 $11 (42... Qd3 {also not bad, but the game continuation is convincing enough}) 43. Qa8 Nb4+ 44. Bxb4 Qxb4 45. Rg2 (45. Rc4 Qd2+ 46. Ka3 Kc7! 47. Qf8 Rxg3 48. Qxf7+ Qd7 49. Qxd7+ Kxd7 $11) 45... Rxg3 46. Rxg3 Qd2+ 47. Ka3 Qc1+ (47... Qc1+ 48. Kb4 Qxf4+ 49. Kc5 Qf2+ $11) 1/2-1/2

