1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. c4 Bb4+ 4. Nbd2 b6 5. a3 Bxd2+ 6. Qxd2 Bb7 7. e3 O-O 8. b4 d5?! {Quite illogical. Black opens up the position against White's bishop pair...} (8... d6 {is common.}) 9. Bb2 Nbd7 10. Bd3 dxc4 11. Bxc4 c5 12. O-O Rc8 13. Qe2 Qe7 (13... Rc7 14. dxc5 bxc5 15. b5 Qe7 16. Rfd1 e5 17. Nd2 e4 18. Nf1 (18. Nb3!?) 18... Ne5 19. Ng3 g6 20. Rd2 h5 21. h3 h4 22. Nf1 $14 {½-½ Huss,A-Karlsson,P/Graz 1972/MCD (42)}) 14. Rfc1 cxd4 15. Nxd4 h6 16. Ba6 {[%mdl 32] Trading one of the bishops but weakening light squares in Black's camp.} Bxa6 17. Qxa6 Rxc1+ 18. Rxc1 Nb8 19. Qc4 {[%csl Rb8][%cal Gc1c8]} Rd8 20. h3 Ne8 21. b5 Rd5 22. Qe2 Rc5 23. Rd1 Rc8 24. Qf3 Rd8 25. Rc1 Nd6 26. a4 e5?! {Black can't stand the pressure and tries to break through. As often happens, it leads to new weaknesses.} 27. Nf5 (27. Nc6 Nxc6 28. bxc6 Ne8 29. Qe4 f6 30. f4! $16 {is another attractive option.}) 27... Nxf5 28. Qxf5 f6 29. Qe4 {Black can't defend all his weaknesses, from the a7 pawn to his weak kingside. White needn't hurry, his only concern is to prevent Black's b8 knight entering the game.} Qf7 30. Ba3 Kh8 31. Kh2 Kg8 32. Bd6! Qd7 33. Bc7 Rf8 34. Rc2 (34. Qb7!?) (34. Qa8!?) 34... Re8 35. Rc4 (35. Qa8 $18) 35... Qf7 36. Bd6 Rd8?! (36... Qd7 $8 $16) 37. Rc7 Nd7 38. Qc6 Qe6 39. Rxa7 Kf7 40. Qxb6 {An instructive game!} 1-0

