1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be2 Bg7 7. O-O Nc6 8. Nb3 O-O 9. Kh1 a6 10. f4 b5 11. Bf3 Bb7 12. a4 {Black's pieces are developed so harmoniously, that White's chances to get the advantage must be minimal.} (12. Qe1 Rc8 13. Nd5 Nxd5 14. exd5 Nd4 15. Nxd4 Bxd4 16. c3 Bg7 17. a4 Qd7 18. Qd1 Rc4 19. axb5 axb5 20. Qb3 Rfc8 {0-1 Koenig,I-Napolitano,M/Munich 1936 (49)}) (12. Be3 Nd7 13. Nd4 Na5 14. b3 Rc8 15. Qd2 Nc6 16. Nce2 Qc7 17. Rac1 Nxd4 18. Bxd4 e5 19. Ba1 Nc5 20. f5 Bxe4 21. f6 Bh8 22. Nc3 Bxf3 23. Rxf3 {1-0 Sznapik,A (2440)-Sax,G (2550)/Baile Herculane 1982 (42)}) (12. e5 dxe5 13. Nc5 Qb6 14. Nxb7 Qxb7 15. fxe5 Nd7 16. e6 fxe6 17. Ne4 Rad8 18. Qe1 Nde5 19. Ng5 Qc8 20. Qh4 h6 21. Ne4 Nd4 $19 {0-1 Cherniaev,A (2410)-Serper,G (2560)/St Petersburg 1994 (27)}) (12. Nd5 e6 13. Nxf6+ Bxf6 14. c3 Qc7 15. Be3 Rad8 16. Qe2 Nb8 17. Qf2 Nd7 18. Rfd1 Be7 19. Bd4 e5 20. Be3 exf4 21. Bxf4 Ne5 $11 {1-0 Barua,D (2528)-Bakre,T (2443)/Calicut 2003 (61)}) 12... b4 13. Nd5 Nxd5 {Both other alternative moves make an equally good impression, the only problem was the burden of choice.} (13... e6 14. Ne3 Qc7 15. Bd2 a5 $11) (13... Nd7 14. a5 (14. f5 e6 15. Nf4 Qe7 $15) 14... Rb8 15. Be3 $13) 14. exd5 Na5 15. c3!? {Nisipeanu felt, that his position could become endangered and thus he chooses somewhat shocking solution.} (15. Qd3 Rc8 16. Nd4 Qb6 17. Rd1 Qc5 18. Nb3 Nxb3 19. Qxb3 a5 20. c3 Ba6 21. cxb4 Qxb4 22. Qxb4 axb4 23. Rb1 b3 $17 {0-1 Boulay,D-Charbonneau,P/Montreal 1997 (79)}) (15. Nxa5 Qxa5 16. Re1 Bf6 17. Qd3 Rfc8 $15) 15... bxc3 16. bxc3 Rc8 (16... Bxc3 17. Rb1 Rb8 18. Be3 Nc4 19. Bd4 $15) 17. Rb1 Ba8 18. Nd2?! {White was playing really slowly at this stage and the insecurity has kept going till the last move. A much better idea was to crown his risky concept with the logical move 18.f5!? with chances for full compensation.} (18. f5!? Bxc3 (18... gxf5 19. Nd4 Qd7 20. Qd3 e6 21. Qxa6 $14) 19. Bh6 Bg7 20. Bxg7 Kxg7 21. Qd4+ Kg8 22. fxg6 hxg6 23. Nd2 $44) 18... Nc4 (18... Bxc3 19. Qe2 Qd7 20. Qxa6 Qxa4 21. Ne4 Qc2 $15) 19. Nxc4 Rxc4 20. Bd2 (20. Qd3 Qc7 21. Bd2 Rxa4 $15) 20... Qa5 21. Qe1 (21. Qe2 Bxd5 22. Qxe7 Bxf3 23. Rxf3 Qd5 24. Qe2 Rxa4 $17) 21... Bxd5 22. Be2 Qxa4! {[%mdl 1024] Young Carlsen is well known for his positive attitude towards exchange sacrifices. At this moment the trade is nearly forced, but the consequences will be very pleasant for Black.} (22... Rxa4 23. c4 Bxg2+ 24. Kxg2 $13) 23. Bxc4 Bxc4 24. Rf2 (24. Rg1 Qc2 $17) 24... e6 25. Be3 (25. Rb4 Qc6 26. Be3 Qd5 $17) 25... Bd5 26. Rfb2 Qe4 27. Qd2 h5 {The optimal spread of the black forces is clearly favourable for the activity with two bishops.} (27... a5 $17) 28. Bd4 (28. Rb8? Bxc3! $19) 28... Bh6 29. Be3 $138 (29. Rb8 Bxf4 30. Rxf8+ Kxf8 31. Rb8+ Ke7 $19) 29... a5! 30. Rb8?! {Time trouble is seldom a relief in difficult positions. Nisipeanu would love to exchange the pieces in order to ease the pressure from his opponent.} (30. Ra1 Ra8 31. Kg1 e5 $17) 30... Kh7 31. h3 (31. Rxf8? Qxb1+ $19) 31... Rxb8 32. Rxb8 Bg7 33. Bd4 Bxd4 34. cxd4 a4! {The true consequence of exchanges is the situation, in which White has no way of preventing the black pawn from reaching the a2-square.} 35. Kh2 a3 36. Rb4 a2 37. Ra4 Qb1 {White is just able to stop the pawn from promoting, but not the disaster on the kingside.} (37... Qb1 38. Qc3 Qf1 $19) 0-1

