1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Nd5 Be7 10. Nxe7 Nxe7 11. Bd3 {Played by Anand once long ago (well, the Indian GM tried virtually everything against Sveshnikov Sicilian), this variation gained popularity lately among several young and strong GMs. I can mention Naiditsch, Timofeev and Areshchenko.} Nd7 {Carlsen has his own view on the position. Usually Black starts with 11...b7. Another option is 11...d5.} 12. c4 $146 {Morozevich goes a new way already. In this variation White usually first blockades opponent's pawn on b5 (with b2-b4) and only then plays c2-c4.} ({Although Carlsen's previous experience had a happy end, his position in that game was highly suspicious, to say the least:} 12. O-O Nc5 13. b4 Nxd3 14. Qxd3 f6 15. Be3 d5 16. Bc5 dxe4 (16... d4 17. f4 O-O 18. fxe5 fxe5 19. Rxf8+ Qxf8 20. Qg3 $16) (16... Be6 17. f4!? exf4 18. Rae1 d4 19. e5 $36) 17. Qxe4 Qd5 18. Qe2! (18. Qxd5 Nxd5 19. c4 $14) 18... Bb7 (18... Ng6 19. f4! $16) 19. f3 Ng6 20. Rfd1 Nf4 21. Qf1 Qe6 22. c4 Kf7 (22... bxc4? 23. Nxc4 $18) 23. Rd6 Qf5 24. cxb5 axb5 25. Nxb5 Kg6 (25... Ba6 26. Qc4+ Kg6 27. a4 $16) 26. Rd2 Rhd8 27. Rxd8 Rxd8 28. Nd6 Rxd6 (28... Qd7 29. a4 $18) 29. Bxd6 Qg5 30. Kh1 h5 31. Bc5? (31. a4 h4 32. h3 Qg3 33. Ra3 $18) 31... e4 32. Be3?? ({After} 32. a4 {[%cal Ga1a3] with the idea a3. White still should be winning.}) 32... exf3 33. gxf3 Qg4! {0-1 Naiditsch,A (2657)-Carlsen,M (2625)/Wijk aan Zee 2006}) (12. b4!? h6 (12... O-O 13. O-O Nb6 14. c4 f6 15. Be3 Nxc4 16. Nxc4 bxc4 17. Bxc4+ Kh8 18. f3 $16 {1-0 Varga,Z (2540)-Pal,P (2285)/Zalakaros 1996 (32)}) 13. Be3 d5 14. exd5 Nxd5 15. Qf3 N7f6 (15... Bb7 $142 $13) 16. Bc5 Bg4 17. Qg3 Nf4 18. h3 Bh5 19. Nxb5! Nxd3+ 20. cxd3 axb5 21. Qxe5+ Kd7 22. Qd6+ Kc8 23. Qc6+ Qc7 24. Qxa8+ Kd7 25. Qa7 $18 {½-½ Gurevich,I (2495)-Vaisser,A (2565)/London 1991 (58)}) 12... b4 13. Nc2 a5 {A similar pawn structure arises in the variation 10.xf6 xf6 11.c4 b4 12.c2 a5, which was actually played by Morozevich before as well (versus Leko in San Luis 2005). The difference is that here White has the dark-squared bishop instead of the knight on d5 and Black has a knight instead of the dark-squared bishop. This is not a problem for Black, since in the mentioned variation he is often happy to exchange his bishop for one of the opponent's knights. On the other hand White has the bishop pair here and Morozevich shows that this is an important factor. Black should play accurately in order to keep the equality.} 14. Ne3 f6 15. Bh4 O-O 16. O-O Nc5 17. f3 Be6 18. Re1 Qb8 {The pawn formation is of such a nature that it is difficult for either side to make real progress without creating severe weaknesses in their own positions.} 19. Bf1 Kh8 20. Re2 Rc8 21. Rd2 Ra6 {The only weakness on d6 is well protected. Morozevich finds a way to put some more pressure on Black's position, though.} 22. Rc1 Rac6 23. Bf2 Bf7 24. Nd5! Nxd5 (24... Qb7 {allows White to activate his pieces:} 25. Bxc5!? dxc5 (25... Rxc5 26. Nxe7 Qxe7 27. Rxd6 $16) 26. Nxe7 Qxe7 27. Rd7 {and although after} Qe8 {this might be not so dangerous for Black, it looks pretty scary to play like that.}) 25. cxd5 ({After} 25. exd5?! R6c7 {Black has blockaded opponent's queenside pawn majority and later he can concentrate on making progress on the other side. It is White here who must hold the balance.}) 25... R6c7 26. Rdc2 $14 Be8 27. b3 Bd7 28. g4!? {With the open c-file White's actions on the kingside can hardly create serious problems for Black now, but nevertheless this is the only attempt to make some progress. Later White can choose the right moment to combine plans on both wings.} Kg8 29. Qe2 {Now Black will have to reckon always with the exchange on c5 followed by a6.} ({Deserved attention also} 29. Bc4 {trying to avoid exchanges on the c-file for the moment.}) 29... Nb7 {This is a somewhat unexpected decision, allowing White to penetrate with the queen. However, Carlsen shows a very concrete approach - the Norwegian considers that Black will have everything well protected and White won't be ableto achieve anything even after the activation of his queen.} (29... Qb7 $142 $14 {[%csl Ra6] /=}) 30. Rxc7 Rxc7 31. Rxc7 Qxc7 32. Qa6 Nc5 33. Qa8+ Kf7 34. h4 h6 35. Bc4 ({After} 35. Qh8 a4 36. bxa4 Nxa4 37. g5 hxg5 38. hxg5 fxg5 39. Qh5+ Kf8 40. Qxg5 (40. Qh8+ Kf7 41. Qh5+ $11) 40... Nc3 {Black creates counterplay on the queenside.}) 35... Be8 {Locking the queen on a8. White cannot release it easily, because the exchange on c5 obviously favours Black. However, Morozevich still has resources to fight.} 36. Be3 Ke7 37. g5 hxg5 38. hxg5 Kf7 39. Kg2 {This position will soon occur in the game again.} Ke7 40. Kg3 {The march of the king forwards brings nothing.} Kf7 41. Kg4 Ke7 42. Kh4 Kf7 43. Kh3 Ke7 44. Kg2 Kf7 {Second time, only that now it is White to move.} 45. Bd2 ({White had to try} 45. g6+ Ke7 $8 46. Bb5 (46. Bf1 Bxg6 47. Qg8 Bf7 48. Qxg7 Qc8 49. Bc4 Nd7 $11 {[%cal Gc8g8]} (49... Qg8? 50. Qxg8 Bxg8 51. Bxc5 dxc5 52. d6+ $18)) 46... Bxg6 (46... Bxb5 47. Qg8 Be8 48. Qxg7+ Kd8 49. Qh8 $18 {[%cal Gg6g7]}) 47. Qg8 Bf7 48. Qxg7 Qc8 (48... Qb8? 49. Bxc5! dxc5 (49... Qxb5 50. Bxd6+ Kxd6 51. Qxf6+ $18) 50. Bc4 Qd6 51. Kf2 {and Black is close to zugzwang:} Ke8 52. Bb5+ Ke7 53. Ke1 Qd8 54. d6+ Qxd6 55. Bc4 $18) 49. Kf2 Qg8 50. Qh6 {and Black still must work before reaching the draw. Possibly Morozevich wanted to postpone his decision to complicate the position, eventually to bring the king on f2 first. But an unexpected thing happened...}) 45... Qb6 46. Be3 Qc7 {and Carlsen claimed the threefold repetition, wrongly accepted by the chief arbiter Geurt Gijssen. Morozevich signed the scoresheets and left the playing area. Soon it was discovered that the arbiter shouldn't have accepted Carlsen's claim, since in the first case (after 39.g2) it was Black's turn to move. Carlsen agreed to continue the game. However, Morozevich wasn't there anymore and later didn't want to resume the game... It is clear that the normal course of the battle was broken, even if the result would have been a draw anyway.} 1/2-1/2

