1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 d5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 dxc4 ({Another variation that becomes more and more popular lately is} 6... c5) ({Adams usually prefered the main} 6... Ne4) 7. Qxc4 b6 {Nigel Short's idea. Black tries to use the exposed position of the white queen.} 8. Bf4 {The most principled answer.} (8. Nf3) 8... Ba6 ({Earlier this year Carlsen achieved advantage in a blitz game against Onischuk after} 8... Nd5 9. Bg3 Ba6 10. Qa4+ Qd7 11. Qc2 Qb5 12. Nf3 Nc6 13. Rc1 Nde7 14. b4 Qh5 15. Qa4 Bb5 16. Qb3 Qd5 17. Qxd5 exd5 18. e3 Bxf1 19. Kxf1 Rc8 20. Ke2 {½-½ Carlsen,M (2710)-Onischuk,A (2650)/Biel (blitz) 2007 (54)}) 9. Qxc7 Qxc7 10. Bxc7 {Black has compensation for the pawn thanks to the lead in development. However, the present game reminds that the development advantage is a temporary factor and can easily disappear.} O-O {With this novelty Black keeps the possibility to develop the knight either on d7 or c6. However, Adams failed to show the advantages of the knight on d7.} ({Short played} 10... Nc6 11. Nf3 Rc8 12. Be5 ({The critical variation seems to be} 12. Bf4 Na5 13. b4) 12... Nxe5 13. dxe5 Nd7 14. b4 Bb7 15. e3 Bxf3 16. gxf3 Nxe5 17. Bb5+ Ke7 18. f4 Nd7 {and Black soon achieved a draw in Baburin,A (2590)-Short,N (2670)/Port Erin 1998.}) 11. Nf3 Rc8 12. Bf4 Nbd7 ({Another way to look for compensation is} 12... Nc6 13. e3 Bxf1 14. Rxf1 Na5) 13. Nd2 {The begin of an impressive maneuver.} Rc2 ({Deserves attention to chase away the bishop from the diagonal c1-h6:} 13... Nd5 14. Bg3 Rc2 15. Rb1 Rac8 16. Kd1 f5 {although after} 17. e3 Bxf1 18. Rxf1 {White will most likely find a way to coordinate his pieces and keep the extra pawn (appart from e2-d3, followed by b3 and fc1, White also has e1-e2, or in some cases even b3 and c1, using the pin on the c-file).}) 14. Rb1 Rac8 {Adams achieved a visible activity, but Carlsen finds a strong defensive plan: the knight goes to a1 in order to control square c2.} 15. Nb3! Bc4 (15... Nd5 16. Bd2 N7f6 17. f3 $16 {[%cal Gb3a1]}) 16. Na1! Ba2 17. Nxc2 Bxb1 18. Na1 Nd5 19. Bd2 {A rare case when White can be proud having a knight in the corner. Indeed, it controls the vital square c2 and cannot be disturbed there. The bishop controls square c1, so in fact the yound Norvegian is just a healthy pawn up. White's position contains no weaknesses and he will soon complete development.} e5 20. e3! ({Stronger than} 20. dxe5 Nxe5 21. e3 Bd3 (21... Nd3+ 22. Bxd3 Bxd3 23. f3 $16)) 20... exd4 21. exd4 Nb8 (21... Re8+ 22. Kd1 $16) (21... Nf8 22. Ba6 Re8+ 23. Kd1 Ne6 24. Re1 Ndc7 $14 {/}) 22. f3 Nc6 23. Bc4 Rd8 24. Kf2 Bf5 25. Nb3 {White has everything protected and the game enters in the phase of converting the extra pawn. This is not a trivial task, though, since the isolated d-pawn is blockaded and it is difficult for White to find targets for attack.} Be6 26. Rc1 f6 27. a4! {Now Black faces an unpleasant choice. Allowing the further advance of the a-pawn will open files for white pieces. Adams chooses to blockade it, but then the b-pawn remains weak.} a5 28. Bc3 Bf7 29. Nd2 Nde7 30. Bf1!? {Carlsen decides to give up the d4-pawn in order to open the position, where his bishop pair together with the weakness of pawn b6 will secure the advantage.} ({White could have kept the extra pawn with} 30. Rd1 $16 Nxd4 $140 ?! 31. Bxf7+ Kxf7 32. Nc4 Nec6 33. Nxb6 $18) 30... Nxd4 31. Re1 Ndc6 32. Nc4 Nd5 33. Rb1 Kf8 (33... Nxc3 34. bxc3 $16 {[%csl Rb6]}) 34. Be1 Ke7 (34... Ncb4 35. Rd1 (35. Kg1!? Re8 36. Bf2 Re6 37. Rd1 $14) 35... Nc2 36. Bd2 $14 (36. Nxb6?! Nxe1 37. Nxd5 Nxg2 $11)) 35. Kg1! {Again Carlsen finds a powerful way to improve the position.} Nb8 36. Bf2 Nd7 37. Re1+ Kf8 38. Rd1 Ke7 39. Re1+ Kf8 40. Nd6 Ne5 (40... Nc5 41. Nxf7 Kxf7 42. Bxc5 ({or} 42. Bb5 $14 {/}) 42... bxc5 43. Bc4 Rd7 44. Rd1 Ke6 45. f4 g5 46. g3 $16) 41. Nxf7 Kxf7 $16 42. Rd1 {The bishops are much stronger than the knights and in spite of material eqiality White has a clear advantage.} Ke7 43. f4 (43. Ba6 $16) 43... Ng4 (43... Nxf4 44. Rxd8 Kxd8 45. Bxb6+ $18) 44. Re1+ Kf8 45. Bd4 Rd6 ({Again taking on f4 loses:} 45... Nxf4 46. Bxb6 Rd5 47. b4! axb4 48. a5 {and the knights can't fight against the marginal passed pawn.}) 46. h3 Nh6 ({Here} 46... Nxf4 {was worth a try, at the same time containing a trap.} 47. Re4 ({After} 47. Rd1? {(threatening 48.c5) Black has a strong answer} Ke7! {which is also the only defense} (47... Ne6 48. Bc5 $18) (47... Ke8 48. hxg4 Ne6 49. Re1 $18) 48. hxg4 Ne6 {and it is White who must look for escape}) 47... Ne6 48. Rxe6 Rxe6 49. hxg4 $16 {White is clearly better, but winning it might be a difficult task.}) 47. Rd1! Nf5 (47... Nxf4? 48. Bc5 $18) 48. Bf2 Ke7 (48... Nxf4 49. Rxd6 Nxd6 50. Bxb6 $16) 49. g4 Nh6 50. f5 Nf7 51. Bg2 Nf4 52. Rxd6 Nxd6 53. Bxb6 {In spite of Adams' efforts the inevitable happened. Now the extra pawn secures a technical win.} Nc4 54. Bc5+ Kd7 55. Bf1 ({Even stronger is} 55. b3 Nxg2 (55... Nd2 56. Be3 Nxg2 57. Bxd2 $18) 56. Kxg2 Nd2 57. b4 $18) 55... Nxb2 56. Bb5+ Kd8 ({More stubborn is} 56... Kc7 {although after} 57. Bf8 ({or} 57. h4 {[%cal Gc5f8]}) 57... g6 58. fxg6 hxg6 59. h4 {Black's position is difficult anyway}) 57. Bb6+ Ke7 58. Kh2 $18 Nd5 59. Bxa5 Kd6 60. Bd2 Kc5 61. Kg3 Nc7 62. Be3+ Kb4 63. Bd2+ Kc5 64. Bc1 Nc4 65. Bxc4 Kxc4 66. Bd2 Na6 67. a5 Kb5 68. Kf3 Nc5 69. Bc3 h6 70. Ke3 Kc4 71. Bd4 Na6 72. Ke4 Nb4 73. h4 Kb5 74. Bc3 Na6 75. Kd5 Nc5 76. Bd4 Nd3 77. Ke6 1-0

