1. c4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. Bg5 h6 6. Bxf6 Qxf6 7. e3 Nd7 8. Qc2 Qd8 {After this move White keeps a small advantage.} (8... g6 {is more common.}) 9. Bd3 Be7 10. O-O O-O {This position was examined in CBM 119 (annotations to Gelfand - Kasimdzhanov) and in CBM 121 (annotations to Delchev - Kosic by GM L.Ftacnik).} 11. Rad1 dxc4 (11... a6!? {is probably more flexible, e.g.} 12. cxd5 (12. e4 dxc4 13. Bxc4 b5 14. Be2 Qb6 15. a3 c5 16. d5 c4 $132 {0-1 Plachetka,J-Sveshnikov,E/Dubna 1979/EXT 1999 (29)}) 12... exd5 13. Rfe1 Nf6 14. e4 dxe4 15. Bxe4 Nxe4 16. Qxe4 Bd6 17. d5 c5 18. Qd3 Bg4 19. h3 Bh5 20. Re3 b5 21. Rde1 Bf4 22. R3e2 $13 {1-0 Furman,S-Rizzi,D/IECG 2003/Telechess CBM 105 (75)}) 12. Bxc4 b5 13. Be2 $146 (13. Bd3 {is also possible:} Bb7 14. Ne4 (14. a3!? a5 15. a4 b4 16. Ne4 Qb6 17. Rc1 Rac8?! 18. Nc5 $14 {1-0 Gonzalez Zamora,J-Mendoza Moreno,J/Merida 2005/EXT 2007 (32)}) 14... Qb6 15. Rc1 Rac8 (15... Rfd8 $142) 16. Nc5 Nxc5 17. dxc5 Qc7 18. Nd4 Bf6 19. Be4 Rcd8 20. Rfd1 g6 21. b4 Bg7 22. Nb3 f5 23. Bf3 e5 24. e4 Bc8 25. Na5 f4 26. Qb3+ Kh7 27. h3 $14 {1-0 Petrosian,A-Farago,I/Yerevan 1982/MCD (52)}) 13... Bb7 14. Ne4 Qb6 15. Rc1 {White seizes the c5 square preventing ...c6-c5 but that now leads to simplifications.} Rfd8 {This is definitely more useful than} (15... Rac8 {as the d-file will open up in a moment.}) 16. Rfd1 (16. Ne1!? {[%cal Ye1d3]}) 16... Rac8 17. Nc5 Nxc5 18. dxc5 Rxd1+ 19. Rxd1! {[%mdl 512] An interesting pawn sacrifice, which Black should have probably rejected.} Qxc5 (19... Qc7!? {[%cal Yc8d8] with good chances to equalize.}) 20. Qxc5 Bxc5 {[%mdl 4096]} 21. Rd7 Ba8 22. Ne5 Bb6 (22... f6 23. Ng6 {[%cal Yg6f4,Yf4e6]} (23. Nd3 Bb6 24. Nf4 Rd8! 25. Re7 Kf8 26. Ng6+ Kg8) 23... e5 24. Ne7+ Bxe7 25. Rxe7 a5 26. Kf1 $44) 23. Nxf7 Rc7 24. Ne5 (24. Nxh6+!? gxh6 25. Rd8+ Kg7 26. Rxa8 Rd7 27. Bg4 Rd2 28. b3 Rxa2 29. Kf1 $14 {White has a stronger pawn chain and therefore his bishop is more active.}) 24... Rxd7 25. Nxd7 Bc7 26. Nc5 {White's advantage (Black's "bad" a8 bishop) should not be enough to win the game.} Kf7 27. Bg4 e5 28. Bf3 Ke7 29. Kf1 Bb6 30. b4 a5 31. a3 Bxc5 32. bxc5 Bb7 33. Ke2 Ba6 {Passive defence is probably good enough, too.} 34. Kd2 Kd7 35. Be4 g5 36. Kc3 Bc8 37. g3 Kc7 38. f4 g4?? {Putting this pawn on a light square is a terrible positional mistake.} ({White can't win after} 38... exf4 39. exf4 gxf4 40. gxf4 Bd7 41. f5 Kd8 42. f6 (42. Kd4 Ke7 43. Ke5 Be8) 42... Ke8 43. Bg6+ Kf8 44. Kd4 {and now the simplest} a4) 39. f5 Bd7 40. f6 Kd8 41. Kd3 a4 (41... Ke8 42. Bg6+ Kf8 43. Ke4 Be8 44. Bf5 b4 45. axb4 axb4 46. Kxe5 b3 47. Kd4 $18) 42. Bg6 Be6 43. Kc3? (43. Ke4 {was quite possible:} Kd7 (43... b4 44. Kxe5 Bb3 45. Kd6 $18) 44. Kxe5 Bb3 45. Bf5+ Ke8 46. Kd6 b4 47. axb4 a3 48. Bb1 $18) 43... Kd7? {It was unclear how White was going to win after} (43... Bd5 {e.g.} 44. Kb4 Bf3 45. Ka5 Kd7 46. Bf7 (46. Kb6 Bd5) (46. Bf5+ Ke8 47. Be6 Bd1 48. Kb6?! Bb3) 46... Be4 47. Kb6 Bg2) 44. Be4 $22 {Black now loses his ill-fated g4 pawn due to the zugzwang.} Bf7 (44... Kc7 45. Bf5!) (44... h5 45. Bg6) 45. Bf5+ Ke8 (45... Be6?? 46. f7) 46. Bxg4 Bd5 47. Bh5+ Kd7 48. Bg6 e4 49. g4 Ke6 50. f7 Ke7 51. h4 Kf8 (51... Bxf7 52. Bxe4) 52. Kd4 Ke7 53. h5! {[%cal Rg4g5]} Bxf7 54. Bxe4 Be8 55. Bf5 Kf6 56. e4 Kg5 57. Bc8 Kf4 58. e5 {[%cal Re5e6,Rc8d7]} b4 59. axb4 a3 60. Kc3 Kxe5 61. Kb3 1-0

