1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Qc2 Bd6 7. b3 O-O 8. Be2 b6 9. Bb2 Bb7 10. O-O Qe7 11. Rad1 Rad8 {This is the most natural way to develop the queen's rook. One round earlier, Topalov preferred to place it on c8.} 12. Rfe1 Rfe8 13. e4 Nxe4 14. Nxe4 dxe4 15. Qxe4 Bb4 16. Rf1 Bd6 ({This position usually arises through a different move order, with 12.e4 instead of 12.fe1. In this concrete position, the last move clearly shows that Leko does not have the least intention of depose the surprising leader of the tournament. The more ambitious} 16... f5 {is not without drawbacks. After} 17. Qc2 Bd6 18. c5 bxc5 19. dxc5 Bxc5 20. Bc4 {White obtained excellent compensation for the sacrificed pawn, in view of the weakness of the dark squares from Black's camp, Rustemov-Gurevich, Copenhagen 2001.}) 17. Rfe1 {A brave decision dictated by the impetuosity of youth. Previously, 17.d3 and 17.e5 have been tried.} Bb4 18. Rf1 Bd6 19. Rfe1 Bb4 {Excellent fighting spirit! Black could have claimed a draw by repetition already, but he prefers to continue this fierce duel of nerves for a while.} 20. Rf1 Bd6 1/2-1/2

