1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 c6 3. d4 d5 4. e3 Bf5 5. Nc3 a6 {Magnus likes to play this.} 6. Ne5 ({A few days later in the blindfold game versus Carlsen, Ivanchuk achieved an advantage by less committal means:} 6. Bd2 e6 7. Nh4 Be4 8. f3 Bg6 9. Qb3 Ra7 10. O-O-O Be7 11. Nxg6 hxg6 12. Kb1 O-O 13. g4 $14 {½-½ Ivanchuk,V (2750)-Carlsen,M (2690)/Blindfold Monte Carlo MNC 2007 (58)}) (6. Qb3 $14) 6... Nbd7 {This developing move is better than 6...h6, which Carlsen played in the Corus tournament.} (6... h6 7. Qb3 Ra7 8. cxd5 cxd5 9. Bd2 e6 10. Rc1 Nbd7 11. Bb5 Bd6 12. Nxd7 Nxd7 13. Bxd7+ Qxd7? ({Black has good chances to defend after the surprising} 13... Kxd7!) 14. Na4 O-O 15. Nb6 Qd8 16. Nc8 Qxc8 17. Rxc8 Rxc8 18. Qb6 Bb8 19. O-O $18 {1-0 Ponomariov,R (2723)-Carlsen,M (2690)/Wijk aan Zee 2007 (30)}) 7. g4 $146 {This new attempt to refute Black's set-up will soon backfire. However, it led to a very exciting game.} Nxe5 8. dxe5 ({Considering what happened in the game, preferable is} 8. gxf5 Nxc4 9. e4 {with more chances to prove compensation}) 8... Nxg4 9. cxd5 Nxe5 10. f4?! {Uncharacteristically for Leko (but rather typically for the spirit of the Amber tournament) White forgets completely about strategical principles. Now his king becomes too exposed.} ({It was still possible to fight for compensation with the help of normal means:} 10. e4 Bg4 11. Be2 Bxe2 12. Qxe2 {and although Black is better, White retains enough resources to put up a fight.}) 10... e6! {With this piece sacrifice Magnus cleverly underlines the drawbacks of White's concept} ({Possibly the Hungarian GM came to the conclusion that in the event of the natural continuation} 10... Bg4 11. Be2 Bxe2 12. Qxe2 Nd7 {White has compensation, although is seems that Black should be able to complete development without problems}) (10... Ng6 11. e4 $44) 11. fxe5 Qh4+ 12. Kd2 O-O-O {There are many attractive possibilities for Black, but Carlsen's play is very energetic.} 13. Qe1 {This allows a nice refutation.} (13. Bd3 Bb4 14. Bxf5 (14. d6 Bxd3 15. Kxd3 Bxd6 16. Qe2 Bxe5+ 17. Kc2 {Three pawns and the attack for the knight secure Black the advantage.}) 14... Rxd5+ 15. Bd3 Rxd3+ (15... Rhd8 16. Kc2 Rxd3 17. Qxd3 Rxd3 18. Kxd3) 16. Kxd3 Rd8+ 17. Kc2 Rxd1) 13... Rxd5+! {Very strong and not an obvious idea.} 14. Nxd5 Qe4 {[%cal Ge4c2] A good "human" move.} ({However, the computer finds the fantastic} 14... Qd8!! {creating several threats, the main one being a check with the bishop on b4. Black is winning in all variations. For instance after} 15. Ke2 Bg4+ 16. Kf2 {the queen returns} Qh4+! {Here is the main difference to the game: after giving the check with the bishop on g4 from d8 the queen can access square h4 again.}) 15. Nb6+? {The knight had to remain on d5 to control the vital square b4.} ({The only move to defend was} 15. Ke2! {Then} cxd5 (15... Qg4+ 16. Kf2 Qh4+ 17. Ke2 $11) (15... Qxh1 16. Nb6+ Kc7 {transposes to the game}) 16. Qc3+ Kb8 17. Rg1 Bg4+ 18. Kf2 Qf3+ (18... Bb4? 19. Rxg4! Qxg4 20. Qd4) 19. Ke1 Be7 20. Rxg4 Qxg4 21. Qd4 Bh4+ 22. Kd2 {and Black keeps the initiative, but there is nothing forced}) 15... Kc7 16. Ke2 Qxh1? ({Missing the simple} 16... Qg4+ 17. Kf2 Qh4+ 18. Ke2 Bg4+ 19. Kd2 Bb4+ {and Black wins the queen}) 17. Qa5! {Suddenly White achieves some counterplay.} Qxh2+ 18. Kf3 Qh5+ 19. Kf2 Qh4+ (19... Kb8 20. Nd7+ Ka7?? 21. Qb6+ Ka8 22. Qd8+ Ka7 23. Qb8#) 20. Ke2? {The computer shows the difference again: closing the diagonal a6-f1 could be fatal for White.} (20. Kg1! Kb8 21. Nd7+ Ka8 (21... Ka7 22. e4! $132 {[%cal Gc1e3]}) 22. Bxa6 {and Black must repeat the position by giving checks}) 20... Qg4+ ({After the triangular manoeuvre with the king, Black escapes from White's checks:} 20... Kb8! 21. Nd7+ Ka8 22. Nb6+ Ka7 23. Nc8+ Kb8 {The material is about equal, but White's exposed king makes his position hopeless:} 24. Nd6 Be7 25. Nxf5 exf5 26. Bd2 Qg4+ $19) 21. Kf2 Qh4+ 22. Ke2 Qh2+ 23. Kf3 Bg4+ {Nice, but Black has no more pieces left with which to attack and must force perpetual anyway.} 24. Kxg4 Qg1+ 25. Kf3 Qxf1+ 26. Kg3 Qg1+ 27. Kf3 Qf1+ 28. Kg3 Qg1+ 1/2-1/2

