1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. c4 b6 4. g3 Bb4+ 5. Bd2 Bxd2+ 6. Qxd2 Ba6 7. b3 {This combination between ...a6 and ...b4+ is not very popular nowadays.} O-O ({Recently, there have been a couple of games with the rather ambitious continuation} 7... c5 8. Bg2 d5 9. Nc3 Nc6 10. cxd5 exd5 11. O-O O-O 12. dxc5 bxc5 {(This position can be reached via several move orders, of course). The placement of the knight on c6 is slightly unusual for positions with hanging pawns (...a6 or ...bd7 are more popular ways of developing this knight), but here it seems to make sense because of Black's general activity.} 13. Rfd1 (13. Rfe1 Re8 14. Nh4 Qa5 15. Rad1 Rad8 16. Nf5?! (16. Nxd5 Qxd2 17. Rxd2 Nxd5 18. Rxd5 Rxd5 19. Bxd5 Nb4 $132 {[%csl Ya2,Ye2]}) 16... Re5 $132 17. Nxd5? Qxd2 18. Rxd2 Nxd5 {0-1 Zubarev,A (2074)-Kengis,E (2589)/Polanica Zdroj 2000/EXT 2001}) (13. Nh4 Rc8 14. Rac1 Re8 15. Rfe1 d4 16. Na4 c4 17. bxc4 Ne5 18. Nb2 (18. c5 d3 $132 19. e3? Nc4 $17) 18... Nxc4 19. Nxc4 Rxc4 20. Rxc4 Bxc4 21. Nf5 d3 22. Ne3 Bb5 23. exd3 Qxd3 $11 {Gyimesi,Z (2614)-Pelletier,Y (2570)/Turin 2006/CBM 113 (½-½, 34)}) 13... d4 (13... Re8!?) 14. Na4 Re8 (14... Ne4!? 15. Qc2 Re8 16. Nxc5?! Nb4! {[%csl Gc5]}) 15. Nxc5 Bxe2 16. Nxd4! Nxd4 17. Bxa8 Bxd1 18. Rxd1 Re2 19. Qd3 Qxa8 20. Qxd4 $16 {Kempinski,R (2619)-Kengis,E (2528)/Hamburg 2005/CBM 108 ext (1-0, 40)}) 8. Nc3 d5 9. cxd5 exd5 10. Bg2 Re8 11. Ne5 c5 12. O-O Bb7 13. Rfd1 Na6 (13... Qe7 {CBM 36-5.b3 b4 6.d2 d2 7.d2/Opening 1993/CBM 036/[Dautov]}) 14. Qf4 Nc7?! $146 {Black must have been just too happy to threaten ...e6 with gain of tempo, probably overloking White's answer.} (14... Qc7 15. Nb5!? (15. Nc4 Rad8 16. Ne3 (16. Qxc7 Nxc7 17. dxc5 bxc5 18. Na5 Ba8 19. Na4 Rxe2 20. Nxc5 $14 {van der Wiel}) 16... Qxf4 17. gxf4 Nc7 $13 {Tukmakov,V (2590)-Van der Wiel,J (2540)/Rotterdam 1988/CBM 009 (½-½, 36)}) 15... Qe7 16. e3 (16. Ng4 Nxg4 17. Qxg4 Qxe2 18. Qxe2 Rxe2 19. Nd6 Bc6 20. Bf1 Re6 21. Nxf7! $14 {Van der Wiel. Maybe too optimistic?!}) 16... Rec8 17. Bh3 Rd8 18. Rac1 Nb4 19. a4?! (19. Nc3 $14) 19... a6 20. Nc3 {Rovchakov,V-Arzumanyan,S/RUS corr 1991/Corr Nr. 1 (0-1, 34)} a5!? $13) 15. Ng4! Nxg4 16. Qxg4 {White has obtained strong pressure against Black's hanging pawns. The exchange of two pairs of minor pieces favours him, by restricting Black's possibilities of counterplay in the centre.} Qf6 17. e3 Rad8 18. Rac1 Qe7 19. dxc5 bxc5 20. Qa4 Rd7!? ({An indirect way of defending the a7-pawn. However, placing the rook on d7 has a major drawback, as shown by Leko's next move.} 20... a6 21. Qa5 {, threatening Na4 would not have been much fun either.}) 21. Ne4! ({Black would have obtained certain counterplay after} 21. Qxa7 Ne6 {, with the threat of d4.}) 21... d4 ({Black could not defend his centre anymore.} 21... Ne6 {loses the c5-pawn to} 22. Nxc5! Nxc5 23. Rxc5) ({while} 21... c4 {drops an exchange to} 22. Nc5!) 22. exd4 Bxe4 23. Bxe4 {Since the d7-roo is hanging, White just won a pawn.} Ne6 24. d5!? {An ambitious move.} ({Leko might have feared that after} 24. dxc5 Rxd1+ 25. Rxd1 Nxc5 26. Qa3! $16 {his winning chances would not be 100% sure. In case of 26...xe4 27.xc5. Of course,} Nxe4?? {is impossible because of} 27. Qxe7 Rxe7 28. Rd8+) 24... Nd4 {Black's counterplay should not be underestimated. The knight is very well placed, while the white queen is somewhat isolated from the rest of the army (notably, the king).} 25. Re1 Qd8 26. Kf1 Rde7 27. Bd3 {White has managed to avoid the knight forks on e2 and f3 and get out of the pin. However, Black has not exhausted his resources.} Qc8 28. g4!! ({This paradoxical move is the only way to prevent the deadly check on h3. Black cannot capture the pawn because of xe8!+ followed by mate; a curious way of coordinating White's queen with the other pieces. There was no time for the intermediate} 28. Rxe7 {because of} Qh3+ {when the king's return to g1 would be met by ...Nf3+ followed by mate, forcing him to go to the centre} 29. Ke1 Rxe7+ {with a powerful attack for Black.}) 28... Rxe1+ 29. Rxe1 Rf8 30. h3 {White has consolidates his position, maintaining his extra-pawn. However, the technical part is quite difficult, especially beacuse of the weaknesses induced by the move g4.} Qd8 31. Be4 Qb6 {Black should not have allowed the infiltration of the enemy queen to d7.} 32. Bg2 g6 33. Kg1 Kg7 34. Qd7 Rd8 35. Qe7 a5 36. Kh1 Nb5 37. Re6 Nd6 38. Qf6+ Kg8 39. Re7 {White has an extra-pawn and a strong attack. The game is practically decided, despite the mutual time trouble.} a4?! 40. bxa4?! ({Just before the time control, Leko misses an immediate win with} 40. Rxf7!) 40... c4 41. Kh2 Qb8 42. f4 Qc8 43. Re3 Re8 44. Rxe8+ Nxe8 45. Qc6 Qxc6 46. dxc6 {Black has managed to exchange the intruders, but the ending is just as hopeless for him as the middlegame.} Kf8 47. Kg3 Ke7 48. Kf2 Kd6 49. Ke3 Kc5 50. g5 Nc7 51. a3 Ne6 52. Be4 c3 53. f5 1-0

