1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. g3 c5 5. Nf3 cxd4 6. Nxd4 O-O 7. Bg2 d5 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. Qb3 Qa5 10. Bd2 Nc6 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. O-O Bxc3 13. bxc3 Ba6 14. Rfd1 Qc5 15. e4 Bc4 {This line was examined in CBM 106 by GM V.Gavrikov (annots to Bacrot-Grischuk).} 16. Qc2 $146 (16. Qa4 {is usually played. Here is the latest example:} Nb6 17. Qb4 Qh5 18. Bf4 e5! $146 19. Be3 Be2 20. Re1 Nc4 21. Qc5 Rfd8 22. Rab1 h6 23. h3 Nxe3 24. Qxe3 Bc4 25. a4 a5 26. Qc5 Be6 $11 {0-1 Najer,E-Jakovenko,D/Moscow RUS 2006/The Week in Chess 631 (50)}) 16... Nb6 17. Bf4 {White provokes ...e6-e5 to clarify the situation and open the h3-c8 diagonal for his bishop. However,} (17. Be3!? {was probably better. If} Qa5 (17... Qa3 18. Bf4!?) {then} 18. Qb2 {[%cal Yb2b4]}) 17... e5 18. Be3 Qa3 19. Bh3 Rab8 20. Qc1 Qxc1 21. Rdxc1 Bd3 22. f3 Rb7 23. Rd1 Bc2 24. Rdc1 Bd3 25. Rd1 Bc2 26. Re1? {White has absolutely no grounds to play for a win.} (26. Rdc1 $11) 26... Nc4 27. Bc5 Rd8 $15 {[%csl Gc4][%cal Gd8d2,Gb7b2]} 28. Bf1 Nd2 29. Bg2 a5 30. Re2 Bb1 31. Re1 Bc2 32. Re2 Bb1 33. Re1 h6 34. Ba3 {it is not easy to find a move for White!} (34. Be3 Rb2 $15) 34... Rd3 {Black has created a strong pressure but it is still difficult to break down White's position.} 35. Rc1 Rd8 (35... Rxf3!? 36. Bxf3 Nxf3+ 37. Kf2 Bxe4 $15 {Kasparov}) 36. Re1 Bc2 37. Bc1 (37. Re2 Nb1! $17) 37... Nb1 38. c4 Rd4 (38... Bd3! 39. Rd1 (39. c5 Nc3 $17) 39... Nc3 40. Rd2 Ne2+ 41. Kf2 Nxc1 42. Rxc1 Rd4 $17 {Jakovenko}) 39. c5 a4 40. Bf1 a3 41. Re3 Ra7?! {Too passive. After this move White gets a drawish endgame with opposite-coloured bishops.} (41... Rd1!? 42. Bxa3 Ba4 {Dvoretzky} 43. Kf2 Rd2+ 44. Kg1 {Emelin} g5!? $36 {[%cal Yh6h5,Yh5h4]}) 42. Re2 Bd3 43. Re3 Bxf1 44. Rxb1 Rd1 45. Kf2 Bd3 46. Rb8+ Kh7 47. Re1 Rxe1 48. Kxe1 Bc4 49. Rd8 Ra5 50. Rd2 f6 51. Rc2 Be6 52. Kd2 g5 53. Rc3 Kg6 54. Rxa3 Rxa3 55. Bxa3 Bxa2 1/2-1/2

