{[%evp 0,103,26,44,38,-31,-12,-14,-11,-35,-35,-35,-31,-31,-37,-53,-40,-10,-9,-18,-28,-33,34,-41,-22,-22,-38,-15,-3,-15,-15,-18,20,22,8,-5,1,-15,2,0,78,81,92,71,86,68,68,1,86,86,86,99,124,124,176,176,209,154,188,188,183,95,95,95,128,113,128,128,128,131,156,169,167,190,176,198,185,198,195,195,198,186,192,198,195,190,191,217,220,221,211,218,218,202,218,219,299,267,288,241,285,330,426,465,466,465] I first met Magnus at the European Cup in 2003 and we even played his Norwegian team in round 1. His rating was then approaching 2400 and although Emil Sutovsky eventually ground him down in a long endgame, he was clearly a great talent going places. In some 3+ years I saw him again in Wijk aan Zee 2007. His ELO was almost 2700 and he was playing in the A-group for the first time, having qualified from the lower group. Even this time he didn't do too well, not winning a single game. However, again it was just a minor setback. Starting from Linares 2007 a few months later, Carlsen was very much able to hold his own even against the very best. His improvement was rapid and obvious, the following game is from Wijk aan Zee 2008, where he already shared 1st place with Levon Aronian. With a tactically astute and technically flawless performance he seemingly easily outplayed the top female player in chess history.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 d5 {At that time Carlsen tried the text move with both colours. Black in Wijk 2008 played all the main alternatives:} (4... c5 {is rare nowadays, but used to be Portisch's favourite throughout his career:} 5. dxc5 Nc6 (5... O-O 6. a3 Bxc5 {is more usual}) 6. a3 Bxc5 7. b4!? Be7 8. Nf3 b6 9. e4 Qc7 (9... d6!? $14) 10. Bb2 O-O 11. Bd3 Qb8? ({It's too late for} 11... d6 12. Nd5 $16 {, but the complications will favour White.}) (11... Ng4 $142 $14) 12. b5 Na5 13. e5 Ng4 14. Bxh7+ Kh8 15. Be4 Nxc4 16. Bxa8 Qxa8 17. Ne2 Nxb2 18. h3! (18. Qxb2?! Bc5 (18... Bb7 19. h3 $16) 19. O-O Bb7 $44) 18... Nxe5!? 19. Nxe5 Bf6 (19... Qxg2 20. Rg1 Qd5 21. Qxb2 Bf6 22. Qb4 Qc5 (22... d6? 23. Nc6 Bxa1 24. Qh4+ Kg8 25. Ne7#) 23. f4 $16) 20. Ng4! Nd3+ 21. Qxd3 Bxa1 22. O-O f5 (22... Bf6 23. Nxf6 gxf6 24. Qd4 $36) 23. Nf4 Kg8 24. Ng6 Re8 25. Rxa1 fxg4 26. hxg4 $16 {[%csl Rg8] /+-, Ljubojevic,L (2543)-Portisch,L (2530) Wijk aan Zee 2008 Black's pieces lack coordination and his  is too vulnerable.}) (4... O-O {is even now the most popular reaction:} {The standard} 5. a3 ({The sharp} 5. e4 d5 6. e5 Ne4 7. Bd3 c5 8. Ne2 (8. cxd5 {or}) (8. a3 {are considered harmless, while}) (8. Nf3 {and the text lead to the same positions}) 8... cxd4 (8... Nc6 9. cxd5 exd5 10. O-O cxd4 11. Nxd5 Qxd5 12. Bxe4 Qxe5 13. a3 $14) 9. Nxd4 Nd7 10. Bf4 {was initially met by} Ndc5 (10... Qh4 11. g3 Qh5 $13 {is the current fashion, with theory running to around move 30.}) 11. O-O Bxc3 12. bxc3 Nxd3 (12... Bd7!?) 13. Qxd3 b6 14. cxd5 Qxd5 15. Rfd1 Bb7 16. c4 Qa5 (16... Qd7 17. Qe3 Qa4 18. f3 Nc5 19. Nb5 Qxc4 20. Nd6 Qc2 (20... Qa4 21. Rd4 Qd7 22. Ne4 Qe8 23. Nf6+ gxf6 24. Bh6 Kh8 25. Bg7+ {1-0, Abbasov,F (2565)-Ibrahimov,R (2568) Baku 2009}) 21. Rac1 Qxa2 22. Bh6 gxh6 23. Qxh6 Nd7 24. Nxb7 Nxe5 25. Nd6 Rfd8 26. Rd4 Qa3 27. Rcd1 Qb3 {Grachev,B (2652)-Korotylev,A (2597) St Petersburg 2009} 28. h4! $18 {[%cal Rh6g5]}) 17. f3 Nc5 18. Qe3 Rfd8 $142 ! (18... Ba6? 19. Bh6! {[%cal Re3g5]} gxh6 20. Nc6 Qa4 21. Qxh6 Qxc6 22. Rd4 $18) (18... Rfc8 19. Nb5 Bc6 20. Nd6 Rc7 21. h4 Nd7 22. Kh2 Qc5 23. Qe1 h5 24. Qg3 $36 {Krush,I (2473)-Van der Wiel,J (2490) Wijk aan Zee 2008}) 19. Nb3 Rxd1+ 20. Rxd1 Qxa2 21. Nxc5 bxc5 22. Qxc5 h6 {½, Salzmann,S (2310)-Siebarth,M (2131) email 2017}) 5... Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 {gave White nothing after} b6 (6... d5 {is currently the most popular line among the elite.}) 7. Bg5 Bb7 8. Nf3 ({One year earlier in 2007 M.Gerevich twice lost with White the endgame after} 8. f3 h6 9. Bh4 d5 10. e3 Nbd7 11. cxd5 Nxd5 12. Bxd8 Nxc3 13. Bh4 Nd5 14. Bf2 c5 $132) ({White's main try for already more than one decade is} 8. e3 d6 9. Ne2) 8... d6 9. Nd2 Nbd7 10. f3 Rc8 11. e4 h6 12. Bh4 c5 13. Bd3 d5 14. exd5 (14. e5 Ne4 15. Bxd8 Nxc3 16. Bh4? cxd4! 17. bxc3 Nxe5 $17) 14... exd5 15. O-O dxc4 16. Nxc4 cxd4 17. Qxd4 Nc5 18. Qxd8 Rcxd8 19. Be2 Rfe8 20. Rfe1 g5 21. Bg3 Nh5 22. Bf2 Nf4 23. Bf1 Ncd3 24. Rxe8+ Rxe8 25. Rd1 Nxf2 26. Kxf2 Bc8 27. g3 $11 {½, Van Wely,L (2681)-Leko,P (2753) Wijk aan Zee 2008}) 5. a3 {Then considered the most principled continuation.} ({The Rubinstein & classical combination} 5. e3 {is not too effective:} c5 6. Nf3 O-O 7. a3 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 Qc7 9. a4 b6 10. cxd5 exd5 11. dxc5 bxc5 12. Ba3 Re8 (12... Bg4!?) 13. Bb5 Rd8 14. O-O Bg4 15. Be2 c4 16. Nd4 Bxe2 17. Qxe2 Nc6 18. Nxc6 Qxc6 19. Be7 Rd7 20. Bxf6 Qxf6 $11 {Kortschnoj,V (2605)-Timman,J (2561) Wijk aan Zee 2008}) ({Subsequently White turned his sights to} 5. cxd5 Qxd5 6. Nf3 Qf5 7. Qb3 {, after} c5 ({After} 7... Nc6 8. Bd2 O-O {Anand's novelty} 9. h3!? {came only later that year:} b6?! 10. g4 Qa5 11. Rc1 Bb7 12. a3 Bxc3 13. Bxc3 Qd5 14. Qxd5 Nxd5 15. Bd2 Nf6 16. Rg1 Rac8 17. Bg2 Ne7 18. Bb4 c5 19. dxc5 Rfd8 20. Ne5 Bxg2 21. Rxg2 bxc5 22. Rxc5 $16 {Anand,V (2783)-Kramnik,V (2772) WCh Bonn 2008 For details see the notes to this game in CBM 127 by Postny.}) 8. a3 Ba5 9. Qc4 Bxc3+ 10. Qxc3 Nbd7 11. g4! (11. g3 cxd4 12. Qxd4 b6 13. Bg2 Bb7 $11) 11... Qxg4 12. dxc5 Nd5 {another strong novelty} 13. h3! ({Too soft is} 13. Qc2 Qf5!? 14. Qc4 Ne5 15. Nxe5 Qxe5 16. Bg2 Bd7 17. Bxd5 {½, Eljanov,P (2692)-Anand,V (2799) Wijk aan Zee 2008}) 13... Nxc3 14. hxg4 Nd5 15. b4 a5 16. e4 N5f6 17. Bb2 axb4 18. axb4 Rxa1+ 19. Bxa1 O-O 20. g5 Nxe4 21. Bd3 f5 22. Nd2 Nxd2 23. Kxd2 Rd8 24. Ke3 $16 {Kotanjian,T (2578)-Shavtvaladze,N (2435) Kavala 2008 gave White powerful pressure for a single pawn.}) 5... Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 Ne4 {In general Black seeks active counterplay to balance the opponent's bishop pair. This remains valid even today, only the preferred methods have changed. The text-move, once considered the main line, has faded from the limelight.} ({The direction, in which this line developed further, was then only beginning to appear:} 6... dxc4 7. Qxc4 b6 8. Nf3 (8. Bf4 Nd5 9. Bg3 O-O 10. Nf3 Ba6 11. Qa4 Qd7 12. Qc2 c5 13. Ng5 Nf6 14. Be5 cxd4 15. Bxf6 d3! 16. exd3 gxf6 17. Ne4 Qd4 18. Qc3 {½, Beliavsky,A (2646)-Rozentalis,E (2581) Austria 2007} Qxc3+ 19. bxc3 Nd7 $15 {is already very pleasant for Black}) 8... O-O (8... Ba6 9. Qa4+!? Qd7 10. Qc2 Qc6 11. Qxc6+ Nxc6 12. Bf4 $14) 9. Bg5 Ba6 10. Qc2 c5!? {was Topalov's novelty in 2007:} (10... Nbd7 11. e4 Bxf1 (11... Qc8 $132) 12. Rxf1 h6 13. Bh4 Qc8 14. Qc6 Rb8 15. Rc1 Qa6 $132) 11. dxc5 bxc5 12. Rd1 ({A later attempt is} 12. Qxc5!? Nbd7 13. Qd4 Qb8?! (13... Qc7! 14. Rc1 Qb7 $44) 14. g3 e5 15. Qb4 e4 16. Nd4 Qe5 17. Bf4 Qh5 18. Qd6 Bb7 19. Bg2 Rfd8 20. Rd1 Nb6 21. Qe5 Qxe5 22. Bxe5 Nc4 23. Bxf6 gxf6 24. Nf5 $16 {Dreev,A (2654)-Grigoryan,K (2559) Legnica 2013 For details see the notes to this game in CBM 155 by Krasenkow.}) 12... Qa5+ 13. Bd2 Qb6 14. Bc3 Nbd7 15. e4 Bxf1 16. Kxf1 Qb7 17. Re1 Ne8 18. h4 Nd6 19. Rh3 Nb5 20. Rg3 f6 21. e5 f5 22. Ng5 Rfe8 23. Qe2 Nf8 24. h5 h6 25. Nh3 Nc7 $132 {Karpov,A (2670)-Topalov,V (2769) Vitoria Gasteiz 2007}) ({The current treatment is illustrated by} 6... O-O {is currently the most popular line among the elite.} 7. Bg5 dxc4 8. Qxc4 b6 9. Rd1 Ba6 10. Qa4 h6 11. Bh4 Qd7 (11... Qe7 12. Nf3 Rd8!? 13. e3 Bxf1 14. Rxf1 c5 15. dxc5 Rxd1+ 16. Qxd1 g5 17. Bg3 Qxc5 {is reliable equality -} 18. Qd8+ Kg7 19. Be5 Qc1+ 20. Ke2 Qc4+ {and White either accepts a perpetual, or a sterile endgame after} 21. Qd3 (21. Kd2? Nd7 22. Qxa8 Qxf1 $19) 21... Qxd3+ 22. Kxd3 Nd7 $11) 12. Qc2 Qc6 13. Qxc6 Nxc6 14. Bxf6 gxf6 15. e3 (15. Nf3 Ne7!? ({Black has a choice, he can also play} 15... Rac8 {or}) (15... Rfc8) 16. e3 Bb7!? $11) 15... Bxf1 (15... Bb7!? {[%cal Yf8c8,Yc6e7,Yc7c5] is also playable, Black should gradually equalize.}) 16. Kxf1 Na5 17. Nf3 Nc4 (17... Rac8 18. Ke2 c5 19. dxc5 bxc5 20. Rd7 Nc4 21. Rc1 Nxb2 22. Rc2 Na4 23. Rxa7 Ra8 24. Rxa8 Rxa8 25. Nd2 Nb6 26. Rb2 Ra6 27. Ne4 Nd5 28. Ra2 c4 29. Kd2 f5 30. Nc3 Nxc3 31. Kxc3 Ra4 $11 {Wang,H (2763)-Radjabov,T (2765) chess.com INT 2020}) 18. Rb1 c5 19. Ke2 Rfc8 20. Rhc1 Nd6 (20... cxd4 21. Nxd4 $14 {[%csl Rc6]}) 21. dxc5 bxc5 22. Rc2 Kf8 (22... Rab8! 23. Nd2 c4 24. Rbc1 Kg7! 25. Kf3 c3!? 26. bxc3 Nc4 27. Nxc4 Rxc4 {is a drawish  due to} 28. Rd1 Rb3 29. Rd7 Rxa3 30. Rb2 Rc8! 31. Rbb7 Rf8 $11 {Giri}) 23. Nd2 c4 24. Rbc1 Rab8 25. Kf3 c3?! (25... f5 26. Nxc4 Rxc4 27. Rxc4 Nxc4 28. Rxc4 Rxb2 29. Rc7 f4 30. Rxa7 fxe3 31. fxe3 $14 {gives Black drawing chances, but no guaranteed half point.}) (25... Rc5 $142 ! 26. b4 Rc6 $132 {[%csl Ra3][%cal Yc6a6] } 27. Nxc4? Rbc8) 26. bxc3 Rc5 ({With the  on f8 the line} 26... Nc4 27. Nxc4 Rxc4 28. Rd1! $36 {[%cal Yd1d4,Yd4d7] is different and rather unpleasant.}) 27. c4 $14 {/,Carlsen,M (2863)-Caruana,F (2828) Stavanger 2020/ Carlsen hasn't played 4.c2 since he was a teenager, so Caruana might have been at least slightly surprised...}) ({This concrete position arises less often today (Black prefers the 4...0-0 5.a3 Bxc3+ 6.Qxc3 d5 move-order, while after 4...d5 he has to reckon with the aforementioned 5.cxd5), apart from the aforementioned continuations Black has also Romanishin's sharp pawn sacrifice} 6... c5 7. dxc5 d4 8. Qg3 (8. Qc2 e5 9. e3 Nc6 10. b4 O-O {and it's difficult for White to proceed with his development without concessions:} 11. Nf3 Re8 {[%cal Yd4d3]} 12. e4 a5 13. Rb1 axb4 14. axb4 Bg4 15. Nd2 Nh5 16. b5 d3 17. Qxd3 Nd4 18. Nb3 f5 19. Nxd4 exd4 20. Be2 Bxe2 21. Kxe2 fxe4 22. Qh3 Ra2+ 23. Kd1 e3 24. Bb2 Qa5 25. Qxh5 Qd2# {0-1, Lazarev,V (2501)-Romanishin,O (2567) Arco 1999}) 8... Nc6 (8... O-O 9. Bh6 Ne8 10. h4 (10. O-O-O!? Nc6 11. e3 Qc7 12. Qxc7 Nxc7 13. Bf4 e5 14. Bg3 dxe3 15. fxe3 a5 16. Ne2! $36 {} Na6 17. Nc3 Nxc5 18. Rd5) 10... Nd7 11. h5 Qc7 12. Rh3 f5 13. Qxc7 Nxc7 14. Bg5 e5 15. Be7 Re8 16. Bd6 Ne6 17. Rd1 Nexc5 18. f4 exf4 19. Nf3 d3 20. h6 g6 21. Ng5 Ne4 22. Bxf4 Ne5 $142 ! {} (22... dxe2 23. Bxe2 Nxg5 24. Bxg5 $16 {[%csl