[PlyCount "76"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 dxc4 5. a4 Bf5 6. Nh4 Bg4 7. h3 Bh5 8. g4 Bg6 9. Nxg6 hxg6 10. e3 e6 11. Bxc4 Bb4 12. Bf1? $146 {The strange thing about this bad novelty is that Carlsen has played the position twice before. Generally in my opinion this opening variation looks healthy for Black - a good development and White already has created weaknesses.} (12. Qf3 $13) (12. Bd2 Nbd7 13. g5 Nd5 14. e4 N5b6 15. Bb3 a5 16. Qe2 c5 (16... e5 17. dxe5 Nxe5 18. O-O-O Nd3+ 19. Kb1 Nc5 20. Nb5! Nxb3 21. Bxb4 axb4 22. Rxd8+ Rxd8 23. Nc7+? (23. Rd1! O-O 24. Nd6 $16) 23... Ke7 24. Qc2 Nc4! $132 {0-1 Carlsen,M (2385)-Andriasian,Z (2311)/Budva 2003 (34)}) 17. d5 c4 18. Bxc4 Nxc4 19. Qxc4 O-O 20. O-O-O Rc8 21. Qe2 exd5 22. Kb1 Bxc3?! (22... dxe4 23. Nxe4 Qc7 24. Bxb4 axb4 25. Nd6 Ra8 $11) (22... Qc7 23. exd5 Nb6 $11 {(P. Nielsen)}) 23. Bxc3 Nc5 24. Rxd5 Qe8 25. f3 $14 {1-0 Carlsen,M (2552)-Nielsen,P (2628)/Malmo/Copenhagen 2004 (40)}) 12... Qd5! {Taking advantage of Black's considerable lead in development. White is forced to create more weaknesses in his own camp.} 13. f3 {Making such a move means that most likely White will have to advance soon the pawn further.} Qa5 14. Bg2 c5! {Possibly Carlsen expected Black to take the pawn immediately, after which White will have compensation. But Gelfand first completes development and only then takes the pawn, when White won't be able to create anything in return.} (14... Bxc3+ 15. bxc3 Qxc3+ 16. Bd2 Qc4 17. Rb1 b6 18. f4 $44) (14... Nd5 15. O-O Nxc3 16. bxc3 Bxc3 17. Rb1 Bb4 (17... b6 18. Ba3 (18. f4 O-O 19. Ba3 Rd8 20. Qc2 $44) 18... Bd2 19. Rb3 Qxa4 20. Qb1 $44) 18. Qb3 Na6 19. f4 $44) 15. dxc5 Nc6 16. O-O O-O 17. f4 ({White had to prevent other weaknesses by} 17. Bd2 Rfd8 18. Qe2 {with good chances to equalize later on.}) 17... Qxc5 18. g5? {Certainly not Carlsen's best game. His move backwards 12.f1 was bad, now a move forwards is even worse, only helping Black to further improve the position.} ({He could still be in the game with} 18. Qe2 {For instance:} Bxc3 (18... Rfd8 19. Bd2 $15 {/=}) 19. bxc3 Qxc3 20. Bb2 Qa5 21. f5 {with activity for the pawn, probably enough to hold the balance thanks to very strong bishops.}) 18... Rfd8 19. Qe2 Bxc3! 20. bxc3 Nd5 {Now Black has a large advantage. White cannot prevent a loss of a pawn.} 21. Qb5 (21. Bd2 Na5 $17 {[%csl Rc3,Rd2,Re3][%cal Ga5c4,Ga5b3]}) (21. c4 Nc3 22. Qc2 Qxc4 {[%cal Gc3e2]} 23. Kh2 Nb4 $19) 21... Qxc3 22. Rb1 (22. Ra3 Qc2 $17 23. Qxb7 $140 ? Rab8 24. Qa6 Ndb4 $19 {catching the queen.}) 22... Ndb4 23. Qe2 Rac8 24. Be4 Na5 25. Bb2 Qc4 $17 26. Qg2 Nb3 {The conversion of the huge advantage is a matter of style and possibly mood. Gelfand decides to return the extra pawn and activate all his pieces to their maximum.} (26... Nd5 $17) 27. Bxb7 Rd2 28. Qf3 ({Tougher is} 28. Qg3 {with some interesting tactical ideas:} Rcd8 (28... Rc7 $142 29. Bh1 Nd3 $17) 29. f5 Nd3 {(otherwise White could play 30.e7} 30. fxg6 Nxb2 (30... fxg6 31. Ba3 $13) 31. gxf7+ Kf8 32. g6 $44) 28... Rcd8 {With such active pieces and an exposed king on g1, Black's win is out of the question.} 29. Be5 Nc5 30. Rbc1 Nc2 31. Bc6 a6 {A very rare case when White has problems to find squares for his light-squared bishop on the long diagonal.} (31... Nb3! 32. Rb1 (32. Bb5 Qc5 $19) 32... Nxe3! $19) 32. Rf2 Nb3 33. Rcf1 Nb4 ({Again} 33... Rxf2 34. Rxf2 Nd2 {[%csl Rc6,Re3] wins on the spot}) 34. Qe4 Qxc6 35. Qxb4 Rxf2 36. Rxf2 Qc1+ 37. Kg2 Qxe3 38. Qb7 Nd2 0-1

