1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Be7 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Bf4 O-O 6. e3 Nbd7 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Nxd5 exd5 9. Be2 Bb4+! {An important move, that lures the white knight on to the lesser square d2.} 10. Nd2 Nf6 11. O-O Bd6 12. Bg5 h6 13. Bh4 c6 14. Qc2 Re8 (14... g6 15. f3 Bf5 16. e4 g5 17. Bf2 Bg6 18. Bd3 dxe4 19. fxe4 Ng4 20. Nf3 Nxf2 21. Rxf2 Bf4 22. Kh1 Be3 23. Re2 Bxd4 24. e5 Bxd3 25. Qxd3 c5 26. e6 Bg7 27. Qc4 {1/2 Tregubov,P (2609)-Gonzalez Garcia,J (2541)/Merida 2007}) 15. Rab1 g6 16. Bxf6?! {The eternal optimism of youth! The exchange of the bishop for the knight f6 is typical for this pawn structure, but usually White has more minor pieces left supporting the queenside attack.} (16. Bd3 Kg7 17. Bg3 Bxg3 18. hxg3 $11) 16... Qxf6 17. b4 a6 18. a4 h5 19. b5 (19. Bd3 Kg7 20. Qc3 h4 21. h3 Bc7 $15) 19... Bf5 20. Bd3 Bxd3 21. Qxd3 cxb5! {Brave, but absolutely the correct solution. The passed pawn on the a-file is strong and the d5 weakness defendable.} (21... axb5 22. axb5 Ra3 23. Rb3 Rea8 24. Nf3 $11) 22. axb5 a5 23. Qb3 Qf5 (23... a4? 24. Qxd5 Qe7 25. Nc4 $14) 24. Qa4 Rec8 (24... Qd3 25. Nf3 (25. Rfd1 Rec8 26. b6 Qc2 27. Qd7 Qc6 28. Qxc6 Rxc6 29. e4 $15) 25... Qc4 26. Qxc4 dxc4 27. Rfc1 Rec8 28. e4 a4 $15) 25. b6 Qc2 26. Nb3! {Carlsen deserves full praise for composing his mind enough for the difficult defensive task.} (26. Qxc2 Rxc2 27. Nb3 a4 $17) 26... Qc6 (26... Qc4!? 27. Qa1 (27. Ra1?! Qxa4 28. Rxa4 Bb4 29. Rfa1 Rc4 30. g3 Bc3 31. R1a2 Rxa4 32. Rxa4 Ra6 $17) 27... Qa6 (27... a4 28. Rfc1 axb3 29. Rxc4 dxc4 30. Qc3 Ra2 31. g3 Bf8 $15) 28. Qa4 Rc6 29. g3 Bb4 (29... Qc4 30. Qa1 Rxb6 31. Rfc1 Qe2 32. Nc5 $13) 30. Rfc1 Rxb6 31. Nc5 Bxc5 32. dxc5 Rxb1 33. Rxb1 Qc6 $17) 27. Qa1 Bb4 (27... a4 28. Rfc1 Bxh2+ 29. Kxh2 Qd6+ 30. Kg1 axb3 31. Rxc8+ Rxc8 32. Rxb3 Rc2 $15) 28. Nc5!? Qxb6 (28... Bxc5 29. dxc5 a4 (29... Qxc5 30. Rfc1 Qd6 31. Rxc8+ Rxc8 32. Qxa5 $11) 30. Rfc1 Re8 31. Qa3 Re4 $17) 29. Nd3 Qd6 {Alekseev must have been satisfied with the progress he has made. A better position and an extra pawn promise him fair winning chances.} 30. Qa2 Ra6 31. g3 Rc3 32. Rb3! (32. Nf4 Rc4 33. Qa4 Qc6 $17) 32... Rxb3 33. Qxb3 Rc6 34. Qa4 (34. Nf4 Rc4 35. Ra1 Qc6 $17) 34... Kg7 (34... Rc4 35. Nxb4 axb4 36. Qa8+ Kg7 37. Qxb7 $15) 35. Rb1 {[%mdl 256] Amazingly enough White has managed to organize his resistance on the queenside so flexibly, that Black will find it very challenging to make progress.} Rc4 (35... Rb6 36. Rc1 f6 37. Rc8 Rc6 38. Ra8 $15) 36. Ne5 Rc7 (36... Rc3 37. Qb5 Rc7 38. Qa4! (38. Kg2 f6 39. Nd3 Qc6 40. Qxc6 bxc6 $17) 38... f6 39. Nd3 $14) 37. Nd3 Qc6 38. Qa2 {White's queen has to stay on the board to create tension against the black pawns.} (38. Qxc6 bxc6 $17) 38... Qb5 (38... Qc4 39. Nxb4 axb4 40. Qa5 $11) 39. Qb3 Rc6 (39... Rc4 40. Ne5 Rc7 41. Nd3) 40. Kg2 (40. Nxb4 axb4 41. Qxb4? Rc1+ $19) 40... Qc4 41. Qd1 {White is not dreaming about activity, as his defensive set up is ideal.} (41. Nxb4 Qxb3 42. Rxb3 Rb6 43. Rc3 axb4 44. Rb3 Kf6 $17) 41... Qb5 (41... Rb6 42. Ne5 Qa2 43. Nd3 Rf6 44. Rb2) 42. Qb3 Rc4 43. Ne5 Rc7 44. Nd3 Rc4 45. Ne5 Rc7 {Frustrating for Alekseev, but ideas for improvement are simply lacking.} 1/2-1/2

