1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 Bc5 5. Nxc6 bxc6!? {Not a very common move.} (5... Qf6 {is almost authomatically played here, which is quite curious. The move itself is not bad, as proven by years and decades of practice. However, for the taste of certain players, it looks a bit like a premature commitment, which makes one wonder why the move played by Kamsky has not been taken too seriously before.}) 6. Bd3 {Just one of the possible moves here.} Qh4 {This is a more active way to attack the f2-pawn, leaving the f6-square available for the knight at the same time.} 7. Qe2 {The most natural, but not the only move tried here.} (7. O-O Nf6 8. Qd2?! {If we compare with the lines starting with 5...f6, this move leads to a less favourable position for White.} (8. h3 d6 {[%cal Gc8h3]} 9. Qf3 {looks more active.}) 8... O-O 9. Qf4 Qxf4 10. Bxf4 d6 11. Nd2 Nh5 12. Bg5 h6 13. Bh4 Nf4 14. Nb3 Nxd3 15. cxd3 Bb6 $15 {Alatortsev,V-Lilienthal,A/Leningrad 1935/[ChessBase] (0-1, 65)}) (7. Qf3 {places the queen on a slightly exposed square.} Nf6 8. Bf4 {This move allows Black open the centre with all the comfort.} (8. Nc3!? {deserves being investigated. The consequences of} Ng4 {are not entirely clear after} 9. g3 Ne5 10. Qxf7+! Kxf7 11. gxh4 d5!? 12. Be2! {[%csl Gf3]}) 8... d5! (8... O-O 9. Bg3 Qg5 10. Bf4 Qh4 11. Bg3 Qg5 12. Bf4 Qh4 {½-½ Ris,R (2395)-Winants,L (2504)/Netherlands 2007/CBM 120 ext}) 9. Nd2 O-O 10. O-O Bb6 11. h3 Be6 $11 {Mullon,J (2318)-Radulov,I (2359)/Clichy 2002/CBM 089 ext (0-1, 36)}) 7... Nf6 8. Be3 ({The attempt to harass the queen with the help of the pawns is rather time consuming:} 8. h3 O-O 9. g3 Qh5 10. g4 (10. Qxh5 Nxh5 11. Nc3 {Godena,M (2558)-Volke,K (2490)/Dresden 2007/CBM 118 (½-½, 15)} d5!? 12. exd5 Re8+ 13. Kf1 cxd5 14. Nxd5 Bb7 15. c4 Nf6 16. Nxf6+ gxf6 17. Rh2 Rad8 $44) 10... Qh4?! (10... Qe5!? 11. g5 Ne8 12. f4 Qe6 $132 {[%cal Gd7d5,Gf7f6] White's delay in development prevents him from maintaining his advantage of space.}) 11. Nd2 Ne8 12. Nb3 Be7 13. Nd4 Bc5 14. Nf5 $14 {Motylev,A (2641)-Liang Chong (2531)/Moscow 2001/CBM 086 (1-0, 53). White has almost completed his development and stands actively.}) 8... Bxe3 9. Qxe3 O-O 10. Nd2 Re8! (10... d6 {defines the central structure too soon.} 11. Nf3 (11. h3 Ng4 12. Nf3 Qh5 13. Qd4 c5 14. Qc3 Re8 15. O-O Ne5 16. Nxe5 Qxe5 17. Qxe5 Rxe5 $11 {Van der Wiel,J (2570)-Winants,L (2495)/Netherlands 1994/EXT 98 (½-½, 38)}) 11... Qh5 12. O-O Re8 13. Rfe1 Bg4 14. Nd2 Bd7 15. Qg3 Rab8 16. b3 c5 17. Nc4 $14 {Lecroq,M (2578)-Martin Clemente,J (2569)/ICCF corr 2000/ICCF Telechess CBM 94 (½-½, 28). White has an harmonious display of forces and the better structure.}) 11. O-O ({Now,} 11. Nf3 Qh5 12. O-O {could be met by} d5! $36 {without loss of time.}) 11... d6 12. Rae1 $146 ({It is questionable whether this novelty, concentrating the forces on the kingside in a mechanical way, is really better than the previously played} 12. Rfe1 Ng4 (12... a5!? 13. Nf3 Qh5 14. h3 {[%cal Ga1d1] offers White a better version of the game.}) 13. Qg3 Qxg3 14. hxg3 Rb8 15. b3 Ne5 16. Be2 c5 17. f4 Nc6 18. c3 h6 19. Bf3 Bb7 20. Nc4 $14 {Chytilek,R (2386)-Timoscenko,G (2513)/Czechia 2001/EXT 2003 (½-½, 48). White has consolidated his advantage of space and retains the more flexible pawn structure.}) 12... a5 {[%csl Ga2]} 13. Qg3 (13. Nf3 Qh5 {[%csl Gf1] leaves the f1-rook misplaced now.}) 13... Qxg3 14. hxg3 {We have reached the same structure as in Timoscenko's game, but Black has avoided the losses of time cause by the knight's jumping around.} a4 $11 {Besdies, the pressure exerted against the enemy queenside will soon allow him get rid of his main structural weakness, the a-pawn and open the a-file for the rook.} 15. f3 Be6 16. b3 Nd7 17. Re3 axb3 18. axb3 Ne5 19. Be2 Ra2 20. Rc3 c5 21. b4 cxb4 (21... cxb4 22. Rxc7 Rc8 23. Rxc8+ Bxc8 24. Rc1 Be6 $11 {[%cal Gg8f8,Gf8e7]}) 1/2-1/2

