{[%evp 0,107,58,42,26,-29,44,66,75,58,60,56,47,-26,-26,-26,12,-1,32,32,40,45,5,5,3,-5,1,1,31,-35,83,67,22,-19,-29,-29,1,14,40,58,58,52,80,48,38,16,28,8,27,22,6,22,32,14,63,4,60,80,55,55,37,37,114,43,77,31,59,12,52,57,50,50,48,48,58,56,85,43,57,74,43,91,104,154,154,154,154,168,168,113,133,151,151,120,136,180,180,141,128,170,190,188,233,239,229,212,230,213,250,239]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. Bg5 Ne4 6. Bh4 (6. Bf4 {was Carlsen's choice in the only other game where he tried 5.Bg5 against the Grunfeld.} Nxc3 7. bxc3 c5 8. cxd5 cxd4 9. cxd4 Qxd5 10. e3 O-O 11. Be2 Nc6 12. O-O Bf5 13. Qa4 Qa5 14. Qxa5 Nxa5 15. Rfc1 Rfc8 {1/2 (74) Carlsen,M (2881)-Grischuk,A (2792) Stavanger NOR 2014}) 6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 dxc4 8. Qa4+ (8. e3 b5 9. a4 c6 10. Qb1 a6 11. Be2 O-O 12. O-O Re8 (12... Nd7?! 13. Qe4!) 13. Rd1 Nd7 {0-1 (40) Nguyen Duc Hoa (2436)-Gupta,A (2606) Xingtai CHN 2019 White's compensation is insufficient.}) 8... Qd7 (8... c6 9. Qxc4 Qa5 10. e4 (10. e3 Be6 11. Qd3 (11. Qb4 Qxb4 12. cxb4 Nd7 {[%cal Ya7a5]} (12... a5 {0-1 (51) Mirzoev,A (2510)-Safarli,E (2664) Nakhchivan AZE 2016})) 11... Nd7 12. Be2?! (12. Qc2 Nc5 $13) 12... Nc5! 13. Qc2 Bf5 14. Qb2 Nd3+ $17 {0-1 (30) Makhmutov,R (2442)-Antipov,M (2607) Sochi RUS 2020}) 10... Be6 (10... Na6 11. Rc1 Be6 (11... Bh6!?) 12. Qd3 Rd8 13. Qe3 Qxa2 14. Bd3 $44 {1/2 (26) Grivas,E (2431)-Safarli,E (2667) Rio (Achaia) GRE 2018}) 11. Qd3 (11. Qb4 Qxb4 12. cxb4 Nd7 $15 {[%csl Rd4][%cal Ye6g4]}) 11... Nd7 {followed by c5. Black gets a perfectly fine position.}) 9. Qxc4 b6 10. e3 (10. Bg3 c5 11. Ne5 Bxe5 12. Bxe5 Ba6 (12... O-O 13. dxc5 Ba6 14. Qe4 Qe6! {[%cal Yb8d7]} (14... Nc6 15. Bf4 $13 {1-0 (39) Le Quang Liem (2709)-Mikhalevski,V (2565) lichess.org INT 2020}) 15. Qxa8 (15. Qe3 Nd7 $17) 15... Nc6 $19) 13. Qb3 O-O $15) 10... Ba6 11. Qb3 Bxf1 12. Kxf1 O-O (12... Nc6!? 13. Ke2 O-O 14. Rhd1 Na5 15. Qb4 Rfe8 16. Rac1 Rac8 $13 {0-1 (45) Georgiev,V (2525)-Eljanov,P (2675) Wijk aan Zee NED 2007}) 13. Ke2 c5 14. dxc5 (14. Rhd1 {is a safer choice for White. The pawn on d4 is restricting Black's bishop while White's pawn structure remains perfectly healthy.} Qb7 (14... cxd4 15. cxd4 Nc6 16. Qb5 Rfc8 17. Rab1 e6 18. Kf1 $14 {1-0 (70) Papaioannou,I (2597)-Kazhgaleyev,M (2640) Dresden GER 2008}) 15. Kf1 Rc8 $13 {[%cal Yb8c6,Yc6a5]}) 14... Na6! 15. Rhd1 (15. cxb6? Nc5 16. Qc4 axb6 17. Rhd1 Qb7 18. Kf1 Rfc8 $19) (15. c6 Qc7 {Followed by Nb8 or Nc5. Black is already preferable.}) 15... Qb7?! (15... Qc7 16. cxb6 axb6 $44 {[%csl Ra2,Rc3][%cal Ya6c5,Yf8c8,Ya8a4] Black has excellent compensation for the missing pawn and he will be able to exercise pressure in the open files ( a and c).}) 16. c6! Qxc6?! (16... Qc7 17. Qb5 Nc5 18. Bg3 e5 19. Rd5 Rfe8 20. Rad1 Rac8 $13) 17. Bxe7 Rfe8 (17... Rfc8!?) 18. Ba3 Qxc3 19. Qxc3 Bxc3 20. Rac1 Bb4 (20... Bg7 21. Rd7 $36) 21. Bb2 (21. Bxb4?! {Exchanging the bishops doesn't make much sense since White doesn't have enough time to conquer the 7th rank with his rooks.} Nxb4 22. Rc7 (22. Rc4 Nxa2 (22... Na6 {is good enough as well}) 23. Ra1 b5! 24. Rc5 Nb4 {should be about equal.}) 22... Rec8 23. Re7 Nxa2 24. Rdd7 Rd8 $11) 21... Bf8 22. Nd4 Nc5 $14 {[%csl Rc5,Gd4] [#] Both players have a strong knight in the centre ( Nd4 vs Nc5). In my opinion White keeps a small advantage partly because it is easier for White to expand on the kingside than for Black to expand on the queenside. If Black tries a plan with a6/b5, his knight on c5 will lack the necessary stability. Another important difference between the two knights is that White has the chance at some point to play Ba3 and take on c5 while it will be quite difficult for Black to play Bg7 and Bxd4 because of his weakness on the dark squares.} 23. g4! {Carlsen shows that his priority is to restrict Black's kingside.} (23. Nb5!? {Nb5 is the suggestion of Stockfish 12. White can redirect his knight to d5 via c3 or go to d6 while his bishop becomes more active.} Rec8 (23... Rad8 24. g4 $14) 24. g4 (24. Bd4!? h5 25. Bxc5!? {leads to a forcing line in which Black seems to hold just in time.} Bxc5 26. Rd7 a6 27. Nc7 Ra7 28. Ne8 Rxd7 29. Nf6+ Kg7 30. Nxd7 {[%cal Yd7b6]} Rc7 31. Nxc5 bxc5 32. Kd3 Rd7+ $11) 24... a6 25. Nd6 Rd8 26. Be5 f6 (26... Bg7 27. Bxg7 (27. Bf4!?) 27... Kxg7 28. e4 $14 {[%cal Yf2f4,Ye4e5]}) 27. Bg3 $14 {Although Stockfish is quite satisfied with Black's position I would feel that White is certainly pressing, Black who must play quite accurately.}) 23... Re4? {Eljanov places his pieces in a rather awkward way that will limit their mobility while not creating any serious counterplay.} (23... a6!? {[%cal Ya8d8] would have kept White's advantage minimal.}) (23... Na4 24. Ba1 Rac8 (24... Nc5 25. Nb5 $14) 25. Nb5 $14) 24. Kf3 Rae8 {Black's doubling rooks on the e-file gives an illusion of activity, but in reality the black rooks will be needed on other files.} 25. h3 f6?! {Eljanov decides to permanently weaken his pawn structure in order to bring his king closer to the centre. Following a waiting policy would have been preferable although this is not easily done because most of the black pieces lack mobility.} (25... Na4 26. Ba1 Nc5 27. Rc2! {followed by Nb5. Black is under pressure.} (27. Bc3 {[%cal Yc3b4]} Na4 28. Nb5 Nxc3 29. Rxc3 (29. Nxc3 Rc4 30. Nb5 Ra4) 29... Ra4 30. Rc7 Rxa2 31. Rdd7 $13 {Here the position becomes very concrete with White trying to mate the black king and Black running with his queenside passed pawns.})) 26. Ba3! {Carlsen makes clear his intention of removing Black's knight from c5.White has other choices as well but Ba3 seems to be very annoying for Black.} (26. Nb3 R4e6 (26... Kf7 27. Bd4) 27. Nxc5 (27. Bd4 Nxb3 28. axb3 Bd6 $14) 27... bxc5 (27... Bxc5 28. Rd7 $16) 28. Rc4 {[%cal Yc4a4,Yd1d7]} Ra6! $14) 26... Kf7 {[#]} 27. Rc2! {For me this is the most instructive moment of the game.The white rook on c2 protects the pawn on a2 in case Black can play Ra4 at a later point. If I had to guess, Carlsen didn't calculate 27.Bxc5 Bxc5 28.Nc6 because he didn't find a useful move for Black against 27.Rc2 or any other waiting move. Carlsen at the age of 17 shows patience and improves his position move by move avoiding concrete variations. It is very difficult for Black to find a move especially when there are no immediate threats from White.} (27. Bxc5 Bxc5 (27... bxc5?! {is illogical since the black bishop on f8 will be miserable and the pawn on c5 a permanent weakness.} 28. Nb3 R4e5 29. Rc4!? $16 {[%csl Ra7,Rc5][%cal Yc4a4] Black faces serious problems}) 28. Nc6 {[#]} g5! {A difficult move which gives Black decent chances of survival.} (28... a5 29. Rd7+ Kg8 30. Rb7 {[%cal Yc1d1,Yd1d7]} R4e6 (30... Ra4 31. Rd1! Rxa2 32. Rdd7 Bf8 33. Rxh7 a4 34. Rhf7 $16) 31. Nb8!? $16 {[%cal Yb8d7,Yb8a6]} (31. Nd4 $16 {[%cal Yd4b3]})) (28... Ra4 29. Rd7+ Kg8 30. Rc2 {[%cal Yc2d2,Yd7b7,Yd2d7] In my eyes this is beyond repair for Black.}) 29. Nxa7 (29. Rd7+ Kg6 30. Rxa7 h5 31. Rd1 hxg4+ 32. hxg4 Bxe3! 33. fxe3 Rxe3+ 34. Kf2 Re2+ 35. Kf1 R2e4 36. Rd2 R8e6 {Black has good chances for a draw.}) 29... Kg6 {[%cal Yh7h5] Black has solved most of his issues with the safety of his king and can count on some counterplay on the kingside.}) (27. Rc3) 27... Na6? {Eljanov opens the c-file with devastating consequences since the white rooks will invade and White gets a winning advantage. Na6 might be the losing mistake but it is very difficult to suggest a better plan.} (27... R4e7 {is Stockfish 12's proposal. I find it difficult to believe that Black can hold in a practical game after 28.Bxc5 bxc5 29. Nb3.} 28. Bxc5 bxc5 29. Nb3 (29. Rxc5? Rxe3+ $11) 29... Rc7 30. Rd4 $16 {[%csl Ra7,Rc5][%cal Yd4a4] This is probably better than what black got in the game but still very difficult to defend.}) (27... h5 {Doesn't bring any relief since the pawn on h5 will probably become a new target for white pieces.} 28. gxh5 (28. Nc6 $16) 28... gxh5 29. Ne2 Ra4 30. Bxc5 Bxc5 31. Nf4 h4 32. Rcd2 a5 33. Rg1 $16 {[%cal Yg1g4]}) 28. Bxf8 Kxf8 29. Rc6!? {Carlsen prefers to activate his rooks on the 6th rank than getting the a7 pawn. Although Stockfish prefers to get the pawn by playing 29.Nb5 I really like Carlsen's decision of activity over material.} (29. Nc6!? Nb4 (29... Nc5 30. Nxa7 $18) 30. Nxb4 Rxb4 31. Rc6 $18) 29... Kg7 30. Nb5 R4e7 31. Rdd6 {[#]} Nc5? {Black protects f6 by tactical means but will not stop White from building even more pressure.} (31... Rf8 32. h4 $16) (31... Nb4! {This was the only way for Black to ease the pressure against f6 and force the white rook out of c6.} 32. Rc4 Na6 $16) (31... Nb8 32. Rc7 Rxc7 33. Nxc7 Re7 34. Nd5 Rf7 35. e4 $18 {is similar to what happenned in the game.}) 32. Nc7 {White's knight joins the rooks in the attack against f6.} (32. Rxf6? Nd7 $17 {[%cal Yd7f6,Gd7e5]}) 32... Rf8 {[#]} 33. h4 {With the black pieces tied up in the defence of f6 Carlsen keeps on improving his position by gaining even more space on the kingside.} (33. Rxc5? {Simplifying into a rook ending with a pawn up would have been a serious technical mistake since Black would get decent drawing chances as it is often the case with rook endings.} bxc5 34. Ne6+ Rxe6 35. Rxe6 Rc8 36. Re7+ Kh6 37. Ke2 Rb8! 38. Rxa7 Rb2+ 39. Kf3 g5 {The worse is over for black.}) 33... Rff7 (33... f5 34. g5 $16 {Followed by Nd5 and h5. Black's pawn structure on the kingside will soon be in ruins.}) 34. Nd5 Rd7 {Exchanging a pair of rooks may bring, temporarily, some relief to Black but it doesn't solve his problems with his weaknesses. The exchange of rooks is welcomed by Carlsen who will be able to activate his king} 35. Rxd7 (35. e4!?) 35... Nxd7 36. Kg3 {Carlsen clears the way for his f pawn.} Nc5 (36... Nb8 37. Rd6 Nd7 38. e4 $18 {[%cal Yf2f3,Yh4h5]}) 37. f3 {Again Carlsen chooses prophylaxis first!} (37. h5!?) 37... h6 (37... f5 38. g5 Nd7 39. Nf4 $18 {[%cal Yh4h5]}) (37... Nb7 38. e4 Nd8 39. Rc8 Rd7 40. Nf4 {[%cal Yc8d8]} Kf7 41. g5 $18 {[%cal Yf4d5,Yd5f6]}) 38. Nf4 (38. h5!? g5 39. e4 $18 {[%csl Rf6,Rh6][%cal Yd5e3,Ye3f5]}) 38... g5 39. Nh5+ Kg6 40. f4 (40. Kh3! {was more accurate but I think that the result is already difficult to change in Black's favour.} Nd7 41. Rd6 Re7 42. Ng3 $18 {[%cal Yg3f5,Yh4h5]}) 40... gxf4+ (40... Ne4+! 41. Kf3 Re7 {[#] would have been a more stubborn defence and would force Carlsen to find the not so trivial 42.Re6!} 42. Re6! Nd2+ (42... Rxe6 43. f5+ Kh7 44. fxe6 Nd6 45. Nxf6+ Kg6 46. Ne4 Nc4 47. h5+ Kg7 48. Ng3 $18 {[%cal Yg3f5]}) 43. Ke2 Rxe6 44. f5+ Kf7 45. fxe6+ Kxe6 {[#]} 46. hxg5! (46. Kxd2? gxh4 $11 {[%cal Yb6b5] Black will create passed pawns on both wings getting enough counterplay to secure the draw.}) 46... Ne4 47. gxh6 Ng5 48. Nf4+ Kf7 49. Kd3 {is winning for White.}) 41. exf4 Kh7 42. f5 (42. Nxf6+? Rxf6! 43. Rc7+ Kg8 44. Rxa7 $11) 42... Kg8 {[#]} 43. Kf3! {White's king is the last piece to join the action. Carlsen will use it as an attacking piece with great success.} Nd7 44. Ke4 Kf8 45. Rc8+ Ke7 46. Kd5! $18 {Black's lack of mobility is overwhelming.} b5 (46... Rh7 47. Rc7 $18) 47. Rh8 Nb6+ 48. Kc6 Nc4 (48... Nd7 49. Kxb5 Ne5 50. Kc5 Nxg4 51. Ra8 $18) 49. Ra8 Ne5+ 50. Kc5 Nd7+ 51. Kxb5 Kd6 52. Rxa7 Rf8 53. Kb4 Nc5 54. Kc4 {A very mature game by the 17-year-old Carlsen who, effortlessly, outplayed his strong opponent .} 1-0

