1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Bf4 {The old and good London System, quite common these days.} e6 4. e3 Bd6 5. Bg3 c5 6. c3 Nc6 7. Nbd2 Bxg3?! {Honestly it is hard to understand why Black would play this move over the simple 7...O-O:} (7... O-O 8. Bd3 b6 {with many recent games in this position. More ambitious moves like e2, e4, e5 and the standard O-O have been played, generally with satisfactory results for White. For example:} 9. Qe2 Bb7 10. Rd1 Re8! (10... Qe7 11. e4 dxe4 12. Nxe4 Nxe4 13. Bxe4 cxd4 14. Bxd6 Qxd6 15. Nxd4 $14 {1-0 (30) Artemiev,V (2663)-Savchenko,B (2613) Sochi 2016; with a comfortable advantage.}) 11. e4 Be7! {the typical reaction against e5. Black is ready to play ...h5 if White goes for e5:} 12. e5 Nh5 13. a3 g6 14. Nf1 f5! 15. exf6 Bxf6 16. Ne3 e5! {Wesley So is a player who shows tremendous energy in every game; I can guess it feels like you are playing against a computer} 17. dxe5 Nxe5 18. Nxe5 Nxg3 19. hxg3 Rxe5 {with the bishop pair and the initiative, Black's position is already preferable} 20. Bc4 b5!? 21. Bxb5 Qb6 22. O-O Kg7! $44 {0-1 (34) Sedlak,N (2537)-So,W (2782) Baku 2016; threatening ...d4 and preparing strong pressure on the e-file, as soon as the bishop leaves b5.}) 8. hxg3 Qd6 9. Bb5 {White guarantees access to e5.} Bd7 10. Bxc6 Bxc6 11. Ne5 Qc7 (11... Nd7 {doesn't change the evaluation of the weird 7...xg3:} 12. f4?! {I didn't like this approach} (12. Ndf3!? Nxe5 (12... f6? 13. Ng6 Rg8 14. Rxh7 O-O-O 15. Qc2 $16 {0-1 (41) Umetsubo,C (2374)-Matsuura,E (2486) Blumenau 2015. White is a pawn up, but the knight on g6 is somewhat awkward.}) 13. Nxe5 {and White is simply better} O-O? 14. Qh5! h6 15. Ng4! $16 e5 16. Qxe5 (16. dxc5!? Qe6 17. Nxe5 Rae8 18. Nf3 $16) 16... Qg6 17. Qh5 Qc2? (17... Qe6!?) 18. Nf6+ Kh8 19. Qg5! Qxb2?? 20. Rxh6+ {1-0 (20) Bosque Ortega,X (2197)-Acebal Muniz,A (2140) Benidorm 2013}) 12... Nxe5 13. fxe5 Qe7 {I guess the main reason to prefer 12.df3 or even 12.xd7 is that now the d2-knight lacks a good square:} 14. Qg4 f5 15. Qh5+ Kd7 16. a4 Kc7 17. dxc5 Qxc5 18. Qg5 Qf8 (18... h6!? {was more precise} 19. Qxg7+ Bd7 20. Kf2 Rag8 21. Qf7 Rf8 22. Qg7 Rfg8 $11 {already guaranteeing the draw} 23. Qf6 Rxg3 24. Kxg3 Qxe3+ 25. Kh2) 19. Nf3 h6 20. Qg6 $11 {1-0 (42) Kamsky,G (2741)-Potkin,V (2647) Khanty-Mansiysk 2013; but this too looks acceptable. White ended up winning this blitz game.}) 12. Qf3 {Now White has easy play, and a clear plan with g4.} h6 13. Qf4 Qe7 14. g4 {It looks bad for Black already. Naturally White takes advantage of the fact that he hasn't castled yet and makes full use of his h1-rook in the play.} Nh7 15. Qg3 Rg8? (15... cxd4! {was the last chance for an acceptable position} 16. exd4 (16. cxd4 Rc8 17. O-O O-O $11) 16... O-O-O {looks dangerous, but maybe Black will survive after ...c7 and ...b8 (of course being careful not to run into xf7! taking advantage of the g3-queen pinning the c7-queen). Perhaps for that matter, ...e8 could be useful, if White isn't in time to open up the position with c1-c4.} 17. Rc1 (17. Nxc6 bxc6 18. Nb3 Qc7 19. Qd3 Nf6 20. Nc5 Nd7! {looks playable, after all}) 17... Qc7 {threatening f6} 18. c4 (18. O-O f6 (18... Be8? 19. c4! $16) 19. Nxc6 Qxg3 20. Nxa7+ Kb8 21. fxg3 Kxa7 $14 {and I would be very happy to play this after such a dangerous position from the opening}) 18... f6 19. Nxc6 (19. cxd5 Rxd5 20. Nxc6 Qxg3 21. fxg3 (21. Nxa7+ Kb8 22. fxg3 Kxa7 $14 {with excellent defensive chances for Black}) 21... bxc6 22. Rxc6+ Kb8 23. Nf3 Rc8 24. Rxc8+ Kxc8 25. Ke2 $14 {and Black is definitely in the game.}) 19... Qxg3 20. Nxa7+ Kb8 21. fxg3 Kxa7 $14 {similar to the lines mentioned above.}) 16. O-O $16 {Now Black will be defenceless against the further opening of the centre. It is important to notice that the h1-rook has already performed its duty from there, and can now join the central action. Also, the king finds a safe shelter on g1.} Nf6 17. Rac1 Rc8 18. c4! {Choosing a nice moment to open up the position. Nobody said it was easy to play against the world champion with the black pieces!} dxc4 (18... cxd4 {isolating d4 doesn't help} 19. exd4 dxc4 20. Ndxc4 Nd5 21. Rfe1 $16) 19. dxc5 Qxc5 20. Ndxc4 Ke7 21. b4! Qxb4 22. Nd3 {Now the queen can't move because of the deadly d6+.} Ne4 {Forced.} (22... Qb5 23. Qd6+ Ke8 24. Qa3! {threatening d6+.} Rd8 25. Nd6+ Rxd6 26. Qxd6 $18) 23. Nxb4 Nxg3 24. fxg3 Bb5? {The position was already difficult, but this loses right away.} (24... Bd7 {White's pawn structure is horrible, but a piece up should be enough to win the game without difficulties} 25. Ne5 $18) 25. Rxf7+! Kxf7 26. Nd6+ Ke7 27. Nxc8+ Kd7 28. Nxa7 $18 Ba4 29. Nd3 1-0

