1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Na6 8. Re1 Qe8 9. Bf1 c6 10. Rb1 Bg4 11. d5 c5 {The pawn structure produces a complicated positional battle. The pawn on c5 makes White's queenside attack less 'automatic' than in the main lines of the King's Indian, but Black's counterplay on the kingside is necessarily slower.} 12. Be2 Kh8 13. a3 Bd7 14. b4 b6 15. Bg5 Ng8 16. Nb5 f6 17. Bh4 (17. Nxd6? {just blunders a piece to} Qe7) 17... Qe7 18. Nd2 ({Over the next few moves} 18. Qa4 {can be met by} Nc7) 18... Nh6 {[%cal Yh6g4] Preventing the positional threat of e2-g4, and f7 is a useful square to cover d6.} 19. Nf1 Rfc8 20. Ne3 Nc7 21. bxc5 Nxb5 {In hindsight, I wonder whether this was a bad decision. I thought Black's rooks would be quite strong on the c-file. However, they lack entry points and White can continue preparing an assault on the queenside pawns.} (21... bxc5 {was a solid alternative, with a tense position.}) 22. cxb5 Rxc5 23. f3 (23. a4 {would allow Black an interesting possibility:} g5 24. Bg3 f5 25. exf5 Nxf5 {and with the e3 about to be exchanged, the rook on c5 becomes much more useful than it was in the game.}) 23... Rac8 24. Bd3! {A strong move, after which I understood that Black is going to face difficulties. There are three ideas behind this move. Firstly, it is harder to achieve .. 6-g5 and ...f6-f5. Secondly (although it never materialised in the game) the maneouvre e3-c2-b4-c6 becomes possible. Finally the rooks are restricted on the c-file.} Qf8 25. Bf2 f5 26. a4 R5c7 27. h3 ({If I remember correctly, I wanted to maintain the tension on f5/e4 to keep the possibility of answering} 27. Nc2 {with} g5 28. Nb4 g4 {with counterplay.}) 27... Bf6 28. Qd2! {The knight is still useful on e3.} (28. Nc2 fxe4 29. fxe4 g5 30. Ne3 (30. Be3 g4 31. Qd2 Nf7 32. Rf1 Bh4 33. hxg4 Bxg4 {looks unclear}) 30... g4!? 31. hxg4 Qg7 32. Nf5 Bxf5 33. gxf5 Ng4 {is a pawn sacrifice I considered during the game. All Black's pieces are active and White has no pressure left on the queenside.}) 28... Bg5 (28... f4 29. Nc2 g5 30. Nb4 Ng8 31. Nc6 (31. a5!?) 31... h5 32. a5 {isn't totally clear, but White's attack seems to be ahead.}) 29. a5 fxe4 30. fxe4 Nf7 {It wasn't easy to decide whether to leave a weakness on a7 or b6.} (30... bxa5 31. Qxa5 {is also unpleasant as the a7-pawn is very weak.} Qe7 {aims to exchange the dangerous bishop with ...g5-h4 but White maintains a little pressure. Still, maybe this was better than the way I played.}) 31. axb6 axb6 32. Qe2 Rb7 33. Nc4 Qd8 {Naturally, I wasn't delighted by this grim defence of the b-pawn, but there is nothing better.} 34. Rf1 Kg7 35. Kh1 (35. Bxb6 {would be too eager} Rxb6 36. Qf2 ({Or} 36. Rxf7+ Kxf7 37. Qf2+ Kg7 38. Nxb6 Bh4 (38... Rc3 {[%cal Yg5e3]} 39. Nxd7 Rxd3 40. Qf8+ Qxf8 41. Nxf8 Kxf8 42. b6 {looks drawn too}) 39. Qe3 Bg5 {promises at least a draw}) 36... Rxb5 37. Qxf7+ Kh6 38. Nxd6 Qb6+ 39. Kh1 Rxb1 40. Nxc8 (40. Qxd7 Rxf1+ 41. Bxf1 Rf8 42. Nf7+ Rxf7 43. Qxf7 Qf6 (43... Bf4!?) 44. Qxf6 Bxf6 {is very similar}) 40... Rxf1+ 41. Bxf1 Bxc8 42. Qf8+ Kh5 43. Qxc8 Qf2 {must be drawn.}) 35... Be8 36. Qb2 Nh6 37. Bxb6! {I must admit that I hadn't seen this move was possible right here. Under time pressure my last few moves had to be made rather quickly.} Qe7! {A good fighting decision. Clearly with an extra pawn White is doing very well, but the bishop is rather awkward to extricate from b6 so Black will continue to have fighting chances.} (37... Rxb6 38. Qf2 Bxb5 39. Nxb6! (39. Qxb6 Bxc4 40. Qxd8 Bxd8 41. Rb7+ Kg8 42. Rc1 Bxd3 43. Rxc8 Nf7 44. Rd7 {looked decisive to me during the game, but Black can keep the game going with} Kf8! 45. Rdxd8+ Nxd8 46. Rxd8+ Ke7 {and it is not at all obvious that White will be able to win.}) 39... Bxd3 40. Nxc8 Bxf1 41. Qa7+ Kh8 42. Rb7 Qf8 (42... Qg8 43. Nxd6 {[%cal Yb7b8] is also hopeless}) 43. Nxd6 {[%cal Yd6f7,Yb7b8,Ya7g1] and Black's position is hopeless.}) 38. Qf2 Rcb8 {[%csl Rb5][%cal Yb8b5] Somehow these miserable rooks are pulling their weight much more on the b-file than they ever were on the c-file!} 39. Rb3 Ng8 40. Be2?! {Black's potential counterplay with ...f6-h5 was the reason for this maneouvre, but the bishop was already doing a useful job supporting the b5-pawn.} (40. Ra1 {was probably stronger.} Nf6 41. Ra6 (41. Ba7 Rc8 {a reasonable defence} (41... Ra8!? {leads to similar lines to those with 41...c8} 42. b6 Nd7 43. Rbb1 (43. Na5 Rbxa7 44. bxa7 Nc5 45. Rba3 Rxa7 46. Nc6 Rxa3 47. Rxa3 Qf6) 43... Nc5) (41... Rxb5? 42. Bxb8 Rxb3 43. Bxd6 $18) 42. Ne3 {[%csl Rg7][%cal Ye3f5,Yf2a7,Ya1a7] intends b6 and d3-a6 but it's not so easy, e.g.} Ra8 43. b6 Nd7 44. Rbb1 (44. Ba6 Rbxa7 45. bxa7 Nc5 46. Rba3 Rxa7 {with clear compensation for the exchange}) 44... Nc5 45. Bc2 Rbxa7 46. bxa7 Rxa7 47. Rxa7 Qxa7 {and again it will certainly be hard for White to win despite the extra exchange.}) 41... Rc8!? {[%csl Rc4][%cal Yf6e4]} (41... Nh5 {is another way to fish for counterplay but I don't entirely trust it}) 42. Qe1 {[%cal Ye1e4]} Rcb8 {and it still isn't totally obvious how White should improve his position.}) 40... Nf6 41. Bf3 {Now we had passed move 40 I had a while to try to figure out how to defend.} Rxb6!? {This was certainly a good practical try, but since I can't see any real improvement for Black in the game, it might have been better to keep grovelling.} (41... Nd7 42. Ba7 Ra8 43. b6 {this looks very bad but the bishop is still quite awkward on a7 so Black has good chances to sacrifice an exchange, for example.} Nc5 44. Rbb1 (44. Ra3?! Bb5 45. Qc2 (45. Rc3 Na4! 46. Rc2 Qd8 {and the b6-pawn seems to drop off in a couple of moves}) 45... Bxc4 46. Qxc4 Rxb6 47. Rfa1 {and now Black has a few different approaches to try to save the position, although it isn't an easy choice.} Rb7 (47... Rxa7 48. Rxa7 Rb1+!? (48... Rb7) 49. Rxb1 (49. Kh2 Rxa1 50. Rxe7+ Bxe7 {yet again gives good drawing chances}) 49... Qxa7 {etc.}) 48. Bxc5 Rc8 49. Bxd6 Rxc4 50. Bxe7 Bxe7 51. Ra7 Rxa7 52. Rxa7 Kf6 {with some chances to hold}) 44... h5 {and it still isn't obvious how White should move forward.} (44... Rbxa7? 45. bxa7 Rxa7 46. Rb6 {causes a big problem just here, because} Rd7 47. Nxd6 Rxd6 48. Qxc5 {wins}) 45. Na5 {it might be better to delay this} Rbxa7 46. bxa7 Qxa7 47. Nc4 Qc7 {with a resilient position.}) 42. Nxb6 Qc7 43. h4! {Undermining the defence of f6.} (43. Na4 Rxb5 44. Rxb5 Bxb5 45. Ra1 {is exactly what I was hoping for. Black's active minor pieces offer good compensation. In practice it would be extremely difficult for White to win.}) 43... Bh6! (43... Bf4 44. g3 Rxb6 (44... Bxg3 {I didn't consider, but} 45. Qxg3 Qxb6 46. Be2 {and White's b-pawn combined with pressure on the f-file is likely to be decisive.}) 45. gxf4 exf4 46. Be2 {should win quite easily for White}) 44. Na4! {Keeping the e2-b5 diagonal open...} (44. Be2 Bf4! {is the nice point of g5-h6. Opening the f-file removes the e-pawn's defence, so Black is threatening f6xe4.}) (44. Nc4 Rxb5! {offers Black nice compensation again.} (44... Qxc4? 45. Be2! Qxb3 46. Qxf6+ Kg8 47. Qe6+ Kg7 48. Qxd6 Bf4 49. Qc7+! $16)) 44... Rxb5 45. Be2! (45. Rxb5 Bxb5 46. Be2 Bf4! {is a key resource as usual. I saw that after} 47. Bxb5 Nxe4 48. Qe1 (48. Qb6? {loses material} Ng3+ 49. Kg1 Qxb6+ 50. Nxb6 Be3+ 51. Kh2 Nxf1+ 52. Bxf1 Bxb6 $11) 48... Ng3+ (48... Qe7!? {isn't a bad idea either}) 49. Kg1 Nxf1 (49... Qa7+!? 50. Rf2 Be3 {also gives practical chances}) 50. Kxf1 {and Black has reasonable compensation for the piece. White's king is never going to be safe. In fact} Qf7!? {[%csl Rd5][%cal Yf4g3] wins a second pawn immediately, though White can try to win the endgame after} 51. Qe4 Bg3+ 52. Qf3 Qxf3+ 53. gxf3 Bxh4) 45... Rxb3 46. Qxf6+ Kg8 47. Nc5! {A crucial (and very pretty) tactical shot. It didn't quite knock me off my chair as I had seen it somewhere betwen playing 41...xb6 and here (I forget exactly where) but my moves had been forced up to here. Now I faced a difficult decision about where to place the rook.} (47. Bg4 Bg7 48. Qe6+ Bf7 {and the defence holds. White must play} 49. Qxf7+ Qxf7 50. Be6 Qxe6 51. dxe6 Rb8 {with a probable draw}) 47... Rg3!? {I arrived at this odd looking move by the need to prevent the e2-g4-e6 idea, as well as the ideas with f6-d8-g5.} (47... Rc3 {is the obvious candidate, hitting the knight, but it's not good enough.} 48. Ne6 (48. Bg4 {also seems to be good enough as after} Bg7 49. Ne6 Bxf6 50. Nxc7 Rxc7 51. Rxf6 {the d-pawn is falling.} g5!? (51... h5 52. Be2! Rd7 53. Bb5 Rf7 54. Rxg6+ Kh7 55. Bxe8 Kxg6 56. Bxf7+ Kxf7 {is a hopeless pawn ending for Black}) (51... Bd7 52. Bf3 Ra7 53. Rxd6 Kf7 $16 {may be toughest}) 52. Rxd6 Bg6 53. Rc6 $18) 48... Qf7 49. Qd8 Qd7 50. Qg5! Bg7 (50... Qxe6 51. Qxh6 Qe7 52. Bg4 Bf7 53. Be6! Bxe6 54. dxe6 {[%cal Yf1f7] and Black can resign}) 51. Nxg7 Qxg7 (51... Kxg7 52. Qf6+ $18) 52. Qd8 (52. Bg4 Rc7 53. Qd8 Qe7 54. Qxe7 Rxe7 55. Rf6 {should also win.}) 52... Qd7 53. Qf6 Bf7 (53... h5 54. Qf8+ Kh7 55. Rf6 {should also win}) 54. Bg4 Qe8 55. Qxd6 {White wins.}) (47... Rb2 {was interesting but also insufficient.} 48. Bg4! (48. Ne6 Qf7 49. Qd8 Qd7 50. Qg5 Bg7 51. Nxg7 (51. Bg4 h6! {is OK for Black}) 51... Qxg7 52. Bg4 (52. Qd8?! Qd7 53. Qf6 h5 {seems to be fine.}) 52... Rb8 {and with d8 covered, nothing disastrous is happening immediately. After} 53. Be6+ Kh8 {White still has a positional advantage and practical chances but there's no concrete follow-up. Black will continue with h6 and h7.}) 48... Bg7 49. Ne6 Qf7 50. Nxg7 Qxf6 (50... Qxg7? 51. Be6+ Kh8 52. Qd8 {wins}) 51. Rxf6 Kxg7 52. Rxd6 $16 {and White should probably win this endgame.}) ({Hopeless is} 47... Qxc5?? 48. Qe6+ Kg7 49. Qe7+ Kg8 50. Qxe8+ Kg7 51. Rf7#) (47... dxc5?? {is no better} 48. Bc4! {[%cal Yd5d6] with too many threats.}) 48. Ne6 (48. h5!? {is a odd looking move pointed out by the engines but it clears the h4-square for the queen.} Bf4 49. Ne6 Qf7 50. Qd8 Qd7 51. Qh4 g5 {other moves just lose a bit more slowly} 52. Qxg3! Bxg3 53. Rf8#) 48... Qf7 49. Qxf7+ (49. Qd8 Qd7 {leads nowhere.}) 49... Bxf7 50. Rb1! {Even without queens, the attack goes on.} Be8 (50... Bxe6 51. dxe6 {and the pawn cannot actually be stopped.} Kf8 52. Rb7 $18 {[%cal Ye2b5,Ye6e7]}) 51. Bf3 {This is the downside to the rook being on g3. It's immobilised by the bishop and can't contribute to the defence.} Kf7 52. Rb7+ Kf6 53. Rxh7 Bf4 {This position was likely to arise after 47...g3 and I retained hopes of saving the game here. The rook is trapped on g3 but ...g6-g5 may extricate it at some point and White's winning plan isn't totally obvious. Unfortunately Magnus found an elegant way to finish things off.} (53... Be3 54. Kh2 Bf2 55. Rb7 {also wins.}) 54. Nxf4 (54. Ng5 Rxg5! (54... Bxg5 55. hxg5+ Rxg5 56. g4) 55. hxg5+ Bxg5) 54... exf4 55. e5+! dxe5 (55... Kxe5 56. Re7+ $18) 56. d6 {There's no way to stop this pawn.} e4 (56... g5 57. hxg5+ Kxg5 58. d7 Bxd7 59. Rxd7 Kf5 {If Black could achieve ...e5-e4 here it might be difficult for White to win the game. But after} 60. Rf7+ Ke6 61. Rf8 {[%cal Yf8e8] as Magnus pointed out, Black will never get e5-e4 in, so White will gradually bring up his king and win the pawns.}) (56... Kf5!? {is tricky} 57. Rf7+! {is the way to win.} (57. d7? Bxd7 58. Rxd7 e4 59. Rd5+ Ke6 60. Bxe4 Re3 61. Rg5 Rxe4 62. Rxg6+ Kf5 {and Black should hold}) 57... Bxf7 (57... Ke6 58. Re7+ $18) 58. d7 e4 59. Be2 f3 (59... Re3 60. d8=Q Rxe2 61. Qg5+ Ke6 62. Qg4+ $18) 60. gxf3 exf3 61. Bd3+ Ke5 62. d8=Q $18) (56... Ke6? 57. Re7+ $18) 57. Bxe4 Re3 58. Bd5! Kf5 (58... Re5 59. d7! Bxd7 60. Rf7# {would have been a pretty finale.}) 59. Kh2 (59. d7 Bxd7 60. Rxd7 Kg4 61. Rd6 {was also sufficient.}) 59... Re5 60. Bf3 Kf6 61. d7 1-0

