1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 a6 5. Nf3 Bf5 6. Ne5 ({Another critical test for Black's quite dubious opening set-up is} 6. Qb3 b5 7. cxd5 cxd5 8. a4 b4 9. Qxb4 Nc6 10. Qc5 Na5 11. Bxa6 Rxa6 12. Qb5+ Rc6 13. Ne5 Bd7 14. Nxd7 Nxd7 15. Bd2 Rc8 16. Nxd5 Nc4 17. Rc1 Nd6 18. Rxc8 Qxc8 19. Qa6! Qxa6 20. Nc7+ Kd8 21. Nxa6 {The game 1-0 Radziewicz,I (2421)-Kononenko,T (2417)/Ekaterinburg 2006 (48) showed that Black is struggling in this endgame.}) 6... h6 (6... e6 {runs into} 7. g4 Bg6 8. h4 $16) ({Perhaps instead Black should develop a piece with} 6... Nbd7 {although his position looks pretty shaky anyway, in spite of the draw in the following game:} 7. Qb3 Qc7 8. cxd5 Nxe5 9. dxe5 Nxd5 10. Nxd5 cxd5 11. Bd2 e6 12. Rc1 Qd7 13. Be2 (13. Qb6 Rc8 14. Rxc8+ Qxc8 15. Be2 Bc5!) 13... Be7 14. Qb6 Bd8 (14... Rc8 15. Rxc8+ Qxc8 16. O-O $36) 15. Qb4 Be7 16. Qa5 Rc8 (16... Bd8 17. Qa3 Be7 18. Bb4 Bxb4+ 19. Qxb4 $16) 17. Rxc8+ Qxc8 18. O-O Bd8 19. Qa3 f6 20. Rc1 Qd7 21. exf6 gxf6 $14 22. Ba5 Kf7 23. Bxd8 {½-½ Gelfand,B (2723)-Svidler,P (2765)/Monte Carlo (rapid) 2006}) 7. Qb3 Ra7 (7... b5 8. cxd5 cxd5 9. a4 $16) 8. cxd5 cxd5 9. Bd2 e6 10. Rc1 Nbd7 (10... Bd6 11. Qa4+ Nbd7 12. Nb5 $18) (10... Be7 11. Qa4+ Nbd7 12. Nb5 $18) 11. Bb5 (11. Nxd7 Nxd7 12. Ne2 Nb8 13. Nf4 Bd6 14. Bb4 Bxb4+ 15. Qxb4 Nc6 16. Qc5 Ra8 $11 {0-1 Radjabov,T (2610)-Morozevich,A (2716)/Moscow 2002 (34)}) (11. Na4 Be7 (11... Nxe5 12. dxe5 Nd7 13. Nb6! Nxb6 (13... Qxb6 14. Rc8+ Ke7 15. Bb4+ $18) 14. Ba5 $16 {1-0 Kachiani Gersinska,K (2444)-Ionescu Brandis,I (2366)/Istanbul 2003 (36)}) (11... b5 12. Nc6! bxa4 13. Qd1 Qb6 14. Nxa7 Qxa7 15. Rc8+ Ke7 16. Bb4+ $18)) (11. Qa4 Ra8! {brings White nothing.}) 11... Bd6 (11... Be7 12. Bxd7+ Nxd7 13. Nxd7 Qxd7 14. Na4 Qb5 15. Nb6 Qxb3 16. axb3 a5 17. Nc8 Ra6 18. Nxe7 Kxe7 19. Rc7+ Kf6 20. Rxb7 a4 21. bxa4 Rxa4 22. Ke2 $16 {1-0 Pert,N (2503)-Tippleston,C (2203)/Swansea 2006 (50)}) 12. Nxd7 Nxd7 13. Bxd7+ Qxd7? $146 {A bad novelty. It remains a mystery whether Carlsen tries to improve over the existing theory, or he simply didn't know the variation. Considering the fact that the theory of this line is based on a game of PH Nielsen, who is Carlsen's coach, it would be very surprising for Magnus to know nothing about it. In any case 12...xd7 is a result of a superficial homework, since now White achieves a large advantage by force.} (13... Kxd7 14. Na4 b5 15. Nc5+ Ke7 {should be better for White, but things aren't simple and this is definitely preferable to 12...xd7.} 16. f3 (16. a4!? Qb8 17. a5! Rc8 18. Bb4 Kf8 19. Kd2 Kg8 20. Nd3 Bxb4+ 21. Nxb4 Rxc1 22. Rxc1 Qxh2 23. e4! Bxe4 24. Qg3 Qh5 25. f3 Bf5 26. Qf4 $44 {[%csl Ra6] 1-0 Vaganian,R (2653)-Frolov,D (2418)/Togliatti 2003 (36)}) 16... Re8 17. e4 dxe4 18. fxe4 Bg6 19. e5 Bxc5 20. Bb4 Kd7 21. Bxc5 Qh4+ 22. g3 Qe4+ 23. Kf2 Qf5+ 24. Qf3 {½-½ Nielsen,P (2625)-Hansen,S (2537)/Malmo 2003. It would have been interesting to see where Ponomariov would have improved White's play here.}) 14. Na4! O-O (14... Bc7 15. Nb6 Qd8 16. Qa4+ Ke7 17. Ba5 Bxb6 18. Qb4+ Qd6 19. Qxb6 Qxb6 20. Bxb6 Raa8 21. Rc7+ Kf6 22. Kd2 {The endgame looks very difficult for Black:} Rhc8 23. Rhc1 Rxc7 24. Rxc7 Rb8 25. b4 (25. Ba7 Re8! 26. Rxb7 Re7 $11) (25. Kc3!? $16 {[%cal Gc3b4,Gb4a5,Yb6a7,Ra2a4,Ga5b6]}) 25... g5 26. a4 h5 27. Kc3 {leads to a probably lost endgame for Black:} (27. a5 g4 28. b5 axb5 29. Ba7 Ra8! (29... Re8? 30. Rxb7 Re7 31. a6 $18) 30. Rxb7 b4) 27... Be4 28. f3 Bf5 29. b5 axb5 30. axb5 Ra8 31. Rxb7 {and White should be winning.}) (14... b5 15. Nb6 $18 {[%cal Gc1c8]}) 15. Nb6 Qd8 16. Nc8! {Possibly Magnus simply missed this. White wins material.} ({After} 16. O-O a5! (16... Bc7 17. Ba5 Qd6 18. g3 {still requires accuracy from Black} (18. Rc3 {would be a mistake due to the unexpected} Qxh2# {A very rare mate for the Slav Defense indeed...})) 17. Nc8 (17. Qb5 Bd3! 18. Qxd3 Qxb6 $11) (17. h3 Bc7 $15) 17... Ra8 18. Nxd6 Qxd6 {White's advantage disappears}) 16... Qxc8 (16... Ra8 17. Nxd6 Qxd6 18. Bb4 $18) 17. Rxc8 Rxc8 18. Qb6! ({The quiet} 18. O-O Rc6 19. Rc1 Rxc1+ 20. Bxc1 b5 {[%cal Ga7c7] allows Black to activate the pieces and reach a perfectly playable position.}) 18... Bb8 19. O-O Kh7 20. Rc1 $18 {Ponomariov's precise play leaves Black with no hopes to survive.} Bc2 21. Ba5 Kg6 (21... Rc6 22. Qd8 Ra8 23. Qd7 $18 {[%csl Rb7,Rf7]}) 22. Qb4 Rc4 (22... b5 23. Qd2 $18) 23. Qd2 Bf5 24. Rxc4 dxc4 25. Bb6 Ra8 26. Qe2 e5 ({Black cannot even take his pieces out, since} 26... Bd6 27. e4 {loses the bishop.}) 27. Qxc4 exd4 (27... Bd6 28. Qd5 $18) 28. Qxd4 f6 29. f4 Bb1 30. Qd5 1-0

