1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. a4 {This is a rare line of the Classical system. White starts an offensive on the queenside without weakening the a1-h8 diagonal, which seems to be logical comparing to the Bayonet attack. However the line also has a drawback - White weakens the b4-square, which Black exploits by means of} a5 10. b3!? {[%cal Gb3b4,Gc1a3] Preparing a3 followed by b4. Curiously this idea was almost never being tested before.} ({Earlier White played mostly} 10. Ne1 Nd7 11. Nd3 f5 12. exf5 gxf5 13. Ra3 Ng6 14. Kh1 Rf7 15. f4 b6 16. Nb5 Nf6 17. fxe5 dxe5 18. Nf2 Bf8 19. Rb3 Bd7 20. Bg5 Bc5 21. Qb1 e4 22. Nh3 Be7 23. Qc1 $16 {Larsen,B-Gabriel,C, Bad Homburg 1998/CBM 66[Huzman].}) ({One recent game saw the immediate} 10. Ra3 {, which doesn't look too promising.} Nd7 11. Nh4 Nc5 12. g3 f5 13. exf5 Nxf5 14. Nxf5 Bxf5 $15 {0-1 Gonzalez Zamora,J-Dominguez Aguilar,G/Mexico City MEX 2006(49). and Black obtained a good position.}) 10... Nd7 11. Ba3 {Magnus is consistent in his plans.} ({A mix of two plans in the following game doesn't look convincing.} 11. Ne1 Nc5 12. Ba3 b6 13. b4 axb4 14. Bxb4 f5 15. f3 Bd7 16. a5 Na6 17. Ba3 bxa5 18. c5 (18. Nd3!? {[%cal Gc4c5] preparing c5.}) 18... Nxc5 19. Bxc5 dxc5 20. d6 cxd6 21. Qxd6 Bc6 $17 {Lehikoinen,P-Virtanen,A/Finland 2003. and White doesn't have sufficient compensation for the sacrificed material.} (21... Rf6!?)) 11... Bh6 {[%csl Rg5][%cal Gf7f5] This idea is also typical for the Bayonet attack. Black takes control of the g5-square, which White could exploit in case of the immediate f5 playing g5, like in the game below.} ({In a fresh game Black tested} 11... f5?! 12. Ng5! Nf6 (12... Nc5!?) 13. c5! h6 14. cxd6 cxd6 15. Ne6 Bxe6 16. dxe6 $16 Nxe4 17. Nxe4 fxe4 18. Qxd6 Nf5 19. Qxd8 Rfxd8 20. e7 Rd7 21. Bb5 $18 {1-0 Oosterom,E-Nijs,N/Vlissingen NED 2006.}) 12. b4 axb4 13. Bxb4 {It's interesting that the same position arises in the Bayonet attack (9.b4) with an extra tempo since here White played first b3 and only then b4. So either Carlsen is so sure in this position even a tempo down or he's unfamiliar with this line of the Bayonet attack. The latter seems to me less likely.} f5 14. Nd2 {White supports the centre and sometimes brings the knight to b3 to prepare c5.} Kh8 {A useful prophylactic move. Black takes his king away from the a2-g8 diagonal to a safer square in the corner of the board.} 15. a5 Rf7 {For those who are familiar with the similar line of the Bayonet attack all these ideas are well known. Black makes another useful move and leaves the knight on d7 for one more move in order to prevent c5.} 16. Nb5 {White not only activates his knight and creates some pressure on the queenside, but also frees the c3-square for the dark-squared bishop.} Nf6 {Black finds no useful moves anymore and attacks the centre. The move however has a serious drawback - Black allows c5.} 17. c5 dxc5 18. Bc3!? {[%csl Re5] Trying to exploit the weakness of the e5-pawn.This retreat in the Bayonet attack is a speciality of Vladimir Kramnik. Carlsen shows that he's familiar with this idea and thus refutes my earlier assumption that he's unfamiliar with this line of the Bayonet attack.} ({In case of} 18. Bxc5 {White has to be ready to sacrifice a piece after} Bxd2 19. Qxd2 Nxe4 20. Qb2! {[%csl Re5] It's very difficult to find this move.} Nxc5 21. Qxe5+ Kg8 22. Nxc7 {and it turns out that White is slightly better. I.e.} Rb8 ({or} 22... Nd7 23. Qc3 Ra7 24. Rfd1 Nf6 25. d6 Nc6 26. Bc4 $16) 23. d6 (23. Rfb1!?) 23... Ne4 24. dxe7 Qxe7 25. Qxe7 Rxe7 26. Bc4+ Kg7 27. Nd5 $14) 18... c6 19. dxc6 bxc6?! {After this inaccurate move Black will be unable to protect the e5-pawn and thus White obtains a serious initiative.} (19... Nxc6! {was necessary and after} 20. Nc4 Rd7 21. Qc2 {Black sacrifices an exchange and gets serious compensation.} Nxe4 22. Nb6 Rb8 23. Nxd7 Bxd7 {[%cal Gc6d4] with d4 to follow. The resulting position is very complicated and both sides have their chances.}) 20. Na3 {[%cal Ga3c4] From here the knight is ready to jump to c4.} fxe4?! {Black wants the d5-square for his knights. However, Black had to distract the white pieces from the e5-pawn.} (20... Bxd2! {I understand that exchanging the dark-squared bishop looks bad, but in this case it seems to be playable and even necessary. Sometimes concrete approach prevails on positional aspects of the position and this seems to be the case.} 21. Nc4 {[%csl Rd8] Exploiting the pin along the d-file.} (21. Bxd2 Nxe4 22. Bc3! Qxd1 23. Bxe5+ Kg8 24. Rfxd1 Rxa5 25. f3 Ng5 26. Nc4 Rxa1 27. Rxa1 Nd5 28. Nd6 Rf8 29. Ra7 Ne6 {and Black should be able to hold the position.}) 21... Nxe4 22. Bxe5+ Kg8 23. f3 Qd5! 24. fxe4 Be3+ 25. Nxe3 Qxe5 26. Ng4!! fxg4 27. Qd8+ Kg7 28. Rxf7+ Kxf7 29. Rf1+ Nf5 30. Bc4+ Kg7 31. Qg8+ Kh6 32. exf5 gxf5 (32... Ba6? 33. Qxa8 Bxc4 34. Qf8+ Kg5 35. fxg6! $18) 33. Kh1 $14 (33. Qf8+ Kg5 34. Kh1 Ba6! 35. Qxa8 Bxc4 36. Rg1 Bd5 37. Qd8+ Kg6 38. a6 Bxg2+! 39. Rxg2 Qe1+ 40. Rg1 Qe4+ 41. Rg2 $11)) 21. Nac4! Ned5 22. Bxe5 Bg7 (22... Kg8!?) 23. Nd6 Re7 24. N2c4 {White is only a pawn down, but all his minor pieces are nicely situated.} Be6? {This is already a serious mistake. The a-pawn shouldn't be left unattended.} 25. a6! {[%csl Ga6] The a-pawn goes forward immediatly.} (25. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b2] with the idea of b2 also looked strong.}) 25... Nb4!? (25... Rea7? {would be strongly met by} 26. Na5! {[%csl Rc6]}) 26. Qc1 Nd3?! ({White's idea behind 26.Qc1 can be seen in the following line.} 26... Rxa6 27. Rxa6 Nxa6 28. Qa1! {with a double attack on a6 and f6.} Ra7 29. f3! e3 30. Nxe3 $16 {and White is clearly better.}) ({Probably Morozevich should have tried} 26... Nxa6 27. Qf4 Rea7) 27. Bxd3 exd3 28. Qc3?! {This seems to be an inaccuracy.} (28. Rd1!? {would be more precise.}) 28... Bxc4! 29. Qxc4 Qg8! {Covering the f7-square.} 30. Qxc5 d2 (30... Rxe5 {would probably lead to a position similar to the game.} 31. Qxe5 Nd5 {[%csl Ra1,Re5]} 32. Qe1 Bxa1 33. Qxa1+ Qg7 34. Qa4 Qc7 35. Qd4+ Kg8 36. Ne4 Rxa6 37. Qxd3 $14 {[%csl Rg8] and White is better thanks to the vulnerability of the black king.}) 31. Rad1?! (31. Nc4! {deserved serious attention.}) 31... Rxa6?! {Returning the favour.} (31... Qd5! {was much better. However it was very difficult to spot the following idea.} 32. Qc3 Rxe5! 33. Qxe5 Rxa6! {and it turns out that White can't win the d2-pawn.} 34. Qxd5 cxd5 35. Nf7+ Kg8 36. Ng5 Ra2 $11 {[%csl Gd2][%cal Gg7h6] with the idea of h6 and Black looks ok.}) 32. Rxd2 $16 {Now the unsecure position of the black king and activity of white pieces yield White chances for a win.} Nd5 33. Bxg7+ Rxg7?! (33... Kxg7 34. Qd4+ Nf6 {was better.}) 34. h3?! {Now it's White's turn to return a favour.} ({After} 34. Re2! {it would be very difficult for Black to defend his king.} Re7 (34... Nc7!? 35. Qe5 Ra8 $16 {is the only defense.}) 35. Rxe7 Nxe7 36. Ne8!! {[%csl Rh8]} Qxe8 37. Qc3+ Kg8 38. Qc4+ $18) 34... Qe6! 35. Rb1! h6! {Black finds only moves to stay in the game.} 36. Qc4! Rb6 ({After} 36... Rga7! {White would have to find} 37. Rdb2!! {and eventually a position with ++1 extra pawn on the kingside arises as well as it could happen in the game.} Ra1 (37... Qxd6 38. Rb8+ Kg7 39. R1b7+ Kf6 (39... Ne7 40. Rg8+! Kf6 41. Rf8+! {and mate on the next move.}) 40. Qd4+ Kg5 41. Rxa7 Rxa7 42. Qxa7 $18) 38. Qxc6 Rc7 39. Qe8+ Qxe8 40. Nxe8 Rc1+ 41. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 42. Kh2 $16) 37. Rxb6 Qe1+ 38. Kh2 Nxb6? {The decisive mistake.} (38... Qxd2 {was necessary.} 39. Rxc6 Qf4+ 40. Qxf4 Nxf4 $16 {and Black still maintains chances for a draw.}) 39. Qf4! {[%cal Gf4f8]} Nd5? {Loses on the spot, however Black's position was already hopeless.} (39... Qe7 {against f8 was Black's best defense. Still after} 40. Qxh6+ Kg8 41. Qf4 Nd5 42. Qg3 $18 {White should be able to convert his extra pawn in a win.}) 40. Rxd5! cxd5 41. Qf8+ Kh7 (41... Rg8 42. Qxh6#) 42. Ne8 {[%cal Ge8f6] A nice mating construction. Black can't prevent huge material losses and so he resigned. The line introduced by Carlsen deserves further tests.} 1-0

