1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Kh1 {Earlier in the tournament Carlsen introduced a new idea in the side line with 9.a4 against Morozevich, this time he comes with an even more rare continuation, which was a speciality of Miles.} Nd7 {[%cal Gd7c5,Gf7f5] Black prepares f5 and brings the knight to c5. What else can be more typical for the KID?} (9... Kh8) 10. g4!? Kh8 {Black frees the g8-square for the knight and moves the king away from the g-file and thus prepares f5.} (10... Nc5 {usually leads to transposition after} 11. Rg1 a5 12. Be3 Kh8) 11. Rg1 {White also prepares himself for opening of the g-file.} a5 {Securing the outpost of the knight on c5.} 12. Be3 Nc5 ({Black also tested} 12... b6?! {preparing c5.} 13. Qd2 (13. a3! {with the idea to meet} Nc5 {by means of} 14. b4 {would be the most logical refutation of Black's slow play.}) 13... Nc5 14. Rg3 Bd7 15. Rag1 $14 {1-0 Lafuente,P-Fiorito,F/Mar del Plata 2001/CBM 081 ext (28)}) (12... Ng8 13. Qd2 Nc5 14. Ne1 f5 15. f3 fxg4 16. fxg4 $14 b6? 17. g5 $16 {1-0 Miles,A-Becerra Rivera,J, Capablanca mem 34th 1999/ CBM71 [Huzman].}) 13. Rc1 {This turns out to be a novelty. Frankly speaking it's not easy to understand the idea behind this move. Probably White decided to keep all his plans open and made a useful move, taking his rook away from the a1-h8 diagonal.} ({Earlier White tested} 13. Qd2 Ng8 14. Rg3 b6 15. Rag1 {As you can see doubling of rooks along the g-file is a typical idea in this line, which is aimed against f5.} Nf6 16. Qc2 Bd7 17. Nd2 (17. Ne1!? {[%cal Ge1d3] Heading to d3.}) 17... c6 18. f3 (18. dxc6!? Bxc6 19. Rh3 $14) 18... Qc8 19. Qd1 Qb7 20. Qf1 Rab8 21. Qf2 Kg8 22. Rh3 Rfe8 23. Qh4 h5 24. dxc6 Qxc6 25. Nd5! $16 {Tosic,M-Kolev,A/Vrnjacka Banja 1990. with a clear edge for White.}) (13. Ne1 f5 14. f3 fxe4 15. fxe4 c6 16. Nd3 cxd5 17. cxd5 b6 18. Nxc5 bxc5 19. Qd2 Rb8 20. Raf1 Bd7 21. Rxf8+ Qxf8 22. Rf1 Qc8 23. h3 $16 {Miles,A-Gheorghiu,F/Crans Montana 2001. and all Black's minor pieces are out of play.} (23. g5!? $16)) (13. a3 {[%cal Gb2b4] preparing b4 deserved serious attention. Now Black has either play} f5 {immediately.} ({or} 13... a4 {sacrificing a pawn and only after} 14. Bxc5 dxc5 15. Nxa4 b6 {[%cal Gf7f5] preparing f5.}) 14. gxf5 gxf5 15. Bxc5 dxc5 16. Bd3 $14 {with a slightly better position for White.}) 13... Bd7 14. b3 {White is ready to meet a4 with b4. The plan with a3 and b4 is not very dangerous for Black right now as a3 will be always met by means of a4 b4 b3.} Ng8 15. Nd2 {[%csl Ye4][%cal Ga2a3,Gb3b4,Gd2e4] Getting ready for f5 and at the same time covering the b3-square and thus preparing a3 and b4.} (15. a3 a4 16. b4 Nb3 17. Rb1 {would have been a double-edged idea as the knight is close to the d4-square and Black may always try to open the position by means of f5.}) 15... f6! {[%cal Gg7h6] Black prepares the exchange of the dark-squared bishops by means of h6 and so the text is made in order to prevent g5.} 16. h4 f5 {[%csl Rh4] The waste of a tempo is not essential as h4 weakened the king.} ({Now} 16... Bh6 {can be met by means of} 17. g5) 17. g5 {White has to close the position this way since the h4-pawn was hanging.} Nxe4!? {Otherwise White plays f3 and stabilizes the position in the centre.} 18. Ndxe4 fxe4 19. Nxe4 Ne7 {[%csl Re3,Rh4][%cal Ge7f5] The knight comes to f5.} 20. Bg4 {This move connected with the following exchange is a risky idea. Probably all of this was caused by White's somewhat bit careless play in the early stages of the game.} (20. h5!? Nf5 21. Bd3 {deserved attention.}) 20... Nf5 21. Bxf5 Bxf5 {This allows White to consolidate the position.} (21... gxf5!? {looks like an interesting attempt to play for a win.} 22. Qh5! (22. Nc3 f4 23. Bd2 f3! 24. Rg3 h5! 25. Rxf3 (25. gxh6? Qxh4+ 26. Kg1 Bxh6 $19) 25... Bg4 26. Rxf8+ Qxf8 $36 {with a strong initiative.}) (22. Nf6? f4! 23. Bd2 Bxf6 24. gxf6 Qxf6 $19) 22... f4! (22... fxe4?? 23. g6 $18) (22... Be8 23. g6 Bxg6 24. Rxg6 fxe4 25. Rg2 (25. Rcg1 Qe8!) (25. Rg3 Rf3!) 25... Qf6 26. Rcg1 Rf7 27. Rg3! {with the idea of g4 looks good for White.}) 23. Bd2 Bf5 24. Qf3 (24. Rce1!? Bg6 25. Qf3 Qe8) 24... Qe8 {[%cal Gf5g6,Ga5a4,Gb7b5] with a complicated position in which Black looks at least not worse.}) 22. f3 Qd7 23. Rg3! {[%csl Rh3] Covering the h3-square.} Rf7 (23... Bxe4 24. fxe4 Rf4 {is hardly a good idea to play for a win.} 25. Bxf4 exf4 26. Qg4 Qe7 27. Rf3 Be5 28. Rcf1 $16 {and then h3 with h5 to follow.}) 24. Kg2 b6 25. Qe2 Raf8 26. Rh1 Kg8 27. a3 Rb8 {[%cal Gb6b5] Preparing b5. So} 28. a4 Rbf8 29. Qd1 Rb8 30. Qd3 {Neither side can make progress and so a draw was agreed. An interesting game, which however leaves many questions open. Hopefully we'll see more games on the subject.} 1/2-1/2

