1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 g6 5. Nf3 Bg7 6. Be2 O-O 7. O-O a6 {A useful move asking White to declare his intentions.} 8. b3 ({I myself preferred} 8. Bd2 {against S.Conquest.}) 8... Ne4 9. Bb2 Nxc3 10. Bxc3 Bg4 11. h3 Bxf3 12. Bxf3 e6 {Very solid. Black has got rid of two pairs of "less necessary" pieces, now he has a good bishop and no weaknesses.} 13. Qd2!? {An interesting plan. White puts his bishop on a5 in order to provoke the weakening ...b7-b6.} (13. Qc2 Nd7 14. e4 dxe4 15. Qxe4 Rc8 16. Rfd1 Nf6 17. Qe3 Re8 18. Rd3 Re7 19. Rad1 Ne8? (19... Rd7 $142) 20. Bb4 Nd6 21. Qf4 Rd7 22. d5! cxd5 (22... e5 23. dxc6 exf4 24. cxd7 Qxd7 25. Rxd6 $16) 23. Bxd5! Nf5 24. Bxe6! Rxd3 25. Rxd3 Qxd3 26. Bxc8 $18 {1-0 Riazantsev,A-Sergienko,S/Sochi 2005/CBM 107 (31)}) 13... Re8 ({In case of} 13... a5 14. a4 {Black's a5 pawn will require permanent attention} (14. e4 $143 dxe4 15. Bxe4 a4)) 14. Ba5! Qe7 15. Rac1 Nd7 16. Qb4 {The queen exchange looks logical to make use of White's bishop pair but Black finds a good reply.} Qxb4 17. Bxb4 a5 18. Bd2 (18. Bd6 Bf8!?) 18... dxc4!? 19. bxc4 e5 20. d5?! (20. Bc3! {was the only way to fight for an edge.}) 20... e4 21. Be2 Red8 (21... cxd5 22. cxd5 Re5 {was a simpler way to equality, e.g.} 23. d6 (23. Bc4 Nb6 24. d6 Rd8) 23... Rd5 24. Rfd1 Rxd6 25. Bxa5 Rxd1+ (25... Bf8!?) 26. Rxd1 Rxa5 (26... Ne5!?) (26... Nf8!? 27. Rd5 $140 b6 28. Bxb6 Rxa2) 27. Rxd7 Rxa2 28. Bc4 Ra1+ 29. Kh2 b5 30. Bxf7+ Kf8 31. Be6 Be5+ 32. g3 Ra6 33. Bd5 Rd6 $11) 22. Rfd1 cxd5 23. cxd5 Nf6 24. Be1 Nxd5?! {Starting a series of inaccuracies, which eventually lead to a lost endgame.} (24... Rxd5 25. Rxd5 Nxd5 26. Rc5 Nb4 27. Bxb4 axb4 28. Bc4 Rd8! $11) 25. Rc5 Nb4 26. Rxa5! $14 Nc2 27. Bd2 Rxa5 (27... Rac8 28. Rb5 Rc7 29. Ba5 Rxd1+ 30. Bxd1 Na3 31. Bxc7 Nxb5 32. Bb6 $14) 28. Bxa5 Ra8 29. Rd5 Bf8?! {It was absolutely crucial not to allow the white bishop to the a2-g8 diagonal.} (29... Na3! 30. Bb4 (30. Bd1 Nc4 31. Bb4 Bf8 $14) 30... Bf8 $14 31. Bxa3 Rxa3 32. Bc4 Rc3 33. Bb3 Kg7! 34. g3 (34. Rd7 Rc1+ 35. Kh2 Rc7!) 34... Rc7 35. Rb5 f5 $16 {with a better defense than he had in the game.}) 30. Bd1 Nb4 31. Bxb4 Bxb4 32. Bb3 {[%csl Gb3,Re4,Rf7][%mdl 4096] Despite the opposite-coloured bishops, Black's position is difficult because White's bishop is much stronger and the f7 pawn is under pressure. Material losses are inevitable.} b6 (32... Re8 33. Rb5 Bd6 34. Rxb7 {is hardly a lesser evil.}) 33. Rd7 {White is not hurrying to take the e4 pawn. It is more important to force the black rook to f8.} Rf8 34. g4 g5 35. Rb7 Bc5 36. Bd5 Kg7 37. Kf1 ({Again not} 37. Bxe4 Re8 {[%cal Ye8e7]}) 37... h6 38. Ke2 Kf6 39. a4 Kg7 40. Rc7 Bb4 41. Bxe4 {It's time now!} Rd8 (41... Re8 42. Bd5 Re7 43. Rc6 Ba5 (43... Bc5? 44. a5) 44. e4 {[%cal Ye2b5,Yc6b6]}) 42. Bd3 Kf8 43. Rb7 Rd6 44. Bc4 Rf6 {The rook is not much more active on f6 than on f8. White's winning plan is: f2-f4-f5 and then preparation of the exchange sacrifice on b6.} 45. Bd5 Be7 46. f3 Bb4 47. Kd3 Be1 48. Ke4 Bg3 49. Bc4 Be1 50. f4 gxf4 51. exf4 Bg3 52. f5 Be1 53. Bd5 Kg7 54. Kd4 h5 55. Kc4 hxg4 56. hxg4 Bf2 57. Be6 Be3 58. Kd5 Kf8 59. Ke5 Kg7 60. Ke4 Bd2 61. Kd3 Bf4 62. Kc4 Kf8 63. Bd5 Be3 64. Kb5 Kg7 65. Be6 Kf8 66. Bc4 Bf2 67. Bd5 Kg7 68. Bc6 Kh6 69. Rxb6! {Such an exchange sacrifice, creating a passed pawn, is a typical winning method in + vs + engings, with opposite-coloured bishops.} Kg5 70. a5! Rd6 (70... Kxg4 71. Bd7! Kg5 (71... Rxb6+ 72. axb6 Bg3 73. Ka6 Kf3 74. Kb7! $18) 72. Rxf6 Kxf6 73. Kc6 Ke7 74. a6 Kd8 (74... Be3 75. Kc7!) 75. f6! Bg1 76. Bg4 Bf2 77. Kb7 Be3 78. Bh5 $18) 71. Rb7 Kxg4 72. Rxf7 Kg5 73. Rd7 {The bishop endgame is now absolutely winning for White.} Rh6 74. Be4 Rh8 75. a6 Rb8+ 76. Kc4 Kf4 77. Re7 Rc8+ 78. Kd3 Rd8+ 79. Ke2 Bd4 80. Bd3 Bc5 81. Re6 Rh8 82. f6 Rh2+ 83. Kd1 Rh6 84. f7 1-0

